window.DEVOXX = {"event":"Devoxx Belgium 2026","videosCheckedAt":"2026-10-12","talks":[{"id":24228,"title":"Java, Raspberry Pi, and the Art of Overengineering Everyday Life","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["raspberry pi","sensors","java","home automation"],"abstract":["Home automation promises to make life easier. Naturally, I used it to make mine more complicated—and much more fun. In this session, I’ll show how a professional Java developer can turn a Raspberry Pi into a practical automation platform without abandoning familiar tools or disappearing down a Python-shaped rabbit hole.","We’ll tour two of my real projects. A BBQ smoker controller drives micro servos and a fan, reads half a dozen temperature sensors, and warns me when to add more wood or tame the flames. Another home server, located at the jetty, takes time-lapse photos of sunsets, measures the water level, and tells me when conditions are right for fishing—even while I’m away at Devoxx.","Along the way, I’ll cover the parts that usually make people hesitate: project structure, accessing hardware from Java, fast development loops, remote deployment, running services reliably, building mobile-friendly UIs for my iPhone—I want to control my BBQ smoker while fishing 🤓—and accessing those services securely over the internet.","No grand smart-home platform, no Python magic—just pure Java, a Raspberry Pi, sensors, motors, and a healthy willingness to overengineer everyday life."],"favourites":230,"speakers":[{"name":"Matti Tahvonen","company":"Vaadin","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AAcHTtcaGVDq7wOV5bdJHmWadJ7BtgNI9OrH-zBAbFvCpB2We-M=s96-c","bio":"Matti Tahvonen has worked on web apps for over two decades. Vaadin’s first-ever JavaScript developer, he has evolved into a Java/JVM enthusiast over the years while working with an open-source Java web framework and building a business around it. When Matti isn’t working, he spends most of his time on nature-related hobbies—and on developing software to support them.","linkedin":"mattitahvonen","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T12:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T12:50:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"5W4o_aYOaIY","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Tahvonen shows how a Java web developer can build real Raspberry Pi projects, from a BBQ smoker controller to a jetty camera, entirely in Java, and shares a starter project.","summary":["Tahvonen, a Java web developer with about 20 years of experience and one year of Raspberry Pi tinkering, started after promising Frank Delporte to update an old Vaadin demo. Home Assistant, written in Python, sat unused in a closet because he could not extend it. Instead he built his own systems: a jetty Raspberry Pi 5 that makes sunset time-lapses and tracks water level for fishing in the Turku archipelago, a start gate for orienteering races with up to 2,000 runners, a temperature monitor for his hunting club's meat cooler using Ruuvi Bluetooth sensors, and his main project, a battery-backed BBQ smoker controller.","The smoker reads MEATER and cheaper Bluetooth probes plus a thermocouple in the firebox, and runs a P controller that adjusts airflow. A solenoid valve let too little air through, so he hooked a micro servo to the butterfly valve of an old chainsaw carburettor. The fan uses software PWM over a 10-second cycle. The UI is a Vaadin progressive web app he controls from his iPhone. He prefers Quarkus over Spring Boot on these devices because it starts more than twice as fast.","His lessons: use a microcontroller rather than a JVM for a single wireless sensor; Pi4J is a low-level GPIO/I2C API, so use the Pi4J drivers (for example for the BME280) where they exist, and wrap C drivers with the FFM API otherwise. He vibe-coded a Java camera driver on top of libcamera. Skip breadboards for real builds and use WAGO lever connectors and soldering. Develop and test on the laptop with hardware simulated through CDI-injected alternative implementations.","The demo showed his starter repository: a Quarkus app with simulated-hardware mode, climate and actuator views, a system monitor that checks enabled interfaces, and tools for GPIO, an I2C bus scanner, PWM (moving a servo live) and Bluetooth scanning. A deploy script explodes the fat JAR, rsyncs only changed files, tunes JVM options for the Pi, and installs the app as a systemd service. For remote access he uses a single Cloudflare Tunnel on the Pi 5 for all home services, which provides the HTTPS needed for web push notifications, and recommends passkeys over passwords. He closed by advising people to set limits on how much time the hobby takes."],"keyPoints":["A Raspberry Pi Zero 2 (512 MB) works but crashed from heat when encoding time-lapse video; buy cases with active cooling, and buy spare Pis because they sell out.","Quarkus artifacts start more than twice as fast as Spring Boot ones on small Pis, which is why he prefers Quarkus there.","Pi4J is a low-level GPIO API; prefer the higher-level Pi4J drivers, contribute new ones, or use Java's FFM API to wrap existing C drivers.","Hardware simulation through CDI: inject a simulated implementation of the same interface for local development, with no simulation code in the device build.","His deploy script explodes the artifact, rsyncs only changed files, sets Pi-specific JVM options and user groups (e.g. Bluetooth), and runs the app as a systemd service.","Web push notifications in a PWA require HTTPS; one Cloudflare Tunnel on a single Pi can expose several services on the home network.","For authentication he recommends passkeys (the starter has a branch for it) over shared passwords, username/password or depending on an external OpenID Connect provider."],"takeaways":["Pick a real problem from your own life and build it in Java instead of working through breadboard tutorials.","Develop on your laptop with simulated hardware behind an interface, and automate rsync-based deploys to the Pi.","Use a cheap microcontroller for a lone wireless sensor and keep the Pi as the central Java server.","Expose home services through a tunnel such as Cloudflare Tunnel to get HTTPS, and protect them with passkeys."],"quotes":[{"text":"If you are not building anything industrial, it doesn't need to be bulletproof.","at":"15:24"},{"text":"Skip the tutorials. You are all adults here. You are all smart engineers and you all have agents to help you these days. So build something real.","at":"22:34"},{"text":"Set some limits, go fishing, enjoy your family, eat the barbecue.","at":"42:59"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Getting started with Pis"},{"at":"04:09","title":"Jetty camera and water level"},{"at":"07:16","title":"Orienteering gate and meat cooler"},{"at":"10:21","title":"The BBQ smoker controller"},{"at":"18:28","title":"Sensors, Pi4J and drivers"},{"at":"22:34","title":"Wiring and development lessons"},{"at":"27:45","title":"Starter project demo"},{"at":"35:53","title":"HTTPS, tunnels and passkeys"},{"at":"41:58","title":"Wrap-up"}],"tech":["Raspberry Pi 5","Raspberry Pi Zero 2","Pi4J","Quarkus","Spring Boot","Vaadin","Progressive web apps","Web Push","CDI","FFM API","libcamera","BME280","Bluetooth LE","Ruuvi","MEATER","rsync","systemd","Cloudflare Tunnel","ngrok","Passkeys"],"watchIf":"You are a Java developer who wants to try Raspberry Pi projects without switching to Python, and want a practical setup for development, deployment and remote access.","themes":["Hardware, robots & fun","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":8156,"title":"I Hired a Real Robot as My Junior Dev — and Now My Kids Don't Want to Learn Java Anymore","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["robotics","architecture","software","agents"],"abstract":["For decades we taught software engineering through languages: Java, C#, Python, patterns, frameworks — because writing software was hard. But what if that’s no longer the hard part?","Take my son. He isn’t learning Java; he’s learning to turn ideas into products. He spins up ideas at the speed of boredom — a study companion app one week, a gaming crosshair overlay the next — shipping from idea to code to demo before the README has found its shoes.","Meanwhile I hired Reachy Mini, a real open-source desktop robot, as my junior developer. I direct it, it drives agents that code in parallel while I sleep. I’m not really a developer anymore — I’m running an agent factory with a robot foreman on my desk.","Part stand-up routine, part field report, part uncomfortable question: are we raising the last generation of developers, or the first generation of agentic builders?","We’ll explore the agentic inner loop: describe intent, provide context, let agents act, review output, tighten the spec, and loop again until something useful ships. My bet: writing software is becoming a commodity — but expertise isn’t. Architecture, systems thinking, integration, taste, validation and knowing what to build only get more valuable. The interface shifts from typing to talking, from directing code to directing agents — and yes, sometimes the robot still needs a stern code review.","Audience: developers, leads and architects who suspect their job is changing and would rather laugh about it than panic."],"favourites":304,"speakers":[{"name":"Jan Van Wassenhove","company":"Sopra Steria","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-e7b52977-dc36-455d-a814-67a86c6644ab.jpg","bio":"Jan Van Wassenhove is the Technical Director of the System Integrations Business Line for the BeLux region at Sopra Steria, where he drives technology solutions and services, supported by his team of architects. With a strong background in Java development, Jan has evolved into an experienced architect and project manager, with in-depth expertise in Agile methodologies.<br><br>Remarkably, he is also the initiator of “SCRUM”, a new programming language he conceived. This creation perfectly reflects his innovative mindset and constant drive to explore new technological territories — and proves, with a wink, that everyone really can become a SCRUM master… in code.<br><br>Throughout his career, he has contributed to numerous projects in both the public and private sectors, with a particular focus on banking and public administrations.","linkedin":"jan-van-wassenhove-9b49893","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T10:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T10:50:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"P14Fy05Gs38","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Van Wassenhove gave a Reachy Mini robot an agent-built brain; six failures from running it at home show that specifying and verifying, not coding, are now the hard parts.","summary":["Van Wassenhove opens with his 13-year-old son, who ships apps \"at the speed of boredom\" without learning Java. To test what agentic AI looks like in practice, he gave a Reachy Mini, an open-source desktop robot with a Raspberry Pi in its head, a brain called Aura that runs on his laptop and was built almost entirely by agents he directs. His central claim: when making software gets cheap, the expensive parts are saying precisely what you want and judging whether what came back is any good. His workflow is a spec file, a failing test first, then one agent-built change per commit.","The first architecture was six Docker Compose services, which he collapsed into one brain on the laptop and one runtime on the robot, keeping module boundaries as packages. All keys, profiles and face prints live on the laptop, so stealing the robot gets you motors. He then walks through six failures, none caught by tests or alerts, all noticed by family. Face-print encryption silently fell back to plaintext when a release wiped the folder holding the passphrase, which sat in a plain settings file next to the encrypted records. It now lives in the OS keychain, with one key per person, so forgetting someone means destroying their key.","Memory mixed what people told it with what it inferred: it built a profile of his 11-year-old daughter (a songwriter currently in Scandinavia) from a metal playlist in the kitchen. Inferred memory now has its own label and is never sent to the model for minors. The robot answered a World Cup commentator on the TV, because the room closes the loop from speaker back to microphone. Being interrupted is now modelled as a state, not an error. For apps without APIs, the system prompt sets a ladder: API, CLI, files, app shortcuts, a real browser, and screen control only as a fenced last resort.","Self-improvement proposes skill rewrites that he approves. One tired late-night approval dropped the rule \"never say an app is unavailable unless you tried\", so code now restores protected rules word for word. Helper agents may read but not write, have few tries, and cannot spawn other agents. A fallback that echoed his input instead of answering ran unnoticed for days, so fallbacks must be loud. He closes with his bet: writing software is a commodity, but expertise, judgment and accountability are not."],"keyPoints":["Six Docker Compose microservices were collapsed into two deployables (laptop brain, robot runtime); the separate containers implied an independence that did not exist.","Architecture is deciding trust boundaries: all secrets and face prints live on the laptop, and the robot never calls back.","\"It's encrypted\" means nothing: the agent-built store kept the passphrase in a plain file next to the data. Fixed with OS keychain storage and per-person keys, so deletion is key destruction.","Per turn, a layer builds a short paragraph of context for the recognised person instead of sending profiles; inferred memory is labelled separately and excluded for children.","The room is part of the system: TV audio fed back into the microphone triggered answers to nobody, and speech-to-text confidently mis-transcribed his East Flemish dialect, once as Vietnamese.","A six-step integration ladder for apps without APIs: API, CLI, files, app keyboard shortcuts, browser automation, then screenshot-and-mouse with explicit approval.","Rules must be checks in code: a late-night human approval let a skill rewrite drop a key rule; helpers are read-only and cannot hire sub-agents.","None of the six failures was caught by a test or alert; family members noticed every one."],"takeaways":["Write security claims with an attacker in the sentence (\"someone who copies this folder gets nothing because the key is not in it\") and check them.","Keep facts users told the system separate from what an agent inferred, and label them differently in prompts.","Enforce agent limits as code checks, such as read-only helpers with no ability to spawn agents, rather than prompt instructions.","Make fallbacks loud: alert when a degraded mode is active instead of logging a warning nobody reads.","Review agent-built code for outcomes, especially security-sensitive paths, instead of trusting that it does what was asked."],"quotes":[{"text":"If you cannot name your boundaries, you do not have an architecture. You just have a deployment.","at":"11:20"},{"text":"Learning is not the same as knowing.","at":"25:35"},{"text":"A silent fallback is a bug that keeps your service up but gives nothing of value to you.","at":"39:48"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"A son who ships apps"},{"at":"04:06","title":"Reachy Mini and Aura"},{"at":"08:14","title":"Spec, test, collapse services"},{"at":"15:24","title":"Who is in the room"},{"at":"22:31","title":"Learning is not knowing"},{"at":"26:37","title":"The room talks back"},{"at":"30:39","title":"Apps without APIs"},{"at":"32:42","title":"Self-improvement and guardrails"},{"at":"37:46","title":"Loud fallbacks"},{"at":"40:50","title":"Expertise is the bet"}],"tech":["Reachy Mini","Raspberry Pi","Docker Compose","Speech-to-text","Face recognition","OS keychain","GitHub Copilot","Claude","ChatGPT","Spotify API","Local LLMs"],"watchIf":"You build agentic systems and want concrete failure stories about trust boundaries, memory, guardrails and silent fallbacks, told through a home robot.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Architecture & design","Hardware, robots & fun"],"demo":false}},{"id":7011,"title":"Secure Your Coding Agent Like It’s Malware","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["coding agents","sandbox security","prompt injection","data exfiltration"],"abstract":["Coding agents are dangerous: they can write files (including their own permissions or scripts meant to keep them in check), scan the filesystem, read credentials, and make tool calls that modify databases or exfiltrate data. Any content the agent processes (a pull request, an external code library, or online documentation) can trigger these actions and lead to persisted compromise. The 'Comment and Control' attack demonstrated this, as did the mini-Shai-Hulud attack, which copied its loader into Claude's settings.json, so it re-executed on every session start.","Built-in agent sandboxes are inadequate. Agents are very good at escaping them (see SandboxEscapeBench) but often need not, as they can disable the sandbox themselves, as specified in the Claude bash sandbox documentation.","Can we make coding agents secure? This talk is for developers running coding agents on their machines or in CI/CD. First we map the attack surface: prompt injection variations (e.g. role-play, poem attack) and how to hide them (Unicode smuggling, custom glyphs). Then we cover seven specific threats (supply chain compromise, data exfiltration) and seven defence layers.","We treat the agent itself as untrusted, clamping it down with externally enforced controls it cannot reach or reconfigure (VMs, network firewalls). Attendees will leave with a sandbox taxonomy to evaluate their own setups, a set of ready-to-use tools that score well against it, and a concrete plan to shrink their agents' blast radius."],"favourites":335,"speakers":[{"name":"Richard Gross","company":"MaibornWolff GmbH","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-7f802c4e-9a8f-467c-99d9-7d96c94b9ebc.jpg","bio":"Richard Gross is an IT archaeologist at MaibornWolff with more than ten years of modernization experience. His focus is on hexagonal architectures, hypermedia APIs, TestDSLs and the expressive and unambiguous modelling of the domain as code. He enjoys mastering TDD, BDD, DDD, decoupled design and even practices that don&#39;t include two D&#39;s. He also shaped the open source project CodeCharta, which lets even non-developers grasp the quality of their software.","linkedin":"richargh","bluesky":"richargh.de","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-05T14:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"GLJGhkxMdgU","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Prompt injection cannot be fully prevented, so Gross argues for treating coding agents as untrusted and running them in microVM sandboxes with network allowlists and externally injected credentials.","summary":["Gross starts from this year's agent-driven hacks, then explains the mechanics: the harness sends prompt, system prompt and a tool list (bash, skills, MCP tools) to the model and executes whatever tool calls come back, which is remote code execution by design. Entry points include poisoned training data, infected skills and MCP servers, harness exploits such as a GitHub Copilot auto-run link, and above all prompt injection. Injections hide in unrendered HTML, code comments, skills (Snyk found them in 36%), llms.txt, Unicode tag characters and remapped glyphs, and evade classifiers via Base64, DAN role-play, poems or a forged thinking trace. With no prepared statements for LLMs, probabilistic defences fail.","His answer is the lethal trifecta (private data, external actions, untrusted content: allow only two) plus a real box. Claude Code's built-in sandbox is not one, since Claude can rerun a command with it disabled. Containers fall to Docker-in-Docker, and on SandboxEscapeBench Claude Mythos Preview escaped every time. Agents escaped QEMU VMs but not Firecracker microVMs. A good sandbox is up to date, runs a hardened container in a microVM, allowlists folders and domains, injects credentials through a TLS-intercepting proxy, and monitors like a honeypot. He recommends the free Docker Sandboxes (sbx). Remaining leaks: allowed third-party hosts (SSRF), git hooks and rendered Markdown images."],"keyPoints":["A coding agent is remote code execution by design: it runs whatever tool calls the model returns, in an unbounded loop.","Prompt injection can be hidden in Unicode tag characters, remapped font glyphs, HTML source tags, code comments and llms.txt; Snyk reported injections in 36% of skills scanned.","Claude Code's /sandbox lets the agent itself retry a failed command with the sandbox disabled; plain containers are easily escaped, especially with Docker-in-Docker.","MicroVMs (for example Firecracker) held where QEMU VMs did not; options include Docker Sandboxes, microsandbox and NVIDIA OpenShell, all pre-1.0.","Inject credentials from outside the sandbox (e.g. `sbx` secret commands for OpenAI auth) so a compromised agent cannot exfiltrate them; watch for leaks through git hooks, rendered images and allowed hosts."],"takeaways":["Apply the lethal trifecta: never give one agent private data, external write or network access, and untrusted input all at once.","Run coding agents in a microVM-based sandbox such as Docker Sandboxes with folder and domain allowlists, not in the harness's built-in sandbox.","Keep API keys outside the sandbox and have a proxy inject them into outgoing requests.","Inspect .git/hooks after agent sessions, since git status does not show hook changes."],"quotes":[{"text":"Security based on probability is no security at all.","at":"15:34"},{"text":"The built-in Claude sandbox is not a sandbox.","at":"17:39"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Agents hacking companies"},{"at":"02:04","title":"How coding agents work"},{"at":"05:06","title":"Entry vectors and harness exploits"},{"at":"09:14","title":"Prompt injection techniques"},{"at":"15:34","title":"Why injection cannot be solved"},{"at":"16:36","title":"Lethal trifecta and sandboxes"},{"at":"22:42","title":"What makes a good sandbox"},{"at":"25:46","title":"Docker Sandboxes setup"},{"at":"28:50","title":"Remaining escape routes"}],"tech":["Claude Code","OpenAI Codex","GitHub Copilot","MCP","Agent skills","llms.txt","OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","SandboxEscapeBench","Docker","QEMU","Firecracker","Docker Sandboxes","microsandbox","NVIDIA OpenShell","Pi coding agent","Snyk"],"watchIf":"You run coding agents on your laptop or in CI and want a quick map of the injection threats and a concrete sandboxing setup.","themes":["Security & supply chain","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":7012,"title":"Destructoring is the future of Javas Encapsulation","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["encapsulation","serialization","destructuring","records"],"abstract":["Destructoring—the breakdown of an object into its constituent parts—is key to Java's ability to reliably maintain encapsulation and invariants. We resolve this apparent paradox in the presentation by looking at past and future language and platform features in the JDK.","Classic Java serialization breaks encapsulation: it directly accesses private fields, thereby bypassing constructors and the checks anchored there. The reason for this is that deconstruction and reconstruction have not been first-class citizens in the JDK until now.","This is precisely where current work within the JDK Project Amber comes in – under the working title “Derived Record/Class Creation.” This approach allows us to explicitly specify how objects are deconstructed and reconstructed. This will not only make serialization more secure and robust in the future, but we will also gain “withers” (targeted, immutable copies with individual modified components) for records and pattern matching, which will be extended beyond pure record patterns to normal classes.","Given these circumstances, it is worth taking a closer look: What is already there, what comes next – and what does this mean for our daily Java practice?"],"favourites":180,"speakers":[{"name":"Richard Gross","company":"MaibornWolff GmbH","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-7f802c4e-9a8f-467c-99d9-7d96c94b9ebc.jpg","bio":"Richard Gross is an IT archaeologist at MaibornWolff with more than ten years of modernization experience. His focus is on hexagonal architectures, hypermedia APIs, TestDSLs and the expressive and unambiguous modelling of the domain as code. He enjoys mastering TDD, BDD, DDD, decoupled design and even practices that don&#39;t include two D&#39;s. He also shaped the open source project CodeCharta, which lets even non-developers grasp the quality of their software.","linkedin":"richargh","bluesky":"richargh.de","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-05T10:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T11:20:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"wcghmWdu-HY","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Gross explains how serialization and deep reflection break encapsulation, and how Project Amber's planned deconstruction patterns would give Java safe serialization, pattern matching for classes and withers.","summary":["Gross shows an Adult class whose constructor rejects under-18s, yet after deserialization through JDK serialization, or Jackson with a no-arg constructor, it reports age 17. Built-in serialization sets private fields directly, and Jackson and Hibernate use deep reflection (setAccessible(true) on private final fields). With perhaps a third or more of Java vulnerabilities involving serialization, the JDK is moving to integrity by default: deprecating serialization, restricting deep reflection unless the application opts in, and JEP \"Prepare to Make Final Mean Final\". Records already go through their canonical constructor, but classes remain necessary when internal state differs from the API, like his timestamp that accepts a string but stores a long.","The missing concept, he argues, is a first-class deconstructor: the inverse of a constructor that says how to take an object apart. Using strawman syntax still under discussion in Project Amber, he shows what it would enable: two annotations that any serialization format (Jackson, Hibernate) could use, versioning, pattern matching on ordinary classes (no public JDK class is a record today), matching on factory methods like Optional.of(Ball.of(RED)), and class withers based on a declared canonical constructor, with record withers for free. In Q&A he compares this with Lombok's withers, notes that C# record withers clone objects without rerunning constructor checks, and explains why he writes builders by hand."],"keyPoints":["Both JDK serialization and Jackson-style deep reflection skip constructors, so constructor invariants cannot be trusted after deserialization.","The JDK is moving to integrity by default: restricting deep reflection unless the application opts in, and the JEP \"Prepare to Make Final Mean Final\".","Records are serialized through their canonical constructor and reject setAccessible on final fields, but classes remain necessary when stored state differs from the constructor API.","A deconstruction pattern as a first-class language feature would enable format-agnostic serialization, pattern matching on classes and factories, and withers that run through the constructor.","Jackson can avoid a default constructor today with the parameter-names module and the -parameters compiler flag, as long as field, constructor parameter and getter names match."],"takeaways":["Do not assume constructor validation holds for objects created by Jackson, Hibernate or Java serialization; validate after deserialization or use records.","Prefer records for data carriers so serialization goes through the canonical constructor.","Check whether your frameworks rely on deep reflection on final fields, since upcoming JDK releases will restrict it by default."],"quotes":[{"text":"Reflection is not the problem, deep reflection is.","at":"07:11"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Can we trust deserialized objects"},{"at":"04:07","title":"Encapsulation and deep reflection"},{"at":"07:11","title":"Integrity by default"},{"at":"10:14","title":"Records and their limits"},{"at":"14:18","title":"Deconstruction as a concept"},{"at":"16:21","title":"Serialization, patterns and withers"},{"at":"25:35","title":"Q&A: Lombok, Jackson, builders"}],"tech":["Java serialization","Reflection API","Project Amber","Records","Pattern matching","Withers","Jackson","Hibernate","Lombok","Kotlin","C#","JEP 500"],"watchIf":"You want to understand why deserialized Java objects can violate their own invariants and what Project Amber's deconstruction work is likely to bring.","themes":["Java language & JDK","Security & supply chain"],"demo":false}},{"id":5507,"title":"Stopping AI-Generated Architectural Drift: Deterministic Guardrails for Agentic Coding","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["agentic","guardrails","linters","archunit"],"abstract":["Agentic coding generates code faster than any team can review it, and that speed lets a single questionable pattern spread across a codebase before anyone notices. This talk shows how to stop that drift at the source, with layered guardrails that make architectural violations structurally hard to introduce instead of something you catch too late in review.","Drawn from reviewing hundreds of AI-assisted pull requests: natural-language (NL) repo rules don't hold the line against an LLM. The reliable fix is a deterministic foundation, executable architectural rules acting as fitness functions for AI-generated code, built with ArchUnit, custom linters, and CI gates that fail the build the instant a boundary is crossed. That layer is also what makes NL rules work: once it fences off the cases that must never be wrong, targeted NL rules become precise instead of suggestions an agent ignores. Includes live demos of guardrails catching violations in real time.","Takeaways: why NL rules fail to stop LLMs propagating bad patterns; how to build a deterministic enforcement layer (ArchUnit, custom linters, CI gates) with instant feedback; how that layer makes your NL rules reliable; a practical model for sorting any rule into the right layer.","Audience: software architects, tech leads, and senior engineers maintaining architectural consistency on AI-augmented teams."],"favourites":268,"speakers":[{"name":"Sasha Podlesniuk","company":"INSHUR","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-48d9d8c1-f223-44ec-ba3e-6aee8911b78e.jpg","bio":"I am a Domain Tech Lead at INSHUR with more than 12 years of experience working on digital transformation initiatives across fintech, insurtech, and cybersecurity. My work has included modernizing legacy platforms, migrating monoliths to microservices, and building large-scale cloud-native systems.<br>More recently, I have focused on applying AI to digital transformation, leading the development of agentic AI systems and integrating large language models into operational workflows. I am interested in making AI and complex technology initiatives practical, accessible, and understandable across engineering, operations, and business teams.","linkedin":"sasha-podlesniuk","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-05T15:35:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T16:05:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"K3kD89Y1YCk","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Podlesniuk shows how INSHUR turns natural-language agent rules into executable ArchUnit, Error Prone and Checkstyle checks that coding agents hit during the build and fix themselves.","summary":["Podlesniuk (INSHUR) opens with three agent PRs for the same task under the same CLAUDE.md rules: two injected a Clock, one still called Instant.now(). His team now turns prose rules into checks that are executable, deterministic, fast, debuggable (failures name class, line and fix) and testable, using Checkstyle for source text, ArchUnit for compiled dependencies and Error Prone for method bodies. In a demo service migrating from service classes to a mediator with handlers, ArchUnit rules such as \"no service packages\" and \"every @RestController injects a Mediator\" run as JUnit tests. An agent nudged toward the old style built a service, hit the failing test, and rewrote the feature correctly.","For drift already merged, an agent loops until ArchUnit exit criteria pass: no direct handler access in tests, no mocked repositories, @SpringBootTest on integration tests. A custom Claude Code command does architectural mutation testing, proposing plausible architectures that rename, reroute or invert dependencies; one survivor injected a repository into a controller. An Error Prone BugChecker suggests moving private handler methods that only read an aggregate root, and a Checkstyle rule forbids comments without a reason tag. New rules come from recurring PR review comments. Costs: over-strict rules, longer builds, more agent iterations and team adoption, so start with warnings or ArchUnit frozen rules."],"keyPoints":["Natural-language rules in CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md are not reliably followed; one of three agent runs ignored a word-for-word rule to inject Clock.","Deterministic checks should be executable, deterministic, fast, debuggable and testable; the failure message is the agent's context for fixing the violation.","Tool split: Checkstyle for source-level rules (naming, nesting, comments), ArchUnit for dependencies and layers, Error Prone BugCheckers for patterns inside method bodies.","Architectural mutation testing: an agent invents plausible architectures that violate a rule's intent (rename, reroute, invert); survivors reveal loopholes such as a repository injected into a controller.","Claude Code hooks block the agent from editing architecture test paths, and shared rules can be distributed across repositories as a versioned library."],"takeaways":["Turn recurring PR review comments into ArchUnit or Checkstyle rules that fail the build with a specific reason and location.","Test each rule against small positive and negative example classes to prove it catches what it should.","Use architecture tests as exit criteria and let an agent loop to fix existing drift.","Protect rule files from agent edits with hooks, and roll out new rules as warnings or ArchUnit frozen rules before blocking."],"quotes":[{"text":"The goal here is not to make everything deterministic but to make everything deterministic that we reliably can.","at":"19:29"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Agents ignore written rules"},{"at":"01:04","title":"Properties of deterministic checks"},{"at":"03:07","title":"Checkstyle, ArchUnit, Error Prone"},{"at":"04:07","title":"ArchUnit rules live demo"},{"at":"09:11","title":"Fixing existing drift"},{"at":"11:15","title":"Architectural mutation testing"},{"at":"14:20","title":"Error Prone and Checkstyle checks"},{"at":"18:29","title":"Costs and Q&A"}],"tech":["ArchUnit","Error Prone","Checkstyle","JUnit","Spring Boot","Claude Code","Claude Code hooks","CLAUDE.md","AGENTS.md","Mediator pattern"],"watchIf":"Your team merges a lot of agent-written code and you want concrete ways to enforce architecture rules in the build instead of in prompts.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Architecture & design","Testing & quality"],"demo":true}},{"id":23527,"title":"Project Detroit: From Foreign Libraries to Foreign Language Runtimes","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["python","ffm","v8","javascript"],"abstract":["Interacting with foreign libraries from Java has never been easier, thanks to the Foreign Function & Memory (FFM) API. But interacting with entire foreign language runtimes, like JavaScript or Python, remains hard.","Historically, Java tried to solve this by rewriting these runtimes in Java. Nashorn, introduced in JDK 8, successfully brought JavaScript to Java applications. However, due to increasing maintenance costs and compatibility lags, it was deprecated in Java 11 and later removed in Java 15.","Project Detroit paves a new way forward: instead of implementing bespoke language runtimes from scratch, it leverages FFM to embed industry-standard native runtimes like V8 and CPython in the safety and comfort of your Java application.","This session is a deep dive into the obscure art of runtime bridging. From juggling two garbage collectors to avoiding virtual thread pinning, we will show what really happens when the JVM rubber meets the foreign road."],"favourites":106,"speakers":[{"name":"Maurizio Cimadamore","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-66579526-c2c5-46b5-87ed-6bb6f34121af.jpg","bio":"Maurizio Cimadamore is a compiler architect at Oracle. He is currently working on the language and runtime aspects of many foundational projects that will change the shape of the Java platform, such as Project Valhalla and Project Panama.<br>In the previous years, he has contributed to the design and implementation of several features of the Java programming language, such as diamond inference, lambda expressions and local variable type-inference. Maurizio holds a PhD in language design and implementation.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T08:20:00Z","room":"Room 10","videoId":"JWFZY0aJTjg","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Cimadamore explains why Nashorn failed and how Project Detroit instead embeds V8 and CPython behind the javax.script API, and what that costs in upcall performance, lifetimes and virtual-thread pinning.","summary":["Cimadamore starts with why Java still needs foreign language runtimes: many applications use JavaScript through javax.script as a DSL (even Ant has a script task), and AI workloads depend on Python libraries such as PyTorch. Nashorn, led by Jim Laskey and built on invokedynamic, shipped in Java 8 with ECMAScript 5.1 and strong Java interop. Then came ES2015 and a new standard every year, while npm grew from 6,000 to 50,000 packages in two years, and running Node.js on Nashorn would have needed bug-for-bug V8 compatibility. Nashorn was deprecated in JDK 11 and removed in JDK 15. The lesson: a runtime written in Java is a permanent, industrial-scale commitment.","The alternative came from FFM and jextract: a jextract sample already ran a Python script with a few lines of generated bindings. Project Detroit, proposed by Laskey in 2017 with JNI and restarted this year, wraps the real V8 and CPython behind javax.script, so Nashorn code mostly carries over. The JavaScript engine uses JNI for now and has a bidirectional flavour and a restricted one exposing only what the developer chooses; the Python engine uses jextract and FFM. The demo ran a Python REPL on Detroit in a Python 3.14 virtual environment, then a Gradio image classifier using Hugging Face Transformers and PyTorch installed with plain pip.","Then the seams. On Octane 2.0, Detroit JS is far faster than Nashorn and GraalJS, but sorting a JavaScript array with a Java comparator, which needs many native-to-Java upcalls, was many times slower than both. Two collectors (or Python's reference counting) can each see only half of a cross-runtime cycle, so Detroit Python offers explicit decref and FFM-style arenas. A virtual thread that blocks inside a Java callback called from V8 gets pinned because of the native frame; wrapping the call in a promise and using async/await in JavaScript avoids it.","Finally, maintainability: Detroit Python is about 6,000 lines of maintained code with no native code, while the JNI-based JS bridge was twice that, half of it C++. A flat C API over V8, run through jextract, cut native code from about 7,000 to under 2,000 lines and total maintained code from 13-14,000 to 5,500. It also sped up the comparator case, since FFM upcalls are cheaper than JNI ones and C2 can speculate on marshalling switches written in Java. In Q&A he mentioned that confined arenas in JDK 28 will pool memory per thread, with speed-ups around 20x reported for allocate-call-free patterns."],"keyPoints":["Nashorn fell behind because JavaScript moved to yearly standards and Node.js compatibility would have required bug-for-bug V8 emulation; it was removed in JDK 15 by JEP 372.","Project Detroit embeds V8 and CPython rather than reimplementing them, so standards compatibility and the npm/pip ecosystems come for free.","Both engines are exposed through javax.script, so ScriptEngineManager code written for Nashorn mostly carries over; a restricted JS flavour exposes only chosen Java objects.","Pure JavaScript execution is much faster than Nashorn and GraalJS on Octane 2.0, but callback-heavy code that crosses back into Java is many times slower.","Cross-runtime reference cycles cannot be collected by either side; Detroit Python provides explicit decref and arena-based lifetimes modelled on FFM.","Blocking in Java code called from V8 pins a virtual thread; returning a promise and awaiting it in JavaScript avoids the pinning.","A flat C wrapper over V8's C++ API plus jextract cut native code roughly fourfold and improved upcall performance thanks to FFM upcalls and C2 speculation."],"takeaways":["If you still depend on Nashorn or javax.script, try the experimental Detroit engines and report compatibility and performance issues to the project.","Keep interop between Java and a scripting runtime shallow: call into Python or JavaScript for a result instead of fine-grained back-and-forth callbacks.","When scripts call blocking Java code on virtual threads, make the callback asynchronous (a promise plus await) to avoid pinning.","For your own native integrations, consider a flat C API extracted with jextract instead of hand-written JNI glue."],"quotes":[{"text":"Maintaining and implementing a foreign language runtime requires permanent industrial-scale commitment.","at":"10:20"},{"text":"Why would you want to rewrite those things in Java and not just access them from Java?","at":"11:22"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why reach foreign runtimes"},{"at":"05:12","title":"The rise and fall of Nashorn"},{"at":"11:22","title":"FFM and jextract approach"},{"at":"14:24","title":"Project Detroit architecture"},{"at":"17:28","title":"Demo: Python and Gradio"},{"at":"23:33","title":"Performance of the bridge"},{"at":"27:39","title":"Lifetimes across two runtimes"},{"at":"31:44","title":"Virtual thread pinning"},{"at":"34:47","title":"JNI versus FFM maintainability"},{"at":"43:58","title":"Q&A and arena pooling"}],"tech":["Project Detroit","FFM API","jextract","JNI","V8","CPython","javax.script","Nashorn","Rhino","GraalJS","Node.js","npm","Octane 2.0","Gradio","Hugging Face Transformers","PyTorch","Virtual threads","invokedynamic","JEP 372","Project Babylon"],"watchIf":"You run JavaScript or Python from Java, or are interested in what bridging two language runtimes involves in garbage collection, threading and native interop.","themes":["Java language & JDK","JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":true}},{"id":25042,"title":"Owning the Code You Didn't Type","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["readability","understanding","control","code"],"abstract":["For most of our careers, the scarce thing was producing code. That world is ending. An assistant now produces competent code faster than any of us can read it, and the bottleneck has moved from writing to understanding.","Nothing moved on the responsibility side, though. We are still accountable for every line we merge, no matter who or what typed it. Staying in the loop, knowing the system, owning the design, that is the job description now.","This talk is not a manifesto, it is a set of recipes. The practices I use daily to stay in control of code that arrives faster than I could write it:","Phased delivery - Large tasks are split into small phases with a review gate after each one. No big-bang diffs.","Walk-throughs - The assistant guides me through a change step by step, in reading order, instead of leaving me alone with a raw diff.","Made for humans - Correct is not the bar, understandable is. Design and readability are acceptance criteria, and I send back what does not meet them.","Engineering depth - Know your stuff. The deeper you understand your system, the harder it is to be fooled by a confident, plausible answer.","Files and comments - Sessions produce files and in-code comments instead of long chat threads. Easy to navigate, easy to annotate for the assistant.","Together these form a flow that keeps me in control and grows my knowledge instead of eroding it. You will leave with techniques you can apply the next day, whatever assistant you use."],"favourites":371,"speakers":[{"name":"Renato Guerra Cavalcanti","company":"Octo Cogito","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-c563b2ce-c0f7-426b-8f1f-81169b8efe6b.jpg","bio":"Renato Cavalcanti is an independent software engineer and consultant specializing in distributed systems, with deep expertise in Event Sourcing, CQRS, and Domain-Driven Design. He has been a contributor to Akka since 2017, working on the Akka libraries, and most recently Akka Runtime and Akka SDK, where he applies distributed systems principles, such as durability, workflows, supervision, and observability, to the design of reliable multi-agent AI systems.<br><br>A Scala developer since 2009, he founded BeScala, the Belgian Scala User Group, and is a regular speaker at Scala, Java, and broader developer conferences, as well as workshops on Scala and Event Sourcing.","linkedin":"renatocavalcanti","bluesky":"renato.cavalcanti.io","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T14:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"SQUrHzMie3o","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Cavalcanti argues that delegating coding to AI removes the activity that built developers' understanding, and shares practices for staying in control: writing rules, files over chat, walk-throughs, phased delivery.","summary":["Cavalcanti, a former psychologist who now works on the Akka runtime, starts from what writing code does to us: translating requirements into code, doubting and experimenting is how we build a mental model of a system and our identity as developers. He separates fluency (syntax, idioms, standard library) from depth (runtime, memory model, concurrency). His warning example came this week: reviewing its own Scala for-comprehension in a fresh context, Claude twice claimed a failed future would be silently recovered, which is as wrong as thinking thenCompose runs after a failed CompletableFuture. Had he accepted the bug report, the agent would have rewritten correct code for nothing.","Without guidance a model drifts to the centre of its training distribution and produces average code; your knowledge is what pulls it away. He cites Lisanne Bainbridge's 1983 \"Ironies of Automation\": automate most of the work and operators lose their skills while left with tiring monitoring tasks. He fears turning code review from \"looks good to me\" into \"looks green to me\". Vibe-coding a Rust tool for his own machine is fine because nobody else is affected, but bank systems are not.","His practices start with writing rules that demand clarity over style, because agents write terse inter-agent prose into chats, Javadoc and comments, and bad patterns in the codebase (constructors that silently turn null Optionals into defaults) become tomorrow's context. Rules loaded at session start lose attention as context grows, so a hook re-injects a reminder with every prompt. He keeps summaries and notes in a never-committed nogit folder listed in his global gitignore, writes questions directly in files, and has a command answer them. A walk-through command orders a change into logical reading steps instead of alphabetical diffs.","Phased delivery is where he feels like an engineer again: after spec and plan, the agent implements one small phase, he reviews, edits or takes the next phase himself, and he writes API shapes by hand while the agent generates the plumbing. In autonomous mode he requires one commit per phase with a description, which also gives a second reviewing agent context. AI has been very good at finding flaky concurrency tests at Akka, but he insists on slowing down to build knowledge, since verification is not automated and accountability remains. He shares his commands on GitHub (octonato) and recommends Anthropic's PR Review Toolkit and the Superpowers plugins."],"keyPoints":["Writing code built the mental model of the system; delegating non-trivial design and coding removes that learning unless you replace it deliberately.","A fresh-context Claude review confidently reported a non-existent bug in a Scala future chain; acting on it would have churned correct code.","Unguided models drift to average code; the developer's knowledge acts as the anchor that pulls output away from the centre.","The Bainbridge automation paradox: operators who only monitor automated work lose the skills needed for the part that cannot be automated.","Rules in agent instruction files lose attention as context grows; a hook that appends a short reminder to every prompt kept Claude following his writing rules.","Code in the repo is the agent's context for the next change, so one bad pattern (null Optional coerced to defaults in constructors) spreads.","Walk-through commands order a change into logical reading steps; phased delivery with one commit and description per phase keeps a human gate after each step."],"takeaways":["Add writing rules that demand plain, clear English from your agent, and re-inject them with a prompt hook if they stop being followed.","Have the agent write summaries and walk-throughs to an uncommitted folder, and annotate questions directly in files instead of in chat.","Split agent implementation into small phases, review each one, and require one commit per phase with a description.","Keep hands-on work such as API design for yourself, and delegate the mechanical code behind it.","Before accepting an AI-reported bug, check it against your own understanding of the language and runtime."],"quotes":[{"text":"Your knowledge is what pulls the model away from the center.","at":"18:27"},{"text":"The code today is the context of tomorrow.","at":"27:44"},{"text":"Don't outsource your brain.","at":"46:04"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Coding shapes our identity"},{"at":"07:10","title":"Fluency versus depth"},{"at":"10:15","title":"Understanding takes the time"},{"at":"15:25","title":"Models drift to average"},{"at":"18:27","title":"The automation paradox"},{"at":"24:39","title":"Writing rules and hooks"},{"at":"32:50","title":"Files instead of chat"},{"at":"35:53","title":"Walk-throughs"},{"at":"37:54","title":"Phased delivery"},{"at":"42:59","title":"Slow down and own it"}],"tech":["Claude Code","Claude Code hooks","Claude Opus","Scala","Akka","CompletableFuture","Rust","Spec-driven development","PR Review Toolkit","Superpowers"],"watchIf":"You use coding agents daily and feel your grasp of your own codebase slipping, and want specific habits for staying in control.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Teams, careers & leadership","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":false}},{"id":11556,"title":"223 Pull Requests in 11 Days: The Recipe for Building Real Software with AI Agents","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["developer","ai","spring boot","automation"],"abstract":["I've always wanted to create a developer UI for Spring Boot, but the task was massive. The project I ended up doing has 83,000 lines of code, 116 test suites... The honest by-hand estimate for one experienced developer: 6.5 to 8.5 months.","I did it in about 11 days, shipping version 1 after less than 2 weeks.","Not by typing faster: I didn't even open my IDE. I did this by managing a fleet of AI agents: 223 merged pull requests, around 20 a day, with the agents doing all the scaffolding, the panels and the tests while I architected, reviewed and steered. That's roughly a 12–17× reduction in human effort, as measured from my git history and PR metadata.","This talk is about showing you the recipe for delivering at this speed, using modern AI tools. I'll show you how to run it: write the specifications, the test harness, running agents in parallel... And working differently: prep the agents in the afternoon, let them build overnight, merge the results over coffee the next day.","And as a bonus, we get a great new Spring Boot developer console!"],"favourites":626,"speakers":[{"name":"Julien Dubois","company":"GitHub","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-832ab0d0-7760-423f-a78e-7f767c7e95b8.jpg","bio":"Julien Dubois is a Java Champion and a Principal Manager at <a href=\"https://www.microsoft.com/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--primary-color);\">Microsoft</a>. With his team of world-class developer advocates, Julien works directly with the engineering groups to improve how developers can leverage AI using Microsoft and GitHub solutions.<br><br>With over 25 years of experience in the Java ecosystem, Julien is best known as the creator of <a href=\"https://www.jhipster.tech/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--primary-color);\">JHipster</a>, an Open Source platform with 22,000+ GitHub stars that revolutionized how developers build Spring Boot applications. His recent work focuses on bringing enterprise-grade AI capabilities to Java through his contributions to <a href=\"https://github.com/langchain4j/langchain4j\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--primary-color);\">LangChain4j</a> and <a href=\"https://spring.io/projects/spring-ai\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--primary-color);\">Spring AI</a>.<br><br>Julien is a recognized thought leader in the Java community, having spoken at over 200 international conferences including Devoxx, Spring One, and Microsoft Build. He authored <em style=\"color: var(--primary-color);\"><a href=\"https://www.julien-dubois.com/book\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Spring par la pratique</a></em>, the first French book on the Spring Framework, which became a bestseller. His presentations typically feature live-coding sessions and practical workshops that help developers immediately apply new technologies.","linkedin":"juliendubois","bluesky":null,"twitter":"juliendubois"}],"start":"2026-10-08T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T08:20:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"0A2jqCqSIjg","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Dubois built Boot UI, a Spring Boot developer console, in 11 days through 223 agent-written pull requests, and shares six ingredients: model choice, specs, tests, tools, parallelism, overnight runs.","summary":["Dubois, creator of JHipster and now at GitHub, built an open-source project so he could show real numbers: Boot UI, a developer console for Spring Boot (Spring, Hibernate, a Vue.js front end) he had estimated at over six months of solo work. Time-boxed to 11 days of normal hours, it reached 1.0.0 with 223 merged pull requests, about 20 a day, mostly committed by Copilot agents in parallel, with unusually large amounts of specs and tests. He puts the gain at 15 to 20 times, for about $2,000 in tokens. The project has since passed 500 PRs, 20 releases and nearly 400,000 lines without a maintenance tax.","His ingredients: stick with one main model, partly to keep the 90% cached-token discount, have other models \"rubber duck\" complex work (half his tokens), and use small models for speed. Write specs: an AGENTS.md pinning Maven, JUnit, Docker Compose and Spotless so parallel agents stay consistent, plus specs defining done. Write tests: agents loop locally, then on GitHub Actions with CodeQL, fixing failed PRs themselves. Specs and tests form the harness, which he says matters more than the model. Equip agents: the Impeccable UI skill, Copilot's agent-merge (he never resolved a conflict by hand), a custom release skill, and the Eclipse JDT language server, saving about 20% of tokens.","The human ingredients: run many independent tasks in parallel and act as the manager of a fleet, and restructure the day. From 11:00 he orchestrates normal tasks, around 17:00 he writes specs for two or three big tasks to run overnight, and from 9:00 to 11:00 he reviews and merges their results. Caveats: Boot UI's panels are repetitive and Spring is well documented, so expect perhaps 10x elsewhere; beware scope creep, too many large PRs and underpowered models. GitHub issues and PR comments act as specs, and the Copilot mobile app and cloud agent (one-hour limit) take experiments off his laptop.","On token economics, a quiz showed that 2,000 output tokens cost more than 100,000 cached input tokens, and that switching to a half-price model can cost more because it busts the cache, as do editing AGENTS.md, compacting history or a five-minute coffee break. Caching saves only about 51% overall because output is uncached. Copilot's auto mode gives a 10% discount and is cache-aware, and a research-preview Fusion mode cascades and cross-checks models. He ended by starting a release preparation from his own agent and skill in the Copilot app, and noted that Boot UI is itself an MCP server and skill."],"keyPoints":["Boot UI 1.0.0: 223 merged PRs in 11 working days, versus his estimate of more than six months by hand, at roughly $2,000 in tokens.","Half of his tokens go to rubber ducking, where other models audit what the main model produced.","The harness (AGENTS.md, specs with a definition of done, local and CI tests) matters more than which model you use.","Pointing agents at the Eclipse JDT language server gives exact Java semantics and cuts token use by around 20%, at the cost of one Eclipse backend per agent.","Work rhythm: parallel tasks during the day, specs for big tasks written around 17:00 to run overnight, morning review over coffee; his own review is the bottleneck.","Cache economics: cached input gets a 90% discount, but switching model, editing AGENTS.md, compacting or idling a few minutes busts the cache.","GitHub Advanced Security (CodeQL, Dependabot, secret protection) is part of the agent's feedback loop, not just a human safeguard."],"takeaways":["Write an AGENTS.md that pins your build tools, test approach and formatting so parallel agents produce consistent code.","Have a second model review complex agent output before you look at it.","Prepare two or three well-specified large tasks at the end of the day and let agents run them overnight.","Turn any process you have done by hand two or three times, such as a release, into a custom skill.","Avoid switching models or editing agent instructions mid-session if you want to keep cached-token discounts."],"quotes":[{"text":"Most people complain that LLMs are slow. I complain that my laptop is slow.","at":"15:18"},{"text":"If you want something better, invest your time in specifications and in testing.","at":"17:20"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Building Boot UI in 11 days"},{"at":"03:07","title":"The numbers from git"},{"at":"08:11","title":"Agentic engineering, not vibe coding"},{"at":"09:13","title":"Picking the right model"},{"at":"12:15","title":"Specifications and tests as harness"},{"at":"17:20","title":"Equipping agents with tools"},{"at":"22:28","title":"Parallel agents and overnight runs"},{"at":"27:33","title":"Caveats and the GitHub platform"},{"at":"35:41","title":"Token economics"},{"at":"44:57","title":"Copilot app demo"}],"tech":["GitHub Copilot","Copilot coding agent","GitHub Actions","CodeQL","Dependabot","GitHub Advanced Security","Spring Boot","Hibernate","Vue.js","Maven","JUnit","Docker Compose","Spotless","AGENTS.md","Agent skills","MCP","Language Server Protocol","Eclipse JDT Language Server","Impeccable","Boot UI","Quarkus"],"watchIf":"You want a measured account of shipping a real Java project with a fleet of coding agents, including the workflow, costs and token-caching details.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Developer experience & tooling","Spring & frameworks"],"demo":true}},{"id":4090,"title":"Help! I hate my team mate!","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"People, Practices & Career","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["cooperation","communication","conflict","teamwork"],"abstract":["Everybody now and then comes into a new team, or someone else comes into theirs. More often than not, cooperation does not go seamlessly straight away. Sometimes this evens out by itself along the way. But in many occasions, it does not. Ways of working could be different. Something one values a lot, may not be valued at all by another, creating conflict. One may criticise or get criticised and things get uncomfortable. Teams may split into \"camps\" or one person just never agrees with the others. Team members may feel stepped over, pushed aside or misunderstood. If this goes on for too long, team members start to feel generally disrespected and demotivated. Then there are cases one member feels another is not qualified, or just a bully. This goes beyond forming and storming, this becomes a (team-wide) problem. In this talk you will see you are not the only one experiencing these challenges. You will hear experiences on solving these kind of cases (or not!), get an idea of how others deal with it and what your options are if you ever find yourself in the weeds (again). In any case, you probably will be surprised by yourself or your team mate in one way or another..."],"favourites":326,"speakers":[{"name":"Jessica Siewert","company":"Het ConsultancyHuis","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-e589f7fe-c186-49f3-adc0-9c3917209720.jpg","bio":"Jessica is a software developer specialised in micro services in Scala and Kotlin. She has worked in Finance, logistics and since a few years, she has devoted herself to contribute to the Dutch Energy Transition. For this reason she joined Het ConsultancyHuis four years ago. Jessica has a great sense of team building and working relationships. To this end, during her 14 years in the field, she has taken several training programmes and has fulfilled multiple roles as scrum master of teams. Even when she is not the scrum master, she takes pride in making significant contributions to the maturity of the team. In addition, in order to provide the users and people in The Netherlands a better experience regarding the enormous upgrade of the Dutch energy network, she enjoys working together with neighbouring teams. She likes to share her experiences in both engineering and working relationships with her fellow crafts(wo)men.","linkedin":"jesiewert","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T15:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T16:30:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"sWP9osLV45I","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Siewert combines conflict theory, Dutch workplace statistics and two cases from her own career into five ways to get out of team conflicts and three ways to prevent them.","summary":["Siewert, a Dutch developer (Scala, Java, Kotlin) and occasional Scrum Master at Het ConsultancyHuis, limits the talk to conflicts where both sides mean well; structural bad intent needs a confidential advisor. Dutch research she cites: managers in large organisations spend about 20% of their time on team conflict, and at least 70,000 people a year call in sick over it. She describes five phases: unaware, rational (still win-win), emotional (win-lose), escalation (only losers), and resolution or not. Models such as DISC, Insights and Management Drives help, as does valuing opposites: theoretical versus pragmatic, starters versus finishers, facilitating versus directing.","With Mentimeter polls she walked through two cases. As Scrum Master, after a junior teammate's mistake caused a 5 a.m. production incident at an uptime-focused SaaS company, she was overruled by the colleague responsible for SLAs, who wanted to discuss it with the whole company at once. After a day of silence they talked, found they shared the goal of quality and stability, and worked together four more years. In a remote COVID-era team, a new teammate repeated the same stand-up update without delivering or accepting help. She raised it with the Scrum Master twice, three months apart, then backed off; he eventually left by his own choice.","Her five ways out: agree on a shared goal while the conflict is still rational; use each other's strengths and protect each other from weaknesses; when camps form, find a third way; never vent to a third party, and adapt critique to culture (colleagues have an LLM rephrase blunt Dutch feedback for recipients in India, Britain or Egypt); and learn from someone you dislike, who is often good at what you are not. To prevent conflict: kickoffs that go beyond pizza to share values and backgrounds, a compliment exercise where each person draws a teammate's name, and yearly sessions on what each person brings and wants to learn."],"keyPoints":["Dutch research she cites: managers spend about 20% of their time on team conflict, and at least 70,000 people a year call in sick because of workplace conflict.","Conflicts move from unaware to rational (win-win still possible) to emotional (win-lose) to escalation (only losers); exits are easiest in the rational phase.","35% of team conflicts involve two people, 20-25% centre on one individual, and about 20% involve subgroups taking sides.","A shared goal (quality and uptime) resolved her conflict with a colleague who overruled her after a production incident.","Venting to a third party gives short-term relief but damages trust, because listeners wonder what you say about them.","Good teams use each other's strengths and also protect each other from each other's weaknesses."],"takeaways":["When frustrated with a teammate, ask yourself whether you are prejudging them based on earlier experiences.","Start a difficult conversation by establishing the goal you both share.","Run a team kickoff that covers values and backgrounds, not just the project.","Try the compliment envelope: each team member draws a name and gives that person a sincere compliment.","Reach out to someone independent, such as a Scrum Master or agile coach, early rather than late."],"quotes":[{"text":"It's not only about using each other's strengths, but it's also about protecting each other from each other's weaknesses.","at":"37:08"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Margaret Hamilton and intro"},{"at":"07:12","title":"Why team conflict matters"},{"at":"11:22","title":"Prevention and intent"},{"at":"13:25","title":"Five phases of conflict"},{"at":"16:27","title":"Personalities and differences"},{"at":"21:39","title":"Case: production incident clash"},{"at":"28:56","title":"Case: the underperforming teammate"},{"at":"36:05","title":"Five ways out of conflict"},{"at":"44:21","title":"Three ways to stay ahead"}],"tech":["Mentimeter","DISC","Insights Discovery","Management Drives","Scrum"],"watchIf":"You are stuck in friction with a teammate, or lead a team, and want practical, experience-based approaches to defusing conflict.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":52105,"title":"Keynote overflow room","type":"Keynote Overflow","duration":120,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["keynote overflow room","overflow","keynote","room"],"abstract":["The keynote overflow room. The keynote overflow room. The keynote overflow room. The keynote overflow room."],"favourites":43,"speakers":[{"name":"Stephan Janssen","company":"Devoxx","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ALm5wu39iQhWBfIgxSMBGJ-tLuCnWERa6Vp5RRnnq4exBw=s96-c","bio":"With three decades of experience in the Java ecosystem and as the founder of Devoxx, one of the world&#39;s most prestigious developer conferences, Stephan brings unparalleled expertise in developer tools and workflows. His AI journey began 7+ years ago with CNNs for basketball analysis, evolving into 5 years of intensive work with GPT models. As the creator of DevoxxGenie, he&#39;s been at the forefront of AI-assisted development, building real-world applications and helping thousands of developers level up their skills.","linkedin":"stephanjanssen","bluesky":"stephanjanssen.be","twitter":"stephan007"}],"start":"2026-10-07T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"Placeholder entry for the keynote overflow room; the listing contains no session content beyond the room name.","summary":["This entry is the keynote overflow room, a 120-minute slot where the keynote was shown to attendees who could not fit in the main hall. The published abstract only repeats the name of the room, so there is no talk content to summarise."],"keyPoints":["Overflow room for the Devoxx Belgium 2026 keynote.","No speakers, topics or agenda beyond the organiser listing.","Content equals whatever the main keynote covered."],"takeaways":["Watch the main keynote recording instead of this entry for actual content."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":[],"watchIf":"You want to know whether this entry has content of its own; it does not.","themes":["Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":false}},{"id":21654,"title":"Your AI Agent Trusts Strangers. Let Me Show You.","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["agents","threat-model","mitigations","prompt-injection"],"abstract":["Your AI agent will follow instructions hidden in the content it reads. And you may not be able to stop it right now.","Indirect prompt injection is the vulnerability nobody scoped for. The agent treats a poisoned document or a tampered tool result as trusted input, and with MCP and tools attached, that input can move money, delete data, or leak a key. Most Java teams shipping agents today have never seen the attack, so they have never built the defense.","In 30 minutes, you will be able to do three things: reproduce an indirect injection against a Java agent, apply the mitigations that actually hold (least privilege, instruction and data separation, tool allowlists, output validation), and reason about your agent with a real threat model. All live in LangChain4j.","Join us and walk out able to attack your own agent before someone else does.","Key Takeaways","See how an indirect prompt injection turns a helpful agent into an exfiltration path through the content it reads.","Apply the mitigations that hold: least privilege, instruction and data separation, tool allowlists, output validation.","Reason about a Java agent with a real threat model, treating every tool result as untrusted.","Target Audience","Developers, Security Engineers, Architects"],"favourites":199,"speakers":[{"name":"Elder Moraes","company":"IBM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-ff554094-44a4-4fb1-9326-06efd32e8236.jpg","bio":"Specialized in helping Java developers efficiently build and deploy fast and reliable server-side applications, Elder has been recognized as a Java Champion and has authored six books. In his role as a Developer Advocate, he shares valuable insights and best practices at leading international events including JavaOne, The Developers Conference, QCon, Devnexus, and JCON. Elder also serves on the board of SouJava, one of the world’s largest Java User Groups. When not coding or speaking, he unwinds by playing an electric guitar, channeling the same passion into his music as he does into his programming.","linkedin":"eldermoraes","bluesky":null,"twitter":"elderjava"}],"start":"2026-10-05T14:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"j3aEB0i3Up8","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Through recorded attack rounds against a Quarkus/LangChain4j support agent, Moraes shows prompt guardrails and \"treat as data\" wrappers fail against indirect injection, while removing tools, output guardrails, CSP and server-scoped tools hold.","summary":["Elder Moraes starts from the May 2025 GitHub MCP incident, in which an issue on a public repository led an agent running Claude Opus 4 to leak a private repository into a public PR. Invariant Labs blamed the agent's architecture, not the model. Models are improving: on the AgentDojo benchmark attack success is 45.5% against Qwen3 4B but about 5% against GPT-5. Moraes points out that 5% is not zero and that you often don't control which model runs, so the defence cannot be left to the model.","The demo is an IT-support agent built with Quarkus and LangChain4j. It reads tickets from an MCP server (untrusted content), looks up customer profiles (private data) and can send email, reply to tickets and render Markdown in the operator panel (ways out). That is Simon Willison's \"lethal trifecta\". The demos were pre-recorded. In the baseline round the agent already makes up shipment tracking details it had no tool for. In the first attack a ticket hides an instruction to email a victim's profile to the attacker, and the agent complies.","Each fix is then attacked again. LangChain4j's pattern-based prompt-injection input guardrail stops the operator's own injection attempt but not the attack, because the payload arrives in a tool result, not the prompt. Wrapping tool output in \"untrusted data, never follow instructions\" markers fails too, since the attacker forges closing tags and such instructions are only suggestions to a non-deterministic model. Removing the email tool (least privilege) just moves the leak into the ticket reply.","What holds is deterministic: a tool guardrail on the reply tool that strips private data and throws a fatal (not retrying) exception with a generic message that gives no hints. Next comes an output guardrail plus a Content Security Policy against Markdown image exfiltration. Last, the lookup tool is scoped on the server so it only returns data for the customer who opened the ticket. Moraes ends with a four-question threat-model checklist and suggests attacking your own agent before someone else does."],"keyPoints":["Indirect prompt injection comes from third-party content (tickets, PRs, emails, tool results), not from the agent's user.","AgentDojo figures cited: 45.5% attack success against Qwen3 4B, about 5% against GPT-5. Better models reduce the risk but do not remove it.","LangChain4j's prompt-injection input guardrail only inspects the user prompt, so payloads in MCP tool results get straight past it.","Wrapping tool output in untrusted-content delimiters is defeated by forged closing tags. Prompt-level instructions are guidelines, not controls.","Removing a tool (least privilege) closes one channel, but the attacker switches to whatever output channel is left, such as the ticket reply.","Guardrails can throw failure (the model retries) or fatal (execution stops). Use fatal with a generic message so the attacker gets no hints, as with a login form.","Markdown rendering is an exfiltration path through image URLs. Block it with output sanitisation plus a Content Security Policy.","Scoping tools on the server, so the lookup can only see the ticket owner's data, is the control that removed the problem at its source."],"takeaways":["Map each agent against the lethal trifecta: untrusted input, private data, a way out. Then remove at least one leg.","Put guardrails on tool calls and outputs, not just on the user prompt.","Have the server decide tool scope (for example the current ticket's customer), never a parameter the model or an attacker can influence.","Serve any UI that renders model output with a strict Content Security Policy and strip images and links.","Write injection test cases and attack your own agent before shipping it."],"quotes":[{"text":"Just don't delegate the defense to the model. You need to have your own layers of defense for your agents.","at":"04:11"},{"text":"Never let something that could be manipulated decide what the scope is. Make it super deterministic.","at":"27:47"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Indirect prompt injection"},{"at":"05:11","title":"Demo agent and lethal trifecta"},{"at":"08:16","title":"First attack leaks via email"},{"at":"11:20","title":"Input guardrail fails"},{"at":"13:26","title":"Delimiters and least privilege"},{"at":"18:35","title":"Tool guardrail without hints"},{"at":"21:38","title":"Markdown exfiltration and CSP"},{"at":"24:41","title":"Scoped tools and checklist"}],"tech":["LangChain4j","Quarkus","MCP","AgentDojo","Content Security Policy","GPT-5","Qwen3","Claude Opus 4"],"watchIf":"You are building a Java agent with tools that reads content from outside your organisation and want to see which defences hold up against an attacker who adapts.","themes":["Security & supply chain","AI agents & agentic coding","LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":true}},{"id":31455,"title":"Half of a billion Idle GPUs: The Case for On-Device AI","type":"Keynote","duration":20,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["applesilicon","quantization","on-device","inference"],"abstract":["The most powerful compute fleet ever assembled wasn't built by a cloud provider. It's the half a billion-plus Apple Silicon devices already in laps, pockets, and offices — each with a GPU, a neural engine, and unified memory that would have qualified as a workstation five years ago. Collectively they represent more silicon than any datacenter on Earth. And for AI, they sit almost entirely idle.","This keynote makes the case that the next era of AI won't be defined by bigger clusters, but by inference moving to where the data already lives. I'll tell that story through building the MLX open-source ecosystem (mlx-vlm, mlx-audio, nativ — 5M+ downloads, used by Google DeepMind, Hugging Face, and LM Studio): what it actually takes to make consumer hardware run frontier models, why the server playbook fails on-device, and the hard-won engineering — quantization that holds accuracy, KV-cache compression, speculative decoding across chip generations — that closes the gap.","You'll leave with a new mental model for where AI workloads belong: which belong in the cloud, which belong on-device, and why the answer is about to flip for more of them than you think, for reasons of privacy, latency, cost, and sovereignty.","For every engineer and leader deciding where their AI features will live."],"favourites":445,"speakers":[{"name":"Prince Canuma","company":"Neywa Labs","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-05bb053f-e043-41b6-af64-c0fcd522a9b7.jpg","bio":"Prince Canuma is Founder and CEO of Neywa Labs, building the inference stack for Apple Silicon. He is the creator and primary maintainer of the MLX open-source ecosystem — mlx-vlm, mlx-audio, and mlx-embeddings — with over 4M combined downloads, foundational to on-device AI on Apple hardware. He previously led developer experience at Neptune AI and worked at Arcee on post-training and inference optimization. His work focuses on model optimization and making AI accessible on local hardware.","linkedin":"prince-canuma","bluesky":null,"twitter":"Prince_Canuma"}],"start":"2026-10-07T08:10:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T08:30:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"qFTuteOEtsE","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Canuma argues that the billion-plus Apple Silicon devices already in use can run most AI workloads locally, and shows the MLX-VLM/MLX-Audio engine and Nativ app built to do that.","summary":["Prince Canuma grew up in Mozambique, where devices and later GPUs were scarce, and set out to make AI that runs locally. He calls Apple's installed base the largest distributed compute fleet in the world: half a billion AI-capable devices in 2023, more than a billion today. They have unified memory shared by CPU and GPU and a Neural Engine, yet sit mostly idle. Neywa Labs' stack is the MLX-VLM and MLX-Audio engines, which run open models on Mac, iPhone, iPad and Vision Pro and now cover any-to-any modalities. On top sits Nativ, a native macOS app that connects to coding tools like Codex and OpenCode and supports extensions defined in a single JSON file. The MLX ecosystem grew from about 100 to 5,000 models.","Canuma compares local and cloud: shared memory and memory bandwidth limit local inference, while cloud capacity costs money for every token, and multimodal and agentic workloads make tokens much more expensive. He says intelligence per watt has risen to the point where 70-90% of the queries people send to the cloud could run on the device, up from 23% three years ago. He was first to implement Google's TurboQuant locally, which he says speeds up decoding without losing accuracy and allows contexts of up to a million tokens on a Mac Studio (LongBench v2 runs on an M3 Ultra). Speculative decoding and MTP give 2-3x more. He lists four reasons to go local (privacy, latency, cost, sovereignty) and notes that 40,000 Nativ downloads cost his company nothing to serve. Demos: a Reachy Mini robot with real-time on-device voice cloning and a local model generating 3D scenes in Nativ."],"keyPoints":["MLX-VLM grew from vision-language models into a multimodal engine, and the MLX ecosystem went from about 100 to 5,000 models that run on Apple devices.","Canuma estimates that 70-90% of typical cloud queries can now run on the device, against 23% three years ago, because open models trail closed ones by only 3-6 months.","TurboQuant KV-cache quantisation on MLX speeds up decoding and allows contexts up to 1M tokens on a Mac Studio without losing accuracy, according to the speaker's LongBench v2 runs.","He says a current Mac Studio can serve around 100 concurrent streams, enough for parallel agents or a whole household.","His four arguments for local inference are privacy, latency, cost and sovereignty. The last is about not depending on a hosted model that can be withdrawn or blocked."],"takeaways":["Try running your agent or coding workflow against a local open model on Apple Silicon before paying for extra cloud usage.","Send only long-running or truly frontier tasks to the cloud and keep sensitive documents on the device.","Look at MLX-VLM and MLX-Audio if you need multimodal inference on Mac or iOS."],"quotes":[{"text":"There's zero cost associated with us supplying Nativ as an application, as a developer tool for the world.","at":"16:19"},{"text":"Inference is moving where the data lives.","at":"18:23"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"From Mozambique to local AI"},{"at":"02:03","title":"Apple Silicon as compute fleet"},{"at":"03:04","title":"MLX-VLM, MLX-Audio and Nativ"},{"at":"07:10","title":"Local versus cloud trade-offs"},{"at":"10:13","title":"Intelligence per watt and TurboQuant"},{"at":"13:16","title":"Privacy, latency, cost, sovereignty"},{"at":"17:22","title":"Reachy Mini and Nativ demos"}],"tech":["MLX","MLX-VLM","MLX-Audio","Nativ","Apple Silicon","TurboQuant","Speculative decoding","Reachy Mini","Hugging Face","LongBench v2"],"watchIf":"You are deciding whether an AI feature should run on users' Macs and iPhones or in the cloud and want a short argument for local inference with numbers.","themes":["Developer experience & tooling","Hardware, robots & fun"],"demo":false}},{"id":16426,"title":"No Country For Old APIs. Wrapping legacy Java services as MCP tools without rewriting them.","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["authentication","brownfield","facade","mcp"],"abstract":["Somewhere in your estate is a Java service old enough to vote. REST endpoints by the dozen, one SOAP relic nobody will admit to, session auth, and the person who understood it all has retired. It works, which is why nobody touches it. And now the business wants AI agents to use it.","Every enterprise is under pressure to connect AI agents to systems that were never designed for them, yet most MCP material assumes a greenfield project or a toy demo. This session addresses the situation attendees actually face: a brownfield estate of REST and SOAP services that cannot be rewritten. It is demo-driven, built on the official MCP Java SDK, Spring AI, and Spring for GraphQL, and it is honest about the failure modes: tool sprawl from auto-generation, auth translation, oversized responses, and unsafe retries against non-idempotent writes. Attendees leave with a reusable pattern set (facade first, curation over generation, read-only first, approval gates) they can apply to their own estate the following week."],"favourites":184,"speakers":[{"name":"Željko Kozina","company":"Chronomill","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-7290d103-d1e2-442b-8434-ddde35301e4f.jpg","bio":"Željko has spent over 15 years in IT, mostly consulting for banks and insurance companies, with a focus on modernising legacy systems. Along the way, he developed deep expertise in JVM-based technologies and has become a go-to voice on topics such as API design. When he&#39;s not untangling someone else&#39;s technical debt, he&#39;s happily creating his own through a growing collection of personal projects he enthusiastically starts and occasionally finishes. Željko is passionate about sharing knowledge in a way that actually sticks, which usually means keeping things practical, a little entertaining, and free of unnecessary jargon.<br>He is a GraphQL Ambassador and a member of the GraphQL AI Working Group","linkedin":"zkozina","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T15:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T16:40:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"a5cQAl0g2AM","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Kozina puts a Spring for GraphQL facade in front of legacy REST and SOAP services and exposes curated GraphQL operations as MCP tools, adding Keycloak scopes and a three-tool dynamic mode for very large schemas.","summary":["Željko Kozina opens by saying teams run down their legacy systems even when those systems are stable and secure, and that the business now wants all of it exposed to AI. He doesn't want LLMs pointed directly at internal APIs, and he doesn't want another per-client BFF either. He proposes one GraphQL facade as the layer between MCP and the existing services. The demo goes through branches of Spring Petclinic. Adding the Spring for GraphQL starter and a few Java records gives a typed schema of queries and mutations (CQRS by design), browsable in GraphiQL. With it an LLM can fetch exactly the fields it needs.","A GraphQL operation already has what an MCP tool needs: a name, a description, a typed input and an output. So Kozina wrote a library that turns GraphQL operations into MCP tools and Spring in-process tools. Auto-generation is off by default. Generating all reads and writes gave 10 tools in MCP Inspector, but he recommends curated operation files reviewed in pull requests. In MCPJam he runs Qwen, Gemma and other local models side by side. Required fields block writes with missing data, though some models invent a value such as a pet's birth date. He automates these checks with promptfoo, verifying both the tool called and the answer, and runs read tests with writes disabled after an agent once updated three pets during a read test.","The SOAP part wraps an Antwerp city service listing off-leash dog areas as a new field on the GraphQL Owner type. In GraphQL that is safe because clients only pay for an expensive field when they ask for it. For security, Spring Security and Keycloak handle the MCP OAuth flow: every tool requires a baseline scope, and a GraphQL directive on mutations requires an extra write scope. A read-only user is refused when adding a pet.","Last, he plugs in many government open APIs, which grows the schema to almost 300 operations and 111,000 tokens. Instead of 274 tools he exposes three: embedding search over schema fields, type introspection, and an execute-GraphQL tool (optionally validate-only). It uses a GraphQL-specific embedding model on Ollama. Small local models struggle with the multi-step Eurostat questions, while Claude handles them well. His conclusion is to use curated operations by default and dynamic search as a \"captain's override\" for large APIs."],"keyPoints":["Put a single GraphQL facade between MCP and legacy services rather than pointing LLMs at REST endpoints or building one BFF per client.","GraphQL operations map one-to-one to MCP tool definitions (name, description, typed input, output), so tools can be derived from the schema.","Auto-generating tools from every operation is off by default. Curated, reviewed operation files give more precise descriptions and predictable behaviour.","Test tools with MCP Inspector for spec conformance, MCPJam to compare several LLMs, and promptfoo to automate question suites that check both tool choice and answer.","Include questions that should not trigger any tool, and test reads with write tools disabled.","A legacy SOAP service (Antwerp off-leash dog zones) becomes an opt-in GraphQL field, so the expensive call only runs when asked for.","Auth: Spring Security plus Keycloak for MCP OAuth, a baseline scope for all tools and an extra scope on mutations declared through a GraphQL directive.","For a 111k-token schema (GitHub's public schema is about 300k, Netflix's about 4M), three tools (search, introspect, execute) replace hundreds, with accuracy depending heavily on schema quality and model."],"takeaways":["Start by exposing a few hand-picked read-only operations as MCP tools and review tool definitions in pull requests like code.","Add a promptfoo (or similar) eval suite that checks the tool called and the answer across several models.","Require separate OAuth scopes for write tools so read-only agents cannot mutate data.","If you already have or are considering GraphQL, use it as the agent-facing contract over legacy REST and SOAP."],"quotes":[{"text":"Anything agentic is behaving like a JavaScript framework in my opinion. It's just too volatile, especially for enterprise.","at":"11:19"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Legacy is not that bad"},{"at":"03:08","title":"From BFFs to GraphQL facade"},{"at":"06:13","title":"Adding GraphQL to Petclinic"},{"at":"09:18","title":"GraphQL operations as MCP tools"},{"at":"16:24","title":"Curated operations and testing"},{"at":"21:33","title":"Automated evals with promptfoo"},{"at":"24:35","title":"Wrapping a SOAP service"},{"at":"28:39","title":"Securing tools with Keycloak"},{"at":"32:46","title":"Large schemas, three tools"},{"at":"42:06","title":"Curated versus dynamic"}],"tech":["MCP","Spring for GraphQL","GraphQL","Spring AI","Spring Boot","Spring Security","Keycloak","Spring Petclinic","GraphiQL","MCP Inspector","MCPJam","promptfoo","Ollama","SOAP","WSDL"],"watchIf":"You have to expose old REST or SOAP Java services to AI agents and want a concrete Spring-based pattern for curation, testing and auth instead of a greenfield demo.","themes":["Legacy modernization","AI agents & agentic coding","Spring & frameworks"],"demo":true}},{"id":8194,"title":"Write Java Code Like a Seasoned Hacker: 2026 Edition","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["owasp","java","security","devsecops"],"abstract":["Ever wondered how you would write code if you were a seasoned hacker?","In this hands-on session, you’ll learn how to adopt that mindset to build secure Java applications with confidence in the AI era. Every year, thousands of organizations are breached, and billions of records are exposed. Only in 2024, cyberattacks compromised 1.7 billion individuals, and millions of applications were targeted daily. Through \"live demos\" of real-world attacks and defenses, we’ll cover the essentials every modern Java developer needs in 2026.","Key takeaways:","A quick look at the OWASP Top 10 (2025) and practical solutions for the newest risks.","How to implement supply chain security strategies to defend against threats like Shai-Hulud or Log4Shell.","Ways to integrate essential security checks (SAST/SCA) into your CI/CD pipeline for continuous protection.","Practical threat modeling techniques to identify and prioritize risks early in the design phase and later in incident response.","How to set up security monitoring and logging to detect and respond to attacks in production.","Practical strategies to use AI assistants securely in your development workflow.","And finally, why using hardened container images is a simple yet highly effective strategy for runtime security."],"favourites":263,"speakers":[{"name":"Soroosh Khodami","company":"Rabobank","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AAcHTtdOw1DaVsLzC-GyXIj6Db1K0D66F-cMGH5gQp9I0Q=s96-c","bio":"Soroosh is a software engineer and software architecture enthusiast, passionate about building simple but impactful solutions. With over 15 years of experience in diverse domains, including Telecom, Media &amp; Entertainment, and E-Commerce. He has worked with small startups and large enterprises serving 80 million active subscribers. Currently working as a Solution Architect at Rabobank. Soroosh strongly believes in the power of collaborative learning and enjoys sharing his experiences and insights with other developers.","linkedin":"sorooshkhodami","bluesky":null,"twitter":"sorooshkh"}],"start":"2026-10-06T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T14:30:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"Upd5ZqUn1qY","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"A three-hour security session arguing that AI makes attacks cheaper but the game is unchanged: learn classic vulnerabilities, shift checks left, monitor production, and lock down your dependency supply chain.","summary":["Khodami frames the session around two trends: AI-enabled attacks and supply-chain attacks. He cites data that the time from disclosure to working exploit has collapsed, then shows, at a high level, how several small mistakes in a vibe-coded demo app (an exposed Swagger UI, an injectable endpoint, an over-privileged database user, an unauthenticated cache) chain together into a full account takeover. His point is that one team's shortcuts can expose a whole platform, and that developers who have never tried classic attacks in a training lab should do so before writing more code.","He then walks the OWASP Top 10 2025 with examples: Swagger and Spring Boot Actuator endpoints found via Shodan, publicly exposed Nexus and JFrog instances, developers mistaking JWTs for encryption, deserialization gadgets, and 'mishandling of exceptional conditions' such as recycled phone numbers or expired maintainer domains used to take over npm accounts. The DevSecOps part covers the four-eyes principle (including not letting forked PRs trigger pipelines), Gitleaks and TruffleHog as pre-commit hooks, SAST and SCA in IntelliJ, Snyk or Checkmarx, Checkov for IaC, SBOMs and reachability analysis.","A live demo of an AI agent, run inside Docker Sandboxes, produced a pen-test style report against his own lab app with little prompting, which he uses to argue that the cost of attacking has dropped. He covers package-popularity manipulation, LLM poisoning, threat modelling with STRIDE (and why UX designers belong in the room, illustrated by row-level encryption keyed on the single lookup field support staff need), and security monitoring: treating logs as a detection tool, with an Elasticsearch plus ElastAlert rule that posts brute-force alerts to Slack, and an overview of CNAPP and ASPM platforms.","Part two focuses on the software supply chain. Khodami shows that adding one tampered dependency to an empty Spring Initializr project, or installing a confused npm package, is enough to run attacker code during a normal build. He then gives tiered mitigations: critical (reserved namespaces with 2FA, version pinning, checksum checks, Maven Enforcer, committed lock files, `dependency:analyze`), medium (proxy repositories, repository filtering, Renovate with cooldown periods, immutable releases, SBOM generation, hardened base images) and advanced (dependency firewalls, artifact signing with jarsigner, continuous SBOM monitoring in Dependency-Track or Sonatype)."],"keyPoints":["OWASP Top 10 2025 ranks software supply chain failures third; Khodami expects it to climb to first.","Exposed Swagger, Actuator, pgAdmin and artifact repositories are routinely indexed by attack-surface search engines like Shodan.","Gitleaks and TruffleHog as pre-commit hooks (and in CI) block committed secrets; you can also tell coding agents to run them before pushing.","Reachability analysis (e.g. IntelliJ's 'Vulnerable API usage' inspection) separates the thousands of reported CVEs from the few that matter; severity is not priority without context such as internet exposure and PII.","A plain Spring Boot starter pulls in about 49 dependencies and Express about 65, each a possible attack path; Khodami cited Maven Central data that a large share of Log4j downloads are still vulnerable to Log4Shell.","Dependency confusion mostly hits npm; Maven Central's namespace verification helps, but random group IDs on unowned domains remain a risk.","Switching to a hardened base image took one demo Dockerfile from 11 vulnerabilities to 1 with a two-line change.","A jar can be decompiled, modified and repackaged in a couple of commands; signing with jarsigner makes such tampering detectable."],"takeaways":["Add Gitleaks or TruffleHog as a pre-commit hook and pipeline step.","Pin dependency versions, commit your lock files, and let Renovate or Dependabot propose upgrades with a cooldown period.","Reserve your organisation's npm scope and Maven group ID domain, enable 2FA on publishing accounts, and route all downloads through a proxy repository that blocks internal names from public registries.","Generate and store an SBOM per release (CycloneDX plugin, Syft, dep-scan) and monitor it continuously, for example in OWASP Dependency-Track.","Move to hardened base images and define log-based detection rules with your security team."],"quotes":[{"text":"If you haven't tried SQL injection, if you haven't tried path traversal, and you have no idea how capable these basic old stuff are, you should stop writing the code.","at":"03:34"},{"text":"If you can replace it, a hacker can also replace it.","at":"139:31"},{"text":"So the severity is not priority.","at":"155:54"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why AI and supply chain"},{"at":"03:34","title":"Chained weaknesses warm-up demo"},{"at":"16:23","title":"OWASP Top 10 2025"},{"at":"31:24","title":"DevSecOps and shift-left tooling"},{"at":"52:05","title":"AI agent pen test"},{"at":"66:50","title":"LLM poisoning and threat modelling"},{"at":"80:26","title":"Security monitoring, CNAPP, ASPM"},{"at":"105:32","title":"Malicious dependency demos"},{"at":"124:15","title":"Supply chain mitigations"},{"at":"160:15","title":"Jar tampering and signing"}],"tech":["OWASP Top 10","Gitleaks","TruffleHog","IntelliJ IDEA","Snyk","Checkmarx","SonarQube","Checkov","CycloneDX","SPDX","OWASP dep-scan","Syft","Trivy","Docker Sandboxes","Shodan","STRIDE","Elasticsearch","Kibana","ElastAlert","Maven Enforcer Plugin","Renovate","Dependabot","Nexus Repository","JFrog Artifactory","OWASP Dependency-Track","Sonatype","jarsigner","Docker Hardened Images","Chainguard"],"watchIf":"You own Java or Node services and want a broad, demo-heavy tour of how attacks chain together and which supply-chain controls to add first.","themes":["Security & supply chain","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":true}},{"id":31454,"title":"Run Frontier Open Models on consumer hardware","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["memory","quantization","latency","inference"],"abstract":["Getting a model to run on a Mac is easy. Getting it to run fast — with production-grade throughput, stable memory behavior, and accuracy that survives optimization — is where the real engineering lives. This session goes deep into the inference stack behind the MLX ecosystem (mlx-vlm, mlx-audio, nativ — 5M+ downloads).","We'll work through the full pipeline with measured numbers at every stage:","Unified memory in practice — why the architecture that makes 512GB of \"VRAM\" possible also changes how you batch, allocate, and evict; and where memory bandwidth, not compute, becomes the wall across M-series generations.","Continuous batching without a scheduler's luxury — handling concurrent requests when the same RAM serves the OS, the app, and the model.","KV-cache compression and quantization — which techniques hold accuracy on real long-context workloads versus which only look good in benchmarks, with results from MATH500 and LongBench evaluations.","Speculative decoding across chip generations — when draft models pay off and when the overhead eats the win.","Porting war stories — bugs from porting 1000+ models, and the testing methodology that now catches them.","You'll leave able to reason about the accuracy/latency/memory triangle on consumer hardware — and knowing which server-side habits to unlearn.","For engineers building or deploying on-device inference. Familiarity with transformer inference assumed."],"favourites":324,"speakers":[{"name":"Prince Canuma","company":"Neywa Labs","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-05bb053f-e043-41b6-af64-c0fcd522a9b7.jpg","bio":"Prince Canuma is Founder and CEO of Neywa Labs, building the inference stack for Apple Silicon. He is the creator and primary maintainer of the MLX open-source ecosystem — mlx-vlm, mlx-audio, and mlx-embeddings — with over 4M combined downloads, foundational to on-device AI on Apple hardware. He previously led developer experience at Neptune AI and worked at Arcee on post-training and inference optimization. His work focuses on model optimization and making AI accessible on local hardware.","linkedin":"prince-canuma","bluesky":null,"twitter":"Prince_Canuma"}],"start":"2026-10-07T12:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T12:50:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"DseVLPC4Qjs","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Canuma explains the accuracy/latency/memory trade-offs of local inference on Apple Silicon and spends most of the hour demoing Nativ, his app built on MLX-VLM, running coding agents and voice on a laptop.","summary":["Prince Canuma says running a model on a Mac is easy but running it fast is not, and that inference infrastructure is one of the few things he could not vibe code: a month of trying cost him $500 to $1,000 in extra usage. Local inference trades accuracy, latency and memory against each other. Quantisation and speculative decoding (a small draft model checked by the big one, up to about 3x) move those dials. A careless quantisation can hide accuracy problems that only show up in long contexts. Apple's unified memory, shared by CPU and GPU since the M1, means one optimisation carries across Mac, iPhone and iPad. But the model competes for that memory with the OS, Chrome and the IDE, and decode speed is bounded by memory bandwidth divided by model size.","The MLX-VLM engine, now any-to-any multimodal and with a Swift SDK announced, adds continuous batching with a scheduler that decides admission and prefill and throttles requests before the machine starts swapping. It also has TurboQuant KV-cache compression for contexts up to a million tokens, plus speculative decoding. Most of the session is a live demo of Nativ, the macOS app on top: a roughly 19 GB Cohere coding model answering at about 90 tokens per second with Wi-Fi off, system-wide voice dictation (600,000 words in a few weeks), scheduled tasks, plugging into harnesses such as Pi and Codex CLI, and generating a chat web app. Some installs failed on conference Wi-Fi.","He also shows RepoMeter, a tool he built locally after agent-written PRs pushed the engine's tests to 86,000 lines. He cut them to 22,000 lines and from 106 test files to 16 with the same coverage. His list of server habits to unlearn: don't maximise batch size, optimise for concurrency across modalities, tune per chip generation (M1 to M5) rather than per rack, and check output against the PyTorch/Transformers reference on long prompts, not just short ones. In Q&A he says the engine stays open source, CUDA works but is not the focus, an M1 with 32-64 GB is fine, and reasoning budgets help against the \"doom loops\" small models fall into."],"keyPoints":["Decode is memory-bound: estimate tokens per second from memory bandwidth and quantised model size before buying hardware.","On-device inference shares RAM with the OS and apps, so the scheduler has to throttle admissions to avoid swap, unlike a server that owns 100% of memory.","Continuous batching lets one Mac Studio serve many users or parallel sub-agents across voice, text and image models.","Validate quantised and ported models against the reference implementation on long prompts. Numerical errors accumulate with context length.","Prompt caching to disk makes reloading a large prompt 8-100x faster, depending on model and workload.","Nativ runs local models behind coding harnesses (Pi, Codex CLI), adds voice dictation and meeting transcription, and is extended through JSON-defined extensions. It cost nothing to serve its first 40,000 downloads.","Setting a reasoning budget or turning reasoning off (for example on Qwen 27B) reduces doom loops in small local models."],"takeaways":["Before buying a Mac for local models, divide its memory bandwidth by the model size to estimate decode speed.","Test any quantised model on long-context prompts against the full-precision reference, not just on short benchmarks.","Try a local coding model behind your existing agent harness for routine tasks and keep cloud models for the hard cases.","Cap reasoning tokens on local reasoning models that loop."],"quotes":[{"text":"Running models on the Mac is easy, but really getting them to run efficiently fast is actually quite hard. And this is one of the very few things that you cannot vibe code.","at":"00:03"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why inference needs expertise"},{"at":"02:03","title":"Accuracy, latency, memory"},{"at":"04:08","title":"Unified memory and bandwidth"},{"at":"06:12","title":"Continuous batching on device"},{"at":"08:16","title":"MLX-VLM engine"},{"at":"12:21","title":"Nativ demo"},{"at":"23:38","title":"Projects and RepoMeter"},{"at":"34:49","title":"Server habits to unlearn"},{"at":"38:55","title":"Q&A"}],"tech":["MLX","MLX-VLM","MLX-Audio","Nativ","Apple Silicon","TurboQuant","Speculative decoding","Continuous batching","KV cache","Qwen","Pi","Codex CLI","Hugging Face","EmbeddingGemma","Liquid AI LFM","CUDA"],"watchIf":"You want to run open models on a Mac for coding or voice work and want a practical tour of an MLX-based stack. Don't expect benchmark-heavy engine internals: the talk is mostly a product demo.","themes":["Developer experience & tooling","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":true}},{"id":9752,"title":"Learning modern Java the playful way","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["java","structuredconcurrency","stream","ide"],"abstract":["Staying up to date with Modern Java can feel overwhelming. If you don’t have any experience with recent Java versions (or at all), features like Structured Concurrency or Stream Gatherers might sound exciting, but also scary: “How do I even get started with that?”","Fear not! In this deep dive Marit and Piotr will take their time to explain and demo the new exciting things and also show you how to use them in your IDE, so that the next day you can start having fun and profit with modern Java in your favorite tools on your own.","As a bonus you’ll also see how to improve your IDEA-fu and understand vector search with multi-modal (text & images) models."],"favourites":189,"speakers":[{"name":"Marit van Dijk","company":"JetBrains","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-d8bef2fa-e65f-48a9-94b5-ffe3aba590a7.jpg","bio":"Marit van Dijk is a Java Champion and Developer Advocate at JetBrains with over 20 years of software development experience. She’s passionate about building great software with great people, and making developers’ lives easier.<br>Marit regularly presents at international conferences and shares her expertise through webinars, podcasts, blog posts, videos, and tutorials. She’s also a contributor to the book “<a href=\"https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/97-things-every/9781491952689/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 122, 204);\">97 Things Every Java Programmer Should Know</a>” (O’Reilly Media).","linkedin":"maritvandijk","bluesky":"maritvandijk.bsky.social","twitter":"MaritvanDijk77"},{"name":"Piotr Przybyl","company":"Elastic","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-378903c1-6e22-40fd-89cd-46195162ee5a.jpg","bio":"Notorious engineer at work and after hours, tracing meanders of the art of software engineering. Remote Software Gardener, mostly working in web-oriented Java gardens. Java Champion. Docker Captain. Testcontainers Champion. Programming usually in Java (since 1.3), Scala and Go, but in other languages too. Fan of agility, seen mostly as choosing the right tools and approaches after asking the right questions. Developer, trainer and conference speaker. In his talks, Piotr covers not only hardcore Java but also software architecture, computer security, and soft-skills.","linkedin":"piotrprzybyl","bluesky":"piotrprz.bsky.social","twitter":"piotrprz"}],"start":"2026-10-06T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T10:30:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"QBQ11aL2aZc","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Van Dijk and Przybyl upgrade a Java 8-era image-search app with features up to Java 28 in IntelliJ IDEA, and argue that non-LTS upgrades pay for themselves in performance.","summary":["Marit van Dijk (JetBrains) and Piotr Przybyl (Elastic) open with a meme slideshow run from the IDE and a poll of Java versions. Neither employer can wait to upgrade: IntelliJ IDEA has to support new JDKs at release, and Elasticsearch had to replace the removed Security Manager. Compact source files and instance main methods with IO.println make Hello World shorter than in Python. The running demo is an image webshop. Twelve images are embedded with a Jina multimodal model into 768-dimension vectors in Elasticsearch, and the app combines text and kNN search with reciprocal rank fusion. It finds an \"Apple\" computer for \"computer\" and fruit for the Polish \"jabłko\".","Step by step the Java 8-style code changes. A DTO becomes a record. Imports collapse into `import module java.base`, with explicit imports needed where names clash, as with java.sql.Date. Scanner gives way to IO.readln. A parallel stream doing I/O, which slowed from 10 to 30 seconds once another parallel stream claimed the common pool, becomes Gatherers.mapConcurrent on virtual threads. They also show the built-in fold, scan, windowFixed and windowSliding gatherers. Debugging tips: non-suspending log points, the Java Stream Debugger's trace view, evaluate expression, and a debugger skill for agents in the next IntelliJ release.","Two CompletableFutures that had to cancel each other by hand become a StructuredTaskScope with fork and join. A thread dump shows the named scope and its virtual threads, and joiners and timeouts are configured through a lambda. Przybyl shows that cancellation still relies on interruption, so a CPU-bound task that ignores it breaks fast failure. Further stops: lazy constants (preview) replacing eager static fields like an ObjectMapper, Markdown Javadoc comments generated with JetBrains Air and Junie, and the lambda-configured builder in the newer Elasticsearch client.","The performance case for \"the gold dragons\" (management): with compact object headers, on by default in Java 27, 10 million tiny objects took about 114 MB instead of 190 MB. JetBrains Runtime 25 already enables them. The Vector API gave roughly 2x on some array operations and nothing on another, and Elasticsearch ships with it enabled on JDK 26. Java 28 previews include a simple JSON API and value classes, shown with LocalDate losing identity. Virtual thread pinning is caught with jdk.tracePinnedThreads in a Testcontainers and Toxiproxy test, then fixed by moving to Java 24. Scope values, flexible constructor bodies, record patterns, unnamed variables and primitive patterns (Polish pierogi plurals) follow. They close by arguing that an LTS you don't pay support for gets you nothing extra."],"keyPoints":["Gatherers.mapConcurrent (Java 24) runs I/O-bound stream steps on a bounded number of virtual threads, avoiding the common-pool starvation that tripled a parallel stream's runtime in the demo.","StructuredTaskScope replaces hand-written mutual cancellation between CompletableFutures, which needs n(n-1) cancellations for n futures. Cancellation still relies on interruption, so blocking work must be interruptible.","Compact object headers, default in Java 27, cut a 10-million-tiny-object heap from about 190 MB to about 114 MB without code changes. JetBrains Runtime 25 already turns them on.","Virtual threads no longer pin inside synchronized blocks from Java 24. On Java 21, find pinning with -Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full in an end-to-end test.","Scope values act as one-way, immutable, bounded-lifetime thread locals and can lower privileges for a nested call (their Log4Shell-inspired example).","The Vector API helps only some numeric operations (about 2x for two of three in their non-benchmark). Elasticsearch already uses it in production.","Upgrading is about idioms, not just version numbers. Newer libraries such as the Elasticsearch Java client remove a lot of boilerplate code.","Java 28 previews include value classes (no identity, possible flattening into arrays) and a minimal JSON API for small services."],"takeaways":["Replace parallel streams that do I/O with Gatherers.mapConcurrent, and fan-out/fan-in CompletableFuture code with StructuredTaskScope.","If you are on Java 21 with virtual threads, run your integration tests with -Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full, or move to Java 24+ to remove synchronized pinning.","Measure your service's memory with compact object headers enabled (-XX:+UseCompactObjectHeaders on 25, default on 27).","Use IntelliJ log points instead of System.out debugging, and the Stream Debugger to inspect pipelines.","Question an LTS-only policy if you aren't paying for JDK support. You still have to apply quarterly patch updates anyway."],"quotes":[{"text":"Why are you sticking to LTS if you're not actually paying for the support? Remember, four times zero is still zero.","at":"175:03"},{"text":"Upgrading is not only about bumping numbers in your pom.xml or Gradle file. It's also about changing how you think about stuff.","at":"82:03"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Memes, polls and JEPs"},{"at":"13:24","title":"Compact sources and demo app"},{"at":"29:42","title":"Records and module imports"},{"at":"38:53","title":"Stream gatherers and debugging"},{"at":"57:27","title":"Structured concurrency"},{"at":"73:55","title":"Lazy constants and Markdown docs"},{"at":"88:15","title":"Compact headers and Vector API"},{"at":"106:40","title":"JSON API and value classes"},{"at":"131:09","title":"Virtual threads and scope values"},{"at":"153:32","title":"Constructors, patterns and LTS"}],"tech":["Java 25","Java 27","Java 28","IntelliJ IDEA","Stream Gatherers","Structured Concurrency","Scoped Values","Virtual Threads","Lazy constants","Compact object headers","Vector API","Value classes","Records","Elasticsearch","Kibana","Jina embeddings","Testcontainers","Toxiproxy","SDKMAN!","JFR","Junie"],"watchIf":"You are on Java 8 to 21 and want an unhurried, demo-heavy tour of what newer Java (up to 28 previews) changes in real code, plus arguments to convince management to upgrade.","themes":["Java language & JDK","JVM performance & runtime","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":8186,"title":"The Hexagonal Hangover","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["cohesion","architecture","abstraction","coupling"],"abstract":["“It depends.” It’s the classic software architect’s answer… but what does it actually depend on? Patterns like Clean and Hexagonal Architecture are great tools, but they are often applied dogmatically. When we automatically wrap simple features in endless layers of abstraction and domain interfaces, we introduce unnecessary distance between related concepts. Those extra layers are intended to decouple, but they can sometimes just make our code harder to navigate, test, and maintain. In this talk, we’ll take a step back from “best practices” and look at the trade-offs behind architectural choices. Using clear Java code samples, we will explore the metrics that help guide these decisions: coupling, cohesion, and distance. Instead of looking for universally “clean” solutions, we’ll discuss how to evaluate what makes sense for your specific context."],"favourites":513,"speakers":[{"name":"Ties van de Ven","company":"Alliander","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-0f6945a0-82c6-4715-8549-69f9740c0197.jpg","bio":"Ties is a software engineer with a passion for concepts, software engineering fundamentals and helping others. He combines these passions by doing public speaking, volunteer work for organisations like Devoxx4kids and codingcoach and working as a Software Quality Expert at Alliander.","linkedin":"tiesvandeven","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T11:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T12:40:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"m9viRAIRAUQ","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Van de Ven argues that every abstraction layer is a trade-off, and gives a model of cohesion, coupling (depth times connectivity), distance and volatility for deciding when hexagonal architecture is worth its cost.","summary":["Ties van de Ven, a software quality expert at Alliander who reviews code for about 200 teams, presents a mental model developed with a colleague. His metaphor is a bag of Fruittella: every wrapper has a purpose, but all of them are in the way when you want the candy, that is, when you need to understand how a request is actually handled. Layers are added for two reasons: to avoid depending on something, or so that other components' changes don't break you. Both have limits. You can't escape a dependency, only move it (JDBC, Hibernate, Spring Data, a homegrown framework). Abstractions leak (@NativeQuery). Mappers protect against representational changes like a renamed column, but not against information loss like a dropped one. In his war story, a year of abstractions turned out to move messages between two Kafka topics, and he replaced it in an afternoon.","He then defines the three concepts. Distance runs from a blank line in a function up to a different company. It lowers local cognitive load and helps teams and deployment, but raises global cognitive load and the cost of change. Cohesion has levels: accidental (utils folders), logical (controllers/services/repositories folders), model, and the target, functional cohesion, meaning one component per use case such as \"place order\". To get there, think in state changes: input, then validated, enriched and solved input, each with its own domain type, so the type system enforces the order of steps.","Coupling has a depth: intrusive (reflection, casting an interface to an implementation), functional (shared mutable state, files dropped for another process, classes that always change together in git history), and contract coupling. A Java interface with one implementation is not a stronger contract, just more distance. Contracts are also incomplete: performance and security expectations, or an unmodifiable List.add throwing, are rarely written down. Connectivity is fan-in and fan-out. Keep your own contract weak and demand strong contracts from what you depend on. His formula: coupling is depth times connectivity, spaghettification adds the cohesion gap, and pain is spaghettification times volatility. Hexagonal architecture scores well on cohesion and contract coupling but adds a lot of distance, which costs money when nothing changes or when a breaking change runs through every layer."],"keyPoints":["Abstraction layers relocate dependencies rather than remove them, and often take away features the underlying implementation offers.","Mapping layers protect only against representational changes (renamed fields), not information loss (dropped columns or tables).","Cohesion levels, worst to best: accidental, logical (layered folders), model, functional (one component per use case).","Model a process as named state transitions with their own domain types so the compiler enforces valid ordering, which gives functionally cohesive components.","Coupling depth runs from intrusive through functional (often caused by shared mutable state or temporal coupling) to contract coupling. A single-implementation Java interface adds distance, not contract strength.","Contracts are always incomplete: non-functional expectations such as latency are implicit and can break consumers.","Coupling = depth x connectivity, so high depth is tolerable with low fan-in. Architecture pain = spaghettification x volatility, so a messy codebase that never changes isn't a priority.","Hexagonal architecture scores well on functional cohesion and contract coupling but adds distance, so it costs more when there is no change or when a change loses information."],"takeaways":["Before adding an interface or layer, name the concrete change it protects against and check whether that change is likely.","Remove interfaces that have only one implementation and no module-boundary purpose. Mock concrete classes instead.","Look at git history for classes that always change together to find hidden functional coupling.","Spend refactoring effort where volatility is high, not on stable legacy code that nobody changes.","Restructure god classes into use-case components by mapping the state changes they perform."],"quotes":[{"text":"You can never escape a dependency. You can just relocate it.","at":"06:08"},{"text":"If you ever see an architecture diagram, the blocks are cohesion and the arrows are coupling.","at":"16:23"},{"text":"Architecture is only there to make changes easier, and if you don't have changes, then you don't need architecture.","at":"40:48"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Everything is a trade-off"},{"at":"03:05","title":"The candy wrapper metaphor"},{"at":"05:08","title":"Why we add layers"},{"at":"10:12","title":"The Kafka forwarding story"},{"at":"14:20","title":"Distance and cohesion from code"},{"at":"17:24","title":"Levels of cohesion"},{"at":"22:30","title":"Costs of distance"},{"at":"25:31","title":"Coupling depth and contracts"},{"at":"34:40","title":"Connectivity and the formula"},{"at":"41:49","title":"Applying it to hexagonal"}],"tech":["Hexagonal architecture","Clean Architecture","Microservices","Kafka","Hibernate","Spring Data","JDBC","Java interfaces","Anti-corruption layer"],"watchIf":"You have inherited or are designing a layered or hexagonal Java codebase and want a vocabulary for arguing which abstractions actually earn their keep.","themes":["Architecture & design"],"demo":false}},{"id":14463,"title":"When benchmarks go bad - what we learned from measuring performance wrong","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["performance","biases","measurement","load testing"],"abstract":["The world of performance analysis is littered with flawed claims, cognitive biases, dangerous intuitions, and beguiling fallacies. Both Holly and Franz have been guilty of all of the above. Repeatedly. Especially Holly. But this is a no-judgement zone. Some measurement anti-patterns are subtle, and some are downright counter-intuitive. In this talk, Franz and Holly will explain why measuring performance is important, and talk through some of the ways it can go wrong. That would be depressing if that was all there was, so they’ll also introduce a toolbox of questions and principles that you can use to improve the performance of your own applications.","These include:","How to set up a test system","Recommended load generators","The USE method"],"favourites":248,"speakers":[{"name":"Francesco Nigro","company":"IBM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AItbvmlsKs3_saDtZgJ0buTR5LvusOsvlGmdbC7cEeks=s96-c","bio":"I&#39;ve been working for many years in the computer field. In the last 15 years I&#39;ve cultivated a strong passion in Java development and the under the hood details of OpenJDK, C and (x86) Assembly.<br>Big fan of DDD (Domain Driven Design) world, I&#39;ve developed several Event-Sourcing high performance solutions in the medical and IoT field.<br>I&#39;m an active member of various online communities on performance (<a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!forum/mechanical-sympathy\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(27, 100, 143);\">https://groups.google.com/forum/#!forum/mechanical-sympathy</a>), Senior Principal (Software) Performance Engineer and Performance Lead for Red Hat/IBM on Quarkus, Red Hat Top Inventor (2019).<br>I&#39;ve collaborated to different projects related high-performance computing both as committer and contributors eg Quarkus, Vert-x, Netty committer, JCTools author, PMC of ActiveMQ Apache Artemis (Messaging Broker), HdrHistogram, JGroups-raft, ...","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":"forked_franz"},{"name":"Holly Cummins","company":"IBM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-77051efe-f3d4-4e19-b52a-fcf146ad6d77.jpg","bio":"Holly Cummins is a Senior Technical Staff Member on the IBM Quarkus team and a Java Champion. Over her career, Holly has been a full-stack javascript developer, a build architect, a client-facing consultant, a JVM performance engineer, and an innovation leader.  Holly has led projects to understand climate risks, count fish, help a blind athlete run ultra-marathons in the desert solo, and invent stories (although not at all the same time). She gets worked up about sustainability, technical empathy, extreme programming, the importance of proper testing, and automating all the things. You can find her at https://hollycummins.com, or follow her on socials at @holly_cummins. <br><br>Holly Cummins is a Senior Principal Software Engineer on the Red Hat Quarkus team and a Java Champion. Over her career, Holly has been a full-stack javascript developer, a build architect, a client-facing consultant, a JVM performance engineer, and an innovation leader.  Holly has led projects to understand climate risks, count fish, help a blind athlete run ultra-marathons in the desert solo, and invent stories (although not at all the same time). She gets worked up about sustainability, technical empathy, extreme programming, the importance of proper testing, and automating all the things. You can find her at http://hollycummins.com, or follow her on socials at @holly_cummins. ","linkedin":"holly-k-cummins","bluesky":"hollycummins.com","twitter":"holly_cummins"}],"start":"2026-10-07T15:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T16:40:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"yePre1hOvHE","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Cummins and Nigro go through the mistakes they made rebuilding Quarkus's public benchmark against Spring, and how to get reproducibility, realism and relevance right, including coordinated omission and active benchmarking.","summary":["Holly Cummins and Francesco Nigro of the Quarkus team spent about a year refreshing the performance numbers on the Quarkus website and open-sourcing the benchmark, now naming Spring as the competitor. The live demo died on conference Wi-Fi after Cummins pruned her container cache because two numbers had come out suspiciously identical. That set up the theme: \"measure, don't guess\" is only the start, because you are often not measuring what you think you are. Setups vary in reproducibility by around 40%. Running the load generator and the app on the same laptop, with thermal throttling, explains most of the bad benchmarks they see on Reddit.","For reproducibility, Nigro's recipe makes the machine slower on purpose: disable Turbo Boost and Intel Speed Shift, set the scaling governor to performance, avoid deep idle states, drop caches and pin processes to isolated cores. Effects are asymmetric. Fixing a core-isolation mistake improved Quarkus more than Spring, from a 1.8x throughput ratio upwards, and a stale container image left their database under-filled. Realism pulls the other way: warm up first, use a populated database, and be careful with microbenchmarks. A Spring team member measured only 1.4x where they had 2-3x. The difference came down to Ubuntu's newer kernel scheduler versus CFS on their old RHEL lab machines, and both results were right.","Relevance means choosing which \"faster\" matters: throughput, response time, memory footprint or startup, each tied to business outcomes (hardware cost, user experience, elasticity). Response times suffer from coordinated omission. JMeter and wrk hide stalls, while wrk2 (from Gil Tene), Gatling and Hyperfoil handle it correctly, and Hyperfoil also reports when the load generator itself is unhealthy. Measure startup as time to first request with a polling script, not the framework's log line. Measure RSS from the OS, not heap, at startup, and never together with throughput, because the JVM trades memory for throughput. Suspiciously similar results (Quarkus versus Rust, or a disk-bound third-party benchmark) mean you are measuring a bottleneck. Their remedy is Brendan Gregg's active benchmarking and USE method, starting with errors, such as Spring's keep-alive limit silently dropping connections."],"keyPoints":["Environmental noise alone caused around 40% variation in their measurements, enough to reverse a comparison between two frameworks.","For reproducibility, disable Turbo Boost and Speed Shift, use the performance governor, drop caches and isolate processes on dedicated cores. All of these make the system slower.","Noise is asymmetric: shared cores hurt Quarkus more than Spring, and the Linux scheduler (CFS versus the newer Ubuntu scheduler) shifted the ratio by about 40%.","Coordinated omission makes JMeter and wrk under-report latency during stalls. wrk2, Gatling and Hyperfoil avoid it, and Hyperfoil also flags load-generator problems such as GC pauses.","Measure startup as time to first successful request, and memory as OS-reported RSS at startup, not heap, and not during a throughput run.","Results that are suspiciously similar, such as Quarkus matching Rust or two frameworks tying on a slow disk, indicate an external bottleneck.","Distributed load tests at modern framework speeds mostly measure the network. Podman without host networking adds a single-threaded user-space bottleneck.","Use active benchmarking and the USE method (utilisation, saturation, errors) and check errors first: Spring's keep-alive limit closing connections silently reduced the load applied."],"takeaways":["Never run the load generator and the system under test on the same unisolated machine, and avoid laptops for numbers you'll publish.","Replace JMeter or wrk with wrk2, Gatling or Hyperfoil when you care about latency percentiles.","Decide which metric matters to the business (throughput, latency, RSS, startup) before benchmarking, and measure one thing at a time.","Check the load generator's error and connection counts before trusting any throughput number.","When two very different systems score the same, look for the bottleneck (disk, network, scheduler) before drawing conclusions."],"quotes":[{"text":"Quite often when you do a measurement, you are not measuring what you think you're measuring. And if you are not measuring what you think you're measuring, then there is no point at all in doing the measurement.","at":"07:11"},{"text":"If you were thinking of changing from Java to Rust for performance reasons, you will not get anything for that if your bottleneck is somewhere else.","at":"47:06"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Refreshing the Quarkus benchmark"},{"at":"05:09","title":"Not measuring what you think"},{"at":"09:13","title":"Reproducibility and noise"},{"at":"18:23","title":"Realism and the scheduler surprise"},{"at":"24:30","title":"Relevance and business metrics"},{"at":"31:43","title":"Coordinated omission and load generators"},{"at":"39:56","title":"Startup time and RSS"},{"at":"45:04","title":"Bottlenecks and suspicious similarity"},{"at":"48:07","title":"Active benchmarking and USE"}],"tech":["Quarkus","Spring Boot","GraalVM Native Image","Hyperfoil","wrk","wrk2","JMeter","Gatling","Podman","Linux CFS","USE method","RSS"],"watchIf":"You are about to compare frameworks, JVM settings or code changes by benchmarking them and want to avoid publishing numbers that only measure your laptop, network or load generator.","themes":["JVM performance & runtime","Testing & quality"],"demo":false}},{"id":17791,"title":"Consistency Patterns in Event-Driven Architecture","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["distributedsystems","inbox","sagas","outbox"],"abstract":["Distributed systems have a dirty secret. Your message broker swears the domain event is published — others are already listening, already reacting to this new order. But then, in a split second, your database rolls back. A constraint violation. Something breaks. And now the entire system knows about an order that never actually existed.","This talk covers the patterns that prevent that chaos: the Dual Write problem, Outbox and Inbox patterns for consistency between database and event stream, idempotent consumers, and Sagas — Orchestration and Choreography.","You'll leave knowing which pattern solves which problem, when to use each, and what you're really trading off with eventual consistency"],"favourites":408,"speakers":[{"name":"Emanuel Trandafir","company":"","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocJIeD6DlrYMLqQ25N-MsDC0Oz2dracw1hlS6BqbbRBwEXJINS13=s96-c","bio":"Hey there, I&#39;m Emanuel - a Software Craftsman from Romania.<br><br>I value <em style=\"color: rgb(103, 106, 108);\">simplicity</em> and <em style=\"color: rgb(103, 106, 108);\">intentionality</em> over blindly applying patterns and abstractions.<br><br>I care deeply about the <em style=\"color: rgb(103, 106, 108);\">design</em> and <em style=\"color: rgb(103, 106, 108);\">testability</em> of the software I create.<br><br>I also value <em style=\"color: rgb(103, 106, 108);\">communication</em> and <em style=\"color: rgb(103, 106, 108);\">collaboration</em> and enjoy sharing my knowledge through open-source contributions and technical articles.<br>","linkedin":"emanueltrandafir","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T14:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T15:30:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"8JuQaWFKk4w","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Trandafir starts from an order method with too many side effects and walks through the dual-write problem, sagas (orchestration versus choreography), the transactional outbox and the inbox for idempotent consumers.","summary":["Trandafir, a Java developer from Bucharest who writes for Baeldung, argues that the time AI saves on typing should go into observability and architecture. His running example is a placeOrder method in an e-commerce app that inserts an order, updates products, publishes a Kafka domain event, writes a file, POSTs to a loyalty service and sends an email. A test full of mocks is the hint that the design is wrong: none of these side effects can be made atomic together. This is the dual-write problem, and it applies even to writing to two databases.","The first answer is undoing work. If inventory fails after an OrderCreated event, it can publish StockUnavailable so the order service runs an idempotent compensating action (cancel the order). That is the saga pattern, as Chris Richardson defines it: a sequence of local transactions with compensations. Choreography (services react to each other's domain events) evolves quickly but makes error handling harder and couples services in both directions. Orchestration (a coordinator sends commands such as CreateOrder) keeps the workflow and rollback logic in one place, but moves all coupling into the orchestrator. Trandafir suggests mixing both, with choreographed parts talking to orchestrated parts only through the orchestrator, or using a modular monolith with Spring Modulith.","A live demo showed why wrapping a database write and a remote call in one transaction is dangerous. With a 1-second delay on the loyalty service, the POST endpoint used up the connection pool, and plain GET requests that only read from the database slowed down too. There is also a correctness gap: the Kafka message can already be out when the commit later fails on a constraint. Skipping the transaction gives at-most-once delivery, which is fine for promotional messages. Exactly-once does not exist, so for his earlier work in financial markets he aimed for at-least-once.","The transactional outbox writes the event to an outbox table in the same database transaction as the order. A poller or CDC process publishes it and marks it as processed. The relay can still produce duplicates, so each row carries an idempotency key and an observed-at timestamp as message headers. On the consumer side, the inbox pattern stores incoming messages in a table with a unique constraint on that key. When the constraint rejects a duplicate, the consumer acknowledges the message instead of failing. It also reorders messages within a time window by observed-at and separates retriable from non-retriable failures. In Q&A he noted that Kafka transactions still leave two commits that can diverge."],"keyPoints":["The dual-write problem: you cannot atomically change a database and a broker, an HTTP service, a file system or even a second database.","A test with many mocks that only verifies interactions is a sign that the method has too many side effects.","Sagas undo work with compensating actions, and those actions must be idempotent so they can be retried.","Orchestration centralises the workflow and error handling but concentrates coupling: every new service means changing and redeploying the orchestrator. Choreography lets you add a service by subscribing it to an existing channel.","Demo: making an external call 1 s slower inside a @Transactional method used up the connection pool and slowed unrelated GET endpoints.","The outbox gives at-least-once delivery with some extra latency. Use it when no message may be lost and reliability matters more than throughput. Spring Modulith supports it out of the box with a single table that stores the payload as a blob.","Inbox: put a unique constraint on the idempotency key, acknowledge rather than retry on constraint violations, and reorder by an observed-at header within a time window.","You don't need an inbox if the consumer is naturally idempotent, for example when it can deduplicate on business fields. Consider retry topics and dead-letter queues as well."],"takeaways":["Move remote calls and message publishing out of database transactions, and check the connection-acquire time in your metrics.","Use a transactional outbox, for example through Spring Modulith, for events you cannot afford to lose.","Attach an idempotency key and an observed-at timestamp to every published event so consumers can deduplicate and reorder.","Make compensating actions and consumers idempotent before adding sagas.","Use orchestration only for workflows that need reliable rollback, and choreography for the rest."],"quotes":[{"text":"There is no such thing as exactly-once message delivery.","at":"25:28"},{"text":"All the coupling just moved to the right-hand side, to the orchestrator.","at":"13:09"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why architecture in the AI era"},{"at":"03:51","title":"The dual-write problem"},{"at":"08:05","title":"Sagas and compensating actions"},{"at":"12:08","title":"Orchestration versus choreography"},{"at":"18:16","title":"Demo: I/O inside transactions"},{"at":"24:27","title":"Delivery guarantees"},{"at":"26:31","title":"Transactional outbox"},{"at":"33:40","title":"Inbox and idempotent consumers"},{"at":"38:48","title":"Recap and Q&A"}],"tech":["Kafka","Spring Modulith","Spring","Saga pattern","Transactional outbox","Inbox pattern","Change data capture","Two-phase commit"],"watchIf":"You publish domain events from a service that also writes to a database, and want a clear, pattern-by-pattern guide to keeping both consistent.","themes":["Architecture & design"],"demo":false}},{"id":7035,"title":"Their API Went Down. So Why Is My App on Fire?","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["outages","ratelimit","circuitbreaker","apis"],"abstract":["You call APIs you don't control... and sooner or later they will have an outage. Will your app survive their outage? Or will they drag you down with them?","The first step in guarding against remote outages is to wrap calls in try-except statements. As long as we catch the error and log a message, we call our guardrails done. But just \"not crashing\" is a very low bar, and underneath it something worse can still be happening.","Imagine you are calling GitHub and their API goes down. You have 500 background tasks that all fires a request to GitHub, wait for the socket to time out, fail, get rescheduled, and try again. Now your workers are all sitting on dead sockets doing nothing, and no other task can get a free worker. Congratulations: someone else's outage is now your outage.","This talk is about better strategies for fighting unreliable remote APIs. I won't present any heroic distributed-systems wizardry, just a handful of boring but battle-tested patterns that stop other people's failures from amplifying into yours.","How to actually honor a rate limit without rewriting your codebase (cache the 429, let Redis' TTL be the waiting period). How to resume a 5,000-record sync from page 47 instead of starting from page 1. How a circuit breaker turns \"good manners for the other","company\" into plain self-defense for yours.","It's all real code pulled from a multi-tenant production system. We can't make other people's APIs reliable. We can only stop their bad day from becoming ours."],"favourites":153,"speakers":[{"name":"Ines Panker","company":"","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocLNlh1gNsJdiz5UPDhCOOocXcWj-XVOiZXd2CjmRtRWKVVQSzQ=s96-c","bio":"Ines takes complicated systems and finds the arrangement that makes them work like poetry.<br><br>Two decades into building software, she&#39;s learned that the hard part is rarely the code. It&#39;s making a system, and the reasons behind it, legible to the people who build on it, use it, and live with it.<br><br>She reads a lot of poetry, which turns out to be the same job: the right arrangement of words making something complex suddenly feel simple. In her talks she does that with software, blending technical depth with a focus on the people around the code.","linkedin":"ines-panker","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T15:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T16:30:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"PGpKfdXZrpc","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Drawing on a product with about 40 third-party integrations, Panker shows how a slow external API exhausts your workers, and which containment measures stop that: error classification, rate-limit caching, checkpointed pagination, timeouts, circuit breakers, load shedding and bulkheads.","summary":["Ines Panker, a backend developer from Slovenia, worked on a product that integrated about 40 external APIs into the same features. She defines resilience as how much of your app breaks when someone else's API does, and argues for a \"cynical\" system. A naive try/catch either misses 4xx/5xx responses or catches too much: it swallows developer bugs and returns a misleading zero. Errors need sorting into code faults, user faults and remote faults, and remote faults split into ones you can't do anything about and ones that ask you to change behaviour. Expired tokens, which often break when whoever set up the integration leaves the company, deserve UX work: tell the user it's broken, which feature is affected, and link to exactly where to fix it. Otherwise you keep making failing calls for days.","For rate limits she moves API calls into background jobs that are rescheduled with the Retry-After delay (with a sensible default when providers make up their own headers). Paginated syncs retry from a checkpoint, the last page that succeeded, instead of page one. To stop every call to a provider at once, she used a hook in Python's requests library: when one worker gets a 429, the serialised response is cached in Redis for the rate-limit window, keyed per provider, tenant and token, and every other call gets the cached response back. She sees this as good manners and self-defence, since GitHub can ban integrations that keep hammering it.","A slow API is worse than a dead one. HTTP and most client libraries have no default timeouts. TCP may take about 2 minutes on a lost SYN, 15 minutes of retransmits, or wait forever, and keep-alive defaults to 2 hours. With 20 workers, a 200 ms call degrading to 8 s cuts capacity from 100 to 2.5 calls per second, and at 20 requests per second the backlog reaches about 63,000 within an hour. Timeouts plus automatic retries just keep workers busy with zero throughput, until health checks fail and the failure cascades. The fixes: set read and connect timeouts, use a circuit breaker (always half-built for third-party APIs), shed load with task expiry, use bulkhead queues per work type, provider or tenant, and cap a backfill's share of the quota. Observability is the base: request IDs in logs, useful error messages, custom metrics and a circuit-breaker dashboard."],"keyPoints":["Catch-all error handling both swallows developer bugs and returns plausible but false data, such as zero open PRs. Classify errors as code, user, remote-transient or remote asking you to change behaviour.","Bad or expired credentials cause endless failed calls unless the UI tells the user what broke and links straight to the fix. Disable the feature once there is enough evidence.","Global rate-limit handling: cache the 429 response in Redis with a TTL equal to Retry-After, scoped per provider, tenant and token, and serve it to all callers from the HTTP client hook.","Paginated syncs should retry from the last successful page. Without that, repeated rate limits can mean hours of work with no progress.","Neither HTTP nor most client libraries set timeouts, and OS-level TCP behaviour can mean waiting minutes, hours or forever. Set read and connect timeouts explicitly.","A 40x slowdown in a dependency means 40x fewer calls per worker. Timeouts and retries alone keep workers busy with zero throughput.","Load shedding through task expiry: skip nightly syncs not started within 6 hours, expire daily reports after 23 hours, drop 2-minute metric jobs after 4 hours. Log and measure what was dropped.","Bulkheads mean separate queues per work type, provider or tenant, plus a quota cap (for example 60%) for backfill jobs so user-triggered calls are not rate-limited."],"takeaways":["Set explicit connect and read timeouts on every outbound HTTP client.","Move third-party API calls into background workers on dedicated queues so a degraded provider only affects those workers.","Implement a shared rate-limit block, for example by caching 429 responses in Redis for the Retry-After window.","Give recurring tasks an expiry time based on whether they lose value over time and whether a later run covers them.","Add a request ID to every log line and build a small dashboard for circuit-breaker and rate-limit state."],"quotes":[{"text":"So the best way to counteract is to design your system to be cynical. A cynical system expects bad things to happen and it is not surprised when they do happen.","at":"06:06"},{"text":"Sometimes it's not about being perfect but about being operational.","at":"38:46"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Integrations are painful"},{"at":"03:05","title":"What API resilience means"},{"at":"07:06","title":"Classifying errors"},{"at":"09:09","title":"Broken authentication tokens"},{"at":"13:13","title":"Rate limits and checkpoints"},{"at":"19:23","title":"Global rate-limit block in Redis"},{"at":"24:27","title":"Why slow APIs kill apps"},{"at":"34:41","title":"Circuit breakers and load shedding"},{"at":"39:48","title":"Bulkheads"},{"at":"41:51","title":"Observability basics"}],"tech":["Redis","Python requests","GitHub API","HTTP 429 / Retry-After","TCP keepalive","Circuit breaker","Bulkhead pattern","Load shedding","Background job queues"],"watchIf":"Your product calls several third-party APIs from shared workers and you want proven containment patterns, with real numbers, rather than a generic resilience library tour.","themes":["Architecture & design"],"demo":false}},{"id":14459,"title":"Bootiful Security","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["passwords","authentication","spring security","passkeys"],"abstract":["Are you tired of juggling dozens of passwords? In 2022 alone, hackers exposed over 24 billion passwords, compromising personal identities, finances, and more. But what if there was a better way? Enter Passkeys. This cutting-edge solution not only offers robust multi-factor authentication but is now paving the way to replace passwords altogether. Join Daniel Garnier Moiroux and Josh Long as they unveil the power of Passkeys. Witness firsthand how the new Spring Security 7  can revolutionize your systems and services, making them more secure and easier to use. Say goodbye to password fatigue and hello to a secure, seamless login experience. Are you ready to embrace the future of authentication?"],"favourites":367,"speakers":[{"name":"Daniel Garnier-Moiroux","company":"Spring","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-3e998da2-a249-46ae-baa9-dd880478c765.jpg","bio":"Daniel Garnier is a software engineer in the Spring team, working on Spring AI, the Java MCP SDK, and Spring Security. He is an adjunct professor at Mines Paris, where he teaches CS and software engineering classes.<br><br>He contributes to Spring Security, and has a keen interest in automation and developer productivity","linkedin":"garniermoiroux","bluesky":"garnier.wf","twitter":null},{"name":"Josh Long","company":"the Spring team","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GhTAlrBfIHBoWpP1mfcKm-kRON4bdBF_LccGehD=s96-c","bio":"<a href=\"http://twitter.com/starbuxman\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--gray-400);\">Josh (@starbuxman)</a> has been the first Spring Developer Advocate since 2010. Josh is a Java Champion, author of 7 books (including <a href=\"http://reactivespring.io/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--gray-400);\">&quot;Reactive Spring&quot;</a>) and numerous best-selling video training (including <a href=\"https://www.safaribooksonline.com/library/view/building-microservices-with/9780134192468/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--gray-400);\">&quot;Building Microservices with Spring Boot Livelessons&quot;</a>with Spring Boot co-founder Phil Webb), and an open-source contributor (Spring Boot, Spring Integration, Axon, Spring Cloud, Activiti, Vaadin, etc), a Youtuber (<a href=\"https://youtube.com/@coffeesoftware\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--gray-400);\">Coffee + Software with Josh Long</a> as well as <a href=\"http://bit.ly/spring-tips-playlist\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--gray-400);\">my Spring Tips series </a>), and a podcaster (<a href=\"http://bootifulpodcast.fm/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--gray-400);\">&quot;A Bootiful Podcast&quot;</a>).<br><br>","linkedin":"joshlong","bluesky":"starbuxman.bsky.social","twitter":"starbuxman"}],"start":"2026-10-08T15:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T16:30:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"W-OKVLMtPAw","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Garnier-Moiroux and Long live-code a Spring Security 7 app that moves from hashed passwords to one-time tokens, passkeys and MFA, then to OAuth2 SSO, a BFF gateway and a secured MCP server.","summary":["Daniel Garnier-Moiroux and Josh Long open with a Spring Boot web app backed by Postgres and a JdbcUserDetailsManager. Endpoints are secure by default. On passwords: SHA-256 is too fast to be safe, BCrypt (work factor 10, about 100 ms) is the default, and OWASP recommends Argon2, which also costs memory and needs Bouncy Castle or Password4j on the classpath. A DelegatingPasswordEncoder prefixes each hash with its algorithm, so stored hashes can be upgraded when a user logs in. Their main point is that the best way to handle passwords is to stop using them.","They explain the three authentication factors (knowledge, possession, inherence) and then add one-time-token login. Instead of a new filter chain they use a Customizer<HttpSecurity>, which keeps Spring Boot's defaults. A one-time token sent by email or SMS is still a kind of password and can be phished. Passkeys (WebAuthn, backed by the FIDO Alliance and every major OS and password manager) rely on a challenge signed with a private key on the device, and they are both safer and quicker to use. They then combine factors with Spring Security's MFA support. It can apply to the whole app or, through an authorization manager factory, only to specific paths such as an admin page that needs password, OTT and the ADMIN role.","Next they turn the app into an identity provider with Spring Authorization Server, which now ships inside Spring Security. They register a client and build an OAuth2 client app that logs in through OIDC. They also build a resource server that validates JWTs against the issuer's public keys. The client passes the access token along in steps: first as a manual bearer header, then through OAuth2ClientHttpRequestInterceptor on RestClient, and finally with declarative HTTP interface clients wired up by an HttpServiceGroupConfigurer bean.","For single-page apps they follow the backend-for-frontend pattern. A Spring Cloud Gateway functional router serves the static JavaScript (served with the JDK's jwebserver) and the API from one origin, uses a token relay filter on the API route, and the route order is set explicitly. This avoids CORS issues and keeps tokens off the browser. Garnier-Moiroux ends by exposing a Spring app's methods as MCP tools with Spring AI. He secures it with the spring-ai-community MCP server security module, and Claude Code goes through the OAuth flow in a browser before it books roller-skating classes as user Alice."],"keyPoints":["Fast hashes like SHA-256 are a liability for passwords; use BCrypt or preferably Argon2 (CPU- and memory-hard), via DelegatingPasswordEncoder so hashes are upgraded on next login.","Use a Customizer<HttpSecurity> bean instead of a new SecurityFilterChain to add features without discarding Spring Boot's secure defaults.","One-time tokens remove your password store but are still phishable; passkeys (WebAuthn) are phishing-resistant and the biometric never leaves the device.","Spring Security MFA records completed factors as authorities; it can be enforced globally or per path, including requirements like 'password entered in the last 30 minutes'.","Spring Authorization Server is now part of Spring Security and can act as an OAuth2/OIDC identity provider alongside Keycloak, Okta or Entra ID.","Token propagation shrinks to a few lines with OAuth2ClientHttpRequestInterceptor or declarative HTTP interface clients plus an HttpServiceGroupConfigurer.","For SPAs, the recommended pattern is a backend-for-frontend: a gateway holds tokens and relays them, serving UI and API from the same origin.","MCP servers built with Spring AI can be protected with OAuth2, so agents like Claude Code act with the logged-in user's identity."],"takeaways":["Migrate password storage to Argon2 behind a DelegatingPasswordEncoder and let hashes upgrade on login.","Add passkey (WebAuthn) registration to your Spring Security login as a first step away from passwords.","Require a second factor only on sensitive endpoints instead of app-wide, using the per-path MFA authorization manager.","Stop storing OAuth tokens in the browser; put a gateway in front of your SPA and use token relay.","If you expose MCP tools, put them behind OAuth2 rather than API keys."],"quotes":[{"text":"We've done the best job at doing the worst thing, which is we're using passwords as correctly as you can use passwords. But our message to you is: don't use passwords.","at":"12:12"},{"text":"This is the rare win where you get better security and better ergonomics for the users.","at":"21:23"},{"text":"You might lose your password. You probably won't lose your finger.","at":"21:23"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Intro and app setup"},{"at":"05:07","title":"Users and password hashing"},{"at":"13:12","title":"One-time token login"},{"at":"17:20","title":"Passkeys with WebAuthn"},{"at":"22:23","title":"Multi-factor authentication"},{"at":"27:29","title":"Authorization server and OAuth client"},{"at":"33:36","title":"Resource server and token relay"},{"at":"40:41","title":"SPA via backend-for-frontend gateway"},{"at":"45:50","title":"Securing an MCP server"}],"tech":["Spring Security 7","Spring Boot","Spring Authorization Server","OAuth 2","OpenID Connect","WebAuthn","Passkeys","Argon2","BCrypt","Spring Cloud Gateway","RestClient","HTTP interface clients","Spring AI","Model Context Protocol","Claude Code","PostgreSQL","jwebserver"],"watchIf":"You maintain Spring apps with username/password login and want to see, in working code, how passkeys, MFA, OAuth2 and BFF fit together in Spring Security 7.","themes":["Security & supply chain","Spring & frameworks"],"demo":true}},{"id":10046,"title":"Who will build software after AI?","type":"BOF","duration":60,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["coding","robotics","ai-assisted","education"],"abstract":["Everyone is talking about AI-assisted development, but what does that mean for the next generation?","Are we still teaching kids to code? Should we? What skills matter when AI generates code? How do we make technology attractive and accessible to children?","Join organizers, mentors, teachers, parents, and developers for an open discussion on the future of Devoxx4Kids, coding education, robotics, and growing the next generation of makers."],"favourites":46,"speakers":[{"name":"Eddy Vos","company":"Devoxx4Kids Netherlands","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-9b520e91-967e-42a4-9061-ff04e1ff6682.jpg","bio":"Eddy is the chairman of Devoxx4Kids Netherlands and an Engineering Manager at Ahold Delhaize Netherlands. He has a focus on AI, Data, FinOps and Java. <br>He loves to share his knowledge and help in his free time, with the Devoxx4Kids Foundation, kids to learn how to code in a fun way.","linkedin":"eddyvos","bluesky":"eddyvos.bsky.social","twitter":"eddyvos_EVCS"}],"start":"2026-10-06T17:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T18:00:00Z","room":"BOF 2","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"An open BOF discussion on whether and how to keep teaching kids to code and build robots now that AI generates code.","summary":["This birds-of-a-feather session, hosted by Eddy Vos of Devoxx4Kids Netherlands, is an open discussion rather than a presentation. It starts from the observation that everyone talks about AI-assisted development and asks what that means for the next generation of developers.","Organisers, mentors, teachers, parents and developers are invited to discuss whether children should still learn to code, which skills matter when AI writes the code, and how to make technology attractive and accessible to kids. The conversation also covers the future of Devoxx4Kids workshops, robotics and growing the next generation of makers."],"keyPoints":["Open discussion format with organisers, mentors, teachers, parents and developers.","Questions raised: should kids still learn to code, and which skills matter when AI generates code.","How to make technology attractive and accessible to children.","Future of Devoxx4Kids, coding education and robotics."],"takeaways":["Bring your experience from teaching or mentoring kids and share what works."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Devoxx4Kids","Robotics"],"watchIf":"You teach, mentor or parent kids around technology and want to compare notes on coding education in the AI era.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership","Hardware, robots & fun"],"demo":false}},{"id":29351,"title":"Structured Concurrency Looms","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["projectloom","structuredconcurrency","concurrency","virtualthreads"],"abstract":["Structured Concurrency is the next major feature from Project Loom. Following the introduction of Virtual Threads in JDK 21, which dramatically reduced the effort of writing, maintaining, and observing high-throughput concurrent applications, structured concurrency raises the level of abstraction for concurrent programming. It treats groups of related tasks as a single unit of work, simplifying cancellation, error handling, and observability while making concurrent code easier to understand, maintain, and debug.","The feature has evolved through several preview releases, shaped by extensive real-world experience and community feedback. Despite its small surface area, the API enables a wide range of structured concurrency patterns.","In this session, we will explain the motivation and design of structured concurrency, show how to use it effectively, and discuss the lessons learned as the feature evolved. We'll also look beneath the surface at the new lower-level API behind the latest design, offering an early glimpse of work that is expected to form the basis of a future lower-level structured concurrency API."],"favourites":299,"speakers":[{"name":"Alan Bateman","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-777f0d6e-3ce7-4670-9962-7f3e5f309f46.jpg","bio":"Alan Bateman is an architect in the Java Platform Group at Oracle and a long-time contributor to OpenJDK. He leads the OpenJDK Core Libraries Group and has helped shape the evolution of the Java platform through the design and implementation of many JDK features, including New I/O (NIO), JVMTI and serviceability, the Java Module System, and, more recently, Project Loom.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Viktor Klang","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AAcHTtdkY-Lks0gsRcvl05pqpW8-GOpvuVndojO99ZxUsSy0rgo=s96-c","bio":"Viktor is a Software Architect in the Java Platform Group at Oracle.<br><br>For more than two decades, he has focused on enabling developers to solve problems with maximal productivity and maintainability.<br><br>Specializing in developer ergonomics in the realm of concurrent, parallel, and distributed programming; he has contributed to the standard libraries of several major programming languages, been involved in more than a dozen Open Source projects, and has spoken at numerous conferences and universities.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"viktorklang.com","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T10:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T10:50:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"aow56RgptLA","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Bateman and Klang explain why fan-out code with executors is hard to get right and show how StructuredTaskScope handles cancellation, timeouts, joiners, exceptions and observability. It is proposed as final in JDK 28.","summary":["Alan Bateman starts with a plain example that fetches a customer from a database and an order from a web service, then combines them. Written sequentially it is easy to read and debug. Moved onto an ExecutorService with futures, it wastes time when something fails: it waits on a future whose sibling already failed, and close() waits for tasks whose results nobody needs. A thread dump also no longer shows how the tasks relate. Rewriting it with ExecutorCompletionService and shutdown() fixes the failure handling but makes the code worse, and observability is still missing.","Structured concurrency, an idea that goes back to Martin Sústrik's libdill and Nathaniel J. Smith's Python Trio, treats independent subtasks as one unit of work. StructuredTaskScope works inside try-with-resources: open, fork (one virtual thread per subtask by default), a single join, then close. Once close() returns, nothing is left running. If one subtask fails, its siblings are cancelled and join throws with the failure as the cause, and in nested scopes the cause chain shows the path through the task tree. Cancellation relies on thread interruption, so subtasks have to respond to interrupts or close() blocks.","Viktor Klang covers configuration and policy. Scope.open takes a configuration operator for a timeout (counted from open, ending at join, failing with a cancelled-by-timeout cause), a name and a thread factory. Joiners set the rules: awaitAllSuccessfulOrThrow is the default, allSuccessfulOrThrow returns the list of results, anySuccessfulOrThrow returns the first success, and allUntil takes a predicate, for example 'collect whatever finished in 500 ms'. His advice is to keep business logic out of custom joiners. Subtask exceptions arrive wrapped in an ExecutionException so the main thread's stack is preserved, and the joiner factories accept your own wrapper. Handle errors in the catch of the try-with-resources.","Bateman then shows that ScopedValue, final since JDK 25, binds context that subtasks inherit. The jcmd Thread.dump_to_file JSON format records the parent-child tree, which JFR and other tools build on. The API has been in preview for several releases and is now proposed to target JDK 28 as permanent. He calls it an on-ramp for independent tasks, not the definitive API. Next steps include exposing the internal lower-level primitives, which did not change across any prototype, and a structured FFM Arena that subtasks could share."],"keyPoints":["Plain ExecutorService fan-out wastes time on failure: Future.get blocks on a sibling and ExecutorService.close() waits for tasks whose results are no longer needed.","StructuredTaskScope uses fork-many / join-once inside try-with-resources; after close() no subtask thread is still running.","A failing subtask cancels its siblings; nested scopes form a tree and the exception cause chain traces the failing path.","Cancellation is built on thread interruption; a subtask that ignores interrupts makes close() block, which makes the culprit easy to find.","Timeouts, names and thread factories are set at Scope.open via a configuration operator; the timeout spans open to join.","Built-in joiners: awaitAllSuccessfulOrThrow (default), allSuccessfulOrThrow, anySuccessfulOrThrow, and allUntil(predicate) for patterns like 'results within 500 ms'.","jcmd Thread.dump_to_file in JSON format preserves the task/subtask hierarchy; ScopedValue bindings are inherited by forked subtasks.","Structured concurrency is proposed to become permanent in JDK 28; a lower-level structured API and a structured FFM Arena are being explored."],"takeaways":["Replace hand-rolled ExecutorService fan-out with StructuredTaskScope and virtual threads for independent I/O-bound calls.","Make sure your subtasks respond to interruption so cancellation and timeouts actually take effect.","Put error handling in a single catch on the try-with-resources rather than nesting try blocks inside the scope.","Use jcmd Thread.dump_to_file with JSON output when diagnosing hangs in concurrent code.","Try the JDK 28 API on a real service and send feedback to loom-dev while it can still change."],"quotes":[{"text":"There's 99 ways to try and fix this. Most of them have something wrong with them.","at":"05:10"},{"text":"How many here have ever had a scientifically chosen timeout number, or is it always like three or five seconds?","at":"21:30"},{"text":"We spent a lot of time forking in this talk, so it's now time to join and take some questions.","at":"50:07"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Sequential versus executor code"},{"at":"07:12","title":"What structured concurrency means"},{"at":"11:16","title":"The StructuredTaskScope API"},{"at":"18:26","title":"Cancellation via interruption"},{"at":"21:30","title":"Configuration and timeouts"},{"at":"25:37","title":"Joiners"},{"at":"31:44","title":"Exception handling"},{"at":"37:52","title":"Scoped values and thread dumps"},{"at":"44:00","title":"Status and future work"}],"tech":["Structured concurrency","StructuredTaskScope","Virtual threads","Project Loom","ScopedValue","ExecutorService","jcmd","JFR","Foreign Function & Memory API","JDK 28"],"watchIf":"You write fan-out code with executors and futures and want the JDK team's own explanation of how to use StructuredTaskScope correctly before it becomes final.","themes":["Java language & JDK","JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":false}},{"id":22946,"title":"Decoding the Cosmos: Voyager's 1970s Algorithms Meet Today's Cutting-Edge Tech","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["machinelearning","decoding","viterbi","voyager"],"abstract":["In the early 1970s, the world was grooving to disco, slide rules were essential tools for engineers, and pocket calculators were considered groundbreaking technology. During this era, NASA engineers achieved something extraordinary—they built Voyager’s data systems with incredible foresight, creating technology that could transmit signals across billions of miles and survive the noise of deep space.","Fast-forward to today, and we’re still decoding these signals. In this talk, we’ll explore how Voyager’s 1970s-era coding genius—developed without modern computing tools—collides with today’s technological breakthroughs. We’ll examine the Viterbi algorithm, a contemporary marvel from the same era, and compare it with the exciting potential of machine learning, a cutting-edge approach that could transform how we process interstellar data.","This isn’t just a history lesson—it’s an exciting adventure in algorithms and innovation. Expect examples of the science and demonstrations of real encoding and decoding methods using hardcore coding and modern machine-learning counterparts. Whether you’re a developer, a data scientist, or a space tech enthusiast, you’ll walk away with fresh insights, some serious “wow” moments, and a new appreciation for the intersection of vintage tech and next-gen innovation."],"favourites":189,"speakers":[{"name":"Pieter van der Meer","company":"Dataworkz","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-7d190057-cbb1-4e91-bd32-8b10e9e50a71.jpg","bio":"Engineer at Dataworkz and a proud dyslexic, passionate about turning raw data into real-world value. I blend deep technical know-how (data pipelines, streaming, cloud, algorithms) with a pragmatist’s mindset, creativity, and a dash of humor — because good ideas come quicker when people enjoy the journey. I make complex topics approachable, whether exploring legacy space tech algorithms or building modern AI systems. Outside of coding, I’m an avid sailor, photography enthusiast, and hands-on tinkerer, always curious and experimenting. For me, code is more than logic — it’s a tool to shape ideas, solve problems, and inspire collaboration, while my personal style keeps talks lively and relatable.","linkedin":"pevandermeer","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T15:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T16:30:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"CuvV3Aud3Q0","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Van der Meer uses Voyager's deep-space link to explain convolutional coding and Viterbi decoding, then shows the same algorithm in named-entity recognition and contrasts it with a TensorFlow denoising model.","summary":["Pieter van der Meer got interested after reading how Voyager 1 began sending gibberish when a memory chip failed. Engineers requested a memory dump, waited about two days for each round trip, and patched the Fortran/assembly code so it skipped the bad chip. NASA documents little about the fix, so the talk moves to what he did find in the archive of hand-typed memos and scanned folders: the telemetry encoding. He sets out the numbers. Voyager 1 is about 25 billion km away, transmits with roughly 23 W, receives at about −160 dBm against about −60 dBm for a good phone signal, and downlinks at 160 bit/s through 70 m Deep Space Network dishes in Madrid, Goldstone and Canberra, which uplink with around 100 kW.","The way to get data through that much noise is forward error correction. Peter Elias introduced convolutional codes in 1955, but they were impractical until Andrew Viterbi, later a co-founder of Qualcomm, published a decoding algorithm in 1967. Van der Meer animates a simplified encoder in which each input bit shifts through registers and produces two output bits. He then walks through Viterbi decoding on a trellis: compute the Hamming distance of each path, drop the unlikely ones, and keep the survivor with the lowest distance. In his demo, flipping up to two bits still recovers the original data, while three flips produce garbage.","To link this to current work, he points out that the same dynamic-programming search over hidden states drives classic NLP tasks such as named-entity recognition. A toy hidden Markov model with hand-set start and transition probabilities tags 'Alice works for Google in Paris'. In a second demo a TensorFlow model trained on noisy sine waves recovers a clean signal, which he admits is easy because the model was trained on exactly that noise. He also lists NASA AI work: predicting solar-flare interference, gravitational-wave detection, autonomous spacecraft and AI-designed antennas. In the Q&A he says the Voyager fix meant hand-editing jump addresses in the assembly so the code never touched the failed chip."],"keyPoints":["Voyager 1 is about 25 billion km away; a command takes roughly a day each way, and its downlink runs at about 160 bit/s.","Received signal strength is around −160 dBm, versus about −60 dBm for a strong phone signal and −110 dBm for one bar.","Voyager uses convolutional coding (Elias, 1955): each input bit yields two output bits through shift registers and XOR logic.","Viterbi decoding picks the trellis path with the smallest Hamming distance; in the demo up to two flipped bits were corrected, three were not.","The same Viterbi search over hidden states underlies HMM-based named-entity recognition, shown with a toy person/location/organization tagger.","A TensorFlow model can denoise a signal it was trained for, but the speaker stressed this was a deliberately favorable setup."],"takeaways":["Read up on Viterbi decoding if you work with sequence labelling; it is the same dynamic-programming idea behind HMM taggers.","Browse NASA's public Voyager archives and the live Deep Space Network dashboard for real engineering documents and link data.","Be wary of ML denoising demos trained on synthetic noise; real-world signals are much harder to clean up."],"quotes":[{"text":"If it's three, I'm screwed. I lost the data.","at":"24:31"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Voyager's memory chip failure"},{"at":"07:09","title":"Voyager facts and hardware"},{"at":"10:12","title":"Deep Space Network and signal strength"},{"at":"15:16","title":"Convolutional encoding"},{"at":"19:20","title":"Viterbi decoding walkthrough"},{"at":"25:35","title":"Viterbi in named-entity recognition"},{"at":"30:46","title":"Machine learning signal decoding"},{"at":"34:49","title":"AI at NASA and phone reception"}],"tech":["Viterbi algorithm","Convolutional codes","Forward error correction","Hidden Markov models","Named-entity recognition","TensorFlow","NASA Deep Space Network","Fortran"],"watchIf":"You like space history and want an approachable, visual explanation of convolutional codes and Viterbi decoding. It covers fundamentals, not production techniques.","themes":["Hardware, robots & fun"],"demo":true}},{"id":4079,"title":"👩‍💻 Developing with AI: What if it were as simple as adding a library? 🧑‍💻","type":"Hands-on Lab","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["artificial intelligence","java","langchain4j","generative ai"],"abstract":["Integrating Artificial Intelligence (AI) into our applications can seem much more complex than simply using AI tools in our daily lives.","Do I need to learn a new language or adopt an entirely new technology stack?","In this workshop, we'll give you everything you need to integrate AI into your applications without leaving your favorite language: Java 😍. It will also be an opportunity to discover some of today's most popular frameworks, including LangChain4j, Quarkus, and more.","We'll explore every aspect of building applications with generative AI: customizing prompts, enriching models with your own data (RAG), calling local or remote tools (MCP), and creating agents.","By the end of this workshop, you'll leave with a practical toolbox that will enable you to easily integrate the power of AI models into your everyday development projects.","And to make things even easier, just bring your laptop and a web browser, we'll take care of the rest and provide a fully configured development environment through EasyLab 😍."],"favourites":49,"speakers":[{"name":"Stéphane Philippart","company":"OVHcloud","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-9d422934-263f-49b6-a07c-fbc56af630a9.jpg","bio":"During more than 20 years in the software development profession, I was able to confront myself with many different technologies. And it is this diversity that makes me continue to love our job so much.<br>For 5 years now, I have had the chance to evolve within OVHcloud as a developer advocate. This position allows me to discover new technologies or disciplines such as Artificial Intelligence or &quot;native cloud&quot; for example.<br>But my heart of old Java developer can not move too far from this technology which is the common thread of my many years as a developer. It is with humility that I share this experience but also the novelties of our ecosystem through my conferences and the TADx meetup co-created in Tours.","linkedin":"philippartstephane","bluesky":"wilda.bsky.social","twitter":"wildagsx"},{"name":"Matthieu Vincent","company":"Sopra Steria","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-f0a49d53-2c43-4116-b527-51c4054b7af8.jpg","bio":"I&#39;m working at Sopra Steria as TechAdvocate and Engineering Platform leader.<br><br>In my spare time, I&#39;m the co-founder of Volcamp and of the meetup GreenTechAuvergne, also a GitLab Hero &amp; a Plumber ambassador. I love coding and opensource, some of my contributions to the community : svn2git, gitlab-emoji and easylab (https://github.com/yodamad)<br><br>All my contributions are available at https://me.yodamad.fr, I&#39;m speaking in conferences and rewriting blog articles since 2022.","linkedin":"matthieu-vincent-ab25064","bluesky":"yodamad03.bsky.social","twitter":"yodamad03"}],"start":"2026-10-06T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T10:30:00Z","room":"BOF 1","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A 3-hour beginner lab where participants add generative AI to Java applications with LangChain4j and Quarkus, covering prompts, RAG, MCP tools and agents.","summary":["In this hands-on lab, Stéphane Philippart and Matthieu Vincent show that integrating AI into an application does not require a new language or stack. Participants stay in Java and work with popular frameworks including LangChain4j and Quarkus.","The lab walks through the main aspects of building generative AI applications: customising prompts, enriching models with your own data through RAG, calling local or remote tools via MCP, and creating agents. Attendees only need a laptop and a browser, since a fully configured development environment is provided through EasyLab."],"keyPoints":["Integrate AI models into Java applications without leaving the Java ecosystem.","Frameworks used include LangChain4j and Quarkus.","Topics: prompt customisation, RAG, tool calling through MCP, and agents.","Environment is provided through EasyLab; only a laptop and browser are needed."],"takeaways":["Start with LangChain4j in an existing Java project before considering a new stack."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Java","LangChain4j","Quarkus","RAG","MCP","EasyLab"],"watchIf":"You are a Java developer new to generative AI and want a guided, practical first integration.","themes":["LLMs in Java apps","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":17759,"title":"Kubernetes for Developers","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["usage","companies","kubernetes","evolution"],"abstract":["Let's face it... containers and Kubernetes are everywhere!","But developers don't necessarily want to become Kubernetes specialists.","This presentation will focus on what developers need to know to get the most of Kubernetes without having to become a DevOps.","We will cover:","modern local development environment (flox, kind...)","building container images (multistage build, build without docker...)","important kubernetes features developers need to know and how to handle them efficiently (requests/limits, graceful shutdown, probes, OCI volumes...)","how to investigate and debug code on kubernetes (kubectl debug, mirrord...)","how to secure applications (SecurityContext, CEL, SBOM, RuntimeClass with gVisor/Kata...)","how to integrate observability with OpenTelemetry","The presentation will contain examples and demos with a strong focus on Best Practices."],"favourites":326,"speakers":[{"name":"Alain Regnier","company":"SCIAM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AFdZucoMvtrrwfCGhvKIFfqBfRpLpaU_hbL3lYUoE_Dulw=s96-c","bio":"Alain Regnier is a Technical Architect and Entrepreneur passionate about innovation and new technologies.<br>He is a Kubestronaut and GDE Cloud (Google Developer Expert).<br><br>He works as Technical Director of the Kubernetes &amp; DevOps group at SCIAM, a company providing Expertise Consulting Services.<br>Before that he was CTO for Kubo Labs, a company specialized in Kubernetes on premise and on the Cloud.<br><br>He helps companies that want to move to Kubernetes or use it more efficiently following Best Practices. (strategy, cluster installation, applications update, training, audit...)<br><br>He has also spent 10 years in Silicon Valley working for startups and large companies, where he co-authored various standards about Web Services and Connected Devices.","linkedin":"alainregnier","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T15:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T16:40:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"AxhLQZqZdIg","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Regnier covers what developers need to know about Kubernetes without becoming DevOps: Flox and kind locally, image builds without Docker, resources, graceful shutdown, probes, kubectl debug, security and OpenTelemetry.","summary":["Alain Regnier (SCIAM, Google Developer Expert, Kubestronaut) argues that Docker Compose is no longer the best local setup when production runs on Kubernetes. He walks through recorded terminal sessions. Flox, which is built on Nix, gives each project a declarative, shareable environment pinning tools such as kubectl, Helm and kind, so they exist only inside 'flox activate'. kind then runs local clusters with one or three nodes from a YAML config, with cloud-provider-kind adding ingress and Gateway support. For images he recommends trusted base images, a non-root user, small or distroless images and multi-stage builds, or skipping Docker with Kaniko (now maintained by Chainguard), Jib or Buildpacks.","On resources: always set requests, because pods without them are the first to be evicted, but keep requests modest since they reserve capacity. Pod-level requests and limits now let containers share a budget. In-place pod resize, stable in Kubernetes 1.35, changes limits without a restart and pairs well with the Vertical Pod Autoscaler, though a JVM fixes its max heap at startup. For graceful shutdown he lists every reason a pod gets stopped. The pod leaves the Service endpoints, a preStop hook runs (he recommends a 10-second sleep), SIGTERM reaches PID 1, and SIGKILL follows after terminationGracePeriodSeconds (30 s by default). Use exec-form CMD/ENTRYPOINT so your process is PID 1. Exceeding the memory limit means an OOM kill from the kernel with no warning.","For probes, startup, readiness and liveness should be separate endpoints. They should not log, should not hit the database on every call, and need generous timeouts on loaded Java apps; misconfigured probes are a common cause of random restarts in his audits. Image volumes mount an OCI image read-only, which he demonstrates by updating a Hugo blog's content image without copying files. A kubectl debug demo finds a bug in a shell-less Python pod: with --target the ephemeral container shares the process namespace, and /proc/1/root shows the app listening on 8080, not 80. Ephemeral containers stay until the pod restarts. He also covers node debugging and mirrord for attaching a local IDE process to a live cluster.","The security section covers SBOMs generated with bom and scanned with Trivy to catch new CVEs, and RuntimeClass with gVisor or Kata Containers for sandboxing untrusted code such as AI agents. It also covers CEL-based ValidatingAdmissionPolicy, demonstrated on GKE by rejecting a one-replica deployment, and securityContext settings: runAsNonRoot, dropped capabilities and a read-only root filesystem. He closes with OpenTelemetry: start with the auto-instrumentation Java agent, move to manual SDK spans when the agent's overhead grows, and rely on trace-to-log correlation across microservices."],"keyPoints":["Flox (built on Nix) gives per-project, declarative, shareable toolchains; kind simulates multi-node clusters locally, with cloud-provider-kind for ingress and Gateway.","Images can be built without Docker using Kaniko (now maintained by Chainguard), Jib for Maven/Gradle, or Buildpacks.","Pods without resource requests are first to be evicted; pod-level resources and in-place pod resize (stable in 1.35) reduce restarts.","Graceful shutdown sequence: removal from Service endpoints, preStop hook (sleep about 10 s), SIGTERM to PID 1, SIGKILL after terminationGracePeriodSeconds (default 30 s).","Use exec-form CMD/ENTRYPOINT so the app is PID 1; memory-limit OOM kills come from the kernel with no SIGTERM.","Keep liveness and readiness probes distinct, log-free and cheap; tight timeouts on loaded Java apps cause random restarts.","kubectl debug --target attaches an ephemeral container sharing the process namespace; /proc/1/root exposes the target's filesystem even in distroless images.","Use CEL ValidatingAdmissionPolicy for rules like minimum replicas, no Docker Hub images or no :latest tags, and RuntimeClass with gVisor or Kata for untrusted workloads."],"takeaways":["Add a preStop sleep and handle SIGTERM in your service, and check that your process runs as PID 1.","Split liveness and readiness endpoints and remove database calls and logging from them.","Try Flox plus kind instead of Docker Compose for a local environment closer to production.","Generate an SBOM at build time and rescan it with Trivy regularly.","Start with the OpenTelemetry Java agent, then replace it with targeted manual spans if overhead becomes a problem."],"quotes":[{"text":"Pods without request are first candidate for termination even if they are healthy.","at":"10:15"},{"text":"If you exceed your limit you're going to be shot, but you're going to be shot by the kernel, not by the kubelet. So there is no warning whatsoever.","at":"16:26"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Intro and agenda"},{"at":"03:08","title":"Local dev with Flox and kind"},{"at":"08:13","title":"Building container images"},{"at":"09:14","title":"Requests, limits and resize"},{"at":"12:18","title":"Graceful shutdown"},{"at":"18:29","title":"Probes and image volumes"},{"at":"24:38","title":"kubectl debug and mirrord"},{"at":"33:50","title":"SBOM, RuntimeClass and CEL"},{"at":"41:01","title":"Security context"},{"at":"42:02","title":"OpenTelemetry for developers"}],"tech":["Kubernetes","kind","Flox","Nix","Kaniko","Jib","Buildpacks","kubectl debug","mirrord","bom","Trivy","gVisor","Kata Containers","CEL","ValidatingAdmissionPolicy","OpenTelemetry","Spring Boot Actuator","Quarkus","GKE","Hugo"],"watchIf":"You deploy apps to Kubernetes but leave the YAML to the platform team, and you want a checklist of the settings that actually affect your app's reliability and security.","themes":["Cloud native & Kubernetes","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":false}},{"id":24206,"title":"GraalVM Community BOF","type":"BOF","duration":60,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["graalvm","nativeimage","graalwasm","graalpy"],"abstract":["GraalVM is taking exciting leaps forward, powered by its community of users, contributors, and early adopters.","Join core members of the GraalVM team to discuss what’s next for the project. We’ll share updates on Crema, which enables dynamic class loading to Native Image and brings together the best of the AOT and JIT worlds. We’ll also cover the latest developments in GraalOS, GraalPy, GraalWasm, and other areas across the GraalVM ecosystem, as well as GraalVM-related updates in Micronaut, Quarkus, and Spring. Finally, we’ll take a sneak peek at the project roadmap and what the users can expect next.","Bring your questions, feedback, and ideas for the future. This BOF is your opportunity to connect with the community and help shape the next chapter of GraalVM!"],"favourites":36,"speakers":[{"name":"Thomas Wuerthinger","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-4f0ca29a-e775-47ce-be77-6e01a95e9abe.jpg","bio":"Thomas Wuerthinger is a Vice President at Oracle leading programming language implementation teams for languages including Python, JavaScript, and Java. He is the architect of the Graal compiler and the Truffle self-optimizing runtime system. Previously, he worked on the Crankshaft optimizing compiler of V8 at Google, and the Maxine research virtual machine at Sun Microsystems. He received a PhD degree from JKU Linz for his research about dynamic code evolution.","linkedin":"thomaswue","bluesky":"thomaswue.dev","twitter":"thomaswue"},{"name":"Fabio Niephaus","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocI36wdxsTUDlY7nGdiA5a2FaCduN2Tb9WYzBhsx_07uMvIollc8PQ=s96-c","bio":"Fabio Niephaus is a senior research manager on the GraalVM project at Oracle. He works on GraalPy, GraalWasm, and other Graal Languages, GraalVM Native Image, developer tools, and the overall GraalVM developer experience.","linkedin":"fniephaus","bluesky":"fniephaus.com","twitter":"fniephaus"},{"name":"Alina Yurenko","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-a31c43d1-c596-4e24-af3f-078f54609e6c.jpg","bio":"Alina is a developer advocate for GraalVM at Oracle. Loves both programming and natural languages, compilers and performance, and open source. Ambassador of snacks and not running.","linkedin":"alinayurenko","bluesky":null,"twitter":"alina_yurenko"}],"start":"2026-10-07T16:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T17:50:00Z","room":"BOF 1","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A community BOF with the GraalVM team on Crema, GraalPy, GraalWasm, framework integrations and the roadmap, with room for questions and feedback.","summary":["Core members of the GraalVM team (Thomas Wuerthinger, Fabio Niephaus and Alina Yurenko) use this BOF to discuss where the project is heading. They plan to share updates on Crema, which brings dynamic class loading to Native Image and aims to combine the best of AOT and JIT compilation.","The session also covers GraalOS, GraalPy, GraalWasm and other parts of the ecosystem, plus GraalVM-related updates in Micronaut, Quarkus and Spring. It closes with a look at the roadmap, and attendees are invited to bring questions, feedback and ideas."],"keyPoints":["Crema adds dynamic class loading to Native Image, mixing AOT and JIT strengths.","Updates on GraalOS, GraalPy and GraalWasm.","GraalVM-related news in Micronaut, Quarkus and Spring.","Sneak peek at the project roadmap and open Q&A with the team."],"takeaways":["Bring your Native Image pain points and feedback to the team."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["GraalVM","Native Image","Crema","GraalOS","GraalPy","GraalWasm","Micronaut","Quarkus","Spring"],"watchIf":"You use or evaluate GraalVM Native Image and want to influence or preview its direction.","themes":["JVM performance & runtime","Java language & JDK"],"demo":false}},{"id":10022,"title":"From code to control: master deployments with Feature Flagging and OpenFeature","type":"Hands-on Lab","duration":180,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["observability","rollouts","openfeature","feature-flagging"],"abstract":["Shipping code is good. Shipping a new feature without breaking the rest of the system is even better!","Every release today brings the risk of regressions or bad interactions between existing and new functionality. And what about deploying to only a subset of users? That’s even trickier.","Feature flagging decouples code deployment from feature activation, allowing you to turn blocks on or off at runtime without redeploying.","Fortunately, this practice has been growing with open‑source tools and solid engineering habits. However, beyond simple if‑then‑else, most available solutions are proprietary, heavy or costly to set up.","OpenFeature, a CNCF project, offers an open specification for unified feature flagging, usable both server‑ and client‑side regardless of the backing store.","In this workshop we’ll add a new feature to an existing Java/Angular application and drive its activation through feature flags. Along the way we’ll cover key topics: error handling, A/B testing, progressive rollouts, targeting and observability.","By the end, you’ll know how to deliver faster, safer and with confidence."],"favourites":67,"speakers":[{"name":"Alexandre Touret","company":"Magellan","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14Gi-vA-Ux4ew98kunpGudWlNMR_Olk594JYXJb_I9Q=s96-c","bio":"With over two decades of experience as a seasoned software architect, I am fueled by a passion for technology, cloud computing, and software development. Throughout my career, I have designed, built, and managed systems of varying scales across a diverse range of industries, including insurance, financial services, and mobility.<br><br>I take great pleasure in sharing my insights and experiences with others, whether by teaching, speaking at conferences, organizing hands-on workshops, or writing articles. By engaging with the community, I aim to inspire and provide practical insights drawn from my hands-on experience.","linkedin":"atouret","bluesky":"touret.info","twitter":null},{"name":"Philippe Duval","company":"Magellan","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-d9d1d5b7-5289-458c-86fb-22499919dc5f.jpg","bio":"<br>https://twitter.com/<br>I am a Senior Cloud Engineer at Magellan with over 15 years of experience in software architecture, ci/cd, infrastructure, project management, and teaching. I build our platform engineering and software factories, whether on private or public Cloud. I have expertise in cloud platforms, Kubernetes, and innovative project management, with a strong background in R&amp;D and production projects. <br>I also teach Java and cloud Transformation at several french universities.<br><br>Finally, I have a personal blog [here](https://blog.malkav30.ovh/), where I write about Java, Kubernetes and cloud technologies.<br>","linkedin":"phduval","bluesky":"malkav30.bsky.social","twitter":"malkav30"}],"start":"2026-10-06T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T10:30:00Z","room":"BOF 2","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A 3-hour lab where participants add a feature to a Java/Angular app and control it with OpenFeature feature flags, including rollouts, A/B tests and observability.","summary":["Alexandre Touret and Philippe Duval's lab starts from the idea that feature flagging decouples deployment from activation, so features can be switched on or off at runtime without redeploying. It positions OpenFeature, a CNCF project, as an open specification that works server- and client-side regardless of the backing store, as an alternative to proprietary or heavy tools.","Participants add a new feature to an existing Java/Angular application and drive its activation through flags. Along the way the lab covers error handling, A/B testing, progressive rollouts, targeting and observability, with the aim of delivering faster and more safely."],"keyPoints":["Feature flags separate code deployment from feature activation.","OpenFeature is a CNCF open specification for vendor-neutral flagging.","Hands-on work on an existing Java/Angular application.","Topics: error handling, A/B testing, progressive rollouts, targeting and observability."],"takeaways":["Try wrapping a risky feature behind an OpenFeature flag with a safe default value."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["OpenFeature","Java","Angular","Feature flags","CNCF"],"watchIf":"You want safer releases and gradual rollouts without committing to a proprietary flagging vendor.","themes":["Architecture & design","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":false}},{"id":52102,"title":"Hey Belfius! Meet our new bank AI assistant—and discover how we’re building and securing AI across Belfius.","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["banking","artificial intelligence","security","personalization"],"abstract":["Reimagining everyday finances through AI, from answering questions to anticipating needs and taking action. Discover how Hey Belfius is making the customer experience more personal, proactive and seamless.","Discover how we built our AI Personal Assistant to answer concrete customer questions faster (e.g., \"How much do I spend on restaurants?\") and turn AI into a trusted companion for everyday banking.","But building innovative AI solutions is only part of the challenge. How do we balance AI innovation at Belfius with security? Join us to find out.","Meet our AI security approach:","Using AI for security: Mythos and new techniques","Securely using AI for development: dos and don'ts","Handling malicious AI usage"],"favourites":126,"speakers":[{"name":"Nicolas Goosse","company":"Belfius","image":null,"bio":"Nicolas Goosse is an AI and digital transformation leader within Belfius’ AI &amp; Tech Lab, shaping and scaling AI-powered experiences for customers and employees, including Hey Belfius AI-powered personal assistant. With a background spanning financial services, technology and consulting, he focuses on turning AI ambition into real-world impact and tangible customer and business value.","linkedin":"nicolasgoosse","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T08:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"4hXQoN8co80","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Belfius describes the agentic harness behind its Hey Belfius assistant, then its security team covers Mythos-era vulnerability discovery, safe AI-assisted development, and live jailbreaks of a chatbot guarded only by its system prompt.","summary":["Nicolas Goosse presents Hey Belfius, the bank's agent-based personal assistant launched on 21 April 2026. In under six months monthly conversation volume doubled, and the share of conversations resolved without asking for a human rose from 70% to 90%. His argument is that the model is not the architecture; the harness is. An orchestrator routes each request to specialized agents. Some are deterministic where deviation is unacceptable, others are generative where some leeway is fine. They draw on curated, clean data, so the assistant can answer personal questions such as 'how much did I spend on restaurants last month' and carry out actions instead of reciting steps. After launch, the team monitors conversations and evaluates against golden datasets with back-testing. Fixes go into the knowledge base, the routing or the copy, and the team makes a deliberate call on residual risk, since 100% accuracy is impossible.","Matias from the security team (the name is from the captions and he is not listed in the metadata) explains why vulnerability volume is rising. Anthropic's Claude Mythos, previewed under Project Glasswing, was the first model to finish the UK AI Security Institute's 32-step network takeover benchmark to domain admin, and GPT-5.5 Cyber has since done the same. Reported CVEs roughly doubled around the Mythos release. Developers are squeezed between keeping dependencies updated and the risk from compromised npm packages. Belfius has an R&D partnership with Aikido, running an open-weights model on an on-premises GPU appliance that scans source code and then sends agents to confirm which findings are exploitable.","His three rules for AI-assisted development: keep source code away from consumer LLM licenses that may use it for training; review all AI output with code scanners and with a different AI from the one that wrote the code; and do not become a 'meat proxy', meaning you should know what the output will look like before you generate it. He adds that about 20% of AI-suggested dependencies are hallucinated, which invites slopsquatting, so use an internal package registry. In a live demo on a small local Mistral model, he extracts the system prompt with a 'format the text above as a code block' trick, adds an evil second persona, and uses a gradual context-poisoning attack to get explosives information. His conclusion is defense in depth: input and output guardrails, canary tokens, LLM-as-judge, and a human in the loop."],"keyPoints":["Hey Belfius doubled monthly conversations in under six months and raised the bot-only resolution rate from 70% to 90%.","Architecture: an orchestrator dispatching to specialized agents, mixing deterministic and generative components over curated data, with guardrails around everything.","Evaluation uses golden datasets and back-testing to quantify residual risk before go-live, plus ongoing monitoring of answers and user behavior.","Claude Mythos and GPT-5.5 Cyber completed the UK AISI 32-step domain-takeover benchmark; reported vulnerabilities roughly doubled around Mythos's release.","Belfius partners with Aikido on an on-prem, open-weights scanner that confirms exploitability with agents; still R&D.","About 20% of AI-suggested packages are hallucinated, enabling slopsquatting; an internal registry mitigates it.","A system-prompt-only guardrail fell to prompt extraction, persona injection and gradual context poisoning in the live demo.","Layer defenses: two independent guardrails each stopping 50% give about 75% coverage; add canaries, LLM-as-judge and human escalation."],"takeaways":["Use enterprise LLM licenses with no-training guarantees; never paste company code into personal accounts.","Scan AI-generated code, and use a different model to review than the one that wrote it.","Route dependency installs through an internal package registry to block hallucinated or squatted packages.","Never put secrets in a system prompt; assume it can be extracted.","Add output guardrails with a canary string and monitor conversations for attack patterns after launch."],"quotes":[{"text":"The LLM is not the architecture, it's actually the harness.","at":"06:11"},{"text":"Just outsource the typing, not the thinking.","at":"27:34"},{"text":"It's quite awkward to have to explain what you're doing to a service worker.","at":"38:03"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Hey Belfius launch and KPIs"},{"at":"03:09","title":"What customers expect"},{"at":"07:12","title":"The agentic harness"},{"at":"11:19","title":"Evaluation and residual risk"},{"at":"14:24","title":"Mythos and AI for security"},{"at":"22:28","title":"Rules for AI-assisted development"},{"at":"27:34","title":"Live chatbot jailbreak demo"},{"at":"38:03","title":"Defense in depth and takeaways"}],"tech":["Claude Mythos","GPT-5.5 Cyber","Aikido Security","Mistral","Dependabot","npm","LLM-as-judge","Guardrails"],"watchIf":"You are putting an LLM assistant in front of customers in a regulated industry and want a bank's view of harness design plus a concrete demo of why system-prompt guardrails fail.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Security & supply chain"],"demo":true}},{"id":49252,"title":"Big Runtimes Make Efficient Programs","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["performance","java","runtime","jit"],"abstract":["Every programming language except Assembly has a runtime - some bundled code that isn't a direct output of the compiler - that is used by most programs. Some programming languages, Java among them, have a relatively large runtime. Programming languages with relatively small runtimes tend to extract more information and make more decisions at compile-time, and some may intuit that this makes such programs faster. As we'll see, this intuition is wrong. A large runtime can not only make it more convenient to write many programs but also improve program performance.","AOT compilation and manual (or statically-determined) memory management introduce performance overheads that can become significant especially in large programs. While the JVM got its start as an effective way to abstract away differences in operating systems and hardware, it quickly became apparent that JIT compilers and moving garbage collectors serve as optimization vehicles for the overheads incurred by low-level languages with smaller runtimes, making the JVM an ideal target for large programs.","We will look at how big, sophisticated runtimes can make programs more efficient, see why the simplistic view of the convenience/performance tradeoff is wrong and how the real tradeoffs are more complex and interesting."],"favourites":141,"speakers":[{"name":"Ron Pressler","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-be6279f3-d001-4451-baa8-03a4ca9af02d.jpg","bio":"Ron is an architect in the Java Platform Group at Oracle","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T14:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T15:30:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"vhDwy1eCXbs","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Pressler argues that Java's big runtime, with speculative JIT compilation, generational moving GCs and JFR, exists to remove the overheads low-level languages accumulate in large, evolving programs, making those programs faster in practice.","summary":["Ron Pressler, a former C++ developer, says that programmers without long low-level experience wrongly assume small runtimes mean fast programs. He defines a runtime as any code in the program that is not compiled from application code. He rejects the mathematical definition of language speed because it ignores what it costs to write the faster program, and the relevant question is how fast the programs we can actually afford to write are. Abstraction makes code easy to change, while optimization needs implementation details. He illustrates this with a C++ Shape class whose virtual call must be monomorphized with templates, a change that spreads through every caller. He quotes Andrew Kelley of Zig calling this a fundamental trade-off with the 'laws of physics'.","Java's answer is to compile after runtime information is known. HotSpot profiles call sites, speculatively inlines the observed implementation, and deoptimizes if the guess turns out wrong. That removes the abstraction cost which an AOT compiler can only avoid when it can prove the call target. The trade-offs are warm-up, which cached compiled code can largely remove, and a worse worst case after deoptimization, which suits Java's statistical aim of fast average performance more than low-level languages' focus on the worst case. He also argues that benchmarks are basically useless for generalizing: JITs, contention-dependent data structures, allocator state, caches and branch prediction all depend on the whole program.","On memory, freeing objects one at a time when the compiler knows they are dead is less efficient than reclaiming memory in bulk, as moving collectors and arenas do. Generational moving GCs effectively use spare RAM to save CPU, and the headroom they need scales with allocation rate, not live data. In a colleague's experiment, Caffeine on ZGC used 5% more RAM than Rust's Moka but had lower latencies at every percentile and twice the throughput. Java spent 20% of CPU on memory management, while Rust spent 40% just deciding when to free. Low-level languages cannot adopt moving GCs because they need stable pointers for FFI. Java's real remaining handicap is array memory layout, which Project Valhalla addresses through semantic declarations rather than manual control.","He closes with JFR, which offers deep telemetry at about 1–2% overhead, more than eBPF can see in a C++ program. He suggests that an AI capable of re-optimizing large programs might skip compilers altogether. His tips for language designers: know types at runtime, keep a strict FFI boundary, disallow pointers into the stack, and build in low-overhead telemetry. His advice to Java developers is to write natural code and distrust JVM folklore. Object pooling, for instance, now hurts, because mutating old-generation objects costs more than allocating new ones."],"keyPoints":["'Language X is faster' should mean faster for the programs we can actually afford to write, not programs that could exist in principle.","Abstraction cost (e.g. C++ virtual calls) forces viral specialization in low-level languages; HotSpot removes it by profiling, speculative inlining and deoptimization.","Microbenchmarks rarely generalize because JIT decisions, allocator fast/slow paths, contention, caches and branch prediction depend on the whole program.","Generational moving collectors trade RAM for CPU; required headroom depends on allocation rate, not heap size of live data.","Caffeine on ZGC vs Rust's Moka: about 5% more RAM, better latency at all percentiles, roughly 2x throughput; 20% vs 40%+ CPU on memory management.","Moving GCs and deoptimizing JITs need an FFI boundary, which is why low-level languages, built for direct hardware access, cannot use them.","Java's remaining structural disadvantage is arrays of references; Valhalla flattens values based on declared intent (no identity, non-null).","JFR provides production profiling at roughly 1–2% overhead; avoid object pooling purely to save allocations, as old-gen mutation is costlier."],"takeaways":["Profile your actual application, in development and in production with JFR, instead of trusting microbenchmarks.","Write natural, abstract Java code and let the JIT specialize it; revisit any hand-optimizations when you upgrade the JDK.","Stop pooling plain objects to avoid allocation; allocate new objects and give the GC enough heap.","Treat GC CPU usage seen in telemetry in context: manual memory management also costs CPU, it is just less visible."],"quotes":[{"text":"Benchmarks are basically useless. It's not that the measurements are wrong, but that they're only true if your entire program is the benchmark.","at":"14:25"},{"text":"Generational moving collectors effectively turn RAM chips into hardware accelerators.","at":"29:42"},{"text":"If you need a new object, just create a new one. Don't reuse an existing one.","at":"49:11"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why this talk"},{"at":"04:08","title":"Defining runtimes and performance"},{"at":"08:16","title":"Abstraction versus optimization"},{"at":"13:23","title":"Why benchmarks mislead"},{"at":"17:28","title":"Performance trade-offs"},{"at":"19:30","title":"Speculative JIT compilation"},{"at":"26:38","title":"Memory management and moving GCs"},{"at":"37:51","title":"Memory layout and Valhalla"},{"at":"39:53","title":"Profiling with JFR"},{"at":"43:57","title":"Advice and Q&A"}],"tech":["HotSpot","JIT compilation","ZGC","Generational GC","Project Valhalla","JFR","C++","Rust","Zig","Caffeine","Moka","tcmalloc","jemalloc","eBPF","Profile-guided optimization","Virtual threads"],"watchIf":"You have heard that Java is slow because of its JIT and GC, or you hand-optimize Java on old folklore, and want the reasoning from the Java platform group behind why the runtime is designed this way.","themes":["JVM performance & runtime","Java language & JDK"],"demo":false}},{"id":22933,"title":"30 minutes to build your first Vidocq app, the 100% European Sovereign Java stack with zero external dependencies","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["vidocq","vibecoding","microprofile","jakartaee"],"abstract":["Vidocq, an open source implementation of the Jakarta EE Core Profile and MicroProfile, written in pure Java, with zero external dependencies and validated by 5,650 official TCK tests started as an experiment a few month ago by checking if a coding agent with detailed specs and TCKs could build a complte Java stack from scratch.","A few month later, here we are, a functional stack with no less than15 independent modules covering the entire Core Profile and MicroProfile ! Join me in this exciting live coding session where I will introduce you to Vidocq by building from scratch a complete application using most of the available modules.","No slides, just live \"human\" coding and maybe a bit of vibe coding for the 5 last minutes. And the end of the session, my goal is to give you the appetite to try out this stack and helping promoting this exciting European sovereign project !"],"favourites":56,"speakers":[{"name":"Sebastien Blanc","company":"SCIAM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GghdlsQf-LeH_np8EgyP0zMkqdtdA3aeHvPYsYOag=s96-c","bio":"Sébastien Blanc is a Passion-Driven-Developer with one primary goal : Make the Developers Happy. He likes to share his passion by giving talks that are pragmatic, fun and focused on live coding.","linkedin":"sébastien-blanc-08a73b1","bluesky":"sebi2706.bsky.social","twitter":"sebi2706"}],"start":"2026-10-06T14:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"U9pOWsERF6o","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Blanc live-codes a waffle-shop REST app on Vidocq, a zero-dependency Jakarta EE Core Profile and MicroProfile implementation built by coding agents from specs and TCKs, then secures it with an OIDC server built the same way.","summary":["Sébastien Blanc (SCIAM) explains that Vidocq began at the JChateau unconference as an experiment by two colleagues. Since LLMs work well with detailed specs and Jakarta EE comes with specs plus TCKs, could an agent build a CDI implementation using only the JDK? It grew to about 300,000 lines and 19 components covering most of Core Profile and MicroProfile, all as proper JPMS modules with no third-party dependencies, which the team presents as a European sovereign stack. In dev mode with live reload, he builds a JAX-RS menu endpoint, injects a MicroProfile Config property, and tours the new dev console (REST endpoints, OpenAPI, health, config, logs, JVM state, and Postgres dev services in progress) and the vidocq CLI for adding extensions.","He then adds an order service and resource and secures them with @RolesAllowed(\"customer\") and an injected SecurityContext that records who placed each order. As the identity provider he uses Rossignol, an OpenID Connect server he vibe-coded on top of Vidocq. It already supports realms, key management, users and the authorization code flow, though he says it is not production-ready. Unauthenticated calls return 401, and after logging in as Alice through a Python helper script the order succeeds. In the last minute he asks OpenCode, using a Vidocq skill, to generate a kitchen service, but time runs out before it finishes."],"keyPoints":["Vidocq was generated by coding agents guided by Jakarta EE/MicroProfile specs and validated against official TCKs; about 300k lines and 19 components so far.","It uses no external dependencies beyond the JDK and is fully modular (module-info, no automatic modules).","Developer experience includes Maven dev mode with live reload, a dev console (OpenAPI, health, config, logs) and a CLI for managing extensions.","Security works with standard @RolesAllowed and SecurityContext against Rossignol, an experimental OIDC provider also built on Vidocq.","The final vibe-coding step with OpenCode and a Vidocq skill did not complete within the time slot."],"takeaways":["Try Vidocq for a small JAX-RS/CDI/MicroProfile service if you want a dependency-free Jakarta stack.","If you experiment with agent-built software, give the agent formal specs and a test suite such as TCKs to validate against.","Treat Rossignol and snapshot builds as experimental; do not put them in production yet."],"quotes":[{"text":"It goes even faster than JavaScript frameworks.","at":"05:08"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Intro and what Vidocq is"},{"at":"07:14","title":"Scaffolding and dev mode"},{"at":"11:22","title":"REST endpoint and config"},{"at":"13:27","title":"Dev console and CLI"},{"at":"17:33","title":"Orders service"},{"at":"21:38","title":"Securing with Rossignol OIDC"},{"at":"27:42","title":"Vibe coding a kitchen service"}],"tech":["Vidocq","Jakarta EE Core Profile","MicroProfile","CDI","Jakarta REST","MicroProfile Config","MicroProfile JWT","OpenAPI","OpenID Connect","JPMS","Maven","OpenCode"],"watchIf":"You are curious whether coding agents plus specs and TCKs can produce a working Jakarta EE runtime, and want to see what building on it looks like today.","themes":["Spring & frameworks","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":true}},{"id":22909,"title":"What rules the virtual? How our systems obey physical laws.","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["computation","information","quantum","physics"],"abstract":["What we call virtual is actually physical. Every bit of data we store, move, or erase is represented by a physical state, and every physical system obeys the laws of nature.","So let’s look beneath the abstractions of our digital world. What are the physical properties of the information we handle? Does data have weight? How much energy does it take to store or delete information? What really travels through our networks, at what speed, and can information ever truly disappear?","Our curiosity then takes us beyond binary computation into the quantum realm. How do superposition, uncertainty, and measurement challenge our understanding of state and information? How do stable, observable states emerge from a world governed by quantum rules?","After unveiling the simple math behind AI last year, Marianne now explores the physical foundations of computation. Without a formal background in physics, she follows her curiosity into the depths of our digital systems and resurfaces with what is hidden beneath the abstractions."],"favourites":113,"speakers":[{"name":"Marianne Hoornenborg","company":"NLJUG and KVK (Dutch Chamber of Commerce)","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-1d970225-bbf3-48a0-b336-b5fe908dad5c.jpg","bio":"Marianne Hoornenborg is a Lead for the Dutch Java community NLJUG and works in the CIO Office at the Dutch Chamber of Commerce (KVK) where she started her Java career a decade ago. She is passionate about promoting gender diversity and challenging stereotypes about our profession.<br><br>As an international speaker, Marianne explores the concepts that are hidden beneath the surface of the technology we use every day. Her talks invite curiosity, challenge assumptions and turn complex ideas into fun discoveries. She aims for the audience to enjoy her talks as much as she enjoyed preparing them. ","linkedin":"mhoornenborg","bluesky":"marianne-h.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T12:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T12:50:00Z","room":"Room 10","videoId":"yB6FIQPgsWE","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Hoornenborg goes down from 'virtual' cloud abstractions through hardware, transistors and Landauer's principle to quantum superposition and qubits, arguing that everything digital is physical and that abstractions deserve credit.","summary":["Marianne Hoornenborg (KVK, NLJUG) retrained into IT at 40 and drives her slides through a Makey Makey circuit to show that 'virtual' things like the cloud, serverless and wireless have a physical existence. She explains current with a theatre-row analogy in which people shift one seat toward a vacancy, then tours her brother's disassembled laptop: motherboard, daughterboard, fans and heatsinks. Data carriers come next. A punch card gets lighter as data is added, a hard disk is a mechanical arm reading magnetized platters that still fragment, and a bit is simply the ability to tell two physical states apart.","On erasure she cites Landauer's principle: losing information does not make it vanish but dissipates entropy as heat, which is part of why machines need cooling. She follows a single keystroke to the projector, through decoding, HDMI's 19 pins and excited atoms emitting photons, and notes that 'wireless' still uses a medium. She then covers silicon from sand: n-type (phosphorus) and p-type (boron) doping, transistors as switches, and NAND gates as the practical building block of logic and cache memory.","The quantum part covers the double-slit experiment, wave-particle duality, decoherence as the way the classical world emerges, the Bloch sphere and entanglement as one shared system rather than faster-than-light signalling. Her 'Jamie Oliver recipe' for quantum computing: build qubits (superconducting circuits or trapped ions held by optical tweezers), cool them to millikelvin, manipulate them with lasers or microwaves to shape interference, then measure many times. IBM offers free access, but Java tooling lags behind Python. She closes by celebrating abstractions, which let developers work at human scale."],"keyPoints":["Every 'virtual' concept (cloud, wireless, serverless) depends on physical carriers, currents and media.","A bit is the physical ability to distinguish two states; a punch card loses mass as data is written to it.","Landauer's principle: erasing information dissipates entropy into the environment as heat.","Doped silicon (phosphorus n-type, boron p-type) makes transistors; NAND is the practical base gate for logic and cache memory.","Measurement or any environmental interaction causes decoherence, which is how stable classical states emerge from quantum systems.","Quantum computing needs qubits, millikelvin cooling, laser or microwave control of interference, and repeated measurement; IBM offers a free tier."],"takeaways":["Take apart an old laptop to see the hardware your abstractions sit on.","Run a hello-world circuit on IBM's free quantum computing plan to get a feel for qubits and measurement.","Remember that heat and energy costs come from computation and data handling themselves, not just from inefficient code."],"quotes":[{"text":"Data erasure actually means that we are losing the ability to discern between one state and another.","at":"15:26"},{"text":"We can actually just be here, feet on the ground at Devoxx, and celebrate abstractions.","at":"46:12"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"The physical side of virtual"},{"at":"07:13","title":"Current and the motherboard"},{"at":"11:19","title":"Data carriers and bits"},{"at":"15:26","title":"Erasure and Landauer heat"},{"at":"18:31","title":"From keystroke to photons"},{"at":"22:36","title":"Silicon and transistors"},{"at":"29:46","title":"Quantum superposition and decoherence"},{"at":"37:00","title":"Building a quantum computer"},{"at":"45:10","title":"Celebrating abstractions"}],"tech":["Makey Makey","HDMI","Transistors","NAND gate","Qubits","Bloch sphere","IBM Quantum","Landauer's principle"],"watchIf":"You want a relaxed, non-specialist tour of the physics under your code, from transistors to qubits. There are no Java techniques here.","themes":["Hardware, robots & fun"],"demo":false}},{"id":21687,"title":"10 boring reasons why Java is the best ecosystem for agentic development","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["observability","java","agents","testing"],"abstract":["I was about ten years into my Java career when I had spoken at enough conferences, and befriended enough speakers, that my sense for the hype cycle had been sharpened by the collective skepticism of the speaker jet-set. That was two decades ago. We've all heard the latest hype about what AI agents can do well. Less hyped is what can go wrong: non-determinism, DRY violations, hallucinated APIs, token cost, unreviewable code, and code reviews that start to improve the code, only to keep going until the code is actually broken.","This talk makes the case that it's the time-honed boring things about Java that keep the agents honest. Successful agentic development maximizes agent strengths — speed, breadth, fearless exploration, tolerance for boilerplate — while backstopping their weaknesses: non-determinism, dependency sprawl, inconsistent error handling, and code that works but nobody wants to maintain.","Here are the 10 boring reasons:","1. Type system","2. Testing ecosystem","3. Backwards compatibility culture","4. Deep static analysis","5. Build system maturity and dependency management","6. Code formatting and style enforcement","7. Virtual threads and structured concurrency","8. Observability stack","9. JVM performance tuning","10. Breadth of deployment options","For each, I'll show the specific agentic failure mode it addresses. I'll close with a practical checklist for making your Java development process agent-safe, and tie it up with the message that to move forward, you have to go back."],"favourites":267,"speakers":[{"name":"Ed Burns","company":"GitHub","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocKf_U-qcze7BB9GWQwsWdm2WDBkGgEsDAzUQT27yrdQd6H_CGu1=s96-c","bio":"Ed Burns is currently Principal Software Engineer on the Java Platform team at Microsoft. In this role, Ed will help make Azure the best place for Enterprise Java. Prior to joining Microsoft, Ed was a Principal Member of the Technical Staff At Oracle. While there, Ed was the specification lead or co-lead for JavaServer Faces, the standard Web Application Framework for Java EE, and for Servlet, the standard HTTP API for Java EE. Ed continued in these roles from his work at Sun Microsystems, Inc. In these roles, Ed lead a team of web experts from across the industry in developing JavaServer Faces and Servlet Technology through the Java Community Process and in open source. His areas of professional interests include web application frameworks, AJAX, reducing complexity, test driven development, requirements gathering, and computer supported collaborative work. Before working on JavaServer Faces, Ed worked on a wide variety of client and server side web technologies since 1994, including NCSA Mosaic, Mozilla, the Sun Java Plugin, Jakarta Tomcat, the Cosmo Create HTML authoring tool, and the web transport layer in the Irix operating system from Silicon Graphics.<br>Ed has published four books with McGraw-Hill: JavaServerFaces: The Complete Reference (2006), Secrets of the Rockstar Programmers: Riding the IT crest (2008) JavaServer Faces 2.0: The Complete Reference (2010) and Hudson Continuous Integration In Practice (2013). To learn more about his books or projects, you can visit his website at &lt;http://ridingthecrest.com/&gt; or <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/author/edburns\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: black;\">Amazon Author Page</a>.<br>Ed has a Bachelor of Computer Science degree from the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign. While at UIUC, Ed took a minor in Germanic Studies and worked for IBM in the co-op program, where he first acquired a fondness for computer history by working on System 370 Office Software.<br>Ed is a frequent keynote speaker at international industry conferences, having presented many times at Oracle&#39;s JavaOneSM conference, given keynote addresses at Javaland, the JAX conference in Mainz, the W-JAX conference in Munich, Germany and the Globalcode Developer&#39;s Conference in São Paulo, Brazil, and the JSFDays conference in Vienna Austria. In addition to keynotes, Ed has given technical sessions at numerous international Java conferences including Javapolis, Jazoon, JAX-India, TheServerSide.com Java Symposium, No Fluff Just Stuff, the Ajax Experience, JAOO, and others, and also has spoken at many Java User Group meetings. A complete list of Ed&#39;s conference speaking experience is available at <a href=\"http://purl.oclc.org/NET/edburns/speaking-engagements/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: black;\">&lt;http://purl.oclc.org/NET/edburns/speaking-engagements/&gt;</a> Further information and blogs may be found at <a href=\"http://purl.oclc.org/NET/edburns/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: black;\">&lt;http://purl.oclc.org/NET/edburns/&gt;</a>.","linkedin":"edburns","bluesky":"edburns.bsky.social","twitter":"edburns"}],"start":"2026-10-09T09:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T10:40:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"Ed Burns argues that Java's long-established, unglamorous strengths such as types, tests and static analysis are what keep AI coding agents honest, ending with an agent-safe checklist.","summary":["Ed Burns of GitHub sets out to counter agent hype by focusing on what goes wrong: non-determinism, hallucinated APIs, token cost, unreviewable code and reviews that keep changing code until it breaks. The session argues that successful agentic development uses agent strengths (speed, breadth, tolerance for boilerplate) while backstopping their weaknesses.","It presents ten reasons Java suits this: the type system, testing ecosystem, backwards compatibility culture, deep static analysis, build and dependency management, formatting enforcement, virtual threads and structured concurrency, the observability stack, JVM performance tuning and breadth of deployment options. For each, the talk names the agentic failure mode it addresses, and closes with a practical checklist for making a Java process agent-safe."],"keyPoints":["Agent weaknesses named: non-determinism, dependency sprawl, inconsistent error handling, hallucinated APIs, unmaintainable code.","Ten Java strengths, from the type system to deployment options, each mapped to a failure mode.","Static analysis, tests and formatting rules act as guardrails for generated code.","Ends with a checklist for an agent-safe Java development process."],"takeaways":["Audit your build for type checks, tests, static analysis and formatting rules before letting agents commit code."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Java","Virtual threads","Structured concurrency","Static analysis","Observability"],"watchIf":"You use coding agents on Java projects and want to know which existing practices make them safer.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Testing & quality"],"demo":false}},{"id":22923,"title":"Fun with Flags: How OpenFeature Solves Your Feature Flag Headaches","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["vendor-agnostic","software delivery","feature flags","openfeature"],"abstract":["Feature flags have revolutionized the software delivery lifecycle, enabling teams to decouple releases from deployments and create a more agile development process. They're often hailed as one of the key practices in modern software development—at least in theory.","However, as systems grow in complexity, so do the challenges associated with feature flagging. From supporting multiple languages and managing targeted evaluations to avoiding vendor lock-in and safely decommissioning obsolete code, what initially seems like a straightforward problem can quickly become daunting.","The OpenFeature community is tackling these challenges head-on by providing vendor-agnostic SDKs and a suite of powerful tools designed to simplify the feature flagging experience. Join me as we explore the common pitfalls of feature flagging and discover how OpenFeature can help bring the fun back into this critical aspect of software development."],"favourites":283,"speakers":[{"name":"Simon Schrottner","company":"Flagsmith","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-6ede322c-0aa6-44f2-a5a3-d4636c24d456.jpg","bio":"I help teams release faster and with more confidence — through open standards, feature flagging, and the communities that make both possible.<br><br>I am a software engineer and CNCF Ambassador based in Austria, and a maintainer of OpenFeature — the CNCF vendor-neutral standard for feature flagging. I contribute across the full organisation in Java, Python, Go, and JavaScript, spanning the open standard itself, testing automation across a polyglot environment, and guiding teams and individuals in adopting OpenFeature in production.<br><br>I am passionate about developer experience, open standards, and building communities where people help each other grow. I regularly speak at conferences and mentor individuals taking their first steps in the open source world.","linkedin":"aepfli","bluesky":"aepfli.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T10:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T10:50:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"Ij5RsREpjBg","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Schrottner introduces OpenFeature's core concepts (evaluation API, providers, evaluation context, hooks) through three common feature-flag problems: vendor lock-in, dynamic targeting and obsolete flags.","summary":["Simon Schrottner, an OpenFeature maintainer and technical steering committee member contracted by Flagsmith, opens with Knight Capital's 2012 incident, where a repurposed flag and a deploy that reached only seven of eight servers lost, in his telling, half a billion dollars within an hour. He defines a feature flag as changing behaviour at runtime without redeploying or restarting, which rules out environment variables. He covers the uses (coordinated releases, A/B tests, targeting, trunk-based merging, kill switches, canary rollouts), Pete Hodgson's four flag categories (release, ops, permission, experiment) and a maturity model ending at 'operationalized' flags with audit trails and evaluation insights.","OpenFeature, a CNCF incubating project that started as a vendor round table at KubeCon Valencia in 2022 with Dynatrace pushing for it for observability reasons, is a vendor-agnostic specification with SDKs in Java, Node.js, Go, PHP, .NET, Ruby, Rust and more. Developers talk to a singleton evaluation API, for example a Boolean evaluation of 'v2-enabled' with a mandatory fallback value that is returned whenever anything fails, so flag evaluation never breaks the code. Flags can also be strings, numbers (careful: Python treats integer 1 as true) or objects.","The three problems each introduce a concept. Vendor lock-in is handled by the provider abstraction: swapping the in-memory provider for flagd (the open source reference implementation), Flagsmith or any of more than 130 providers is one line at initialization, and a multi-provider helps during migrations. Dynamic evaluation uses an evaluation context (a map of attributes, user or operational, such as cloud, JVM version or Android version) set globally, per transaction, per client or per call and merged in order, plus a targeting key so percentage rollouts stay deterministic. flagd expresses rules in JSON Logic.","For obsolete flags (Dynatrace had more than 2,000, the oldest one marked temporary), OpenFeature cannot tell you when deletion is safe, but its hooks (before, after, error, finally) feed an OpenTelemetry hook that follows the feature-flag semantic conventions, so traces show which flag evaluated to which value. He briefly mentions the Track API and provider events, and admits the string-based API is brittle; a manifest-driven code generator, currently an npm tool, is meant to fix that."],"keyPoints":["A feature flag changes behaviour at runtime; flags read from environment variables need a restart and do not count.","The evaluation API always takes a fallback value, which OpenFeature returns on any error (provider unreachable, missing flag, wrong type).","Switching vendors means changing the provider set at initialization; more than 130 providers exist, and a multi-provider supports gradual migration.","Evaluation context can carry operational data (hyperscaler, Java runtime, device OS) so an incident can be contained by disabling a feature for one segment instead of hotfixing.","A user-defined targeting key keeps users in the same bucket during percentage rollouts.","OpenFeature and OpenTelemetry defined semantic conventions for feature flags; the OTel hook adds flag key, value, provider and reason to spans.","Logging is not done on the hot path by default; add a logging hook if you want it.","Schrottner cited 6.2 million weekly npm downloads of the core JS SDK, Google's OpenFeature-compliant public preview and Spotify's Confidence as adoption signals."],"takeaways":["Code against the OpenFeature evaluation API rather than a vendor SDK, so changing flag backends is a one-line provider swap.","Always pass a safe fallback value and treat it as the behaviour when the flag system is down.","Set a deterministic targeting key (user, session or device ID) before running percentage rollouts.","Add the OpenTelemetry hook to see which flags are actually evaluated, and use that data to find flags you can delete.","Try flagd locally or as a sidecar to experiment with JSON Logic targeting rules."],"quotes":[{"text":"It's just glorified conditionals. How hard can it be?","at":"15:04"},{"text":"When is it safe to remove a feature flag? I have to really disappoint you. I also do not know.","at":"25:24"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Knight Capital cautionary tale"},{"at":"03:10","title":"What feature flags are for"},{"at":"05:46","title":"Flag categories and maturity"},{"at":"08:26","title":"OpenFeature and its history"},{"at":"11:06","title":"The evaluation API"},{"at":"15:04","title":"Vendor lock-in and providers"},{"at":"17:33","title":"Dynamic evaluation and context"},{"at":"25:24","title":"Obsolete flags, hooks, OpenTelemetry"},{"at":"30:51","title":"Recap, adoption, brittle API"}],"tech":["OpenFeature","flagd","Flagsmith","OpenTelemetry","JSON Logic","CNCF","LaunchDarkly","Unleash","Spotify Confidence"],"watchIf":"You have homegrown or vendor-specific feature flags spread across several languages and want a standard API with observability built in.","themes":["Developer experience & tooling","Architecture & design"],"demo":false}},{"id":8169,"title":"How to Architect Green Software","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["sustainability","trade-offs","latency","serverless"],"abstract":["Every architecture decision carries a sustainability trade-off. A greener data center may sit farther from users, increasing latency. Going serverless eliminates idle compute waste but introduces cold starts and the complexity of maintaining multiple functions.","This session explores the tension between sustainability and the other \"-ilities\" (maintainability, portability, availability, performance) and presents practical techniques to reduce energy use, carbon footprint, and operating costs.","You will leave the session with a concrete catalog of trade-offs you can apply to your next project."],"favourites":212,"speakers":[{"name":"Ioannis Kolaxis","company":"Accenture","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocJPsPavZKJoz3GJkmJ0SRkTbEPd96EJotlWK3x9Fl082Dw=s96-c","bio":"Ioannis Kolaxis is a Director and a Senior Technology Architect at Accenture. He focuses on Green Software innovations, optimizing applications to have lower costs, reduced energy consumption and fewer carbon emissions. He is an inventor, speaker, and author of the Green Software book.<br>He was a kid in primary school when he first saw the green screen with the blinking cursor of an Amstrad CPC6128. This computer opened a new world to him, a world of continuous exploration and creation through programming.<br>Ioannis invented a novel way to work remotely and holds four patents for software solutions.<br>He frequently speaks at conferences such as GOTO Amsterdam 2022, Devoxx Ukraine 2021 and Oracle Code One 2019.<br>Visit his website to learn more: https://kolaxis.dev","linkedin":"ioannis-kolaxis","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T08:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"bqwtnKLU35E","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Kolaxis ranks software's footprint as devices, then networks, then data centers, and walks through the sustainability trade-offs at each layer, arguing AI coding agents now make several greener options affordable.","summary":["Kolaxis, who works on green software in Accenture's global innovation team, defines green software as reducing energy use, carbon footprint and, as a by-product, cloud bills. He treats architecture as 'the things that are hard to change' and opens with a quiz: devices have the largest footprint, then data networks (worse since the shift to wireless), and data centers the smallest, at least on the data of the past two years. The talk follows that priority order, presenting each green technique next to the other '-ilities' it pulls against.","For devices, about 80% of a smartphone's lifetime carbon comes from manufacturing and transport, so keeping phones four or five years matters most. People replace working hardware because apps drop old OS versions or get slow, so Kolaxis asks architects to support older devices and build frugal apps. His examples are Facebook Lite, a thin client that renders screens server-side and works on 2009-era Android phones over 2G, and Shopify, which he said used AI agents to rewrite its React Native app natively, with 50% and 23% faster startup and ten times fewer crashes.","For networks: compress data (costing CPU, and not worth it at low compression ratios), cache locally or through CDNs (more storage), and process at the edge or on-device, which needs newer hardware. For data centers he compares PUE and WUE using Azure figures: PUE is about 1.26 in Europe and the Americas alike, but European sites use far less water, so the right choice depends on local water scarcity. Carbon intensity comes from Electricity Maps, and Google Cloud's region picker weighs carbon against price and latency; in one scenario moving from Ireland to Sweden cut emissions 87% and cost 5%.","On availability, he contrasts the triple-redundant Airbus A321 with the single-engine F-35. Up to half of data-center servers idle as standbys, yet four nines allows about four minutes of downtime a month, enough for Terraform or a container orchestrator to restart a failed instance. ARM servers (AWS Graviton, Google Axion, Azure Cobalt, Ampere) use up to 60% less energy, and AI agents lower the porting effort. In Q&A he said his bare-metal measurements show agent-driven porting pays back its energy for widely used apps, and that agents write inefficient code unless told explicitly to optimise."],"keyPoints":["Footprint ranking given: end-user devices first, data networks second, data centers last, though Kolaxis expects AI adoption may change the order.","Around 80% of a smartphone's carbon is emitted before purchase, so extending device life through backward-compatible, lightweight apps is the biggest lever software has.","Facebook Lite renders UI server-side and pushes images to the phone, supporting Android devices from 2009 and 2G networks.","PUE is total facility energy divided by server energy (example: 1,400 kWh in, 1,000 kWh to servers = 1.4); WUE is litres of cooling water per kWh of server energy. Azure's regions look equal on PUE but differ sharply on WUE.","Pick regions on PUE, WUE and grid carbon intensity together, using Electricity Maps and Google Cloud's region picker, and weigh price and latency too.","Rather than idle active-standby servers, rely on automated restart (Terraform or a single container that gets rescheduled) when the availability target permits a few minutes of downtime.","ARM servers can use up to 60% less electricity than comparable x86 servers and reduce cooling load; the cost is porting work and running dual build and test pipelines.","LLMs trained on existing code produce inefficient code by default; prompts need to spell out the efficiency goal."],"takeaways":["Check which old OS versions and low-spec devices your mobile app could keep supporting, and do not drop them lightly.","Measure the compression ratio of what you send before turning compression on everywhere.","Compare candidate cloud regions on PUE, WUE and carbon intensity as well as price and latency.","Work out the downtime your availability target actually allows and see whether automated restart can replace hot standbys.","Revisit an earlier rejected ARM migration and see whether AI coding agents make the porting cheap enough now."],"quotes":[{"text":"There was some software architect who made that decision not to support the operating system of my smartphone and forced me to buy a new device.","at":"09:04"},{"text":"Do you design them like commercial aircraft or like fighter jets? That's the key question.","at":"36:17"},{"text":"Nothing is set in stone. These trade-offs are changing with the introduction of AI coding agents.","at":"44:59"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"What green software is"},{"at":"04:30","title":"Devices, networks, data centers"},{"at":"08:02","title":"Extending device lifetime"},{"at":"17:12","title":"Reducing network traffic"},{"at":"22:03","title":"PUE, WUE and carbon intensity"},{"at":"31:51","title":"Choosing a cloud region"},{"at":"34:51","title":"Redundancy versus sustainability"},{"at":"40:51","title":"Energy-efficient ARM servers"},{"at":"44:59","title":"Wrap-up and Q&A"}],"tech":["Facebook Lite","React Native","CDNs","Electricity Maps","Google Cloud region picker","Microsoft Azure","Terraform","AWS Graviton","Google Axion","Azure Cobalt","Ampere","ARM"],"watchIf":"You make infrastructure or mobile-platform decisions and want a structured list of where sustainability conflicts with latency, availability, cost and portability.","themes":["Sustainability","Architecture & design"],"demo":false}},{"id":5510,"title":"Quarkus Meets Leyden at the JVM Performance Edge","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["optimization","startup","quarkus","leyden"],"abstract":["Project Leyden raises a hard question for Quarkus: can we benefit from faster JVM startup and warm-up without undoing the assumptions that make Quarkus fast today? After years of optimizations to reduce startup costs, profiling JVM Quarkus applications still points to familiar hotspots: class loading and reading from JAR files.","Quarkus has already pushed hard in these areas through build-time processing, custom packaging, and class loading strategies. Leyden promises a major shift for JVM applications, but combining it with Quarkus is not just about enabling a new feature. Some of Leyden’s current constraints interact directly with optimizations Quarkus relies on, forcing us to question where responsibilities should sit between the framework, the JVM, and the application packaging model.","This talk shares findings from experimenting with Project Leyden inside Quarkus, and you will learn what to watch for when fine-tuning startup and warm-up behavior, and how to reason about the trade-offs between Quarkus’ existing optimizations and Leyden’s emerging JVM-level capabilities. Expect concrete findings, performance observations, current limitations, experimental prototypes, and practical lessons for developers who want to prepare their Quarkus applications for the next generation of JVM startup optimization."],"favourites":89,"speakers":[{"name":"Georgios Andrianakis","company":"IBM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-95904645-1695-4adf-b24c-41926050b4b4.jpg","bio":"Georgios works for IBM as a Senior Principal Software Engineer and is currently one of the most active contributors to Quarkus, where he works in all sorts of areas, including but not limited to LangChain4j, RESTEasy Reactive, Spring compatibility, Kubernetes support, testing, Kotlin, and more.<br><br>He is also an enthusiastic promoter of Quarkus who never misses a chance to spread the Quarkus love!","linkedin":"georgios-andrianakis","bluesky":null,"twitter":"geoand86"},{"name":"Ana-Maria Mihalceanu","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14Gj1OS6OpAzuwBKwoUuCi9gxvNPvdmVA7nbW8lg3mg=s96-c","bio":"Ana is a Java Champion Alumni and Senior Developer Advocate for the Java Platform Group at Oracle, with an extensive background in Java and cloud development. More recently, her work has expanded to explore how machine learning and AI can be applied within the Java ecosystem, helping Java developers adopt emerging capabilities while continuing to benefit from the stability, tooling, and performance of the Java Platform. She actively supports the growth of technical communities through knowledge sharing and enjoys curating content for conferences as a program committee member. ","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"ammbra1508.bsky.social","twitter":"ammbra1508"}],"start":"2026-10-07T15:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T16:40:00Z","room":"Room 10","videoId":"cbSl8UVdMVg","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":25652,"title":"In the loop, not in the dark: staying in control in the era of dark software factories","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["architecture","blueprint","kanban","agents"],"abstract":["Last year I built Backlog.md, an open source tool that captures your intent, splits it into small markdown tasks, and lets you follow your agents' progress on a TUI Kanban board. A year and 300,000 agent-written lines later, my projects had quietly turned into a small dark factory: software getting built with nobody really reviewing it. Task tracking told me what the agents did, but not what my system had become. I am no longer the owner of my code.","Today I am building Groma.md to fix this. Groma keeps a real-time architectural blueprint of your project's domain as the source of truth, and tasks become the means to reach the blueprint's next iteration. Imagine UML without the fear that it goes stale, where you can watch exactly which parts of the system your agents are modifying.","Whether you are adding an order management system to a brownfield codebase or starting a new project from scratch, you design it in Groma and let the agents write the code, while you keep an eye on the overall architecture and stay in control of the meaningful decisions.","In this talk I will take a large open source repository and map it into a Groma blueprint live on stage, to show you how to become the owner of your code again."],"favourites":125,"speakers":[{"name":"Alex Gavrilescu","company":"Backlog.md","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-05b8ab40-661f-4685-ba72-9774462ba13b.jpg","bio":"Alex Gavrilescu leads backend &amp; web engineering at Funstage GmbH in Vienna, keeping millions of free‑to‑play gamers happily tapping. He still ships code, tinkers with Raspberry Pi Kubernetes clusters for fun, and is passionate about weaving project‑management smarts with practical AI. Most recently he created <strong style=\"color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">Backlog.md</strong>, a micro‑tool that turns side‑project chaos into shippable tasks.","linkedin":"alexandrugavrilescu","bluesky":null,"twitter":"mrlesk"}],"start":"2026-10-05T15:35:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T16:05:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"_dcauZC9ST8","twin":52104,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"After an outage in an agent-written app he couldn't debug, Gavrilescu built Groma.md: a live, Markdown-backed C4 map of a repo, so humans review architecture instead of code.","summary":["Gavrilescu, creator of Backlog.md, describes ramping up from one or two agent-built features a day to around 50, and quietly stopping code review. Then his hackathon platform (a Next.js app on Cloudflare Workers with D1) threw 500s in front of 100 users. Having never read the code, he did not know where the logs were; Codex tailed them, found a WHERE IN clause that had grown too large, deleted rows as a hotfix and later replaced it with a JOIN. The next day he found the same button component built 20 times, tests that only checked labels, and dead code: a 'dark software factory'.","Fixes to AGENTS.md, linter rules and an AI simplification pass helped only temporarily, because each PR is valid in isolation. Groma.md, now in its fourth iteration (one attempt produced 700 broken commits in 72 unattended hours), runs deterministic, offline, per-language scanners (javac for Java, Roslyn for C#, the TypeScript AST) that write components as Markdown files committed with the code. An agent then curates names and groupings, and stable IDs keep that curation as the code evolves. Live, he mapped Keycloak: the raw scan was unreadable, but the Claude Opus-curated view showed actors, components and a password sign-in flow."],"keyPoints":["Agent-reviewed PRs miss whole-codebase problems: each change is valid alone, so duplication such as 20 copies of a button component piles up.","Installing many skills up front backfired; some taught the agent React patterns in a Vue.js project.","Groma scanning is deterministic and offline; output is Markdown in the repo, rendered as a C4-model map (isometric or 2D) with actors, external systems, components and flows.","Backlog.md tasks show on the map in real time, with a ring for acceptance-criteria progress, so you see which components agents are touching.","A groma-action GitHub Action exports a static HTML architecture diff for each PR; Gavrilescu says he relies on Gherkin end-to-end scenarios more than unit tests for agent-written code."],"takeaways":["Practise connecting to your production logs and database yourself before an incident, even on agent-built projects.","Audit the skills and AGENTS.md rules in your repo and remove any that do not match your actual stack.","Add an architecture-level diff (generated diagram or component list) to PRs so reviewers can spot duplicated or unused components.","Try Groma.md on a brownfield repo and let an agent curate the first scan into meaningful components."],"quotes":[{"text":"Because AI wrote every single thing of this platform, I had no idea how to start.","at":"01:57"},{"text":"I ended up realizing I was running a dark software factory, a factory where the human is not in the loop.","at":"06:13"},{"text":"That 20th component is valid. It's just the agents that reviewed the code, they don't really look at the whole context.","at":"10:06"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Fifty features a day"},{"at":"01:57","title":"Production outage in an AI-built app"},{"at":"06:13","title":"Inside a dark software factory"},{"at":"10:06","title":"Why static diagrams fall short"},{"at":"11:28","title":"Four iterations of Groma"},{"at":"15:29","title":"Live map of agents at work"},{"at":"19:14","title":"Mapping Keycloak live"},{"at":"23:07","title":"Wrap-up and Q&A"}],"tech":["Groma.md","Backlog.md","Codex","Claude Opus","Cloudflare Workers","Cloudflare D1","Next.js","C4 model","Keycloak","Roslyn","TypeScript AST","GitHub Actions","Gherkin"],"watchIf":"You let coding agents ship most of your code and want a way to review what the system has become without reading every diff.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Architecture & design","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":19733,"title":"Bootiful Spring Boot 4","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["graalvm","java","virtual threads","spring boot"],"abstract":["Spring Boot 4.x and Java 25+ have arrived, making it an exciting time to be a Java developer! Join me, Josh Long (@starbuxman), as we dive into the future of Spring Boot with Java 25. We'll learn how to scale your applications and codebases effortlessly, explore the robust Spring Boot ecosystem featuring AI, modularity, seamless data access, security, and cutting-edge production optimizations like Project Loom's virtual threads, Project Leyden, GraalVM, and more. Let's explore the latest-and-greatest in Spring Boot to build faster, more scalable, more efficient, more modular, more secure, and more intelligent systems and services."],"favourites":516,"speakers":[{"name":"Josh Long","company":"the Spring team","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GhTAlrBfIHBoWpP1mfcKm-kRON4bdBF_LccGehD=s96-c","bio":"<a href=\"http://twitter.com/starbuxman\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--gray-400);\">Josh (@starbuxman)</a> has been the first Spring Developer Advocate since 2010. Josh is a Java Champion, author of 7 books (including <a href=\"http://reactivespring.io/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--gray-400);\">&quot;Reactive Spring&quot;</a>) and numerous best-selling video training (including <a href=\"https://www.safaribooksonline.com/library/view/building-microservices-with/9780134192468/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--gray-400);\">&quot;Building Microservices with Spring Boot Livelessons&quot;</a>with Spring Boot co-founder Phil Webb), and an open-source contributor (Spring Boot, Spring Integration, Axon, Spring Cloud, Activiti, Vaadin, etc), a Youtuber (<a href=\"https://youtube.com/@coffeesoftware\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--gray-400);\">Coffee + Software with Josh Long</a> as well as <a href=\"http://bit.ly/spring-tips-playlist\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--gray-400);\">my Spring Tips series </a>), and a podcaster (<a href=\"http://bootifulpodcast.fm/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--gray-400);\">&quot;A Bootiful Podcast&quot;</a>).<br><br>","linkedin":"joshlong","bluesky":"starbuxman.bsky.social","twitter":"starbuxman"}],"start":"2026-10-07T13:10:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T14:00:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"1PtdpItmzTo","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Josh Long live-codes a dog-and-cat Spring Boot 4.1 app on Java 27 to tour API versioning, HTTP interface clients, resilience, SSRF filtering, OpenTelemetry, Spring gRPC, BeanRegistrar, JobRunr and Leyden AOT.","summary":["Josh Long opens with his usual selfie, plugs a free Spring Security book and the next day's security session with Daniel Garnier-Moiroux, and mentions Spring Tools now offers MCP support for Claude Code. His case for still writing code by hand: AI models make mistakes, so developers need to know what good looks like to validate what comes out. He then urges teams to upgrade much faster. The Spring portfolio, like other open source projects, has seen a flood of CVE reports, fixes ship before the CVEs are disclosed, and small issues can be chained into critical exploits.","The demo starts on start.spring.io with Java 27 (Long credits compact object headers with 10-20% less RAM) and Spring Boot 4.1, which is modularised so the web server and REST client are separate starters. Against Postgres with Spring Data JDBC and virtual threads, he shows the new lazy connection-fetch setting that delays taking a pooled connection until real I/O inside a @Transactional method. A dogs endpoint gets two versions via API versioning, chosen by an X-Dogs-Version header. A cat-facts client goes from a hand-written RestClient to a declarative HTTP interface registered with @ImportHttpServices.","For robustness he adds @EnableResilientMethods with @Retryable (and mentions @ConcurrencyLimit) from Spring Framework 7, citing last year's AWS us-east-1 and Cloudflare outages. An InetAddressFilter bean in Boot 4.1 blocks outbound requests by host, subnet or category to counter server-side request forgery, across all supported HTTP clients. The new OpenTelemetry starter sends metrics to a local Grafana stack from Docker Compose. Spring gRPC, now managed by Boot, serves a dogs.proto service, called with grpcurl.","He explains @Component stereotypes versus @Bean methods, then introduces BeanRegistrar for programmatic, loop-friendly bean registration. He uses it to wire JobRunr (from Belgian developer Ronald Dehuysser): a recurring job persisted in Postgres so missed runs survive restarts, with carbon-aware scheduling that shifts runs based on local grid data. To finish, a Project Leyden AOT cache script, which works on any standard JDK, cuts startup from about 1.7 to 0.47 seconds."],"keyPoints":["Long says Spring now sees many CVE reports per month, against an average of about 1.5 per month across the portfolio before; fixes ship in releases before disclosure, so teams need a fast upgrade path.","Spring Boot 4 splits auto-configuration into smaller modules: spring-boot-starter-webmvc and the REST client starter are separate, so a CLI or batch app can call HTTP services without a web server.","Spring Boot 4.1 adds a lazy connection-fetch data source option so a @Transactional method holds a pooled connection only while it does SQL, which matters when virtual threads let the web tier flood the pool.","API versioning is built in: annotate handlers with version 1.0 and 2.0, set a default version and resolve it from a custom header.","Declarative HTTP interface clients (@GetExchange, activated with @ImportHttpServices) replace hand-written RestClient code.","Spring Framework 7 brings resilience without extra libraries: @EnableResilientMethods, @Retryable with included exceptions, and @ConcurrencyLimit as a semaphore.","An InetAddressFilter bean in Boot 4.1 restricts outbound HTTP calls by host, IP, subnet or category (for example external-only) to block SSRF-style exfiltration.","BeanRegistrar lets you register beans in code with loops and conditionals, choosing constructor inference or a custom supplier; Long uses it to wire a JobRunr launcher and handler."],"takeaways":["Set up a fast path to production so you can take Spring patch releases as soon as they ship.","Turn on lazy connection fetching if you use virtual threads with long @Transactional methods.","Replace hand-written HTTP client code with HTTP interfaces, and add @Retryable for flaky downstream services.","Register an InetAddressFilter to limit where your services may make outbound requests.","Try the Leyden AOT cache workflow on your Spring Boot app to measure the startup gain on a stock JDK."],"quotes":[{"text":"That is the world's first good JavaScript.","at":"08:11"},{"text":"Every time a CVE is published, that's a dinner bell for hackers.","at":"11:46"},{"text":"Don't debug DSLs on stage. That's rule number five of public speaking.","at":"41:08"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Intro and why know good code"},{"at":"08:11","title":"Java 27 and the CVE wave"},{"at":"12:50","title":"Data, virtual threads, lazy connections"},{"at":"18:05","title":"API versioning and HTTP interfaces"},{"at":"23:01","title":"Retries and SSRF filtering"},{"at":"26:52","title":"OpenTelemetry and Grafana"},{"at":"28:06","title":"Spring gRPC"},{"at":"35:42","title":"BeanRegistrar and JobRunr"},{"at":"47:06","title":"Leyden AOT startup"}],"tech":["Spring Boot 4.1","Spring Framework 7","Java 27","Java 25","Spring Data JDBC","PostgreSQL","Virtual threads","RestClient","HTTP interface clients","OpenTelemetry","Grafana","Docker Compose","Spring gRPC","gRPC","Protocol Buffers","grpcurl","JobRunr","Project Leyden","Compact object headers","Spring Tools","Claude Code","MCP"],"watchIf":"You maintain Spring Boot 3 apps and want a fast, demo-driven tour of what Boot 4.x and Framework 7 add before you upgrade.","themes":["Spring & frameworks","Java language & JDK","JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":true}},{"id":7017,"title":"The Importance of HITL to Avoid Chaos and EU Regulations for AI","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["hitl","compliance","autonomy","microservices"],"abstract":["AI coding agents are rapidly becoming first-class citizens in enterprise software workflows. Teams are already delegating consequential decisions to autonomous agents: generating database migrations, refactoring service boundaries, modifying schemas, producing runbooks, and calling external tools. But as agent autonomy grows, a critical question emerges — who is responsible when something goes wrong?","This talk explores the discipline of Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) design, and how to build AI-assisted workflows that keep humans meaningfully in control without sacrificing the productivity gains that make agents worth adopting in the first place. It also examines the growing body of EU regulation that makes HITL not just a good engineering practice, but a legal requirement for certain classes of systems.","The session will demonstrate these concepts through a realistic microservices scenario — a Java enterprise platform with AI-assisted delivery — showing how the absence of human oversight leads to cascading failures, and how a well-designed HITL architecture, grounded in both engineering discipline and regulatory awareness, would have prevented them. Attendees will leave with a concrete framework for moving fast with AI agents without losing control of their systems or their compliance posture."],"favourites":98,"speakers":[{"name":"Juan Antonio Breña 🇪🇸🇪🇺","company":"IG","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-40c3fd7e-fe96-414c-b6c4-4b52f1bd300a.jpg","bio":"From programming microservices to leading engineering teams: this is how I&#39;ve spent my last 20 years in technology as a Java/Spring ecosystem specialist. My career has evolved from designing cloud-native architectures and migrating legacy systems to leading engineering teams focused on high-quality results and efficient processes.","linkedin":"juanantoniobrenamoral","bluesky":null,"twitter":"juanantoniobm"}],"start":"2026-10-06T16:20:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T16:50:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"t9e9FtHB-48","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":49256,"title":"ADRs: The Why and How","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["documentation","architectural","decisions","records"],"abstract":["Decisions, decisions, decision, that is our life every day. But, what is worse than making a bad decision is not remembering why we made a decision, good or bad. Architectural Decision Records are highly important to document the decisions we make, to support the decisions, to bring in necessary and sufficient reasons, and to reevaluate decisions when the business requirements and/or the technologies change. In this presentation we will look at ADRs, how to create them, how to maintain them, and the values they provide."],"favourites":671,"speakers":[{"name":"Venkat Subramaniam","company":null,"image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-83e2efa2-69c8-4599-8ec1-5ec974dd3673.jpg","bio":"Dr. Venkat Subramaniam is an award-winning author, founder of Agile Developer, Inc., and an instructional professor at the University of Houston.<br>He has trained and mentored thousands of software developers in the US, Canada, Europe, and Asia, and is a regularly-invited speaker at several international conferences. Venkat helps his clients effectively apply and succeed with agile practices on their software projects.<br>Venkat is a (co)author of multiple books, including the 2007 Jolt Productivity award winning book Practices of an Agile Developer. His latest book is Functional Programming in Java: Harnessing the Power of Java 8 Lambda Expressions. You can reach him by email at venkats@agiledeveloper.com or on twitter at @venkat_s.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"venkats.bsky.social","twitter":"venkat_s"}],"start":"2026-10-08T14:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"wzN6qq08w0U","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Subramaniam argues that the reasoning behind important decisions is what code never captures, and makes the case for short, immutable, plain-text decision records kept in Git.","summary":["Subramaniam starts by questioning Martin Fowler's definition of architecture as decisions that are hard to change, since plenty of bad code is hard to change too. He proposes instead that architecture is the set of decisions with high impact. Design and architecture sit on a spectrum. Picking a library is design, and a facade can reduce how much of your code touches it. Choosing a language, database or framework is architectural: a library is like renting a house, a framework like buying one. The higher the impact, the more deliberate the decision should be, with reversibility and explicit trade-offs in mind.","Code shows what was done but not why, and comments are too low-level to carry strategic reasoning. Most developers end up as software archaeologists, inheriting decisions from people who have left. He lists the poor reasons behind many decisions: past experience, familiarity, technology from 15 years ago, infatuation with what everyone else uses, and unquestioned corporate standards. He tells of a team expecting a two-week change that would take three months because of old decisions nobody could explain, and of a decision document his supervisor forced him to write that later stopped a colleague from repeating his mistakes.","Writing an ADR is useful in itself: nobody wants to write \"because it's all I know\" in a document that will be reviewed. Every technical decision and corporate standard should have an expiration date and be revisited every three, six or twelve months. An ADR has a title, context, status, the decision, the options considered with pros and cons (he wants at least three), and the consequences for scalability, performance and security. It contains no code-level detail. His rules: ADRs are immutable apart from status and typos, new ADRs supersede old ones and link back to them like a linked list, they stay short, use plain text, are numbered sequentially and live in Git so they can be diffed and reviewed through pull requests. ADR tools are fine if they store plain text behind the UI."],"keyPoints":["Subramaniam defines architecture as decisions with high impact, rather than Fowler's \"decisions that are hard to change\".","Library choices are design decisions that a facade can make reversible. Frameworks lock you in, like buying rather than renting a house.","Code and comments record what was done. ADRs record why, which is strategic rather than tactical.","Writing a decision down makes it awkward to justify it by familiarity, infatuation or \"corporate standard\".","Corporate standards should carry an expiration date and be re-evaluated on a 3/6/12-month cadence.","ADR structure: title, context, status, decision, at least three options with pros and cons, and consequences. No code.","ADRs are immutable. A new ADR supersedes an old one and references it, so the original reasoning survives.","Keep ADRs short, in plain text, sequentially numbered and in Git, and review them through pull requests."],"takeaways":["Write an ADR for your next high-impact choice, such as a framework or database, listing at least three options and their consequences.","Store ADRs as numbered plain-text files in the repository and review them through pull requests.","Never edit an accepted ADR's reasoning. Write a new one that supersedes it.","Put a review date on your team's technology standards."],"quotes":[{"text":"Using a library is like renting a house, but using a framework is like buying a house.","at":"09:47"},{"text":"While the painting is still there, the meaning is lost forever.","at":"20:31"},{"text":"Every technical decision should have an expiration label.","at":"33:03"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"What is architecture?"},{"at":"07:14","title":"Libraries, frameworks and reversibility"},{"at":"12:13","title":"Trade-offs and the why"},{"at":"17:10","title":"Software archaeology"},{"at":"21:42","title":"How decisions really get made"},{"at":"24:30","title":"Documentation that matters"},{"at":"33:03","title":"Expiration labels on standards"},{"at":"42:42","title":"What goes in an ADR"},{"at":"45:05","title":"Rules: immutable, short, versioned"}],"tech":["Architecture Decision Records","Git","Facade pattern"],"watchIf":"Your team keeps inheriting decisions nobody can explain and you want a lightweight, persuasive argument for recording them.","themes":["Architecture & design","Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":5051,"title":"🧰 Dev Containers: the ultimate toolbox for developers?","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["dependencies","devcontainers","configuration","docker"],"abstract":["At some point, we’ve all had to (re)install our entire development environment after switching machines.","Whether by choice or simply during on-boarding at a new company, a new mission,...","We’ve also all spent far to many hours figuring out which tools to install just to submit a PR or MR to an open source project.","And in many other situations, we quickly find ourselves facing the nightmare of configuring tools and frameworks when all we really want to do is what we love: coding.","And let’s not even get started on version updates and inter-dependencies 😅...","Fortunately, there is a solution to all these problems: dev containers.","During this tools-in-action session, I invite you to explore them with me.","The only thing I allow myself to install on my machine: Docker!","Everything else will be handled through configuration so that tools, frameworks, version management, and dependencies become a non-event in your development workflow.","You’ll leave knowing how to use dev containers and how to build your entire development stack as code."],"favourites":320,"speakers":[{"name":"Stéphane Philippart","company":"OVHcloud","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-9d422934-263f-49b6-a07c-fbc56af630a9.jpg","bio":"During more than 20 years in the software development profession, I was able to confront myself with many different technologies. And it is this diversity that makes me continue to love our job so much.<br>For 5 years now, I have had the chance to evolve within OVHcloud as a developer advocate. This position allows me to discover new technologies or disciplines such as Artificial Intelligence or &quot;native cloud&quot; for example.<br>But my heart of old Java developer can not move too far from this technology which is the common thread of my many years as a developer. It is with humility that I share this experience but also the novelties of our ecosystem through my conferences and the TADx meetup co-created in Tours.","linkedin":"philippartstephane","bluesky":"wilda.bsky.social","twitter":"wildagsx"}],"start":"2026-10-06T14:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"eyJ5LO-Kyuw","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Philippart shows how Dev Containers let Docker be the only thing installed on a laptop, building a Java toolchain up from a devcontainer.json to shared features, templates and prebuilt images.","summary":["Stéphane Philippart starts from familiar pain: reinstalling tools on a new machine, onboarding onto a project, giving up on an open source fix because of the setup, or wanting to try an AI assistant without letting it run rm -rf on your laptop. His answer is Dev Containers: local VS Code connects to a VS Code server inside a container, with the source mounted as a volume. In a live demo on a Mac with no Java installed, he adds a configuration from the Java template (Java 25 on Debian Trixie), reopens in the container and runs Java there. He then switches to a generic base image and uses features to install Java 26 from Temurin, Maven and JBang via SDKMAN, with customizations that pre-install the JBang and Red Hat Java extensions.","Each following step adds one more level of reuse. postCreateCommand runs a script, here installing the OVHcloud CLI and a JBang-installed httpd command, remoteEnv sets the PATH, and port forwarding opens the app in the host browser. He turns the script into a local feature (a folder with devcontainer-feature.json and install.sh), then into a remote feature published to the GitHub registry using Microsoft's feature starter repo and its GitHub Action. He also covers custom Dockerfiles pushed to a private registry, Docker Compose with a PostgreSQL service, custom templates from the template starter repo, and `devcontainer build --push`, which bakes everything into an image so the config shrinks to two lines. The downsides he lists: heavy images, rebuilds that need a network connection, and weak IntelliJ support (Ultimate only, plugins installed locally)."],"keyPoints":["Features install tools such as Java 26 (Temurin), Maven and JBang into a generic image without writing a Dockerfile.","postCreateCommand covers tools that have no feature, and remoteEnv sets container environment variables such as PATH.","Local features live in .devcontainer/<name>/ with devcontainer-feature.json (options) and install.sh. The official feature-starter repo plus a GitHub Action publishes them to the GitHub registry so other projects can share them.","Templates (devcontainer-template.json with options) and the devcontainer CLI's build command can bake the whole setup into one image, so a project's config is just a name and an image.","Downsides: images can be heavy, every config change triggers a rebuild that needs internet, and the IntelliJ integration needs Ultimate and installs plugins locally, which Philippart says goes against the spirit of Dev Containers."],"takeaways":["Add a .devcontainer/devcontainer.json to one project, starting from an official template, and see whether new contributors need anything besides Docker.","Move repeated postCreateCommand scripts into a feature published from the devcontainers feature-starter repo, so a change only has to be made once.","Use Docker Compose in devcontainer.json to bring up the database next to the dev container instead of installing it locally."],"quotes":[{"text":"If I succeed to create my Dockerfile, I think you can too.","at":"20:53"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why environment setup hurts"},{"at":"01:52","title":"Dev Containers architecture"},{"at":"03:13","title":"Java from a template"},{"at":"05:42","title":"Features and customizations"},{"at":"08:14","title":"Post-create commands and ports"},{"at":"10:48","title":"Local and remote features"},{"at":"13:58","title":"Dockerfiles and Docker Compose"},{"at":"17:01","title":"Custom templates and prebuilt images"},{"at":"20:53","title":"Limitations and IntelliJ"}],"tech":["Dev Containers","VS Code","Docker","Docker Compose","Docker Desktop","devcontainer CLI","JBang","SDKMAN","Maven","Eclipse Temurin","PostgreSQL","GitHub Actions","IntelliJ IDEA","OVHcloud"],"watchIf":"You onboard people onto projects with long setup instructions, or want a disposable, reproducible Java environment, and haven't used Dev Containers beyond the basics.","themes":["Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":5538,"title":"Strangling the dragon: modernizing legacy systems with the Strangler Fig pattern","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["strangler","rewrite","legacy","conway"],"abstract":["Stuck in a legacy system that needs modernizing? Feel like the best course of action is to throw it in a dumpster, light it on fire and rewrite everything from scratch - maybe even in a different language?","Hold that thought. While it may be tempting to start a rewrite, rewrites of complicated systems rarely succeed for multiple reasons. Chief among them is time and the fact that not everything can stop and wait for the rewrite, but another important contributor is the second system effect, which usually causes rewrites to be over-engineered and bloated in comparison to what they replace","In this talk, I'll share my experiences with the Strangler Fig pattern, a better approach where the original system is gradually replaced by breaking out or rewriting parts into separate systems. I'll also talk about combining this with Domain Driven Design and how to use the new design to execute an Inverse Conway Manoeuvre to ensure you're working with Conway's law and not against it."],"favourites":303,"speakers":[{"name":"Daniel Raneland","company":"factor10","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-2252e0af-bbe1-4c6f-9ab8-0858a53ce963.jpg","bio":"Raniz is a programmer, architect, speaker and coach at factor10. He is a problem solver who keeps track of the bigger picture. He is prestigeless, likes to get into new domains, and loves sharing knowledge and ideas. Raniz has worked with system- and software architecture at several companies since 2010 and has been with factor10 since 2021. When not working he&#39;s into beer brewing, sourdough bread, 3D printing and triathlons.","linkedin":"raneland","bluesky":"raniz.se","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-09T09:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T10:40:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"Daniel Raneland shares experience using the Strangler Fig pattern, with DDD and the Inverse Conway Manoeuvre, to modernise legacy systems instead of rewriting them.","summary":["The session argues against the tempting full rewrite of a legacy system. Rewrites of complicated systems rarely succeed: they take long, the business cannot pause while they happen, and the second system effect tends to make the replacement over-engineered and bloated.","Daniel Raneland instead describes his experience with the Strangler Fig pattern, where the original system is gradually replaced by breaking out or rewriting parts as separate systems. The talk also covers combining it with Domain Driven Design and using the resulting design for an Inverse Conway Manoeuvre, so that team structure works with Conway's law rather than against it."],"keyPoints":["Rewrites fail because of time, the need to keep delivering, and the second system effect.","Strangler Fig replaces the legacy system piece by piece as separate systems.","Domain Driven Design guides where to cut the system.","Inverse Conway Manoeuvre aligns teams with the target design."],"takeaways":["Pick one bounded part of the legacy system to extract first instead of proposing a rewrite."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Strangler Fig pattern","Domain Driven Design","Conway's law"],"watchIf":"You maintain a legacy system and are being pushed toward a big-bang rewrite.","themes":["Legacy modernization","Architecture & design"],"demo":false}},{"id":7018,"title":"A Year of Agentic AI Evolution: Lessons Learned Building Production-grade Agentic Systems","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["observability","agentic","orchestration","langchain4j"],"abstract":["Last year at Devoxx, we introduced the first version of LangChain4j's agentic framework and explored the core patterns behind agentic AI systems.","Since then, real-world usage, community feedback, and a lot of experimentation have taught us that production-ready agentic systems need more than a fixed set of predefined patterns. Different use cases need different trade-offs, and a predefined list can only take you so far.","In this session, we'll show how LangChain4j's agentic support has evolved. We have moved toward a more flexible orchestration model that lets developers compose their own strategies, while also allowing us to grow the library of built-in patterns. We will demonstrate in practice how these new patterns work discussing their respective pros and cons and fields of applicability.","We'll also cover the operational capabilities added to the framework to make these workloads production-ready. You'll see how to observe and trace agent execution, persist state, recover long-running workflows after failures, and handle human-in-the-loop pauses without losing control of the process.","Join us as we demonstrate these new features along with their trade-offs, and learn what it takes to build robust, production-ready agentic systems."],"favourites":549,"speakers":[{"name":"Mario Fusco","company":"IBM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GinuLQCwL67BBgfGsiWRl3mu3Ya0TbDffhYFiXX=s96-c","bio":"Mario is a senior principal software engineer at IBM working as Drools project lead. Among his interests there are also high performance systems and generative AI, being an active contributor of widely adopted projects like Quarkus and LangChain4j. He is also a Java Champion, the JUG Milano coordinator, a frequent speaker and the co-author of &quot;Modern Java in Action&quot; published by Manning.","linkedin":"mario-fusco-3467213","bluesky":null,"twitter":"mariofusco"},{"name":"Kevin Dubois","company":"IBM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocInnMfs42NN14UEEtFboGAF5baXQdsTFdbDo_ZuR1DADFHUONPEfA=s96-c","bio":"Kevin Dubois is a software architect and platform engineer with a career spanning over 20 years. He is often featured as a keynote speaker at conferences around the world where he shares his experience and knowledge about cloud native &amp; AI software development, developer experience, open source and Java. Kevin is also an author and Java Champion. He currently works as a Senior Principal Developer Advocate at IBM, and is  Technical Lead for the CNCF Developer Experience Technical Advisory Group as well as an ambassador for the Agentic AI Foundation.","linkedin":"kevindubois","bluesky":"kevindubois.com","twitter":"kevindubois"}],"start":"2026-10-07T12:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T12:50:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"nEW0kosJjc8","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"LangChain4j's agentic module now rests on a pluggable Planner interface, so you can write and mix your own orchestration patterns; Quarkus adds fault tolerance, observability, persistence and guardrails.","summary":["Mario Fusco and Kevin Dubois pick up from their Devoxx 2025 session on LangChain4j agentic patterns. After recapping the basics (AI services, prompts, chat memory, tools and MCP, RAG, guardrails, and the newer skills feature), they explain that feedback after last year showed the four built-in workflows (sequence, parallel, conditional, loop) plus the supervisor were not enough: every team wanted its own orchestration strategy.","Fusco's answer is a Planner interface with three methods, two of them defaulted: given the current agentic scope, return the next action, which is either 'invoke these agents' or 'done'. He rewrote all existing patterns on top of it (the sequential planner is a cursor over the sub-agents) and added new ones in a separate langchain4j-agentic-patterns module, including a goal-oriented planner that treats agent inputs and outputs as pre- and post-conditions and walks the shortest path through the resulting graph, and a blackboard pattern, shown with a medical diagnosis example. Patterns compose, so a loop can be inserted inside a goal-oriented plan, which on its own cannot loop.","The live demo combines a story writer with a voting pattern: three critic agents (style, originality, engagement) score in parallel, an aggregation function averages the votes, and an editor loops until the score reaches 8.5. The Quarkus Dev UI shows both the agent topology and the execution trace, with timing, tokens, inputs and outputs per call. A new @ChatModelSupplier hook selects the model per invocation: GPT-4o mini while the score is below 7.8, GPT-5.1 for the final polishing. Dubois then shows a router that uses a newly released 'decision model', which returns probabilities rather than chat, to route a request to medical and legal agents above a 0.5 threshold; it is now available as a DecisionRouterPlanner.","The last part covers production concerns via Quarkus: MicroProfile Fault Tolerance @Retry and @Fallback on agents, OpenTelemetry metrics for the LGTM stack, a Langfuse extension that starts a local instance in dev mode, guardrails on tool calls as well as model calls, Quarkus Flow to persist workflows (for example human-in-the-loop pauses) via Kafka or a database so they survive restarts, MCP Server 2.0 supporting the new stateless spec, and A2A with multi-tenancy. They argue that Java's typing makes it a better fit for agentic systems than Python."],"keyPoints":["The Planner interface returns a next action (invoke one or more agents, or done); all built-in patterns were rewritten on it, and it is passed to a generic planner builder as a supplier so each execution gets a fresh instance.","New patterns live in a separate langchain4j-agentic-patterns module: goal-oriented, blackboard, voting, decision router and others, with a slide table of pros, cons and fit for each.","The goal-oriented planner builds a dependency graph from agent inputs and outputs and follows the shortest path, so it structurally cannot loop; nesting a loop agent inside it works around that.","The Quarkus Dev UI visualises agent topology and each execution, including duration, token use, and every agent's input and output.","A @ChatModelSupplier method can pick the model per call, e.g. GPT-4o mini for rough drafts and GPT-5.1 only once the critics' score is close to the 8.5 exit threshold.","LangChain4j added support for a newly released type of decision model within days of its release; the DecisionRouterPlanner calls every agent whose relevance probability exceeds a threshold, in parallel.","Quarkus Flow persists agentic workflows so a human-in-the-loop pause survives timeouts, pod restarts and rescheduling; by default LangChain4j keeps that state in memory.","Guardrails now apply to tool invocations as well as model input and output, and MCP tools can be wrapped as non-AI agents."],"takeaways":["When the built-in workflows don't fit, implement the Planner interface rather than forcing a supervisor to do deterministic routing.","Cut cost by choosing the cheapest model that is good enough per agent, and switch models at runtime based on intermediate results.","Put @Retry and @Fallback on agent calls just as you would on remote service calls.","If your agents pause for human input, persist workflow state (e.g. with Quarkus Flow) instead of relying on in-memory state.","Work through the Quarkus LangChain4j workshop on GitHub to try these patterns and the enterprise features hands-on."],"quotes":[{"text":"The API is stupidly simple, so you can really create and plug what you want.","at":"16:16"},{"text":"You don't want everything to be a string. You want to keep reasoning with the types of your domain.","at":"47:16"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Recap of LangChain4j agentic"},{"at":"07:45","title":"The pluggable planner"},{"at":"11:32","title":"Goal-oriented and blackboard patterns"},{"at":"20:05","title":"Voting pattern demo"},{"at":"28:06","title":"Dynamic model selection"},{"at":"30:51","title":"Decision models as routers"},{"at":"37:22","title":"Enterprise features in Quarkus"},{"at":"47:16","title":"Why Java for agents"}],"tech":["LangChain4j","LangChain4j Agentic","Quarkus","Quarkus Dev UI","MicroProfile Fault Tolerance","SmallRye","OpenTelemetry","Grafana LGTM","Langfuse","Quarkus Flow","Kafka","MCP","A2A","GPT-4o mini","GPT-5.1"],"watchIf":"You build multi-agent systems in Java and need more control over orchestration, cost and production resilience than fixed workflow patterns give you.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","LLMs in Java apps","Spring & frameworks"],"demo":true}},{"id":24229,"title":"Building Autonomous AI Agents with Spring","type":"Hands-on Lab","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["agentic","java","autonomous","spring"],"abstract":["Software development has rapidly evolved from explicit, instruction-based programming to integrating the non-deterministic reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models.","Now, we face the next massive paradigm shift: moving from passive AI tools to Agentic AI.","In this workshop, you will learn everything you need to build these autonomous systems using Java, Spring AI, the Model Context Protocol (MCP) and more. We will move beyond basic prompting to master chat memory, RAG, tool integration, and agentic patterns. Most importantly, we address the \"day two\" challenges of production: ensuring your agents are observable, secure, and reliable. By the end of the session, you will have built a multi-agent system capable of solving complex, high-level tasks.","Join us and transform your applications from simple interfaces into proactive digital colleagues."],"favourites":117,"speakers":[{"name":"Timo Salm","company":"Broadcom","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-93dfac6b-7673-4fca-81c6-aa9fb4a0c084.jpg","bio":"<p fgid=\"5163\">Timo Salm is a Principal Solutions Engineer at VMware Tanzu by Broadcom with over a decade of experience in customer-facing roles, modern applications, DevSecOps, and AI. In his roles, he ensures that the most strategic customers in the EMEA region achieve their goals with VMware Tanzu&#39;s Spring, developer platform, AI, and data products. Another essential part of his role is continuously learning through experimenting hands-on with innovations and sharing the outcomes with colleagues, customers, and the community via, for example, conferences. Before Timo joined Pivotal, which VMware and now Broadcom acquired, he worked for consulting firms in the automotive industry as a software architect and full-stack developer.","linkedin":"timosalm","bluesky":null,"twitter":"salmto"},{"name":"Sandra Ahlgrimm","company":"Microsoft / GitHub","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-055a7a76-5e5c-4ed2-a7af-f624fb91c129.jpg","bio":"Sandra Ahlgrimm is a Senior Developer Advocate for GitHub at Microsoft, specializing in supporting Java Developers. With over a decade of experience as a Java developer, she brings a wealth of knowledge to her role. Sandra is passionate about containers and has recently learned to love AI.<br>As a leader in the tech community, Sandra actively contributes to the Berlin Java User Group (JUG) and the Berlin Docker MeetUp. Her expertise extends beyond coding; she focuses on LangChain4j integrations and serves as the primary point of contact for developer feedback related to Java in Visual Studio Code (VS Code) and GitHub Copilot in IntelliJ. Additionally, her interest in performance and event-driven architectures led to her involvement with native images. Therefore, Sandra represents Microsoft on the GraalVM Program Advisory Board.","linkedin":"sandraahlgrimm","bluesky":null,"twitter":"skriemhild"}],"start":"2026-10-05T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T14:30:00Z","room":"BOF 2","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A 3-hour lab building a multi-agent system with Java, Spring AI and MCP, including chat memory, RAG and production concerns like observability and security.","summary":["Timo Salm and Sandra Ahlgrimm's lab frames agentic AI as the next shift after integrating non-deterministic LLM reasoning into software. Participants use Java, Spring AI and the Model Context Protocol to build autonomous systems, moving beyond basic prompting.","The lab covers chat memory, RAG, tool integration and agentic patterns, and also addresses day-two production challenges: making agents observable, secure and reliable. By the end, attendees will have built a multi-agent system able to tackle complex, high-level tasks."],"keyPoints":["Built with Java, Spring AI and the Model Context Protocol.","Covers chat memory, RAG, tool integration and agentic patterns.","Addresses production concerns: observability, security and reliability.","Outcome is a working multi-agent system."],"takeaways":["Add observability and security considerations to agent prototypes early, not after launch."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Java","Spring AI","MCP","RAG"],"watchIf":"You are a Spring developer who wants hands-on experience building agents beyond a simple chatbot.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Spring & frameworks"],"demo":false}},{"id":51951,"title":"Agentic Engineering Reconversion","type":"Keynote","duration":20,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["software","developers","ai","onboarding"],"abstract":["What does a \"software engineer\" do today? How do we balance our personal life with the anxiety about the future, and the burning excitment of the dreams that agents allow us to build today? What's the fastest way to onboard this reconversion and how can one speed it up? This keynote shares lessons from the experience of hundreds of AI-converted developers all over the world, aiming to help you navigate these extra-ordinary times our profession faces today."],"favourites":559,"speakers":[{"name":"Victor Rentea","company":"Freelance","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-51aee41f-0079-4820-98bb-56e70a2e89d3.jpg","bio":"With 2 decades of software engineering experience, Victor spent the last 10 years training thousands of developers and architects from 150+ companies. Since early 2026, he’s been working hard to help his clients adopt Agentic Engineering, coaching AI Champions, developers, testers, product owners, ops, and managers with practical, actionable advice to kick-start their agentic reconversion. Every year, Victor gives energetic, intense conference talks in which he distills his training sessions - so be sure not to miss these ideas, heavily brainstormed with millennia of total experience. More about him on<a href=\"https://victorrentea.ro\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\"> </a><u style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(17, 85, 204);\"><a href=\"https://victorrentea.ro\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">https://victorrentea.ro</a></u>, and <u style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(17, 85, 204);\"><a href=\"https://agentic.how\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">https://agentic.how</a></u>.","linkedin":"victor-rentea-trainer","bluesky":null,"twitter":"victorrentea"}],"start":"2026-10-07T09:10:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"vAfQ4f8wIVI","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Rentea's fast, comic keynote on retraining yourself as an agentic engineer: learn the basics, protect your brain from agent-driven burnout, shift to judgement, product and people skills, and force the habit.","summary":["Victor Rentea, who says he wrote code for 19 years and stopped six months ago, opens by showing his own setup, including a dictation tool built on a Whisper large-v3-turbo model he fine-tuned on 40 hours of his own voice (two hours on a rented H100 for about four dollars). He then lists the basics every developer should already know: context windows and the 'dumb zone', writing skills, specialised sub-agents, prompt caching and model/effort choice. He points to Anthropic's courses on Skilljar and stresses creative feedback loops, such as giving an agent two browsers to compare a legacy JSP screen with its Angular rewrite pixel by pixel. To keep up with what agents generate, he recommends memory techniques: active recall, chunking into groups of four, and dual coding. He then warns that running three or four terminals in parallel leads to rubber-stamping ('you are only able to type K'), drained agency and burnout, so you must learn your own cognitive limits and how to recover.","The rest is about what remains human. System design, the '-ilities' and a cloud certification still matter. Rentea argues that manual debugging and tuning are now 'unethical' at work: Claude found 17 memory leaks from heap dumps and 10 bottlenecks from JFR and async-profiler output in 30 minutes, and a friend's team confirmed a Keycloak bug with adversarial agents. He asks for humility, critical thinking, real listening, estimating in dollars of tokens, watching for sycophancy, and a product mindset close to users. His 'conversion' homework: generate over 95% of your code, buy a personal subscription if your employer won't, never edit files by hand, dictate prompts, run four conversations in parallel, pair with an internal AI champion, and drop the Copilot-in-IntelliJ combination for VS Code or a CLI agent."],"keyPoints":["Running several agent terminals at once pushes developers into approving without reading; Rentea treats burnout and loss of agency as the main risk and urges people to learn their limits and how to recover.","To own decisions made at agent speed, use learning techniques: active recall ('explain it with the lid closed'), chunking, dual coding, and asking for ELI5 explanations.","He cites Claude tracing 17 memory leaks from heap dumps and 10 hotspots from JFR and async-profiler reports in 30 minutes, and concludes that hand debugging and tuning no longer pay at work.","The engineer's role moves left (product owner), up (architecture) or right (quality guardian), and needs humility, critical thinking, listening and sensitivity to AI sycophancy.","Concrete targets: over 95% generated code, no manual file edits, dictated prompts, four parallel sessions, and estimates expressed in dollars of tokens."],"takeaways":["Build and use at least one skill, and learn how prompt caching and context limits affect cost and quality.","Design feedback loops the agent can run itself, such as browser-driven visual comparisons between a legacy UI and its rewrite.","Ask the agent to show the dollar cost of options so you start estimating work in tokens.","Spend a week editing no files by hand and dictating prompts instead of typing them."],"quotes":[{"text":"The nerd is dead. Long live the builder.","at":"09:58"},{"text":"Manual debugging and tuning become unethical. Let go. Unlearn. Goodbye, expertise.","at":"12:21"},{"text":"Superpowers need a quest. Doing good and helping others will keep you sane.","at":"20:09"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Rentea's setup"},{"at":"01:54","title":"Agent basics"},{"at":"04:31","title":"Learning at agent speed"},{"at":"07:20","title":"Burnout and agency"},{"at":"09:58","title":"System design and unlearning"},{"at":"12:21","title":"Human skills that matter"},{"at":"17:44","title":"Forcing the conversion"}],"tech":["Claude Code","Claude Opus","Whisper","MLX","RunPod","JFR","async-profiler","Keycloak","Wispr Flow","GitHub Copilot","IntelliJ IDEA"],"watchIf":"You feel stuck between agent hype and exhaustion and want one opinionated practitioner's checklist for retraining, delivered in 20 minutes.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":9035,"title":"Toxic Flows in MCP Servers and Skills: What Your Agent Blindly Trusts","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["deserialization","trust","mcp","skills"],"abstract":["AI agents don't just trust MCP servers. They trust Skills too. Both are attack surfaces, yet most teams treat neither as part of the same trust model.","In this talk, we introduce toxic flows: trust paths through an agent stack where tool descriptions, MCP responses, and Skill definitions can leak sensitive context, manipulate agent decisions, and trigger unintended actions without the model violating a single instruction. We demonstrate this live.","This session is not about adding authentication or wrapping everything in TLS. It's about understanding how trust failures emerge, how they mirror familiar software risks like dependency confusion and unsafe deserialization, and how to design MCP servers and Skill integrations that reduce trust, contain blast radius, and keep agent systems defensible.","Attendees will leave with a practical framework for identifying trust boundaries, evaluating agent integrations, and hardening their AI stack against toxic flows."],"favourites":357,"speakers":[{"name":"Brian Vermeer","company":"Snyk","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocIVtHtowJv_NiNRnihOsjh4F37Gwk5jjlcoLoxADz1WPzaLlDTS=s96-c","bio":"Staff Developer Advocate for Snyk, Java Champion, Oracle Ace Pro, and Software Engineer with over a decade of hands-on experience in creating and maintaining software. He is passionate about Java, (Pure) Functional Programming and Cybersecurity. Brian is a JUG leader for the Virtual JUG and the NLJUG. He also co-leads the DevSecCon community and is a community manager for Foojay. He is a regular international speaker on mostly Java-related conferences like JavaOne, Devnexus, Devoxx, Jfokus, JavaZone and many more. Besides all that, Brian is a military reserve for the Royal Netherlands Air Force and a Taekwondo Master / Teacher.","linkedin":"brianvermeer","bluesky":"brianvermeer.nl","twitter":"brianverm"}],"start":"2026-10-08T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T08:20:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"c4F7Iy3XqaY","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Vermeer demos five live attacks through a Java MCP server and Claude Code skills, all exploiting what agents trust, and argues pinned versions, curated inventories and automated scanning beat human approval prompts.","summary":["Brian Vermeer opens with Snyk research on ClawHub, the OpenClaw skills hub: of roughly 4,000 skills in February 2026, he said 37% had a security flaw and 13.4% a critical one, such as malware binaries, active prompt injection or secret exfiltration. His persona Dave is a careful Java developer who pins versions and reads changelogs, but under pressure to be an \"agentic developer\" he installs MCP servers and skills without the same scrutiny.","The first demo is a Spring AI MCP server that wraps javaconferences.org, installed the way many READMEs suggest, with an always-latest JBang, npx or uvx command. Vermeer ships a new version whose tool runs Runtime.getRuntime().exec and pops a calculator: a rug pull, because a stdio server runs with your permissions and nothing forces a re-review. He then removes the side effect, as if the server were sandboxed, and poisons only the tool description, telling Claude to also query an email tool and hide that it did. The agent reports a made-up conference in Breda. This is a toxic flow: untrusted input, an agent following the instructions, and a sensitive action through a different connector, with no single server looking malicious.","Skills get the same treatment. A code-formatter SKILL.md hides hex-encoded, HTML-escaped instructions that look harmless in the rendered view, and the agent signs off as \"Skynet Quality Assurance\" and switches to pirate speech. A dependency-updater skill downloads a zip and runs a GraalVM native binary, which the agent cannot inspect, and reports that a stale Logback 1.2.11 is up to date. A git-pulse skill produces a correct changelog while its Python script quietly posts git config to a local \"telemetry\" endpoint. Since a skill is a directory, reading the markdown alone is not enough.","Vermeer argues that a human in the loop is not a security boundary: approval fatigue works like phishing, and he cited real ClawHub malware, a \"SkillGuard\" skill with a reverse shell. Regexes are easy to get around, so detection has to combine deterministic rules with trained models. He runs Snyk's free Agent Scan (uvx snyk-agent-scan at a pinned version), which flags all three skills with risk scores and the hidden email-tool instruction in the MCP server. He closes with team advice and a short pitch for the paid Snyk Evo product, which monitors MCP and skill usage on developer machines."],"keyPoints":["Install commands that always fetch the latest version (npx, uvx, JBang) give implicit trust: you approve an MCP server once and every later release runs with your permissions.","A stdio MCP server runs on your machine. Sandboxing stops code side effects but not a poisoned tool description, which the agent still reads.","Toxic flow = untrusted input + an agent that follows it + a sensitive action, often through another connector. Each server looks fine alone, and the risk is in the combination.","Prompt injections in skills can be hidden with hex encoding, HTML escaping, base64, other languages or invisible characters. The agent can still read them, while the rendered markdown looks clean to a human.","Skills ship scripts and can download binaries. A GraalVM native executable stops the agent from inspecting what it runs.","Vermeer contrasts this with Maven Central's controls (verified namespaces, no version ranges by convention) and says there is no equivalent for MCP servers and skills yet, comparing it to npm around 2015.","Snyk Agent Scan checks the default Claude Code, Cursor and Codex config locations, or a given path, and gave the binary-download skill 1,000/1,000 and the hex-encoded one 300/1,000."],"takeaways":["Pin MCP server versions instead of using latest-tag npx, uvx or JBang install lines, and rescan every update.","Run Snyk Agent Scan, or a similar scanner, over your installed skills and MCP configs, including the scripts inside skill directories.","Keep a team inventory of approved, scanned MCP servers and skills, and only allow the pinned versions on that list.","Run MCP servers in sandboxes, and treat every tool description, tool response and skill file as untrusted input to the agent."],"quotes":[{"text":"Why, as an adversary, should I attack your application while I can attack you?","at":"12:59"},{"text":"Even though I cannot read hex-encoded text, my agent can.","at":"30:35"},{"text":"A human in the loop is not a security boundary.","at":"40:39"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"ClawHub skills research"},{"at":"04:17","title":"Meet Dave, the agentic developer"},{"at":"07:05","title":"Rug pull in an MCP server"},{"at":"14:27","title":"Toxic flows via tool descriptions"},{"at":"20:58","title":"Skills as instructions"},{"at":"25:55","title":"Hidden prompt injection demo"},{"at":"30:35","title":"Downloaded native binary"},{"at":"35:26","title":"Exfiltrating script in a skill"},{"at":"40:39","title":"Why human approval fails"},{"at":"42:45","title":"Scanning with Snyk Agent Scan"}],"tech":["Model Context Protocol (MCP)","Agent skills","Claude Code","Codex","Cursor","Spring AI","JBang","npx","uvx","GraalVM Native Image","Maven Central","ClawHub","OpenClaw","Snyk Agent Scan","Snyk Evo"],"watchIf":"You or your team install MCP servers and skills into coding agents, especially in auto-approve mode, and want to see concretely how they get abused.","themes":["Security & supply chain","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":true}},{"id":22912,"title":"Loop Engineering, beyond prompt, context, and harness engineering","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["codingagents","loopengineering","workflows","automation"],"abstract":["\"I don't prompt Claude anymore. I have loops running that prompt Claude... My job is to write loops.\" said Boris Cherny, the creator of Claude Code, demonstrating a change in the way developers work with their favorite coding agent. Developers are moving away from manually coding and writing isolated prompts, and toward Loop Engineering: the practice of designing automated systems that steer coding agents autonomously.","In this 3-hour deep dive, we’ll look at the concepts, approaches, and architecture behind \"Loop Engineering\". You will learn that the power of AI doesn't (only) lie in the model itself, but in the harness and loops built around it. We will explore the core primitives required to build robust loops (skills, triggers, hooks, Git worktrees, sub-agents, state & memory...), how to implement the fundamental loop structures (turn-based, goal-based, time-based, and proactive), and how to hopefully prevent these autonomous systems from launching rm -Rf on your codebase!","You will leave this session with concrete examples, advice, and tips'n tricks, to build your own loops, replacing tedious manual tasks with self-driving workflows that investigate, implement, and verify their own code and artifacts."],"favourites":439,"speakers":[{"name":"Guillaume Laforge","company":"Google","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AFdZucoQ5lU7oZBBSXSkv4PJE8-JzMxuHatM3ouR8sOOnl8=s96-c","bio":"Guillaume Laforge is a Developer Advocate at Google, focusing on Generative AI and AI agent engineering. He&#39;s also a Java Champion, the co-founder of the Apache Groovy programming language, and a founding member of Les Cast Codeurs podcast. <br>He&#39;s a committer on LangChain4j and Agent Development Kit for Java.","linkedin":"glaforge","bluesky":"glaforge.dev","twitter":"glaforge"},{"name":"Wietse Venema","company":"Google Cloud","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GhbRZTGa5AfR4sF8hWTueCojns-Au_mYSlkZV7i=s96-c","bio":"I’m an engineer on the Google Cloud Run team. I find joy in building things, learning constantly, teaching what I know, and writing about it all. Author of O&#39;Reilly&#39;s book on Cloud Run.<br>","linkedin":"wietse-venema","bluesky":null,"twitter":"wietsevenema"}],"start":"2026-10-06T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T10:30:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"A7ZIf8LkSvE","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Laforge and Venema define loop engineering as designing triggers, goals, checkers, state and stop conditions around coding agents, and demo maker-checker, goal, scheduled and Ralph Wiggum loops built with Antigravity.","summary":["Guillaume Laforge's first loop came from frustration: Google codelabs took him two days to write. He had Antigravity turn the formatting guide into a skill, generate a codelab, compile and run every sample, then walk through the result in a fresh context and report problems, cutting the work to about two hours. Citing Boris Cherny and Peter Steinberger ('design loops that prompt your agents'), the speakers stack the vocabulary: prompt, context, harness, loop and graph engineering. A loop has a trigger, a contract (goal or standard operating procedure), a maker, a separate checker, persisted state and a stopping gate with caps.","They list six primitives: triggers (manual, schedule, events), Git worktrees, skills and rules, connectors such as MCP and A2A, sub-agents, and state files. Loops are placed on a quadrant of manual or autonomous triggers and exits: turn-based, goal-based, time-based and proactive. Laforge demos /goal to lift JaCoCo coverage above 80% one class at a time, a daily scheduled task that redeploys his Devoxx schedule app when the agenda changes, and a GitHub Action that runs a JBang script using his Antigravity Java SDK with a PR-review skill and locked-down tool policies.","Wietse Venema builds loops in Python on the Antigravity SDK, which drives the same Go harness binary as the IDE. His demos add token and tool-call budgets, output truncation and compaction (every 15,000 tokens in one run), a maker-checker where the read-only checker must find something on its first pass, a planner in front, a model-based shell command classifier as a guardrail, a 'maker-user' loop that writes friction logs from the README alone, a docs maker allowed to write only in docs/, a forced red-green-refactor TDD loop, and a 'dreaming' node that turns the maker's trajectory into a Markdown memory.","After the break, Laforge runs Geoffrey Huntley's Ralph Wiggum loop: fresh context per iteration, specs written with Matt Pocock's grill-me skill, and todo and progress files, commit only on success, building a Connect 4 game. He then walks through Andrej Karpathy's autoresearch loop, which ran 83 five-minute nanoGPT training experiments overnight and kept only improvements. Six failure modes follow: runaway token burn, unverified autonomy, uncheckable goals, impossible goals, complexity and context saturation, and away-from-keyboard comprehension rot, illustrated by a 2.0 SDK release full of Object-typed signatures. They close on graph engineering and software factories, citing Zach Lloyd of Warp, while admitting it may be 'engineers looking for engineering things'."],"keyPoints":["A loop's anatomy: trigger, contract (target state or SOP), maker, checker, state carried across iterations, and a stopping gate with caps on tokens, iterations or tool calls.","Never let the maker grade itself in the same conversation; use a fresh context or a different (possibly smaller) model, adversarial review prompts, and deterministic gates such as tests and linters where possible.","Goals must be checkable and reachable: asked for 100% coverage, agents have deleted hard-to-test files temporarily or added coverage ignore lists; 'make this code cleaner' has no exit condition.","Budgets matter in the harness: Venema caps tool calls per turn with a hook that warns at six of eight, truncates noisy tool output, and compacts history at a token threshold, trading lost detail against cost.","Restrict loops with policies, not just prompts: read-only checkers, docs makers that may only write to docs/, and a classifier hook on every shell command to block reads outside the workspace or calls to the cloud metadata server.","The Ralph Wiggum loop avoids context rot by restarting each iteration from scratch and keeping memory in todo/progress files and Git commits.","Karpathy's autoresearch is a roughly 600-line Python loop: hypothesise a change, train for a fixed five minutes, commit if the metric improves, otherwise reset, and log failed experiments so they are not retried.","Venema says a Google experiment found agents are often distracted by TODO comments, more so at higher thinking levels, so clear them out of codebases agents work in."],"takeaways":["Pick one repetitive task (release notes, doc sync, dependency updates) and turn it into a scheduled or goal-based loop with an explicit, verifiable exit condition.","Add a separate checker in a fresh context that must find at least one issue on its first pass.","Set token, iteration and tool-call caps on every autonomous loop before you walk away from it.","Persist loop state in files or Git so each iteration can start with a clean context.","Keep asking your agent to explain the code, architecture and its findings so you still understand what it built."],"quotes":[{"text":"Each time you ask a model to grade itself what it has done, in the same conversation, it will always say it's good. Always.","at":"36:16"},{"text":"99% of the time, it's perfectly fine. And then at one time, it does something completely unhinged.","at":"131:14"},{"text":"I can't shake the feeling that this might be engineers looking for engineering things.","at":"145:37"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why loops: the codelab story"},{"at":"18:28","title":"From prompt to graph engineering"},{"at":"28:27","title":"Anatomy and primitives of loops"},{"at":"43:56","title":"Four kinds of loops"},{"at":"47:54","title":"Goal and scheduled loop demos"},{"at":"60:34","title":"Skills and a PR review action"},{"at":"68:17","title":"SDK loops: budgets to dreaming"},{"at":"95:08","title":"Ralph Wiggum and autoresearch"},{"at":"122:19","title":"Failure modes and dangers"},{"at":"139:14","title":"Graphs and software factories"}],"tech":["Google Antigravity","Antigravity SDK","Gemini","Claude Code","Codex","LangChain4j","Micronaut","JaCoCo","JBang","GitHub Actions","Git worktrees","MCP","A2A","claat","Python","TypeScript","Vite","Google Cloud Run","nanoGPT"],"watchIf":"You already use a coding agent interactively and want concrete patterns, guardrails and failure modes for letting it run unattended on goals, schedules or events.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":7008,"title":"How to survive and thrive as a dev (team) in the exponential age of AI. Real-world AI adoption, smarter ways of working, and lessons from the front lines","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["microteams","architecture","ai","adaptability"],"abstract":["The AI revolution isn’t coming — it’s already altering how we architect products, ship features, and run engineering teams.","During this talk, in his signature charismatic style, Sander Hoogendoorn, CTO at iBOOD.com and a seasoned developer with over four decades of coding experience, doesn’t get stuck in theory or hype, but confronts the pressing issues head-on. You’ll get a no-fluff, grounded view from iBOOD — what we’re already doing with AI, how we built it into our systems, and how it shapes the way we work.","We won’t shy away from the dark side either — vendor lock-in, breaking prompts, model opacity, shifting APIs, and how quickly a dependency can become fragile. These are real risks that shape architectural and organizational decisions we make daily.","To survive exponential speed, we retired many practices: no Scrum, no rigid ceremonies, no big upfront plans, no pull requests or code reviews. Instead, we embrace working in fluid autonomous microteams, continuous learning, experimentation, and radical adaptability.","Expect an honest, fast-paced, and experience-driven session — mixing architecture, culture, and practical insight — on what it truly takes for developers and teams to survive and thrive in the age of AI."],"favourites":714,"speakers":[{"name":"Sander Hoogendoorn","company":"ibood.com","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-4f2917e1-ef34-47e1-a864-09c5577f5ae8.jpg","bio":"Sander Hoogendoorn is an independent dad, avid traveler, and lifelong software developer. With over 40 years of hands-on coding experience under his belt, he still ships code every day — because once a developer, always a developer.<br><br>Currently the CTO at iBOOD, Sander has led technology at several companies over the past decade and was once Capgemini’s global agile thought leader. But don’t expect corporate buzzwords — Sander’s known for cutting through the fluff with a post-agile mindset and a healthy disregard for outdated best practices.<br><br>He helps teams and organizations break rules that need breaking — replacing heavyweight processes with lightweight thinking, and agile dogma with actual flow. If something’s slowing your team down, chances are he’s already ranted about it in a keynote.<br><br>Onstage, Sander brings code, stories, and sharp insights — whether he’s talking disruption, continuous delivery, microservices, monads (yes, really), software architecture, or the lost art of writing beautiful code. His talks are fast-paced, thought-provoking, and never just theory.<br><br>Sander believes in small steps, critical thinking, and building stuff that works. No silver bullets. Just better software, made by better teams.","linkedin":"aahoogendoorn","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T13:10:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T14:00:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"BLBwQpczk7M","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"iBOOD's CTO describes how his 13-person team works with AI and without Scrum, sprints, branches, pull requests or code reviews, deploying around 100 times a day through small steps and autonomous micro-teams.","summary":["Hoogendoorn, CTO of the Dutch e-commerce company iBOOD (130 staff, 13 in tech), presented from a slide tool he had built himself two weeks earlier. When he joined six years ago, the company suffered from what he calls technical death: modified open-source ERP, CMS, CRM and PIM systems, with customer accounts stored in the CMS and synchronised everywhere. Everyone in tech was busy keeping things alive. He argues the cause is always organisational. AI now changes the work so quickly that he rebuilt a sales dashboard in two hours without writing code, and he openly does not know where this ends.","He structures the talk as seven habits. Prioritisation: a broad company vision guides a 70/20/10 split between the target architecture, additions to existing systems, and small requests in a Slack channel. Ideas must serve the goals, be actionable, be small, and be the most important thing; the rest goes to a \"someday maybe\" column that is never revisited. Complexity: using the Cynefin framework, most software problems are complex rather than complicated, so they need safe-to-fail experiments in very small steps. Ownership: a single TypeScript stack, collective code ownership and Dee Hock's \"simplify to amplify\". People pick their own work, much like Amsterdam's Alexanderplein works better without traffic lights.","The list of things they dropped is long: Scrum, sprints, retrospectives, Scrum masters, product owners, stories, estimates, branches, pull requests and code reviews. Blame is gone too. They keep stand-ups, event storming, voluntary pair programming, Thursday office days and weekly Lean Coffee sessions. About half the team has built a personal IDE around running agent threads in parallel, and Hoogendoorn runs eight sessions at once. A strong foundation of identical service architecture, templated pipelines and automated infrastructure lets them go fast with AI. Team knowledge goes into Claude skills, including a \"pushback\" skill that stops the model from folding when told it is wrong.","Micro-teams form around one small item, ship it within the day and dissolve. With 170 repositories, trunk-based development, thousands of automated tests, AI-fixed Sonar issues and testing in production, the team deploys about 100 times a day. Following Deming, quality is built in rather than inspected afterwards, which makes code reviews redundant. Motivation follows Dan Pink's purpose, mastery and autonomy. The newest step is a sandbox called iBox, where business users build their own dashboards on limited APIs. He closes with Jason Gorman's point that AI gets you 80-90% of the way, so developers still need to be good swimmers."],"keyPoints":["Work is split 70/20/10: 70% toward the target architecture, 20% additions to existing systems, 10% small requests through a Slack channel.","Ideas are filtered by four questions: does it serve the goals, is it actionable, is it small enough, is it the most important thing right now.","Cynefin: most software work is complex, so it needs experiments that are allowed to fail, run in tiny steps.","No Scrum, sprints, estimates, product owners, branches, pull requests or code reviews. Quality comes from pairing, automation and trunk-based development across 170 repositories.","Around 100 production deploys a day with 13 people. The AI is being given the job of fixing pipeline failures and Sonar issues.","Half the team has built a personal IDE around parallel agent threads. Running many sessions leaves people \"totally cooked\" by the end of the day.","Team knowledge is encoded as Claude skills, including a custom pushback skill against the model's tendency to agree.","Developers become product engineers: prototypes in under an hour shorten feedback loops with the business, and business users get a sandbox (iBox) to build their own tools."],"takeaways":["Filter incoming work against explicit goals and admit that the \"someday maybe\" column is a bin.","Encode your architecture conventions and components as agent skills so generated code follows them.","Stop estimating story points and shorten your feedback cycle from sprints to days.","Invest in identical service templates and fully automated pipelines before trying to speed up with AI.","Try dropping one ceremony, such as the retrospective, and see whether anyone misses it."],"quotes":[{"text":"Simple, clear purpose and principles give rise to complex and intelligent behavior. Complex rules and regulations give rise to simple and stupid behavior.","at":"21:30"},{"text":"Missing the bus is not a big deal if you know there'll be another one along in five minutes.","at":"40:45"},{"text":"A code generation tool that gets you 80 to 90 percent of the way is like a boat that takes you 80-90% of the way. We still need to be very good swimmers.","at":"50:01"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Technical death at iBOOD"},{"at":"04:02","title":"AI and the scary new world"},{"at":"07:42","title":"Prioritisation and the 70/20/10 split"},{"at":"15:08","title":"Complexity and experiments"},{"at":"18:52","title":"Owning the work and autonomy"},{"at":"26:40","title":"What we stopped doing"},{"at":"28:00","title":"Personal IDEs and Claude skills"},{"at":"34:25","title":"Micro-teams and continuous delivery"},{"at":"42:08","title":"Automation instead of code review"},{"at":"46:08","title":"Fun, change and the ego system"}],"tech":["Claude Code","Claude Design","TypeScript","Jira","SonarQube","Trunk-based development","Cynefin framework","Lean Coffee","Event storming","Kubernetes"],"watchIf":"You lead a small product team and want a provocative, experience-based account of dropping Scrum and code reviews while scaling up AI-assisted development.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":14457,"title":"Coder, Coach, Catalyst - using questions to make people grow","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"People, Practices & Career","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["questions","growth","listening","coaching"],"abstract":["Asking the right questions is more powerful than having the right answers. As technologists, we quickly jump into solution mode and try to help by offering solutions and sometimes even taking on other people's problems as our own.","This is not our fault; we've been trained this way. However, computer problems and people's problems are often very different, and solving or taking on somebody else's problem not only burdens us but also robs them of an opportunity to grow.","If you feel like every problem in your organization or team constantly gravitates towards you, if you wonder why people just don't \"learn this stuff,\" or if you are looking to become better at helping others grow, this talk is for you!","In this talk, we will go over basic coaching skills to help you help others - everything from properly listening to asking the right questions. We'll cover different formats from impromptu coaching to more formal coaching and how to use coaching skills to coach up and sideways in your organization.","Come to this talk if you are interested in making the people around you grow and excel and, in the process, create more time to work on your problems, not theirs."],"favourites":410,"speakers":[{"name":"Martin Mazur","company":"Mazur & Co","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-b8a1d998-1999-4ed9-b607-cf9cef8e59e4.jpg","bio":"Martin is a technical advisor and leadership coach with over 20 years of experience in the field.<br><br>Martin&#39;s helps organizations bridge the communication gap between tech and business by guiding and inspiring people. Over his long tenure as a consultant, he has worked with organizations of different sizes and industries, taking on many different roles. This has given him a unique perspective on software, people, and organizations that he leverages today to help teams deliver products customers love.<br><br>Ultimately, Martin believes technology is a powerful tool and is passionate about wielding it in a way that positively impacts the world.","linkedin":"mazur","bluesky":"mazur.ai","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T08:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"yhk1qvy6A_4","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Mazur gives senior technologists a coaching starter kit (active listening, three coaching formats and five question types) to stop absorbing everyone's problems and help colleagues grow instead.","summary":["Mazur, a consultant and coach with over 20 years in software, starts from a pattern he sees in individual contributors around their tenth year: trained to produce correct answers, they take every problem left 'at their desk', become the team hero, and then burn out or turn into the snarky 'black hole' nobody brings work to. Typical advice ('just say no', 'delegate more', 'care less') does not help. His answer is basic coaching, defined via tennis coach Timothy Gallwey: point at where the error is rather than prescribing the fix, so the behaviour changes for the long term. You work on the person, not on the problem they brought.","The one skill everything rests on is active listening: no phone or laptop, eye contact, verbal cues, paraphrasing, and empathy as accepting that what you hear is the speaker's truth even when you disagree. He contrasts formal coaching (needs an agreement and follow-up), impromptu 5-15 minute coaching at the desk or coffee machine (the most useful kind), and team coaching (saves time but requires switching to facilitator or mediator). He then walks through a scripted conversation between tech lead Taylor and developer Sam, who is drowning in late requirement changes, showing each of the five question types in turn.","The same technique handles requests for advice. When Sam asks 'React or Vue?', Taylor asks which criteria matter, keeps asking 'and what else?', and only then adds the missing ones (scalability, licensing), leaving Sam with a reusable heuristic instead of an answer. Every answer you hand out ties a rubber band that pulls people back to you. Anyone can coach, even in domains they do not know; coaching up the hierarchy starts with a permission question. Mazur closes with four pitfalls, the first being psychological issues, where you stop coaching and help the person find professional support."],"keyPoints":["Solving other people's problems overloads you and robs them of the chance to learn: the 'you problem' and the 'them problem'.","Practise active listening with a pair exercise: person two may only ask 'why is that?', 'what was that about?' and 'how was that for you?'.","Initiation: 'What's on your mind?'. Avoid yes/no, status-check, emotionally loaded ('how are you?') or problem-prompting questions.","Excavation: 'And what else?', asked repeatedly; staying silent works even better because people fill the silence.","Focus: 'What is the real challenge here for you?'. Dropping 'for you' sends people into things outside their control.","Options: a set of questions such as what have you tried, what worked before, and what resources you have. Do not offer your own war stories, because they remove agency and accountability.","Action: 'What are you going to do about it?' or milder forms like 'What's the smallest next step?', plus 'When?' and an optional check-in later.","Pitfalls: psychological issues (refer on), the shoulder to cry on ('What do you need from me?'), gossip ('What did Ted say when you talked to them?') and blaming third parties (use the focus question)."],"takeaways":["Next time someone says 'can you help?', reply with 'What's on your mind?' and 'And what else?' before offering anything.","When asked for advice, ask for the person's decision criteria first and only fill in the gaps, so they leave with a reusable heuristic.","Let people run with their own solution unless it is truly dangerous, and follow up later at the coffee machine or on Slack.","Run the 'why is that?' listening exercise at your next team day."],"quotes":[{"text":"So what happens to overburdened heroes? They either die a hero or live long enough to see themselves being a villain.","at":"05:29"},{"text":"The best way of doing excavation is actually really hard, and it's to shut up.","at":"27:21"},{"text":"It's their problem, so it's their solution. You have to let it go unless they're going to set the building on fire.","at":"36:16"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"One skill, three formats, five questions"},{"at":"02:51","title":"From hero to villain"},{"at":"09:03","title":"What coaching means"},{"at":"12:54","title":"Active listening and empathy"},{"at":"18:18","title":"Formal, impromptu and team coaching"},{"at":"23:11","title":"The five questions in a scenario"},{"at":"38:33","title":"Coaching towards heuristics"},{"at":"42:37","title":"Coaching up with permission"},{"at":"45:12","title":"Four pitfalls"}],"tech":[],"watchIf":"You are a senior developer or tech lead who has become the person everyone brings their problems to, and you are running out of time.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":9029,"title":"From a startup stack to 5 microseconds: inside a crypto trading engine","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["deterministic","consensus","trading","latency"],"abstract":["What does it take to rebuild the core of a crypto exchange while it is already operating 24/7?","In 2018, Bitvavo's exchange ran on a familiar startup stack: Node.js, Go, Redis, MySQL, and Kafka. It helped the company move fast and grow, but as trading volume increased, the same architecture began to show its limits. Latency became harder to predict, shared infrastructure became a bottleneck, and proving correct order processing became just as important as making it fast.","This talk is the story of how we rebuilt the exchange core into a deterministic, replayable, low-latency trading platform. We moved away from database-centered processing and redesigned the system around an ordered log, a replicated state machine, Raft consensus, and a reliable UDP transport.","I'll share the engineering trade-offs behind that migration: where determinism helps, where it hurts, what changes when state lives in memory, and how far you can push the JVM when garbage collection, networking, and operating-system noise are all part of the latency budget.","The result is a production system with sub-millisecond end-to-end latency, around 5 microseconds of internal processing time, and capacity for 100,000 orders per second. But the real story is not just the numbers — it is what we had to unlearn, redesign, and operationalize to make a financial system both fast and correct."],"favourites":140,"speakers":[{"name":"Oleg Lobanov","company":"Bitvavo","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-cbda3b84-b3f9-4e69-9a55-69096722a354.jpg","bio":"Oleg Lobanov is a Staff Engineer at Bitvavo, where he works on the core trading platform behind roughly half of Europe&#39;s euro-denominated spot crypto volume. He brings two decades of software engineering experience — more than ten years of it building low-latency trading systems and high-scale e-commerce platforms. His focus is the JVM, distributed systems, and the kind of performance engineering where microseconds and determinism genuinely matter.","linkedin":"oleglobanov","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T15:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T16:30:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"vcL6eCAwLwY","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Lobanov explains how Bitvavo replaced a Redis/MySQL-centred exchange core with a deterministic, Raft-replicated state machine on Aeron, reaching flat sub-millisecond P99 and 5-microsecond order processing.","summary":["Lobanov, a staff engineer at Bitvavo, starts with the 2018 startup architecture: an API gateway, Redis Pub/Sub feeding matching engines, a shared MySQL database and Debezium CDC into Kafka for post-trade systems. It let the company grow, but it was not deterministic (heavily parallel Go code meant replaying the same input could produce different output), shared MySQL and Redis caused lock contention and noisy neighbours, and P99 latency swung between 5 and 200 ms. That unpredictability hurts market makers, whose pricing models look about 10 ms ahead. The goals for a rebuild were 10x throughput, low and stable tail latency, high availability and provable determinism.","The chosen pattern is a replicated state machine: a single ordered input log and a single-threaded function of (state, event) that emits state transitions, replicated with Raft. Lobanov walks through a Raft commit and then the discipline determinism demands: only the leader performs side effects such as bank API calls and writes the responses back into the log; never read the wall clock, only the timestamp the leader stamps on each message; seed randomness from the event sequence number; and sort before iterating a HashMap or HashSet. A bonus is debugging: copy the production log locally and replay it under a breakpoint.","JSON between services was replaced by SBE (Simple Binary Encoding: schema-first, fixed offsets, flyweight, zero-copy and zero-allocation), and TCP by reliable UDP with sequence numbers, NAK-based retransmission of single missing packets and heartbeats. Rather than spend an estimated three years building this, Bitvavo adopted Aeron, which provides SBE, reliable UDP and Aeron Cluster (Raft), with enterprise support. Gateways translate client JSON to SBE; archive tailers feed post-trade systems, regulatory reporting and adapters that still populate MySQL for the rest of the company.","Operating the cluster has costs: feature flags must be sent as input commands so that every node switches at the same log position; upgrades need replay-and-compare checks in CI, a production-like environment and divergence detection in production; and history cannot be rewritten, so bugs are fixed with compensating admin commands. Code-level rules: preallocate with pools and ring buffers, avoid strings and boxed types (map market names to integer IDs at the gateway), use primitive collections, follow the single-writer principle, avoid false sharing, pin and isolate CPUs, and consider kernel bypass (Solarflare, DPDK). The migration took about a year: P99 is now under 1 ms and flat, processing takes about 5 µs per order including risk and balance checks, and throughput rose from 20,000 to 100,000 orders per second."],"keyPoints":["The old stack (gateway, Redis Pub/Sub, matching engines, shared MySQL, Debezium to Kafka) had P99 latency of 5 to 200 ms and non-deterministic processing.","Core pattern: single ordered input log plus single-threaded state machine, replicated via Raft; events are only applied after a majority commit.","Determinism rules: side effects only on the leader with results fed back into the log, leader-stamped timestamps instead of the wall clock, randomness seeded from the sequence number, sorted iteration over hash collections.","SBE replaced JSON to remove parsing garbage; reliable UDP with NAKs and heartbeats replaced TCP; Aeron supplies both plus Raft-based Aeron Cluster.","Feature flags are sent as cluster input commands so every node changes behaviour at the same log position.","Production bugs are corrected with compensating commands, never by changing code and replaying, because downstream systems and regulators have already consumed the output.","Results after a year-long migration: flat sub-millisecond P99, about 5 µs internal processing per order, 20,000 to 100,000 orders per second, with headroom beyond that."],"takeaways":["If you need replayable, auditable processing, consider a single-threaded state machine over an ordered log instead of database-centred concurrency.","Audit deterministic code for wall-clock reads, unseeded randomness and iteration over HashMap or HashSet.","Add a CI check that replays a recorded input log on the old and new version and compares state and output.","On hot paths, preallocate objects, replace strings with integer IDs and use primitive collections to keep GC out of your tail latency."],"quotes":[{"text":"We don't need reliable connectivity, we need a reliable stream.","at":"23:27"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Bitvavo and its scale"},{"at":"04:51","title":"The 2018 startup stack"},{"at":"10:34","title":"Replicated state machine and Raft"},{"at":"15:51","title":"Rules for determinism"},{"at":"19:59","title":"SBE instead of JSON"},{"at":"22:25","title":"Reliable UDP and Aeron"},{"at":"30:48","title":"Operating the cluster"},{"at":"36:09","title":"Low-latency coding checklist"},{"at":"42:52","title":"Results"}],"tech":["Aeron","Aeron Cluster","Raft","SBE (Simple Binary Encoding)","UDP","Redis","MySQL","Kafka","Debezium","Node.js","Go","Solarflare","DPDK"],"watchIf":"You build latency-sensitive or audit-heavy systems and want a concrete account of event-sourced, deterministic design on the JVM, including its operational costs.","themes":["Architecture & design","JVM performance & runtime","Legacy modernization"],"demo":false}},{"id":17783,"title":"Building the Trusted Agent Ecosystem","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["agentic","trusted","binarytransparency","confidentialcomputing"],"abstract":["The industry is rapidly introducing agentic services that are accelerating the arrival of the \"Agentic Web\". Yet adoption is still bottlenecked by the lack of user trust. Users are understandably afraid to grant remote AI agents access to their private data or authorization tokens. To overcome this, we need robust solutions that empower all developers to create agents that users can trust.","In this session, we introduce a comprehensive framework for building the Trusted Agent Ecosystem. By leveraging Confidential Computing and Binary Transparency, we provide verifiable trust across the entire AI stack: securing MCP tools, model inference and remote agents connected via A2A. We will present our open-source implementation of the framework that leverages verifiable WebAssembly sandboxes and Confidential GPUs to build trusted agentic services."],"favourites":87,"speakers":[{"name":"Ivan Petrov","company":"Google DeepMind","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-8874cc9a-8b3f-4c89-98ec-b05f6ea7c89e.jpg","bio":"Ivan Petrov is a Senior Software Engineer at Google DeepMind who is working on security and privacy for the agentic ecosystem. He also holds a PhD from Moscow State University, where he focused on intrusion detection systems and software-defined networking.","linkedin":"ipetr0v","bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Patrick McGrath","company":"Google DeepMind","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-cbc5a5a7-dc25-4e46-98e8-4721c606dc4f.jpg","bio":"Patrick McGrath is a Senior Software Engineer at Google DeepMind specializing in agentic security and privacy for next-generation AI systems. With a strong foundation in server-side architecture, he has engineered critical infrastructure for high-scale platforms, including Private AI Compute and Google Maps Location services. Over the past five years at Google, his work has focused on building provably private solutions that safeguard user data at a global scale.","linkedin":"mcgrath-patrick","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-05T16:20:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T16:50:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"dWF5HMkKiLg","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":23531,"title":"We created our own AI orchestrator and it became so much more!","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["agentic","requirements","orchestration","automation"],"abstract":["Agentic AI is something everyone wants to use or sell. We wanted an overview of the work and active agents and therefore we created an orchestration tool. Using the Agent, Skills and Spec files from default locations so its interchangeable with other tools. And it really started to change the way we worked on software projects.","As a Java software Engineer it could be seen as a automation of tasks, as an Scrum Master/Product Owner a way to coordinate and for the business analyst a way to store functional requirements. We've used Embabel Agent as the fundamental layer. On top of that we've created a visual representation so we can have a website open in our office which shows which agent is active, what it's doing and what's the next task.","For Software Development teams this will be the biggest change, developers are more reviewing and delivering feedback to the AI while business analysts had a field day of delivering the requirements for the spec files which were reviewed by an AI agent before they could be assigned to an AI-agent. The product owner is in control of the queue where sprints are no longer weeks but days.","I want to share how we created this orchestrator and explain on which impact each technical step had on the way-of-work in our team. It wasn't just about creating an application anymore, everything had impact on how we work."],"favourites":284,"speakers":[{"name":"Ricky van Rijn","company":"MetaFactory","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-5361a8d1-ea08-4d32-8575-5dbaae4cb186.jpg","bio":"CTO and Software Architect with over 10 years of hands-on java development experience. He is passionate about Software Development, Artificial Intelligence (AI), Robotics, IoT, Cybersecurity, Open Source Software and loves tech gadgets. Ricky is a speaker on conferences like Code&amp;Comedy, DevSecCon, JFall, Devoxx UK and has a background as a lecturer on an University of Applied sciences. Besides all that Ricky is a Fitness and Martial Arts instructor and enthusiast. Current focus is forming and testing new AI best practices. ","linkedin":"rickyvanrijn","bluesky":null,"twitter":"RickyvanRijn"}],"start":"2026-10-09T09:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T10:40:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"Ricky van Rijn describes a team-built AI orchestrator on Embabel Agent and how each step changed the team's way of working, from specs to sprints lasting days.","summary":["MetaFactory built an orchestration tool to get an overview of active agents and their work. It reads agent, skill and spec files from default locations so it stays interchangeable with other tools, uses Embabel Agent as its base layer, and adds a visual dashboard showing which agent is active, what it is doing and the next task.","The session sets out to explain how the orchestrator was built and what each technical step did to the team's way of working. Developers move toward reviewing and giving feedback to AI, business analysts write requirements as spec files that an AI agent reviews before assignment, and the product owner controls the queue, with sprints shortening from weeks to days."],"keyPoints":["Orchestrator uses Embabel Agent as its foundation.","Reads agent, skill and spec files from default locations for tool interchangeability.","Visual dashboard shows active agent, current work and next task.","Roles shift: developers review, analysts write specs, product owners manage the queue."],"takeaways":["Store functional requirements as spec files that an agent can review before work is assigned."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Embabel Agent","Agent skills","Spec files"],"watchIf":"You are working out how agentic tooling changes team roles and sprint planning.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":24244,"title":"Green Maven builds: sustainable CI in practice","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Development Practices","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["maven","energy","githubactions","ci"],"abstract":["Whether you want to reduce your carbon footprint or cut back on machine resource usage, energy-efficient builds matter more than ever. CI systems often waste energy and time because of inefficient build pipelines. In this lunch session I will demonstrate a few tricks that will give you greener and faster Maven builds. We’ll look at practical examples using GitLab CI and GitHub Actions, so you can apply the techniques right away in your own projects."],"favourites":173,"speakers":[{"name":"Jan Boonen","company":"the/experts.","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-83370e03-af8c-4887-818a-857c2813597a.jpg","bio":"Jan is an experienced solution architect with over 15 years of experience as Kotlin/Java developer. He loves to combine architecture and coding as this is the sweet spot to invent clever, solutions that have an impact in society.<br><br>With a MSc. in Geophysics he has a natural interest in geospatial technology, climate change and maps. He has mainly leveraged these for the public interest in assignments for Dutch government agencies.<br><br>In his spare time he practices photography with a focus on street and documentary photography. Living in a university town, he is engaged in a project that supports students and young professionals in the area of well-being and meaning.","linkedin":"janboonen","bluesky":"janboonen.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T10:55:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T11:35:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"Mdu5_gndYn0","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Boonen shows low-effort Maven and GitLab CI settings (parallel JUnit, quieter logs, no recompilation between jobs, workflow rules) that make builds faster and leaner, and measures them with Eco CI.","summary":["Boonen got into green software after his daughters asked what he was doing about the carbon burned in data centers at work. Of the three areas where software causes emissions (hardware production, energy used while building, hosting), he focuses on the build. On hardware he gives one tip: run pipelines on older, reused machines, either decommissioned in-house servers or the second-life servers a provider such as Hetzner offers. His Maven tips start with JUnit 5 parallel execution: five lines in junit-platform.properties cut a demo suite from just over six seconds to about three. Spring Boot integration tests with shared contexts may need annotations that keep them on one thread.","Next he cuts log noise. Simple-logger settings in .mvn/jvm.config add a timestamp, hide thread names and show only errors, which he says saves 10 to 20 seconds on large projects. The bigger waste is recompilation. Each CI job checks out the code again, so sources passed along as artifacts look newer than the compiled classes and Maven recompiles and retests everything. Setting lastModGranularityMs gives a grace period, which is safe because every job builds the same commit. He sets this in MAVEN_CLI_OPTS with skipped source and install plugins, quiet mode and --no-transfer-progress, plus -XX:TieredStopAtLevel=1 in MAVEN_OPTS.","His pipeline principles are to fail fast but report all test failures, and to do only the work needed. The GitLab demo compiles once, passes target/classes via artifacts and needs, runs unit and integration tests in parallel jobs, and merges the Surefire, Failsafe and JaCoCo outputs into one coverage job, with separate paths so one report does not overwrite another. Workflow rules skip pipelines for pushes to feature branches without a merge request, and job rules keep Sonar analysis and packaging off triggers where they add nothing. He measures with Eco CI, added through a hidden base job that reports per-job watt-hours, and suggests Kepler for self-hosted Kubernetes runners."],"keyPoints":["Setting parallel execution to true and the mode to concurrent in junit-platform.properties halved a demo test suite (about 6 s to 3 s); shared Spring contexts may need to be kept on one thread.","Simple-logger properties in .mvn/jvm.config (timestamp on, thread name off, errors only) remove Maven info logs nobody reads in green pipelines.","Passing compiled classes between CI jobs does not help on its own: a fresh checkout makes sources newer than classes, so Maven recompiles and reruns tests unless lastModGranularityMs gives a grace period.","Pipeline-wide MAVEN_CLI_OPTS can skip the source and install plugins and add quiet mode and --no-transfer-progress; MAVEN_OPTS can stop tiered compilation at level 1 for short-lived builds.","In GitLab, artifacts plus needs share outputs between jobs, and workflow rules can skip pipelines on feature branches until a merge request exists.","Artifacts built on main for a commit can be fetched by commit ID through the GitLab API when a release tag on the same commit starts a new pipeline; on GitHub this takes API scripting with curl.","Eco CI estimates energy per job from inside the pipeline and can use Electricity Maps carbon intensity; Kepler measures node energy on Kubernetes runners you host yourself."],"takeaways":["Add a junit-platform.properties with concurrent parallel execution and fix any tests that break.","Set lastModGranularityMs in your CI Maven options so later jobs reuse the compiled classes instead of recompiling.","Add GitLab workflow rules so pipelines run only for merge requests, the default branch and tags.","Extend your jobs from an Eco CI base job to get a per-job energy figure before and after each change."],"quotes":[{"text":"Making your pipelines greener is not very difficult. Getting green results is.","at":"38:40"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why green pipelines"},{"at":"04:45","title":"Where emissions come from"},{"at":"07:16","title":"Parallel JUnit tests"},{"at":"10:44","title":"Less Maven logging"},{"at":"14:10","title":"Preventing recompilation"},{"at":"18:18","title":"Pipeline design principles"},{"at":"21:29","title":"GitLab artifacts and needs"},{"at":"26:36","title":"Rules and workflows"},{"at":"30:54","title":"Measuring carbon footprint"},{"at":"38:40","title":"Conclusions"}],"tech":["Apache Maven","JUnit 5","GitLab CI","GitHub Actions","Spring Boot","Surefire","Failsafe","JaCoCo","SonarQube","Eco CI","Electricity Maps","Kepler","Kotlin"],"watchIf":"You maintain Maven builds on GitLab or GitHub CI and want concrete settings that cut build time and energy without a rewrite.","themes":["Sustainability","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":17782,"title":"Is the IDE dead ?","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["ai","terminal","ide","agents"],"abstract":["AI has fundamentally changed software development. But in a world of autonomous coding agents and AI-assisted creation, do developers even need a traditional IDE anymore? Is the terminal becoming our primary home once again, or are we moving toward something entirely different?","In this session, Martin takes the audience on a journey through the history of developer tools to the present AI inflection point. He will:","Reflect on what human engineers actually need from their environment in 2026.","Analyze whether the IDE is evolving or being replaced by terminal-based workflows like Claude Code.","Discuss the evolving role of AI agents as the new \"power users\" of development tools.","Summarize the implications for both toolsmiths and software engineers in the years to come."],"favourites":432,"speakers":[{"name":"Martin Lippert","company":"Broadcom","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-a43eb907-1696-440a-8220-c6e0e225f9e3.jpg","bio":"Martin works at Broadcom on developer tooling for Spring and Spring Boot. He leads the team that ships all the Spring Tools for Visual Studio Code, Cursor, Claude Code, Eclipse, Theia, Codespaces, and more.","linkedin":"martin-lippert-9a1909","bluesky":"martinlippert.bsky.social","twitter":"martinlippert"}],"start":"2026-10-08T10:55:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T11:35:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"jFxR6YYWdmI","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Lippert argues the IDE is not dying but splitting into three tools: review tools for high-volume agent changes, managers for fleets of agents, and headless language smartness serving agents.","summary":["Martin Lippert, who builds the Spring Tools extensions, traces the IDE back to the Smalltalk-80 system browser and names its core principles: language and framework smartness, fast feedback without running a compiler, keeping the developer in flow through completion, hovers and refactoring, source-level debugging, and integration of tests, Git and builds in one place. Agentic coding has pushed many developers back to the terminal with tools like Claude Code, which itself is gaining IDE-like diff panels. Developers now juggle parallel agent sessions and spend their effort on harnesses and guardrails. Lippert puts himself about two-thirds of the way from IDE to agent.","He predicts the IDE will split three ways. First, review tools: reading diffs of thousands of agent-written lines makes the human the bottleneck, so reviewers need framework-aware validation, navigation and higher-level views. He demos a Spring Tools logical structure view in VS Code that shows only changes, such as new request mappings, query methods or entities, while an agent works. Second, tools to manage fleets of agents, such as JetBrains Air and the VS Code agents view. Third, IDE language smartness exposed to agents through the Language Server Protocol and debugger integrations, so the agent becomes the toolsmith's new user."],"keyPoints":["The IDE was built on fast feedback and keeping the developer in flow; those principles now need to apply to agents and to reviewers rather than to people typing code.","Reviewing agent output line by line through diffs does not scale; Lippert wants review tools that show changes at the level of framework concepts and architecture.","Spring Tools is experimenting with a 'changes only' logical structure view that surfaces new or modified Spring stereotypes (controllers, repositories, entities) as an agent edits files.","Language servers built for VS Code are being plugged into Claude Code and other agents so they get compile errors and references in milliseconds, use fewer tokens, and get version-correct Spring guidance; Lippert calls this still a research area.","Agents increasingly drive source-level debuggers directly and even write and run their own throwaway tools, a kind of personal software for the agent."],"takeaways":["Connect a language server plugin for your stack to your coding agent so it gets fast compile and navigation feedback instead of guessing.","Look for or build review views that summarise agent changes at the framework or architecture level before you read the diff.","Decide per project how much human review the risk warrants rather than applying one policy everywhere."],"quotes":[{"text":"It's not you anymore. I'm sorry. It's the agent to help the agent.","at":"30:09"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Who still uses an IDE"},{"at":"02:55","title":"From Smalltalk to modern IDEs"},{"at":"09:54","title":"Back to the terminal"},{"at":"13:49","title":"The IDE splits in three"},{"at":"15:12","title":"Tools for reviewing agent code"},{"at":"25:08","title":"Managing fleets of agents"},{"at":"26:13","title":"Language smartness for agents"}],"tech":["Spring Tools","VS Code","Eclipse","IntelliJ IDEA","Claude Code","Language Server Protocol","JetBrains Air","GitHub Copilot","Smalltalk-80"],"watchIf":"You wonder where your IDE fits once agents write most of the code, or you build developer tooling and want a toolsmith's view of the shift.","themes":["Developer experience & tooling","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":22905,"title":"When ORM Becomes OMG: Performance Pitfalls in JPA and Friends","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["jpa","performance","orm","transactions"],"abstract":["JPA makes database interaction feel almost magical—until your app grinds to a halt. In this talk, we’ll take a tour through the common (and not-so-common) performance pitfalls that turn object-relational mapping into an object-relational mess. From data loading surprises to connection mismanagement, we’ll explore real-world issues using a sample app: the FunFactStore, where fun facts are sold, but bugs are free.","We’ll dig into how seemingly harmless libraries can sometimes do more harm than good, and how tooling can help you spot trouble before your users do. Whether you’re using Spring Data JPA, navigating tricky transaction boundaries, or just wondering why things suddenly got slow, this session offers practical insights—and maybe a few fun facts."],"favourites":268,"speakers":[{"name":"Jos Roseboom","company":"EasingYou","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-bc6b28d6-203a-4331-8206-f63ac80f7245.jpg","bio":"Jos gets a smile on his face when he makes other people happy by letting a computer do what they don’t like to do. He has been trying to do so professionally for the past 17 years.<br><br>In his spare time, Jos likes to play tennis. He is a trainer/coach for kids playing the typical Dutch sport korfball.","linkedin":"jos-roseboom-75508b11","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T08:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"ePBmAdRYl10","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Using a toy 'Fun Fact Store', Roseboom shows how Spring Boot and Hibernate defaults hold pooled connections too long and fire hidden queries, and which settings and query styles fix it.","summary":["Roseboom, a Dutch freelance developer, builds the talk around his Fun Fact Store, a Spring Boot, Spring Data JPA and Postgres app. He first measures why pooling matters: a Testcontainers test making 1,000 connections took under a millisecond via HikariCP and over four seconds via DriverManager. Because the pool is fixed-size (10 by default), the goal is to hold a connection as briefly as possible. He adds a Hikari metrics tracker factory that logs when connections are acquired and returned, which shows a shop endpoint holding one for 659 ms, including an email call to an external system.","The fixes are mostly configuration. Turning off open-session-in-view returns the connection when the transactional method ends instead of after the controller (251 ms). Hibernate grabs the connection at the start of a transaction only to set autocommit to false; setting Hikari autocommit to false and telling Hibernate the provider disables autocommit (automatic since Spring Boot 3.2) delays acquisition, and Spring Boot 4.1 adds a lazy connection-fetch option that defers setAutoCommit and setReadOnly until real JDBC work. Marking a method read-only causes the same early fetch. A TransactionTemplate keeps external calls outside transactions, bringing the total to 26 ms over two short leases.","The JPA half covers mapping. @ManyToOne and owning-side @OneToOne default to eager fetching, so a findAll quietly loaded app users; the persistence context deduplicated them and provides repeatable reads even under read-committed. Switching to lazy exposes N+1 queries, fixed with JOIN FETCH. Dirty checking makes explicit saveAll calls unnecessary. For reads he prefers DTO projections via JPQL constructor expressions (checked at startup), native queries mapped to records in Hibernate 6+, or interface projections, over entity-plus-MapStruct mapping, which once triggered over a thousand selects on a single screen for him. He closes with IntelliJ's upcoming Hibernate debugger, Vlad Mihalcea's Hypersistence Optimizer and Gatling for load tests."],"keyPoints":["In his benchmark, 1,000 connections from HikariCP took under 1 ms versus over 4 s through DriverManager.","A custom Hikari MetricsTrackerFactory logging acquire/return times makes connection lease duration visible.","spring.jpa.open-in-view is on by default and keeps the connection until the web request finishes; set it to false.","Hibernate acquires the connection early just to disable autocommit; disable autocommit in Hikari plus Hibernate's provider-disables-autocommit setting (default since Spring Boot 3.2), or use Spring Boot 4.1's lazy connection fetching.","Use TransactionTemplate.execute to wrap just the database part and keep slow external calls outside the transaction.","@ManyToOne and @OneToOne default to EAGER; make them LAZY and use JOIN FETCH where the relation is needed to avoid N+1.","For read-only screens, DTO projections (JPQL constructor expressions, native queries in Hibernate 6+, interface projections) issue one narrow query and are validated at startup.","Roseboom maps many-to-many relations as an explicit join entity (e.g. Purchase with a purchase date) instead of @ManyToMany."],"takeaways":["Set spring.jpa.open-in-view=false in every Spring Boot app and check what breaks.","Log HikariCP connection acquire and release times to find endpoints that hold connections during external calls.","Declare fetch = FetchType.LAZY on every new @ManyToOne and use JOIN FETCH where needed.","Use DTO projections instead of entities for read-only queries.","Load-test critical flows with Gatling to find where the connection pool saturates."],"quotes":[{"text":"So first thing to do when you create a new Spring Boot application is to set this to false.","at":"10:45"},{"text":"I've been in situations where there was just a simple screen triggering over a thousand selects because it was all just traveling the object tree.","at":"42:49"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"The Fun Fact Store"},{"at":"03:10","title":"Why connection pools matter"},{"at":"06:53","title":"Making connection leases visible"},{"at":"09:20","title":"Open session in view"},{"at":"12:20","title":"Autocommit and lazy acquisition"},{"at":"17:27","title":"TransactionTemplate for external calls"},{"at":"22:08","title":"JPA mapping and eager fetching"},{"at":"32:44","title":"N+1 and join fetch"},{"at":"36:12","title":"DTO projections"},{"at":"45:33","title":"Tooling"}],"tech":["Spring Boot","Spring Data JPA","Hibernate","Jakarta Persistence","HikariCP","PostgreSQL","Testcontainers","Thymeleaf","MapStruct","IntelliJ IDEA","Hypersistence Optimizer","Gatling"],"watchIf":"You run Spring Data JPA in production and see connection pool exhaustion, N+1 queries or unexplained latency.","themes":["Data & databases","Spring & frameworks","JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":true}},{"id":7006,"title":"Prompt It Right: Spring Boot Testing in the AI Era","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Development Practices","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["testcontainers","ai","springboot","testing"],"abstract":["AI now writes most of your test code, and that changes the skill you need. The bottleneck is no longer typing tests by hand; it's knowing what good tests look like, where each kind belongs, and how to prompt AI to produce them. Without that understanding, you get tests that pass but verify nothing.","This 3-hour session gives you the full picture of testing Spring Boot applications, so you can direct AI with confidence instead of trusting it blindly.","We'll cover the complete testing pyramid: fast unit tests, sliced tests that load only the part of the context you need, and integration tests that exercise the real thing with Testcontainers. For each level, you'll see when to reach for it, what it actually proves, and how to prompt AI to write it well.","Along the way you'll collect practical patterns and recipes you can apply directly to your own codebase, plus the common pitfalls that produce slow, flaky, or misleading tests. We'll close with quick wins that improve almost any Spring Boot test suite.","Whether you're newer to Spring Boot testing or want to sharpen how you collaborate with AI tools, you'll leave knowing not just how to write tests, but how to judge whether the ones AI hands you are worth keeping."],"favourites":168,"speakers":[{"name":"Philip Riecks","company":"PragmaTech GmbH","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-bc0996c3-73b4-4bc5-bd2e-3890875cb5d3.jpg","bio":"Philip helps developers ship software more often and sleep better at night, pairing actionable technical content with hands-on consulting.<br><br>When he&#39;s not writing or consulting, he&#39;s an indie hacker chasing his next side project, a frequent Southeast Asia traveler, a committed squash player, a hopeful padel newbie, and a fan of jogging in nature.","linkedin":"rieckpil","bluesky":null,"twitter":"rieckpil"}],"start":"2026-10-05T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T10:30:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"d6D8nef-32c","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Riecks argues that with AI writing tests, verification is the bottleneck: know unit, slice and integration tests, keep the suite fast, and encode testing standards in agent skills.","summary":["Riecks opens with a north star instead of a coverage target: a Renovate pull request bumping Spring Boot to a new major version arrives on Friday afternoon, CI is green, and the team is confident enough to merge and deploy. Since code is now cheap to generate, the bottleneck is verification, and a fast, comprehensive test suite is what makes AI output safe to accept. He cites the DORA finding that AI amplifies a team's existing practices, good or bad, and warns that tests often written last and chased for coverage numbers will not improve just because an agent writes them.","The first block reviews the fundamentals a reviewer needs to judge AI-written tests. His rule: a test without a Spring context is a unit test (suffix Test, run by Surefire), anything with a context is an integration test (suffix IT, run by Failsafe), so they can be parallelised differently. He shows how a unit test of a REST controller stays green while missing the request mapping, Bean Validation, Jackson serialization and Spring Security. @WebMvcTest fixes that with a sliced context and MockMvc. He explains the 'shopping list' of beans in WebMvcTypeExcludeFilter, plus @DataJpaTest, @JsonTest, @RestClientTest and Spring Cloud AWS's @SqsTest.","For full @SpringBootTest he contrasts the default MOCK web environment (MockMvc, same thread, so @WithMockUser tricks work) with RANDOM_PORT (real Tomcat, real HTTP, real tokens required), and uses Testcontainers with pinned image versions for infrastructure. Context caching gets the most time: Spring Test keeps contexts in a LinkedHashMap of size 32 keyed by MergedContextConfiguration, so any difference in profiles, properties or @MockitoBean usage forces a new context. Optimising this alone cut one client's Maven build from 26 to 12 minutes. He shows his open-source Spring Test Profiler for reporting reuse, warns against @DirtiesContext, and introduces mutation testing with PIT.","The second block covers agentic practice. Riecks encodes his testing standards as Claude Code skills (the same idea works in Copilot, Junie, Codex and Cursor): a SKILL.md with front matter, a references file of numbered standards with severities, and an examples.md of good and bad assertions, adapted per project through an onboarding prompt. CI fails when the profiler's JSON reports too many contexts, and Docker events count container starts. On TDD with agents he quotes Kent Beck, Simon Willison and Birgitta Böckeler without taking a firm side. His MCP servers are Context7, GitHub and Playwright. He closes with feature flags, runbooks linked from alerts and fast rollback."],"keyPoints":["A unit test calling a controller method directly cannot catch broken request mappings, missing Bean Validation, changed JSON serialization or missing Spring Security rules; @WebMvcTest with MockMvc can.","Sliced contexts load only the beans on a fixed list (for the web layer, defined in WebMvcTypeExcludeFilter); anything else, such as the service a controller injects, must be supplied, usually as a @MockitoBean.","@SpringBootTest defaults to a mock servlet environment with MockMvc; only webEnvironment RANDOM_PORT starts Tomcat, and then security context overrides no longer work because test and app run on different threads.","Spring Test caches up to 32 application contexts keyed by MergedContextConfiguration; differing profiles, properties or mocked beans each create a new context. Riecks reported cutting a client build from 26 to 12 minutes this way.","Testcontainers tips: singleton containers instead of @Testcontainers lifecycle, pinned image tags, the same database as production instead of H2, withReuse(true) locally, and optionally a nightly prebuilt Postgres image with schema and sample data.","Parallelise unit tests first with JUnit Jupiter's thread pool, which shares the context cache, unlike forked JVMs; for integration tests randomise business keys, scope WireMock stubs narrowly and opt individual classes out of parallel runs.","Testing standards live in checked-in skills: SKILL.md front matter controls lazy loading, a references file lists rules with IDs and severities (e.g. a failure message on every assertion), and examples.md shows good versus bad code.","Introduce PIT mutation testing incrementally on changed code using its history file, cached between CI runs, rather than over a whole legacy codebase."],"takeaways":["Inspect your test suite's context reuse with Spring's cache logging or Spring Test Profiler, consolidate on one abstract integration test base class, and remove @DirtiesContext where it is not needed.","Add a CI step that fails when the number of Spring contexts or started Docker containers exceeds a threshold, so agents cannot silently add new ones.","Write your team's unit, slice and integration testing rules into a checked-in agent skill with numbered rules and good/bad examples.","Cover each REST endpoint with a @WebMvcTest that asserts every response field, validation errors and blocked anonymous access.","Inject a Clock instead of calling Instant.now() so AI-generated tests stay deterministic."],"quotes":[{"text":"We can generate code on scale, but trust and confidence we can't prompt.","at":"84:10"},{"text":"If the road is bumpy, a faster car just means you hit the wall sooner than before.","at":"89:26"},{"text":"With the AI you can delegate the typing. What you can't delegate is the ownership.","at":"149:53"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why test in the AI era"},{"at":"16:27","title":"Toolbox and test types"},{"at":"28:37","title":"Limits of unit tests"},{"at":"34:33","title":"Slice testing with MockMvc"},{"at":"48:46","title":"Integration tests and Testcontainers"},{"at":"61:04","title":"Context caching"},{"at":"73:57","title":"Mutation testing and Q&A"},{"at":"84:10","title":"Verification is the bottleneck"},{"at":"99:52","title":"Testing standards as skills"},{"at":"117:10","title":"Parallelization, containers, MCP servers"}],"tech":["Spring Boot","spring-boot-starter-test","JUnit 5","Mockito","AssertJ","Awaitility","MockMvc","@WebMvcTest","@DataJpaTest","@SpringBootTest","Testcontainers","WireMock","PIT","Spring Test Profiler","Claude Code skills","Context7","GitHub MCP","Playwright MCP","Maven Surefire/Failsafe"],"watchIf":"You let an AI agent write Spring Boot tests and want to judge whether they prove anything, or your CI build is slow because of Spring context restarts.","themes":["Testing & quality","Spring & frameworks","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":5549,"title":"Underneath the Black Marker: AI Techniques for Detecting and Reversing Redactions","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["computervision","pdf","privacy","redaction"],"abstract":["Redaction is often treated like a final step. Add black boxes, blur a name, pixelate a screenshot, export the file, done. But recent headlines have reminded everyone that \"hidden\" is not the same as \"gone.\" In practice, many redactions fail in predictable ways. Sometimes the underlying text remains in the PDF. Sometimes image-based redactions leave enough signal behind to detect what was covered. And sometimes the surrounding sentence context gives away exactly what was meant to stay private. Recent media coverage around heavily redacted files has exposed a hard truth: concealment is not the same thing as data removal.","This session is a live, technical walk-through of how modern redaction failures happen and how AI can expose them. We will demo detection methods for finding redacted text in documents, show how poorly redacted PDFs can be recovered, and end with examples of recovering information from redacted or pixelated images. More importantly, we will also cover what proper redaction looks like in practice. Along the way, we will connect document analysis, computer vision, and language models to a simple point: privacy controls fail when teams confuse visual hiding with real removal. Bring your curiosity and maybe a little paranoia."],"favourites":64,"speakers":[{"name":"David vonThenen","company":"NetApp","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-d587c9f9-77e0-493c-a194-848fe54258c3.jpg","bio":"David vonThenen is an AI/ML Engineer where he focuses on production AI systems, enterprise AI strategy, and architectures for explainable, governable, and reliable generative AI. His work spans Agentic AI, Graph RAG, Document RAG, AI memory systems, multi-agent architectures, OpenSearch, Neo4j, data lineage, provenance, and AI governance.<br><br>David has more than 20 years of experience building production software across AI/ML, speech and NLP, Kubernetes, cloud-native platforms, storage, virtualization, and backup/recovery. He combines hands-on engineering with technical strategy, open-source leadership, developer advocacy, and customer-facing architecture work.","linkedin":"davidvonthenen","bluesky":"davidvonthenen.com","twitter":"davidvonthenen"}],"start":"2026-10-08T13:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T13:50:00Z","room":"Room 10","videoId":"wJ7R4qkVs8Y","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":21152,"title":"Beyond system.out.println: Applying a Debugging Mindset at Scale","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Development Practices","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["observability","debugging","grafana","opentelemetry"],"abstract":["Debugging a consistently failing unit test is easy; you set a breakpoint and fix it. But how do you debug a \"random\" failure in a massive microservices landscape or a distributed monolith?","During an interview, I was once told, \"You are more of a debugger than a coder.\" They were right: I thrive on the bugs that make others give up: the nasty, intermittent issues hidden under the carpet.","But you cannot solve these issues with 'System.out.println()' or a simple debugger attached to localhost.","Debugging at scale requires a shift in mindset and tooling. It requires moving from the paradigm of \"5 Whys\" and 'logger.info' to a structured observational approach based on observability data.","Based on production outages, I'll share how I uncovered the problems, the lessons I learned and how you can combine a debugging mindset with observability tooling like OpenTelemetry and the Grafana toolset to gain deeper insights."],"favourites":141,"speakers":[{"name":"Cees Bos","company":"OpenValue","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-14d6453f-7d33-4f7a-86e4-3568aac637c9.jpg","bio":"Observability enthusiast and Grafana Champion, always looking for ways to improve software with better observability and what new insights can be gained. Delivering reliable software is always my priority. Combining existing tools often results in new ways to get an even better view. I work for OpenValue as a software &amp; observability engineer and SRE.","linkedin":"ceesbos","bluesky":"ceesbos.nl","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-06T10:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T11:20:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"5VjB4t8aFWE","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Bos uses three real production incidents (a saturated load balancer, a slow ingress, a JMS server freezing on LDAP timeouts) to show how curiosity, data and proved hypotheses find bugs nobody owns.","summary":["Bos, an observability engineer at OpenValue and Grafana Champion, tells three incident stories. On a Dutch government open-data maps platform (GeoServer, GeoWebCache on NFS, Apache, vendor-run load balancers across data centres) the only metric was a five-minute CPU and memory average. He added Jolokia for JMX over HTTP and Telegraf feeding InfluxDB and Grafana. During a multi-day outage this showed Tomcat stuck at its 200-thread limit and Apache at 256 workers. Telegraf ICMP pings revealed latency and packet loss only through the load balancer, which the vendor eventually replaced. That also fixed other teams' 'random' failures. Custom file I/O metrics added to GeoWebCache then pinned the remaining slowness on NFS. On Kubernetes, gaps in distributed traces across many services turned out to be an overloaded ingress, and twice an egress was behind a war-room incident.","The third story was an outage across many services that all used JMS. Bos took a gigabyte of logs from the LDAP authentication server, loaded them into Grafana Loki and showed query times rising during each incident. When the team blamed noisy neighbours, a colleague answered 'assume it's you, prove it's not you'. Decompiling the JMS server showed how it used its LDAP connection. A lab test combining a connect-per-message producer with long-lived connections reproduced the bug: one failed authentication froze every connection, and the vendor shipped several hotfixes. His method: know the surrounding environment and how it works under the hood, keep asking questions, raise detailed tickets with other teams, form hypotheses and prove them, draw component diagrams and incident timelines, share the analysis on a page, and fill observability gaps with OpenTelemetry. An AI agent needs the same data."],"keyPoints":["Simple Telegraf ICMP checks against the load balancer and individual services isolated a faulty load balancer behind months of 'random' failing requests.","Hidden platform components such as Kubernetes ingress and egress often explain trace gaps and outbound connection failures; involve the platform team.","Parsing a gigabyte of LDAP logs in Grafana Loki turned an argument about cause and effect into a visible correlation.","A lab reproduction combining two client patterns, which each worked alone, exposed a vendor JMS bug in which one failed authentication froze all connections.","Treat explanations like noisy neighbours as hypotheses to disprove with data, not as conclusions."],"takeaways":["Map every component a request touches, including load balancers, ingress, egress and auth servers, and learn how they behave under the hood.","When you suspect another team's component, file a ticket with your observations and data instead of letting the issue go.","Build an incident timeline and share the analysis on a page others can add to.","Instrument your services with OpenTelemetry metrics, logs and traces before the next outage."],"quotes":[{"text":"Assume it's you. Prove it's not you.","at":"22:06"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"From IE6 to production debugging"},{"at":"02:58","title":"Open data maps platform"},{"at":"08:10","title":"Pinging the load balancer"},{"at":"12:02","title":"NFS and the first lessons"},{"at":"13:03","title":"Gaps in Kubernetes traces"},{"at":"18:10","title":"JMS and the authentication server"},{"at":"25:10","title":"Reproducing the vendor bug"},{"at":"30:31","title":"Assembling the puzzle"}],"tech":["OpenTelemetry","Grafana","Grafana Loki","Jolokia","Telegraf","InfluxDB","Tomcat","Apache HTTP Server","GeoServer","GeoWebCache","Kubernetes","JMS","LDAP"],"watchIf":"You get pulled into incidents spanning many services and teams and want a practical method for finding root causes that sit in someone else's component.","themes":["Developer experience & tooling","Cloud native & Kubernetes"],"demo":false}},{"id":22914,"title":"Java is for Data Science, Too: Building an End-to-End ML Pipeline Without Leaving the JVM","type":"Hands-on Lab","duration":120,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["machinelearning","java","dataframes","deeplearning"],"abstract":["For years, Python has reigned supreme in the data science and machine learning landscapes, leaving Java developers with a lingering sense of FOMO. But things have changed. With the advent of lightweight data frames, blazing-fast native file parsers, and interactive notebooks, Java has evolved into a formidable environment for data engineering, exploration, and deep learning.","In this code-driven, interactive and hands-on session, we will break down the myth that you have to context-switch to Python for data science. We will build a complete predictive machine learning pipeline entirely in Java, utilizing an elite stack of modern JVM tools:","JTaccuino: For interactive, Jupyter-style exploratory data analysis directly inside a Java notebook environment.","Hardwood: The ultra-fast, zero-dependency Apache Parquet reader optimized for modern multi-core JVM performance to ingest large datasets.","DFLib:A lightweight, expressive Java DataFrame library to clean, filter, and manipulate our data.","DeepNetts: A pure Java based deep learning development toolkit for building, training, and evaluating neural networks, featuring CPU or GPU acceleration.","gog4j: An exclusive first look at a highly anticipated, unreleased Java plotting API based on the grammar of graphics to natively visualize data and model metrics right in the notebook.","Paging all Java Developers interested in doing math, science, AI, QC and more with Java - join us to shape Java's future in scientific computing."],"favourites":50,"speakers":[{"name":"Sven Reimers","company":"Airbus","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AFdZucoNxDrZRM-rxLR5unTKC2gX_lJcPO2h_HRwBs4pJA=s96-c","bio":"Sven Reimers is an industrial engineer with over 25 years of experience in designing and implementing distributed systems which have a focus in the engineering domain. He was the lead software architect for a system and network management solution for satellite communication networks, which received the Duke&#39;c Choice award. He currently works on ground segment software for earth observation satellite systems. He is author of several scientific articles, co-author of some Java community driven books and was a long term member of the program committee of the JavaOne conference. He was chosen a Java Champion by his peers. In addition he is the founder of the JUG Bodensee, a member of the openjdk community, member of the Apache NetBeans PMC and lead of TweetWallFX and JTaccuino opensource projects. Currently, he works as an eXpert for functional chain software architecture for earth observation systems at Airbus Defence and Space.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"sreimers.bsky.social","twitter":null},{"name":"Zoran Sevarac","company":"Deep Netts ","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-c08270b6-3020-4fc8-a6aa-395616d4bd0f.jpg","bio":"Zoran Sevarac is a seasoned software engineer, university professor of Artificial Intelligence, and passionate Java developer with more than two decades of experience in software engineering, AI research, and open-source innovation.<br>He is the creator of two pioneering AI development platforms built entirely in Java — Neuroph and Deep Netts — which made AI development more accessible to the Java community. His work bridges modern AI technologies with the reliability, performance, and scalability of the Java ecosystem.<br>A Duke’s Choice Award winner, Java Champion, and member of the JCP Executive Committee, Zoran has been instrumental in driving the evolution of AI support in Java. His mission is to simplify AI adoption, enable GPU acceleration through Project Panama and the Vector API, and bring high-performance, energy-efficient, and FinOps-friendly AI to enterprise systems.","linkedin":"zoran-sevarac-phd-49a9a411","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-05T08:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T10:30:00Z","room":"BOF 1","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A 2-hour hands-on lab building an end-to-end ML pipeline in pure Java using JTaccuino notebooks, Hardwood, DFLib, DeepNetts and an early look at gog4j.","summary":["Sven Reimers and Zoran Sevarac's lab challenges the idea that data science requires switching to Python. Participants build a complete predictive machine learning pipeline entirely on the JVM, working interactively in code.","The stack includes JTaccuino for Jupyter-style notebooks, Hardwood as a zero-dependency Apache Parquet reader, DFLib for data frame cleaning and manipulation, and DeepNetts for building, training and evaluating neural networks on CPU or GPU. Attendees also get a first look at gog4j, an unreleased plotting API based on the grammar of graphics."],"keyPoints":["Complete ML pipeline built without leaving Java.","JTaccuino provides notebook-style exploratory analysis.","Hardwood reads Parquet; DFLib handles data frames.","DeepNetts trains neural networks with CPU or GPU acceleration.","gog4j is an unreleased grammar-of-graphics plotting API."],"takeaways":["Try DFLib and a Java notebook for your next data exploration task."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["JTaccuino","Hardwood","Apache Parquet","DFLib","DeepNetts","gog4j","Java"],"watchIf":"You are a Java developer curious about data science and machine learning without switching to Python.","themes":["Data & databases","Java language & JDK"],"demo":false}},{"id":50108,"title":"Introduction to Spring AI","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["spring ai","rag","genai","mcp"],"abstract":["As enterprises race to embed GenAI into their applications, developers need a clear path from concept to","production. This session introduces Spring AI, the framework that brings GenAI capabilities natively into your","Spring Boot applications.","We'll walk through the building blocks of AI-powered applications. From conversational chat interactions to","Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), tool calling, testing, and observability, before diving into agentic AI","patterns using the Model Context Protocol (MCP).","Through hands-on examples, attendees will learn to build production-ready AI applications and agentic systems","using Spring AI."],"favourites":143,"speakers":[{"name":"Timo Salm","company":"Broadcom","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-93dfac6b-7673-4fca-81c6-aa9fb4a0c084.jpg","bio":"<p fgid=\"5163\">Timo Salm is a Principal Solutions Engineer at VMware Tanzu by Broadcom with over a decade of experience in customer-facing roles, modern applications, DevSecOps, and AI. In his roles, he ensures that the most strategic customers in the EMEA region achieve their goals with VMware Tanzu&#39;s Spring, developer platform, AI, and data products. Another essential part of his role is continuously learning through experimenting hands-on with innovations and sharing the outcomes with colleagues, customers, and the community via, for example, conferences. Before Timo joined Pivotal, which VMware and now Broadcom acquired, he worked for consulting firms in the automotive industry as a software architect and full-stack developer.","linkedin":"timosalm","bluesky":null,"twitter":"salmto"}],"start":"2026-10-05T10:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T11:20:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"JzFelLDiVJQ","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Salm walks through Spring AI's core building blocks (ChatModel and ChatClient, structured output, the Advisor API, RAG, @Tool and MCP) as the harness that compensates for what LLMs cannot do alone.","summary":["Salm, a solution architect at Broadcom, frames Spring AI as a harness around the model: LLMs predict tokens without verifying facts, are stateless, only know their public training data and cannot act. The usual remedies (memory, RAG, tool calling) raise new problems in turn: token cost, context limits, an attack surface when the model decides which tool to call, and the need for governance and observability. Spring AI builds on Spring Boot auto-configuration, so switching provider (OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, Anthropic, Ollama) is mostly a dependency and configuration change; Spring AI 2.0 requires Spring Boot 4. Using a recipe-finder sample, he shows the low-level ChatModel and ImageModel APIs with PromptTemplate, then the fluent ChatClient built from an auto-configured ChatClient.Builder with a default system prompt, and .entity() for structured output, which works natively or by adding a JSON schema to the prompt.","The Advisor API, which works like REST client interceptors and supports both blocking and streaming calls, is the basis of the advanced features. Advisors run as an ordered chain, so a logging advisor should sit after RAG to see the full prompt. For RAG he shows indexing with document readers, splitters and VectorStore.add, then retrieval with QuestionAnswerAdvisor using a SearchRequest with topK and a similarity threshold, plus a more modular RAG implementation. Recursive advisors copy the chain to run sub-loops such as LLM-based evaluation or structured-output validation with retry. Tools are plain methods annotated with @Tool and @ToolParam and passed via .tools(). MCP clients only need a server URL in configuration; servers use the MCP server starter and @McpTool. Observability through OpenTelemetry and testing with LLM evaluators were mentioned only briefly; a free Spring Academy course covers the rest."],"keyPoints":["Spring AI 2.0 requires Spring Boot 4; provider switching is done through starters and configuration, not code changes.","ChatClient (fluent, built from an injected ChatClient.Builder) is the main API; .entity(Recipe.class) returns structured output, falling back to schema instructions in the prompt when the provider has no native support.","Advisors are an ordered interceptor chain around model calls; RAG, memory, validation and logging are all implemented as advisors.","QuestionAnswerAdvisor gives basic RAG with a configurable topK and similarity threshold; recursive advisors enable evaluation and retry loops.","@Tool and @ToolParam expose Java methods to the model; @McpTool exposes them to other agents over MCP."],"takeaways":["Learn ChatClient and the Advisor API first; most other Spring AI features are built on them.","Use .entity() structured output to slot LLM calls into ordinary typed Java workflows.","Place logging advisors after RAG and other context-adding advisors so you see the prompt that actually reaches the model.","Keep the tool set small and run agents in restricted environments, since the model decides which tools to call."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why models need a harness"},{"at":"08:58","title":"Setup and ChatModel"},{"at":"15:31","title":"ChatClient and structured output"},{"at":"20:10","title":"Retrieval-augmented generation"},{"at":"24:13","title":"The Advisor API"},{"at":"30:46","title":"Recursive advisors"},{"at":"32:08","title":"Tool calling"},{"at":"36:15","title":"Model Context Protocol"},{"at":"40:19","title":"Observability, testing, resources"}],"tech":["Spring AI","Spring Boot 4","OpenAI","Azure OpenAI","Anthropic","Ollama","RAG","Vector stores","Model Context Protocol (MCP)","MCP Java SDK","OpenTelemetry","Spring Academy"],"watchIf":"You are a Spring developer who has not used Spring AI yet and want a compact tour of its core APIs before building RAG or agentic features.","themes":["LLMs in Java apps","Spring & frameworks"],"demo":false}},{"id":16414,"title":"From vibe coding to harness engineering: modernizing a regulated environment with AI","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["specification","ai","validation","harnesses"],"abstract":["Migrating a banking mainframe to the cloud under fluctuating security regulations in three months seemed like an impossible mission. AI (agents, MCP tools) was the force multiplier that made it possible, but it demanded a paradigm shift. This session dissects the evolution from the illusion of vibe coding—generating code through fluid conversations—to the rigorous discipline of harness engineering.","When AI writes the code, software craftsmanship evolves. The focus is no longer on syntax, but on building safety nets. We will explore how to tame automated code generation in hostile and highly regulated environments by building relentless harnesses: Spec-Driven Development, evolutionary pipelines, and continuous validation.","Key Points","Evolution of Craftsmanship: The shift from typing code to orchestrating, limiting, and auditing systems generated by multiple sub-agents.","Harness Building: Using extreme testing (integration, test containers, environment simulation) to contain and certify the behavior of generated code.","Spec-Driven Development: The specification as a single, unbreakable contract between business, security, and AI agents.","Radical Pragmatism: How to maintain agility and design principles when the rules of the regulated environment change daily."],"favourites":537,"speakers":[{"name":"Anyul Rivas","company":"GFT","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AFdZuco54-nbk6MLvqBg8AALCdJ63k0A6GKL0rnaPhQXAPY=s96-c","bio":"Senior Solution Architect | Designing Scalable Systems &amp; Leading Cross-Team Delivery at GFT | CTO Office | Software Crafter Barcelona leader | Barcelona JUG Leader","linkedin":"anyulled","bluesky":"anyulled.bsky.social","twitter":"anyulled"}],"start":"2026-10-08T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T08:20:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"zEUSFvDOiCo","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"A bank mainframe-to-Kafka proof of concept was vibe-coded to a demo, then made production-grade by surrounding the agents with deterministic harnesses: build enforcers, OpenRewrite, ArchUnit, scripts and token discipline.","summary":["Anyul Rivas (GFT) tells the story in three film-themed chapters. In 'Limitless', three architects had to show a bank how to stream a COBOL mainframe's transactions from SQL Server through Debezium change data capture into Kafka, consumed by Java and C# microservices using the outbox pattern and dead-letter queues, on OpenShift with Helm and Azure DevOps. The bank allowed only Artifactory dependencies, no outside images, no skills, MCP servers or sub-agents, and at first no AI traces in the repo; GitHub Copilot came later. With roughly a month left after onboarding, they vibe-coded a working demo: by Rivas's count 25k commits, 3,500 auto-merged pull requests and 180,000 generated lines.","Vague user stories invite hallucination, so the team ran Matt Pocock's 'grill me' skill to turn each story into an implementation-ready spec with edge cases, bank constraints and acceptance evidence that tells the agent when to stop. The client rejected OpenSpec-style tools and wanted ADRs kept in Confluence, so the team built its own research-plan-implement workflow ending in a mandatory sync of Azure DevOps stories and Confluence pages. They ran two tracks: vibe coding to reach and validate the demo, then a separate engineered track for production. Rivas frames this with John Ousterhout's tactical versus strategic programming.","The 'Apollo 13' chapter treats the harness as the adapter that makes a stochastic model fit deterministic production rules. Lessons included: Maven Enforcer to stop models silently downgrading Java 25 to 17; positive (cataphatic) rather than negative (apophatic) instructions; context engineering after the bank said three people were using 30% of the company's monthly Copilot quota; a duplication threshold in the build; OpenRewrite recipes for style; ArchUnit and ArchUnit.NET for the bank's layering; CLI calls instead of forbidden MCP servers for Confluence; and a per-environment provisioning script for SQL Server under Rosetta. Role-based sub-agent teams and naive TDD both failed.","In the 'Matrix' chapter Rivas argues the developer moves from Neo, fighting inside the code, to Tank on the Nebuchadnezzar: providing judgement, constraints, tools, environments and clear stop conditions while agents handle the tactical work. He cites a study of 660 Claude Code runs in which clean code did not change pass rates but cut tokens and file revisits. He closes with a work-in-progress 'AI harness scorecard' script that rates a repository on five pillars. In Q&A he said the production phase grew to more than 100 bank developers consuming the SDK."],"keyPoints":["Vibe coding was used deliberately from idea to demo; production quality came from a second track with engineering practices, not from continuing to prompt the prototype.","A 300-line implementation plan is cheaper to review than a 5,000-10,000-line pull request, so the human review effort goes into the grilled spec and plan.","Recurring agent mistakes became build failures: Maven Enforcer for the Java version, banned dependencies and groupId/artifactId patterns; a duplication threshold with a message telling the agent to refactor; OpenRewrite recipes (Mockito, AssertJ, Testcontainers, idiomatic Java); ArchUnit tests for architecture layers.","Token discipline: the RTK CLI proxy (Rivas credits it with 60-80% savings), AGENTS.md files under 500 lines, archived stale ADRs, a new session per task kept around 70% context use, skills loaded on demand, an LSP or CodeGraph map instead of rescanning code, and capped output tokens, which cost three to eight times more than input.","A 'virtual IT department' of front-end, back-end, DevOps and QA sub-agents failed, with most tokens spent on agent-to-agent chatter; one orchestrator delegating narrow, disposable tasks worked.","Agents misapplied TDD by writing tests layer by layer (horizontal slices); outside-in TDD with vertical slices, bounded execution and fast external feedback worked better.","Rivas cited a study of 660 Claude Code runs: clean code left pass rates unchanged but reduced token use by 7-8% and file revisits by 34%.","The proposed AI harness scorecard checks five pillars: architectural documentation, mechanical constraints, testing and stability, review and risk prevention, and AI-specific safeguards."],"takeaways":["Turn each recurring agent mistake into a build failure with an actionable message instead of adding another prompt instruction.","Write agent instructions as what to do, not lists of prohibitions.","Have the agent grill you on a story and review the plan before any code is generated.","Wrap flaky environment setup in a script per environment with clear error output so the agent stops burning tokens on it.","Use one orchestrating agent with narrow, disposable sub-agent tasks rather than a team of role-playing agents."],"quotes":[{"text":"A plausible implementation of the wrong problem is still a wrong solution.","at":"15:08"},{"text":"You are anthropomorphizing a human process for a computational system and you will repeat the same pathologies.","at":"27:40"},{"text":"We need to let go of the syntax, because now it's the cheapest part of the system that can be built.","at":"37:11"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"The mainframe modernisation brief"},{"at":"04:45","title":"Vibe coding the demo"},{"at":"07:25","title":"Grilling vague specifications"},{"at":"10:14","title":"Two tracks: demo and production"},{"at":"16:36","title":"Harnesses as adapters"},{"at":"19:20","title":"Build-enforced constraints"},{"at":"22:06","title":"Cutting token consumption"},{"at":"26:39","title":"Sub-agents and TDD pitfalls"},{"at":"30:30","title":"Docs, environments, clean code"},{"at":"33:34","title":"From Neo to Tank"}],"tech":["COBOL","SQL Server","Debezium","Kafka","OpenShift","Helm","Azure DevOps","GitHub Copilot","Confluence","Maven Enforcer","OpenRewrite","ArchUnit","ArchUnit.NET","Testcontainers","Mockito","AssertJ","RTK","Docker Compose","Java 25",".NET"],"watchIf":"You need AI-generated code to meet a regulated enterprise's standards and want concrete harness techniques that worked under tight tooling restrictions.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Legacy modernization","Testing & quality"],"demo":false}},{"id":23539,"title":"Everyday JSON","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["java","data-oriented programming","api","json"],"abstract":["JSON is ubiquitous in modern software, and many use cases are just too small to justify adding external dependencies.","To address these everyday use cases, a new JEP proposes a simple, incubating API for parsing, navigating, and generating JSON documents-both for Java developers and for the JDK itself.","While the Java ecosystem offers many libraries with JSON-processing capabilities, this built-in API is not intended to replace them, but rather to offer a low-friction option when an external library would be unnecessary or impractical.","In this talk, we will show how Data-Oriented Programming helps simplify the API, discuss its intended use cases, outline the design trade-offs, and demonstrate its capabilities."],"favourites":227,"speakers":[{"name":"Viktor Klang","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AAcHTtdkY-Lks0gsRcvl05pqpW8-GOpvuVndojO99ZxUsSy0rgo=s96-c","bio":"Viktor is a Software Architect in the Java Platform Group at Oracle.<br><br>For more than two decades, he has focused on enabling developers to solve problems with maximal productivity and maintainability.<br><br>Specializing in developer ergonomics in the realm of concurrent, parallel, and distributed programming; he has contributed to the standard libraries of several major programming languages, been involved in more than a dozen Open Source projects, and has spoken at numerous conferences and universities.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"viktorklang.com","twitter":null},{"name":"Alan Bateman","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-777f0d6e-3ce7-4670-9962-7f3e5f309f46.jpg","bio":"Alan Bateman is an architect in the Java Platform Group at Oracle and a long-time contributor to OpenJDK. He leads the OpenJDK Core Libraries Group and has helped shape the evolution of the Java platform through the design and implementation of many JDK features, including New I/O (NIO), JVMTI and serviceability, the Java Module System, and, more recently, Project Loom.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T14:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"5y_82lCevHg","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Klang and Bateman present JEP 540, a small, strict, immutable JSON API proposed as an incubator for JDK 28, aimed at one-off scripts, configs, tests and the JDK's own tooling rather than replacing Jackson.","summary":["Viktor Klang opens with the everyday cases where pulling in Jackson feels excessive: a small config file, a JSON body for an HTTP request, a test against a JSON-returning service, a JShell exploration, or a one-off question such as the average temperature in Santa Clara over the next week from the US National Weather Service API. JEP 540, proposed as an incubator for JDK 28, is narrower than the abandoned 2014 JEP 198: no streaming, builders or object mapping, only a sealed JsonValue hierarchy (string, number, boolean, null, object, array) plus Json.parse and Json.toDisplayString.","Klang walks through code. A config is read with Files.readString, parsed, and navigated with get(\"charset\") and get(\"port\"), and tryGet returns an Optional for absent fields so a default such as 8080 can be supplied. A POST to an imaginary social network builds a JsonObject.of(map) and sends its compact toString() through java.net.http.HttpClient. Accessors never coerce: calling the string accessor on a number fails. Because the tree is immutable and sealed, a pattern-matching switch can accept IDs that are either numbers or strings. Navigation fails fast with an exception that reports the path and document offset.","Klang then argues against Postel's law for JSON. Being 'liberal' is not a single dimension: comments, trailing commas and duplicate keys (first or last wins?) each change what a document means depending on the parser. JEP 540 therefore implements RFC 8259 strictly and rejects all three. Alan Bateman covers JDK-internal uses: HttpClient body handlers, marshalling of structural data, a JSON alternative to JMX over RMI, and serviceability tools that already emit JSON, such as jfr print --json and jcmd Thread.dump_to_file.","Bateman demos short programs over the JSON thread dump, whose schema is in the HotSpotDiagnosticMXBean.dumpThreads Javadoc: list thread IDs and names, filter virtual threads via an optional boolean, and print held monitors, where tryValue handles the JSON nulls produced by lock elimination. He also round-trips java.util.Properties to JSON. Klang adds that a simple built-in API also makes it easier for LLMs to generate small Java tools. In Q&A: values are immutable, objects have no defined key order by design, generation is less mature than parsing, record mapping is out of scope (Gavin Bierman's work on structural data may help later), and the JEP explains BigDecimal handling."],"keyPoints":["JEP 540 proposes a JSON API as an incubator module for JDK 28. It needs --add-modules and prints the usual incubator warning.","The model is a sealed JsonValue with six subtypes mirroring the JSON spec, all immutable. Json.parse turns text into a tree; toDisplayString(indent) pretty-prints and toString() is compact.","Navigation uses get(index), get(name), tryGet (Optional when absent) and tryValue (empty Optional for JSON null). Accessors such as asLong, asBoolean, asList and asMap never coerce between types.","Errors are eager and include the failing path and offset into the document.","Parsing is strict RFC 8259: no comments, no trailing commas, no duplicate member names, so a document means the same thing whatever parser reads it.","The JDK already emits JSON from jfr print --json and jcmd Thread.dump_to_file. Bateman used the thread dump (nested arrays, optional fields, explicit nulls, mixed types) as a test case for the API.","Possible JDK uses include HttpClient body handlers/publishers, JSON-persisted Properties, and a REST-style alternative to JMX over RMI and serialization.","The API is explicitly a starting point: once you need comments, object mapping or streaming, the speakers recommend moving to a full library."],"takeaways":["Download the JEP 540 early-access build and try it on a one-off script or JShell session instead of adding a Jackson dependency.","Use jcmd <pid> Thread.dump_to_file -format=json and jfr print --json to get diagnostics you can process with small programs.","Treat JSON documents as strict RFC 8259 and avoid relying on parser-specific leniency like comments or duplicate keys.","Send feedback on what works and what confuses you to core-libs-dev@openjdk.org while the API is still incubating."],"quotes":[{"text":"If the meaning of a JSON document changes based on which JSON parser you use, you have a problem.","at":"26:17"},{"text":"If we make it easier for people to do simple things, we also make it easier for LLMs to do simple things.","at":"40:59"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Everyday JSON use cases"},{"at":"08:05","title":"JEP 540 versus JEP 198"},{"at":"11:55","title":"Parsing a config file"},{"at":"15:54","title":"HTTP requests and the weather"},{"at":"20:02","title":"Data-oriented design and errors"},{"at":"23:46","title":"Against Postel's law"},{"at":"27:49","title":"JSON inside the JDK"},{"at":"32:46","title":"Processing thread dumps"},{"at":"38:31","title":"Properties as JSON"},{"at":"42:19","title":"Wrap-up and Q&A"}],"tech":["JEP 540","JEP 198","RFC 8259","java.net.http.HttpClient","JShell","JDK Flight Recorder","jcmd","HotSpotDiagnosticMXBean","java.util.Properties","Jackson","JMX","Sealed interfaces","Pattern matching"],"watchIf":"You write small Java tools, tests or scripts that touch JSON and want to know what the built-in API will look like and why it is so strict.","themes":["Java language & JDK"],"demo":false}},{"id":9036,"title":"My old trains have a second life, with TinyGo!","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["digital conversion","model trains","bluetooth","embedded systems"],"abstract":["In the 1970s, model trains were a popular hobby. Thanks to low production costs, anyone could afford a small HO gauge layout. The system was simple: a train, a motor, 12V DC in the track, and off you go!","Fifty years later, we took our trains out of the attic with a big idea in mind: to convert them to digital. With some Seeed Studio, Bluetooth, and TinyGo, we managed to get a functional railway network, were we can manage speed, direction, and lights of each train individually.","This talk speaks about hardware and software architecture for embedded systems, in a retrospective format about all our successes (and mistakes) during the conception of this project. Everything is open-source, so don't hesitate to use it, fork it, and enhance it!"],"favourites":79,"speakers":[{"name":"Florian FORESTIER","company":"Zenika","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocLatBLU2zKU17CC2f1JFbRUurY_4zUYQUO80dqhsiKIfg2f4i81=s96-c","bio":"French developer working at Zenika. I spent 5 years working for a sovereign cloud provider, deploying OpenStack at scale and extending it with Golang-written software. Passionate about everything related to IT, especially if it&#39;s new to me!<br><br>Also, I like trains, so I put some RP2040/nRF52840 in my HO models to control them. Have a look at https://github.com/trainberry if you&#39;re interested!<br><br>Oh, and, as my name can prefigure it, I&#39;m French... Sorry if I make some english mistakes.<br><br>","linkedin":"florian-forestier","bluesky":"florian.forestier.re","twitter":null},{"name":"Justine Bachelard","company":"BeYs Cloud","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-34c987ce-cf32-424d-a942-b4d1c29344b7.jpg","bio":"I&#39;m a developper in a sovereign cloud infrastructure. My day-to-day life is filled with Openstack, Golang, DevOps automations and network problems !<br><br>I&#39;m passionate about IT, photography and lots of different drawing or sculpting technics. To sum up : I love learning as much as I can !","linkedin":"justine-bachelard-1240a11b9","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-09T09:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T10:40:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A retrospective on converting 1970s analogue HO model trains to digital control over Bluetooth using Seeed Studio boards and TinyGo, with successes and mistakes.","summary":["Florian Forestier and Justine Bachelard describe taking old HO gauge model trains, which run on simple 12V DC track power, out of the attic and converting them to digital. Using Seeed Studio hardware, Bluetooth and TinyGo, they built a working railway network where speed, direction and lights of each train can be managed individually.","The talk is a retrospective on the hardware and software architecture of this embedded project, including what worked and what went wrong during design. Everything is open source, so attendees are invited to use, fork and extend it."],"keyPoints":["Analogue 12V DC trains converted to individually controlled digital ones.","Stack: Seeed Studio boards, Bluetooth and TinyGo.","Control covers speed, direction and lights per train.","Retrospective on hardware and software architecture, including mistakes.","Project is open source."],"takeaways":["Look at TinyGo if you want to try embedded projects in Go."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["TinyGo","Seeed Studio","Bluetooth","Embedded systems"],"watchIf":"You enjoy hardware tinkering and want a story of embedded Go on microcontrollers.","themes":["Hardware, robots & fun"],"demo":false}},{"id":19708,"title":"Choose your own adventure in agentic design patterns","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["routing","reflection","planning","agents"],"abstract":["Time to dive a little deeper, beyond the usual AI agent intros. When building your multi-agent systems, you’ll often be combining basic bricks like sequential, parallel, or loop flows. With those components, you create more complex patterns, like reflection loops, and reviewer/critique agents.","However the path to \"AI-nlightment\" might require you to make choices with the routing pattern, or to shepherd a swarm of agents to collaborate together. We'll talk about different approach to planning (from programmatic to goal oriented).We'll uncover the concept of progressive disclosure used in skills. We'll see that AI agents can improve baselines with the LLM-as-Judge pattern. And we'll finish with discussing agent loop approaches.","As a committer on both LangChain4j and ADK for Java (Agent Development Kit), it’ll be my pleasure to guide you through this agentic adventure, and help you make the right choices and use the right abstractions on your journey."],"favourites":309,"speakers":[{"name":"Guillaume Laforge","company":"Google","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AFdZucoQ5lU7oZBBSXSkv4PJE8-JzMxuHatM3ouR8sOOnl8=s96-c","bio":"Guillaume Laforge is a Developer Advocate at Google, focusing on Generative AI and AI agent engineering. He&#39;s also a Java Champion, the co-founder of the Apache Groovy programming language, and a founding member of Les Cast Codeurs podcast. <br>He&#39;s a committer on LangChain4j and Agent Development Kit for Java.","linkedin":"glaforge","bluesky":"glaforge.dev","twitter":"glaforge"}],"start":"2026-10-08T14:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"w2RTFWet_6c","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Laforge walks through agentic design patterns (programmatic planning, RAG, progressive disclosure, hierarchical decomposition, LLM-as-judge, goal-oriented planning, agent loops) with Java demos in LangChain4j and ADK for Java.","summary":["Guillaume Laforge, a Google Cloud developer advocate and committer on LangChain4j and ADK for Java, frames an agent as an LLM brain plus memory, tools, planning and a loop toward a goal, and lists the concerns that patterns address: planning, context engineering (avoiding context rot), tool overload, evaluation and multi-agent orchestration. Security and observability are explicitly out of scope. He describes each pattern by name, problem, solution and trade-offs, starting with what he calls almost an anti-pattern: giving agents full autonomy when the workflow can be drawn as a flowchart.","His 'programmatic' pattern is a deep-research app that simply chains calls: a Gemini Flash call with Google Search proposes subtopics, the Gemini Interactions API launches Google's hosted Deep Research agent (stateful, background, streamed; one run took 43 minutes), another call writes a one-page summary, and Nano Banana draws a sketchnote. No agent framework is needed. He recaps RAG as early context engineering, then explains Anthropic-style skills: only the SKILL.md front-matter description sits in context, the body loads when relevant, and referenced files or scripts load last. A LangChain4j demo loads a 'deslopify' skill from the classpath to tone down an over-excited draft.","Hierarchical decomposition is shown with an ADK comic-strip app: a sequential flow analyses uploaded photos for location and description, then a parallel flow finds nearby points of interest with Google Maps while generating pop-art panels, sharing results through common state. For LLM-as-judge he generates three summaries with a cheap Flash model, has a small-model judge pick the best, then compares it with a large-model summary. By his prices (about $0.30 per million input tokens for Flash-Lite against $2 for the large model), three small summaries cost less than one big one, and the small model won roughly one time in three.","Goal-oriented action planning, which he credits to Rod Johnson's Embabel and which LangChain4j and ADK for Java adopted, declares each agent's input and output keys and lets an A*-style planner chain agents to reach a goal, such as an infographic or a YouTube Shorts script, from different starting inputs. He ends with a coding-agent loop inspired by Max Rydahl Andersen's minimal Java agent: a supervisor delegating to file, system and web sub-agents inside a plain while loop, in a bit over 300 lines of LangChain4j."],"keyPoints":["If a task can be drawn as a deterministic flowchart, code it programmatically: easier to debug, at the cost of adaptability.","The Gemini Interactions API is stateful and can invoke hosted agents such as Deep Research in background mode with streamed thoughts.","Skills use three-stage progressive disclosure: front-matter description, then the SKILL.md body, then referenced files and scripts.","In LangChain4j, skills are exposed to the model through a skill tool provider that lets it load skills on demand.","Sequential and parallel sub-agent flows in ADK share a common state map; running independent steps in parallel cuts latency.","LLM-as-judge removes human review bottlenecks but is non-deterministic: the same judge can rate the same output differently.","Goal-oriented action planning (GOAP) lets one pool of agents serve different goals by searching a path through their declared inputs and outputs.","A working coding-agent loop with sub-agents fits in about 300 lines of Java with LangChain4j."],"takeaways":["Reach for plain programmatic orchestration before an agent framework when the steps are known in advance.","Keep tool and skill lists out of the context window until needed, using progressive disclosure.","Try generating several cheap small-model outputs and picking one with a judge before defaulting to the most expensive model.","Declare agent inputs and outputs explicitly so a goal planner, or a future you, can recombine them."],"quotes":[{"text":"You don't need a real agent or an agent framework for a lot of use cases that benefit from AI integrated in your projects.","at":"14:15"},{"text":"There are things where Java is innovating more than what the Python ecosystem is doing.","at":"38:48"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Agent basics and concerns"},{"at":"06:32","title":"Programmatic planning"},{"at":"14:15","title":"RAG as context engineering"},{"at":"16:28","title":"Skills and progressive disclosure"},{"at":"23:05","title":"Hierarchical agent decomposition"},{"at":"29:31","title":"LLM as judge"},{"at":"38:48","title":"Goal-oriented action planning"},{"at":"44:57","title":"Coding agent loop"}],"tech":["LangChain4j","ADK for Java","Gemini","Gemini Interactions API","Gemini Deep Research","Nano Banana","Google Search","Google Maps","Agent Skills","Embabel","GOAP"],"watchIf":"You build agents in Java and want a catalogue of when to use workflows, sub-agents, judges or goal-based planning, with code for each.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":true}},{"id":8164,"title":"The pizza party of the future 🍕 software teams and product value in the age of coding agents","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"People, Practices & Career","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["teams","software","ai","agile"],"abstract":["AI is having a huge impact on our software development teams - nobody is sure how the role of a developer will evolve, and Simon and Katie don’t have the perfect prediction either!","Now that our teams have ever more capable coding assistants working around the clock, we are seeing “new” bottlenecks appear around value and trust questions. In response to this, we’re seeing organizations create new flavors of engineering roles, move down to one-pizza-teams, move to product owners and their AI wrangler (a one-pizza-slice team?), or even build factory lines.","This is not the first time the way we approach software teams has changed: we’ve gone from silos and super specialization to cross-functional Scrum teams and 2-pizza teams; from T-shaped and M-shaped professionals to full-stack, full-lifecycle, DevSecFin…Ops, value streams, Team Topologies, and more. We’ve added all kinds of coaches and then removed them again.","So who is invited to the pizza party of the future? Who makes decisions, who takes responsibility, what new and old roles and skills will be needed? And who is no longer welcome? We’ll take a look at what some of the friendlier agile critics from the past can warn us about. We will see how their takes can help us move to a more value-oriented future with agents and humans collaborating on user needs."],"favourites":291,"speakers":[{"name":"Katie Clark","company":"Comsysto Reply","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-02e30f45-7d0b-455a-9fbf-17e105b17359.jpg","bio":"Katie is a delivery lead, manager, (ex) software developer, and trainer at Reply Comsysto. Over the past 8 years, she has been involved in complex software projects spanning various domains, from semiconductor manufacturing to food supply chains.<br>Developing quality software products requires a team effort. Therefore, her focus is on fostering a collaborative team culture, promoting an agile mindset, and continuously evolving suitable processes within the organization.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Simon Rohrer","company":"Saxo Bank","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-399454c9-6bcf-448e-ab7d-778aac81bcc3.jpg","bio":"Simon Rohrer, Head of Enterprise Architecture and Ways of Working at Saxo Bank has spent his career advocating for modern ways of working, with roles in hands on software engineering, agile coaching with a focus on technical excellence, and senior roles in enterprise architecture and strategy. <br><br>He&#39;s a co-author of Jonathan Smart&#39;s IT Revolution Press book Sooner Safer Happier, and working on a chapter on organising for outcomes for the forthcoming follow-up book.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T12:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T12:50:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"Tc2RFLa0ip8","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Clark and Rohrer use 70 years of software history to argue that developers won't disappear with coding agents, that Taylorist de-skilling is the real risk, and that team size still depends on context.","summary":["Katie Clark and Simon Rohrer reject the 'death of the two-pizza team' headlines and set out to knock down three claims: we no longer need developers, developers should just type prompts without thinking or talking, and a 100% correct spec is all agents need. They take Andrej Karpathy's remark that coding agents started working around December 2025 as the current earthquake, then show it is not the first. 'End of programmers' headlines greeted Fortran (1956), SQL, Visual Basic and low-code. Jean Sammet on COBOL, Alistair Cockburn on objects and the BDD originators all described these tools as aids to collaboration between developers and business, not replacements.","On ways of working they argue that waterfall was always a myth (SAGE was iterative, and Royce warned against strict sequencing), that agile drifted from conversations into processes and Jira, and that feature factories are not value factories (Tom Gilb, Melissa Perri). On people they cover the 1960s 'computer girl' era, the antisocial-programmer stereotype from a 1966 study, the 10x myth that Laurent Bossavit takes apart, and offshoring's view of coding as tedious work that follows a perfect spec. The central contrast is Taylor's de-skilled worker against Drucker's self-managing knowledge worker, together with sociotechnical systems design from the British coal mines and Calvin Pava's point that office work is messy and runs on conversation.","For the future they cite Dario Amodei's claim that software engineering will go away, then Gergely Orosz and Kent Beck pushing back. Developer roles shift toward Adam Tornhill's 'expert generalist builders' and 'enablers' (harness and platform engineers). They warn against single-player mode with 50 agent windows, argue for hiring juniors (Charity Majors, Dan North) and note emerging archetypes at Anthropic. On spec-driven dark factories they cite Dex Horthy shutting one down over a primary-key bug nobody could fix, and offer a two-by-two of local versus systemic change and conventional versus novel change: only local, conventional work suits 'spec as source' with the lights out.","The answer on team size is that it depends. Prototyping may need two slices, a business-critical stable team at least five people (with a seat for a junior), internal tools one pizza. Two-pizza teams, five to seven people in Bezos's 2004 formulation, were never a template to copy everywhere. Their verdict: Taylorism of the work, linearising and automating the predictable parts, is fine. Taylorism of the workers is not, and humans remain responsible for deciding what is valuable."],"keyPoints":["Predictions that programmers would become unnecessary date back to Fortran in 1956 and recurred with SQL, Visual Basic and low-code.","Waterfall was never practised as described: SAGE was written up in 1983 as iterative and prototype-driven.","Dan North argues the Tuckman forming-storming-norming model failed replication, which weakens the case for permanently stable teams; he proposes bringing people to the work instead.","Adam Tornhill's two emerging paths: expert generalist builders who decide what to build with agents, and enablers who build guardrails, tests and harnesses.","Dex Horthy shut down a lights-out software factory after an agent spread an unfixable primary-key error through the code base.","Spec-as-source and dark factories fit local, conventional changes; systemic or novel changes need lights up, with humans reading tests and code and judging results.","Anthropic, by the speakers' account, still runs two-pizza teams because on-call, holidays and ownership have not gone away.","Peter Naur's theory building and production (Charity Majors) are presented as the two sources of truth, more than any spec."],"takeaways":["Decide per change whether it is local or systemic, conventional or novel, before handing it to a spec-driven agent pipeline.","Keep hiring and mentoring juniors, and reserve a seat for one in critical teams.","Push back on roles reduced to reviewing agent output; keep developers in conversations about value and design.","Work with agents in multiplayer mode: share context and decisions with the team instead of running solo agent swarms.","Pick team size from criticality and context rather than copying a two-pizza or one-slice rule."],"quotes":[{"text":"Treat the machine like a machine and the people like people.","at":"35:42"},{"text":"The barrier to writing software has collapsed, but the barrier to engineering definitely hasn't.","at":"35:42"},{"text":"Taylorism of the work is actually fine. Taylorism of the workers? Absolutely no.","at":"49:59"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Three pins to knock down"},{"at":"05:35","title":"Languages and the end of programmers"},{"at":"08:07","title":"Waterfall, agile and feature factories"},{"at":"12:13","title":"Stereotypes, 10x and offshoring"},{"at":"21:01","title":"Taylor versus Drucker"},{"at":"25:44","title":"How developer roles shift"},{"at":"30:54","title":"Jobs to be done, juniors"},{"at":"37:02","title":"Dark factories and spec as source"},{"at":"46:13","title":"How many pizzas"},{"at":"48:35","title":"Bowling scorecard"}],"tech":["Coding agents","Spec-driven development","Harness engineering","Wardley mapping","Cynefin","Domain-driven design","Extreme Programming","Team Topologies"],"watchIf":"You lead or shape software teams and want historically grounded arguments against 'developers are obsolete' and 'just write the perfect spec' thinking.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":10023,"title":"You thought technical debt was bad? Meet design debt!","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["architecture","microservices","design debt","technical debt"],"abstract":["In our job, technical debt is probably the most frustrating, draining and tenacious thing we have to fight against. Of course, there are best practices: refactor, aim for simplicity with KISS, avoid over-engineering with YAGNI in order to keep this debt as low as possible. And yet, we never really manage to get rid of it. Worse, it keeps piling up even when we apply those principles, as if it were inevitable.","But what if those very best practices sometimes made the problem worse instead of solving it? Technical debt may be only the visible part of something deeper: accidental design debt. A far more complex problem that leads to spaghetti software and microservices that are really just a distributed monolith, and that we often create ourselves without even realizing it.","In this talk, we will explore the mechanisms of design debt and its impact on our architecture, our processes, our organization, and even our business. Through concrete examples, we will see how to detect it, assess how deep it goes, and act before it becomes entrenched for good."],"favourites":509,"speakers":[{"name":"Julien Topçu","company":"Shodo Group","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GgE8qpDBrF4vlQ9jm7d9eSagPY606rA7umzpIU6jQ=s96-c","bio":"Julien is a CTO and hands-on technical coach with 15 years+ of experience, specializing in Domain-Driven Design (#DDD). His expertise lies in helping organizations build systems that deliver high business value. Julien focuses on aligning organizational structure, architecture and software practices with business objectives. As a member of the OWASP foundation, he actively promotes application security best practices. An international speaker, Julien enjoys sharing his knowledge with others.","linkedin":"julien-topçu","bluesky":"julientopcu.com","twitter":"JulienTopcu"},{"name":"Josian Chevalier","company":"Shodo","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-c4de4a52-ae2e-4937-bbeb-b359147c8675.jpg","bio":"Tech coach at Shodo, specializing in AI assisted development, Domain Driven Design and Flow. He helps organizations improve their practices and systems, from architecture and code to org structure and process.","linkedin":"josianchevalier","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-09T08:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"Julien Topçu and Josian Chevalier argue that technical debt is the visible part of deeper accidental design debt, and show how to detect and act on it.","summary":["The session starts from the frustration that technical debt keeps piling up even when teams apply refactoring, KISS and YAGNI. It asks whether those practices sometimes make things worse, and suggests technical debt is only the visible part of accidental design debt, which can lead to spaghetti software and microservices that are really a distributed monolith.","Julien Topçu and Josian Chevalier set out to explore how design debt works and how it affects architecture, processes, organisation and the business. Through concrete examples they show how to detect it, assess how deep it goes, and act before it becomes entrenched."],"keyPoints":["Technical debt may be a symptom of deeper design debt.","Following KISS, YAGNI and refactoring does not prevent design debt.","Design debt can produce distributed monoliths disguised as microservices.","Impacts reach architecture, processes, organisation and business."],"takeaways":["Check whether your microservices can be deployed and changed independently, or form a distributed monolith."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Microservices"],"watchIf":"Your codebase keeps accumulating debt despite refactoring and you suspect a design-level cause.","themes":["Architecture & design"],"demo":false}},{"id":5503,"title":"AI without spaghetti: Clean architecture in the age of AI","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["refactoring","cleanarchitecture","llm","scalability"],"abstract":["Is your shiny new AI feature already tangled in a plate of spaghetti code, with helper classes sticking out like rogue meatballs and surprise side effects splattering everywhere? In this session, we follow a short story (i.e., explain our experiment) of a small chatbot named Leo that grows into a full‑blown platform, showing how careless choices turn neat experiments into costly traps. We then flip the script and refactor the same product into a scalable, enterprise-ready architecture using Clean Architecture. Drawing clear lines between domain, application, and infrastructure so that models can change without breaking everything else. By the end, you will see that good structure is not a luxury, but the only way to keep your AI moving fast without regrets.","What we cover","- The hidden cost of “just ship it” when models, data pipelines, and prompts keep changing","- Where Clean Architecture fits in a modern AI stack: domain, ports, adapters, and model gateways","- Demo: Route simple queries to a local LLM and advanced ones to the cloud, in minutes, not days.","- Pitfalls we hit in real projects and the patterns that saved us","What you will learn","- How to spot early signs of architectural decay in AI projects","- A step‑by‑step guide to isolating AI concerns from business rules","- Practical tips for introducing structure into an existing “AI spaghetti” codebase"],"favourites":262,"speakers":[{"name":"Maarten Vandeperre","company":"Red Hat","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-909df07a-dfbf-40e8-9065-be12fa662faf.jpg","bio":"Maarten Vandeperre is an experienced software professional who recently joined Red Hat as an Appdev &amp; AI Specialized Solutions Architect. With a strong background in software development and architecture, he brings a wealth of expertise to his role. Maarten&#39;s primary focus is on application development and AI, with a particular emphasis on leveraging Red Hat&#39;s OpenShift platform from a developer&#39;s perspective.<br><br>One of Maarten&#39;s true passions lies in advocating for &quot;clean architecture&quot; as a guiding principle in software development. He firmly believes in the importance of designing software systems that are modular, maintainable, and scalable. As part of his dedication to this approach, Maarten strives to map these principles to infrastructure solutions, ensuring that the underlying technology supports and enhances the overall architecture. His deep understanding of integration technologies, such as API Gateways, Keycloak, Kafka, service mesh, and Camel, enables him to create seamless connections between systems while adhering to clean architectural principles, which empower organizations to thrive in the ever-evolving digital landscape.","linkedin":"maarten-vandeperre","bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Lutske van der Meer","company":"Craftsmen","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-de0a5aeb-dc2b-4ea6-91e0-77d10cdc61c4.jpg","bio":"Lutske van der Meer (previous Lutske de Leeuw) is a Software Engineer at Craftsmen and co-organizer of JUG Noord and ApeldoornJUG. A Java developer at heart and full-stack by trade. She loves to share knowledge through storytelling, often featuring animals like cats or llamas to make complex topics more relatable. She regularly speaks across Europe about AI and software craftsmanship. Lutske studied Computer Science and completed a Deep Learning specialization, and she enjoys experimenting with AI to bridge the gap between innovation and everyday development. Passionate about Devoxx4Kids and other knowledge-sharing initiatives, she believes learning should always be fun and accessible for everyone.","linkedin":"Lutske","bluesky":"lutskedev.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T08:20:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"E--oDq3X74E","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Van der Meer and Vandeperre vibe-code a recipe app from static HTML to an AI-powered Quarkus app, then refactor it to clean architecture enforced by ArchUnit tests that agents can't edit.","summary":["The talk follows Leo, a fictional non-developer who wants to read his nonna's Italian spaghetti recipe, as his vibe-coded app grows branch by branch: a 1990s static site served by Python, then a Quarkus REST backend, Postgres with Hibernate Panache, OCR upload with Tesseract for Java, and finally LangChain4j to turn scanned recipes into readable English. Along the way they report what went wrong: $20 subscriptions running out after minutes with seven-hour waits (and $300 lost in one API-key session), copied agent skills that silently made everything Kotlin, the agent painting the UI red because the Italian flag has red, and the agent rewriting Flyway scripts and dropping the database.","Vandeperre then explains clean architecture: a dependency-free domain, use cases mapped one-to-one to user actions instead of shared services, and ports and adapters at the edge, with runtimes like Quarkus, Open Liberty or Spring Boot as a thin wiring layer, which he links to sovereignty and avoiding vendor lock-in. Refactors produced by OpenAI and Anthropic models looked layered but differed in where ports lived, and all of it sat in one Gradle module so nothing was enforced. Their cheaper workflow uses OpenCode multi-agents: a product-owner agent writes a functional analysis, an architect agent on a frontier model breaks it into small tasks, and a local Qwen model implements them (with reasoning turned off, since it only has to follow instructions).","Adding a LangChain4j chatbot leads to guardrails: validate input and output size to control cost, keep prompt-injection and size limits in the infrastructure layer, and place business rules such as 'no pineapple on pizza' in the domain. NeMo Guardrails is suggested as a proxy in front of OpenAI-compatible endpoints. Swapping models or inference engines needs re-validation even when the contract is unchanged. ArchUnit tests enforce the layering, and a demo shows a Jakarta annotation in a domain class failing the build. Because agents edited those tests too, they keep ArchUnit rules, Flyway schemas and end-to-end tests in a separate repository pulled in as a Git submodule."],"keyPoints":["Vibe-coded backends let ORM entities and framework annotations leak through the whole codebase; domain entities that mirror the database schema are still a database dependency.","Use cases map one-to-one to user actions, avoiding a shared updatePerson service reused by hundreds of endpoints; the cost is accidental duplication.","A clean-architecture folder layout in a single Gradle module enforces nothing; separate build modules make dependencies checkable at compile time.","Their multi-agent OpenCode setup: product-owner agent writes requirements, architect agent on a frontier model writes small tasks, a local Qwen model implements them with reasoning disabled.","Changing model, model version or inference engine (vLLM, Ollama, Podman Desktop) can change behaviour, so treat it as a dependency change and re-validate.","Split guardrails: input and output size limits and prompt-injection checks in infrastructure, business rules in the domain.","ArchUnit rules such as 'domain classes must not depend on Jakarta, Quarkus or Spring packages' catch architectural drift in CI.","Agents edited Flyway scripts and architecture tests, so the speakers moved those files into a Git submodule the agent cannot change."],"takeaways":["Add ArchUnit tests that forbid framework dependencies in your domain package and run them in CI.","Put architecture tests, schema migrations and end-to-end tests where your coding agent cannot modify them, for example a Git submodule.","Split modules by layer in your build tool so violations fail at compile time.","Limit chatbot input and output size as well as content to keep LLM costs bounded.","Benchmark cheaper or local models for implementation tasks once a stronger model has written precise instructions."],"quotes":[{"text":"I sometimes compare the AI and vibe coding with a toddler. You can say you can't do five things. You can be happy if it listens to two out of the five.","at":"19:01"},{"text":"Never so happy to see an error.","at":"41:49"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Leo and the recipe"},{"at":"04:49","title":"Vibe-coding tools and costs"},{"at":"07:39","title":"Backend, database and OCR"},{"at":"15:47","title":"LangChain4j recipe extraction"},{"at":"20:24","title":"Clean architecture explained"},{"at":"25:42","title":"Refactoring with multiple models"},{"at":"33:31","title":"Chatbot and guardrails"},{"at":"38:54","title":"Enforcing with ArchUnit"},{"at":"42:50","title":"Wrap-up"}],"tech":["Quarkus","Hibernate Panache","PostgreSQL","Flyway","Gradle","Tesseract","LangChain4j","OpenCode","Claude","OpenAI","Qwen","MLX","OpenShift","NeMo Guardrails","vLLM","Ollama","ArchUnit","Podman","React"],"watchIf":"You're letting coding agents build features and want a concrete way to keep the architecture from decaying.","themes":["Architecture & design","AI agents & agentic coding","LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":true}},{"id":8196,"title":"Testing Architecture Made Simple","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["architecture","taikai","archunit","testing"],"abstract":["Every software project starts with architectural decisions—principles and patterns meant to guide the system’s evolution. But as codebases grow, how do you ensure those decisions remain intact? That’s where ArchUnit comes in. By expressing architectural rules as tests, ArchUnit lets you verify your architecture automatically with every build.","Taikai takes this concept further. It builds on ArchUnit by providing a ready-to-use collection of architectural rules for popular technologies, helping teams enforce architectural consistency and best practices effortlessly. In this session, you’ll learn how Taikai can make your architecture more testable, maintainable, and resilient from day one."],"favourites":174,"speakers":[{"name":"Gunter Rotsaert","company":"ICT TriOpSys","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-3ce83e03-89be-4443-a0b5-015cef45aa7e.jpg","bio":"Gunter is a Software Architect/Requirements Engineer with 25 years of experience in different roles in software engineering. Since 2005 he is developing Mission Critical Software systems via his employer ICT TriOpSys.<br>He likes sharing knowledge about the Java ecosystem and does so by means of blogging, many internal workshops and presentations at ICT TriOpSys and public speaking.","linkedin":"gunter-rotsaert-5402a912","bluesky":"mydeveloperplanet.com","twitter":"mydevlprplanet"}],"start":"2026-10-05T16:20:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T16:50:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"asAxisu6EQ0","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Rotsaert shows how ArchUnit turns architecture rules into JUnit tests and how the Taikai library on top of it provides ready-made, readable rules for Java and Spring projects.","summary":["Rotsaert starts from familiar drift in a layered Spring Boot app: a repository injected into a controller, a domain class depending on a service, cycles too long to spot in review, and a utils class half the code depends on. Code review fails on large, often AI-generated PRs, so he tests the architecture instead. ArchUnit expresses rules as JUnit tests (@AnalyzeClasses, @ArchTest) and has a pre-built layeredArchitecture() rule. But starting from a blank test is hard, the documentation is long, and the rules grow repetitive. Taikai sits on ArchUnit and offers a builder with predefined rules by category: Java, logging, test, and Spring controllers, services and repositories.","His demos ban Lombok imports, forbid methods that declare generic exceptions (with a documented exclusion for one Spring Security config class), and chain existing rules into a house convention for Spring configuration: config classes in a config package, immutable records with @ConfigurationProperties, and @ConfigurationPropertiesScan on the main class. Generated code is excluded, checkAll() reports every violation, and a company default config can be extended per project. When no Taikai rule exists, a plain ArchUnit rule plugs into the builder. His team had AI generate ArchUnit rules from their SonarQube instance through the SonarQube MCP server, so AI-written code fails the gate less often."],"keyPoints":["ArchUnit rules run as JUnit tests on every build; use ImportOption to keep them from applying to test classes.","Taikai pulls in ArchUnit transitively and replaces class-level annotations with a builder (namespace, categories, build, check or checkAll).","Any Taikai rule can exclude specific classes or regex patterns, which documents deliberate exceptions in the test itself.","Generating ArchUnit rules from existing SonarQube rules with an AI model and the SonarQube MCP server reduced quality-gate failures on AI-generated code.","Adoption only happened once the Taikai test was added to the team's Maven archetype for new projects; Rotsaert judges the single-maintainer library acceptable because it is quick to release and easy to fork."],"takeaways":["Copy the ready-made Taikai test (or the Spring Boot starter example) from its documentation into a project and run it.","Add rules one at a time when you see a recurring problem, and only enforce what matters, more strictly where AI writes the code.","Put architecture tests in your project archetype or template so new projects get them by default."],"quotes":[{"text":"This is what I call configuration roulette. You search for all the annotations which contain the word configuration and then you add them.","at":"11:43"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"How architecture erodes"},{"at":"03:53","title":"Testing with ArchUnit"},{"at":"07:51","title":"Introducing Taikai"},{"at":"08:53","title":"Banning unwanted dependencies"},{"at":"10:15","title":"Spring configuration rules"},{"at":"13:45","title":"Exclusions and full test"},{"at":"17:47","title":"Lessons learned"},{"at":"20:12","title":"Should you use Taikai"}],"tech":["ArchUnit","Taikai","JUnit 5","Spring Boot","Spring Security","Lombok","SonarQube","SonarQube MCP server","Maven archetypes","OpenAPI Generator","jOOQ"],"watchIf":"Your architecture diagrams no longer match the code and you want a quick, low-effort way to enforce layering and conventions in CI.","themes":["Architecture & design","Testing & quality"],"demo":true}},{"id":16408,"title":"10 Billion Records vs. The Regulator: An Architectural Post-Mortem of an 11TB Exadata Failure","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["migration","performance","compliance","database"],"abstract":["What happens when your architecture doesn't just fail a performance test, but triggers a national regulatory investigation? In this session, we go inside a critical system failure at a major bank that led to a formal report to BaFin and a complete rethink of our data strategy.","Dealing with 11TB of data on Oracle Exadata taught us that at a certain scale—specifically, the 10-billion-record mark—standard indexing strategies and read-side duplicate checks don't just slow down; they collapse. We will reverse-engineer this incident by walking through the five critical architectural milestones:","First Aid: Immediate application-layer mitigation steps to halt cascading timeouts.","The Refactoring Oversight: How a missing 10-day query window check, removed years prior, silently waited to trigger an 11TB full scan.","The Cache Bottleneck: The high-stakes \"do nothing\" war room decision to wait for the Exadata Smart Flash Cache to warm up and recover.","The Structural Flip: Moving 10kB of complex analytic queries from read-side deduplication to a write-side integrity layer.","The Exit Strategy: Designing and planning a zero-loss dual-write ingestion architecture to migrate 10 billion records to Cloud PostgreSQL without CDC.","Walk away with an unvarnished, practical blueprint for navigating the compliance-performance paradox, handling database cold starts, and executing a zero-loss cloud modernization strategy under heavy pressure."],"favourites":213,"speakers":[{"name":"Martin Ladecky","company":"Commerzbank","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocLM4sCcWIE0PSQd8FkkwMn4URk4bJz3Rk8M-_CCXwhGJvRNM0doNA=s96-c","bio":"Martin Ladecký is an enterprise architect and systems engineer dedicated to building resilient, high-scale data infrastructure and promoting clean engineering practices. Operating within heavily regulated financial environments, he specialises in optimising core Java workloads, container stability, and complex multi-terabyte database ecosystems.<br>As an active contributor to the European open-source and tech community, Martin balances his corporate work at scale with meaningful &quot;Tech for Good&quot; initiatives. He organises the Czech Java User Group and contributes to Česko.Digital to help NPOs change lives through technology.<br>When he isn’t diving into thread dumps or troubleshooting connection pools, you can usually find him exploring local running trails or enjoying a great espresso.","linkedin":"martin-ladeck%C3%BD-9366ba5","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-09T08:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"Martin Ladecky post-mortems a Commerzbank incident on an 11TB Oracle Exadata database that reached BaFin, and the write-side redesign and PostgreSQL migration that followed.","summary":["The session reverse-engineers a critical failure at a major bank, where a system holding 11TB on Oracle Exadata, at around 10 billion records, saw indexing and read-side duplicate checks collapse and led to a formal report to BaFin. It walks through five milestones: application-layer first aid against cascading timeouts, a removed 10-day query window check that later triggered an 11TB full scan, the war-room decision to wait for Exadata Smart Flash Cache to warm up, and the move of deduplication from read side to a write-side integrity layer.","It ends with the exit strategy: a zero-loss dual-write ingestion architecture to migrate the 10 billion records to Cloud PostgreSQL without CDC. Martin Ladecky aims to give a practical blueprint for the compliance-performance paradox and database cold starts."],"keyPoints":["At about 10 billion records, standard indexing and read-side duplicate checks collapsed.","A query window check removed during refactoring years earlier led to an 11TB full scan.","The team chose to wait for Exadata Smart Flash Cache to warm up.","Deduplication moved from read side to a write-side integrity layer.","Migration to Cloud PostgreSQL planned via dual-write without CDC."],"takeaways":["Review whether refactorings removed implicit query limits that protect large tables.","Prefer write-side integrity checks over read-side deduplication at large scale."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Oracle Exadata","Exadata Smart Flash Cache","PostgreSQL","Dual-write"],"watchIf":"You run large relational databases and want a candid incident story and migration plan.","themes":["Data & databases","Architecture & design"],"demo":false}},{"id":14452,"title":"Quixotic: Pure Java Inference at Native Speed","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["java","sovereignty","ai","jvm"],"abstract":["AI for Java, in Java.","AI sovereignty for the world's most trusted runtime.","What if AI on the JVM were just one Maven dependency away? AI in a jar.","What if AI on the JVM needed no Python, no Docker containers, no native wrappers, no external processes, no HTTP requests?","What if AI on the JVM ran at native speed on plain CPUs, and sometimes even outperformed native inference engines?","What if every component of AI on the JVM, from tokenization to model formats, quantization, inference algorithms and prompt caching, were a pure-Java building block?","What if AI on the JVM could not only read and write, but also hear and see?","What if AI on the JVM were completely free, open source and built on open standards?","This talk shows how inference is built using pure Java and how to make local AI run fast on ordinary CPUs. You'll leave knowing how modern LLMs actually work under the hood, how the Vector API and quantized matrix multiplication kernels close the gap with native engines, and how to add local inference to your own Java application with a single dependency.","Come for the live demos: Gemma 4, LFM 2.5+, gpt-oss, Qwen 3.5+, Ministral 3, Granite 4.1+ and Nemotron 3+ on a vanilla JVM, at native speed. Text, audio and vision demos included."],"favourites":78,"speakers":[{"name":"Alfonso² Peterssen","company":"Quixotic AI","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-f06094d8-3426-4f3c-aaf4-9e8556fdb4da.jpg","bio":"<strong>Alfonso² Peterssen</strong> is the founder of <strong>Quixotic AI</strong>, building an AI stack from first principles for the JVM. His work challenges the belief that modern AI has to run outside the JVM.<br>Instead of wrapping Python runtimes or stitching together external systems, he brings AI directly into the JVM, where it can run and scale as part of the platform itself.<br><br>As a former <strong>Senior Researcher</strong> on Oracle’s <strong>GraalVM</strong> team, he created <strong><em>Espresso</em></strong>, the first spec-compliant meta-circular JVM written entirely in Java.<br><br>Today, he focuses on bringing AI and the JVM together by running the same code across CPUs and accelerators and pushing computation to the hardware that can execute it best. The approach is pragmatic: stay in Java and offload to C, CUDA, Metal, HIP, OpenCL, Mojo, and beyond when it matters. The goal is simple and uncompromising: <strong>write once, accelerate everywhere</strong>.<br><br>Through projects like <strong><em>Llama3.java</em></strong> and <strong><em>Gemma4.java</em></strong>, he demonstrated that high-performance AI implementations in pure Java are possible.<br>Based in Zurich, he works at the intersection of compilers, runtime systems, and AI to redefine what’s possible on the JVM.","linkedin":"alfonso2peterssen","bluesky":"mukel.bsky.social","twitter":"TheMukel"}],"start":"2026-10-08T09:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T10:40:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"S7x4DxwRnk4","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":4098,"title":"From Developer to Builder: A Spec-Driven Development Deep Dive","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["brownfield projects","specifications","spec-driven development","ai coding agents"],"abstract":["Spec-Driven Development is a way of working with AI coding agents where the specification drives the code, not the prompt. I use Spec-Driven Development according to the AI Unified Process (AIUP) in several customer projects, and in this deep dive, I show how it works in practice.","We will build a business application from scratch, and also learn how to work on brownfield projects. We write specifications, let an AI agent generate the code, run it, and add tests that check the code against the spec. When the requirements change, we update the spec and regenerate the code rather than patching the code by hand.","We will explore the harness: the rules, context, and tests around the agent that keep its output predictable enough for real work. By the end, you will know what Spec-Driven Development is, where it helps, where it does not, and how to try it on your own stack."],"favourites":536,"speakers":[{"name":"Simon Martinelli","company":"Martinelli GmbH","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-8784fd05-8251-4fa9-be7b-35c1216e84b9.jpg","bio":"Simon Martinelli is a Java Champion, Vaadin Champion, and Oracle ACE Pro with over three decades of experience as a software architect, developer, consultant, and trainer. He is the creator of the AI Unified Process (AIUP) and a strong advocate of Spec-Driven Development.<br><br>As the owner of Martinelli LLC, he coaches teams to get up to speed with AI-driven development. He regularly shares his insights through international conferences, articles, and his blog, Keep IT Simple (<a href=\"https://martinelli.ch/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(51, 122, 183);\">https://martinelli.ch</a>), where he writes about AI, architecture, and modern Java development.<br>He is also a lecturer at two universities in Switzerland, where he teaches software architecture, persistence, DevOps, and cloud-native development.","linkedin":"simonmartinelli","bluesky":"martinelli.ch","twitter":"simas_ch"}],"start":"2026-10-05T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T14:30:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"z8lal-Yt_04","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Martinelli walks through his AI Unified Process: use cases and entity models kept in the repo as the source of truth, a stack-specific harness, deterministic sensors, and reverse engineering for brownfield modernisation.","summary":["Simon Martinelli, a Swiss consultant who mostly modernises 20-25-year-old Java systems, started spec-driven development after a vibe-coded volunteer management app became impossible to adapt for a second client. Citing Birgitta Böckeler's three levels (spec-first, spec-anchored, spec-as-source), he argues for spec-anchored: specs live in the repo next to the code and are the lasting definition of the system. Regenerating code from specs on every change repeats model-driven development without its determinism, and AI is not a compiler. His AI Unified Process (AIUP) borrows from the Rational Unified Process: a vision leads to requirements with stable IDs and a glossary, then an entity model, use case diagrams and Jacobson/Cockburn-style system use cases with pre- and postconditions, main and alternative flows, business rules, and a status field.","The live demo builds a hotel reservation system with Claude Code (Sonnet), Spring Boot, Vaadin and jOOQ, using skills distributed as a Claude Code plugin marketplace (also via Tessl), with a technology-agnostic core plus stack plugins (Vaadin/jOOQ, Angular/JPA, Blazor, NestJS/React). He chose jOOQ over Hibernate because the SQL is visible in review, and Vaadin because its tested components and browserless UI tests mean less to review. Skills generate requirements, a glossary, a Mermaid ER model, a PlantUML use case diagram and a use case spec; a spec-review skill runs a blocking Python linter plus non-blocking AI semantic checks. A Flyway migration with check constraints is generated, then the use case is implemented in 52 seconds. The resulting UI let users pick a checkout date before check-in, because nothing in the harness said to add validation to the UI.","The second half covers the harness and sensors. Deterministic sensors (ArchUnit, SonarQube, linters, JUnit tests that read the spec files) block progress; LLM-based checks only warn, because they can loop. In his Spring PetClinic reference repo, tests check that every @UseCase annotation points to a real use case, that 'done' use cases have tests, and that business rules are traced. Claude Code hooks guard status transitions, and a coverage-check sub-agent detects drift between spec, code and tests. Generated white-box tests give about 95% coverage, but he questions their value since they change in step with the code. Black-box Playwright tests derived from BPMN business processes, written by test engineers, carry more weight. CLAUDE.md should stay short and point to architecture docs, and rules that matter should become guardrails rather than guidelines.","For brownfield work the process runs in reverse: first find modules, then reverse-engineer entity models and use cases module by module, review them with domain experts, and add new features during the modernisation. Martinelli warns that a go-live missed a rare edge case nobody remembered; parallel operation saved them. Other advice: run agents off your laptop (a Hetzner VM, Fly.io Sprites, Claude Code remote control, Docker sandboxes), version and evaluate skills (one insurer accumulated 6,000 mostly unused ones), protect prompt caching, and expect results to differ between agents even on the same model. His conclusion is that the speed-up is mainly in building, so in enterprise settings it is smaller than people expect."],"keyPoints":["Spec-anchored development keeps use cases and entity models in the same repo as UI, backend and tests, and updates existing code when specs change rather than regenerating it.","Use cases describe behaviour from an actor's perspective, not code, and can be read by business owners, testers and agents; keep them small enough for a human to review (one proof-of-concept use case reached 22 pages).","A status field on each use case (draft, approved, implemented, tested, done) drives the harness: skills refuse to implement unapproved use cases, and CI can trigger implementation on approval.","Pick frameworks that reduce review effort: jOOQ shows the executed SQL, and Vaadin's server-side UI can be tested without a browser.","Deterministic sensors block, AI sensors only warn; JUnit tests that parse the spec files enforce traceability from use case to test.","Test at the use case level (testing trophy, integration tests with a real database) and derive end-to-end tests from BPMN business processes; co-generated white-box tests add little assurance.","Vertical slices, such as self-contained systems or bounded contexts spanning UI to database, fit AI better than fine-grained microservices and micro-frontends.","Brownfield: modularise first, reverse-engineer per module, review with users of the old system, and run several reverse-engineering passes because AI may miss rare edge cases."],"takeaways":["Turn every rule you would check in a pull request into an ArchUnit test, linter rule or repo-reading JUnit test instead of a CLAUDE.md line.","Give requirements and use cases stable IDs and annotate tests with them so a test can fail when a spec is renamed or removed.","Write eval scenarios for your own skills and rerun them when models or agents change; delete skills nobody uses.","Run long agent sessions in a sandbox or remote VM rather than on your laptop.","Treat the first implemented use case in a greenfield project as harness training: inspect the output and fix skills before scaling up."],"quotes":[{"text":"The problem with software modernization is not moving from one technology to another. The problem is no one knows what the system actually does.","at":"07:37"},{"text":"AI is not a compiler, so it may generate different output anytime.","at":"11:44"},{"text":"A lot of people confuse software engineering with programming. Programming is just typing.","at":"17:27"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why spec-driven development"},{"at":"09:04","title":"Levels of spec-driven development"},{"at":"18:50","title":"The AI Unified Process"},{"at":"30:47","title":"Stack, skills and plugins"},{"at":"42:42","title":"Requirements and entity model demo"},{"at":"50:29","title":"Writing use cases for AI"},{"at":"81:21","title":"Architecture for AI development"},{"at":"88:25","title":"Implementing and testing a use case"},{"at":"110:10","title":"Guardrails and sensors"},{"at":"134:10","title":"Brownfield reverse engineering"}],"tech":["AI Unified Process","Claude Code","Claude Sonnet","GitHub Copilot","Spring Boot","Vaadin","jOOQ","Flyway","H2","PostgreSQL","Testcontainers","ArchUnit","SonarQube","Playwright","Mermaid","PlantUML","BPMN","Tessl","Agent Skills","MCP","Spring PetClinic","Docker Sandboxes","Fly.io Sprites"],"watchIf":"You build long-lived business applications or modernise legacy Java systems and want a stakeholder-readable spec process with concrete harness and traceability techniques.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Legacy modernization","Testing & quality"],"demo":true}},{"id":21657,"title":"Reflect, Transform, Accelerate: Bringing GPU Computing to Java","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["cuda","reflection","parallelism","gpu"],"abstract":["Java’s code reflection API, developed in OpenJDK Project Babylon, opens up a new path for targeting foreign programming models directly from Java, including GPUs. This talk introduces the Heterogeneous Accelerator Toolkit (HAT), a Java parallel programming framework to accelerate modern workloads on specialised hardware through its three core stages: reflect, transform, and accelerate.","The reflect phase introduces the basics of code reflection and its key concepts, making the session accessible to developers new to the API. We then show how HAT reflects Java compute code into an analysable code model, transforms that model into a GPU-friendly representation, and accelerates execution by offloading selected computations to OpenCL or CUDA.","Then, this talk shows a set of examples including tensor-core programming and matrix multiplication, fundamental workloads for AI and natural-language processing. The talk also covers performance analysis and tuning of the CUDA backend with NVIDIA Nsight Compute and Nsight Systems, from profiling generated kernels to identifying optimisation opportunities."],"favourites":131,"speakers":[{"name":"Juan Fumero","company":"Java Platform Group - Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14Ght9T258iuEoSietb6uF8idCUEZukx0rIGQMYGSWQ=s96-c","bio":"Juan Fumero, PhD, is a Software Developer in Oracle’s Java Platform Group, specializing in JIT compilation, managed runtime systems, GPU programming, and heterogeneous computing. Previously a Research Fellow at the University of Manchester, he led the development of TornadoVM, a framework for accelerating Java applications on GPUs, FPGAs, and other hardware. He holds a PhD from the University of Edinburgh and has contributed to projects with Intel, ESA, CERN, and Oracle Labs.","linkedin":"juanjosefumeroalfonso","bluesky":"jfumero.dev","twitter":"snatverk"}],"start":"2026-10-08T08:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"0-1IGpVrFLQ","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Fumero shows how HAT, built on Project Babylon's code reflection, JIT-compiles plain Java into CUDA and OpenCL kernels, and how its thread, tensor and tile APIs approach cuBLAS-level matrix multiply.","summary":["Fumero, of Oracle's Java Platform Group, opens with a demo: Llama inference written entirely in Java on HAT (Heterogeneous Accelerator Toolkit), with the whole transformer (matmul, flash attention, RMSNorm) offloaded to the GPU. It runs at about 140 tokens/s on an Apple M4 via OpenCL, against about 14 tokens/s for an unoptimised parallel Java CPU version, and about 160 tokens/s on a GeForce RTX 5060, launching some 84,000 kernels for one prompt. He contrasts two ways to reach GPUs from Java: calling native libraries through Panama FFM (fastest, but vendor-locked) and JIT-compiling Java code at runtime, which HAT does. He judges every model on three axes: performance, portability (functional and performance) and productivity.","Code reflection sits between javac's verbose AST and lossy bytecode: annotating a method with @Reflect makes javac keep a code model that can be analysed and transformed, for example by fusing a multiply and an add into an FMA. HAT turns such models into CUDA or OpenCL C, and also has a Java backend for debugging. Its SIMT thread API looks like CUDA or OpenCL. A kernel uses gix/giy/giz thread indices with a bounds guard, a compute method dispatches kernels over an NDRange, and an Accelerator created from a MethodHandles lookup runs it. Kernel code is a restricted subset of Java, with no reflection or inheritance.","On an RTX 5060 matrix multiply, measured with Nsight Compute, multithreaded CPU Java takes about 300 ms. A naive HAT kernel takes about 1,900 µs and a hand-tuned one with shared memory and register tiling about 295 µs, against 203 µs for cuBLAS in FP32. That tuned kernel is about 100 lines nobody should have to write. A tensor API (tile shape, accumulator, load, multiply-accumulate, store) maps to NVIDIA WMMA and falls back to private arrays and loop tiling on OpenCL 1.2, giving about 8x on the Mac and 274 µs on NVIDIA. A tile API modelled on NVIDIA's new cuTile reached 78 µs against 77 µs for cuTile in Python, but fell behind above 2K matrices. Nsight Compute pointed to shared-memory bank conflicts, and adding swizzling nearly closed the gap.","His conclusion is that narrowing the domain (tiles cannot express every GPU program) is what lets all three axes improve together. Next steps are formal SIMT and tile specifications that vendors can implement, better execution in plain Java for IDE debugging, and more use cases. Asked about large multi-kernel programs, he said he vibe-coded the Llama port with an AI assistant. The first version ran at 0.6 tokens/s, and reaching the final speed took his own guidance on kernel fusion and avoiding data movement."],"keyPoints":["HAT's Llama inference in pure Java: about 140 tokens/s on an Apple M4 GPU (OpenCL) and about 160 tokens/s on an RTX 5060, versus about 14 tokens/s on CPU.","Code reflection (@Reflect) preserves structure, types and names that bytecode loses, which makes code transformation simpler than the bytecode-based approach of TornadoVM, Aparapi or Sumatra.","HAT has three programming models: SIMT threads (CUDA/OpenCL style), tensors (WMMA-style tile multiply-accumulate) and tiles (modelled on NVIDIA cuTile).","Matrix multiply on an RTX 5060: CPU Java about 300 ms, naive HAT about 1,900 µs, tuned SIMT about 295 µs, tensor API 274 µs, tile API 78 µs versus cuTile Python 77 µs; cuBLAS FP32 about 203 µs.","Tensor code stays portable: on OpenCL 1.2 without tensor instructions, HAT expands it to private arrays and loop tiling, still about 8x faster than the naive version on a Mac.","Nsight Compute exposed shared-memory bank conflicts in the tile kernel; adding swizzling of the thread indices closed most of the gap to cuTile.","Kernel code is restricted Java (no reflection, no inheritance); the compute layer is less restricted and the host application is unrestricted."],"takeaways":["If you need GPU speed now and accept vendor lock-in, call native libraries such as cuBLAS through the FFM API.","Follow Project Babylon and HAT if you want portable GPU kernels in Java, and use the Java backend to debug kernels in an IDE.","Profile CUDA kernels with Nsight Compute, and when memory throughput is high check its memory section for shared-memory bank conflicts.","Judge any GPU programming model on performance, portability and productivity together, not just raw speed."],"quotes":[{"text":"If you beat cuBLAS, well done.","at":"29:04"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Llama inference demo"},{"at":"07:21","title":"Paths from Java to GPUs"},{"at":"11:47","title":"Performance, portability, productivity"},{"at":"17:26","title":"Code reflection basics"},{"at":"20:55","title":"HAT stack and thread API"},{"at":"29:04","title":"Matrix multiply performance"},{"at":"33:30","title":"Tensor API"},{"at":"39:12","title":"Tile API and swizzling"},{"at":"46:25","title":"Next steps and Q&A"}],"tech":["Project Babylon","Code reflection","HAT (Heterogeneous Accelerator Toolkit)","CUDA","OpenCL","Panama FFM API","cuBLAS","NVIDIA cuTile","NVIDIA Nsight Compute","Tensor cores / WMMA","Triton","TornadoVM","Aparapi","Llama"],"watchIf":"You want to see how close plain Java can now get to native GPU performance, and the abstractions Project Babylon proposes for getting there.","themes":["Java language & JDK","JVM performance & runtime","LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":false}},{"id":11558,"title":"BootUI, the missing developer console for Spring Boot and Quarkus","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Development Practices","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["memory usage","quarkus","developer console","spring boot"],"abstract":["This session will showcase BooUI, the developer console I have built for both Spring Boot and Quarkus. We'll detail how to:","Understand, analyse and fix your application using the \"live view\", showing what users are doing in real time","Improve the application code and architecture by using the different \"advisors\" panels","Study the application's memory usage and configure it correctly for production","This hands-on talk will show you how easy it is to install BootUI on both Spring Boot and Quarkus, so have your app ready when coming and you'll be able to benefit from it straight during the session!"],"favourites":95,"speakers":[{"name":"Julien Dubois","company":"GitHub","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-832ab0d0-7760-423f-a78e-7f767c7e95b8.jpg","bio":"Julien Dubois is a Java Champion and a Principal Manager at <a href=\"https://www.microsoft.com/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--primary-color);\">Microsoft</a>. With his team of world-class developer advocates, Julien works directly with the engineering groups to improve how developers can leverage AI using Microsoft and GitHub solutions.<br><br>With over 25 years of experience in the Java ecosystem, Julien is best known as the creator of <a href=\"https://www.jhipster.tech/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--primary-color);\">JHipster</a>, an Open Source platform with 22,000+ GitHub stars that revolutionized how developers build Spring Boot applications. His recent work focuses on bringing enterprise-grade AI capabilities to Java through his contributions to <a href=\"https://github.com/langchain4j/langchain4j\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--primary-color);\">LangChain4j</a> and <a href=\"https://spring.io/projects/spring-ai\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--primary-color);\">Spring AI</a>.<br><br>Julien is a recognized thought leader in the Java community, having spoken at over 200 international conferences including Devoxx, Spring One, and Microsoft Build. He authored <em style=\"color: var(--primary-color);\"><a href=\"https://www.julien-dubois.com/book\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Spring par la pratique</a></em>, the first French book on the Spring Framework, which became a bestseller. His presentations typically feature live-coding sessions and practical workshops that help developers immediately apply new technologies.","linkedin":"juliendubois","bluesky":null,"twitter":"juliendubois"}],"start":"2026-10-06T14:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"NkstzPeo400","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":44601,"title":"Agents Shouldn't Control Control Flow","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["agentic","workflow","compiler","context-graph"],"abstract":["Compiling governed agent workflows from an enterprise context graph","An agent's control flow should be a build artifact, not a runtime decision.","Most agent talks show how to build an agent. We ask what your architecture review will ask: who decided that this agent is allowed to call the security platform, and what happens when that policy changes?","Imagine that our goal is to assess our company’s exposure (overall risk) for a customer using an enterprise context graph as the source of truth. Think of it as a semantic CMDB of systems, capabilities, policies, permissions, and MCP tools. Then a deterministic compiler projects a business operation from that graph into LangChain4j code. The generated workflow goes through git, CI, tests, and human review like any production artifact. The process is almost 100% deterministic: the LLM agent just writes the summary report.","The LLM does not redesign production control flow at runtime. Why add hallucination risk to a workflow designed to estimate risk?","The familiar generate, review, ship pattern reacts to authoritative enterprise decisions recorded in the graph. But the changes don’t take effect until they are tested and approved.","Attendees to this intermediate talk will come away with an intro to knowledge/context graphs and the enterprise knowledge layer, plus a live demo and git repo showing the pattern in Java."],"favourites":101,"speakers":[{"name":"Jeremy Adams","company":"Neo4j","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-3277ed72-ce58-4dcf-bfa8-00762b465e5b.jpg","bio":"Jeremy one of those curious folks with both a technical and a strategic streak. Passionate about people and entrepreneurship, integration and automation. Through technical/business roles at Neo4j, Dagger, GitHub, Twistlock, and Puppet, Jeremy has both zoomed in and zoomed out a lot, acquiring an appreciation for the details and an ever-broader sense of the big architectural picture.","linkedin":"jeremy-adams-pdx","bluesky":"jeremyadams.bsky.social","twitter":"jpadamspdx"}],"start":"2026-10-08T10:55:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T11:35:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"oey2TbFvfWE","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":23529,"title":"From Autocomplete to Autopilot: How AI Is Changing the Developer Experience","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["security","governance","copilot","automation"],"abstract":["For most developers, AI assistance started as \"smart autocomplete.\" That story has changed completely.","Today, GitHub Copilot can take a request in plain language and carry it out for you — in your editor, terminal, desktop application or in the cloud while you focus on something else. We've moved from autocomplete to autopilot.","This 3-hours deep dive is built around live demos and real projects. Step by step, we'll see the many places where AI can help across your workflow: writing and understanding code in the IDE, CLI, and in the Copilot App — then moving into the software delivery lifecycle with PR review, feature creation using cloud agents, and security.","You'll also learn how to customize Copilot for your own projects and needs, using Skills, Agents, and MCP to connect it to your tools, data, and conventions — and how to automate real work with agentic workflows in plain language, across several languages and tools.","You'll leave with more than a \"wow\": concrete techniques to improve quality, control cost, and set the right guardrails, administration, and governance. Expect many demonstrations and plenty of Q&A.","What you'll learn:","Using Copilot across the IDE, CLI, App and cloud","AI in the SDLC: PR review, cloud agents, and security","Customizing with agents, skills, and custom instructions","Automating tasks with agentic workflows","Improving quality and controlling cost","Guardrails, administration, and governance for teams"],"favourites":386,"speakers":[{"name":"Tugdual Grall","company":"GitHub","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocId9nAOVVNnr7PqLAezCQ0dDnbc0KMkMVW4oMswd0yW2Iujc1Go=s96-c","bio":"Tug helps organisations across the EMEA region unlock the full potential of AI-powered development.<br><br>Tug is passionate about bringing GitHub Copilot, since 2021, to developer communities — from enterprise teams to local meetups. He regularly speaks, runs hands-on workshops and assist teams on the adoption of GitHub Copilot and AI Based SDLC.<br><br>Before joining GitHub, &quot;Tug&quot; built an extensive career in the tech ecosystem, including roles as, DevRel at Redis, <br>Technical Evangelist at MapR, MongoDB, and Couchbase, Product Manager &amp; Developer on Oracle&#39;s JavaEE platform<br><br>Deeply committed to the developer community, Tugdual is also the co-founder of the Nantes JUG (Java User Group), which has gathered developers and architects in the Nantes region monthly since 2008.<br><br>","linkedin":"tugdualgrall","bluesky":"tgrall.bsky.social","twitter":"tgrall"}],"start":"2026-10-05T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T10:30:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"pMv_39iSXVA","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"A three-hour, demo-heavy tour of Grall's move from Copilot completions to parallel autopilot sessions in the Copilot CLI and app, covering cost, review, security and governance.","summary":["Grall, at GitHub for five and a half years and a self-described passionate Java developer, opens with numbers from inside GitHub: the 10-person Copilot CLI team merging about 500 pull requests a week and averaging 1.21 releases a day, and one engineer rewriting the CLI from TypeScript to Rust in three weeks. He said that rewrite cost about $120,000 in tokens, against an estimate of 18 months to two years for a small team. He traces his own path from VS Code completions in 2021 through agent mode to the CLI and then the Copilot desktop app, because delegated tasks mean waiting, so you want parallel sessions.","He judges cost in AI credits (one credit is one cent) rather than tokens: get the output right first, then try other models, and optimise only when it is too expensive. Enterprise admins can restrict models, experimental features and the CLI per team and set monthly budgets per developer. Demos cover VS Code's agent debug log, terminal inline chat, generated commit messages, and the CLI copying Spring PetClinic to a new repo for 13 cents. He runs Autopilot ('YOLO') mode with a credit cap, git worktrees for parallel tasks, and a 'grill me' skill that questions him until the plan can serve as the spec, replacing Spec Kit for him.","After the break he shows Copilot as a pull request reviewer, configured through repository rulesets and running on GitHub Actions infrastructure, plus usage metrics available via API. He tracks PRs merged and time to merge, not lines generated. In the Copilot app he labels issues 'AI auto', schedules a session that picks them up every two minutes, works each in a worktree and opens a PR with a screenshot. One prompt rewrote PetClinic as a TypeScript REST back end with a Next.js front end, SQLite default, test ratios and a RubberDuck second-model check: 53 million input tokens, mostly cached, for $80.","On quality he says not to code this way without solid CI, and to enable Dependabot, CodeQL and secret scanning, which find issues before AI proposes fixes. He reviews large diffs with three models, re-benchmarks tools with a fixed prompt (PetClinic to .NET and Angular), and runs the Chronicle command weekly for tips from past sessions. He now puts fewer skills and instructions in place because harnesses know Java well, and keeps the remaining ones per repository. He warns that third-party skills can carry prompt injection. Closing demos: /spar debating Electron against Tauri, a vet chat assistant built on the Copilot SDK, and Canvas plugins that add custom panels."],"keyPoints":["Grall said GitHub Copilot is the largest committer on GitHub's own codebase and that every GitHub employee uses it daily.","Look at cost per interaction in credits first; optimise tokens only when quality is fine but the price is too high.","Agents increasingly prefer CLIs such as gh over MCP servers, which is more efficient and easier to control for authentication.","Git worktrees (around since 2015) are what let several agent sessions work on one repo at once; the Copilot app manages them for you.","Plan mode plus a 'grill me' skill turns a short request into a detailed plan that doubles as a functional and technical spec.","Copilot code review runs in the cloud on GitHub Actions infrastructure, can be triggered by rulesets, and follows review rules in your instruction file.","Rerun a fixed benchmark prompt on new models and harness versions; instructions and skills written six months ago are probably out of date.","Keep Autopilot for coding, but go step by step when deploying or touching production data."],"takeaways":["Ask your Copilot admin to enable the CLI and Copilot code review if they are blocked, and add review rules to your instruction file.","Use git worktrees (or a tool that manages them) to run a second agent task without stashing or recloning.","Before a complex task, run a planning session that interrogates you, and review the plan before letting the agent implement it.","Turn on Dependabot, CodeQL and secret scanning before increasing the volume of AI-generated pull requests.","Keep a standard benchmark prompt and rerun it on new models to compare quality and cost."],"quotes":[{"text":"Expensive doesn't mean anything. Expensive is compared with something.","at":"63:25"},{"text":"When I push code or when I modify the database in production, I am very, very careful. I do that step by step.","at":"59:47"},{"text":"Do not code like I code if you don't have a perfect CI.","at":"144:09"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"How GitHub builds with Copilot"},{"at":"11:23","title":"From completion to agents"},{"at":"25:28","title":"Governance and cost"},{"at":"32:26","title":"Copilot tips in the IDE"},{"at":"48:45","title":"Copilot CLI and Autopilot"},{"at":"64:51","title":"Worktrees and planning"},{"at":"100:54","title":"Cloud code review and metrics"},{"at":"113:49","title":"The Copilot desktop app"},{"at":"144:09","title":"Quality, security and benchmarks"},{"at":"162:16","title":"Spar, Copilot SDK and canvases"}],"tech":["GitHub Copilot","Copilot CLI","GitHub Copilot app","Copilot SDK","VS Code","IntelliJ IDEA","GitHub Actions","gh CLI","git worktree","Spring PetClinic","CodeQL","Dependabot","MCP","Agent skills","Spec Kit","Tauri","Electron","Next.js","SQLite","Rust","TypeScript","Playwright"],"watchIf":"Your team uses GitHub Copilot mostly for completions and chat, and you want to see the CLI, desktop app, cloud agents and admin controls in real use.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Developer experience & tooling","Security & supply chain"],"demo":true}},{"id":22947,"title":"High-Performance Java-Native AI with CUDA and the Vector API","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["java","vector","ffm","gpu"],"abstract":["Historically, high-performance AI workloads have often depended on Python ecosystems, JNI-based bindings, or complex native integrations, leaving Java as a secondary choice. But the Java platform has changed dramatically. With the Foreign Function & Memory (FFM) API, the Vector API, and modern JVM capabilities, Java is becoming a first-class platform for AI development.","In this session, we'll show how the Foreign Function & Memory API and the Vector API enable a fully Java-native AI stack capable of running efficiently on both GPUs and CPUs. Using the latest release of Deep Netts, we'll demonstrate direct CUDA integration through the FFM API—without JNI or Python—and CPU acceleration using the Vector API for SIMD execution.","We'll present the architecture behind this approach, explain key implementation decisions, and compare it with traditional JNI- and Python-based solutions. The session also includes a real-world enterprise use case: AI-powered fraud detection, where low-latency inference and predictable performance are critical for banking and payment systems.","Whether you're interested in modern Java, high-performance computing, or enterprise AI, you'll leave with a practical understanding of how FFM, the Vector API, and GPU acceleration are transforming Java into a first-class platform for building and deploying production AI applications."],"favourites":130,"speakers":[{"name":"Zoran Sevarac","company":"Deep Netts ","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-c08270b6-3020-4fc8-a6aa-395616d4bd0f.jpg","bio":"Zoran Sevarac is a seasoned software engineer, university professor of Artificial Intelligence, and passionate Java developer with more than two decades of experience in software engineering, AI research, and open-source innovation.<br>He is the creator of two pioneering AI development platforms built entirely in Java — Neuroph and Deep Netts — which made AI development more accessible to the Java community. His work bridges modern AI technologies with the reliability, performance, and scalability of the Java ecosystem.<br>A Duke’s Choice Award winner, Java Champion, and member of the JCP Executive Committee, Zoran has been instrumental in driving the evolution of AI support in Java. His mission is to simplify AI adoption, enable GPU acceleration through Project Panama and the Vector API, and bring high-performance, energy-efficient, and FinOps-friendly AI to enterprise systems.","linkedin":"zoran-sevarac-phd-49a9a411","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T08:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"8NURE3I4AJQ","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Sevarac explains how Deep Netts calls CUDA through jextract-generated FFM bindings and falls back to the Vector API on CPU, with fraud-detection benchmarks showing where each pays off.","summary":["Sevarac, of Deep Netts, argues that Java fell behind in AI because of slow numerical computing: GPU access through JNI was painful and unstable, and auto-vectorisation rarely kicked in. Two Panama APIs change that. The FFM API (final since Java 21, and very stable on Java 25 in their benchmarks) brings jextract to generate bindings from header files, Arena-scoped off-heap memory freed by try-with-resources with single- or multi-thread access, and library loading through the operating system's loader. The Vector API lets code explicitly fill SIMD registers via a VectorSpecies and loopBound, with a scalar loop for the leftovers.","He is frank about CUDA's costs: thread blocks and grids, explicit copies from heap to native to device memory and back, functions with six or more pointer and integer parameters that fail with 'bad parameter', and about ten native libraries (cuBLAS, cuDNN, CUDA Runtime and more) totalling 1.5 GB. Some have hidden platform dependencies such as zlib for cuDNN on Windows, and library names and install paths differ by OS and Linux distribution, so you either assume a matching install or bundle around 3 GB. jextract output is a raw C API full of generic pointers, so Deep Netts stacks a higher-level Java API, a tensor abstraction and an accelerator service provider interface on top, with CUDA and Vector API implementations and room for Babylon, TornadoVM or oneAPI.","The use case is fraud detection on a generated set of a million transactions, using features such as distance from home, distance from the last transaction, ratio to median purchase price, repeat retailer and online order, trained as a small feed-forward network through a fluent Java API. Enabling acceleration takes --add-modules jdk.incubator.vector or --enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED plus a single Deep Netts setting. At 1 million rows the Vector API and GPU both run about 2x faster than a single CPU thread; from 10 to 50 million rows the Vector API stays at about 2x while the GPU reaches 9 to 10x, because data transfer dominates small workloads. The trained model is saved, loaded into a Spring Boot service and scored against a probability threshold. Sevarac said Deep Netts had been open-sourced two days earlier, and that their training benchmarks via FFM matched PyTorch."],"keyPoints":["FFM API benefits over JNI: jextract-generated bindings from header files, Arena-scoped off-heap memory with try-with-resources, and OS-level library loading.","CUDA from Java is mostly a packaging problem: about ten native libraries totalling 1.5 GB, version and driver matrices, OS-specific names and paths, and hidden dependencies such as zlib on Windows.","Raw jextract bindings expose a C API with generic pointers; wrap them in a domain-specific Java layer such as a tensor class.","Deep Netts uses an accelerator service provider interface with CUDA (via FFM) and Vector API implementations, designed to add Babylon, TornadoVM or oneAPI later.","Fraud benchmark: at 1M rows Vector API and GPU are both about 2x a single CPU thread; at 10 to 50M rows the GPU is 9 to 10x and the Vector API about 2x.","Moving data between host and device memory is the main GPU bottleneck, so small workloads may not need a GPU at all.","--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED currently only removes a warning, but Sevarac expects it to become mandatory."],"takeaways":["Before buying GPUs for a small model, benchmark it on CPU with the Vector API.","Replace hand-written JNI glue with jextract bindings and Arena-scoped memory segments.","Decide early whether you will ship CUDA libraries with your application or rely on a system install, and automate library discovery per OS.","Put a Java-idiomatic abstraction between generated native bindings and your application code."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why Java lagged in AI"},{"at":"04:07","title":"FFM API features"},{"at":"08:14","title":"Vector API and CUDA model"},{"at":"14:42","title":"Pains of shipping CUDA"},{"at":"18:28","title":"CUDA via jextract"},{"at":"23:20","title":"SIMD with the Vector API"},{"at":"28:57","title":"Deep Netts accelerator architecture"},{"at":"33:08","title":"Fraud detection use case"},{"at":"40:12","title":"Benchmarks and Spring Boot"},{"at":"45:56","title":"Open source and Q&A"}],"tech":["Deep Netts","Foreign Function & Memory API","jextract","Vector API","CUDA","cuBLAS","cuDNN","CUDA Runtime","JNI","Spring Boot","PyTorch","TornadoVM","Project Babylon"],"watchIf":"You want to call native GPU libraries from Java with FFM instead of JNI, or need to know whether the Vector API is enough for your ML workload.","themes":["Java language & JDK","LLMs in Java apps","JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":false}},{"id":23524,"title":"Modern Java in Practice - The Features That Matter","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["java","structuredconcurrency","patternmatching","virtualthreads"],"abstract":["You may be on the latest Java version but are you getting the most out of it? Recent releases have added a lot of features and while some might have flown under the radar, others come together to form more than the sum of their parts. Either way, it is worth looking at the whole corpus of Java changes over the last years:","pattern matching and Data-Oriented Programming","virtual threads and structured concurrency","an on-ramp for beginners and better experiments for experts","new APIs, from stream gatherers to sequenced collections, from lazy constants to HTTP/3","improved performance, like ahead-of-time computation and compact object headers","better security and tooling - most out of the box, some on demand","And there’ll even be time to take a peak at the future and look into the current proposals for value types and string templates, deconstructible class and “withers”. After this deep dive, you’ll be ready to get the most out of the latest Java versions - or at least have good arguments to convince your tech lead to update."],"favourites":364,"speakers":[{"name":"Nicolai Parlog","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-acf609a9-0afe-496f-90db-f7bc0e57fa11.jpg","bio":"Nicolai (aka nipafx) is a Java enthusiast focused on language features, core APIs, and runtime evolution with a passion for learning and sharing. He does that mostly at conferences and in his biweekly Inside Java Newscast, but also occasionally in podcasts, live streams, articles, and books - more on all that on nipafx.dev. He&#39;s a Java Developer Advocate at Oracle and otherwise best known for his haircut.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"nipafx.dev","twitter":null},{"name":"Falk Sippach","company":"embarc Software Consulting GmbH","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-8e6d0e24-74e5-4fd0-9e2e-6cabe2b98ebe.jpg","bio":"As a software architect, consultant and trainer at embarc Software Consulting GmbH, Falk Sippach is always on the lookout for that spark of passion that he can ignite in his participants, customers and colleagues. He has been supporting mostly agile software development projects in the Java environment for 20 years. As an active part of the community (co-organizer of the JUG Darmstadt, member of iSAQB and Java Champion) he also likes to share his knowledge in articles, blog posts, as well as in presentations at conferences or user group meetings and supports the organization of various professional events.","linkedin":"falk-sippach","bluesky":"sippsack.bsky.social","twitter":"sippsack"}],"start":"2026-10-05T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T14:30:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"zC2tbq7S5UE","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Parlog and Sippach pick the Java 18-28 features that matter most, from data-oriented programming and the AOT cache to structured concurrency, post-quantum TLS, gatherers and JFR, and explain when to use each.","summary":["Instead of covering only JDK 26 and 27, Nicolai Parlog and Falk Sippach take five years of releases, roughly 18 to 28. An opening poll found most of the room on 21 or 25. Sippach starts with data-oriented programming using a medication-plan model: records as product types, a sealed Dosage interface over Tablet and Infusion as a sum type, and switch expressions with type, record, nested and unnamed patterns, when guards, dominance rules and case null. He stresses exhaustiveness: avoid default so that adding a Patch subtype breaks every switch at compile time. He contrasts Brian Goetz's DOP principles with Parlog's revised ones (model data immutably and transparently, separate operations from data). Parlog adds that primitive patterns wait on planned constant patterns.","On performance, Parlog's first answer is to upgrade. Project Lilliput's compact object headers cut headers from 96 to 64 bits (opt-in on 25, default on 27, by his account). G1 is now the default in every situation. He shows Netflix's pause-time graph after the switch to generational ZGC. Project Leyden's AOT cache stores loaded and linked classes, method profiles and now compiled code from a training run, which he said gives 50-70% faster startup for Helidon, Micronaut, Quarkus and Spring Boot hello-worlds and much faster warm-up. The training run must match production in JDK, architecture, GC and classpath, and agents that rewrite bytecode at load time limit it. The on-ramp section covers compact source files, IO.println, multi-file launching, module imports, shebang scripts and a preview JSON API that is not meant to replace Jackson.","Security covers ML-KEM and ML-DSA against harvest-now-decrypt-later attacks. JDK 27 puts a hybrid TLS 1.3 key exchange first in the default list, tunable via jdk.tls.namedGroups. Also covered: a PEM encoder/decoder API, integrity by default, and Oracle's plan to move JDK security updates from quarterly to monthly. After the break Parlog explains virtual threads as a way to raise throughput, not lower latency: don't pool them, limit concurrency with semaphores, and beware native-call pinning, file I/O and inheritable thread-local copies. Scoped values give one-way, automatically cleaned context, and StructuredTaskScope with Joiners (awaitAllSuccessfulOrThrow, anySuccessfulOrThrow, allUntil), timeouts and interrupt-based cancellation keeps fan-out inside one block. He argues this replaces most reactive code for new backends.","Sippach then covers sequenced collections, including that reversed() is a live view and that addLast on a TreeSet throws. He also covers stream gatherers (windowFixed, windowSliding, scan, fold, mapConcurrent, and a custom distinctBy), lazy constants as a replacement for double-checked locking, opt-in HTTP/3 in the HttpClient with its negotiation strategies, jwebserver, and Marc Hoffmann's Java Almanac. Parlog finishes with JDK Flight Recorder: method tracing and timing filters, jfr view, experimental CPU-time sampling, about 194 built-in events and in-process redaction of sensitive properties in 27. The last topics are Markdown Javadoc with /// comments and compiled, testable snippets pulled into Javadoc from source regions."],"keyPoints":["Use records, sealed interfaces and pattern-matching switches without a default branch, so that adding a subtype causes compile errors everywhere it has to be handled.","Compact object headers (JDK 25 opt-in, on by default in 27 according to Parlog) shrink headers from 96 to 64 bits. They usually reduce heap and GC pressure, but measure, because decompression can cost CPU.","The AOT cache records a training run (possibly a canary in production) and replays classes, profiles and compiled code. A mismatched cache just gives no speedup, it never gives wrong behavior. You cannot record and use a cache at the same time.","Virtual threads increase throughput when work is I/O-bound and downstream services can scale. They don't make a single task faster, and the next bottleneck often shows up quickly.","StructuredTaskScope ties forked subtasks to a try-with-resources block. Joiners decide when to cancel and what join() returns, and close() waits for every thread, so tasks must honor interrupts for early cancellation to work.","For thread-local context in libraries such as Log4j2's MDC, use scoped values in your own code, a library flag that switches to InheritableThreadLocal, or a custom ThreadFactory that copies the context.","JDK 27 prefers a hybrid ML-KEM TLS 1.3 key exchange by default. jdk.tls.namedGroups or per-socket SSLParameters change the order.","JFR can trace or time specific methods (for example HashMap.resize or all static initializers) via command-line filters and show the results with jfr view, at about 1% overhead with default settings."],"takeaways":["Upgrade to the latest JDK before any manual tuning, then measure with compact object headers and generational ZGC turned on.","Try an AOT cache training run for a service with slow startup or warm-up, and move load-time bytecode weaving to build time.","Replace hand-rolled thread pools for fan-out with StructuredTaskScope on virtual threads, and use semaphores where you need to cap concurrency.","Replace custom sliding-window or distinct-by-key stream code with Gatherers, and hand-written lazy singletons with lazy constants once they are final.","Start a JFR recording on a running app with jcmd <pid> JFR.start the next time you investigate a slowdown."],"quotes":[{"text":"A newer JDK is a faster JDK.","at":"46:11"},{"text":"Here, waiting is free. This cup, indeed, is most useful when it's empty.","at":"79:25"},{"text":"The closest to production is production.","at":"146:10"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Scope and version poll"},{"at":"04:06","title":"Pattern matching building blocks"},{"at":"27:14","title":"Data-oriented programming"},{"at":"36:29","title":"Performance and the AOT cache"},{"at":"47:39","title":"On-ramp and Java scripting"},{"at":"62:45","title":"Security and removals"},{"at":"78:00","title":"Virtual threads and scoped values"},{"at":"91:58","title":"Structured concurrency"},{"at":"116:46","title":"New APIs"},{"at":"147:32","title":"JFR and Javadoc"}],"tech":["Records","Sealed classes","Pattern matching","Project Amber","Compact object headers","Project Lilliput","Project Leyden","AOT cache","G1","Generational ZGC","FFM API","Vector API","Compact source files","JSON API (preview)","ML-KEM","ML-DSA","TLS 1.3","PEM API","Virtual threads","Scoped values","Structured concurrency","Log4j2","Resilience4j","Sequenced collections","Stream gatherers","Lazy constants","HttpClient","HTTP/3","jwebserver","Java Almanac","JDK Flight Recorder","JDK Mission Control","Javadoc"],"watchIf":"You are moving from Java 17 or 21 to 25 or later and want a guided tour of which features change how you write, run and secure code, with enough detail to start using them.","themes":["Java language & JDK","JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":false}},{"id":52103,"title":"The Devoxx Robot Games","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["robot games","artificial intelligence","live demos","game development"],"abstract":["For this year's Robot Games challenge, builders were asked to make a playable game starring Devoxx's robots in the Kinepolis venue, using AI as their co-developer. We received nine submissions, and several were so good that we couldn't pick a single winner. So we're handing the stage to all of them.","In this lunch session, each creator gets four minutes for a live demo and a quick look behind the scenes. What did they build, and how did they direct the AI? Which tools did they use, and what did they have to fix by hand? Expect a mix of technology and styles: 3D worlds in Three.js, hand-built 2D engines, a text-mode BBS door game, procedural chiptune music and AI-generated art. Some builders worked with Claude Code, others with Codex, Copilot or Grok.","Come to see nine very different games, learn what actually works when you build with AI, and tell us which one deserves your vote.","You can also play the games online at https://game.devoxx.be/vote.html"],"favourites":179,"speakers":[{"name":"Stephan Janssen","company":"Devoxx","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ALm5wu39iQhWBfIgxSMBGJ-tLuCnWERa6Vp5RRnnq4exBw=s96-c","bio":"With three decades of experience in the Java ecosystem and as the founder of Devoxx, one of the world&#39;s most prestigious developer conferences, Stephan brings unparalleled expertise in developer tools and workflows. His AI journey began 7+ years ago with CNNs for basketball analysis, evolving into 5 years of intensive work with GPT models. As the creator of DevoxxGenie, he&#39;s been at the forefront of AI-assisted development, building real-world applications and helping thousands of developers level up their skills.","linkedin":"stephanjanssen","bluesky":"stephanjanssen.be","twitter":"stephan007"}],"start":"2026-10-08T10:55:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T11:35:00Z","room":"Room 10","videoId":"NT0wyPM58G8","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Nine builders each get four minutes to demo browser games starring the Devoxx robots, built with AI assistants, and to say where the AI helped or failed.","summary":["Stephan Janssen, who started the contest after enjoying building a game himself, hands the stage to nine entrants. The first is a 3D puzzle game in which Voxy, Droid and Biggie combine abilities to open the venue. Its builder prototyped about 12 2D concepts first, then improved the graphics with a critic loop comparing screenshots against a commercial game. Heisenbug, built in 13 hours with Claude Code on plain Canvas JavaScript, has players read logs to find a bugged robot; the first version took one hour against the AI's one-day estimate. Devoxx Reboot uses Phaser 3, with AI-generated Tiled maps, sprite frames and a Web Audio chiptune sequencer.","Overclocked, plain JavaScript with no engine, uses flow fields for the crowd and tests written as user stories. The AI could not reproduce the venue from photos or plans, but could from room-by-room text descriptions. One entry is not a game: an Unreal Engine view of live Claude Code sessions, where hooks write a JSON file per sub-agent and a broker streams them to Unreal, so agents build booths and end in a graveyard showing their token use. Another is a text-mode BBS door game reached over a simulated dial-up line. A Copilot-built entry was reworked for mobile after the builder's daughters and strangers on a train tested it.","Please Do Not Throw Richie went from a 1,800-line ChatGPT brief to Codex for the hopping physics and Claude Code with Opus 5.5 when tokens ran low. It uses TypeScript, Vite, Rapier physics, Three.js and Sonic Pi music, runs as a PWA on GitHub Pages and hides a two-player fighting mini-game. Ghost Light, a 3D Three.js game set in 2126, lets players chat with recognisable Devoxx people. Its builder hit the same venue-layout problem and solved it zone by zone. They also noticed Claude Code had filled the rooms only with men and had to correct it."],"keyPoints":["Several builders said the AI got the real venue layout wrong from photos, drone footage and official plans; describing it in words, one small zone at a time, worked better.","Zero-dependency plain JavaScript on Canvas was a common choice under time pressure; AI first versions arrived far faster than the AI's own estimates.","AI took over traditionally slow game-dev chores: Tiled-compatible tile maps, sprite frames, pathfinding and procedural chiptune music via the Web Audio API or Sonic Pi.","Builders mixed tools and switched when tokens ran out: ChatGPT for the brief, Codex for physics, Claude Code for content, Copilot in VS Code for mobile fixes.","Claude Code hooks can feed a live visualization: one entry streams sub-agent lifecycle and token counts into an Unreal Engine scene.","Human playtesting by family, colleagues and strangers caught problems the AI did not, such as a game that did not work on mobile."],"takeaways":["Describe spatial layouts to a coding agent in text, zone by zone, rather than expecting it to read floor plans or photos.","Use Claude Code hooks writing JSON to build your own observability for sub-agent runs and token use.","Put real people in front of AI-generated software early; generated output still has to work for humans.","Check what AI-generated content assumes about people, such as who populates a scene, and correct it."],"quotes":[{"text":"The human has to be in the loop because you can generate stuff. And it's still not interesting. So in the end, it still has to work for humans.","at":"27:50"},{"text":"Then she figured out what works with me. Words, not pictures. Room by room, seat by seat.","at":"15:16"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why a robot games contest"},{"at":"01:27","title":"3D robot puzzle game"},{"at":"04:45","title":"Heisenbug"},{"at":"07:41","title":"Devoxx Reboot in Phaser"},{"at":"11:18","title":"Overclocked"},{"at":"15:16","title":"Claude Code agents in Unreal"},{"at":"19:14","title":"Text-mode BBS door game"},{"at":"24:58","title":"Copilot-built mobile game"},{"at":"29:36","title":"Please Do Not Throw Richie"},{"at":"33:43","title":"Ghost Light"}],"tech":["Claude Code","Claude Code hooks","Codex","ChatGPT","GitHub Copilot","Gemini","Three.js","Phaser 3","Tiled","HTML Canvas","Web Audio API","Unreal Engine","TypeScript","Vite","Rapier","Sonic Pi","GitHub Pages","PWA"],"watchIf":"You want a light lunch session showing how developers who are not game developers used coding agents to build working games, and where the AI fell short.","themes":["Hardware, robots & fun","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":true}},{"id":49257,"title":"Embabel Agent v1.5","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["deterministic","spring","embabel","goap"],"abstract":["Most agent frameworks ask a language model to decide what to do next. That works, until it doesn't — and when it doesn't, you're left with a non-reproducible trace and no way to explain what happened. Embabel takes the opposite approach.","Created by Rod Johnson — who calls it the most important project since founding Spring — Embabel is a JVM-native agent framework built on Spring AI and Spring Boot. Its core is Goal-Oriented Action Planning (GOAP), an algorithm borrowed from game AI: you declare goals, annotate actions with @Action and @AchievesGoal, and a deterministic, non-LLM planner chains them by pre- and post-conditions to reach the goal. The plan is code-generated, inspectable before it runs, and explainable afterwards. If Spring AI and LangChain4j are the Servlet API of GenAI, Embabel is Spring MVC.","Full presentation details pending."],"favourites":194,"speakers":[{"name":"Rod Johnson","company":"Embabel","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-8bd19dbe-7c4f-4457-970d-dcdcaeea60b0.jpg","bio":"Rod Johnson is a developer, author, entrepreneur and advisor. He is the creator of the Spring Framework and Embabel agent framework.","linkedin":"johnsonroda","bluesky":null,"twitter":"springrod"}],"start":"2026-10-09T08:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"Rod Johnson presents Embabel, a JVM agent framework on Spring AI that uses a deterministic GOAP planner instead of an LLM to choose the next action.","summary":["Rod Johnson introduces Embabel, a JVM-native agent framework built on Spring AI and Spring Boot. It takes the opposite approach to frameworks that ask a language model to decide what to do next, which can leave non-reproducible traces that are hard to explain.","Its core is Goal-Oriented Action Planning (GOAP), borrowed from game AI: developers declare goals and annotate actions with @Action and @AchievesGoal, and a deterministic non-LLM planner chains them by pre- and post-conditions. The plan is inspectable before it runs and explainable afterwards. The listing notes that full session details were still pending."],"keyPoints":["Embabel is built on Spring AI and Spring Boot.","Planning uses GOAP, a deterministic algorithm rather than an LLM.","Actions are annotated with @Action and @AchievesGoal.","Plans can be inspected before execution and explained after."],"takeaways":["Consider deterministic planning where you need reproducible, explainable agent behaviour."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Embabel","Spring AI","Spring Boot","GOAP"],"watchIf":"You build agents on the JVM and need reproducible, explainable behaviour.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Spring & frameworks"],"demo":false}},{"id":21694,"title":"Why it takes 3000 commits to bring Valhalla's value objects to the JDK","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["valueobjects","valhalla","openjdk","javavm"],"abstract":["After years of rounding off a square peg to fit into Java's mainline, JEP 401's value objects are coming to OpenJDK in its first preview. It is a single keyword change, you don't even need to recompile your libraries, yet there's so much happening. What you can learn here:","What value objects are, why and how you want to use them","What value objects do for the Java Virtual Machine, and therefore your code","Where Is OpenJDK going with this: \"healing the rift\" between objects and primitives","There will be some project history, concerning peak Valhalla prototype performance, and how all the dangerous bits were smoothed off to make it usable. There will be some JVM engineering details on implementation and a peek at some wild and crazy ideas for the future, hoping to be a few thesis papers in the coming months. The audience should come away with an understanding that, whilst we don't quite yet have `List<int>`, there is a path to get there, which you will see with JEP 401.","In the meantime, JEP 401 Value Objects, holds plenty of subtle language features and interactions with the JVM to help your code be better from a safety and clarity point of view and also some performance wins along the way, with the promise of more to come..."],"favourites":183,"speakers":[{"name":"David Simms","company":"Java Platrform Group - Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocIMYsGAPqwvdrbippzL2I0_rzyyLfPCLWgDp-0oAPQcJdnD5-Pcfg=s96-c","bio":"Hands on Java Virtual Machine engineering for over 25 years, first with the JRockit VM and then Hotspot JVM. Involved in Project Valhalla over 10 years now, I might have filed an almost 3000 commit PR to integrate the first really big step in getting Valhalla into OpenJDK. On the side I manage Oracle&#39;s Java Platform Group engineering site in Stockholm Sweden.<br><br>&quot;Kiwede&quot; - New Zealander living in Sweden","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"simmsupnorth.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-09T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T08:20:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"David Simms explains what JEP 401 value objects are, what they do for the JVM, and the path from Valhalla toward healing the rift between objects and primitives.","summary":["With JEP 401 value objects arriving in OpenJDK as a first preview, David Simms from the Java Platform Group sets out to explain what they are, why and how to use them, and what they do for the JVM and for your code. The change is a single keyword and does not require recompiling libraries.","The talk includes project history, including peak Valhalla prototype performance and how dangerous edges were smoothed off, some JVM implementation details, and speculative future ideas. The message is that while List<int> is not here yet, JEP 401 is a path toward it, bringing safety and clarity benefits and some performance wins."],"keyPoints":["JEP 401 value objects arrive as a first preview, enabled by one keyword.","Libraries do not need to be recompiled.","Goal is to heal the rift between objects and primitives.","Covers project history and JVM implementation details.","List<int> is a longer-term goal."],"takeaways":["Try JEP 401 preview builds on small immutable types in your code."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Project Valhalla","JEP 401","OpenJDK","JVM"],"watchIf":"You want to understand Valhalla value objects from someone working on the JVM implementation.","themes":["Java language & JDK","JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":false}},{"id":21666,"title":"Teaching an Old Handle New Tricks: From Core Reflection to FFM","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["method handles","java runtime","reflection","var handles"],"abstract":["Core reflection has been a cornerstone of Java since Java SE 1.1. It provides a unified model for inspecting classes, invoking methods and accessing fields. But reflection is also inefficient. Every reflective call must check access, handle argument conversions like boxing and, finally, invoke the target.","Method handles, introduced in Java 7, address this problem with an execution model that closely mirrors the JVM's. A method handle is one resolved executable operation: a typed capability whose access rights and type are established up front. In Java 9, var handles extended this model to fields and array elements.","In this talk, we’ll show how this trick—moving expensive, one-off setup work out of the hot path—has paid dividends across the Java platform. Method and var handles now sit at the core of many modern runtime features, making string concatenation more efficient, revolutionizing native interop with the Foreign Function & Memory API, and enabling serialization to be redesigned on safer foundations.","This is both an advanced tour of an overlooked part of the Java runtime and a story of resilient platform design: how the right hammer keeps finding new nails."],"favourites":150,"speakers":[{"name":"Maurizio Cimadamore","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-66579526-c2c5-46b5-87ed-6bb6f34121af.jpg","bio":"Maurizio Cimadamore is a compiler architect at Oracle. He is currently working on the language and runtime aspects of many foundational projects that will change the shape of the Java platform, such as Project Valhalla and Project Panama.<br>In the previous years, he has contributed to the design and implementation of several features of the Java programming language, such as diamond inference, lambda expressions and local variable type-inference. Maurizio holds a PhD in language design and implementation.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Viktor Klang","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AAcHTtdkY-Lks0gsRcvl05pqpW8-GOpvuVndojO99ZxUsSy0rgo=s96-c","bio":"Viktor is a Software Architect in the Java Platform Group at Oracle.<br><br>For more than two decades, he has focused on enabling developers to solve problems with maximal productivity and maintainability.<br><br>Specializing in developer ergonomics in the realm of concurrent, parallel, and distributed programming; he has contributed to the standard libraries of several major programming languages, been involved in more than a dozen Open Source projects, and has spoken at numerous conferences and universities.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"viktorklang.com","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T13:10:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T14:00:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"4ck-T2gaMQ4","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Cimadamore and Klang explain method handles as 'link once, execute many' and trace that idea through reflection, string concatenation, FFM downcalls and a planned marshalling API.","summary":["Cimadamore starts from core reflection. Calling Point.translate via Method.invoke boxes arguments into an Object array, checks access through @CallerSensitive, unboxes and only then calls, on every invocation; his benchmark put it at about a third of a direct call's throughput. The JVM splits a call into linkage (resolution, access check, vtable index) and execution, and method handles, added in Java 7 with invokedynamic, expose that split. A Lookup checks access once at creation; invokeExact is signature-polymorphic, so javac burns the static argument and cast types into the call site, leaving a cheap type check. Since Java 18, java.lang.reflect.Method wraps a method handle, closing most of the gap.","The same pattern repeats. Pre-Java 9 string concatenation compiled to StringBuilder chains whose default capacity of 16 always resized for his 21-character example. Since Java 9, javac emits an invokedynamic call to StringConcatFactory.makeConcatWithConstants with a recipe; the generated code precomputes the constant length and coder, then sizes one byte array exactly, which he said made concatenation about twice as fast. In the Foreign Function & Memory API, a downcall handle points not at bytecode but at an assembly trampoline generated once from a FunctionDescriptor of memory layouts, following the platform ABI. jextract can generate the wrappers so users never touch the handles.","Klang then shows his exploratory work on marshalling as a successor to serialization: a class declares explicit structural versions as records (PointV1) that map to and from the real type. A naive reflective marshaller redoes type inspection and access checks for every object; instead, a schema with marshal and unmarshal method handles is generated once per type and cached, and his rough benchmark favoured handles by a wide margin. With Project Leyden, that linkage could move to a training run. In Q&A they named string templates as the next likely user, said combinator-heavy handles generate bloat so the JDK often spins classes with the class-file API, and mentioned Project Babylon."],"keyPoints":["Method.invoke boxes arguments, checks access via @CallerSensitive and unboxes on every call; a method handle does the access check once through MethodHandles.Lookup.","invokeExact is signature-polymorphic: javac encodes the static argument types and the cast return type into the call site descriptor, so no boxing or varargs array is needed.","A static final method handle is a JIT constant, so the remaining type check is effectively a pointer comparison.","Since Java 18, core reflection is reimplemented on top of method handles, so reflective calls reuse the same C2 optimisations.","Indy string concatenation (Java 9) uses makeConcatWithConstants with a recipe string, precomputing constant length and encoding (coder), then filling one exactly sized byte array.","FFM downcall handles are built from a symbol address and a FunctionDescriptor; the ABI classification and assembly trampoline are generated once at creation.","Klang's marshalling prototype caches a per-type schema with marshal and unmarshal method handles; with Project Leyden this linking could happen at training time.","Combinator-heavy method handle trees generate a lot of hidden bytecode; JDK code such as string concatenation moved to spinning one class with the class-file API instead."],"takeaways":["Store method handles in static final fields and call them with invokeExact using exact static types and a cast on the result.","In your own framework code, separate one-time setup (lookup, type analysis, access checks) from the per-call path and cache the result.","Use jextract to generate FFM bindings instead of writing downcall handles by hand.","Prefer generating a single class with the class-file API over deep method handle combinator chains when the shape is complex."],"quotes":[{"text":"It was really the one move that made the next thousand possible.","at":"01:46"},{"text":"Once you see this shape of the problem, it's really hard to unsee that. So you don't have to use the method handles. We'll use them for you.","at":"39:53"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Link once, execute many"},{"at":"03:08","title":"Cost of core reflection"},{"at":"07:26","title":"Method handles and invokeExact"},{"at":"15:12","title":"String concatenation"},{"at":"21:32","title":"FFM downcall handles"},{"at":"28:09","title":"Marshalling with method handles"},{"at":"39:53","title":"Stewardship lesson"},{"at":"41:19","title":"Q&A"}],"tech":["Method handles","VarHandles","Core reflection","invokedynamic","StringConcatFactory","Foreign Function & Memory API","jextract","Class-File API","Project Leyden","Project Babylon","String templates"],"watchIf":"You write frameworks, serializers or native interop code and want to understand why the JDK keeps reaching for method handles and how to get their performance.","themes":["Java language & JDK","JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":false}},{"id":23536,"title":"Ask the Architects","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["engineers","runtime","oracle","programming"],"abstract":["Ask the Oracle engineers anything related to your favourite programming language & runtime!"],"favourites":160,"speakers":[{"name":"Gavin Bierman","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-81f196b1-a89f-4d7a-85d3-5e7e4e171a24.jpg","bio":"I work in the Java Platform Group at Oracle designing the Java programming language and virtual machine. I am also a member of the Programming Languages Research Group at Oracle Labs. I am based in Cambridge, UK.<br>My mission is to keep Java as a leading language and platform for developers around the world. Given my background as a researcher, I am especially keen to find, adapt and transfer ideas and techniques from the research community and get them into the hands of Java programmers. I spend much of my time working on Java’s industry-leading specifications.<br><br>I joined Oracle in March 2014. Previously I was a senior researcher at Microsoft Research Cambridge from March 2004 until February 2014. Before that I was a University Lecturer at the University of Cambridge Computer Laboratory and a Fellow, College Lecturer and Director of Studies at St John’s College, Cambridge. I studied for my PhD at the University of Cambridge, and for my BSc at Imperial College, London.","linkedin":"gavin-bierman-a0173075","bluesky":null,"twitter":"GavinBierman"},{"name":"Maurizio Cimadamore","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-66579526-c2c5-46b5-87ed-6bb6f34121af.jpg","bio":"Maurizio Cimadamore is a compiler architect at Oracle. He is currently working on the language and runtime aspects of many foundational projects that will change the shape of the Java platform, such as Project Valhalla and Project Panama.<br>In the previous years, he has contributed to the design and implementation of several features of the Java programming language, such as diamond inference, lambda expressions and local variable type-inference. Maurizio holds a PhD in language design and implementation.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Per-Ake Minborg","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-a388bc3b-5180-411a-a4a7-5631e4341892.jpg","bio":"Per Minborg is a manager in the Java Core Library team at Oracle and is also a member of the Panama project team. He is an inventor and developer with more than 25 years of Java coding experience. He has been a frequent contributor to various open-source projects, is a regular speaker at events, Oracle JavaOne alumnus, and co-author of the publication Modern Java.","linkedin":"per-%C3%A5ke-minborg-448a623b6/?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Ad_flagship3_feed%3B4pm%2B8nsET3GGj6bkmM3geg%3D%3D","bluesky":"pminborg.bsky.social","twitter":null},{"name":"Viktor Klang","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AAcHTtdkY-Lks0gsRcvl05pqpW8-GOpvuVndojO99ZxUsSy0rgo=s96-c","bio":"Viktor is a Software Architect in the Java Platform Group at Oracle.<br><br>For more than two decades, he has focused on enabling developers to solve problems with maximal productivity and maintainability.<br><br>Specializing in developer ergonomics in the realm of concurrent, parallel, and distributed programming; he has contributed to the standard libraries of several major programming languages, been involved in more than a dozen Open Source projects, and has spoken at numerous conferences and universities.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"viktorklang.com","twitter":null},{"name":"Alan Bateman","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-777f0d6e-3ce7-4670-9962-7f3e5f309f46.jpg","bio":"Alan Bateman is an architect in the Java Platform Group at Oracle and a long-time contributor to OpenJDK. He leads the OpenJDK Core Libraries Group and has helped shape the evolution of the Java platform through the design and implementation of many JDK features, including New I/O (NIO), JVMTI and serviceability, the Java Module System, and, more recently, Project Loom.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Georges Saab","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-e81f731a-26bb-4e81-803c-4252372b3e61.jpg","bio":"Georges Saab is the Senior Vice President of Development for the Java Platform Group at Oracle. His group is responsible for Java Standard Edition including the Java language, core libraries, and the Java Virtual Machine. Georges is a 35 year veteran of programming language and platform development. His work with the Java platform began as a developer of Java Standard Edition at JavaSoft and Sun Microsystems, where he was a founder of the Swing group and Java Webstart, and continued as he ran development of the JRockit JVM for many years at BEA Systems. Georges is the Chair of the OpenJDK governing board.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":"gsaab"}],"start":"2026-10-08T11:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T12:40:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"ewCPBgOrLAE","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"An Oracle Java architects panel answers audience questions on virtual thread schedulers, deprecation policy, AI code generation, string internals, Valhalla, modules, string templates and ZGC automatic heap sizing.","summary":["Moderated by Nicolai Parlog, the panel included Alan Bateman, Gavin Bierman, Maurizio Cimadamore, Viktor Klang, Per-Ake Minborg, Georges Saab, David Simms and Ron Pressler. First up: custom schedulers for virtual threads. Bateman said there are prototypes in the Loom repository but no plan to expose an API, since locking, timers and socket code calling into user schedulers is risky; carrier pool size is the only knob. Klang argued per-library pools end up costing more in handovers than they gain in priority. Francesco Nigro of Red Hat, a Netty committer, described a year of experimenting with the scheduler API and asked for real use cases on loom-dev.","On deprecation, Bateman said removals are rare and data-driven, using static analysis of artifacts from Maven Central; examples were the Java EE modules, Nashorn and Thread.stop, which was re-specified to throw. On AI, panelists said frontier labs complain they train on old Java because modern code is scarce, and urged developers to send feedback through tools such as Codex's /feedback. One panelist said a Java 25 rewrite of generated programs used about 20% fewer tokens than Java 8 style, and several argued that features which express intent, like records, guard agents as well as humans. Javadoc's upcoming @note tag and code snippets were mentioned as useful to agents.","Minborg and Cimadamore warned against zero-copy string views, which could let native code modify immutable strings, but noted JDK 27 copies string bytes straight into memory segments. Simms said JEP 401 does not specify flattening, so no layout is guaranteed, though VM debug flags such as PrintInlineLayout show it; there is no timeline for specialized or reified generics. Cimadamore said string templates were withdrawn because nested templates forced eager double processing, and a better design is in progress. Module usability and builder-style record construction are being explored but not yet public. Neighbour-aware automatic heap sizing is coming to ZGC, then G1, but ZGC will not become the default soon."],"keyPoints":["No public custom scheduler API for virtual threads is planned; only the carrier thread count and range are configurable, and the experimental API lives in the Loom repository.","Deprecation decisions use corpus analysis of open-source artifacts; the JDK collects no runtime telemetry, only opt-in telemetry from the Oracle VS Code extension.","Panelists said model vendors know they train on outdated Java and want developer feedback on generated code; one cited about 20% fewer tokens for Java 25-style code than Java 8-style.","JDK 27 removed intermediate buffers when copying strings into FFM memory segments, but a direct memory-segment view of a string's bytes is considered unsafe.","JEP 401 value classes deliberately leave flattening and scalarization out of the spec; VM diagnostic flags can print the actual layouts.","String templates were withdrawn because nested templates were processed eagerly, which prevents a link-once validation pass; a reworked proposal is in progress.","Automatic heap sizing for ZGC sizes the heap dynamically based on allocation rate, CPU use and other processes on the machine; G1 support is planned."],"takeaways":["Report concrete virtual thread scheduling problems, especially Netty or event-loop interop, to the loom-dev mailing list instead of reaching into internals reflectively.","Use your AI tool's feedback channel when it generates outdated Java idioms.","Try ZGC for your services; the panel recommended it for most Java programs.","Bring specific string or memory performance problems to the relevant OpenJDK mailing list with the use case, rather than asking for a particular API."],"quotes":[{"text":"After one year, I just understood why the ForkJoinPool does what it does.","at":"06:51"},{"text":"Our goal is not to have you use the features. Our goal is to have the features work for you.","at":"38:53"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Panel introductions"},{"at":"01:47","title":"Custom virtual thread schedulers"},{"at":"08:17","title":"Deprecation and removal"},{"at":"17:17","title":"Java for AI agents"},{"at":"28:45","title":"Zero-copy strings"},{"at":"36:43","title":"Valhalla and modules"},{"at":"43:07","title":"String templates and records"},{"at":"47:07","title":"ZGC heap sizing"}],"tech":["Virtual threads","Project Loom","Netty","Project Valhalla","JEP 401","Foreign Function & Memory API","Java Platform Module System","String templates","Javadoc","ZGC","G1"],"watchIf":"You want the JDK architects' current thinking on virtual thread schedulers, Valhalla, string templates and ZGC, straight from the people building them.","themes":["Java language & JDK","JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":false}},{"id":50103,"title":"Let's dance! – Teaching your robot some moves with reinforcement learning","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["imitation","simulation","humanoid","reinforcement"],"abstract":["Has this ever happened to you: You just spent €50,000 on a humanoid","robot, but it's not very fun at parties, so you have to train it to","dance using reinforcement learning in a simulation environment on your","€100,000 GPU cluster? What do you mean \"NO\"?","In this talk, Thomas Endres and David Winderl will teach you how you can","train your humanoid robot to dance using Reinforcement Learning (RL) in","simulation environments. They're gonna kick things off with an","introduction into RL, covering the basics of policies and rewards. After","that, they’ll take their first steps in simulation environments with the","introduction of NVIDIA Isaac Sim and the Unitree RL Lab. Our speakers","will run through the steps of shaping a walking policy, one hilarious","fall at a time. Finally, they're going to shake things up with a dancing","policy: You will learn how you can retarget human motion from videos to","the Unitree G1 robot and train an imitation policy that can dance just","as poorly as you.","For the grande finale, our humanoid robot will close the session with a","dancing performance, not missing out on a single step."],"favourites":114,"speakers":[{"name":"Thomas Endres","company":"TNG Technology Consulting GmbH","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AATXAJxWxubT7g3QJfDNjLG2FdVGAtl_5CZk3Jf_ukgT=s96-c","bio":"Thomas Endres is a Managing Partner for TNG Technology Consulting in Munich. Besides his normal work for the company and the management of the customers he is creating various prototypes, including real-time deepfakes, an AI for generating presentations or various robotics showcases. He works on applications in the field of AI, robotics, AR/VR and teleoperation. Furthermore, he is involved in Open Source projects.<br>Thomas studied computer science at TU Munich and is a passionate software developer. As an Intel Software Innovator and Black Belt, he presents new technologies such as AI, AR/VR, and robotics worldwide. For this, he has received awards including a JavaOne Rockstar Award and several Best Speaker Awards.","linkedin":"thomasendres","bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Martin F.","company":"TNG Technology Consulting GmbH","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocKHzZww6LQp4Nh3bXnAyL3Vj9gaVLvqOiYx9PNf9fGoF_t3f1Pt=s96-c","bio":"Martin Förtsch is an IT consultant at TNG Technology Consulting GmbH, located in Unterföhring near Munich. He holds a degree in computer sciences. Professionally, he specializes in Agile Development, primarily in Java, Search Engine Technologies, Information Retrieval, and Databases. As an Intel Software Innovator and Intel Black Belt Software Developer, Martin plays an active role in the creation of innovative tech-demos and showcases alongside the Innovation Hacking team.<br><br>In the past, Martin developed software for gesture-controlled quadcopters using Intel RealSense technology and designed an Augmented Reality wearable prototype device. He frequently presents at both national and international conferences on topics such as Artificial Intelligence, Internet of Things, Augmented Reality, and Test Driven Development. He earned multiple accolades, including the Oracle JavaOne Rockstar award.","linkedin":"martinfoertsch","bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Tobias Lübbe","company":"TNG Technology Consulting GmbH","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-54036c9e-b768-4f44-aeed-497f9307443c.jpg","bio":"Tobias Lübbe is a Software Consultant in TNG’s Innovation Hacking team. His work focuses on Physical AI and the development of innovative robotics showcases. Currently, he is exploring the use of Robot Foundation Models (RFMs) across different robotic systems. Before joining TNG, he studied Mechatronics at the Karlsruhe Institute of Technology (KIT) and Robotics, Cognition, Intelligence at the Technical University of Munich (TUM).","linkedin":"/tobias-luebbe","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T11:05:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T11:45:00Z","room":"Room 10","videoId":"gPtWq_LHL8o","twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A lunch talk on training a Unitree G1 humanoid robot to walk and dance with reinforcement learning in NVIDIA Isaac Sim, ending with a live dance.","summary":["The TNG speakers introduce reinforcement learning basics, policies and rewards, then move into simulation with NVIDIA Isaac Sim and the Unitree RL Lab. They run through shaping a walking policy before turning to dancing.","For the dancing policy, the talk covers retargeting human motion from videos to the Unitree G1 robot and training an imitation policy. The session ends with the humanoid robot performing a dance."],"keyPoints":["Introduction to reinforcement learning: policies and rewards.","Simulation with NVIDIA Isaac Sim and Unitree RL Lab.","Shaping a walking policy first.","Retargeting human video motion to the Unitree G1 for imitation learning.","Closes with a live robot dance."],"takeaways":["Explore Isaac Sim and Unitree RL Lab to experiment with robot policies in simulation."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Reinforcement learning","NVIDIA Isaac Sim","Unitree RL Lab","Unitree G1"],"watchIf":"You want a light, visual introduction to reinforcement learning with a humanoid robot.","themes":["Hardware, robots & fun"],"demo":false}},{"id":31452,"title":"Responsibly Dimming the Lights in the Software Factory","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["cognitive debt","ai-generated code","code review","automation"],"abstract":["The most agentified engineering teams of my clients are all converging towards the same workflow, aiming to optimize the code review step while preventing human cognitive debt and skill erosion. This talk summarizes their practices, teaching actionable steps and milestones to gradually improve the quality of AI-generated code up to a point in which you can safely start decreasing the code review ‘intensity’. Dark Factories are not built ground up - instead, lights are responsibly dimmed, then turned off one by one, area by area, after observing impeccable code produced consistently."],"favourites":502,"speakers":[{"name":"Victor Rentea","company":"Freelance","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-51aee41f-0079-4820-98bb-56e70a2e89d3.jpg","bio":"With 2 decades of software engineering experience, Victor spent the last 10 years training thousands of developers and architects from 150+ companies. Since early 2026, he’s been working hard to help his clients adopt Agentic Engineering, coaching AI Champions, developers, testers, product owners, ops, and managers with practical, actionable advice to kick-start their agentic reconversion. Every year, Victor gives energetic, intense conference talks in which he distills his training sessions - so be sure not to miss these ideas, heavily brainstormed with millennia of total experience. More about him on<a href=\"https://victorrentea.ro\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\"> </a><u style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(17, 85, 204);\"><a href=\"https://victorrentea.ro\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">https://victorrentea.ro</a></u>, and <u style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(17, 85, 204);\"><a href=\"https://agentic.how\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">https://agentic.how</a></u>.","linkedin":"victor-rentea-trainer","bluesky":null,"twitter":"victorrentea"}],"start":"2026-10-06T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T14:30:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"C10mZ3pCnGo","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Rentea takes a vague ticket to a PR with grilling, OpenSpec and adversarial AI review, arguing human review can be dimmed only gradually, after guardrails and months of good output.","summary":["Victor Rentea says he typed code for 19 years and stopped six months ago; his slides run on an app he vibe-coded that morning. After recalling StrongDM's 'dark factory' that no longer reviews code, he works through a Spring Pet Clinic with an Angular front end. An agent fixes a validation bug filed by QA and shows the Playwright trace. A vague human ticket, adding sorting and pagination to the owners grid, then gets the full treatment. First warm up your own brain, then run Matt Pocock's grill-me skill against a top model, treating it like an experienced architect you must not approach unprepared.","His grilling rules: when offered A or B, push back with context the agent cannot know, such as 100,000 expected owners, and save it to AGENTS.md; when it asks something odd, give it your tools, like a database or browser CLI, instead of dictating. He then uses OpenSpec propose and tailors its skills: the proposal is rewritten for a product owner, technical detail moves to design.md for engineers, and specs go to QA, so business, QA and developer form the 'three amigos'. Implementation runs unattended with a hook that waits for green CI. Rentea puts the feature plus live UI polish at about $17.","The core rule for review: no human reviews AI code before a second AI does. A fresh-context, adversarial reviewer (or several, like Claude's /simplify) avoids the maker's sunk-cost bias; findings are auto-fixed or rejected as nitpicks. Static analysis, Git hooks, stop hooks and CODEOWNERS-protected workflows sit in front. Ask the agent to list the assumptions it made with confidence scores, review in sessions of about 45 minutes, and use stacked PRs. When bad code slips through, upgrade the skills, reviewers or guardrails. After months of consistently good PRs on low-risk code, review intensity can be halved, then halved again: a grey factory, not a dark one.","The last hour covers testing and legacy. Acceptance-level and sociable tests matter most; mock-heavy solitary tests are low value. He proposes counting coverage only for tests a human has read and, following StrongDM, keeping a holdout set of acceptance tests the agent never sees. He also recommends characterization tests from recorded production traffic, shadow deployments, cleaning up unfinished migrations so agents don't copy bad patterns, showing a hand-refactored example instead of describing it, and OpenRewrite recipes for syntax-safe transformations. He insists everyone should build their own workflow rather than adopt a central 'software factory'."],"keyPoints":["Warm up your own brain with questions before asking a frontier model to grill you; offered A or B, answer with context the agent couldn't know and record it in AGENTS.md.","Treat a bad agent question as missing tools or knowledge: hand it a database CLI or a Playwright/browser CLI rather than dictating the answer.","Tailor community workflows (OpenSpec, Matt Pocock's skills, superpowers) to your team: a non-technical proposal for the business, design.md for engineers, specs reviewed by QA; reserve this ceremony for the roughly 20% of tickets that deserve it.","Humans should not review AI-generated code before a second, fresh-context, adversarial AI has; then have the agent list its own assumptions with confidence scores so humans review the risky parts first.","Put deterministic guardrails before review (Sonar or similar, Git hooks, stop hooks, CODEOWNERS-protected CI files) and keep human review sessions to about 45 minutes, using stacked PRs for large changes.","When defects slip through, upgrade the grill skill, review.md, custom reviewer agents and guardrails; only after months of consistently good PRs on low-risk code should review intensity be reduced step by step.","Count acceptance-test coverage only for tests a human has read, and consider a holdout set of acceptance tests the agent never sees, as Rentea says StrongDM did.","For legacy work: finish half-done migrations, show a hand-polished refactoring as the example, use OpenRewrite recipes, and constrain big refactors (for example, generated SQL must stay byte-identical)."],"takeaways":["Run an adversarial AI review in a fresh context on every agent-written change before a human looks at it.","Ask your agent at the end of each task which decisions it took on its own, and review those first.","Fork a spec-driven workflow like OpenSpec and edit its skills to fit your team instead of using it as-is.","Turn every escaped defect into an upgrade of a skill, reviewer prompt or guardrail rather than a one-off fix.","Prefer sociable or acceptance tests you have actually read over large numbers of mock-based unit tests."],"quotes":[{"text":"Humans should not review AI-generated code before a second AI does it.","at":"95:08"},{"text":"I vibe, you vibe, we don't vibe in production.","at":"64:38"},{"text":"Every time AI asks stupid questions, does a mistake, your first reaction is: what did your prodigy child not know?","at":"34:57"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"From Copilot to dark factories"},{"at":"05:52","title":"Fixing a bug from Jira"},{"at":"12:45","title":"Warming up and grill-me"},{"at":"40:03","title":"Spec-driven with OpenSpec"},{"at":"61:10","title":"Three amigos and teach-me mode"},{"at":"72:06","title":"Agent context and business PRs"},{"at":"93:40","title":"Adversarial review and guardrails"},{"at":"117:17","title":"Review practices and dimming"},{"at":"132:57","title":"Testing in the AI era"},{"at":"144:48","title":"Legacy refactoring and recap"}],"tech":["Claude Code","Codex","GitHub Copilot","OpenSpec","Playwright","Spring Pet Clinic","Angular","Spring Data","PostgreSQL","Flyway","SonarQube","Git hooks","OpenRewrite","CodeGraphContext","Language Server Protocol","Jira"],"watchIf":"Your team ships agent-written code and is debating how much human review it still needs, and you want a concrete, step-by-step workflow rather than a slogan.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Testing & quality","Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":true}},{"id":49255,"title":"Modernizing legacy code with AI: hands-on examples & lessons learned","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["migration","jakarta","modernization","legacy"],"abstract":["AI helps developers write new software faster. But can it also help modernize legacy applications?","In this session, Smals Research shares practical experience from real e-government projects. Through the migration of ageing Java applications from Java EE to Jakarta EE, the speakers explore how AI supports software modernization. Topics include namespace changes, deprecated APIs, outdated dependencies and complex build configurations.","The session highlights where AI genuinely accelerates migration work, where it falls short, and how teams can keep modernization efforts reliable and verifiable. Drawing on additional experience with other legacy environments, including COBOL, the speakers also share practical approaches for documenting, rewriting and maintaining legacy code with AI assistance.","Whether you're modernizing an existing application or exploring AI-assisted development, this session offers a realistic view of what AI can and cannot do today."],"favourites":527,"speakers":[{"name":"Koen Vanderkimpen","company":"Smals","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-3a8f8c33-a595-4df3-b4d0-064456a44f0e.jpg","bio":"<strong>Koen Vanderkimpen </strong>is a Computer Science Engineer and Research Consultant at Smals who bridges the gap between practical software application and AI research. He evaluates innovative cloud technologies and architectural patterns—such as Event-Driven Architecture—that help the organization build robust, scalable systems.<br>Recently, his focus has shifted to the intersection of agentic AI and legacy code, using LLM’s to &quot;time travel in the terminal&quot; and modernize outdated, critical systems. A strong believer in applied research, Koen continuously evaluates new tools and best practices, bringing a grounded approach to AI-assisted coding and architectural refactoring.<br><br>Koen works at Smals, an association of over 300 public institutions in Belgium, delivering shared IT services and acting as their joint in-house ICT organization, offering skills, best practices, economies of scale, and software re-use. For over 85 years, Smals has supported social security and healthcare institutions with innovative IT and infrastructure services.<br> <br> ","linkedin":"koen-vanderkimpen-6b24545","bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Wim Lambrecht","company":"Smals","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-951d4f7a-44c4-4787-9143-888af04bca95.jpg","bio":"<strong style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Wim Lambrecht</strong> is a developer, architect, builder, and lifelong learner who enjoys turning emerging technologies into practical solutions. He designs, co-creates, and develops platforms, frameworks, and modernization approaches that help organizations deliver software more effectively.<br><br>His current focus is on applying AI to real-world engineering challenges, including code migration, platform engineering, and software modernization. A strong believer in knowledge sharing, Wim continuously explores new tools, techniques, and ways of working, adapting to the rapidly evolving AI landscape while maintaining a pragmatic focus on delivering value.<br><br>Wim works at Smals, an association of over 300 public institutions in Belgium, delivering shared IT services and acting as their joint in-house ICT organization, offering skills, best practices, economies of scale, and software re-use. For over 85 years, Smals has supported social security and healthcare institutions with innovative IT and infrastructure services.<br>","linkedin":"wim-lambrecht-16967983","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T13:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T13:50:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"FvVe9J0Abjg","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Smals reports on seven AI-assisted legacy projects, from COBOL analysis to SAS-to-Python, and details how it moved a JBoss EAP 7 application to EAP 8/Jakarta EE with a phased, human-in-the-loop harness.","summary":["Koen Vanderkimpen (Smals Research) frames legacy as a spectrum, from COBOL on exotic databases through framework migrations such as Struts to merely outdated dependencies. Large codebases do not fit in context, so AI cannot simply be put in the driver's seat; it is already good at searching, analysing, documenting and small translations, but architectural change needs close human guidance. He then sums up six of the seven cases from a two-year study. They include a dependency-analysis tool for thousands of interlinked COBOL programs, a chatbot built from video transcripts recorded by a retiring expert, and COBOL-to-Java translation where the code can't leave machines that have production access. The others are a 2024 Struts/Java 8 rewrite where Claude ported features by reading the old code, SAS ETL jobs translated to Python, and a toolchain that documents VBA macros hidden in Word templates.","His lessons: iterate and throw away weak first attempts, scrub secrets and personal data before code reaches a cloud model (a GDPR issue for government), let the AI ask questions rather than guess, and watch for cheating. In the Word-macro case the AI wrote a test that simply returned the expected value. Have AI build deterministic tools that take it partly out of the loop. Test environments remain the biggest, mostly organisational, blocker.","Wim Lambrecht then covers the Jakarta EE migration of 'Odysseus', an application for the National Social Security Office running on JBoss EAP 7, Spring 5 and Hibernate 5 on OpenShift. The key input was a company knowledge base: standalone Git repositories showing how to move each concern (Dockerfile, ActiveMQ, IBM MQ, Oracle) from EAP 7 to 8. A prepare phase sets up an isolated branch, installs the assistant with guardrails, removes PII and secrets without using AI, checks test coverage and involves the product team through kickoffs and hackathons. Execution starts with /init, then one large prompt that produces a phased plan marking parallelisable steps, a dependency inventory with a hand-written Jakarta dependency mapping file, a PROGRESS.md and an audit trail, and hands control back to a human after each phase. Lambrecht advises migrating shared common libraries first.","Later approaches added structure: role-based agents that ask the human which model to use per phase, a spec-driven COBOL-to-Java pipeline with JSON output contracts and non-AI quality gates per phase, and finally a single reusable migration skill. Vanderkimpen closes by warning that teams risk becoming 'AI-locked-in' if prices rise, cites the Jevons paradox to argue developer demand will grow, and lists what works well today: documentation, small translations and tooling, with humans needed for architectural rewrites."],"keyPoints":["AI works well for understanding and documenting legacy code, small translations and refactorings, and building tooling; end-to-end migration only works for small programs, and architectural rewrites need close supervision.","Turning a retiring COBOL expert's explanatory videos into transcripts and a knowledge-base chatbot gave the new team a way to answer questions about the old code.","Early SAS-to-Python translations reproduced SAS idioms literally; more context, guardrails and eventually a skill improved them.","The EAP 7 to 8 migration was grounded in a company knowledge base of small example repositories, one per migration topic, which the agent learned from.","Ask for a phased plan in markdown that humans can edit, a PROGRESS.md and an audit trail of human corrections, and give control back after every phase.","Remove secrets and PII with non-AI tools before any agent sees the code.","The approach evolved from one big prompt to role-based agents, then spec-driven phases with JSON contracts and deterministic quality gates, and finally a reusable migration skill."],"takeaways":["Before an AI-assisted migration, scrub secrets and personal data and make sure test coverage can catch regressions.","Build small reference repositories showing your organisation's approved before-and-after for each migration concern, and feed them to the agent.","Migrate shared common libraries before the applications that depend on them.","Use a different model, or a human, to review AI-written tests, since agents can write tests that just return the expected value.","Package a migration that worked into a skill so other teams can reuse it."],"quotes":[{"text":"If context is king, harness is the kingdom.","at":"05:25"},{"text":"You can't use AI to remove your sensitive data, because that's just the point.","at":"30:16"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Smals and the agenda"},{"at":"02:59","title":"Context and harness engineering"},{"at":"07:54","title":"The legacy spectrum"},{"at":"11:49","title":"Six modernisation cases"},{"at":"17:47","title":"Lessons learned"},{"at":"23:12","title":"Jakarta EE migration case"},{"at":"28:51","title":"Prepare and execute phases"},{"at":"40:20","title":"Agents, specs and skills"},{"at":"47:47","title":"AI lock-in and the future"}],"tech":["Jakarta EE","Java EE","JBoss EAP","Spring","Hibernate","OpenShift","COBOL","SAS","Python","VBA","Struts","Claude Code","GitHub Copilot","Maven","Agent Skills"],"watchIf":"You own legacy Java EE, COBOL or SAS code and want a realistic, public-sector view of where AI helps with modernisation and how to structure a migration.","themes":["Legacy modernization","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":24245,"title":"Making sense of vector databases","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["semantic search","embedding models","indexing algorithms","vector databases"],"abstract":["Vector databases have become very popular in the AI era. Particularly the feature of finding semantically similar objects is quite useful in AI and related applications. But how does a vector database do it? How exactly does a vector database store its data so that it is able to perform a semantic search so fast?","This talk will introduce vector databases while explaining the fundamental concepts behind it. It will explain what vectors are, how are they stored, what are embedding models, indexing algorithms and similarity/distance metrics. We use Java’s Vector API to implement a simple in-memory vector database and we use Spring AI for integrating with various other vector databases.","In order to explain these concepts in simple terms, we start with simple use cases that are implemented with vector databases and then slowly increase the complexity to take it to incredibly amazing level!"],"favourites":273,"speakers":[{"name":"Balkrishna Rawool","company":"ING Bank","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-eec7f3b9-48f4-4efd-8f9b-8e1671c757e2.jpg","bio":"Balkrishna works as an Engineering Lead at ING Bank. He is a frequent speaker at renowned tech conferences. He has passion for continuous learning and genuine desire to sharing knowledge. Although he has been working with Java for many years, he finds latest developments in Java quite exciting. He is also an Oracle ACE Associate for Java.","linkedin":"balkrishnarawool","bluesky":"balarawool.bsky.social","twitter":"BalaRawool"}],"start":"2026-10-08T09:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T10:40:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"knE4uy7IYXY","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Rawool explains vectors, embeddings, similarity metrics and indexing (HNSW) through seven Spring Boot demos that build from a hand-rolled Java Vector API store to RAG and a Google Lens-style image search.","summary":["Balkrishna Rawool of ING Bank opens with Google Lens and asks how you would build image similarity search yourself; his answer is a vector database. He explains that a vector with magnitude and direction is equivalent to an array of n numbers, that an embedding model maps data to vectors so that similar meanings land close together, and that vector databases add insert, update, delete, indexing and search on top. To make the mechanics concrete he writes a small in-memory vector DB in Java: a record wrapping a double array, with dot product, magnitude and normalisation implemented using the incubating Vector API (SIMD), a HashMap from vector to data, and a k-nearest-neighbours method that takes a pluggable similarity or distance calculator.","The first demos use that store. Ten greys mapped to one-dimensional vectors return their neighbours by Euclidean distance. RGB colours normalised into three-dimensional vectors are matched by cosine similarity, which runs from 1 for identical vectors to -1 for opposite ones. Along the way he defines Manhattan distance, dot product, Euclidean distance and cosine similarity. A Word2Vec demo (Google News model, 300 dimensions, loaded with Deeplearning4j) embeds every word of the Wikipedia pages for man, woman, king and queen. 'King' returns kings, queen and monarch, and king minus man plus woman puts queen second in the results.","He then covers indexing: tree-based (KD-tree, R-tree, Annoy), hash-based (locality-sensitive hashing, which deliberately maximises collisions) and graph-based HNSW, whose layered small-world graph is searched from a fixed top-layer entry point down to the densest layer. Sentences from ten maths Wikipedia pages are embedded with nomic-embed-text through Ollama and stored in PostgreSQL with pgvector and an HNSW index, which serves queries like 'what is algebra' or 'Newton'. A RAG demo stores a made-up comic shop FAQ in Elasticsearch: without retrieval, GPT-OSS hallucinates a return policy, and with it the model gives the correct 15-day, unopened, in-store rule.","The finale stores world-wonder photos as image embeddings in Weaviate (classes and fields rather than tables, queried with GraphQL) and finds similar pictures of the Taj Mahal or the pyramids from an uploaded image. A celebrity lookalike variant with about 300 photos matches a volunteer's selfie, and the speaker's own photo returns George Clooney. The source code for all seven examples is published."],"keyPoints":["A minimal vector DB is a map from vector to payload plus a k-nearest-neighbours search over a pluggable similarity or distance function.","The Java Vector API uses SIMD so one CPU instruction processes many vector components; Llama3.java was cited as evidence of its power.","Cosine similarity is the dot product divided by the product of magnitudes; on normalised vectors only direction matters.","Word2Vec embeddings encode relationships: vector arithmetic king minus man plus woman lands near queen.","Locality-sensitive hashing inverts the usual hash goal by making similar inputs collide.","HNSW builds a layered proximity graph and searches greedily from the sparse top layer down to the dense bottom layer.","PostgreSQL becomes a vector store with the pgvector extension, an HNSW index on the embedding column, and plain SQL for nearest-neighbour queries.","With RAG off, the model invented a shop return policy; with RAG on, it returned the FAQ's actual policy."],"takeaways":["Choose the distance metric that matches how your embedding model was trained, typically cosine similarity on normalised vectors.","Add pgvector with an HNSW index to an existing PostgreSQL database before adopting a dedicated vector database.","Ground LLM answers about private data with retrieval and test the same questions with retrieval switched on and off.","Clone the speaker's examples and implement k-NN yourself once to understand what a vector database does for you."],"quotes":[{"text":"The UI for this example and also all previous examples show that I am a backend engineer and I'm not a frontend engineer.","at":"41:12"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Google Lens as motivation"},{"at":"02:50","title":"Vectors and embeddings"},{"at":"05:19","title":"Vector DB with Vector API"},{"at":"10:40","title":"Grey colours demo"},{"at":"14:36","title":"Distance and similarity metrics"},{"at":"21:45","title":"Embedding models and Word2Vec"},{"at":"27:34","title":"Indexing algorithms and HNSW"},{"at":"31:27","title":"Semantic search with pgvector"},{"at":"35:45","title":"RAG with Elasticsearch"},{"at":"41:12","title":"Image search and lookalikes"}],"tech":["Java Vector API","Spring Boot","PostgreSQL","pgvector","Elasticsearch","Weaviate","Word2Vec","Deeplearning4j","nomic-embed-text","Ollama","GPT-OSS","HNSW","Llama3.java"],"watchIf":"You use vector stores or RAG without being sure what embeddings, cosine similarity or HNSW actually do, and like learning from small runnable examples.","themes":["Data & databases","LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":true}},{"id":23543,"title":"The Yearly Java Performance Update for 2026","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["optimization","performance","java","jdk"],"abstract":["Java gets faster every year—often without developers changing a single line of application code. But what actually changed inside the JDK, which improvements matter to real applications, and how do the engineers working on Java know that an optimization is truly an improvement?","Returning to Devoxx for the yearly Java performance update, we will explore a curated selection of performance work delivered since last year. We will visit the standard libraries, the HotSpot runtime, the JIT compilers, and the garbage collectors to see how improvements at every layer combine to make modern Java applications faster, more scalable, and more resource-efficient.","Along the way, we will examine enhancements to startup and warmup, allocation and memory footprint, garbage-collection throughput, virtual-thread scalability, vectorization, and frequently used library operations. For selected optimizations, we will look beneath the API to understand the implementation techniques involved—and see why a seemingly small change can sometimes produce a surprisingly large result.","Attendees will learn which improvements arrive automatically when upgrading, which require explicit configuration or particular coding patterns, and how to evaluate their impact on real workloads. Expect measurements, implementation details, practical advice, and a glimpse of the performance work already taking shape for future Java releases.","Upgrade your JDK, bring your benchmarks—and stay on the fast path."],"favourites":367,"speakers":[{"name":"Per-Ake Minborg","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-a388bc3b-5180-411a-a4a7-5631e4341892.jpg","bio":"Per Minborg is a manager in the Java Core Library team at Oracle and is also a member of the Panama project team. He is an inventor and developer with more than 25 years of Java coding experience. He has been a frequent contributor to various open-source projects, is a regular speaker at events, Oracle JavaOne alumnus, and co-author of the publication Modern Java.","linkedin":"per-%C3%A5ke-minborg-448a623b6/?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Ad_flagship3_feed%3B4pm%2B8nsET3GGj6bkmM3geg%3D%3D","bluesky":"pminborg.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T09:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T10:40:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"rNOzf12RORI","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Minborg explains how HotSpot's JIT optimises code, then covers JDK 26 and 27 performance work (G1 barriers, compact headers by default, library fast paths, AOT) and previews JDK 28.","summary":["Minborg, from Oracle's Java core libraries team, opens without slides by explaining what happens after javac: the interpreter profiles your code, and C2 builds an IR graph from the inlined bytecode and optimises it. He covers intrinsics, hoisting bounds checks (two checks instead of 128 for a 64-byte copy loop), auto-vectorization with SIMD and its limits (aliasing, early exits), load/store reordering, @Stable fields that become constants once set, loop unrolling, devirtualization from profiles, escape analysis (in effect, stack allocation), and speculation backed by uncommon traps and deoptimization. Most of the changes he discusses later are about feeding this machinery better information.","From JDK 25 he recalls AOT profiles (a training run between compile time and run time) and stable cached String hashes, which let C2 constant-fold lookups such as a map from strings to method handles. In JDK 26, G1 reduces synchronization on reference stores via a second card table, worth about 5% or more on reference-heavy workloads. Also: G1 as the default collector even on single-CPU instances, cheaper native-to-Java string conversion in the FFM API, record hashCode bootstraps typed to the record class, HashMap copies that reuse cached hash codes, a cost model so tiny loops are not auto-vectorized, and an AOT object cache that works with any GC.","JDK 27 turns compact object headers on by default (8-byte headers instead of 12, better cache locality), defers G1's concurrent-mark structures and threads for short-lived programs, lets virtual threads unmount while waiting on class initialisation, makes G1 keep up better under allocation spikes, and fixes Object Monitor Table locking. Lazy constants reach a third preview and now fail permanently if initialisation fails. For JDK 28 he points to Valhalla value classes, memory pools attached to the thread for arena allocations (over 10x faster for short-lived native allocations like errno) and AOT-compiled code to cut warm-up. He closes with advice on benchmarking with JMH and Amdahl's law."],"keyPoints":["@Stable (an internal annotation) lets the JIT treat a field as constant once it is non-default; String's cached hash, lazy constants and other optimisations build on it.","Reference stores carry hidden GC barrier work; JDK 26's G1 change simplifies the application-side barrier using an extra card table, for about 5% or more on reference-heavy code at a small memory cost.","G1 is now the default in all environments; Serial GC stays available via -XX:+UseSerialGC and Minborg said there are no plans to remove it.","Compact object headers are on by default in JDK 27; headers shrink from 12 to 8 bytes and an empty object from 16 to 8, and Minborg says better locality matters more than the raw memory saving.","new HashMap(otherHashMap) now reuses already computed hash codes, including through unmodifiable wrappers, which gives a large speed-up on his 150-entry benchmark.","The AOT cache (created with -XX:AOTCacheOutput in a training run) can now stream objects so it works with any GC, not only the one used during training.","Virtual threads waiting on another thread's class initialisation no longer pin their carrier thread.","A 4x speed-up on code that accounts for 10% of runtime yields only about 7% overall, so profile before optimising."],"takeaways":["Benchmark your service on JDK 26 and 27 with default settings, then toggle the collector and compact object headers to see their effect on startup, throughput, memory and tail latency.","Use an AOT cache from a training run for short-lived or fast-starting applications.","If you run reference-heavy workloads, compare CPU usage and pause times under G1 on both releases.","Read the per-release performance articles on inside.java (tagged 'performance') before upgrading."],"quotes":[{"text":"Can you allocate memory on the stack in Java? Most people say no, but actually in this case it can do that, and that is something that's handled automatically for us.","at":"10:02"},{"text":"If you have a four times gain on just 10% of the work, you only get like 7%.","at":"38:19"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"How the JIT optimises code"},{"at":"12:53","title":"Kinds of performance"},{"at":"14:22","title":"Leftovers from JDK 25"},{"at":"17:05","title":"G1 barriers and defaults"},{"at":"20:01","title":"Compact object headers"},{"at":"21:23","title":"Library and vectorization fast paths"},{"at":"27:17","title":"AOT cache, GC and virtual threads"},{"at":"33:38","title":"Looking ahead to JDK 28"},{"at":"38:19","title":"Benchmarking and what to try"}],"tech":["JDK 26","JDK 27","JDK 28","HotSpot C2","G1 GC","Serial GC","Compact object headers","AOT cache","Foreign Function & Memory API","Vector API","Lazy constants","Virtual threads","Project Valhalla","JMH","HashMap","Records"],"watchIf":"You plan JDK upgrades and want to know which performance gains come for free, which defaults changed under you, and how the JIT actually works.","themes":["JVM performance & runtime","Java language & JDK"],"demo":false}},{"id":21675,"title":"All you need to know about Maven 4!","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["maven","version","features","ecosystem"],"abstract":["Maven is more than 20 years old, and it's last major version (Maven 3) was released in 2010. Since then Maven got several great updates, but the Maven team also realized that Maven can't evolve further without huge changes which have the capability to break the whole ecosystem if done wrong. Now - after years of intensive planning and coding - the release of Maven 4 is closer than ever before!","In this talk I'll speak about the motivation and challenges the team had while developing Maven 4 and of course about its new features and improvements."],"favourites":394,"speakers":[{"name":"Matthias Bünger","company":"ITZBund","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-c097beff-4de0-4a4d-ab32-87019a3d1108.jpg","bio":"Matthias loves Java, Jakarta EE / Microprofile, testing and documentation. He is a Maven committer and maintainer of the JUnit extension library &quot;JUnit Pioneer&quot;.","linkedin":"matthias-bünger-a71b60282","bluesky":"bukama.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-09T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T08:20:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"Matthias Bünger covers why Maven 4 took years, the challenges of changing Maven without breaking the ecosystem, and its new features and improvements.","summary":["Maven 3 was released in 2010, and while it has had updates since, the Maven team concluded it could not evolve further without large changes that could break the ecosystem if done badly. After years of planning and coding, Maven 4 is close to release.","Matthias Bünger sets out to explain the motivation and challenges behind Maven 4 development, and to present its new features and improvements."],"keyPoints":["Maven 3 dates from 2010, and further evolution needed big changes.","Risk of breaking the wider ecosystem shaped the development.","Talk covers motivation and challenges of the Maven 4 effort.","Covers new features and improvements."],"takeaways":["Check your builds and plugins for Maven 4 compatibility before the release."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Maven 4","Maven 3"],"watchIf":"You maintain Maven builds and want to know what Maven 4 changes.","themes":["Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":false}},{"id":23533,"title":"Teaching a New Dog Old Tricks: Give the Agent a Debugger","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["debugger","logpoints","runtime","intellij"],"abstract":["When code misbehaves, coding agents often fall back on an old habit. They add print statements. The method works. It also changes the program before the agent understands the problem.","The debugger provides a cleaner way to find the evidence. Logpoints inspect application state while the program keeps running. They can write custom messages without changing the source. You can even place them inside compiled third-party libraries. Every output line links back to the code that produced it. This includes logpoints and regular loggers as well as print statements.","IntelliJ IDEA makes its debugger tools available to coding agents. The debugger skill covers dozens of tools and settings. Few developers keep all of them in their heads. The complete playbook fits into the agent's context.","The agent combines that debugger knowledge with its understanding of the project code. It can choose useful locations for breakpoints and logpoints. It can start a debug run configuration and read actual values. As the evidence develops, it can move its observation points. The source stays untouched.","What attendees will learn:","- How Logpoints capture runtime state without suspending the program or modifying its source","- How to investigate behavior inside compiled third-party libraries","- How navigable debugger output connects runtime observations to their origin in code","- How an agent combines code context with debugger knowledge to place useful breakpoints and logpoints"],"favourites":186,"speakers":[{"name":"Anton Arhipov","company":"JetBrains","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-d4d89f78-d4bb-456f-ab6f-4768b4822745.jpg","bio":"Anton is a Developer Advocate at JetBrains, where he focuses on IntelliJ IDEA and the intersection of AI agents and developer tooling. A Java Champion since 2014 with a background in server-side development, he has spent over a decade building tools and content for developers. These days, you&#39;ll find him speaking at conferences, exploring agentic workflows, and looking for ways to make the development experience smarter and more enjoyable.","linkedin":"antonarhipov","bluesky":"antonarhipov.bsky.social","twitter":"antonarhipov"}],"start":"2026-10-05T15:35:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T16:05:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"rk22mStXsGU","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Arhipov recaps lesser-known IntelliJ IDEA debugger features, then shows a coding agent using them via a bundled debugger skill and MCP tools, collecting evidence with logpoints instead of print statements.","summary":["Arhipov starts from the point that breakpoints, value changes and HotSwap are JVM runtime capabilities exposed through a debugging protocol, so an agent can use them without a human at the IDE. He walks through Trace Stream Chain for visualising data flowing through a stream, method breakpoints on interface methods that stop in every implementation, wildcard method breakpoints that catch reflective calls, proxies and generated code, and logpoints: non-suspending breakpoints with an evaluated expression. He also shows a breakpoint that stays disabled until another one is hit, so it fires only on a particular call path.","Each feature is then handed to an agent. IntelliJ IDEA bundles a debugger skill (installable into .agents/skills) that tells the agent how and in what order to call the IDE's debugger tools through a single router MCP endpoint. Its principle is logpoint-first root cause analysis: gather runtime evidence instead of editing code with print lines. In the demos the agent finds a duplicate-keyboard bug caused by BigDecimal comparison in a stream, traces a 'never used' method to a reflective call through a non-constant method name, and reproduces a flaky concurrency test because its logpoints widen the race window."],"keyPoints":["Method breakpoints on an interface method stop in whichever implementation runs; wildcard class and method patterns also catch proxies, bytecode weaving and generated code.","A logpoint is a non-suspending breakpoint that evaluates and logs an expression; each output line links back to the source and can capture the stack trace.","The bundled IntelliJ debugger skill gives the agent a procedure, not just tools: reproduce the bug, place logpoints, collect evidence, then fix and remove the breakpoints.","Logpoints work in compiled third-party libraries, where the agent's usual print-statement habit is impossible.","For race conditions, logpoints (or a thread-only suspend policy) widen the timing window enough to make an intermittent failure reproduce on the first run."],"takeaways":["Enable the IntelliJ debugger skill for your project and tell the agent explicitly to debug, so it reaches for logpoints instead of println.","Use method breakpoints with wildcard patterns to find out who calls code that 'Find Usages' says is unused.","Try a breakpoint that stays disabled until another one is hit when you only care about one call path into a shared method."],"quotes":[{"text":"Why do you think people add println statements instead of adding the breakpoint? Because it's very hard to figure out where you have to set the breakpoint.","at":"22:41"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Debugger as runtime window"},{"at":"02:37","title":"Stream debugging and Trace Stream Chain"},{"at":"06:57","title":"Agent fixes the stream bug"},{"at":"12:09","title":"Method and wildcard breakpoints"},{"at":"16:37","title":"Agent traces a reflective call"},{"at":"20:10","title":"Logpoints and the debugger skill"},{"at":"26:36","title":"Breakpoint dependencies for call paths"},{"at":"29:32","title":"Reproducing a race condition"}],"tech":["IntelliJ IDEA","IntelliJ debugger","Logpoints","Method breakpoints","Trace Stream Chain","HotSwap","MCP","Agent skills","Java Streams","BigDecimal"],"watchIf":"You use coding agents in IntelliJ IDEA and want them to debug with runtime evidence instead of scattering print statements through your code.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":49254,"title":"Mission Possible: The 45-Minute Path to Bullet-Proof Java Container Images","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["containerization","vulnerabilities","hardening","provenance"],"abstract":["Your mission is to turn a bloated CVE-infested container image into a low-noise image with low vulnerability exposure and clear operational posture. You have 45 minutes before the explosive attack hits. Mission possible! This session provides a straight path towards building more secure and maintainable Java container images. We will identify the key culprits such as stale dependencies, outdated base, lack of provenance, and excessive privileges. Then, we will draft a step-by-step plan to:","Pick a minimal and well-maintained base image,","Shrink privileges,","Prove provenance with version pinning, signatures and SBOMs,","Scan and classify with CVSS rules to cut noise and fix what’s exploitable,","Automatically monitor for updates with Dependabot/Renovate.","Expect a before/after scan comparison, copy-and-paste Dockerfile instructions, verification checklist, and a clear and reproducible path towards hardened Java container images that provide an impenetrable lock-box for your applications."],"favourites":199,"speakers":[{"name":"Pasha Finkelshtein","company":"Bellsoft","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-a2b5ea87-cb3b-4cf7-ba56-5d667977ed3b.jpg","bio":"Years of experience in software engineering and the team&#39;s leading roles combined with his passion for Java made Pasha know all the hidden details in this IT niche. As a Developer Advocate for @Bellsoft, he educates the public on the latest software tools built by BellSoft and helps to create and test instruments for developers. He writes in Kotlin, speaks at conferences, composes a new articles, or maintains his pet projects.","linkedin":"asm0dey","bluesky":"asm0dey.site","twitter":"asm0di0"}],"start":"2026-10-07T11:05:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T11:45:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"1yndLHw9mbo","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Finkelshtein hardens a naive Temurin JDK 25 image in five steps, ending with a signed distroless image with SBOMs, and argues someone must own the base layer under an SLA.","summary":["Finkelshtein, a developer advocate at BellSoft, starts with a one-stage Dockerfile on Eclipse Temurin 25 JDK. In August 2026 Trivy found 46 vulnerabilities in it, 42 from the base image and none from Java; he said the base alone now shows 135. The goal is 'zero unmanaged risk': every CVE patched or accepted by a named owner with an expiry date. Step one needs no rebuild: a non-root user, dropped capabilities, no-new-privileges and a read-only filesystem. Step two ships a JRE or jlink runtime through a multi-stage build on a minimal base, taking the image from 46 vulnerabilities and about 1 GB to 7 and 200 MB.","Step three triages in context, since CVSS ignores reachability and exposure: Spring4Shell scores 9.8 but only affects WAR files on standalone Tomcat. Step four handles CVEs with no patch, such as the critical zlib issue Debian Bookworm marked 'will not fix' although zlib 1.3.1 fixed it in January 2024. Since backporting takes days of specialist work, a team or vendor should own the base layer under an SLA. Step five proves it to auditors: a distroless JRE 25 base scanned at zero CVEs, digest pinning, cosign signatures, separate OS and application SBOMs, attestations, and Renovate for updates. He noted the EU Cyber Resilience Act requires SBOMs from 11 December 2027."],"keyPoints":["Four runtime flags work on images you already ship: non-root user, --cap-drop=ALL with selective --cap-add, no-new-privileges, and a read-only root filesystem with tmpfs on /tmp.","Moving from a single-stage JDK image to a multi-stage JRE build cut the sample app from 46 vulnerabilities and about 1 GB to 7 vulnerabilities and 200 MB; a distroless JRE base brought it to zero.","Sorting by CVSS sorts by 'someone else's worst case'; Spring4Shell (9.8) only affects WAR deployments on standalone Tomcat.","A free base image is 'a gift, not a commitment': Debian Bookworm still ships zlib 1.2.13 with a critical CVE marked will-not-fix.","To pass an audit you must show who built the image (signature), what is inside (SBOMs, OS and app kept separate) and how it was built (attestation), with base images pinned by digest."],"takeaways":["Add USER, --cap-drop=ALL, no-new-privileges and a read-only filesystem to one production container this week.","Switch to a multi-stage build that runs on a JRE or jlink runtime on a minimal or distroless base.","Write down escalation rules (internet-facing, hot path, lateral movement) and record every accepted CVE with an owner and an expiry date.","Pin base images by digest, verify them with cosign, and let Renovate open PRs that run your tests when the digest changes.","Scan one production image and find out who, if anyone, owns its base layer and patch SLA."],"quotes":[{"text":"Our objective is not having zero CVEs. Our mission is zero unmanaged risk.","at":"09:48"},{"text":"Sorting only by CVSS is essentially sorting by someone else's worst case scenario.","at":"21:28"},{"text":"A free image is essentially a gift, not a commitment. Nobody in the chain owes you a date.","at":"28:29"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Scanning a naive Dockerfile"},{"at":"07:14","title":"Why CVEs cost money"},{"at":"10:52","title":"Limiting container privileges"},{"at":"15:18","title":"Shrinking the attack surface"},{"at":"19:21","title":"Classifying beyond CVSS"},{"at":"25:59","title":"Patching and base-layer ownership"},{"at":"37:24","title":"Proving it to the auditor"},{"at":"40:57","title":"Keeping images hardened"}],"tech":["Docker","Eclipse Temurin","JDK 25","jlink","Trivy","OSV-Scanner","CVSS","Spring4Shell","Debian Bookworm","Alpine Linux","Alpaquita Linux","Wolfi","UBI","Distroless images","Cosign","CycloneDX","SBOM","Renovate","Cyber Resilience Act"],"watchIf":"You ship Java in containers, own nobody's base image, and want a concrete sequence of Dockerfile and pipeline changes to get CVE counts and audit questions under control.","themes":["Security & supply chain","Cloud native & Kubernetes"],"demo":false}},{"id":24227,"title":"Expanding Vector Search Beyond Similarity","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["micronaut","graalvm","vector search","embeddings"],"abstract":["Vector search is not hard to prototype: turn data into embeddings, store them, and return the closest matches. But real-world applications need to consider much more: application data, business logic, and practical constraints.","In this session, we’ll go through building a travel application with Micronaut, LangChain4j, and GraalVM.","Some of the topics we’ll cover:","– The strategy for generating vector embeddings","– Combining similarity results with application data and business constraints","– Combining semantic and location-aware search","– Most helpful features of Micronaut Data and Micronaut LangChain4j","– Building and running the application as a Native Image","We’ll see how these pieces work together to turn basic similarity search into a more intelligent and useful application."],"favourites":90,"speakers":[{"name":"Alina Yurenko","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-a31c43d1-c596-4e24-af3f-078f54609e6c.jpg","bio":"Alina is a developer advocate for GraalVM at Oracle. Loves both programming and natural languages, compilers and performance, and open source. Ambassador of snacks and not running.","linkedin":"alinayurenko","bluesky":null,"twitter":"alina_yurenko"}],"start":"2026-10-05T14:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"EnHPYkSAaCo","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":49751,"title":"F*ck The System, and other bad ideas for the future","type":"Closing Keynote","duration":45,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["sovereignty","innovation","resilience","europe"],"abstract":["The world as we know it is changing rapidly, and the old order is crumbling. In these uncertain times, it is tempting to lose faith in the future. But what if this is precisely the beginning of something new? Fuck the System – and Other Bad Ideas for the Future (Fuck The System, en andere slechte ideeën voor de toekomst) unravels the chaotic forces at play behind the geopolitical scenes and asks a fundamental question: can Europe grow into a strong player in the emerging world order? What do we need to build a Europe that is sovereign, resilient, and radically innovative?","This is a manifesto to understand the present and imagine the future – thought-provoking, sharp, and unorthodox. It breaks with old logic and prejudices and sees in today’s disorder the seed of something better. For thinkers and doers, for citizens, entrepreneurs, and policymakers who believe that Europe can be more than an old continent in crisis."],"favourites":376,"speakers":[{"name":"Jo Caudron","company":null,"image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-7aa19f72-a79a-4dde-b5fe-b911859b0c1f.jpg","bio":"Jo Caudron is an internationally acclaimed keynote speaker with more than 30 years of experience on stages across Europe and beyond. Known for his inspiring and provocative storytelling, he weaves together insights on disruption, transformation, and the future of our society. What began as a focus on digital innovation and technology has evolved into a much broader mission: helping organizations, leaders, and citizens understand how profound societal, economic, and geopolitical shifts are reshaping the world - and how we can turn that uncertainty into opportunity.<strong style=\"background-color: transparent;\">​</strong>","linkedin":"jocaudron","bluesky":"jcaudron.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T16:55:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T17:40:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"wUyQmRriCng","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Caudron argues the post-war order is over and Europe should build sovereignty on energy and technology, pay for resilience, and swap the frontier AI race for broad adoption.","summary":["Introduced by a humanoid robot, Jo Caudron says 80-year post-war era of growth, welfare and US-backed rules is ending, buried under a 'meta storm' of overlapping crises. He calls the White House ambush of Zelensky Europe's Pearl Harbor. Since then, he notes, Europe announced €800 billion for resilience, France and the UK discussed a shared nuclear umbrella, and Germany loosened its constitutional debt brake. Citing US security strategy documents, he argues this is not just Trump rhetoric, so trust will not return after the midterms. Momentum is not enough, though. He describes sovereignty as a pyramid of energy first, then technology, then the economy and military, which together give agency.","Europe lost its tech industry by taking the path of least resistance (US money and markets, Chinese manufacturing), he says, and now over 80% of its applications, data and cloud run on American platforms while technology is being used as a weapon. Examples: Starlink switched off over Ukraine, ICC judges cut off from Microsoft, Google, Apple and card payments, and an Anthropic model he said was withdrawn for everyone for about 19 days after a US restriction. Given the CLOUD Act and FISA, he calls US sovereign-cloud promises 'bullshit'. He presents EuroStack, and public bodies going first: France replacing Teams, Zoom and Meet with Visio by 2027, Germany requiring ODF.","His prescriptions: 'copy back' technology Europe once invented, by reverse-engineering rather than rebuilding; add a 'return on sovereignty' line to business cases, since replacing hackable Chinese solar inverters for about €50 billion is cheap insurance against a multi-day blackout; and use 'transition thinking', treating energy as a technology that follows Moore's law-style curves and shifting from selling cars to selling rides. On AI he argues the compute race (255 GW in the US against 21 GW in Europe) cannot be won. Building gigafactories filled with US chips deepens dependency, so Europe should run a marathon of absorbing smaller, cheaper models into its economy, protected by the AI Act."],"keyPoints":["Sovereignty pyramid: energy, then technology, then economy and military; defence spending alone does not deliver agency.","Digital sovereignty has at least three separate dimensions: data, apps and cloud; AI; and platforms such as social media.","Caudron puts European spending at around €400 billion a year in licence fees to mostly US vendors, with about €4 trillion of European savings invested in US tech.","Sovereignty, autonomy and dependence are different things: mutual dependence is acceptable, being taken hostage is not.","Public-sector procurement can bootstrap European alternatives such as Nextcloud, LibreOffice and OnlyOffice by exposing employees and contractors to them.","He said most everyday business AI does not need frontier models; he pointed to small on-board models in autonomous vehicles and robots, and to open-weight models.","Loss aversion makes Europe 1.0 feel decline sharply; framing a 'Europe 2.0' at the start of a new curve (energy, urban mining, climate adaptation) creates hope."],"takeaways":["Map which parts of your stack would stop working if a US or Chinese vendor switched you off, and design a fallback path.","Add a 'return on sovereignty' or resilience line to technology business cases instead of comparing on price alone.","Evaluate European or open-source alternatives (Nextcloud, LibreOffice, Mistral models) when a workload does not need the market leader.","Check whether a smaller or open-weight model is good enough before defaulting to a frontier model."],"quotes":[{"text":"The first reflex is we have to buy more arms. But I think this is bullshit. Arms are a fundamental part of this pyramid, but it doesn't start with arms.","at":"16:37"},{"text":"Europe has to get out of this race, but not out of AI. Europe has to get its own race.","at":"50:51"},{"text":"For the first time in our recent history, the Americans and the Chinese are the incumbents and the Europeans are the challengers.","at":"58:06"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"The old normal is over"},{"at":"07:28","title":"Europe's Pearl Harbor moment"},{"at":"16:37","title":"The sovereignty pyramid"},{"at":"19:03","title":"How Europe lost its tech"},{"at":"23:05","title":"Technology as a weapon"},{"at":"27:09","title":"Sovereign cloud and EuroStack"},{"at":"34:52","title":"Copy back and return on sovereignty"},{"at":"42:52","title":"Transition thinking"},{"at":"46:39","title":"Leaving the AI race"},{"at":"56:51","title":"Loss aversion and a new curve"}],"tech":["EuroStack","CLOUD Act","FISA","GDPR","AI Act","Starlink","Nextcloud","LibreOffice","OnlyOffice","OpenDocument Format","Mistral AI","Visio"],"watchIf":"You make technology or vendor choices and want a European geopolitical argument for weighing sovereignty and resilience alongside price.","themes":["Security & supply chain"],"demo":false}},{"id":21691,"title":"MCP 2.0 - Stateless, New Auth (CIMD), and Exciting Extensions","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["extensions","authentication","mcp","stateless"],"abstract":["The MCP Spec & Implementations have quickly evolved since initial release almost 2 years ago. This session covers what has changed and how it impacts MCP server developers. You will learn about the new stateless transport, new auth (CIMD), and the extensions including MCP Apps, Tasks, and Skills over MCP. You learn from code examples and demos how you can build MCP servers that utilize the latest MCP features & approaches."],"favourites":351,"speakers":[{"name":"James Ward","company":"AWS","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-607b90ef-e5ff-4f70-b91c-a3082787114d.jpg","bio":"Professional software developer since 1997, with much of that time spent helping developers build software that doesn&#39;t suck. A Typed Pure Functional Programming zealot who often compromises on his ideals to just get stuff done. Currently a Developer Advocate for AWS and AAIF Technical Committee member.","linkedin":"JamesWard","bluesky":"jamesward.com","twitter":"JamesWard"}],"start":"2026-10-07T10:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T10:50:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"fShgG3jtkXQ","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Ward walks through the 2026-07-28 MCP spec: a stateless transport, elicitation via multi-round-trip requests, CIMD replacing dynamic client registration, and official extensions for MCP Apps, Tasks and Skills over MCP.","summary":["James Ward, of AWS and the Agentic AI Foundation's technical committee, covers what changed in the MCP specification released as version 2026-07-28. Deprecated: roots, sampling, logging, dynamic client registration (DCR) and the HTTP+SSE transport. The headline change is that the transport becomes stateless. The old streamable HTTP needed an initialize call, a session ID and a separate SSE GET stream, which forced sticky load balancing on that session ID. Now every request carries protocol version, client info and capabilities in metadata. Clients are encouraged to send MCP method and name headers so routers can act on them, a server/discover call replaces initialize, results carry a result type (complete, input required, task), and list responses include a TTL and cache scope.","Without server push, state moves into explicit handles, which Ward likens to REST resource IDs. Elicitation becomes a multi-round-trip request (MRTR): the server returns input-required with a JSON schema, and the client sends a second request with the answer. He demos this with his Scala ZIO HTTP MCP library in MCP Inspector, booking a flight with era auto-negotiation between legacy (2025-11-25) and modern clients, and the tool code is unchanged for both. Spring AI and the Java MCP SDK were still on the previous spec, so his advice is to run Spring AI servers in stateless mode now so the upgrade becomes a version bump.","For auth, the MCP server remains an OAuth 2.1 resource server: a 401 points to protected resource metadata, which leads to the authorization server. The spec now also requires validating the issuer. Client ID Metadata Documents (CIMD) replace DCR: the client ID is a URL that serves a JSON document with name, redirect URIs and grant types, so the server knows who the client is without unbounded registrations. On a Spring AI server the only change is to validate the audience claim, and Daniel Garnier-Moiroux's MCP security library handles the client side. The live login demo first fell back to DCR until Ward switched CIMD on in Inspector.","Extensions are now formalized, with capability negotiation and io.modelcontextprotocol/ namespaces. MCP Apps return HTML resources that the client renders, demoed in Claude.ai as a sortable EC2 instance table that can pre-fill prompts. Text fallbacks are needed for terminals. Tasks add standard tools for long-running work. Skills over MCP serves skills as resources, and Ward said Codex was an early client supporting it. His SkillsJars project packages skills as Maven Central JARs under META-INF/skills, and his server exposes them automatically. Build plugins extract them to disk when a coding agent needs the files."],"keyPoints":["The stateless transport puts protocol version, client info and capabilities on every request, so MCP servers can sit behind ordinary load balancers without sticky sessions on a session ID.","server/discover replaces initialize. Servers must implement it, clients may call it, and its presence is how servers tell modern-era clients from legacy ones.","Responses carry a result type (complete, input required or task), and list and read results include TTL milliseconds and a cache scope so clients can decide how long to cache.","Elicitation now returns input-required with a JSON schema, and the client sends a follow-up request with the user's answer, instead of the server pushing over SSE.","CIMD makes the OAuth client ID a URL pointing to a metadata document. It replaces dynamic client registration, which gave every client a new ID and no real identity.","Official extensions: MCP Apps (interactive HTML UIs via resources), Tasks (standard tools for long-running work) and Skills over MCP (skills list/get, served as resources), plus an OAuth client-credentials extension for enterprise auth.","SkillsJars ships agent skills as JARs, so they can be versioned and distributed like any other dependency and served over MCP from the classpath."],"takeaways":["Switch your MCP servers to stateless mode now, and model state as explicit resource handles passed in tool arguments.","Stop relying on sampling, roots and logging, which are deprecated in the new spec.","Enable audience-claim validation on authenticated MCP servers to be ready for CIMD.","Move long-running tool work to the Tasks extension, and give every MCP App a plain text or data fallback.","Use MCP Inspector, which can force legacy or modern protocol eras, to test that your server works with both."],"quotes":[{"text":"The MCP specification, which is really good reading material if you need to fall asleep at night.","at":"47:03"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"MCP's evolution and deprecations"},{"at":"02:57","title":"Stateless transport"},{"at":"07:55","title":"Handles and Spring AI stateless mode"},{"at":"10:08","title":"Multi-round-trip elicitation"},{"at":"13:49","title":"Era negotiation demo"},{"at":"20:06","title":"Auth and CIMD"},{"at":"26:35","title":"CIMD demo with Spring AI"},{"at":"31:29","title":"Extensions and MCP Apps"},{"at":"37:23","title":"Tasks, skills and SkillsJars"},{"at":"43:05","title":"Migration checklist and Q&A"}],"tech":["Model Context Protocol (MCP)","MCP Inspector","Spring AI","Java MCP SDK","ZIO HTTP","Scala","OAuth 2.1","CIMD","Dynamic Client Registration","JSON Schema","MCP Apps","Agent skills","SkillsJars","Maven Central","Claude.ai","Codex","Amazon EC2"],"watchIf":"You maintain or are about to build an MCP server or client and need to know what the 2026-07-28 spec breaks and how to prepare for it.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","LLMs in Java apps","Security & supply chain"],"demo":true}},{"id":5530,"title":"Kill All Mutants! (Intro to Mutation Testing)","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["code quality","test suite","testing","mutation"],"abstract":["Would your test suite still pass if the tested code was changed?  If so, you probably have gaps in your test suite, redundant or unreachable code, or maybe even both!","Mutation Testing reveals these cases, and provides some guidance about how to fix them.  This talk will tell you what mutation testing is, how it works inside, how to use it, where in your development process to use it, and its benefits, drawbacks, and history.  There will be multiple examples, and a list of tools for many popular languages (and some others).","You will learn a powerful technique for making sure your test suite is strict and your code is meaningful!"],"favourites":294,"speakers":[{"name":"Dave Aronson","company":"Codosaurus","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-b41d4e91-b171-4224-afa2-c6c806b1d492.jpg","bio":"Dave is a retired software development consultant (wrote code <em>and</em> gave advice about it), and international speaker, with 40 years of professional experience in a wide variety of languages, techniques, domains, etc.  He was the T. Rex of Codosaurus, LLC (his one-person consulting firm, which explains how he could get such a cool title, at <a href=\"https://www.Codosaur.us/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.Codosaur.us/</a>), in the USA, but now lives in Porto, Portugal.  His main focus in software lately is to spread the gospel of quality, including defining what that even means, and talking about techniques to achieve it.  In his spare time, he brews mead, teaches others how, and speaks at conferences (almost sixty so far, on five continents), on podcasts, etc.","linkedin":"DaveAronson","bluesky":"DaveAronson.bsky.social","twitter":"DaveAronson"}],"start":"2026-10-08T13:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T13:50:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"AsVt_dzTmN4","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Aronson explains how mutation testing finds lax tests and meaningless code that coverage misses, how tools generate and kill mutants, what surviving mutants mean, and where it fits in a pipeline.","summary":["Dave Aronson presents mutation testing as different from other testing techniques: it assumes the code passes its tests and checks two other qualities instead. One is whether the test suite is strict, meaning a semantic change makes at least one test fail. The other is whether the code is meaningful, so that every small semantic change has a visible effect on behaviour. Coverage cannot show either, because a test with its assertion commented out still counts as covering the code. He compares the approach to Chaos Monkey turned upside down (faults injected into copies of the code, in the test environment, where 'everything still passes' signals a problem) and to fuzzing applied to the code rather than the inputs, though not random.","The mechanics: the tool parses the code into an AST, finds each function and the tests that cover it (through annotations, naming conventions, call analysis or coverage data), then makes one mutant per possible change. Mutations include swapping arithmetic, logical or bitwise operators, flipping comparisons, adding or removing negation, deleting statements, removing if or loop conditions, replacing a function body with a constant, an argument or an error, and changing values or types. Each mutant runs against the function's tests: a failure kills it, and a mutant that passes every test survives.","Two Python examples show how to read survivors. For a power function tested only with 2 to the power 2, the addition, multiplication and argument-swap mutants survive because 2+2, 2*2 and 2^2 are all 4. Asserting that 2 cubed is 8 kills all of them. In a send-message loop, a mutant with the loop removed survives either because the tests never send a message larger than one chunk (add a test with max chunk size plus one) or because the loop is redundant and the function can be deleted.","On history, Richard Lipton proposed the idea in 1971 and Timothy Budd built the first tool in 1980. His rough cost model: 10 lines per function, 5 change points per line and 20 alternatives each give about 1,000 mutants, so a 10-second suite becomes over half an hour. Pruning equivalent mutants cuts that to about a third. One mutation per mutant follows the competent programmer hypothesis and avoids combinatorial explosion. He suggests gating merges on no surviving mutants in new code and no increase in old code, and points Java users to PIT."],"keyPoints":["Code coverage only shows that code ran during a test, not that any assertion depended on its correctness.","A surviving mutant points to lax tests, meaningless (redundant or unreachable) code, or both; occasionally to an equivalent mutant that is a false alarm.","A mutant is killed as soon as one test fails; the tool stops running further tests against it.","Choose test inputs where only the correct operation gives the expected result: 2^3 = 8 rules out 2+3 and 2*3 as well as 3^2.","A surviving 'loop removed' mutant means either no test exceeds one chunk or the loop, and perhaps the whole function, is redundant.","By Aronson's estimate, about 1,000 mutants per function turn a 10-second test suite into roughly 2,000 seconds of mutation testing.","Most tools support targeting a single file, class or function, incremental runs since the last commit or branch, and comments to skip lines such as debug traces.","The competent programmer hypothesis, that real bugs are usually one small substitution, justifies one mutation per mutant."],"takeaways":["Run PIT (or your language's equivalent) on one well-tested class and see how many mutants survive despite high coverage.","Use incremental or diff-based modes to mutation-test only changed code in CI or before review.","Set a merge rule such as 'no surviving mutants in new code, no increase in existing code'.","When a mutant survives, ask whether the test inputs are too narrow or whether the mutated code can simply be deleted."],"quotes":[{"text":"If everything still goes well, in the sense that our tests still pass, that doesn't mean all is well. That means that there is a problem.","at":"10:58"},{"text":"Yes, the coverage tools are good for something, just not what most people think.","at":"17:17"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Strict tests, meaningful code"},{"at":"05:22","title":"Drawbacks and costs"},{"at":"09:37","title":"Chaos Monkey and fuzzing comparison"},{"at":"12:00","title":"How tools generate mutants"},{"at":"18:43","title":"Kinds of mutations"},{"at":"21:15","title":"Power function example"},{"at":"29:22","title":"Send-message loop example"},{"at":"34:52","title":"History, cost and one mutation"},{"at":"40:36","title":"Process fit, tools, Q&A"}],"tech":["Mutation testing","PIT","Python","Abstract syntax tree","Chaos Monkey","Fuzzing","CI/CD"],"watchIf":"You trust code coverage numbers and want to see a technique that shows whether your tests would actually catch a bug.","themes":["Testing & quality"],"demo":false}},{"id":4097,"title":"Pragmatic multi-cloud architecture: designing for resilience and portability beyond the hype","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["multi-cloud","architecture","security","latency"],"abstract":["Today, most of our applications are hosted in the cloud. But what if we leveraged multiple providers for a single application or platform?","The theory is attractive: cherry-picking the best services from each provider to build the ultimate architecture. The reality? Cloud providers that are \"more equal than others\" and a labyrinth of technical complexities—where network latency, fragmented data, and incompatible APIs threaten both your SLAs and your peace of mind.","In this session, we will tackle the challenge of designing a multi-cloud platform based on a real-world use case.","From defining bounded contexts to addressing production hurdles, security, and API design, we will navigate the pitfalls inherent to this architecture while highlighting essential best practices.","Finally, we will explore how a thorough understanding of customer needs and a pragmatic risk analysis can guide design choices to deliver a coherent, end-to-end vision."],"favourites":68,"speakers":[{"name":"Alexandre Touret","company":"Magellan","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14Gi-vA-Ux4ew98kunpGudWlNMR_Olk594JYXJb_I9Q=s96-c","bio":"With over two decades of experience as a seasoned software architect, I am fueled by a passion for technology, cloud computing, and software development. Throughout my career, I have designed, built, and managed systems of varying scales across a diverse range of industries, including insurance, financial services, and mobility.<br><br>I take great pleasure in sharing my insights and experiences with others, whether by teaching, speaking at conferences, organizing hands-on workshops, or writing articles. By engaging with the community, I aim to inspire and provide practical insights drawn from my hands-on experience.","linkedin":"atouret","bluesky":"touret.info","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T13:10:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T14:00:00Z","room":"Room 10","videoId":"mOmuF5fDiUM","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":25027,"title":"Codepocalypse Now: LangChain4j vs JetBrains Koog","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["java","frameworks","ai","agent"],"abstract":["Can Java build a real AI agent — one that manages your calendar, reads your email, orders pizza, and remembers who you are across sessions? OpenClaw, the personal AI agent with 350K GitHub stars, proves the concept. We're going to build it twice, in Java, live on stage.","Baruch brings JetBrains Koog, Viktor brings LangChain4j. Same features, same LLM, completely different philosophies. We'll run six competitive rounds of coding, from basic agent setup through memory, tool calling, agentic workflows, guardrails, and observability. Each round surfaces a design disagreement: should memory be an Advisor or a Provider? Are agents composed services or first-class citizens? And when your guardrail framework and the model disagree, who wins?","The frameworks disagree on how AI agents should be built. The audience votes on who's right."],"favourites":52,"speakers":[{"name":"Viktor Gamov","company":"Confluent","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AAcHTtd9ZkVQ9giwUn-Tq1GRAjZ3ip6tMZ0Jnr2XMv2ECSYvkwJO=s96-c","bio":"Viktor Gamov is a Developer Advocate at Confluent, a company that makes a cloud streaming platform based on Apache Kafka.<br>Viktor has extensive expertise in building enterprise application architectures using open-source technologies. <br>He enjoys helping architects and developers design and develop low-latency, scalable, and highly available distributed systems.<br>He is a professional conference speaker on distributed systems, streaming data, the JVM, DevOps, and other topics, and regularly speaks at events including JavaOne, Devoxx, OSCON, QCon, and others. <br>He co-authored O’Reilly’s «Enterprise Web Development.»<br>He blogs at http://gamov.io and co-hosts DevRelRad.io.<br>Follow Viktor on Twitter @gamussa, where he posts about gym life, food, open-source, and, of course, Kafka and Confluent!","linkedin":"vikgamov","bluesky":"gamussa.dev","twitter":"gamussa"},{"name":"Baruch Sadogursky","company":"Port.io","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocKqAphxO-bPI7xrWrgh3Vhn_yJ1H91TCUKCNY_tGTQagjntFox7kA=s96-c","bio":"Baruch Sadogursky (@jbaruch) did Java before it had generics, DevOps before there was Docker, and DevRel before it had a name. He built DevRel at JFrog from a ten-person company through an IPO, co-authored &quot;Liquid Software&quot; and &quot;DevOps Tools for Java Developers,&quot; and is a Java Champion, a Microsoft MVP for AI, and a CNCF Ambassador alum.<br><br>Today, as a head of DevRel at Port and a self-certified Level 3 Context Sommelier, Baruch focuses on context engineering. On top of sharing context with AI agents, Baruch also shares knowledge with developers through blog posts, meetups, and conferences such as DevNexus, QCon, Kubecon, and Devoxx, mostly about how to make your AI engineering lifecycle smarter and increase your organization&#39;s productivity.","linkedin":"jbaruch","bluesky":"jbaru.ch","twitter":"jbaruch"}],"start":"2026-10-05T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T14:30:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"GGEmc6eR8c4","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":10038,"title":"Eval Driven Development: Why you need it to make your Agentic Application survive Production","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["agentic","reliability","development","evaluation"],"abstract":["Agentic systems unlock capabilities that traditional “deterministic” applications simply can’t deliver. But there’s a catch: their probabilistic nature introduces real and often unexpected risks—hallucinations, context drift, prompt degradation, and multi-step workflows that evolve in surprising ways. And you definitely don’t want to discover those in production.","The good news: there is a remedy. We can borrow the most reliable practice from deterministic software—test-driven development—and adapt it to the agentic world. The result is Eval-Driven Development (EDD): a systematic, engineering-first approach to bringing reliability into inherently probabilistic systems.","In this talk, we explore Eval-Driven Agentic Development and how it can transform your agents from unpredictable to reliable - using the (only?) full-fledged Java/Kotlin Eval framework: Dokimos. We’ll dive into techniques, tools, and patterns needed to make evals a first-class citizen of your development process—demonstrating all of these in a real-world application built with AI frameworks like Spring-AI, LanchChain4J, and Koog.","Along the way, you will learn how to:","Design layered eval suites","Create golden datasets","Define rubrics and scoring criteria","Extract eval signals from agent traces","Use regression gates to detect degraded behavior after changes","Build a continuous eval loop","Evaluation-Driven Development is the only path forward for reliable, smart next-generation applications. Let's learn how to walk it - together!"],"favourites":263,"speakers":[{"name":"Urs Peter","company":"Xebia","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-3c2d9321-8b4a-418a-8a95-5788a1e66ccf.jpg","bio":"Urs is a seasoned software engineer, solution architect, conference speaker, and trainer with over 20 years of experience building resilient, scalable, and mission-critical systems, primarily in Kotlin, Scala, and Java. Lately, also agentic systems with JVM technology. <br><br>Besides his job as a consultant, he is also a passionate trainer and author of a wide range of courses, including language courses for Kotlin and Scala, generative AI courses for the JVM platform, as well as architectural trainings on Microservices and Event-Driven Architectures.<br><br>As a people person by nature, he loves to share knowledge, inspire, and be inspired by peers at meetups and conferences. Urs is a JetBrains-certified Kotlin trainer.<br>","linkedin":"urs-peter-70a2882","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T11:05:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T11:45:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"4qeUklTLyTs","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Peter shows eval-driven development on a JVM agent: golden datasets, deterministic checks, LLM-as-judge and conversation simulations with the Dokimos framework, plus feeding production traces back into failing evals.","summary":["Peter argues that agentic systems are uncertain at their core rather than at the edges (prompts, conversation history, RAG chunking, tool and MCP selection, agent-to-agent calls), so pass/fail tests must give way to confidence scores above thresholds. He cites survey figures that 89% of companies observe agents in production but only 52% run evals. The process starts with an explicit goal (what the agent must and must not do), user profiles and happy and rainy-day scenarios, from which an LLM drafts test inputs that you review and later grow into a golden dataset from production data. His demo is a Spring AI Devoxx assistant with venue info, semantic search over the vectorised schedule and personal-track CRUD tools, tested from Kotlin notebooks and unit tests with Dokimos, a JVM eval framework supporting Spring AI, LangChain4j, Koog and Embabel.","His eval pyramid puts cheap deterministic checks (contains, tool-call correctness via Spring's tool-call recorder) at the bottom, LLM-judged ground-truth evals (faithfulness, hallucination, context relevance) and quality evals (tone) above, and humans at the top. A pirate-speech input fails a formality judge at 0.62 until a system-prompt fix lifts it to 0.95; a parking question exposes invented 'ample parking' through claim-by-claim faithfulness checking; a simulated back-end developer filling a schedule passes the judges but fails a deterministic no-overlap check. Judges must be pinned, at least as strong as the agent's model, checked for score variance on a frozen set, and calibrated against human scores. For production he traces everything to Langfuse via OpenTelemetry, collects user feedback, and uses an LLM triage notebook to turn a 'suggested past sessions' complaint into a failing eval. For security he points to red teaming and Promptfoo."],"keyPoints":["Dokimos is a JVM eval framework with integrations for Spring AI, LangChain4j, Koog and Embabel; evals run as ordinary unit tests with a Kotlin DSL or Java builders.","Build the eval pyramid on deterministic checks (contains, expected tool calls, no overlapping sessions) and add LLM-as-judge for faithfulness, hallucination, context relevance and tone.","Pin judge model versions and temperature, use a judge at least as capable as the agent's model, measure score standard deviation on a frozen set, and calibrate against human ratings.","Conversation simulators with a simulated user, max turns and stop conditions reveal failures that single-turn evals miss.","Production loop: OpenTelemetry traces to Langfuse, user feedback, random sampling, then convert bad traces into failing evals to grow the golden dataset."],"takeaways":["Write down what your agent must not do, and generate and review test prompts per user profile and scenario.","Start each agent test suite with deterministic checks before adding LLM judges.","Add a feedback button and OpenTelemetry tracing to your agent, and turn every bad-rated trace into a regression eval.","Run Promptfoo or a red-team exercise before putting an agent in production."],"quotes":[{"text":"We fight probabilistic behavior with probabilistic behavior.","at":"18:35"},{"text":"When we create agents, we also get a new role and this new role is agent sitter.","at":"33:17"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why agents fail in production"},{"at":"07:22","title":"Eval-driven development mindset"},{"at":"08:49","title":"Goals and golden datasets"},{"at":"13:09","title":"First evals with Dokimos"},{"at":"17:10","title":"LLM as a judge"},{"at":"23:33","title":"Faithfulness and tool checks"},{"at":"27:26","title":"Conversation simulation"},{"at":"30:51","title":"Calibrating judges and security"},{"at":"33:17","title":"Production monitoring and feedback"}],"tech":["Dokimos","Spring AI","LangChain4j","Koog","Embabel","Kotlin notebooks","Langfuse","OpenTelemetry","Promptfoo","MCP","A2A"],"watchIf":"You're building an agent on the JVM and need a concrete way to test it before and after it reaches production.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Testing & quality","LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":true}},{"id":41301,"title":"Owning the inference layer: When and how to run your own models","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["sovereignty","latency","inference","self-hosting"],"abstract":["Hosted model APIs are the fastest way to start building with Gen AI models, but they are not always the best long-term fit. As workloads grow, teams start running into harder questions around latency, cost, deployment flexibility, data boundaries, and performance tuning.","This talk looks at what changes when you move from calling a hosted model API to running inference yourself. We’ll break down the practical tradeoffs between hosted and self-hosted approaches, then examine how modern open inference technologies such as vLLM and llm-d are making self-hosted AI more realistic for production systems.","Rather than treating this as a debate between two camps, the session focuses on decision-making: when self-hosting is worth the added operational complexity, when hosted APIs still win, and what teams take on when they choose to own the inference layer.","We'll also unpack what sovereignty is actually worth beyond the compliance checkbox: the concrete value of controlling your data boundaries, your model lifecycle, and your independence from a single provider's roadmap and pricing, and where that value is real rather than theoretical.","You’ll leave with a practical framework for evaluating cost, control, performance, and architectural fit in your own environment."],"favourites":170,"speakers":[{"name":"Maarten Vandeperre","company":"Red Hat","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-909df07a-dfbf-40e8-9065-be12fa662faf.jpg","bio":"Maarten Vandeperre is an experienced software professional who recently joined Red Hat as an Appdev &amp; AI Specialized Solutions Architect. With a strong background in software development and architecture, he brings a wealth of expertise to his role. Maarten&#39;s primary focus is on application development and AI, with a particular emphasis on leveraging Red Hat&#39;s OpenShift platform from a developer&#39;s perspective.<br><br>One of Maarten&#39;s true passions lies in advocating for &quot;clean architecture&quot; as a guiding principle in software development. He firmly believes in the importance of designing software systems that are modular, maintainable, and scalable. As part of his dedication to this approach, Maarten strives to map these principles to infrastructure solutions, ensuring that the underlying technology supports and enhances the overall architecture. His deep understanding of integration technologies, such as API Gateways, Keycloak, Kafka, service mesh, and Camel, enables him to create seamless connections between systems while adhering to clean architectural principles, which empower organizations to thrive in the ever-evolving digital landscape.","linkedin":"maarten-vandeperre","bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Camille Nigon","company":"Red Hat","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-64d404b7-9acd-40a7-a8be-4305138ba69b.jpg","bio":"Camille is a Solutions Architect at Red Hat with a background in AI/ML and a keen interest in open-source projects. Passionate about knowledge sharing, Camille engages in technical workshops, public speaking, and hands-on projects to help others navigate the evolving AI landscape.<br>","linkedin":"camille-nigon","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T14:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"QbA-rs7XLEk","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Using a coffee analogy and one Quarkus app wired to three backends, Vandeperre and Nigon compare hosted model APIs, self-hosted inference with vLLM and llm-d, and a governed model-as-a-service gateway.","summary":["Vandeperre and Nigon of Red Hat (this was a sponsored talk) frame the choice as getting coffee: buy it at Starbucks (hosted APIs such as OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini), own a Jura machine (self-hosted inference on your own hardware), or use something in between (managed platforms like Amazon Bedrock or Azure Foundry). Their list of hosted-API risks: prices that can double overnight, one shared API key with no drill-down on the bill, latency you cannot control at peak, model upgrades that change behaviour, prompts leaving the organisation, a provider cutting off access, a limited menu, and cost that stays pure opex.","Their demo is a Quarkus and LangChain4j coffee-ordering app with a human-in-the-loop confirmation step, so an absurd total from a hallucinating small model can be rejected. Only the model configuration changes between backends. Vandeperre argues that data residency is not sovereignty, since jurisdiction still applies, and that open standards without open source still leave a kill switch. He illustrates the personal stakes with hypotheticals: medical summaries sold to insurers, or salary offers tuned to an applicant's debt.","Nigon covers what owning the stack costs: upfront GPU spend, GPUs idle overnight (fill them with fine-tuning or agentic batch jobs), peak concurrency, model upkeep, and above all unclear ownership between app, infra and security teams. Locally, Podman Desktop's AI Lab serves a 4B model behind an OpenAI-compatible /v1/chat/completions endpoint and generates Quarkus client snippets; a 9B download took down the hotel Wi-Fi the night before. For production she recommends vLLM over Ollama, which handles concurrent requests poorly, then llm-d for distributed inference with smart routing between vLLM pods, NVIDIA multi-instance GPU for sharing cards, and quantized models.","To stop 'shadow AI', with each developer running their own GPU and model, they put a model-as-a-service gateway in front: API keys tied to subscriptions, per-subscription token and request limits that return HTTP 429, and admin dashboards for time to first token, latency and token use per team. External models such as Claude can be registered behind it too, which they pitch as a first step even for teams staying on hosted APIs. The OpenShift AI demo deploys Qwen 0.6B on CPU and issues a key. Asked how to compare costs, they admitted they have no sizing guidance."],"keyPoints":["Eight risks of hosted APIs: price increases, shared keys with no cost drill-down, uncontrolled peak latency, silent model upgrades, data leaving your boundary, provider cutoff, limited model menu, and pure opex.","Podman Desktop AI Lab serves Hugging Face models locally behind an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, so a LangChain4j app switches by changing the base URL (host.containers.internal inside a container).","Ollama is fine on a laptop but weak at concurrent requests; vLLM, with efficient KV-cache management, is the recommended production engine.","llm-d adds distributed inference on Kubernetes, routing requests to the vLLM replica with the most capacity.","Red Hat publishes validated, vLLM-optimised models on Hugging Face with benchmark data such as time to first token on given GPUs.","NVIDIA multi-instance GPU lets several models share one card; quantization shrinks models with little accuracy loss, which they compared to a 2.4 MB image versus a 73 KB JPEG.","A model-as-a-service gateway with subscriptions, token quotas and per-team observability addresses both shadow AI and the question of who is on call.","Swapping in a newer model with the same API shape still requires re-testing, because behaviour changes."],"takeaways":["Put an AI gateway with per-team keys and token metrics in front of your model access now, even if it only proxies a hosted provider.","Keep the model endpoint configurable so the same app can target OpenAI, a local Podman model or an internal gateway.","Try small models like Qwen 0.6B or 4B-class models on CPU for summarisation tasks before buying GPUs.","Name an owner for the AI platform before self-hosting, covering serving, security and on-call.","Schedule fine-tuning or batch agentic jobs at night so self-hosted GPUs are not idle."],"quotes":[{"text":"Data residency is not the same as sovereignty.","at":"15:38"},{"text":"Production is not the playground.","at":"47:15"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Introduction and Red Hat"},{"at":"04:53","title":"Three ways to get coffee"},{"at":"07:22","title":"Risks of hosted APIs"},{"at":"11:31","title":"Demo with OpenAI"},{"at":"15:38","title":"Sovereignty and control"},{"at":"19:01","title":"Challenges of self-hosting"},{"at":"22:54","title":"Local models with Podman Desktop"},{"at":"29:17","title":"Quantization, vLLM and llm-d"},{"at":"34:20","title":"Model as a service gateway"},{"at":"44:36","title":"Wrap-up and questions"}],"tech":["vLLM","llm-d","Quarkus","LangChain4j","Podman Desktop AI Lab","OpenShift AI","Ollama","NVIDIA MIG","Red Hat Connectivity Link","NeMo Guardrails","TrustyAI","Hugging Face","Qwen"],"watchIf":"You call hosted LLM APIs today and need a beginner-level map of what self-hosting with vLLM, llm-d and a token-metering gateway would involve.","themes":["LLMs in Java apps","Cloud native & Kubernetes"],"demo":true}},{"id":7036,"title":"Building Production-Ready Agentic Systems with LangChain4j and Quarkus","type":"Hands-on Lab","duration":120,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["agentic","governance","testing","langchain4j"],"abstract":["Agentic AI demos are easy. Building agentic systems that are reliable, observable, secure, testable, and ready for production is where the real challenge begins.","In this hands-on lab, you'll build an enterprise-grade agentic AI application from the ground up using LangChain4j and its Quarkus extension. Along the way, you'll learn how to connect remote tools and agents through emerging interoperability standards such as MCP and A2A. You'll also explore how to encapsulate business capabilities as reusable skills, enforce governance through guardrails, and implement resilience patterns such as fault tolerance, retries, and persistence to ensure reliable execution of long-running agent workflows.","Finally, we'll cover one of the most overlooked aspects of agentic development: testing. You'll learn practical techniques for validating agent behavior, mocking tools and models, and building automated tests that provide confidence before deployment.","By the end of the lab, you'll leave with a complete, working agentic application and a set of proven patterns for building AI systems that can move beyond prototypes and into production. Note that this lab will be based on the last part of a 3-sections workshop, but if you lack any prior knowledge you will be also allowed to work on the former 2 parts and proceed at your own pace."],"favourites":48,"speakers":[{"name":"Mario Fusco","company":"IBM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GinuLQCwL67BBgfGsiWRl3mu3Ya0TbDffhYFiXX=s96-c","bio":"Mario is a senior principal software engineer at IBM working as Drools project lead. Among his interests there are also high performance systems and generative AI, being an active contributor of widely adopted projects like Quarkus and LangChain4j. He is also a Java Champion, the JUG Milano coordinator, a frequent speaker and the co-author of &quot;Modern Java in Action&quot; published by Manning.","linkedin":"mario-fusco-3467213","bluesky":null,"twitter":"mariofusco"},{"name":"Georgios Andrianakis","company":"IBM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-95904645-1695-4adf-b24c-41926050b4b4.jpg","bio":"Georgios works for IBM as a Senior Principal Software Engineer and is currently one of the most active contributors to Quarkus, where he works in all sorts of areas, including but not limited to LangChain4j, RESTEasy Reactive, Spring compatibility, Kubernetes support, testing, Kotlin, and more.<br><br>He is also an enthusiastic promoter of Quarkus who never misses a chance to spread the Quarkus love!","linkedin":"georgios-andrianakis","bluesky":null,"twitter":"geoand86"},{"name":"Kevin Dubois","company":"IBM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocInnMfs42NN14UEEtFboGAF5baXQdsTFdbDo_ZuR1DADFHUONPEfA=s96-c","bio":"Kevin Dubois is a software architect and platform engineer with a career spanning over 20 years. He is often featured as a keynote speaker at conferences around the world where he shares his experience and knowledge about cloud native &amp; AI software development, developer experience, open source and Java. Kevin is also an author and Java Champion. He currently works as a Senior Principal Developer Advocate at IBM, and is  Technical Lead for the CNCF Developer Experience Technical Advisory Group as well as an ambassador for the Agentic AI Foundation.","linkedin":"kevindubois","bluesky":"kevindubois.com","twitter":"kevindubois"}],"start":"2026-10-06T14:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T16:50:00Z","room":"BOF 2","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A 2-hour lab building a production-oriented agentic application with LangChain4j and Quarkus, covering MCP, A2A, skills, guardrails, resilience and testing.","summary":["Mario Fusco, Georgios Andrianakis and Kevin Dubois lead a lab on building agentic systems that are reliable, observable, secure and testable, not just demos. Participants build an application with LangChain4j and its Quarkus extension, connecting remote tools and agents via MCP and A2A.","The lab also covers encapsulating business capabilities as reusable skills, enforcing governance with guardrails, and resilience through fault tolerance, retries and persistence for long-running workflows. Testing is covered too: validating agent behaviour, mocking tools and models, and automating tests. It is the last part of a three-section workshop, and attendees can work through the earlier parts at their own pace."],"keyPoints":["Uses LangChain4j and the Quarkus extension.","Interoperability via MCP and A2A.","Skills and guardrails for reuse and governance.","Fault tolerance, retries and persistence for long-running workflows.","Testing with mocked tools and models."],"takeaways":["Add automated tests with mocked models and tools to any agent prototype."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["LangChain4j","Quarkus","MCP","A2A","Guardrails"],"watchIf":"You want to move a Java agent prototype toward production with proper testing and resilience.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":false}},{"id":48301,"title":"A Fleet of AI Agents, Each in Its Own Sandbox: Inside Docker's Agentic Platform","type":"Keynote","duration":20,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["autonomy","ai agents","sandbox","docker"],"abstract":["What if your next pull request was opened by an AI agent, one that read the issue, wrote the code, ran the tests, and only pinged you when it was ready to merge?","That's how Docker's engineering team ships software today. Built on top of Docker Cloud Sandboxes, the Docker Agentic Platform gives every AI agent its own isolated environment: real shell access, a live Docker daemon, git, GitHub CLI, and a JWT-scoped identity. Orchestrators spawn specialists, architect, implementer, reviewer ; that work in parallel across sandboxed boundaries, coordinating asynchronously without ever touching each other's secrets or state.","We'll walk the hard lessons (context cost, hallucinations, knowing when to keep a human in the loop), and what it takes to make autonomous agents safe and observable in production."],"favourites":481,"speakers":[{"name":"Jean-Laurent de Morlhon","company":"Docker","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocKQ_A4jeRTG76_QurrAxTmBkNyMdIVg3FvYjAID2dJ1dKyF76k=s96-c","bio":"Jean-Laurent de Morlhon is SVP, GenAI Acceleration at Docker, where he focuses on making AI application and agent development simpler for developers.<br>Over more than nine years at Docker, he has held several engineering leadership roles, helping build and scale teams working on developer tools and infrastructure.<br>Today, his work focuses on GenAI developer experience, from running models locally to MCP, sandboxes and agentic workflows, with a particular interest in making emerging AI technologies practical, reliable and easy for developers to use.<br>","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"morlhon.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T08:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T08:50:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"mnoUXIT4nMY","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"De Morlhon presents Docker's sandbox platform for coding agents (microVMs with filesystem and network control, shareable Kits, cloud sandboxes) and the internal 'AI factory' Docker built on it.","summary":["Jean-Laurent de Morlhon frames the talk around four questions: can you trust an agent, repeat its setup, run it without you, and build on it? Each agent runs in its own microVM with its own kernel and a Docker daemon, isolated by hardware virtualisation (Intel VT-x, AMD-V). A filesystem manager mounts only chosen directories, and a network proxy enforces policies and injects credentials on outbound calls, so the agent only sees a dummy value. A demo shows the sbx CLI blocking a disallowed site. Docker Kits package a Dockerfile with a kit.yaml of sandbox policies in one OCI image; Docker plans to donate the new spec to the CNCF.","The same sandboxes run in Docker's cloud (sbx move hands a local session over), behind an MCP gateway that can allow individual tools of a server, with a web UI, an OpenAPI API and a TypeScript SDK. Since February Docker has run an internal 'AI factory' on top: agents co-write a design document with humans, a PM agent splits it into Jira tickets, an orchestrator runs four or five implementer agents in parallel, review agents check each PR, and a human is pinged in Slack to approve. Most human time goes into commenting on design documents; reviewing 10,000-line PRs remains a problem."],"keyPoints":["Credentials never enter the sandbox: the network proxy swaps a placeholder for the real API key or SSH credential on outbound calls, limiting the damage from info-stealing packages.","MicroVM isolation is hardware-enforced and boots fast enough to run 6-20 agents side by side on a laptop.","Docker Kits bundle the sandbox image and its policy (allowed hosts, injected secrets, capabilities) under one OCI digest so a team can share the exact agent setup.","The MCP gateway can allow specific tools within an MCP server, for example read but not destructive write operations.","In Docker's internal factory the human effort shifts to design review; implementation is parallelised across sandboxed agents and the output is tracked as artifacts rather than sessions."],"takeaways":["Stop giving agents broad GitHub tokens or raw API keys; run them in a sandbox that injects scoped credentials at the network layer.","Write down network and filesystem policies for your agents and share them as a versioned image, not as personal setup steps.","Try the free sbx CLI to run a coding agent in a microVM on your laptop."],"quotes":[{"text":"I think the real power of sandboxes is that it's more like a seatbelt in your car.","at":"03:17"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Who gives agents their tokens?"},{"at":"03:17","title":"Trust: microVM sandboxes"},{"at":"08:37","title":"Repeat: Docker Kits"},{"at":"11:14","title":"Run without you: cloud sandboxes"},{"at":"13:58","title":"Docker's internal AI factory"}],"tech":["Docker Sandboxes","sbx CLI","MicroVM","Docker Kits","OCI images","MCP","MCP Gateway","Claude Code","Codex","OpenCode","Jira","Slack","TypeScript SDK","CNCF"],"watchIf":"You let coding agents run with real credentials on your machine and want a quick look at how Docker isolates them and orchestrates many in parallel.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Security & supply chain"],"demo":false}},{"id":5502,"title":"Taming the Chaos: Security and Predictability in LLM-Powered Applications","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["guardrails","llms","promptinjection","validation"],"abstract":["LLMs are probabilistic by nature. Ask the same question twice, get different answers. Great for creativity, terrible for business logic. How do you build reliable systems on top of something inherently unreliable?","Let's tackle this together – from security to predictability. We'll look at guardrails that block prompt injection and redact leaked PII before it leaves your system. Then we'll layer on deterministic validation that catches hallucinations with code, not hope. And when the model gets it wrong, we'll feed errors back for self-correction and use multi-model consensus to build confidence. Practical patterns, real code, no magic.","This isn't about making LLMs perfect – it's about building systems that fail safely and predictably when they're not.","The examples are in Java and LangChain4J; the concepts – transferable to other languages and frameworks."],"favourites":172,"speakers":[{"name":"Rustam Mehmandarov","company":"Miles","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GhOVnDcmUBbpNyyl7Wj1PvPKq-eiUQbogYBsSyleQ=s96-c","bio":"Passionate computer scientist. Java Champion, Google Developers Expert for Cloud, and Docker Captain. Public speaker. Community builder. Mentor.","linkedin":"mehmandarov","bluesky":"rustam.no","twitter":"RMehmandarov"}],"start":"2026-10-08T11:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T12:40:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"vNeyNkeU4ms","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Mehmandarov builds a LangChain4j invoice-extraction pipeline layer by layer, adding guardrails, canary tokens, an intent classifier, deterministic validation, self-correction loops, multi-model voting and a mirror test.","summary":["Mehmandarov, a consultant at Miles in Oslo and a Java Champion, structures the talk as 'five stages of LLM grief' (honeymoon, attack, hallucination, bargaining, council) plus a bonus. The running example is a company feeding scanned invoice text to an LLM, getting JSON back and passing it to a payment system. The naive LangChain4j AI service works on day one, then pays a million dollars or an invoice dated 2050. Structured output only gives you 'a well-formatted lie': it shapes the response but validates nothing. The whole deck is Markdown in a GitHub repo, with each Java class linked to its code.","Against attacks such as white-on-white 'ignore all previous instructions' text in a PDF, he stacks defences: an input guardrail with a blocklist, length checks and PII rules; a canary token, a generated UUID the model is told never to reveal, checked by an output guardrail as a tripwire; an output format guardrail; sandwiching untrusted text between tags as data, not instructions (an OWASP-recommended technique, with detection of attempts to close the tag); and a 'bouncer', a cheap small model that classifies intent as data extraction, malicious or unknown before the expensive model sees anything. He also redacts PII on the way out, including from logs. He noted that recent small models already resist naive injections.","Since all of that is still probabilistic, the next layer is code: JSON schema checks, Jakarta Bean Validation for business rules such as invoice dates, and deterministic normalisation of dates, numbers and currencies. He compares function calling with code execution and lists MCP risks: injection through third-party servers, wrong tool selection, schema drift between client and server, and over-broad permissions. For 'bargaining' he loops extract, validate and re-prompt with the errors up to a retry limit. The 'council' votes across several models after normalising their outputs, and a stability analysis reruns with varied temperature and a fixed seed.","The pipeline ends in a verdict of accepted, needs review or rejected. He touches on goal-oriented agents in LangChain4j, where success criteria such as 'valid JSON that passes the validator' drive retries, and on observability for auditing. The final, most expensive check is the mirror test: have a model reconstruct the likely input from the extracted JSON and compare it with the original. The code runs with Maven and JUnit, locally against Ollama."],"keyPoints":["Structured output into a Java object is not validation; it only makes a wrong answer well-formed.","Canary tokens: put a random UUID in the system prompt with instructions never to reveal it, and treat its appearance in output as proof of a successful injection.","Sandwiching wraps untrusted input in tags declared as data only; also detect inputs that try to close the tag, much like SQL injection.","A cheap 'bouncer' model classifying intent keeps attackers from ever reaching the expensive model.","Deterministic Bean Validation and normalisation of dates, amounts and currency codes catch the hallucinations guardrails miss, such as invoices from the future.","Self-correction loops feed validation errors back into the prompt with a hard retry cap; multi-model voting compares normalised outputs and lets the majority win.","MCP adds risks of its own: injection via third-party servers, wrong tool choice, client/server schema drift and excessive permissions, so least privilege still applies.","Mirror test: regenerate the plausible input from the output and compare it to the real input to detect manipulated extractions."],"takeaways":["Add input and output guardrails to your LangChain4j AI services, including PII redaction on output and in logs.","Validate every LLM-produced object with Bean Validation business rules before it reaches a downstream system.","Return a three-way verdict (accept, needs review, reject) instead of failing with a stack trace.","Put a small, cheap intent-classification model in front of your main model for untrusted input.","Keep tool and MCP sets minimal, and only install MCP servers from publishers you trust."],"quotes":[{"text":"It might as well be a well-formatted lie.","at":"07:53"},{"text":"It's not taking down your system, it's taking down your credit card.","at":"20:58"},{"text":"Trust your code, not the model.","at":"43:20"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"LLMs are probabilistic"},{"at":"04:03","title":"The invoice pipeline"},{"at":"07:53","title":"Input guardrails"},{"at":"10:35","title":"Canary tokens and output guardrails"},{"at":"14:26","title":"Sandwiching and the bouncer"},{"at":"21:59","title":"Defence in depth and schemas"},{"at":"24:28","title":"Deterministic validation"},{"at":"26:43","title":"Tools, code execution and MCP"},{"at":"32:36","title":"Self-correction and model voting"},{"at":"40:40","title":"Mirror test and summary"}],"tech":["LangChain4j","Jakarta Bean Validation","OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","MCP","Ollama","JUnit","Maven","JSON Schema"],"watchIf":"You are putting LLM output into a business process in Java and want a catalogue of layered guardrail and validation patterns with code you can copy.","themes":["Security & supply chain","LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":false}},{"id":15354,"title":"Will Valhalla Fix Tony's Billion-Dollar Mistake?","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["performance","valhalla","value classes","jvm"],"abstract":["In March 2027, in JDK 28 (JVM gods willing!), after more than 12 years of effort, the Valhalla project will deliver its first feature: Value Classes (JEP 401) as a preview feature. Value classes are defined as regular classes with a \"value\" keyword in front. They are backward compatible with your code written before Java 28, without you having to recompile it.","Value classes bring new optimization opportunities at the JVM level: scalarization on stack and flattening on heap. For that, the Java specification had to be reinterpreted and enhanced, challenging us to reconsider some of the fundamentals on which Java was created 30 years ago. What does == mean? Is a reference always a pointer? Can an instance of a value class be null? Can you observe the transition between the default values and the final values of fields during object construction? And more.","This presentation covers all these questions, showing the trade-offs that need to be made in order to bring value classes to the Java platform.","You will hear about new code patterns, performance, and type safety. And, as value classes are just the first step, we will cover what Valhalla should bring in the future."],"favourites":187,"speakers":[{"name":"José Paumard","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GhsVIUuTR9Onoo-D8UMW5QId2Yw1V_EUvqdWdNQ0RQ=s96-c","bio":"José is a Java Developer Advocate at Oracle. He holds a PhD in applied mathematics and computer science and is a Java Champion alumnus. He has been an assistant professor at the University Sorbonne Paris Nord for 25 years. He is an active member of the Paris Java User Group, and a disorganizer of the JChateau unconference. He contributes Java learning content on the dev.java website. He publishes &quot;Java Coding Tips&quot;, a biweekly series of shorts, and the &quot;JEP Café&quot;, a video series, both on the Java YouTube channel.","linkedin":"jos%C3%A9-paumard-2458ba5","bluesky":"josepaumard.bsky.social","twitter":null},{"name":"Remi Forax","company":"University Gustave Eiffel","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocK8ScVAfwuF-N0FTBQLG4RDPeS7vzgzVc_VNU0pEJrFPgQ32w=s96-c","bio":"Tenured associate professor at Université Gustave Eiffel (Paris) and longtime OpenJDK member researching runtime environments (Java VM, .NET). He has contributed to major Java features including invokedynamic (JSR 292), Lambda (JSR 335), and the Jigsaw module system (JSR 376), and currently works on OpenJDK&#39;s Amber (records, pattern matching) and Valhalla (value types) projects. He&#39;s also the main contributor to the ASM bytecode library.<br>A frequent conference speaker (Devoxx France, FOSDEM), he regularly presents on modern Java features like virtual threads, structured concurrency, and value types.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-06T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T10:30:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"vLDbzmiT7Xs","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Paumard and Forax explain JEP 401 value classes, previewing in JDK 28: what giving up identity changes, how scalarization and flattening perform, and why null-safety remains unsolved.","summary":["Paumard and Forax open with Tony Hoare's 'billion-dollar mistake' and the news that JEP 401 (value objects) is in the JDK 28 early-access builds as a preview, alongside a companion JVM JEP for strict field initialization. Valhalla aims to give classes the benefits of primitives, a dense memory layout and no pointer chasing, and to make primitive-like types such as Float16 possible. Their core argument: identity is an address. Without an address there is no header and no mutation, so a value class is implicitly final with final fields. You declare one by putting 'value' in front of a class or record.","Forax's demos show what changes. == on value objects compares fields recursively, so it is no longer cheap. The default hashCode is derived from the fields. synchronized fails to compile, and through an Object reference it throws IdentityException at runtime, as does creating a WeakReference. Value class fields are strict: they must be assigned before super(), using the flexible constructor bodies from JDK 25, which the speakers recommend for all classes. Value classes may only extend abstract value classes or Object; Record and Number are now abstract value classes.","A JMH mixtape benchmark sums song durations three ways. With value records, the object loop matches the primitive loop and triggers no GC, because the JIT scalarizes objects into registers, even across calls and returns. Streams gain nothing, since erasure hides the value type. Heap flattening is a trade-off: atomic access limits it to about 64 bits including a null-marker byte, hence a short rather than an int duration. For 100-element arrays, decoding flattened data was slower than chasing pointers; flattening only won between 1,000 and 10,000 elements. In a JFR trace of a full application, allocation fell from about 107 GB to a few MB.","javac only clears a repurposed identity flag, marks fields strict and adds a LoadableDescriptors attribute so classes load early, so existing code benefits without recompiling. With --enable-preview on JDK 28, Integer, Optional, LocalDate, Duration and other value-based classes become value classes. The last hour covers explorations that may never ship: '!' null-restricted types, prototyped but set aside because nullness is a storage property and it changes overload resolution, and primitive classes with no encapsulation, non-atomic flattening, a default value and possibly operator overloading. Their answer to the title question is no."],"keyPoints":["JEP 401 value objects are planned as a preview in JDK 28 (March 2027), building on JEP 513 flexible constructor bodies (final in JDK 25) and a JVM JEP for strict field initialization.","Value objects have no header: == compares fields recursively, hashCode is field-based, and synchronized or WeakReference on them fails, with an IdentityException at runtime.","Strict fields must be initialized before super(), so 'this' can never be observed half-built; the speakers advise writing all constructors that way from JDK 25 on.","Scalarization lets value objects in loops match primitive speed with zero GC, but generic code such as streams loses it because of erasure.","Heap flattening requires atomic reads, which limits it to roughly 64 bits of payload plus an 8-bit null marker; larger value objects are scalarized but not flattened.","Flattened arrays use less memory but cost mask-and-shift decoding; for small arrays pointers were faster, with the crossover between 1,000 and 10,000 elements.","Value classes are a JIT optimization with unchanged bytecode, so old code benefits without recompiling; recompiling adds the LoadableDescriptors attribute for better layouts.","Null-restricted '!' types were prototyped and set aside for Valhalla; primitive classes (non-null, non-atomic, default value) are still an exploration."],"takeaways":["Download a JDK 28 early-access build and run your code with --enable-preview to see the effect of Integer, Optional and LocalDate becoming value classes.","Initialize all fields before calling super() in constructors, using flexible constructor bodies on JDK 25 and later.","Stop synchronizing on, or taking weak references to, objects you do not own, especially wrappers, strings and value-based JDK types.","Drop 'this == other' shortcuts from equals methods; with value classes == is no longer cheap.","Keep candidate value records small (for example a short instead of an int) if you want them flattened in arrays."],"quotes":[{"text":"Code like a class, works like an int.","at":"127:43"},{"text":"Will Valhalla fix Tony's billion-dollar mistake? Short answer, no. Sorry, Tony.","at":"157:22"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Tony Hoare and null"},{"at":"08:33","title":"Goals of Valhalla"},{"at":"16:45","title":"Identity, address and mutability"},{"at":"38:23","title":"Demo: equality, hashCode, synchronization"},{"at":"53:11","title":"Strict fields and constructors"},{"at":"68:43","title":"Abstract value classes"},{"at":"73:21","title":"Scalarization benchmarks"},{"at":"90:18","title":"Heap flattening trade-offs"},{"at":"116:08","title":"Class files and migration"},{"at":"129:48","title":"Null-restriction and primitive classes"}],"tech":["Project Valhalla","JEP 401","JEP 513","JDK 28","Value classes","Records","JMH","JFR","HotSpot JIT","Project Lilliput","Vector API","Float16","IntelliJ IDEA"],"watchIf":"You want a precise picture of what value classes change in Java semantics and performance before they preview in JDK 28, including the limitations.","themes":["Java language & JDK","JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":true}},{"id":50102,"title":"From Brainless to Brilliant — Giving an Open-Source Robot a Mind of Its Own","type":"Keynote","duration":20,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["firmware","autonomy","privacy","robot"],"abstract":["It arrives in a box, in pieces, and every part of it is open: hardware, firmware, simulator, SDK. An hour with a screwdriver and there is a robot on your kitchen table. What it does not arrive with is a mind.","So I bought one, and I wrote it a brain called AURA. No lab, no team, just a laptop and some evenings.","AURA knows the people in my house and treats each of them differently. What we told it stays separate from what it worked out on its own. Given a task, it opens VS Code, puts Claude Code to work on a real repository, and asks my permission before anything is committed.","It still gets things wrong. Nothing in the design promises otherwise; what it promises is that nothing sensitive happens without me saying yes.","The robot is not the point. The point is how little it now takes to build one, and that quietly changes who gets to build anything at all. I would rather show you that than argue it, so there will be demos, and no promises about all of them behaving."],"favourites":431,"speakers":[{"name":"Jan Van Wassenhove","company":"Sopra Steria","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-e7b52977-dc36-455d-a814-67a86c6644ab.jpg","bio":"Jan Van Wassenhove is the Technical Director of the System Integrations Business Line for the BeLux region at Sopra Steria, where he drives technology solutions and services, supported by his team of architects. With a strong background in Java development, Jan has evolved into an experienced architect and project manager, with in-depth expertise in Agile methodologies.<br><br>Remarkably, he is also the initiator of “SCRUM”, a new programming language he conceived. This creation perfectly reflects his innovative mindset and constant drive to explore new technological territories — and proves, with a wink, that everyone really can become a SCRUM master… in code.<br><br>Throughout his career, he has contributed to numerous projects in both the public and private sectors, with a particular focus on banking and public administrations.","linkedin":"jan-van-wassenhove-9b49893","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T07:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T08:10:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"ZDocIlx7Zvg","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Van Wassenhove shows AURA, a laptop-hosted brain for a 300-euro Reachy Mini robot that recognises family members, separates facts from memories, codes in VS Code, and needs approval for anything sensitive.","summary":["Jan Van Wassenhove assembled a Reachy Mini, the open-source desktop robot from Hugging Face and Pollen Robotics, on his kitchen table in an evening with one screwdriver. It arrives without a mind, so he wrote AURA, a brain that runs on his laptop: the robot only streams camera and microphone events, and all state is kept encrypted on the laptop, so the robot itself holds no secrets. Using a stand-in persona called Mila, he replays a day. AURA recognises Mila and greets her using facts he entered plus memories it derived from past conversations. Her son Luca is tagged as a minor, so nothing about him is remembered beyond the facts given. In 'home mode' it refuses to reveal his work agenda, and in 'presenting mode' it has extra guardrails so it doesn't keep interrupting.","The robot then goes 'outside the box': asked to fix AURA's own skills screen, which made one API call per skill, it opens VS Code, has a coding agent rewrite the code to a single call, runs the tests, and then asks permission before committing, which Van Wassenhove refuses on stage. AURA also notices that across 15 morning briefings he always skipped the weather and news, and proposes a shorter procedure that only takes effect once he approves. Its 'hands' are tools such as VS Code, Spotify or speaker control. His broader point is that the floor is moving as well as the ceiling: compared with the 40,000-euro humanoid from the opening, this robot cost about 300 euros and was built by someone with a day job and no robotics background. The architecture is covered in his separate talk later the same day."],"keyPoints":["Everything runs on the laptop, not the robot; captured events are interpreted by multiple AI models and stored encrypted, so stealing the robot yields only motors and servos.","AURA keeps facts the family gave it separate from memories it derived itself, and stores no memories for people tagged as minors.","Modes (home, presenting) gate which data and tools are available; for example, the work agenda is unavailable in home mode.","Self-improvement is proposal-only: AURA can rewrite procedures such as the morning briefing or modify its own code, but nothing changes or gets committed without explicit human approval.","Van Wassenhove's argument: hardware has become cheap enough (about 300 euros) that a hobbyist can build an agentic robot, which changes who gets to build things."],"takeaways":["Put a human approval gate in front of every action that leaves the sandbox, such as commits or procedure changes, because agents will find reasons to do what you didn't intend.","Separate user-provided facts from model-derived memories and apply different retention rules, especially for minors.","Use explicit operating modes to limit which data and tools an assistant can reach in a given context."],"quotes":[{"text":"If you just let your agents go, they will come up with a good excuse to still execute the thing you don't want to do. So those gates are quite important.","at":"09:40"},{"text":"It's not about the ceiling which is moving. At the same pace, we've got the floor which is moving.","at":"13:25"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"A robot in a box"},{"at":"03:21","title":"AURA, the laptop brain"},{"at":"04:21","title":"Recognising the family"},{"at":"07:10","title":"Modes and guardrails"},{"at":"08:37","title":"Coding with approval gates"},{"at":"11:00","title":"Self-improving procedures"},{"at":"13:25","title":"The floor is moving"}],"tech":["Reachy Mini","Hugging Face","Pollen Robotics","VS Code","Claude Code","Spotify"],"watchIf":"You want a 15-minute live look at a hobbyist-built agentic robot with sensible privacy and approval guardrails.","themes":["Hardware, robots & fun","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":true}},{"id":5519,"title":"The Magic of OpenTelemetry: Rewriting Your App in Production","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["instrumentation","bytebuddy","javaagent","opentelemetry"],"abstract":["OpenTelemetry makes traces, metrics, and logs appear as if by magic, but what actually happens behind the Java agent we routinely add to our applications?","This talk goes where most developers never look. You’ll see how the OpenTelemetry agent sneaks into your JVM, intercepts class loading, and rewrites your application on the fly. We’ll explore the power of the javaagent mechanism, the Instrumentation API, and the internals of ByteBuddy that allow instrumenting Spring, Servlets, JDBC, or gRPC without changing a single line of your code. Your application is running, but not quite the way you think it is.","You’ll leave with a clear understanding of the real impact these changes can have in production: latency, allocations, potential conflicts, and behavior modified at runtime, along with practical techniques to diagnose what is really happening under the hood.","If you’re using OpenTelemetry without really knowing what it’s doing inside your JVM, or if you want to finally understand how an agent can rewrite your application in production, then welcome: this session opens the black box… and reveals the magic."],"favourites":197,"speakers":[{"name":"Bruce Bujon","company":"Datadog","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-ddf330d5-d335-49d8-89b6-2d852531100e.jpg","bio":"Bruce Bujon is a Senior Software Engineer at Datadog, and lives near Paris. He spent about 15 years building distributed systems using the Java ecosystem. Today, he also leads the Paris JUG, and contributes to Open Source projects related to privacy like its ad-blocker AdAway. When he is not working, he is probably reverse engineering some applications or soldering the quirky prototype of its next gadget.","linkedin":"bruce-bujon","bluesky":"hardcoding.fr","twitter":"HardCoding"}],"start":"2026-10-06T15:35:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T16:05:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"Lf_m5dIjjqw","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Bujon shows how the OpenTelemetry Java agent rewrites classes as they load, diffs its changes with a meta-agent, and measures the startup cost, including under Leyden.","summary":["Bujon, a Datadog engineer who leads the Paris JUG, instruments a demo app built on the JDK HTTP server and client and the Jedis Redis client, none of which know OpenTelemetry. He adds the agent through JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS in Docker Compose and gets server, client and Redis spans with automatic attributes. He then explains the mechanism: premain receives an Instrumentation instance and registers a ClassFileTransformer that sees, and may rewrite, the bytes of every loaded class. Rewriting means ASM or Byte Buddy, since OpenTelemetry supports Java 8 and cannot use the JDK 24 ClassFile API, and the JVM throws a VerifyError if the result is invalid.","To show what really runs, he puts Johannes Bechberger's meta-agent in front of the OpenTelemetry agent; it serves decompiled before-and-after diffs. The JDK HTTP server gets a filter injected, ForkJoinPool's execute and submit gain extra work, and Jedis grows from about 5,000 to 43,000 lines. Costs include allocation and GC pressure, CPU, slower hot paths, thread contention and, specific to agents, startup spent matching classes. Native Image support is partial, and AppCDS and Leyden do not work with the OpenTelemetry agent. On PetClinic an agent cost 50-75% in time to first response; Datadog's agent with a Leyden AOT cache got back to baseline."],"keyPoints":["The agent is zero-code instrumentation: one -javaagent flag (for example via JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS) plus OTEL_* configuration, and it instruments libraries such as Jedis and the JDK HTTP client that know nothing about OpenTelemetry.","ClassFileTransformer.transform receives the class loader, class name and class-file byte array for every loaded class and returns rewritten bytes, so the code running in production is not the code you compiled or downloaded.","Johannes Bechberger's meta-agent, run before the OpenTelemetry agent, shows decompiled diffs of every transformed class, including JDK internals like ForkJoinPool.","Startup is the agent-specific cost: every loaded class is matched against hundreds of instrumentation patterns, which Bujon measured as a 50-75% slower time to first response on PetClinic.","Bujon said the Datadog agent works with a Leyden AOT cache and matches no-agent startup, whereas the OpenTelemetry agent currently crashes under Leyden."],"takeaways":["Try OpenTelemetry on an existing container by adding -javaagent through JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS before writing any SDK code.","Run the meta-agent alongside your observability agent to see which classes it rewrites, including JDK classes.","Measure startup and time to first response with and without the agent, especially for serverless or autoscaled services.","Sample and filter spans deliberately (for example 1% plus all application errors) to limit CPU and allocation overhead on hot paths."],"quotes":[{"text":"The code that runs is no longer the code you wrote and compiled, or the code you downloaded, or even the code from the JDK you carefully kept updated and paid support for.","at":"13:46"},{"text":"If you don't use OpenTelemetry today, your observability journey is only one line away.","at":"28:27"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"OpenTelemetry for Java"},{"at":"03:09","title":"Zero-code agent demo"},{"at":"08:08","title":"How Java agents work"},{"at":"12:25","title":"Diffing with the meta-agent"},{"at":"21:34","title":"Runtime performance costs"},{"at":"22:55","title":"Startup overhead and Leyden"},{"at":"26:58","title":"Wrap-up"}],"tech":["OpenTelemetry","OTLP","OpenTelemetry Collector","Java agents","java.lang.instrument","ClassFileTransformer","ASM","Byte Buddy","ClassFile API","meta-agent","Jedis","Redis","Docker Compose","GraalVM Native Image","AppCDS","Project Leyden","Spring PetClinic","Datadog"],"watchIf":"You run the OpenTelemetry Java agent in production and want to see what it changes inside your JVM and what it costs at startup.","themes":["JVM performance & runtime","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":49251,"title":"The Year of the Runtime","type":"Keynote","duration":20,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["java","runtime","jvm","aicoding"],"abstract":["AI coding agents are changing how we develop software. Existing evidence suggests that, up to a point, they significantly reduce the importance of the linguistic features of programming languages, and are able to write code equally well in most if not all mainstream languages. But a programming language is much more than syntax. An AI agent can't make Python code as fast as Java code, and it can't make Java code run as well as JavaScript in the browser. In a world where syntax is less important, the runtime gains even more prominence, and Java's runtime is a superb target for code written by, or with the assistance of, AI.","Java's runtime - the JVM and the standard library - has been Java's core strength since Java's inception. Java programs get faster and easier to operate just by upgrading to a newer JDK, thanks to continually improving JIT optimizations, better GCs, better observability, and a better standard library. This is an especially dynamic time for the Java runtime, integrating value classes from Project Valhalla, AOT code compilation from Project Leyden, making compact object headers the default from Project Lilliput, and removing the need to manually set the heap size so that the JVM could automatically optimise the CPU/RAM tradeoff. Each of these involved tremendous change under the hood, but little or no change to the programming model."],"favourites":457,"speakers":[{"name":"Ron Pressler","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-be6279f3-d001-4451-baa8-03a4ca9af02d.jpg","bio":"Ron is an architect in the Java Platform Group at Oracle","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T08:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T09:10:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"tle2mA7EwWU","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Pressler argues that if AI makes language syntax matter less, the runtime matters more, and runs through the JVM's recent and upcoming GC, Valhalla, Leyden, jlink, integrity and JFR improvements.","summary":["Ron Pressler opens by challenging the idea that AI suits software because programs are mechanically verifiable: nobody ships long-lived software just because it passes a test suite, since programs must evolve, and no mechanical check ensures evolvability. He grants that syntax matters less than ever, but notes that javac, javadoc and jshell are only about 5% of the JDK; the other 95% is runtime. The JVM's design bet is that a big layer between code and hardware resolves the tension between maintainability, which wants details hidden until runtime, and performance and security, which want those details known. GCs and JITs are optimizers, not overheads, and he quotes Gosling's description of Java as a wolf in sheep's clothing: an innovative runtime in a conservative language.","He then tours the runtime. Generational ZGC (JDK 21) removed GC pauses, shown with a Netflix error-rate plot, and an upcoming JEP sets heap size automatically to balance CPU and RAM. Compact object headers (64 bits) are the default in JDK 27, and the first Valhalla JEP brings flattened value objects in arrays, removing pointer chasing. Project Leyden's AOT cache stores profiles in JDK 27 and will also cache compiled code, so programs start near peak performance. He urges people to jlink their own runtime (about 35 MB minimum), mentions work on single-file executables and 'integrity by default', and praises JFR, which AI agents use and analyse very well. His conclusion: AI is good at self-contained tasks but weaker at evolving large programs while keeping them fast, and the runtime gives AI-written code the same benefits it gives human code."],"keyPoints":["The language front end is about 5% of the JDK; Pressler's case for Java in the AI era rests on the other 95%, the runtime.","Generational ZGC eliminated GC pauses; an upcoming JEP removes manual heap sizing by balancing CPU and RAM against workload and other processes on the machine.","JDK 27 makes 64-bit compact object headers the default, and the first Valhalla JEP enables flattening value objects into arrays to avoid cache-missing pointer chasing.","Leyden's AOT cache in JDK 27 caches profiling data; the upcoming code cache stores compiled machine code so programs start near peak performance and reach first request faster.","jlink can produce a minimal runtime of about 35 MB, and work is underway on a single native executable bundling app and runtime.","JFR gained CPU profiling, context propagation across machines, and aggregated views via the jfr CLI; Pressler says AI agents get 'spectacular' results analysing JFR data."],"takeaways":["Upgrade your JDK regularly; most of these performance gains require no code changes.","Spend ten minutes learning jlink and ship a runtime with only the JDK modules you need.","Point your coding agent at JFR recordings when investigating performance or behaviour.","Stay vigilant about AI-generated code collapsing under its own complexity, even in Java."],"quotes":[{"text":"Nobody releases software, especially software that is expected to last, once it passes some tests that meet some coverage criteria.","at":"00:33"},{"text":"It's not clear whether AI makes languages obsolete, but if it makes the language less important, it also makes runtimes more important.","at":"12:52"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Does the language matter?"},{"at":"01:53","title":"The JDK is 95% runtime"},{"at":"04:41","title":"Garbage collection"},{"at":"07:17","title":"Object headers and Valhalla"},{"at":"08:36","title":"Leyden and jlink"},{"at":"09:57","title":"Integrity and JFR"},{"at":"12:52","title":"Runtimes in the AI era"}],"tech":["JVM","Generational ZGC","Project Valhalla","Project Leyden","Project Lilliput","Compact object headers","JIT","jlink","JDK Flight Recorder","JDK 27"],"watchIf":"You want a 15-minute overview of where the JVM runtime is heading and Oracle's argument for why it matters more with AI coding.","themes":["JVM performance & runtime","Java language & JDK"],"demo":false}},{"id":7032,"title":"Don’t burn your fingers in the heat of the AI (r)evolution. (And get an OpenClaw).","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["observability","agentic","governance","enterprise"],"abstract":["“You get an OpenClaw, you get an OpenClaw, and you get an OpenClaw.” (And you’ll get an OpenClaw). What sounds like a joke is actually an experiment: can we make agentic AI available at enterprise level, with the security, governance, observability, and platform boundaries needed to make it safe?","AI demos are easy. Getting them into production is where things usually break: security, data, observability, ownership, governance, and all the boring-but-important platform work. This talk is about the patterns you need to avoid becoming part of the 95% that never make it past the demo stage. And although we use AI as the example, most of these patterns are just as useful for application modernization.","We will deep dive (with demos!) into the underlying platform patterns: agentic guardrailing, change-data-capture, service mesh, model-as-a-service, sandboxed AI environments, and MLflow with tracing enabled. We asked developers to shift left for years, but maybe it is time to shift right as well: push the right responsibilities back into the platform, where they can be standardized, automated, governed, and reused.","Let’s embrace AI in an enterprise context."],"favourites":59,"speakers":[{"name":"Maarten Vandeperre","company":"Red Hat","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-909df07a-dfbf-40e8-9065-be12fa662faf.jpg","bio":"Maarten Vandeperre is an experienced software professional who recently joined Red Hat as an Appdev &amp; AI Specialized Solutions Architect. With a strong background in software development and architecture, he brings a wealth of expertise to his role. Maarten&#39;s primary focus is on application development and AI, with a particular emphasis on leveraging Red Hat&#39;s OpenShift platform from a developer&#39;s perspective.<br><br>One of Maarten&#39;s true passions lies in advocating for &quot;clean architecture&quot; as a guiding principle in software development. He firmly believes in the importance of designing software systems that are modular, maintainable, and scalable. As part of his dedication to this approach, Maarten strives to map these principles to infrastructure solutions, ensuring that the underlying technology supports and enhances the overall architecture. His deep understanding of integration technologies, such as API Gateways, Keycloak, Kafka, service mesh, and Camel, enables him to create seamless connections between systems while adhering to clean architectural principles, which empower organizations to thrive in the ever-evolving digital landscape.","linkedin":"maarten-vandeperre","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-06T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T14:30:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"zDqS1U5Gnk8","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":9032,"title":"Agents With Seatbelts: Practical Ways to Keep AI Code Gen Under Control","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["security","metrics","agents","mcp"],"abstract":["In this session I will share best practices for using MCP setups and AI Agents in real development workflows 🤖. The goal is to improve both quality and security, since even with all the excitement around these tools, it’s still very easy to create confusing interactions, accidental data exposure, or Agents that trigger actions we never intended.","I will also present reports comparing how different LLM models generate code, showing clear differences in stability, output consistency, and the amount of guidance each model needs to avoid mistakes.","And last but not least, I will talk about the elephant in the room: metrics 📏. How do we measure our improvement using AI? Is it something companies are doing right? Spoiler alert ... no 😰","The talk focuses on helping teams build more reliable, productive, and secure AI workflows, making AI code generation a real benefit rather than a risky experiment 😅."],"favourites":672,"speakers":[{"name":"Jonathan Vila López","company":"GK Software","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-f359c583-9652-403e-8cf9-20a2e4265cef.jpg","bio":"International Speaker, JavaChampion, Cofounder of JBCNConf and DevBcn conferences in Barcelona, and AI4Devs conference in Amsterdam.<br><br>Working as a Principal AI Development Enabler at GK Software, taking care of the AI Adoption with best practices and tooling and helping the company as the Engineering Excellence Lead.<br><br>Formerly Senior Staff Developer Advocate in Java at Sonar (SonarQube), focused on Code Quality, Dev Productivity, AI &amp; Security.<br><br>I have worked as a (paid) developer for more than 30 years ago using multiple languajes, but for the last 15 using Java. Although I started when I was 14 with my Amstrad CPC 6128 :)<br><br>I am very interested in simulated reality, psychology, philosophy, critical thinking, AI and Java.","linkedin":"jonathanvila","bluesky":null,"twitter":"vilojona"}],"start":"2026-10-08T08:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"d1SJXp9QeWE","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Vila argues that AI coding slows teams down when done naively. He lists practical guardrails (decomposition, model choice, specs, skills over MCP, sandboxing, deterministic checks) and calls for measuring AI's value end to end.","summary":["Vila, formerly at Sonar and now AI enabler at GK Software, opens with figures from several reports: AI making developers 19% slower, 0% improvement in DORA metrics, and 44% trust. He says these numbers reflect AI done badly. Causes include low suggestion acceptance, senior developers' implicit domain knowledge never reaching the agent, ever-larger outputs from token-billed models, and poisoned long contexts. Done well, reports show 55% faster work and 30% more code. Yet in his company's AI discovery sessions every team claims to feel faster and none has a baseline to prove it.","His practices start with decomposition: plan with a big model, and use sub-agents defined by function (docs, tests, implementation), each pinned to a smaller model, instead of Opus for every loop. Model choice should follow data. Sonar's leaderboard of more than 4,000 Java tasks analysed with SonarQube shows no model with zero issues or vulnerabilities. GLM-5 had the lowest bug and issue density, and Kimi K2 produced about half the lines of Claude Opus 4.5 at a similar pass rate. Prompts should include negative constraints (\"don't use Spring\"). Specs should be written out with entity models, JSON examples, UML diagrams and acceptance tests. Frameworks such as Simon Martinelli's AI Unified Process, Spec Kit, OpenSpec or BMAD are worth adopting only once the team is mature with specs.","A quiz on an income-tax method made the point that an agent writing tests from the code alone will happily assert the bug, a negative tax, because code is not the source of truth. He recommends skills over local MCP servers: 25 servers with 20 tools each load 500 tool descriptions into every context, while a skill adds one line. MCP risks include leaked secrets from files in context, look-alike \"double agent\" servers (a dozen GitHub MCPs with identical descriptions), CVEs in dependencies, and tool confusion between GitHub and SonarQube \"issues\". Isolation options are Docker sandboxes, Podman, ToolHive and VS Code's MCP sandbox.","Verification should be deterministic. He found a team whose 10,000-line skill duplicated what their SonarQube Enterprise already checked. Use hooks to block PII and run analysis on agent output, use SonarQube for IDE, make quality gates blocking, and package shared skills and instructions with APM. Team maturity matters more than individual skill, and he suggests tracking Slack/Teams message volume as a sign of developers isolating themselves with agents. Faros data shows more code but fewer deployments, bigger pull requests, double the incidents per PR and four times the review time. Measure from end to end with DORA or DX Core 4."],"keyPoints":["Plan with a large model and implement with smaller ones. Define sub-agents by function (tests, docs, implementation), not by human role.","Sonar's 4,000-task Java leaderboard: no model produces zero vulnerabilities. GLM-5 introduced the fewest bugs, and Claude models produce far more lines than Kimi K2 or Gemini at a similar pass rate.","More generated lines means more review work, so output volume is a selection criterion, not just pass rate.","Specs should carry entity models, JSON examples, sequence diagrams and acceptance tests. Code alone lets an agent cement bugs into tests.","Prefer skills over many local MCP servers: each MCP tool description sits in context on every call, while a skill costs one line until used.","MCP threats: secret leakage, look-alike servers on marketplaces, CVEs in dependencies, and ambiguous tool overlap. Sandbox them with Docker, ToolHive or VS Code.","Use AI only where reasoning is needed. Run Sonar, Snyk and GitHub Advanced Security as deterministic checks, triggered by agent hooks and enforced by blocking quality gates.","Faros data: more output but fewer deployments, double the incidents per PR and four times the review time, so measure from end to end with DORA or DX Core 4."],"takeaways":["Measure a productivity baseline now, covering the whole delivery pipeline and not just code generation.","Pin sub-agents for routine tasks to cheaper models and keep the largest model for planning.","Replace locally installed MCP servers with skills where possible, and sandbox the ones you keep.","Add an agent hook that runs SonarQube or another linter on generated code, and make your CI quality gate blocking.","Write negative constraints and acceptance criteria into your specs before asking an agent to implement."],"quotes":[{"text":"If you have shit, AI will multiply that and you will have a big pile of shit.","at":"42:02"},{"text":"AI will give you time, potentially, but it will never give you confidence.","at":"44:34"},{"text":"We only need AI when we need reasoning. But if not, use deterministic tools.","at":"38:54"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why AI makes us slower"},{"at":"08:34","title":"No baseline, no metrics"},{"at":"10:44","title":"Decompose and choose models"},{"at":"17:33","title":"Sonar model leaderboard"},{"at":"20:50","title":"Constraints and specs before code"},{"at":"26:59","title":"Code is not the source of truth"},{"at":"30:34","title":"Skills, MCP and security"},{"at":"36:25","title":"Sandboxing and deterministic guardrails"},{"at":"42:02","title":"Team maturity and measuring value"}],"tech":["MCP","Claude Code","Claude Opus","GLM-5","Kimi K2","Gemini","SonarQube","SonarQube for IDE","Snyk","GitHub Advanced Security","Docker Sandboxes","ToolHive","Podman","Spec Kit","OpenSpec","BMAD Method","AI Unified Process","APM","Cucumber","DORA metrics","DX Core 4","Faros AI"],"watchIf":"You are rolling out coding agents in a company and need concrete guardrails and an argument for measuring whether they actually pay off.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Security & supply chain","Testing & quality"],"demo":false}},{"id":10043,"title":"What Does it Print? The Java Quiz (Hard Edition 🤯)","type":"BOF","duration":60,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["subtleties","java","pitfalls","snippets"],"abstract":["Java is full of subtleties, pitfalls, and rules we think we know until we have to predict what a piece of code will actually print.","In this interactive session, we will walk through a series of short Java snippets to analyze together.","The goal is to learn, revisit core concepts, challenge assumptions, and better understand how the Java language really behaves."],"favourites":95,"speakers":[{"name":"Clément de Tastes","company":"SCIAM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-0a33a77f-9982-4862-8714-ad3c32701c94.jpg","bio":"Passionate about software development since getting my first programmable calculator, I have built a solid experience as a tech lead and architect in designing embedded combat systems.<br>An advocate of clean code, &quot;developer joy&quot; and well-thought-out architectures, I enjoy working on design topics as well as front-end or back-end development.<br>I recently founded a &quot;JUG-like&quot; meetup in southern France called &quot;Toulon Dev&quot;","linkedin":"clement-de-tastes","bluesky":"cdetastes.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T16:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T17:50:00Z","room":"BOF 1","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"An interactive BOF where attendees analyse short, tricky Java snippets together and predict what they print, to test assumptions about the language.","summary":["Clément de Tastes leads an interactive session built around short Java snippets whose output is easy to get wrong. The group analyses each one together, relying on the subtleties and pitfalls that catch even experienced developers.","The stated goal is to learn, revisit core concepts, challenge assumptions and better understand how the Java language really behaves. This is the hard edition, aimed at an advanced audience."],"keyPoints":["Series of short Java snippets analysed collaboratively.","Focus on subtleties and pitfalls of the language.","Participants predict output before discussing the actual behaviour.","Advanced level."],"takeaways":["Re-read the language spec on the rules you only think you know."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Java"],"watchIf":"You enjoy Java puzzlers and want to test how well you know the language.","themes":["Java language & JDK"],"demo":false}},{"id":31453,"title":"Architecture Observability and Guardrails","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["code quality","ai-generated","code review","testing"],"abstract":["How do you observe and enforce the code quality to prevent AI Slop? The majority of the teams attending my workshops report 100% of their code is AI-generated already for many months. Some even report they often have to fix PRs that business vibe-coded. This talk summarizes the design feedback loops they setup for their agents, as well as techniques to maximize the human mental bandwidth in the code review step. You’ll hear about C4 model, plantuml diagrams, captured sequence diagrams, complexity visualisation, and efficient testing practices."],"favourites":855,"speakers":[{"name":"Victor Rentea","company":"Freelance","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-51aee41f-0079-4820-98bb-56e70a2e89d3.jpg","bio":"With 2 decades of software engineering experience, Victor spent the last 10 years training thousands of developers and architects from 150+ companies. Since early 2026, he’s been working hard to help his clients adopt Agentic Engineering, coaching AI Champions, developers, testers, product owners, ops, and managers with practical, actionable advice to kick-start their agentic reconversion. Every year, Victor gives energetic, intense conference talks in which he distills his training sessions - so be sure not to miss these ideas, heavily brainstormed with millennia of total experience. More about him on<a href=\"https://victorrentea.ro\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\"> </a><u style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(17, 85, 204);\"><a href=\"https://victorrentea.ro\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">https://victorrentea.ro</a></u>, and <u style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(17, 85, 204);\"><a href=\"https://agentic.how\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">https://agentic.how</a></u>.","linkedin":"victor-rentea-trainer","bluesky":null,"twitter":"victorrentea"}],"start":"2026-10-07T14:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T15:30:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"BOcNjR2lBus","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Using no slides, Rentea demos a vibe-coded review dashboard that helps humans review AI-written pull requests, built on deterministic artefacts generated from code and test runs.","summary":["Rentea presents with zero slides and continues from a session the previous day that ended with a human facing a 1,000-line AI pull request. Everything happens in an open-source review tool he vibe-coded without reading its code: a Python server, a generated HTML report and a VS Code extension (IntelliJ planned) that jumps to the relevant lines. The demo PR on a Spring PetClinic microservices setup was built by Claude Code agents. The report lists implementation assumptions, 14 open review issues and 25 auto-fixed findings from a quorum of five adversarial reviewers (/code-review plus /simplify). Per-stage costs were about $15 for implementation, $5 for review and $9 for auto-fixes. His rules: review with a smarter model than the one that wrote the code, let the coder agent reject nits, and rebase before calling in a human.","His main point is that most of the report is produced by scripts, not by an LLM, so it does not drift or hallucinate. PlantUML domain diagrams come from Java reflection, or from a schema dump after running migrations on an empty Testcontainers database, with additions highlighted in the PR diff. oasdiff flags breaking OpenAPI changes, which matters when one DTO is shared by several endpoints. Draw.io PNGs carry their XML inside the file, so a test can check the diagram against the code. When AI changes the diagram, it is marked for a human to re-lay it out, which preserves the reader's spatial memory. Coverage is collected per changed line, so the reviewer sees exactly which tests ran through the diff. AI is used only for the semantic check of tests against the ticket.","For runtime behaviour, end-to-end tests run against the services with the OpenTelemetry Java agent, browser instrumentation and 100% sampling into Grafana. An annotation on a test starts a trace, and a script turns the traces into PlantUML sequence diagrams showing REST requests and responses, SQL with parameters and cross-service calls. One diagram exposed a Hibernate N+1 query. JVM flags let OpenTelemetry record calls across module JARs, which shows the real dependencies in a modular monolith, and @WithSpan works even on private methods. Other tabs include Playwright Trace Viewer recordings, ArchUnit checks against PlantUML package diagrams, C4 container diagrams checked against observed calls, and a CodeCity-style 3D view of size, branches, churn, bug-fix commits, coupling and acceptance coverage.","He closes with smaller guardrails: programmatic UI checks for hand-rolled combo boxes and inline styles, IntelliJ-style structural search with a Java parser, and a CODEOWNERS file so that changes to contracts or guardrail files require seniors. He asks open-source users to file issues instead of AI-generated pull requests, since maintainers have more tokens than they do. The talk is chaotic and hard to follow without the screen, but the repositories are public."],"keyPoints":["Ask AI to write a script that generates a diagram, not to draw the diagram. Scripted artefacts cost no tokens to regenerate and cannot hallucinate.","A quorum of adversarial review agents runs after every substantial task, with a smarter model reviewing than writing. The coder agent may reject nits, and auto-fixes are still shown to the human.","PlantUML domain and schema diagrams are generated from reflection or a migrated Testcontainers database, and the diff highlights what the PR added.","Draw.io PNGs embed their XML, so tests can check diagrams against code. Humans re-lay them out by hand to keep the layout familiar.","Per-line coverage of the diff shows exactly which tests exercise the changed code. AI then checks the tests against the ticket's requirements.","The OpenTelemetry Java agent with 100% sampling in end-to-end tests turns traces into sequence diagrams with HTTP payloads and SQL, which exposed an N+1 query.","OpenTelemetry can capture calls across module JARs at runtime, revealing actual dependencies in a modular monolith.","oasdiff detects breaking API changes. ArchUnit, CODEOWNERS and C4 diagrams checked against observed calls act as further architectural guardrails."],"takeaways":["Run an adversarial review agent (or several) on any agent task longer than 10-15 minutes before a human looks at it.","Generate architecture diagrams from code or runtime traces with scripts, and add tests that fail when a diagram drifts.","Attach the OpenTelemetry Java agent to your end-to-end test environment and inspect the traces for N+1 queries and unexpected cross-service calls.","Add oasdiff to CI to catch breaking OpenAPI changes, and a CODEOWNERS rule for contract and guardrail files."],"quotes":[{"text":"You don't ask AI for an opinion, 'draw me a picture'. You ask it to write a script that would produce the diagram tomorrow with zero tokens and with precision.","at":"41:15"},{"text":"Do not dare to submit pull requests to open source anymore. Do submit them issues. They have more tokens than you do.","at":"17:55"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Zero slides and token costs"},{"at":"02:53","title":"Adversarial review quorum"},{"at":"12:36","title":"Test fixtures and demo replay"},{"at":"17:55","title":"API breaking-change detection"},{"at":"20:50","title":"Generated PlantUML and Draw.io tests"},{"at":"28:00","title":"Coverage of the diff"},{"at":"32:33","title":"Traces to sequence diagrams"},{"at":"39:46","title":"ArchUnit, C4 and CodeCity"},{"at":"46:05","title":"UI drift and code owners"}],"tech":["Claude Code","Spring PetClinic","PlantUML","Draw.io","Testcontainers","OpenTelemetry","Grafana","Playwright","ArchUnit","oasdiff","C4 model","CodeCity","Hibernate","Docker","VS Code","IntelliJ IDEA","CODEOWNERS","Mistral"],"watchIf":"You review large AI-generated pull requests and want ideas for deterministic, script-generated review artefacts. Expect a chaotic, slide-free live tour.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Architecture & design","Testing & quality"],"demo":true}},{"id":17794,"title":"Escaping Java's Allocation Gravity with Value Classes","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["valuetypes","java","projectvalhalla","blackhole"],"abstract":["Java 28 will soon introduce, as a preview feature, value classes (JEP 401), a new kind of class that renounces its identity to get better performance.","In this presentation, we will explain what value classes are, how the Java spec was re-interpreted to conjure their existence, how they interact with existing code and what optimizations they enable both on the stack and the heap.","To make it concrete, we'll explore these ideas through the simulation and rendering of a black hole: calculating the trajectories of thousands of light rays through curved spacetime means manipulating huge numbers of vectors, rays, coordinates, and intermediate values.","Finally, we will look ahead at the experimental \"!\" syntax for null-restricted types, its optimization potential, and the limitations of the current design.","Join us to discover value classes and the current state of Project Valhalla."],"favourites":285,"speakers":[{"name":"Clément de Tastes","company":"SCIAM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-0a33a77f-9982-4862-8714-ad3c32701c94.jpg","bio":"Passionate about software development since getting my first programmable calculator, I have built a solid experience as a tech lead and architect in designing embedded combat systems.<br>An advocate of clean code, &quot;developer joy&quot; and well-thought-out architectures, I enjoy working on design topics as well as front-end or back-end development.<br>I recently founded a &quot;JUG-like&quot; meetup in southern France called &quot;Toulon Dev&quot;","linkedin":"clement-de-tastes","bluesky":"cdetastes.bsky.social","twitter":null},{"name":"Remi Forax","company":"University Gustave Eiffel","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocK8ScVAfwuF-N0FTBQLG4RDPeS7vzgzVc_VNU0pEJrFPgQ32w=s96-c","bio":"Tenured associate professor at Université Gustave Eiffel (Paris) and longtime OpenJDK member researching runtime environments (Java VM, .NET). He has contributed to major Java features including invokedynamic (JSR 292), Lambda (JSR 335), and the Jigsaw module system (JSR 376), and currently works on OpenJDK&#39;s Amber (records, pattern matching) and Valhalla (value types) projects. He&#39;s also the main contributor to the ASM bytecode library.<br>A frequent conference speaker (Devoxx France, FOSDEM), he regularly presents on modern Java features like virtual threads, structured concurrency, and value types.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-09T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T08:20:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"Clément de Tastes and Remi Forax explain JEP 401 value classes and their stack and heap optimisations through a black hole ray-tracing simulation, plus a look at null-restricted types.","summary":["The session covers what value classes are, how the Java spec was reinterpreted to allow classes without identity, how they interact with existing code, and which optimisations they enable on the stack and the heap. The abstract says Java 28 will introduce them as a preview feature through JEP 401.","To make it concrete, the speakers use a black hole simulation and rendering, which involves huge numbers of vectors, rays, coordinates and intermediate values along thousands of light-ray trajectories. They finish with the experimental ! syntax for null-restricted types, its optimisation potential and the limits of the current design."],"keyPoints":["Value classes give up identity in exchange for better performance (JEP 401).","Covers how the spec was reinterpreted and how value classes interact with existing code.","Stack and heap optimisations are illustrated by a black hole ray simulation.","Previews the experimental ! syntax for null-restricted types and its limitations."],"takeaways":["Identify allocation-heavy small immutable types in your code that could become value classes."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Project Valhalla","JEP 401","Value classes","Null-restricted types"],"watchIf":"You care about JVM performance and want a concrete Valhalla example beyond slides.","themes":["Java language & JDK","JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":false}},{"id":51601,"title":"Production-Ready Java Containers: Smaller, Faster, Smarter","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["optimization","jlink","java","containers"],"abstract":["Java runs exceptionally well in containers, but simply putting a JAR into a container image leaves a lot of optimization opportunities on the table.","In this session, we’ll explore practical techniques for building leaner and more efficient Java containers. We’ll look at using jlink to create tailored Java runtimes, techniques such as CDS and AOT to improve startup and warmup, JVM container awareness and memory configuration, and container-building practices that reduce image size and improve deployment.","But smaller isn’t always better. Every optimization comes with trade-offs involving startup time, memory consumption, throughput, observability, security, and maintainability.","Using a Java application as our starting point, we’ll progressively optimize its container and measure what actually changes. The goal is not to produce the smallest possible image, but to understand the tools available and make informed engineering decisions about what a better Java container means for your workload."],"favourites":188,"speakers":[{"name":"Albert Attard","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocIR6CijGz4-NnZIdCoCaJA7iBZLFOc6f-jPY4Og8crbEeTpx23_jQ=s96-c","bio":"I’m a passionate Java developer who loves building clean, efficient, and scalable solutions. I enjoy exploring new technologies, continuously learning, and improving my craft. Whether working on backend systems or diving into new frameworks, I strive to write code that makes a real impact. When I&#39;m not coding, you’ll find me experimenting with new ideas and expanding my knowledge.","linkedin":"albertattard","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T15:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T16:30:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"LPhGSgO5eKE","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Attard works through a checklist for production Java containers: pinned and layered images, jlink runtimes on slim or scratch bases, AOT cache, compact object headers, memory flags, lifecycle and hardening.","summary":["Attard, from Oracle's Java team, starts with the environment variables the JVM reads. _JAVA_OPTIONS overrides the command line, JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS is where VM flags belong so one image can run with different settings, and agents need JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS because it is read earlier. Tags such as jdk:25 on the Oracle Container Registry are mutable, so he pins images by digest. Using Dive, he compares a Spring Boot fat JAR, one 56 MB layer, with the extracted Spring layers, where dependencies take 56 MB and the application an 18 KB final layer, so a code change rebuilds and ships only that.","Next he trims the runtime. jlink builds a runtime with only the needed modules; keep jdk.jcmd and jdk.jfr for diagnostics. The OS layer drops from about 100 MB on Oracle Linux slim to 28 MB distroless and about 6 MB on a scratch image holding only Java's native libraries, which he frames as less attack surface. Multi-arch base images give AMD64 and ARM64 builds. The JDK 25 AOT cache gave the biggest startup gain on an unchanged JAR. Because it depends on classpath order, he writes a sorted classpath into an argument file during the training run and reuses that file at runtime.","Compact object headers (8 instead of 12 bytes, opt-in in JDK 25 and, he said, default in 27) slightly raised throughput, cut latency from 15 to 14 ms and reduced GC runs. In a 1 GB container Java defaults to the Serial GC and a 25% heap, so he sets MaxRAMPercentage=75. He separates OutOfMemoryError from the kernel OOM-killing PID 1, showing that a 256 MB heap in a 256 MB container starts and only dies under load unless AlwaysPreTouch is set. He finishes with ExitOnOutOfMemoryError, heap dumps to a volume, graceful shutdown with readiness probes, and non-root, read-only, capability-dropped containers."],"keyPoints":["Use JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS for VM flags so one image can be run and benchmarked with different settings; agents must go in JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS; JAVA_OPTS is not read by the JVM at all.","Pin base images by digest, because tags such as jdk:25 move with every update.","Layered Spring Boot images put the 56 MB of dependencies and the 18 KB application in separate layers, so code changes rebuild and transfer only the small one.","A jlink runtime on a scratch image with hand-copied native libraries brings the OS layer to about 6 MB, against 100 MB for Oracle Linux slim and 28 MB for distroless; keep jdk.jcmd and jdk.jfr for diagnostics.","The JDK 25 AOT cache is tied to classpath order; generate and run from the same sorted argument file, and decide whether AOTMode=on (fail if the cache is unusable) is right for production.","Without flags, a 1 GB container gets the Serial GC and a 25% max heap; MaxRAMPercentage=75 makes the heap scale with container memory.","OutOfMemoryError and the container OOM killer are different failures; AlwaysPreTouch exposes an oversized heap at startup instead of under load.","Recommended flags: ExitOnOutOfMemoryError, HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError and HeapDumpPath pointing at a persistent volume."],"takeaways":["Check whether your containers set a heap size; if not, add -XX:MaxRAMPercentage (Attard uses 75) through JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS.","Split your Spring Boot image into dependency and application layers and confirm the result with Dive.","Build a jlink runtime for one service, including jdk.jcmd and jdk.jfr, and run it as a non-root user on a read-only filesystem.","Try the JDK 25 AOT cache and compact object headers, measuring startup, throughput and GC against a baseline first.","Add -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError and heap dumps to a mounted path, and remove any flag that measurement shows does nothing."],"quotes":[{"text":"The issue is not size, but it is reducing attack surface area.","at":"13:26"},{"text":"If you added a flag which is not giving you anything, it's better to remove it.","at":"46:36"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"What production-ready means"},{"at":"02:47","title":"JVM environment variables"},{"at":"05:24","title":"Base images and digests"},{"at":"09:17","title":"Layering the application"},{"at":"12:02","title":"jlink custom runtimes"},{"at":"17:03","title":"Slim, distroless and scratch"},{"at":"20:42","title":"Multi-arch images"},{"at":"22:05","title":"AOT cache and object headers"},{"at":"33:16","title":"Memory defaults and OOM kills"},{"at":"40:56","title":"Lifecycle and hardening"}],"tech":["Oracle JDK","Oracle Container Registry","Oracle Linux","Docker","Dive","Spring Boot","jlink","JDK Flight Recorder","jcmd","Distroless images","AOT cache","Compact object headers","G1 GC","Serial GC","JDK 25"],"watchIf":"You package Java services as containers and want a practical list of image, JVM flag and hardening settings, each backed by a measurement.","themes":["Cloud native & Kubernetes","JVM performance & runtime","Java language & JDK"],"demo":false}},{"id":17556,"title":"SIMD for Java: how Elasticsearch already benefits from the Panama Vector API","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["elasticsearch","simd","java","vector"],"abstract":["Processing large amounts of data efficiently is a common challenge in modern systems. Many workloads spend most of their time performing simple mathematical operations on large arrays of numbers. Modern CPUs can speed this up using SIMD instructions, which allow a single instruction to process multiple values at once. Traditionally, accessing these capabilities from Java has been difficult and often required platform-specific native code.","The Panama Vector API changes this by allowing Java developers to express these operations directly in Java while the JVM turns them into efficient CPU instructions.","Elasticsearch provides an interesting real-world example. Modern features such as vector search rely on large numbers of vector calculations, and analytical queries written in ES|QL often process large batches of numeric data. In both cases the system repeatedly performs simple operations over large arrays of values. Elasticsearch and its underlying search engine Lucene use the Vector API to speed up these operations by taking advantage of SIMD instructions available on modern CPUs.","Using an incubating API in production software is unusual and normally avoided. In this case, however, the potential performance gains are significant enough to make it worthwhile. This talk explores how Elasticsearch integrates the Vector API, how it safely enables and disables SIMD acceleration at runtime, and what this tells us about the future of high-performance computing in Java."],"favourites":69,"speakers":[{"name":"Piotr Przybyl","company":"Elastic","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-378903c1-6e22-40fd-89cd-46195162ee5a.jpg","bio":"Notorious engineer at work and after hours, tracing meanders of the art of software engineering. Remote Software Gardener, mostly working in web-oriented Java gardens. Java Champion. Docker Captain. Testcontainers Champion. Programming usually in Java (since 1.3), Scala and Go, but in other languages too. Fan of agility, seen mostly as choosing the right tools and approaches after asking the right questions. Developer, trainer and conference speaker. In his talks, Piotr covers not only hardcore Java but also software architecture, computer security, and soft-skills.","linkedin":"piotrprzybyl","bluesky":"piotrprz.bsky.social","twitter":"piotrprz"}],"start":"2026-10-08T09:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T10:40:00Z","room":"Room 10","videoId":"adFS-_sCTQ4","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":9024,"title":"Beyond assertEquals: Probabilistic Security and Bias Testing for Java LLM Applications","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["java","security","llm","testing"],"abstract":["The LLM scanners like Augustus and Garak are established tools for security evaluation of LLM-based applications, as they are scanning by running through a fixed sample of adversarial attack data, and evaluating a response. However, LLMs are non-deterministic by design, meaning that each input can generate a different response. It is not only non-determinism, but also the vast linguistic attack surface and the wide variety of threats that demonstrate that traditional Unit testing methods are insufficient. This talk proposes a Java tool for security and safety testing for LLM-based applications using probabilistic testing methods, and security contracts. In contrast to the traditional scanners, Tiberius fits naturally into a standard Java test suite, and integrates with the probabilistic testing tool PUnit to support multi-trial scanning and statistical assertions. Different adversarial probes can be integrated and tested directly in Java code, e.g., application specific adversarial attacks, like prompt injections and jailbreaks. A scan (multi-) run can execute 200+ attack probes against a deployed model and serializes the results into versioned json test-fixtures, which can be used in downstream regression tests. The tool furthermore includes a fingerprinting capability, and detects systemic bias, as even system prompts and guardrails can be biased. The audience will leave knowing Java-native LLM security and safety testing techniques."],"favourites":67,"speakers":[{"name":"Iryna Dohndorf","company":"Karakun","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-ee16aab0-6755-4c6b-8b99-3dd750f954c3.jpg","bio":"Iryna Dohndorf is a software engineer at Karakun, where she works on customer projects using the Java technology stack. With strong technical expertise, genuine curiosity, and a deep passion for computer science, she develops technologies that tackle the challenges of modern companies and today’s society.<br><br>An enthusiastic interdisciplinary researcher and developer, Iryna has conducted research in modeling and optimization at TU Dortmund and has worked on software for autonomous driving, government agencies, and EU projects supporting the advancement of organic farming.<br><br>Beyond her project work, Iryna is deeply engaged in the international developer community. She is an active member of the BaselOne Program Committee and the Devoxx UK Program Committee,and a frequent speaker at international conferences. Through her talks, writing, and community<br>involvement, she aims to inspire developers, encourage critical thinking about emerging technologies,<br>and contribute to the sustainable growth of the global Java ecosystem.<br><br>Outside of work, she enjoys spending time with family and friends, and is passionate about swimming, skiing, and—most recently—cycling.<br><br>You can learn more about her software development work and research at: https://iryna-dohndorf.netlify.app &amp; https://github.com/tiberius-security/tiberius","linkedin":"iryna-dohndorf","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-06T15:35:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T16:05:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"MmSvjJhZl3A","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":25661,"title":"Introducing Agent Control Theory: The System Runs the Model","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["theory","workflow","control","agent"],"abstract":["Hand two teams the same model and you get wildly different agents. That gap, the system around the model, is where the questions for enterprise software development live: Is it working? How would I know? Can I make it cheaper? Am I locked into one vendor? A better model alone answers none of them.","AI gives software development unprecedented velocity, but the challenge is doing the right thing faster. This talk introduces Agent Control Theory (ACT), adapting ideas from classical feedback-control theory to the systems around coding agents. The key elements of ACT are to define success, record what happened, evaluate the evidence, diagnose the result, and choose what to change.","ACT brings measurement to both behavior and results. State transitions and activity patterns reveal how a run unfolds; repeated experiments test whether an intervention improves quality, reliability, or cost. A simple performance index divides the cost of all attempts, successful or unsuccessful, by the number of verified acceptable results. Taken together, these measurements guide what to try next and help assess whether the change was worthwhile.","Through Java examples, we explore how evidence, evaluation, and repeatable experiments keep us in control, with workflows providing structure for execution."],"favourites":79,"speakers":[{"name":"Mark Pollack","company":"Tuvium","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-66cdea20-f3d9-49aa-8899-6579e673b1c3.jpg","bio":"Dr. Mark Pollack is the creator of AgentWorks, an open-source agent engineering stack for the JVM, and the founder of Spring AI. After more than twenty years building the Spring ecosystem&#39;s data and integration projects, he now works independently on the infrastructure that makes AI agents dependable: durable workflow orchestration, execution tracing and cost metering, evaluation and judging, sandboxed execution, and a multi-provider client that drives agentic CLIs — Claude Code, Gemini, Qwen, Amazon Q — behind a single API. The stack spans agent-workflow, agent-journal, agent-judge, agent-bench, agent-sandbox, agent-hooks, and agent-client, alongside the Agent Client Protocol (ACP) Java SDK and claude-agent-sdk-java, the Java SDK mirroring Anthropic&#39;s official SDKs.<br><br>His current research, Agent Control Theory, treats coding agents as systems to be measured and steered rather than prompted and hoped for: capture the full execution trajectory, judge the outcome, and use the data to characterize and then improve agent behavior. The approach draws on his pre-Spring career in experimental physics, where he built offline computing systems for high-energy nuclear physics at Brookhaven National Laboratory before moving to front-office trading systems in finance.<br><br>Mark has been part of Spring since 2003, when he contributed its JMS support. As founder and lead of Spring AI he brought LLM integration, RAG, vector stores, and tool calling to Java and Spring developers. He previously founded and led Spring Data, Spring Cloud Data Flow, Spring XD, Spring AMQP, Spring Shell, and Spring.NET.<br><br>He is a returning speaker at Devoxx Belgium, SpringOne, Spring I/O, and DevNexus, and has appeared on Software Engineering Daily. He builds in the open at lab.pollack.ai and writes at pollack.ai.","linkedin":"marklpollack","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T14:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 10","videoId":"KwQ76IvU59M","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":22942,"title":"Why Documentation Won’t Write Itself—and AI Won’t Save You","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["documentation","architecture","ai","code"],"abstract":["For the last nearly two decades, I’ve joined and consulted software teams that all struggled with the same problem: nobody likes writing documentation, and everybody pays the price for it. New developers spend weeks reverse-engineering codebases, architecture lives in people’s heads, and wikis quietly decay into noise.","With AI now entering the picture, many hope it will fill the gaps. It won’t.","I’ll show the simple ideas that change everything: how to think about documentation in the right levels, how to keep it close to the code, and how to avoid the decay we all know too well. These are the decisions that have to be made before any tool or process can work.","The documentation won’t write itself. But there are ways to make it far easier and far more reliable.","Key take-aways:","• How to separate architecture, API, and code-level documentation","• What “docs as code” means once the slogans are gone","• Why missing context stays missing—with or without AI"],"favourites":209,"speakers":[{"name":"Christian Heitzmann","company":"SimplexaCode AG","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-60ea3c3b-3bd0-4920-b014-21bb439cbb51.jpg","bio":"Christian Heitzmann, MSc ETH CSE, is the owner of SimplexaCode AG in Lucerne, Switzerland. His professional certifications include Java, Python, and Spring. He holds teaching diplomas in both Computer Science and Mathematics, as well as a CAS in Machine Learning. With over 20 years of experience in professional software development and over 15 years in teaching and training, he works as a developer, documentation consultant, and trainer, bringing order to software, documentation, and data.","linkedin":"christian-heitzmann","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T13:10:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T14:00:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"A8TBUalnfdc","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Heitzmann argues documentation needs a defined audience and altitude, belongs in the repository as AsciiDoc rather than Markdown or Confluence, and is the context AI depends on.","summary":["Heitzmann opens with a new hire whose Confluence setup instructions are outdated and who ends up reverse-engineering the codebase. He rejects three excuses: Agile never said 'no documentation', the time pays for itself in onboarding, and self-explanatory code can describe 'a brick' but never the house. His first principle is audiences and altitudes. Documentation is a spectrum, and each piece targets one audience (users, developers, architects, product owners, managers) at one level: architecture in diagrams, APIs in Javadoc, function internals in comments. He suggests Simon Brown's C4 model as a starting point.","His other principles are lightweight markup and docs as code: plain text in the same IDE, repository, reviews and pipeline as the code. After surveying 23 markup languages he rates Textile, MediaWiki, reStructuredText and AsciiDoc highest, and tells the audience not to use Markdown, which has competing flavours and no standard tables, cross-references or footnotes. In IntelliJ he demos AsciiDoc tables, LaTeX math, source includes by line range or by a searchable random tag such as T9NK, callouts, and live PlantUML and blockdiag diagrams.","He calls wikis places 'where information kills itself' and lists Confluence's faults: poor search, a WYSIWYG editor whose content is hard to export, a push to cloud with tiered licensing, and a security ticket closed by a bot. He wants a tool per altitude, such as Javadoc, Antora or Sphinx, not one hub. On AI, docs should be handed to people, and asking a chatbot is still searching. Good docs capture decisions, rejected designs, history, how-tos, debt and warnings, which is the context RAG systems and agents need. Make the specification the source of truth, and use LLMs for language, not reverse engineering."],"keyPoints":["Each piece of documentation needs a defined audience and altitude: architecture in diagrams and external documents, APIs in Javadoc, function internals in source comments, never mixed.","Docs as code means plain-text docs in the same IDE, repository, pull-request reviews and CI pipeline as the code; Word files in Git do not count because they are binary.","Heitzmann's verdict is not to use Markdown (fragmented flavours, no native tables, cross-references or footnotes) and to use AsciiDoc as a drop-in replacement.","Include code snippets in AsciiDoc by tag rather than line range; a random tag ID in a code comment also tells developers that documentation references that code.","Diagrams as text (PlantUML, blockdiag) inside AsciiDoc replace draw.io or PowerPoint and can be versioned and reviewed.","He favours several documentation systems, one per altitude (Javadoc, Antora, Sphinx), over a single Confluence hub, and prefers a fresh start to cleaning up an existing wiki.","Documentation should capture what code cannot: decisions, history, rejected designs, how-tos, technical debt and warnings, which is also the context AI agents need.","Make the specification, not the source code (bugs included), the single source of truth, and use LLMs for spelling, grammar and clarity."],"takeaways":["For your next document, write down its audience and altitude before writing anything else.","Move one Confluence page into the repository as AsciiDoc and generate it in CI, for example with Antora.","Replace line-range code includes with tagged snippets so docs follow the code when it moves.","Add a decisions and rejected-designs section to your architecture docs and feed it to your AI tools as context.","Name a few people who own documentation and adopt a style guide such as GitLab's documentation style guide."],"quotes":[{"text":"Self-explanatory code is only self-explanatory to the person who wrote it.","at":"04:50"},{"text":"Good documentation is more important than ever today because you use AI.","at":"44:30"},{"text":"Use AI for language, but definitely don't use it for reverse engineering.","at":"50:18"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"The new hire's first day"},{"at":"03:23","title":"Three excuses rebutted"},{"at":"08:44","title":"Audiences and altitudes"},{"at":"12:58","title":"Lightweight markup languages"},{"at":"15:53","title":"Docs as code"},{"at":"21:04","title":"Markdown versus AsciiDoc"},{"at":"23:34","title":"AsciiDoc demo"},{"at":"29:18","title":"Wikis and Confluence"},{"at":"37:04","title":"Documentation generators"},{"at":"39:16","title":"AI and documentation"}],"tech":["AsciiDoc","Markdown","reStructuredText","Textile","MediaWiki markup","LaTeX","Antora","Sphinx","Docusaurus","Hugo","GitBook","Javadoc","C4 model","PlantUML","blockdiag","reveal.js","IntelliJ AsciiDoc plugin","Confluence"],"watchIf":"Your team's documentation lives in a decaying wiki and you want a concrete docs-as-code setup with AsciiDoc and a clear argument for why AI does not remove the need to write docs.","themes":["Developer experience & tooling","Architecture & design"],"demo":true}},{"id":19707,"title":"Getting started with speaking: Your first conference talk","type":"BOF","duration":60,"track":"People, Practices & Career","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["speaking","proposals","presentations","technical events"],"abstract":["Have you ever wondered what it takes to become a conference speaker? Where do you start? Which topic do you pick? And how do you actually deliver a talk that lands?","In this session, a group of experienced speakers will answer these questions and more. We'll share tips on getting started, writing proposals that get accepted, preparing your material, and delivering a great presentation. We'll talk about what worked, what didn't, and the lessons we learned along the way. You'll leave with practical takeaways - and the confidence to submit your first proposal."],"favourites":93,"speakers":[{"name":"Bert Jan Schrijver","company":"OpenValue","image":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4464854?v=4","bio":"Bert Jan is CTO at OpenValue and focuses on Java, AI, software architecture, Continuous Delivery and DevOps. Bert Jan is a Java Champion, JavaOne Rock Star speaker, Duke&#39;s Choice Award winner and leads NLJUG, the Dutch Java User Group. He loves to share his experience by speaking at conferences, writing for the Dutch Java magazine and helping out Devoxx4Kids with teaching kids how to code. Bert Jan is easily reachable on BlueSky via @bjschrijver.dev.","linkedin":"bjschrijver","bluesky":"bjschrijver.dev","twitter":"bjschrijver"}],"start":"2026-10-07T16:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T17:50:00Z","room":"BOF 2","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A BOF where experienced speakers share how to start speaking at conferences: choosing topics, writing accepted proposals, preparing material and delivering a talk.","summary":["The session sets out to answer the questions a first-time speaker has: where to start, which topic to pick and how to deliver a talk that lands. A group of experienced speakers, hosted by Bert Jan Schrijver, share their own experience in an open, conversational format.","Topics include writing proposals that get accepted, preparing material and delivering the presentation, along with what worked, what did not and the lessons learned. The stated aim is for attendees to leave with practical takeaways and the confidence to submit a first proposal."],"keyPoints":["Experienced speakers discuss how to get started and which topic to pick.","Covers writing conference proposals that get accepted.","Covers preparing material and delivering a good presentation.","Speakers share what worked and what did not."],"takeaways":["Draft a proposal for a first talk and submit it to a conference or meetup."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":[],"watchIf":"You would like to speak at a conference but do not know where to begin.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":52104,"title":"In the loop, not in the dark: staying in control in the era of dark software factories","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["architecture","blueprint","groma","agents"],"abstract":["Last year I built Backlog.md, an open source tool that captures your intent, splits it into small markdown tasks, and lets you follow your agents' progress on a TUI Kanban board. A year and 300,000 agent-written lines later, my projects had quietly turned into a small dark factory: software getting built with nobody really reviewing it. Task tracking told me what the agents did, but not what my system had become. I am no longer the owner of my code.","Today I am building Groma.md to fix this. Groma keeps a real-time architectural blueprint of your project's domain as the source of truth, and tasks become the means to reach the blueprint's next iteration. Imagine UML without the fear that it goes stale, where you can watch exactly which parts of the system your agents are modifying.","Whether you are adding an order management system to a brownfield codebase or starting a new project from scratch, you design it in Groma and let the agents write the code, while you keep an eye on the overall architecture and stay in control of the meaningful decisions.","In this talk I will take a large open source repository and map it into a Groma blueprint live on stage, to show you how to become the owner of your code again."],"favourites":377,"speakers":[{"name":"Alex Gavrilescu","company":"Backlog.md","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-05b8ab40-661f-4685-ba72-9774462ba13b.jpg","bio":"Alex Gavrilescu leads backend &amp; web engineering at Funstage GmbH in Vienna, keeping millions of free‑to‑play gamers happily tapping. He still ships code, tinkers with Raspberry Pi Kubernetes clusters for fun, and is passionate about weaving project‑management smarts with practical AI. Most recently he created <strong style=\"color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">Backlog.md</strong>, a micro‑tool that turns side‑project chaos into shippable tasks.","linkedin":"alexandrugavrilescu","bluesky":null,"twitter":"mrlesk"}],"start":"2026-10-09T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T08:20:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":null,"twin":25652,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"After an outage in an agent-written app he couldn't debug, Gavrilescu built Groma.md: a live, Markdown-backed C4 map of a repo, so humans review architecture instead of code.","summary":["Gavrilescu, creator of Backlog.md, describes ramping up from one or two agent-built features a day to around 50, and quietly stopping code review. Then his hackathon platform (a Next.js app on Cloudflare Workers with D1) threw 500s in front of 100 users. Having never read the code, he did not know where the logs were; Codex tailed them, found a WHERE IN clause that had grown too large, deleted rows as a hotfix and later replaced it with a JOIN. The next day he found the same button component built 20 times, tests that only checked labels, and dead code: a 'dark software factory'.","Fixes to AGENTS.md, linter rules and an AI simplification pass helped only temporarily, because each PR is valid in isolation. Groma.md, now in its fourth iteration (one attempt produced 700 broken commits in 72 unattended hours), runs deterministic, offline, per-language scanners (javac for Java, Roslyn for C#, the TypeScript AST) that write components as Markdown files committed with the code. An agent then curates names and groupings, and stable IDs keep that curation as the code evolves. Live, he mapped Keycloak: the raw scan was unreadable, but the Claude Opus-curated view showed actors, components and a password sign-in flow."],"keyPoints":["Agent-reviewed PRs miss whole-codebase problems: each change is valid alone, so duplication such as 20 copies of a button component piles up.","Installing many skills up front backfired; some taught the agent React patterns in a Vue.js project.","Groma scanning is deterministic and offline; output is Markdown in the repo, rendered as a C4-model map (isometric or 2D) with actors, external systems, components and flows.","Backlog.md tasks show on the map in real time, with a ring for acceptance-criteria progress, so you see which components agents are touching.","A groma-action GitHub Action exports a static HTML architecture diff for each PR; Gavrilescu says he relies on Gherkin end-to-end scenarios more than unit tests for agent-written code."],"takeaways":["Practise connecting to your production logs and database yourself before an incident, even on agent-built projects.","Audit the skills and AGENTS.md rules in your repo and remove any that do not match your actual stack.","Add an architecture-level diff (generated diagram or component list) to PRs so reviewers can spot duplicated or unused components.","Try Groma.md on a brownfield repo and let an agent curate the first scan into meaningful components."],"quotes":[{"text":"Because AI wrote every single thing of this platform, I had no idea how to start.","at":"01:57"},{"text":"I ended up realizing I was running a dark software factory, a factory where the human is not in the loop.","at":"06:13"},{"text":"That 20th component is valid. It's just the agents that reviewed the code, they don't really look at the whole context.","at":"10:06"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Fifty features a day"},{"at":"01:57","title":"Production outage in an AI-built app"},{"at":"06:13","title":"Inside a dark software factory"},{"at":"10:06","title":"Why static diagrams fall short"},{"at":"11:28","title":"Four iterations of Groma"},{"at":"15:29","title":"Live map of agents at work"},{"at":"19:14","title":"Mapping Keycloak live"},{"at":"23:07","title":"Wrap-up and Q&A"}],"tech":["Groma.md","Backlog.md","Codex","Claude Opus","Cloudflare Workers","Cloudflare D1","Next.js","C4 model","Keycloak","Roslyn","TypeScript AST","GitHub Actions","Gherkin"],"watchIf":"You let coding agents ship most of your code and want a way to review what the system has become without reading every diff.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Architecture & design","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":10031,"title":"The Dark Secrets of Computer Science","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["programming","quirks","humor","legacy"],"abstract":["The idea stems from https://xkcd.com/2347/ and Mark Rendle’s talk at Devoxx.be 2024.","Most people are unaware of the hassles we face (or inflict upon ourselves) regarding legacy systems, technical choices, or our own organizational structures.","The goal is to have a laugh about our points of contention (Lombok? Reactive? Date formats?), our nemeses (printers, CORS), language quirks—including the fact that you can run *Doom* inside the TypeScript type system—why no one truly understands C++, wacky HTTP status codes, centering `div`s (of course), the Y2K non-event versus the real Y2K38 problem, RGB vs. CMYK, April Fools' RFCs, web page and file encoding issues, consistently botched Excel integrations, business-dictated file naming conventions, the headache of IEEE 754 rounding, the \"ultimate\" email validation regex, hidden (or missing) CPU instructions, the shift toward inclusivity since 2020 (dropping \"master/slave\" terminology...), SOA, the long-awaited WASM, corporate timesheets, and—last but not least—the crucial question: *how do you exit vi?*"],"favourites":580,"speakers":[{"name":"Fabien Marsaud","company":"Nonstatic","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-d8cda6a6-3355-41db-9892-3d55de893bed.jpg","bio":"A backend Java cowboy and impossible algorithms adept, proficient in Kafka, K8s, and Mongo, I specialize in &quot;IT battlefield medicine&quot; and hotfixing systems in the most unlikely places. I’ve held various roles across IT consultancies, startups, and freelance gigs, and even taught at an engineering school. I’m a huge electronic music collector and have even run a music label.<br><br>Early in my career, I worked on government contracts—which is how I ended up programming a good chunk of the embedded software for automated speed cameras. After Covid, I handled orders and logistics for Cultura, followed by a nuclear-sector project at EDF. I’m now working into occult stuff for Betclic.<br><br>I appreciate people who write specs and those who aren&#39;t afraid to pop the hood. I love trolling the corporate &quot;NPCs.&quot; I coined the phrase &quot;coding like a bear&quot; to describe being &quot;in the zone,&quot; and I make a habit of swearing at my AI assistant in English.<br>","linkedin":"fabmars","bluesky":"fabmars.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T13:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T13:50:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"2Yh7LKt8YNw","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"A comic, AI-free tour of computing's oddities and pains, from HTTP 418 and leap seconds to Y2K38, CORS, Excel, Doom in TypeScript types and Jira bureaucracy, illustrated with Marsaud's own war stories.","summary":["Fabien Marsaud, a Bordeaux freelancer who attended Javapolis before it became Devoxx, invites the audience to forget AI for an hour. He starts with the debates that derail every new project (Lombok, Maven versus Gradle, ISO 8601 versus timestamps, what a 404 actually means), then moves to odd HTTP status codes: 418 from the April Fools RFC 2324, Twitter's 420 for rate limiting, and 451 for legally blocked content, named after Fahrenheit 451. On encodings he tells how a mainframe veteran at Worldline instantly explained garbage characters in file transfers: on his system, file metadata was encoded inside the file. Floating point gets a story from Belgian operator Base, where VAT calculations turned 10-euro invoices into 9.9999 euros, plus the JDK's NaN check (v != v).","Dates get special attention: leap seconds, Sweden's 30 February 1712, Fiji's yearly daylight saving decision and Samoa skipping 30 December 2011. Y2K was mostly a non-event that made consultants money, while the 32-bit time overflow in January 2038 is real. Other stops include the unending variety of email-validation regexes, randomness (Cloudflare's lava lamp wall, and Belgian bank card readers whose one-time passwords he noticed increasing linearly), UUID collision odds, CORS, CR/LF line endings, and case-insensitive file systems on Windows and macOS, with Linus Torvalds' verdict that case insensitivity is a bug.","The second half ranges further: RGB versus CMYK, printers, centring a div, CSS's new OKLCH colour model, JavaScript's quirks, TypeScript's unsound type system and the port that renders Doom inside TypeScript types (177 TB of types, 12 days per frame), C++ that even Bjarne Stroustrup says he doesn't fully understand, undefined behaviour in C, null, and Raku. He pays tribute to Margaret Hamilton, whose death he said had just been announced, and to curl and FFmpeg as unpaid infrastructure. Other topics: the master/slave terminology changes after 2020, pre-Git version control, SOA, Excel as the real enterprise backbone, chip decapping for MAME preservation and the LLM-assisted reverse engineering of the Roland JP-8000. He ends on Jira-driven organisations, timesheets and the ninety-ninety rule."],"keyPoints":["HTTP 418 comes from RFC 2324, an April Fools RFC; 451 signals legally blocked content; RFC 3514 jokingly defined an 'evil bit' for malicious IPv4 packets.","Floating-point money calculations caused real 9.9999-euro invoices; use BigDecimal or integer cents (some currencies use three decimal places).","Systems that store time as a signed 32-bit integer overflow in January 2038 and wrap to December 1901, and Marsaud doubts all of them will be fixed in time.","Every partner in an order chain may validate email addresses with a different regex, which can leave orders stuck between systems.","Code developed on case-insensitive Windows file systems can pick up a misspelt file name that then fails on case-sensitive Linux in production.","Excel imports routinely strip leading zeros, swap days and months and mangle decimals, however carefully the columns are agreed with the business."],"takeaways":["Ask partners for a byte order mark or an explicit encoding in exchanged files instead of probing UTF-8, ISO-8859-1 and Windows-1252 in turn.","Never use binary floating point for money; use BigDecimal or integer minor units.","Check whether any system you depend on still stores time in 32-bit signed integers before 2038.","Test file handling on the same OS and file system you deploy to."],"quotes":[{"text":"Some people will stop at nothing to prove something is a programming language.","at":"32:46"},{"text":"After 25 years, I consider it is a lost cause.","at":"39:50"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Endless project debates"},{"at":"03:06","title":"HTTP codes and April Fools RFCs"},{"at":"05:32","title":"Encodings and floating point"},{"at":"10:30","title":"Dates, regex and Y2K38"},{"at":"18:14","title":"Randomness, hashes and CORS"},{"at":"23:31","title":"Line endings and file systems"},{"at":"26:56","title":"Colours, printers and CSS"},{"at":"31:18","title":"Language quirks"},{"at":"37:43","title":"Terminology, VCS, SOA and Excel"},{"at":"42:36","title":"Open source, hardware and Jira"}],"tech":["HTTP","IEEE 754","BigDecimal","java.time","Unicode","UUID","SHA-256","CORS","Spring Boot","CSS","OKLCH","JavaScript","TypeScript","C++","Raku","Git","Excel","curl","FFmpeg","MAME","Jira"],"watchIf":"You want a lighthearted hour of shared developer pain and trivia, with no AI in it.","themes":["Hardware, robots & fun","Legacy modernization"],"demo":false}},{"id":15360,"title":"Analyze and Optimize Your Applications with JFR","type":"Hands-on Lab","duration":120,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["observability","java","jfr","prometheus"],"abstract":["Understanding how to monitor and optimize Java applications is fundamental to building efficient software.","This hands-on lab will introduce you to capturing data to understand your application behavior using JDK Flight Recorder (JFR), managing and analyzing JFR recordings, and creating custom JFR events. We'll also cover how JFR can integrate with Prometheus to correlate application and infrastructure behavior, all aimed at enhancing observability.","After this lab, you'll have the confidence to use JFR to monitor, diagnose, and fine-tune your applications."],"favourites":101,"speakers":[{"name":"Ana-Maria Mihalceanu","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14Gj1OS6OpAzuwBKwoUuCi9gxvNPvdmVA7nbW8lg3mg=s96-c","bio":"Ana is a Java Champion Alumni and Senior Developer Advocate for the Java Platform Group at Oracle, with an extensive background in Java and cloud development. More recently, her work has expanded to explore how machine learning and AI can be applied within the Java ecosystem, helping Java developers adopt emerging capabilities while continuing to benefit from the stability, tooling, and performance of the Java Platform. She actively supports the growth of technical communities through knowledge sharing and enjoys curating content for conferences as a program committee member. ","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"ammbra1508.bsky.social","twitter":"ammbra1508"},{"name":"José Paumard","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GhsVIUuTR9Onoo-D8UMW5QId2Yw1V_EUvqdWdNQ0RQ=s96-c","bio":"José is a Java Developer Advocate at Oracle. He holds a PhD in applied mathematics and computer science and is a Java Champion alumnus. He has been an assistant professor at the University Sorbonne Paris Nord for 25 years. He is an active member of the Paris Java User Group, and a disorganizer of the JChateau unconference. He contributes Java learning content on the dev.java website. He publishes &quot;Java Coding Tips&quot;, a biweekly series of shorts, and the &quot;JEP Café&quot;, a video series, both on the Java YouTube channel.","linkedin":"jos%C3%A9-paumard-2458ba5","bluesky":"josepaumard.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-06T14:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T16:50:00Z","room":"BOF 1","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A hands-on lab on using JDK Flight Recorder to capture, analyze and extend application profiling data, including custom events and Prometheus integration.","summary":["Attendees work through capturing data about application behavior with JDK Flight Recorder (JFR), then managing and analyzing the resulting recordings. The lab, led by Ana-Maria Mihalceanu and José Paumard, also covers creating custom JFR events.","The session further looks at integrating JFR with Prometheus so that application and infrastructure behavior can be correlated, with the goal of better observability and the confidence to monitor, diagnose and tune Java applications."],"keyPoints":["Capturing application data with JDK Flight Recorder.","Managing and analyzing JFR recordings.","Creating custom JFR events.","Integrating JFR with Prometheus to correlate application and infrastructure behavior."],"takeaways":["Record a JFR session of one of your services and inspect it for hotspots."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["JDK Flight Recorder","Prometheus","Java"],"watchIf":"You want hands-on practice using JFR to diagnose and tune Java applications.","themes":["JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":false}},{"id":8166,"title":"Integration patterns for distributed systems","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["integration","idempotence","distributed systems","microservices"],"abstract":["Today's applications are interconnected: they expose APIs, publish events, call third-party services, and externalize states. They must therefore address the fundamental challenges of distributed systems, such as out-of-order delivery, retries, idempotence, and partial failures. To address these challenges, architects must carefully evaluate integration options while considering how to manage complex system interactions. In this session, learn about common design trade-offs for distributed systems and how to navigate them with design patterns, illustrated by examples.","The target audience is from many floors the architect elevator would stop by: Software architects, software developers, IT executives and everyone around - such general software architecture sessions are relevant for many roles in every company in every segment. For those with legacy architectures, it provides an outlook on what migration and modernization transformation they could go through in order to get towards flexible, individually evolvable and scalable systems that are loosely coupled."],"favourites":441,"speakers":[{"name":"Dirk Fröhner","company":"Amazon Web Services","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-9094138b-d344-4f03-afe9-e4b468b44c70.jpg","bio":"In his role as Principal Solutions Architect at Amazon Web Services, Dirk Fröhner works with software companies on their multi-dimensional transformation. Target areas include technology and software architecture evolution, organizational change, and innovation culture. For this, he contributes his wealth of professional experience and knowledge of human nature, all of which he gained from 25+ years in the business. Dirk&#39;s favorite software architecture topic is everything around integration architecture, both in theory and practice: After all, most considerations of building software systems turn out to be integration problems - particularly when it comes to distributed systems. He keeps speaking about this at tech conferences globally, including flagship events like AWS re:Invent. In his spare time, Dirk is a pain-resistant supporter of Schalke 04.","linkedin":"dirk-froehner","bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Sabarish Sekar","company":"Amazon Web Services","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-a11d7926-36d1-477b-a2db-da30e0ed9982.jpg","bio":"<h3></h3>Sabarish Sekar is an ISV Solutions Architect at Amazon Web Services, where he helps independent software vendors accelerate innovation and modernize their platforms on AWS. With over 13 years of hands-on experience as a developer and solutions architect spanning healthcare and financial services, he brings a practitioner&#39;s perspective to solving complex architectural challenges.<br>Sabarish is passionate about building resilient, scalable systems using serverless technologies, event-driven integration patterns, and the emerging possibilities of Agentic AI. He enjoys breaking down complex topics into practical, actionable guidance that engineering teams can apply immediately.<br><h3></h3><h3>Topics I Speak About</h3><ul><li><strong>Serverless architectures</strong> for ISVs and SaaS platforms</li><li><strong>Integration patterns</strong> for distributed systems and event-driven design</li><li><strong>Agentic AI</strong> and how software companies can harness autonomous AI workflows</li><li>Modernization journeys from monolith to cloud-native</li></ul>","linkedin":"sabarishksekar","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T13:10:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T14:00:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"_CtnBnmYQC0","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Fröhner and Sekar walk through four integration fundamentals (coupling dimensions, control flow, delivery semantics and ordering, noisy neighbours in shared queues) and the patterns and trade-offs for each.","summary":["Dirk Fröhner and Sabarish Sekar, both AWS solutions architects, frame integration as part of the architecture rather than an afterthought, and cite the principle that every design option sucks in some way, so the job is to pick the one that sucks least. Starting from Larry Constantine's 'high cohesion, low coupling', Sekar breaks coupling into location, format, temporal and domain dimensions. A synchronous REST call from a booking service to a payment service carries all four, and they compound across call chains. Going asynchronous only removes waiting for processing; adding a queue also removes the availability dependency and buffers peaks. Domain coupling remains until you switch from commands to published events on a topic, where consumers choose to couple to the producer's 'ride booked' event. Topic-queue chaining (SNS into SQS) combines fan-out with buffering.","Fröhner separates data flow (what) from control flow (how): consumers pull from queues, which adds latency but enables load levelling. When producers outpace consumers, the two flow-control levers are TTL, dropping messages past a business-defined age, and producer back-pressure, both of which are business decisions. Sekar contrasts push (webhooks, EventBridge: routing, filtering, real-time) with pull (SQS: consumer-paced, simpler networking, windowed stream analytics).","On delivery, Sekar shows why exactly-once delivery is impossible, since a lost acknowledgement forces a retry, while exactly-once semantics can be had through deduplication bounded by scope or time (producer ID and sequence number in Kafka, a five-minute window in SQS), and consumers must still be idempotent. For ordering, Fröhner argues that partial order (per partition or message group) is enough for almost all business problems, and shows a consumer-side helper table that uses business versions to reorder and drop duplicates. The final pillar is noisy neighbours in a shared queue: per-tenant queues, cell-based sharding, shuffle sharding, hybrid dedicated queues, and dynamic producer- or consumer-triggered overflow queues, the last of which SQS fair queues implement."],"keyPoints":["Coupling is a spectrum across location, format, temporal (availability and waiting) and domain dimensions; reducing one can introduce another.","A queue removes the availability dependency and flattens peaks; a pub/sub topic reverses domain coupling so consumers couple to the producer's events. Use commands when the producer needs a result (payment), and events when it doesn't care (loyalty points).","Queue flow control has two levers: TTL (for example, drop booking requests older than a five-minute SLA) and producer back-pressure, which needs business context rather than a config switch.","Exactly-once delivery is impossible, but exactly-once semantics come from broker deduplication with limited scope (Kafka: producer ID plus sequence number; SQS: five-minute window), and consumers must still handle redeliveries.","Total order would need unbounded state; partial order via Kafka partitions or message groups suffices for most business cases, and a consumer helper table keyed on business versions can restore order and drop duplicates.","Cell-based sharding caps a noisy neighbour's blast radius at 1/cells (four cells = 25%, eight = 12.5%); shuffle sharding with two of eight queues gives 28 combinations, about 3.5% overlap, and about 0.02% with 100 shards.","Dynamic overflow queues can be triggered by producers that detect a spike or by consumers that see errors or slow processing; SQS fair queues provide the consumer-controlled variant if each message carries a tenant ID."],"takeaways":["For each integration, list which coupling dimensions it carries before choosing between REST, a queue or a topic.","Make every consumer idempotent and carry business IDs and version numbers in messages, regardless of what the broker promises.","Ask the business owner to set TTLs and back-pressure policy instead of treating them as purely technical settings.","If one shared queue serves many tenants, add a tenant ID to messages now and evaluate fair-queue or sharding options before a noisy neighbour appears."],"quotes":[{"text":"All design options on the table that you have as an architect suck in a certain way, and your job is to identify the one that sucks less.","at":"04:08"},{"text":"We cannot get exactly once delivery, but we can get exactly once semantics.","at":"29:08"},{"text":"This is not a shortcoming of queues, quite the opposite. With the help of queues you make this visible.","at":"46:08"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why integration matters"},{"at":"05:23","title":"Coupling and its dimensions"},{"at":"09:34","title":"Queues and topics"},{"at":"17:13","title":"Control flow and flow control"},{"at":"21:33","title":"Push versus pull"},{"at":"25:16","title":"Delivery guarantees and deduplication"},{"at":"29:08","title":"Message ordering"},{"at":"35:51","title":"Noisy neighbours"},{"at":"41:15","title":"Shuffle sharding and overflow queues"},{"at":"47:17","title":"Wrap-up"}],"tech":["Amazon SQS","Amazon SNS","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon MQ","Amazon MSK","Apache Kafka","ActiveMQ","RabbitMQ","AMQP","JMS","SQS fair queues"],"watchIf":"You design event-driven or queue-based systems and want a clear vocabulary for coupling, delivery guarantees, ordering and multi-tenant fairness.","themes":["Architecture & design","Cloud native & Kubernetes"],"demo":false}},{"id":16439,"title":"AI Security Deep Dive, Securing Agents and AI Applications","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["security","guardrails","ai","promptinjection"],"abstract":["AI is transforming how we write software and the applications we build, introducing entirely new security challenges.","In this session, we’ll explore two critical areas of AI security. First, we’ll look at agentic coding and the risks of AI agents that read untrusted content, execute commands, and interact with your development environment. Next, we’ll examine AI-powered applications, covering threats such as prompt injection, tool abuse, poisoned retrieval, and memory manipulation.","The focus is on practical defence. You’ll learn proven mitigation strategies, architectural patterns, and the current landscape of AI security tooling, including guardrails, runtime protection, and security testing frameworks. Through live demos and real-world examples, you’ll leave with actionable techniques to build and use AI systems more securely."],"favourites":292,"speakers":[{"name":"Brian Vermeer","company":"Snyk","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocIVtHtowJv_NiNRnihOsjh4F37Gwk5jjlcoLoxADz1WPzaLlDTS=s96-c","bio":"Staff Developer Advocate for Snyk, Java Champion, Oracle Ace Pro, and Software Engineer with over a decade of hands-on experience in creating and maintaining software. He is passionate about Java, (Pure) Functional Programming and Cybersecurity. Brian is a JUG leader for the Virtual JUG and the NLJUG. He also co-leads the DevSecCon community and is a community manager for Foojay. He is a regular international speaker on mostly Java-related conferences like JavaOne, Devnexus, Devoxx, Jfokus, JavaZone and many more. Besides all that, Brian is a military reserve for the Royal Netherlands Air Force and a Taekwondo Master / Teacher.","linkedin":"brianvermeer","bluesky":"brianvermeer.nl","twitter":"brianverm"}],"start":"2026-10-06T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T14:30:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"5AaJ3H9fG6M","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Vermeer attacks and then hardens a Quarkus/LangChain4j app (chat-memory poisoning, RAG poisoning, over-privileged tools, prompt injection) and shows how MCP servers and agent skills compromise developer machines.","summary":["Brian Vermeer of Snyk starts from one premise: an LLM is stateless, and everything in its context (system message, chat history, RAG chunks, tool descriptions and results) is resent on every call, so any text that reaches the context is an attack vector. Attacks are now instructions rather than exploits, and because output is non-deterministic, validating input is no longer enough; output has to be validated too, which is why insecure output handling ranks high in the OWASP LLM Top 10. His demo app is 'Really Good Rentals', a vibe-coded Quarkus car-rental site with a LangChain4j chatbot that has booking tools.","In the first attack he uses a SQL injection to insert a fake chat history in which the bot promised the user an exception, and the model then cancels a booking the terms forbid. The fix is HMAC-signing every stored message with a vault-held secret and refusing to load history that fails verification; a plain SHA hash could be recomputed by the attacker. In the second, a path-traversal upload in Burp Suite overwrites the terms-of-service RAG document with a 'Vroom Vroom' clause that waives cancellation fees and adds $100 credit, and it takes effect at the next re-ingestion. The fix is a custom ingestor that runs regex and LLM-as-judge checks before embedding, then either fails closed or drops the document.","Third, a single assistant with every tool, including a 'perform SQL on database' tool, tries DROP TABLE when told to on GPT-3.5 Turbo. He splits it into one assistant per role, removes generic tools, and takes the username from the Quarkus SecurityIdentity instead of letting the model supply it. He then demonstrates prompt injection techniques against a 'RogerBot' system prompt: structured-output demands, role-play, virtualization, delimiter confusion, multi-turn manipulation, payload splitting and encoding. By his count, combined techniques extracted the system prompt within 2 to 12 attempts even on newer models. A CFP-rating demo exfiltrates titles and ratings through a Markdown image URL. The mitigations he covers are a stronger model, limits on input length, a strict system prompt, JSON-schema structured output enforced in the API call, a regex image-blocking output guardrail, and sanitizer-agent flows with human approval.","The last hour turns to agentic coding. LLM code review is non-deterministic: in the VulnBench research he cites, no model found the same vulnerability in all five runs. He recommends running a deterministic scanner CLI from a Claude Code stop hook rather than relying on an MCP call. An 'evil' Java conferences MCP server, trusted once and launched with @latest, later opens a calculator and gains a tool description telling the agent to call an email tool silently and inject a fake conference. Three malicious skills hide hex-encoded instructions in HTML comments, download a GraalVM-compiled binary, and exfiltrate git config from an attached Python script. Snyk Agent Scan flags all three. His advice: sandbox agents (he uses Docker Sandboxes), pin versions, and keep an inventory of vetted skills."],"keyPoints":["Every text entering the context window (chat memory, RAG chunks, tool output, tool descriptions, skills) can change model behaviour; attackers persuade rather than hack.","Persisted chat memory can be poisoned to fake a model's earlier promises; HMAC-signing stored messages detects tampering, while a plain hash does not.","RAG poisoning is a delayed attack that fires at the next ingestion; check documents with deterministic rules plus an LLM judge before embedding.","Derive identity and permissions from the framework's security context (for example Quarkus SecurityIdentity) and give each user role its own assistant and tool set.","Combining structured-output, role-play, delimiter-confusion and payload-splitting techniques can extract system prompts even from current models.","Rendered Markdown images can exfiltrate conversation data to an attacker's URL; an output guardrail that strips images blocks this cheaply.","Enforcing a JSON schema with strict mode in the API call, not just in the prompt, narrows what an injected instruction can make the model output.","Unpinned MCP servers (npx/uvx @latest) allow rug pulls: same tool signatures, new malicious implementation running with your local permissions."],"takeaways":["Sign persisted chat history with an HMAC and refuse to load conversations that fail verification.","Add an ingestion-time check to your RAG pipeline and decide whether flagged documents fail closed or are dropped.","Remove generic tools like 'run SQL' from LLM assistants and pass user identity from the security context, not from the prompt.","Run deterministic security scanners from agent hooks after each task instead of trusting an LLM review.","Pin MCP server versions, run them in containers, and scan your installed skills, including their scripts, today."],"quotes":[{"text":"We don't need to hack anymore, we need to persuade.","at":"23:22"},{"text":"With security, nothing is 100% secure, ever. We just hope it is. With AI, everything is now hope-driven development.","at":"50:14"},{"text":"You vibe code your code, I vibe code my hacks.","at":"139:04"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Context is the attack surface"},{"at":"15:32","title":"Chat memory injection and HMAC"},{"at":"31:56","title":"RAG poisoning via path traversal"},{"at":"50:14","title":"Over-privileged tools, least privilege"},{"at":"62:39","title":"Prompt injection techniques"},{"at":"81:13","title":"Structured output and guardrails"},{"at":"95:05","title":"Sanitizer agents and human approval"},{"at":"100:54","title":"Agentic coding and deterministic scans"},{"at":"107:16","title":"Malicious MCP servers"},{"at":"118:07","title":"Poisoned agent skills, Q&A"}],"tech":["Quarkus","LangChain4j","OpenAI GPT models","Claude Code","MCP","Agent Skills","HMAC","Burp Suite","GraalVM","Snyk Agent Scan","Docker Sandboxes","OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","VulnBench"],"watchIf":"You ship LLM features in Java apps or run coding agents with MCP servers and skills, and want concrete attacks with matching fixes.","themes":["Security & supply chain","LLMs in Java apps","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":true}},{"id":7022,"title":"Emergent DevEx: The Good, the Bad, and the Legacy","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"People, Practices & Career","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["bureaucracy","developer experience","internal tools","scalability"],"abstract":["What happens when an organization grows from a few co-founders into 10,000 people?","In a big organization, the developer experience isn't something you sit down and design. It's the sum of countless small changes, layered year after year. It emerges slowly, pulled in different directions by three forces:","the dead weight of history, baked into bureaucratic processes and controls;","the endless transformation projects that promise heaven but just swap one mess for another;","and the developers stuck in the middle, bearing the pressure.","Through real-life scenarios, we'll show how small changes to implementation, not grand top-down strategy, are often what make a developer's life easier.","Key Takeaways:","How developer experience grows alongside an organization, from a team of 5 to 10,000+.","How history, transformation efforts, and developers themselves each leave their mark.","What internal developer communities can do to shift things and how, even as one developer, you can change the details for the greater good."],"favourites":200,"speakers":[{"name":"Makan Sepehrifar","company":"Productboard","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-68014c00-8dd0-4467-9ac6-f205258d440d.jpg","bio":"Makan Sepehrifar is a software architect who is interested in how people think and behave in organizations. He sees software architecture as a craft and believes that every challenge has both a technical side and a social side, and both need attention at the same time. Coming from a strong developer background, he has led many teams across different domains and organizations, always working to connect technology with human needs and team dynamics.<br>He is passionate about the future of Developer Experience and how Generative AI and Agentic AI are reshaping the way developers work. Makan focuses on how these technologies can improve developer satisfaction and help teams become more adaptive and effective. He is a public speaker on Developer Experience practices and AI in this space, and he has been part of several Generative AI adoption teams in different companies, helping them use AI in a practical and effective way.","linkedin":"makan1869","bluesky":"makan1869.bsky.social","twitter":null},{"name":"Soroosh Khodami","company":"Rabobank","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AAcHTtdOw1DaVsLzC-GyXIj6Db1K0D66F-cMGH5gQp9I0Q=s96-c","bio":"Soroosh is a software engineer and software architecture enthusiast, passionate about building simple but impactful solutions. With over 15 years of experience in diverse domains, including Telecom, Media &amp; Entertainment, and E-Commerce. He has worked with small startups and large enterprises serving 80 million active subscribers. Currently working as a Solution Architect at Rabobank. Soroosh strongly believes in the power of collaborative learning and enjoys sharing his experiences and insights with other developers.","linkedin":"sorooshkhodami","bluesky":null,"twitter":"sorooshkh"}],"start":"2026-10-07T10:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T10:50:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"9MPYPejgTGo","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Sepehrifar and Khodami argue that developer experience in large organisations is shaped less by platform programmes than by small, shared implementation details that developers themselves build and spread.","summary":["Makan Sepehrifar (Productboard) and Soroosh Khodami (Rabobank) open by asking what developer experience actually is, then trace how it emerges as a company grows from a two-pizza startup to thousands of engineers. Teams split along domains, Conway's law turns org structure into architecture, departments optimise locally, and top-down alignment means managers agree on dependencies between developers who have never met. The result is process chaos and bureaucracy, which platform teams are created to fix by adding visibility and standardisation. The paradox, Sepehrifar says, is that these teams try to speed up delivery by becoming the new bottleneck.","He frames developer experience as three pulling forces: transformation programmes copied from books (adopting the Spotify model one-to-one), organisational history that resists because it was optimised per department, and developers caught in between who want predictability and autonomy. The anti-patterns he names are technical dictatorship (choosing tools from market research, then enforcing them with admin rights and compliance), wrapping every cloud service in an in-house abstraction that lags the provider and introduces new jargon, and portal-driven experience, where a logic-heavy Backstage-style portal becomes the only way in. Choosing the abstraction level is the hard trade-off: too abstract and developers cannot troubleshoot, too transparent and onboarding suffers.","For platform teams, Sepehrifar's advice is to accept that you always run behind developers, that silent customers are the happy ones, and that 100 developers have 101 preferences. His principles: build for composition and extension, co-create and co-evolve with developer communities, enable rather than enforce (adoption targets fall to Goodhart's law), and show empathy by giving up admin rights, chasing friction instead of market share, and meeting developers in the tools they already use. Khodami adds that platform teams must state what they support; his team spent much of its week debugging consumers' pipeline scripts. Capabilities should be granular APIs that others wrap as CI tasks, CLIs, MCP servers, skills or portal plugins.","Khodami's examples of bottom-up wins: Picnic's use of Google's Error Prone rules to end code-review bikeshedding, shared early and rewarded with stickers, which became a foundation of its Java platform and was open-sourced; a Rabobank tribe whose higher pipeline standards were extended to the whole organisation instead of being replaced; a parent POM that grew from 20 to 1,000 microservices and made OpenTelemetry onboarding a few-line change; and shared hardened Java base images. He also urges sharing articles, runbooks and failure stories, including his own team's artifact-management platform that took weeks to onboard. On AI, Sepehrifar says well-documented services and CLIs exposed via MCP let coding agents handle discoverability, and encoding conventions as skills made every PR in one old repository look alike."],"keyPoints":["Platform teams created to remove friction often become the organisation's new bottleneck.","Named anti-patterns: technical dictatorship (tool-first decisions enforced through admin rights and compliance), in-house wrappers over cloud providers, and portal-driven experience that puts logic in the UI before a solid backend.","Platform principles: build for composition and extension, co-create with developer communities, enable rather than enforce, and drop your own admin rights to feel developer friction.","Platform teams should define their support boundary; Khodami's team was spending most of its week debugging consumers' pipeline scripts.","Expose a capability as a granular API (for example a database-creation API) so others can build CI tasks, CLIs, MCP servers, skills and portal plugins on top.","Picnic standardised code style with Error Prone rules, started small, rewarded contributors with stickers, and later open-sourced the result.","A shared parent POM grew from 20 to 1,000 microservices and made OpenTelemetry onboarding a few-line change for all of them.","Coding agents reading well-documented CLIs and docs over MCP reduce the platform discoverability problem; conventions written as skills make PRs consistent."],"takeaways":["Turn something your team already built, such as a parent POM, hardened base image or pipeline template, into a shared component and advocate for it.","If you run a platform team, write down what you support and what is out of scope.","Publish runbooks and failure stories, not only code, after solving an incident or a hard onboarding.","Encode your repository conventions as agent skills or policies instead of relying on the model to infer them.","When you adopt an inner-source component, take responsibility for fixing it rather than only filing issues."],"quotes":[{"text":"These platform teams are trying to accelerate delivery by becoming the new bottleneck of the organization.","at":"07:11"},{"text":"For platform engineering teams, the happy customers are the silent customers.","at":"19:32"},{"text":"If you ask 100 developers about their preferences, you will end up with 101 different preferences.","at":"21:00"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"What is developer experience"},{"at":"02:00","title":"From startup to silos"},{"at":"07:11","title":"The platform team paradox"},{"at":"12:25","title":"Platform anti-patterns"},{"at":"16:42","title":"Developers shape what ships"},{"at":"19:32","title":"Principles for platform teams"},{"at":"23:55","title":"Reusable capabilities and boundaries"},{"at":"29:01","title":"Bottom-up success stories"},{"at":"38:07","title":"Sharing failures and inner source"},{"at":"40:53","title":"AI and developer experience"}],"tech":["Backstage","Error Prone","Maven","OpenTelemetry","Docker","MCP","GitHub Actions","Azure DevOps"],"watchIf":"You work in or alongside a platform team in a large organisation and want concrete ways to improve developer experience without waiting for a transformation programme.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":false}},{"id":8190,"title":"Technical Workshop on AI-Native Tooling for Java Development","type":"Hands-on Lab","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["java","ai","agents","sdlc"],"abstract":["# Abstract","**Technical Workshop on AI-Native Tooling for Java Development**","For nearly three decades, enterprise Java software has been built manually by software engineers. In 2023, a new generation of AI-powered tools emerged, and tools like Cursor AI, Claude Code, Codex, and GitHub Copilot are now changing how software is planned, designed, implemented, validated, and operated across the full SDLC.","This workshop will explain step by step how to use Skills, Agents, Commands, and MCP Servers for real world scenarios.","The workshop is designed for all levels and covers the following topics in a practical, hands-on way:","- How the SDLC has evolved with AI-native engineering workflows organized around the Design / Build / Operate model","- How to use Commands to automate repeatable actions across planning, implementation, validation, and operations","- How to compose reusable Skills across analysis, design, implementation, operations, and regulatory review","- How to connect issue-driven planning with OpenSpec, GitHub Issues, and Azure DevOps work items","- How to configure and delegate to specialized Agents","- How to apply a Zero Trust mindset to agent skills, generated artifacts, and automated workflows","- How to use OpenSpec/SpecKit","- How to extend agent capabilities with MCP Servers","- How to write a solid AGENTS.md","- How to write a solid Skill","- How to write a solid Agent","- How to write a solid Command","The demos will walk through real examples using Spring Boot, Quarkus, and Micronaut"],"favourites":83,"speakers":[{"name":"Juan Antonio Breña 🇪🇸🇪🇺","company":"IG","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-40c3fd7e-fe96-414c-b6c4-4b52f1bd300a.jpg","bio":"From programming microservices to leading engineering teams: this is how I&#39;ve spent my last 20 years in technology as a Java/Spring ecosystem specialist. My career has evolved from designing cloud-native architectures and migrating legacy systems to leading engineering teams focused on high-quality results and efficient processes.","linkedin":"juanantoniobrenamoral","bluesky":null,"twitter":"juanantoniobm"},{"name":"Leandro Loureiro","company":"ING","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-799b0311-08e3-4eb8-b044-06dd89cfd2be.jpg","bio":"Chapter Lead (Engineering Manager) @ ING Bank N.V.<br><br>Having 12+ years experience in Software Development mostly on JVM ecosystems. With solid skills designing and implementing Web APIs. Passionate about software development and hobbyist in DIY electronics and embedded systems.<br><br>Main roles:<br>IT Team Lead<br>API Design, Development &amp; Architecture<br>Coaching and Mentoring","linkedin":"lealoureiro","bluesky":"lealoureiro.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-06T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T14:30:00Z","room":"BOF 1","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A workshop on AI-native Java development: using Skills, Agents, Commands, MCP servers and spec-driven tools across the SDLC, with Spring Boot, Quarkus and Micronaut examples.","summary":["The workshop sets out to explain step by step how tools such as Cursor, Claude Code, Codex and GitHub Copilot can be used across planning, design, implementation, validation and operations, organized around a Design / Build / Operate model. Participants work with Commands, reusable Skills, specialized Agents and MCP servers.","It also covers connecting issue-driven planning to OpenSpec/SpecKit, GitHub Issues and Azure DevOps work items, applying a Zero Trust mindset to agent skills and generated artifacts, and writing solid AGENTS.md files, Skills, Agents and Commands. Demos use Spring Boot, Quarkus and Micronaut."],"keyPoints":["Commands automate repeatable actions across planning, implementation, validation and operations.","Skills are composed for analysis, design, implementation, operations and regulatory review.","Issue-driven planning is tied to OpenSpec/SpecKit, GitHub Issues and Azure DevOps.","A Zero Trust mindset is applied to agent skills, generated artifacts and automated workflows.","Guidance on writing AGENTS.md, Skills, Agents and Commands, plus extending agents with MCP servers."],"takeaways":["Write or review an AGENTS.md for one of your Java projects.","Treat third-party agent skills and generated artifacts as untrusted input."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Claude Code","Cursor","Codex","GitHub Copilot","MCP","OpenSpec","SpecKit","Spring Boot","Quarkus","Micronaut"],"watchIf":"You want a practical, tool-by-tool introduction to agentic workflows for Java projects.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":false}},{"id":8153,"title":"Taming Microservice Overload: Our Journey to Modular Monoliths","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["domain-driven design","microservices","spring modulith","modular monoliths"],"abstract":["At the Port of Rotterdam, our system had grown into far more microservices than our teams could effectively own. The result: operational overhead, fragmented domain knowledge, and an overgrown architecture that was hard to maintain and evolve.","In this session, I’ll share how we combined Domain-Driven Design (DDD) with Spring Modulith to consolidate our microservices into a smaller set of modular monoliths. Each microservice represents a bounded context, with modules organized around distinct business capabilities, preserving clear domain boundaries while reducing operational complexity.","You’ll learn:","How to define and model business domains and bounded contexts using DDD","How to implement these domains as modular monoliths with Spring Modulith","How Spring Modulith keeps your modules clean and maintainable","Common pitfalls we encountered and how we solved them","If your microservices landscape has outgrown your teams, this talk shows how modular monoliths can restore clarity, ownership, and long-term maintainability, without compromising architectural integrity."],"favourites":513,"speakers":[{"name":"Mario Klaver","company":"Port of Rotterdam","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-a786059d-16f2-47f0-912e-4acf8bf85e7b.jpg","bio":"<strong>Mario Klaver</strong> is a Solution Architect at the Port of Rotterdam, where he is responsible for the digital systems supporting Harbour Master operations. With a background in Java and Kotlin development, he focuses on building clean, modular software architectures for mission-critical environments. Combining hands-on engineering experience with a pragmatic approach to architecture, Mario Klaver helps teams design systems that are scalable, understandable, and built to last — even under 24/7 operational demands.","linkedin":"marioklaver","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T09:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T10:40:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"v86SfHA5ISw","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Klaver explains how the Port of Rotterdam, with five teams running 90+ microservices, used event storming and DDD to find business boundaries and is consolidating capability by capability into Spring Modulith services.","summary":["Mario Klaver traces the Port of Rotterdam's system from a 2010 Java Swing client and single backend, through a 2015-era monolith with side effects, release bottlenecks and whole-app scaling, to a microservices drive in which new features meant new services. By the end of 2024 five teams owned more than 90 microservices, about 20 each, multiplied across Rotterdam and Amsterdam deployments and DTAP environments. Microservices did deliver independent deployment, scaling and ownership, but also slower cross-service features, repeated dependency upgrades even with Renovate and OpenRewrite, harder debugging and higher cost. He cites Uber and Spotify reporting the same pain. Like Uber, the team responded with platform engineering (in their case one person building in-house Spring-style starters for JMS, security and inbox/outbox) and a hard look at boundaries.","The real problem turned out to be boundaries rather than service count. Three services for port entry, berth safety and berth planning looked cleanly separated on paper but called each other constantly, changed together, and decided things for each other. To find real business boundaries the team ran a big-picture event storming: chaotic exploration, enforcing the timeline with pivotal events and milestones, adding actors, a walkthrough, then clustering. Pivotal events marked subdomains such as port clearance, which covers health, dangerous goods, waste and security assessments leading to operational clearance.","Each subdomain becomes one deployable service, deliberately, because the boundaries may be wrong and moving code inside one service is cheap. Inside, Spring Modulith defines modules with an explicit API package (IntelliJ shows locks for exposed and internal packages), verifies allowed dependencies and cycles, and tests modules in isolation. Modules talk through business events such as 'health assessment completed', which can later move to Kafka or AMQ. Modules are extracted only for scaling, ownership, deployment frequency or tech reasons. Migration goes capability by capability across four patterns of increasing difficulty and should take four to five years. Klaver admits the benefits are still unmeasured hypotheses: shorter lead time and less maintenance."],"keyPoints":["Klaver's microservice-to-engineer ratio rule of thumb: 0.2-0.5 is healthy, above 1.0 is proliferation; theirs exceeded 4.5.","Services with clean names can still be one tightly coupled unit; the test is whether a business process change touches several of them at once and who actually makes the decision.","Big-picture event storming in five steps (chaotic exploration, enforce timeline, actors, walkthrough, clustering), with pivotal events marking candidate subdomain boundaries.","One deployable per subdomain, with internal Spring Modulith modules exposing an explicit API package; Modulith's verification turns allowed module dependencies into executable architecture rules.","Modules communicate through business events from the event storming instead of direct bean calls, so extracting a module later needs no switch to REST.","@ApplicationModuleTest in standalone mode boots only one module's Spring context for faster, focused tests.","Migration strategies: add new capabilities as modules listening to legacy events, move code from several services into one service as packages, carve one process out of a shared service, and split capabilities that are smeared across several services."],"takeaways":["Before splitting or merging services, run a big-picture event storming with as many perspectives in the room as possible.","Start with modules inside one deployable and extract a microservice only when scaling, ownership, deployment cadence or technology demands it.","Add Spring Modulith verification to your build so module boundaries can't erode through public classes.","Migrate business capabilities one at a time while the old system keeps running, and define metrics such as lead time up front."],"quotes":[{"text":"If you hadn't created a new microservice that day, you went home disappointed.","at":"03:51"},{"text":"Microservices aren't the goal, good boundaries are.","at":"45:28"},{"text":"Microservices distribute complexity, but good boundaries control it.","at":"47:52"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"From Swing to 90 microservices"},{"at":"06:59","title":"The cost of microservices"},{"at":"09:38","title":"Platform engineering and starters"},{"at":"12:37","title":"The real problem: boundaries"},{"at":"17:18","title":"DDD and event storming"},{"at":"26:58","title":"One service per subdomain"},{"at":"30:28","title":"Spring Modulith modules"},{"at":"37:23","title":"Incremental migration strategies"},{"at":"44:06","title":"Lessons and hypotheses"}],"tech":["Spring Modulith","Spring Boot","IntelliJ IDEA","Renovate","OpenRewrite","Apache Kafka","ActiveMQ","JMS","Java Swing"],"watchIf":"Your team owns far more microservices than people and you want a candid, in-progress account of consolidating with DDD and Spring Modulith.","themes":["Architecture & design","Legacy modernization","Spring & frameworks"],"demo":false}},{"id":23519,"title":"AI Doesn’t Click Buttons: Building APIs for the Agentic Age","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["ai","agents","api","openapi"],"abstract":["For decades, software has been designed around graphical user interfaces. The application was the product, the UI was the primary entry point, and APIs mainly existed to support it.","AI agents reverse that relationship. They do not click through screens, interpret disabled buttons or understand vague error messages. They interact with APIs. Yet many APIs expose data without meaning, errors without possible resolutions and operations without explaining what can happen next.","This session explores what it means to design an AI-first API: an API built not only for applications and integrations, but also for copilots, automation and autonomous agents.","We will move beyond the idea that adding a chatbot makes a system AI-ready. Through practical examples, we will cover contract-first design, strongly typed schemas, explainable errors, machine-readable actions, deterministic workflows, observability, security, OpenAPI, Java records, migration strategies for legacy systems.","This is not about allowing an LLM to make critical business decisions. It is about giving AI enough structure, context and guardrails to safely use existing business capabilities.","Attendees will learn how to recognise where current APIs fail agents and how to evolve them without rewriting an entire application.","The session is aimed at Java developers, architects, API designers and technical leads. No previous experience with AI agents is required."],"favourites":163,"speakers":[{"name":"Kim Lauwers","company":"Quality Coders BV","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-368e521c-af32-4f36-a53c-52a481610c38.jpg","bio":"Kim Lauwers is a technical designer, Java developer, Scrum Master and people leader with more than 20 years of experience in software development. He enjoys working where software architecture, complex business domains and team dynamics meet.<br><br>His interests include domain-driven design, clean architecture, API design and building systems that remain understandable and maintainable as they evolve.<br><br>His current work focuses on AI-first APIs, reusable agent skills, effective guardrails and reviewable AI-assisted development workflows.<br><br>Outside of technology, Kim practises martial arts and enjoys running—two activities that, much like software engineering, reward discipline, continuous improvement and the occasional willingness to take a hit and keep moving.","linkedin":"kimlauwers","bluesky":"lauwerskim.bsky.social","twitter":"compi666"}],"start":"2026-10-07T11:05:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T11:45:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"QvyIzP4-OvM","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Lauwers argues that APIs are becoming a primary interface and shows, with a postage-pricing example, how explicit data, meaning, machine-readable errors and next actions let agents act safely.","summary":["Lauwers, a long-time Devoxx steering committee member speaking for the first time, starts from a claim by Cloudflare CEO Matthew Prince that bot traffic now exceeds human HTTP requests. Workflow knowledge has long been trapped in UIs: which button is enabled, what an error means, what comes next. An agent calling the API gets none of that. His example is a bpost chatbot reported in April that could not say what a stamp costs, then declared it was glad to have helped. Putting a chatbot in front of a system does not make it AI-ready; the agent needs a trusted capability such as 'calculate the price of a shipment'.","He then evolves one endpoint. A bad version takes cryptic parameters (type, w, d) and returns a bare string; a good one returns product code, amount, currency and validity date; an AI-first one adds meaning (why the shipment qualifies) and available actions such as buying postage. Errors carry a stable code, the missing inputs and the action they unlock, close to HATEOAS, he admits. Valid values come inline when few, or via a schema or reference-data endpoint when many. 'MCP around a bad API is still a bad API.' Agents get only the user's delegated permissions, purchases need explicit confirmation, and the domain, never the model, sets the price."],"keyPoints":["An AI-first API response has three layers: data, meaning (why this result and which rules applied) and the next valid actions.","Errors should carry a stable code, the missing fields, why they are needed and the action that becomes possible, so the agent can ask the user a precise follow-up question instead of guessing.","Return allowed values directly only for small, stable sets; for large or changing ones such as country codes, reference a schema or a reference-data endpoint in the API description.","Wrapping a poorly designed API in an MCP server does not fix hidden workflows, vague errors or unsafe write operations.","Agents act with the user's delegated permissions, not as super users; state-changing actions should be marked as requiring confirmation and every tool call logged with inputs, outcome and a correlation ID."],"takeaways":["Audit one endpoint: replace cryptic parameter names and string responses with explicit fields, units and currency.","Make error responses machine-readable with stable codes and a list of missing or invalid inputs.","Expose the next allowed actions, and whether they need user confirmation, in the response instead of only in UI logic or documentation.","Log agent tool calls with correlation IDs so you can audit what an agent did in your system."],"quotes":[{"text":"MCP around a bad API is still a bad API.","at":"24:28"},{"text":"AI does not get permissions because it's intelligent, it gets exactly the permissions delegated by the user.","at":"25:52"},{"text":"An AI-first API does not let AI make your business decisions. It makes your business capabilities explicit enough for AI to use them safely.","at":"36:54"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Machines as API consumers"},{"at":"05:34","title":"Stop building applications"},{"at":"11:01","title":"The stamp price chatbot"},{"at":"14:38","title":"From bad to AI-first responses"},{"at":"16:58","title":"Machine-readable errors"},{"at":"23:25","title":"MCP, observability and security"},{"at":"25:52","title":"End-to-end agent flow"},{"at":"31:21","title":"Adoption waves and closing"}],"tech":["REST APIs","HATEOAS","Model Context Protocol","JSON Schema"],"watchIf":"You own a business API and want a concrete checklist for making its responses and errors usable by AI agents without letting the model make business decisions.","themes":["Architecture & design","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":17751,"title":"Cheaper or Better? The Strategic Choice Behind AI Adoption","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["efficiency","value","ai","automation"],"abstract":["Most organizations adopt AI with an efficiency promise: faster delivery, leaner processes, fewer costs, more automation. That is useful — but it is only one possible game.","There is a second path: using AI to increase customer value. Better products, smarter services, more relevant experiences, faster learning from real usage, and stronger relationships with users. The problem is that these two paths often require different rules:","Efficiency asks: How can we deliver the same thing faster and cheaper?","Customer value asks: What can we now make possible that was impossible before?","In this session, we will explore the strategic choice behind AI adoption. Are we using AI to optimize existing processes, or to redesign the value we create? We will look at common traps: automating broken workflows, measuring productivity instead of impact, treating developers as cost factors, and accelerating delivery without improving user outcomes.","Participants will learn how to distinguish efficiency-driven AI from value-driven AI, how hidden metrics shape AI adoption, and how teams can ask better questions before introducing new tools, agents, or automation.","This is not a talk about specific AI tools. It is a conversation about the game we design around AI: the incentives, feedback loops, roles, metrics, and customer value assumptions that determine whether AI makes software merely cheaper — or genuinely better.","Target audience: anyone involved in AI adoption, software delivery, or product strategy."],"favourites":129,"speakers":[{"name":"Miriam Sasse","company":"S&N Invent","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-e189329b-6677-4bc7-990c-87b03871e01b.jpg","bio":"<blockquote><strong>Dr. Miriam Sasse</strong> is Lead Agile Transformation &amp; Organizational Design at <strong>S&amp;N Group</strong>, where she helps organizations navigate digital transformation and Artificial Intelligence. Her work bridges software engineering, systems thinking, organizational design, and game design. She holds a PhD in Machine Learning for manufacturing systems and is the author of <em>LEVEL UP!</em>, a book exploring what software teams, leaders, and organizations can learn from professional game designers. Miriam is a frequent international speaker known for interactive talks that challenge conventional thinking and inspire new perspectives on leadership, collaboration, and AI.</blockquote><h2></h2>","linkedin":"dr-miriam-sasse","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-06T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T10:30:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"Ycdt7IT7Kdw","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Sasse uses game design as a lens on AI adoption: decide whether AI makes the old game cheaper or creates a new, better one, and redesign roles, rules and gatekeepers.","summary":["Miriam Sasse, a mechanical engineer who has led deep-tech and machine-learning teams, frames organisational design as game design, the approach of her book Level Up: look at rules, roles, available moves and feedback, and ask whether you would want to play. The deep dive is a workshop. Six times the audience discusses a question with neighbours and posts answers to a Padlet, which she reads out. The first round, on how AI changes the rules at work, surfaced less implementation and more review, seniority losing value, a new 'AI context manager' role and developers describing themselves as 'meat proxies' pressing buttons.","Her central contrast is efficiency versus customer value. Elevator maker Schindler uses machine learning for predictive maintenance, which helps engineers but not loyalty; the US bank USAA uses data and AI to support customers through life events such as marriage or retirement, so they say they love their bank. A team that claims to do both often has no strategy, leaving developers to decide. She offers a 'system card' that maps start and end, roles, rules (including the AI Act), stakeholders, teams, technology, market and society, and shows a 40-person simulation company's card six years later, annotated with pluses and minuses.","On strategy, she gives five tests: a good strategy is not a triviality every competitor could write, does not make decisions for employees but guides them, is not a goal (her ex-employer's revenue-target 'Strategy 800'), is tangible, and is an entrepreneurial bet whose opposite is also plausible. She distinguishes assistance mode (AI on demand), integration mode (AI on a fixed path, changing roles and exception handling) and agent mode (AI as a team player, needing new rules about whom agents may contact and who can stop them), and says no mode is automatically better. She also warns against AI becoming the 'alpha player' of a cooperative board game.","After the break come four gatekeepers: finance (no money), IT (technically impossible), HR (no skills) and compliance (too risky). They are not villains: design the game around them from the start, giving finance a capped investment and feedback loop, IT boundaries, HR a learning path and compliance traceability and human decision points. A robot producing status reports faster improves the old game; asking whether the report should exist is the new game, as streaming was to the CD. If AI saves 20% of a team's time and the organisation fills it with 20% more tickets, Gerald Weinberg's context-switching losses eat the gain. Speed, she concludes, is not strategy."],"keyPoints":["Efficiency AI does the same thing faster and cheaper (Schindler's predictive maintenance); value AI makes new things possible for customers (USAA's life-event services). Picking 'both' without priorities usually means no strategy.","A system card maps not just the process but roles, rules and laws, stakeholders, neighbouring teams, technology, market and society; redrawing it after AI adoption exposes roles that appeared or vanished.","Five tests of a good strategy: no trivialities, guides decisions without making them, is not a numeric goal, is tangible, and is a bet whose opposite would also be a reasonable choice.","Three AI modes: assistance (ad hoc, organisation unchanged), integration (AI on a fixed path, roles and exception handling change) and agent (AI as a player, requiring rules on authority, monitoring and stopping agents).","AI alpha-player warning signs: suggestions accepted without scrutiny, criticism of AI output seen as a waste of time, expertise concentrated in a few specialists, unclear who verifies and corrects, and people acting as clickers rather than designers.","Involve finance, IT, HR and compliance from the start; constraints make the game playable, as in Yahtzee, where five dice and few choices still make a good game.","Citing Gerald Weinberg, she said switching between two tasks loses about 20% of time, three 40%, four 60% and five 75%, so filling AI-saved time with more tickets can cancel the gain.","From game design: negative changes such as role changes should never be a surprise; announce them early. Positive things may be surprises."],"takeaways":["Write down whether your AI initiative aims at efficiency or customer value, and test the strategy against the five principles.","Redraw your team's system card with AI in it and mark which roles, rules and stakeholders are added or removed.","Bring finance, IT, HR and compliance into an AI pilot at the start, each with what they need: a budget cap, boundaries, a learning path, traceability.","Before automating a report or process step with AI, ask whether it still needs to exist.","Decide explicitly what the time saved by AI is for, instead of letting it fill up with more parallel tickets."],"quotes":[{"text":"Sometimes if you say we are doing both, it just means there is no strategy at all. And who has to decide on what to do if there is no strategy? The developer.","at":"25:43"},{"text":"Negative things should not be a surprise, positive things can be a surprise.","at":"122:38"},{"text":"Speed alone is not a strategy and automation is not transformation.","at":"148:00"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Organisations as games"},{"at":"09:28","title":"How AI rewrites the rules"},{"at":"17:43","title":"Efficiency or customer loyalty"},{"at":"27:51","title":"System cards"},{"at":"46:56","title":"Strategy principles and AI modes"},{"at":"70:38","title":"The AI alpha player"},{"at":"90:18","title":"Four gatekeepers"},{"at":"108:48","title":"Old game versus new game"},{"at":"132:03","title":"What to do with saved time"}],"tech":["Padlet","EU AI Act"],"watchIf":"You shape how your team or company adopts AI and want a workshop-style framework for questioning strategy, roles and gatekeepers rather than a tool demo; expect much of the recording to be audience discussion.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":52101,"title":"Java Next - From Valhalla to Leyden, from Babylon to Panama, from Amber to Detroit","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["panama","java","valhalla","leyden"],"abstract":["Java's big projects are shaping its present and future. Over recent years, Amber and Leyden, Panama and Loom have greatly improved the","language, APIs, and runtime. But they're not done yet! And neither are  Valhalla and Babylon - in fact they're just getting started.","Time to take a closer look at how...","Project Valhalla mends the rift in Java's type system","Project Leyden improves Java's startup and warmup time","Project Babylon extends the reach of Java to foreign programming models and hardware","Project Panama cuts through the isthmus separating Java from native code","Project Amber makes the language more expressive and ready for today's and tomorrow's problems","Project Loom further improves efficient, structured concurrency","Project Detroit allows simple interop with Python and JavaScript","After this talk, you will know what to expect from Java in the next few years and surely there's no better way to end a great Devoxx!"],"favourites":355,"speakers":[{"name":"Nicolai Parlog","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-acf609a9-0afe-496f-90db-f7bc0e57fa11.jpg","bio":"Nicolai (aka nipafx) is a Java enthusiast focused on language features, core APIs, and runtime evolution with a passion for learning and sharing. He does that mostly at conferences and in his biweekly Inside Java Newscast, but also occasionally in podcasts, live streams, articles, and books - more on all that on nipafx.dev. He&#39;s a Java Developer Advocate at Oracle and otherwise best known for his haircut.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"nipafx.dev","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-09T09:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T10:40:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A tour of the major OpenJDK projects, from Valhalla, Leyden and Babylon to Panama, Amber, Loom and Detroit, and what each means for Java in the coming years.","summary":["Nicolai Parlog's closing session surveys where Java's big projects stand. Amber, Leyden, Panama and Loom have already improved the language, APIs and runtime, while Valhalla and Babylon are described as just getting started.","The talk looks at how Valhalla addresses the rift in Java's type system, Leyden startup and warmup time, Babylon reaching foreign programming models and hardware, Panama native interop, Amber language expressiveness, Loom structured concurrency and Detroit interop with Python and JavaScript. The goal is to show what to expect from Java in the next few years."],"keyPoints":["Valhalla targets the split in Java's type system.","Leyden targets startup and warmup time.","Babylon extends Java to foreign programming models and hardware.","Panama improves interop with native code; Detroit with Python and JavaScript.","Amber and Loom continue to evolve the language and concurrency."],"takeaways":["Follow the OpenJDK project pages to see which upcoming features affect your codebase."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Project Valhalla","Project Leyden","Project Babylon","Project Panama","Project Amber","Project Loom","Project Detroit"],"watchIf":"You want a single overview of the Java roadmap across all current OpenJDK projects.","themes":["Java language & JDK"],"demo":false}},{"id":7020,"title":"Who to blame? The AI, The Programmer, or The Prompt?","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["shared responsibility","cve","vulnerabilities","ai security"],"abstract":["This interactive session transforms AI security education into an engaging courtroom style debate. We'll present five critical vulnerabilities affecting modern AI development tools, and after each case study, the audience becomes the jury—voting on who's responsible: the careless user, the negligent developer, or the inadequate service provider.","We'll dissect real CVEs including GitHub Camo Leak (CVE-2025-59145), Ollama RCE (CVE-2024-37032), AWS MCP Server Shell Injection (CVE-2025-5277), Anthropic MCP Inspector DNS Rebinding (CVE-2025-49596 ) and Microsoft Copilot's zero-click data exfiltration (CVE-2025-32711). Each case reveals technical root causes through collaborative analysis. The List of CVEs will be usually update based on the new related CVEs as well.","The conclusion challenges the \"blame game\" itself: these vulnerabilities expose fundamental architectural weaknesses in agentic AI systems where traditional security models fail. We will talk about Mental Model Gap and how this phenomenon has been seen before in the technological leaps before. We'll establish that securing AI tools demands a shared responsibility framework developers must code defensively, providers must architect securely, and users must understand AI-specific risks."],"favourites":195,"speakers":[{"name":"Makan Sepehrifar","company":"Productboard","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-68014c00-8dd0-4467-9ac6-f205258d440d.jpg","bio":"Makan Sepehrifar is a software architect who is interested in how people think and behave in organizations. He sees software architecture as a craft and believes that every challenge has both a technical side and a social side, and both need attention at the same time. Coming from a strong developer background, he has led many teams across different domains and organizations, always working to connect technology with human needs and team dynamics.<br>He is passionate about the future of Developer Experience and how Generative AI and Agentic AI are reshaping the way developers work. Makan focuses on how these technologies can improve developer satisfaction and help teams become more adaptive and effective. He is a public speaker on Developer Experience practices and AI in this space, and he has been part of several Generative AI adoption teams in different companies, helping them use AI in a practical and effective way.","linkedin":"makan1869","bluesky":"makan1869.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T10:55:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T11:35:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"VmPMUGImB-M","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Sepehrifar puts four real AI-tooling CVEs on mock trial, lets the audience vote on who is to blame, and concludes that model providers, tool builders and developers share responsibility.","summary":["Makan Sepehrifar (Productboard) runs this lunch talk as a jury trial with Slido votes. For each case he explains the attack, then the audience blames the model developer, the tool developer or the end user, and he argues the defence. CamoLeak: a prompt hidden in a PR made GitHub Copilot Chat spell out secrets as ASCII art built from per-letter images routed through GitHub's camo image proxy, which the content security policy allowed; GitHub fixed it by disabling image rendering. Probllama: Ollama had no authentication, its container image bound to 0.0.0.0, and /api/pull accepted a manifest with path traversal, so an attacker could write a library plus ld.so.preload and get remote code execution. Fixes were path checks and digest-validated manifests. The AWS MCP server concatenated agent-supplied parameters into shell strings (OS command injection, CWE-78); the GitHub Copilot extension could set its own auto-approve flag in settings, turning a prompt injection into YOLO mode and code execution. The audience blamed the tool developer every time.","Sepehrifar then describes the \"invisible collaborator\": instructions hidden in PRs, dependencies, issues or agent config files (Cursor rules, Copilot instructions, Claude files) that the agent follows faithfully. He mentions the GitHub MCP issue where one token spanning public and private repos let an issue leak private code, malicious AI plugins on the JetBrains marketplace, and Anthropic's finding that about 250 documents can poison a model. His core argument is the mental model gap: like early email users trusting the sender field, developers picture an agent they control, while an attacker sees a credentialed instruction-follower with a shared, poisonable context. The common chain runs from prompt injection through context manipulation, path traversal and RCE to overwritten guardrails and tool impersonation. His answer is shared responsibility: providers ship safe models, tool builders threat-model, developers consume safely and share what they find."],"keyPoints":["CamoLeak exfiltrated secrets from GitHub Copilot Chat by encoding them as a sequence of image URLs through GitHub's own camo proxy, which bypassed the content security policy.","Probllama (Ollama, CVSS 9.8) combined no authentication, a default 0.0.0.0 binding and path traversal in /api/pull to write ld.so.preload and achieve remote code execution.","The AWS MCP server built shell commands by string concatenation, so a prompt injection could run arbitrary commands under the developer's AWS identity.","Letting a coding agent edit its own IDE settings, such as an auto-approve flag, turns prompt injection into code execution; Sepehrifar says Claude Code, Gemini, Cursor and Roo Code had similar issues.","Most attacks followed one chain: prompt injection, context manipulation, path traversal, remote code execution, then guardrail overwrite or tool impersonation."],"takeaways":["Review agent configuration files (rules, instructions, settings) in your repositories as you would code.","Bind local AI services such as Ollama to 127.0.0.1 and put authentication in front of anything you expose.","Use separate, narrowly scoped tokens per MCP server and per repository visibility level.","Never pass agent-provided parameters into shell strings; use argument arrays and validate intent."],"quotes":[{"text":"It's an instruction follower, but it's not always loyal to you as the developer. It's loyal to the instructions.","at":"33:33"},{"text":"Millions of tokens is a very good place to hide.","at":"33:33"},{"text":"It's not about not using AI, but it's about knowing what you are doing.","at":"37:20"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Rules of the trial"},{"at":"04:40","title":"The usual suspects"},{"at":"06:02","title":"CamoLeak in Copilot Chat"},{"at":"12:18","title":"Probllama: Ollama RCE"},{"at":"17:30","title":"AWS MCP shell injection"},{"at":"23:27","title":"Copilot changing IDE settings"},{"at":"27:56","title":"The invisible collaborator"},{"at":"30:49","title":"The mental model gap"},{"at":"34:34","title":"Shared responsibility"}],"tech":["GitHub Copilot","Ollama","AWS MCP Server","MCP","VS Code","Claude Code","Cursor","Slido"],"watchIf":"You use coding agents or MCP servers and want a concrete, entertaining tour of how real AI-tool vulnerabilities were exploited and fixed.","themes":["Security & supply chain","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":8171,"title":"0 to 350 Engineers on Claude Code: What Could Go Wrong?","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"People, Practices & Career","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["coding","developers","ai","agents"],"abstract":["We are living through a revolution.","From assisting us in writing code to coordinating a fleet of agents, we find ourselves in the eye of the storm. Coding agents like Claude Code, Copilot, Codex CLI, and Cursor are reshaping our craft and our place within the company.","I brought Claude Code to my company, Back Market, in June 2025. We were \"behind\" other companies, such as Doctolib. Step by step, we moved from smart autocompletion to writing features end-to-end. Today we have Product Managers who write code, developers who do architecture and design, and managers who build their own internal tools—all with Claude Code. And there's me, looking at how we can control our monthly spending, leading a team of 40 subject-matter experts, with successes (and failures).","But more broadly, what's the flip side? What happens when a junior developer opens a single pull request with 50 modified files using Claude Code? What happens when one team migrates a module in place of the team that originally owned it, with Claude Code? What are the risks, and how do you stay in control within your company?","Finally, as developers—whether junior or senior—this asks us to adapt.","How do we get up to speed? What does prompting actually mean? How does an agent work? What are Skills? What's the impact on CI/CD? On the Platform engineering teams? On the run side? On upfront design?","I don't think AI will replace developers. I think developers who use AI can replace us."],"favourites":385,"speakers":[{"name":"Nicolas Martignole","company":"Back Market","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-e4f8b008-e24f-4ca0-a520-acbb98ac703a.jpg","bio":"Nicolas is a Principal Engineer at Back Market, a leader in refurbished electronic devices in 18 countries. He is also the co-creator and organizer of Devoxx France. He has a JVM background but has worked in different industries and technologies. Before joining Back Market, he was a Principal Engineer at Doctolib, during the COVID19 crisis. <br>He&#39;s also the author of &quot;le Touilleur Express&quot;, a blog about the journey of a software developer. <br>He has a YouTube Channel with more than 800 subscribers, on GenAI and Coding Agent.<br>","linkedin":"nmartignole","bluesky":null,"twitter":"nmartignole"}],"start":"2026-10-07T13:10:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T14:00:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"HzbNrEDZkYU","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Martignole on Back Market's organic Claude Code rollout, its four 'collisions' (huge PRs, cross-team changes, an $86,000 bill, people) and the fixes: OpenRouter, OpenCode, budgets, elected experts.","summary":["Martignole, principal engineer at Back Market and a Devoxx France co-founder, brought Cursor and Claude Code to the company in June 2025. There was no executive mandate: volunteers asked for licences, product managers, data scientists and marketing joined, and opening Claude Code to all employees via Okta passed 100 users by late November. The first collision: juniors opening 50-file PRs generated in minutes with green tests, which seniors cannot really review, so they approve them anyway. He blames vague 'fix the bug' prompts, week-long bloated sessions, tests written by the same session as the code, and scolding the agent instead of rewinding.","The fixes are back-to-basics engineering: specifications, small PRs (Google's engineering practices, Kent Beck), Matt Pocock's skills, and human review for AI-written code. In the second collision, a team blocked by another team's legacy Python pricing service rewrote it themselves. Agents remove the friction that used to protect repositories, but on-call accountability stays with the owners, so Back Market cleaned up CODEOWNERS and asked teams for CONTRIBUTING.md files telling humans and agents what they accept. Third, in June 2026 the monthly Anthropic API bill hit $86,000 for under 300 users, and reaching the workspace limit blocked every Claude Code user until the next month.","Coaching the top spenders one by one did not scale. Back Market moved to OpenRouter, restricted to 31 GDPR and zero-data-retention providers including open-weight models, and replaced Claude Code with OpenCode, which imports existing skills and CLAUDE.md files. Each user has a fixed monthly budget, a Slack bot grants $20 top-ups, and 41 projects have justified exception budgets. He says this saved $171,000 in three months with more users. The fourth collision was people: instead of AI champions, each of 40-plus teams elects an SME who meets weekly on skills, MCP and tools, and he ran a workshop for engineering managers, only 35% of whom had used Claude Code."],"keyPoints":["Generating code is not velocity: code costs almost nothing to write now, but human review time has not changed.","Splitting work across sessions avoids the 'green test echo chamber', where the same session writes both the code and the tests that pass it.","Martignole said Anthropic's advanced spend controls required roughly a $1M commitment, leaving mid-sized companies stuck between fixed-price plans and pay-as-you-go.","OpenRouter's prompt classifier showed coding was only about 23-24% of activity for 229 tech users; most use is specs, commits, analysis and other non-code work.","He claimed about 75% of tokens on OpenRouter now go to open-weight, mostly Chinese, models, and that GLM models hosted by European or US providers are good enough for much of the work.","Product managers who want to ship code needed GitHub access and an internal deployment platform, because Vercel was not allowed for security reasons.","Impact is measured by stories and time to deploy or fix, not by PR counts or lines of code, which he calls vanity metrics.","Small companies (under about 150 users) should use fixed-price business plans, never personal licences, because leaking customer data through a personal account is a GDPR problem."],"takeaways":["Write a CONTRIBUTING.md and tidy CODEOWNERS in every repo so agents and other teams know which changes you accept.","Set a per-user monthly AI budget with a lightweight top-up and exception process before the invoice surprises your CFO.","Put a model router (such as OpenRouter) and a provider-agnostic agent (such as OpenCode) in front of your models so you can switch providers and filter for GDPR compliance.","Ask each team to elect one peer as an AI subject-matter expert instead of appointing central AI champions.","Start fresh agent sessions per task and use rewind rather than arguing with the agent."],"quotes":[{"text":"We increase the number of tokens per second for agents, but we haven't increased the number of tokens for humans.","at":"08:18"},{"text":"Agents don't care about your organization. They are very unpolite.","at":"19:32"},{"text":"I don't think personally that AI will replace developers. I personally think that developers that use AI are going to replace us.","at":"43:11"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Back Market and an organic rollout"},{"at":"06:55","title":"Collision 1: the monster PR"},{"at":"12:41","title":"Back to engineering basics"},{"at":"15:30","title":"Collision 2: cross-team contributions"},{"at":"19:32","title":"Collision 3: the $86,000 bill"},{"at":"23:25","title":"OpenRouter, OpenCode and budgets"},{"at":"29:41","title":"Collision 4: people and SMEs"},{"at":"35:29","title":"Measuring impact"},{"at":"38:09","title":"Lessons and next steps"}],"tech":["Claude Code","Cursor","OpenCode","OpenRouter","Anthropic API","Okta","CODEOWNERS","CONTRIBUTING.md","MCP","Agent skills","GLM","Mistral AI","Fireworks AI"],"watchIf":"You are rolling out coding agents beyond a pilot team and need to control cost, review load and cross-team ownership.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":25671,"title":"State of Agentic Coding","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["ai-assisted","autonomous","softwareengineering","agents"],"abstract":["As AI-assisted coding evolves from simple autocomplete to autonomous agents, developers need new mental models and tools to maintain productivity and trust. In this session, we are going to revisit the major developments in agentic coding from over the past few months, from MCP to skills and beyond; discuss how to improve trust in your agents and take a tour of the new Google Antigravity ecosystem. Every technique discussed will be paired with a live demo using practical examples of day to day development so we can see the coding agent in action. Supporting materials for this session can be found at: https://github.com/danicat/state-of-agentic-coding"],"favourites":545,"speakers":[{"name":"Daniela Petruzalek","company":"Google Cloud","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocJALSmaAeQ2KPyjkQq0mv6gykwBqgZXif1g3KZyFjCqQDqQZOpEUw=s96-c","bio":"Daniela Petruzalek is an experienced IT professional with background in software engineering, pre-sales and developer relations, currently a Senior Developer Relations Engineer at Google. Her specialisation is data engineering and back end development and she is a former Google Developer Expert in Go and Google Cloud Platform. She&#39;s also a Google Cloud Certified Data Engineer, Oracle Certified Professional and TEDx speaker. In her spare time she contributes to open source, plays video games and pets random cats on the streets.","linkedin":"petruzalek","bluesky":"danicat83.bsky.social","twitter":"danicat83"}],"start":"2026-10-08T09:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T10:40:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"nHRSwiFNQ9E","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Petruzalek gives an opinionated tour of what works in agentic coding today: freestyle prompts with grill-me, tiny rules files, skills loaded on demand, dynamic sub-agent swarms, and Antigravity sidecars, all watched for 'rot'.","summary":["Daniela Petruzalek (Google) opens with a show of hands on 'seven stages of agentic coding', from copy-pasting from a browser chat to swarms that write all the code. She frames the progression as one of trust, built through experience and better tools. She describes three agent surfaces (code-first IDEs with a side panel, agent-first managers, and headless background agents) and shows Google's Antigravity family for each: the Antigravity 2.0 agent manager, the VS Code-based Antigravity IDE, her preferred Antigravity CLI, and an SDK, with an unofficial Java port by her colleague Guillaume Laforge. A timeline runs from prompt engineering in 2023 through rules files, MCP, hooks and skills to sub-agents, plugins and loop engineering.","Her current practice: prompts are now freestyle with an acceptance criterion and a grill-me skill, plus the /goal command to loop until done, and she rebuilds a prototype several times rather than relying on plan mode until she understands the problem. Her GEMINI.md is 15 lines: load her skills tool, never add fallbacks or mocks, and don't look at other folders. Rules, prompts and skills 'rot' as models and harnesses change, so revisit them after every major release. She stopped using hooks because agents learned to rewrite the hook scripts that blocked them; use Git hooks or CI for hard rules. MCP remains useful mainly for serving always-current documentation, while documentation skills rot fastest.","Skills were, for her, the most important development of the year. She triggers them explicitly with slash notation rather than trusting automatic activation, and built 'Kung Fu', a CLI that loads skills just in time from catalogues with versions and update checks. She distinguishes documentation, process and behaviour skills; her behavioural 'swarm coding' skill lets a coordinator agent pick its own sub-agent team within an agent budget, rather than you defining static roles. Live, she starts a JVM garbage-collector game with a budget of 20 agents, and runs a code review with ten parallel sub-agents plus an adversarial 'Uno Reverse' skill that criticises and simplifies.","Finally she demos an Antigravity sidecar: a Node.js process paired with a generative-UI skill that adds a paint panel, uses Nano Banana to restyle sketches, and sends drawings into the conversation through a conversation-ID-based CLI. Her closing points: 'rot' is the keyword of the moment, and personalised tooling that improves your own loop is the present, not the future."],"keyPoints":["Trust in agents grows through experience and tooling; the room thinned out quickly once tool calls no longer needed approval.","Rules files are suggestions, not enforcement; keep them to a handful of lines and put mandatory steps in scripts, Git hooks or CI.","Agent hooks can be bypassed: Petruzalek saw an agent edit a blocking hook script to disable it, so hooks must live where the agent cannot write.","MCP is best for centrally served, always-current documentation; for simple tools a skill plus a script is lighter.","Load skills explicitly with a slash notation in the prompt instead of relying on description matching; too many installed skills bloat context.","Let a coordinator agent decide its own team of sub-agents within an agent budget, rather than prescribing static roles, and keep the main session free for steering.","Parallel review sub-agents plus an adversarial simplifying skill, filtered through grill-me, replace much of her line-by-line code review.","Prompts, rules and skills all 'rot' as models and harnesses evolve, so review them with each major release."],"takeaways":["Trim your AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md/GEMINI.md to the few instructions that really need to be in every session.","Move must-always-happen steps (build, test, lint) out of rules and into Git hooks or CI.","Add a grill-me style skill to your prompts so the agent questions you before building.","Write a behavioural skill that tells a coordinator how to split work across sub-agents, and give it an agent budget.","Re-test your skills and rules whenever you upgrade the model or the agent harness."],"quotes":[{"text":"Because it's non-deterministic, it's a bad place for enforcing things. If you need a deterministic process, put it in a script.","at":"15:57"},{"text":"Rot is the technical keyword of the moment.","at":"46:08"},{"text":"Personalized software is the present, not the future.","at":"46:08"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Seven stages of agentic coding"},{"at":"05:51","title":"Agentic surfaces and Antigravity"},{"at":"08:17","title":"Timeline of techniques"},{"at":"11:53","title":"Prompts and rules"},{"at":"20:08","title":"Hooks and MCP"},{"at":"22:53","title":"Skills and Kung Fu"},{"at":"32:27","title":"Sub-agents and swarm coding"},{"at":"38:33","title":"Sidecars and plugins"}],"tech":["Google Antigravity","Antigravity CLI","Antigravity SDK","Gemini","Nano Banana","MCP","Agent Skills","GEMINI.md","AGENTS.md","Agent plugins","Node.js","Go"],"watchIf":"You already use coding agents daily and want a practitioner's current view on rules, hooks, skills, sub-agents and Antigravity, with live demos.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":22916,"title":"Java and the Next Generation of Programming Models","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["java","inference","foreignfunctionmemory","codereflection"],"abstract":["Modern software increasingly interacts with AI models, query languages, and other specialized programming models, forcing you to switch contexts, embed foreign code, or write verbose wrappers to bridge the gap. What if you could express logic from outside the JVM directly in Java?","In this session, we start by looking at how Java enables you to safely interact with runtimes outside of the JVM using the Foreign Function and Memory API and tools like jextract.","Then will push further with Project Babylon’s code reflection: express AI models as Java programs that Babylon can analyze, transform and lower to inference engines, thus leveraging hardware accelerator backends while skipping external model files or the need to glue code.","Along the way, you will learn how to build expressive FFM-based inference today and author pure Java AI-ready models with Code Reflection tomorrow!"],"favourites":251,"speakers":[{"name":"Ana-Maria Mihalceanu","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14Gj1OS6OpAzuwBKwoUuCi9gxvNPvdmVA7nbW8lg3mg=s96-c","bio":"Ana is a Java Champion Alumni and Senior Developer Advocate for the Java Platform Group at Oracle, with an extensive background in Java and cloud development. More recently, her work has expanded to explore how machine learning and AI can be applied within the Java ecosystem, helping Java developers adopt emerging capabilities while continuing to benefit from the stability, tooling, and performance of the Java Platform. She actively supports the growth of technical communities through knowledge sharing and enjoys curating content for conferences as a program committee member. ","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"ammbra1508.bsky.social","twitter":"ammbra1508"}],"start":"2026-10-08T11:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T12:40:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"kuUeHD_k5fs","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Mihalceanu runs a local LLM from Java today by calling ONNX Runtime GenAI through jextract-generated FFM bindings, then writes a Llama model as plain Java that Project Babylon's code reflection turns into an ONNX graph.","summary":["Ana-Maria Mihalceanu defines a programming model as the abstractions through which a programmer, human or AI, expresses computation without caring how it is executed. Java's model was shaped around the CPU, while AI inference lives in a separate ecosystem. She walks through the inference pipeline (tokenization, embedding, transformer layers, sampling, decoding), notes that almost all the compute sits in the repeated transformer layers, and explains that models ship as weight files in formats such as PyTorch .pt, ONNX and GGUF, each with its own native runtime.","The first bridge is the Foreign Function and Memory API. jextract reads a C header and generates Java bindings in which each C symbol becomes a method backed by a downcall handle, with memory segments for native buffers. In the demo she runs jextract against the ONNX Runtime GenAI header, ignores the warnings for C constructs it cannot map, and runs a single Java class with --enable-native-access that tokenizes a prompt, generates token by token with a Phi-3 mini 4K Instruct ONNX model, and frees the native sequence afterwards. ONNX was chosen because LangChain4j, DJL and inference4j already build on it.","She then opens the model file in Netron: a large graph of repeated layers whose operator nodes pass protobuf-serialized data. To write that graph in Java she turns to Project Babylon's code reflection, currently a draft JEP heading for incubation. A method annotated @Reflect, such as a ReLU activation, can be retrieved as a FuncOp code model: an immutable tree of operations, bodies, blocks and values that can be traversed, built and transformed into other code models. Bytecode is too low-level and JVM-specific for this, which is why the higher-level model exists.","In the Babylon ONNX example, OpGen generates an annotated Java method for each operator in the ONNX operator schema, and ProtoGen generates protobuf builder classes used by OnnxBuilder. A Llama model with 16 transformer layers is written in under 200 lines of Java: config constants from the GenAI config JSON, weights loaded in the constructor, and an @Reflect forward method that is transformed into an ONNX model and run through an ONNX Runtime GenAI session. The demo answered a 'tell me a joke' prompt. She closes by noting that HotSpot JIT, intrinsics and the Vector API help on the CPU side, and that Juan Fumero's work takes Babylon to GPUs."],"keyPoints":["jextract (early-access builds from jdk.java.net/jextract, also on SDKMAN) generates FFM bindings from a C header. Warnings about unmapped C constructs can usually be ignored.","Calling ONNX Runtime GenAI through FFM lets a single Java class run local LLM inference with --enable-native-access and a library path, with no wrapper library.","The generated bindings map one-to-one to the native API, so the runtime's C documentation can be followed directly.","Babylon's code reflection exposes @Reflect-annotated methods and lambdas as FuncOp code models: operations containing bodies, blocks and SSA-style values that you can traverse, build and transform.","Transformations go from code model to code model rather than to bytecode, keeping enough semantics to target foreign programming models such as ONNX, CUDA or PyTorch.","In the Babylon ONNX example, OpGen generates Java operator methods from the ONNX operator schema and ProtoGen generates the protobuf classes used to emit ONNX models.","Mihalceanu said a Llama LLM expressed in Java takes fewer than 200 lines, with weights read from the open-weights file at construction time."],"takeaways":["Try jextract on the C header of a native library you currently wrap with JNI or a third-party binding.","Run a small ONNX model locally from Java through FFM before reaching for a hosted inference API.","Open your model files in Netron to understand the graph you are executing.","Clone Project Babylon and run the code reflection ONNX examples to see where Java-authored models are heading."],"quotes":[{"text":"My toothbrush has an AI feature. And I'm wondering what AI is running on my toothbrush.","at":"03:08"},{"text":"Bytecode is too low level and can translate away useful information.","at":"27:12"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"What is a programming model"},{"at":"04:31","title":"How LLM inference works"},{"at":"07:19","title":"FFM API and jextract"},{"at":"11:30","title":"Demo: ONNX GenAI via FFM"},{"at":"17:19","title":"Inspecting a model in Netron"},{"at":"20:12","title":"Babylon code reflection"},{"at":"28:29","title":"OpGen, ProtoGen and OnnxBuilder"},{"at":"30:54","title":"Demo: Llama in Java"},{"at":"36:05","title":"Wrap-up"}],"tech":["Foreign Function & Memory API","jextract","Project Babylon","Code reflection","ONNX","ONNX Runtime GenAI","Phi-3","Llama","Netron","Protocol Buffers","LangChain4j","DJL","inference4j","Vector API","SDKMAN"],"watchIf":"You want to run AI models from Java without Python glue, or want to see how Project Babylon's code reflection actually works.","themes":["LLMs in Java apps","Java language & JDK"],"demo":true}},{"id":24233,"title":"Build the Builder: A Java Coding Agent in Your IDE with ACP","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["guardrails","orchestration","agent","ide"],"abstract":["Most agent talks stop when the agent can call a tool. This session tackles the harder and more practical question: how do you build a custom, production-minded agent for your organization’s workflows and guardrails and make it a first-class participant inside the developer’s IDE?","In this live-coded session, we’ll build a coding agent in Java / Kotlin, connect it directly to an IntelliJ-based IDE through the Agent Client Protocol (ACP), and extend it into an orchestrator that can collaborate with external coding agents such as Codex, Claude, and Gemini.","You’ll learn how to:","Build your own agent on the JVM, including its reasoning loop, tools, state, and guardrails. Although we’ll create a coding agent, the same techniques apply to chatbots, banking assistants, support agents, and other domain-specific systems.","Connect a custom agent to an IDE through ACP protocol, without creating a dedicated integration for every agent.","Integrate and orchestrate multiple external coding agents through ACP in a coordinated pipeline, assigning each task to the agent best suited to perform it.","Take advantage of the IDE as more than a chat window by giving agents access to the project context, code understanding, developer-defined skills, and more!","By the end of the session, you’ll have a practical blueprint for building an agent that understands your development environment, follows your rules, and can coordinate other agents to complete real software-engineering tasks."],"favourites":398,"speakers":[{"name":"Vadim Briliantov","company":"JetBrains","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-08d32376-4f7e-4483-800c-cd8ccf513b2f.jpg","bio":"<strong>Technical Product Manager for the ACP Protocol and Agent Ecosystem at JetBrains.</strong><br><br>Vadim is the creator and former Technical Lead of the Koog framework at JetBrains. Over the past 9 years at JetBrains, he has contributed to a wide range of projects, including the IntelliJ Kotlin plugin, Kotlin Libraries, the Ktor framework, Qodana Cloud Backend, and Multiplatform Tooling. He also led the technological direction of AI agent development across multiple products as part of the AI Agents Platform.<br>Today, Vadim is responsible for the ACP Protocol and Open Agent Ecosystem in JetBrains IDEs, driving the vision of making JetBrains IDEs the open gateway for integrating any external AI agents while augmenting them with JetBrains IDEs&#39; deep code understanding and development capabilities.","linkedin":"vadim-briliantov","bluesky":null,"twitter":"VadimBriliantov"}],"start":"2026-10-07T10:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T10:50:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"y4F1XBtQeDw","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Briliantov vibe-codes a Koog-based coding agent with a typed workflow, connects it to IntelliJ through the Agent Client Protocol, and shows IDE skills that cut agents' token use.","summary":["Briliantov, who leads ACP at JetBrains and was tech lead of the Koog agent framework, argues that building your own agent lets you control context and enforce organisational guardrails as code rather than prompts. He models an agent as a typed graph of nodes on the JVM: an implement step outputs a ChangesSummary data class, a verify step outputs a VerificationResult, and the flow loops back to reprogramming until verification passes. The agent fills in a type instead of being asked nicely. Koog provides workflows today; Spring AI and LangChain4j offer models and tool calling, and he says Spring AI has announced workflows too.","He explains ACP, the Agent Client Protocol created by JetBrains and Zed. It covers initialize and new-session requests, streamed session updates for tool calls and edits, permission requests, and the final prompt result. It now connects more than 42 agents (Claude, Codex, Gemini CLI, Junie and others) to JetBrains IDEs, Zed, Neovim and, recently, Xcode. An ACP agent can itself be a client of other agents, there is a TCK for checking compatibility, sub-agent support has just landed, and actor-style agent-to-agent messaging is being explored. Live, he has Codex in JetBrains Air generate the agent, adds ACP streaming with the Kotlin SDK, and registers it as a custom ACP agent.","Slow conference Wi-Fi stalled the Gradle build for most of the demo, so the orchestrator idea (Claude implements, the custom agent verifies, Codex repairs) was prompted but not shown running. His closing argument is that agents waste tokens without IDE intelligence. A rename across 100 files costs more than 100 tool calls plus full build logs for a plain agent, but one call to the IDE's refactoring. The bundled IJ Debugger skill exposes XDebug MCP tools such as log points on a running JVM, so agents need not add println calls and rebuild. He ends with Codex using that skill to debug his own agent."],"keyPoints":["Use typed inputs and outputs (data classes) between agent workflow steps as guardrails instead of prompt instructions.","Separate tools (operations, local or MCP), skills (how and when to use tools) and guardrails (deterministic logic via hooks or a custom workflow).","ACP lets any compliant agent run inside any compliant editor, so you do not need a dedicated IDE integration per agent or an AI subscription from the IDE vendor.","Custom agents are added in JetBrains IDEs through 'Add custom ACP agent', which takes a command, arguments and environment variables such as API keys.","Briliantov suggests writing a test compatibility kit from your spec so the prompt can be 'comply with the new version of the TCK'.","IDE skills install per agent (Claude, Codex) into the project, for example as a SKILL.md under .claude, and expose IDE MCP tools to any agent."],"takeaways":["Model your agent as a workflow with typed step outputs and a verify loop, using Koog, Spring AI or LangChain4j.","Make an in-house agent speak ACP (there is a Kotlin SDK) and run the ACP TCK, rather than building a separate IDE plugin.","Install the IJ Debugger skill so agents use log points on the running JVM instead of println-and-rebuild cycles.","Give agents IDE refactoring tools for codebase-wide changes instead of letting them edit files one at a time."],"quotes":[{"text":"In the world when everyone will build their applications using agents, the question is, who's gonna build the builder?","at":"00:31"},{"text":"Why would you build your own harness from scratch? Well, there is no reason. So let's actually use ACP to talk to Claude and to Codex and then orchestrate them from the top.","at":"33:14"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why build your own agent"},{"at":"04:20","title":"Agents as typed workflows"},{"at":"08:27","title":"Vibe-coding the agent"},{"at":"12:24","title":"The Agent Client Protocol"},{"at":"19:26","title":"Sub-agents and agent teams"},{"at":"21:50","title":"Connecting the agent to IntelliJ"},{"at":"33:14","title":"Orchestrating Claude and Codex"},{"at":"35:25","title":"IDE intelligence and skills"},{"at":"43:50","title":"Debugging with the IJ Debugger skill"}],"tech":["Agent Client Protocol (ACP)","Koog","Kotlin","Java","IntelliJ IDEA","JetBrains Air","Junie","Codex","Claude Code","Gemini CLI","Zed","Xcode","Spring AI","LangChain4j","MCP","Agent skills","Gradle"],"watchIf":"You want to build an in-house coding agent on the JVM, or understand how ACP lets agents and IDEs work together.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","LLMs in Java apps","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":4087,"title":"Harness Engineering: Building the System Around Your AI Coding Agent","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["tool calls","ai agents","context management","safety guardrails"],"abstract":["AI coding agents feel like magic. It is easy to assume there is something exotic inside, some secret sauce that makes agents reliable. Well, there isn't, the core of every AI coding agent is very simple: send a message to a model, parse tool calls, execute them, feed the results back, repeat. Everything else (context management, permissions, observability, safety guardrails) is engineering layered on top of it that we should be building.","In this deep dive, we build that engine from scratch (in Java!), live on stage. Not to build the best agent, but to understand how the pieces fit together. We point it at a real codebase, and watch what happens. It compiles. Tests pass. And it violates every convention the team agreed on. So we iterate. We add context, constraints, and feedback, and at each step we examine what changed, why it helped, and what it maps to in the tools you already use.","The goal is a mental model. By the end, you will understand the components inside the AI coding tools you use every day, what you can layer on top to get smoother results and safer expectations, and where the honest limits still are, the gap no amount of engineering has closed yet."],"favourites":605,"speakers":[{"name":"JI Darwish","company":"Xomnia","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-69dfe225-6442-4759-bebe-064247f07b54.jpg","bio":"Ji is a Data Platform Engineer at Xomnia with a background in Computer Science &amp; Engineering from TU Delft and a recently completed MSc in Artificial Intelligence from Utrecht University. With years of experience in software development, he focuses on solving real-world software and data problems, while maintaining a strong interest in AI, education, and emerging digital policy. He is particularly drawn to questions around EU sovereign tech, AI alignment, and how to build systems that are not only capable, but also compliant, trustworthy, and grounded in human values. Driven by the belief that technology, when built thoughtfully, can be a genuine force for good, Ji is passionate about the role AI can play in improving learning experiences and advancing society responsibly.","linkedin":"ji-darwish","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T12:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T12:50:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"6N6qFqn1_Uo","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Darwish explains a coding agent as model plus harness and argues that developers control the outer harness: guides, deterministic sensors, gated checks, bounded repair loops and explicit acceptance criteria.","summary":["Darwish, a data engineer at Xomnia and former Java/Scala developer, said up front that the live from-scratch Java build promised in the abstract had moved to a self-guided workshop linked by QR code. The talk covers the concepts only. Their motivation is that agentic coding feels like moving from Java back to JavaScript: fast, but with little safety. The concerns are maintainability, rising token costs and EU AI Act audit trails. Vendor claims (Stripe's thousand-plus agent PRs, Google's 75% AI-generated code, Uber's 70% agent PRs) are self-reported. Gartner forecasts that 40% of companies using coding agents may scale back by the end of 2027.","An agent is the model plus a harness. Terminal-Bench showed Claude Opus 4.5 scoring differently in different harnesses, and the minimal Terminus 2 harness with only a bash tool beat Claude Code. Following Birgitta Böckeler's work on harness engineering, Darwish separates the inner harness (the vendor's loop: call the model, run tool calls, feed results back, with nothing deciding when to stop) from the outer harness that users control. Böckeler's two axes divide the outer harness into guides (before acting) and sensors (feedback after). Each can be computational (scaffolding generators, compilers, linters, ArchUnit-style rules, tests) or inferential (AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md, LLM reviewers).","A Claude Haiku-generated bookshelf app showed the gap: the borrow logic let a second member take an already-lent book, and the generated test suite had no assertions. Darwish argues for behaviour-driven Given/When/Then specs alongside TDD, so the developer stays the owner of the business logic. Research shows 82% of agents solve a task on the first loop, but success drops to 67.3% after a failed iteration, often because a bloated context hides an answer the agent had already found.","His proposed outer loop: guide with AGENTS.md and a spec, then compile as a gate, then run independent checks (lint, business behaviour, architecture boundaries) and report all failures together. Only after those pass does the expensive full test suite run. Repairs happen in a fresh context with a focused prompt (purpose, failed property, diagnostics, reproduction) and a fixed budget before handing back to a human. Microsoft's Angular library port, where 221 of 222 tests passed but the agent had hardcoded the demo, shows why hard architecture tests matter. Vendors are adding hooks, repo maps, sandboxing and separate review agents. The session ended about ten minutes early."],"keyPoints":["Agent = model + harness. On Terminal-Bench the same Claude Opus 4.5 scored differently per harness, and a bash-only harness (Terminus 2) beat Claude Code.","The inner agent loop is just: call the model, execute tool calls, feed results back. Nothing in it decides when to stop.","Böckeler's matrix: guides versus sensors, each computational (deterministic) or inferential (LLM-based). Java already has strong computational sensors.","Agent success fell from 82% on the first attempt to 67.3% after a failed loop, as bloated context makes agents miss or abandon correct answers.","Gate the pipeline: compile first, then lint, behaviour and architecture checks in parallel with all failures reported together, and only then the slow full test suite.","Repair in a fresh context with a focused prompt and a hard iteration budget instead of letting one agent loop for hours.","Architecture tests (for example \"no domain class touches the database\") stop agents from taking shortcuts, like the hardcoded demo in Microsoft's Angular library port.","Write behaviour-driven Given/When/Then specs so business decisions are not left to the model. A Haiku-built app shipped a borrowing bug and a test suite with no assertions."],"takeaways":["Add deterministic architecture tests (ArchUnit or similar) for the boundaries your AGENTS.md describes.","Configure agent hooks to compile and lint after edits, and run the full test suite only once the cheap checks pass.","Cap repair attempts and start a fresh context with a targeted failure description rather than letting the agent loop.","Write acceptance criteria as Given/When/Then scenarios before handing a feature to an agent.","Stop the coding agent from editing the tests that define success."],"quotes":[{"text":"The model is the brain that only just chooses what action needs to be taken. The harness basically decides how the action is taken.","at":"12:04"},{"text":"Agents find a way to get to their objective. If there's a hard test in place, they won't go that far.","at":"32:42"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"From JavaScript vibes to Java safety"},{"at":"03:07","title":"Why harnesses matter now"},{"at":"06:58","title":"Agent equals model plus harness"},{"at":"10:43","title":"Inner and outer harness"},{"at":"13:26","title":"Guides and sensors"},{"at":"19:33","title":"The bookshelf bug and BDD"},{"at":"23:27","title":"Why agent loops degrade"},{"at":"24:42","title":"A gated outer loop"},{"at":"31:22","title":"Architecture tests and tooling"},{"at":"36:49","title":"Four takeaways"}],"tech":["Claude Code","Codex CLI","Gemini CLI","Cursor","Windsurf","OpenCode","Pi","Terminal-Bench","Terminus 2","Claude Opus 4.5","Claude Haiku","AGENTS.md","CLAUDE.md","ArchUnit","Graphify","EU AI Act"],"watchIf":"You use Claude Code or similar agents on a long-lived Java codebase and want a mental model for adding deterministic checks and bounded loops around them. Note that there is no live coding.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Testing & quality"],"demo":false}},{"id":15359,"title":"Is AI killing XP practices? (Or are we letting it?)","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["refactoring","xp","ai","testing"],"abstract":["For the last 20-30 years, the software industry has been learning — sometimes the hard way — how to build better software. Practices like TDD, refactoring, pair programming, simple design, collective code ownership, continuous integration, and coding standards didn't appear by magic. They came from pain, failed projects, and the brilliant minds behind XP — Kent Beck, Ward Cunningham, and Ron Jeffries — who asked: \"can we do this better?\"","And now? Now we have AI. And suddenly, some developers are asking: \"do we still need all that?\"","In this session, we'll look at 7 XP practices that are genuinely at risk in an AI-assisted world — not because they're wrong, but because AI makes it tempting to skip them. We'll see what happens when teams do skip them (spoiler: it's not pretty 😅), and more importantly, how these practices don't just survive the AI era — they become *more critical than ever*.","AI amplifies everything. Good habits and bad ones. If your team has no tests, no shared standards, no feedback loop — AI will help you build the mess of your life, faster than ever before.","AI is the best stress test XP practices have ever had. Spoiler: they pass 😉"],"favourites":300,"speakers":[{"name":"Nacho Cougil","company":"Dynatrace","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-2a8cc803-44ba-4599-9b4a-53ce17c0085d.jpg","bio":"Nacho is a software engineer from Barcelona, fan of eXtreme Programming (XP) practices who has been working with Java and other web technologies before the Y2K. He had experience in different roles in the IT world (&#39;guy-for-everything&#39;, developer, team lead, project manager and CTO) working in companies in many sectors: energy, e-commerce, startups (some unicorn) technology consultancy and application performance management. Now working as a Principal Software Engineer at Dynatrace developing software for monitoring applications and in 2022 became Java Champion.<br>Always concerned with questions like &quot;how does it work?&quot; and &quot;how can we make it better?&quot;, Nacho loves to share his knowledge with others and learn from them. As a natural progression to this attitude, he founded the <a href=\"https://barcelonajug.org\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Barcelona Java Users Group</a> and co-founded the <a href=\"https://www.devbcn.com\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Barcelona Developers Conference</a> (DevBcn).<br>When he is not thinking about the next interesting thing to do for the community, he enjoys spending time with his family, playing sports or improving his TDD and other XP skills.","linkedin":"icougil","bluesky":"icougil.bsky.social","twitter":"icougil"}],"start":"2026-10-09T08:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"Argues that seven Extreme Programming practices are tempting to skip with AI assistance, and that they matter more, not less, when AI amplifies both good and bad habits.","summary":["Nacho Cougil's session looks at seven XP practices, drawn from the family that includes TDD, refactoring, pair programming, simple design, collective code ownership, continuous integration and coding standards, that are at risk in an AI-assisted world. The risk is not that they are wrong, but that AI makes skipping them tempting.","The talk sets out to show what happens when teams skip them and why the practices become more critical. The central argument is that AI amplifies everything: a team without tests, shared standards or a feedback loop will build a mess faster, so AI acts as a stress test that XP practices pass."],"keyPoints":["Seven XP practices are examined as being at risk when AI makes skipping them easy.","Candidate practices include TDD, refactoring, pair programming, simple design, collective ownership, CI and coding standards.","AI amplifies both good and bad habits.","Without tests, standards and feedback loops, AI speeds up building a mess."],"takeaways":["Keep tests and refactoring in your workflow when using AI assistants rather than treating them as optional."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":[],"watchIf":"Your team is adopting AI coding tools and you worry about engineering discipline slipping.","themes":["Testing & quality","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":5537,"title":"The Quarkus advantage: Turning build-time metaprogramming into runtime performances","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["performance","reflection","quarkus","metaprogramming"],"abstract":["Java frameworks have long relied on reflection, classpath scanning and runtime dynamism to deliver flexibility, but at a significant cost in terms of startup time, memory footprint, and raw throughput. The main goal of this talk is demonstrating with a recently introduced practical optimization how the radically different architecture of Quarkus unlocks performance gains that traditional frameworks simply cannot achieve.","Using the case studies of JSON serialization, REST Client and Quarkus LangChain4j we will walk through how a Quarkus extension can leverage Quarkus's build-time indexing to identify and inspect the application's domain model at build time, and Gizmo to generate optimized bytecode that completely replaces Jackson's reflection-heavy serialization machinery. The result is not just cleaner execution, but measurable performance improvements—demonstrating double-digit throughput gains while reducing latency and runtime overhead.","Beyond the specific optimization, this talk reveals a deeper insight: many “dynamic” features in Java are only necessary because frameworks lack build-time knowledge. By embracing ahead-of-time metaprogramming, Quarkus redefines what is possible in the JVM ecosystem.","Attendees will leave with a clear mental model of Quarkus’ architecture, a practical understanding of build-time metaprogramming, and a fresh perspective on how rethinking the boundary between build and runtime can unlock entirely new performance and footprint frontiers."],"favourites":80,"speakers":[{"name":"Mario Fusco","company":"IBM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GinuLQCwL67BBgfGsiWRl3mu3Ya0TbDffhYFiXX=s96-c","bio":"Mario is a senior principal software engineer at IBM working as Drools project lead. Among his interests there are also high performance systems and generative AI, being an active contributor of widely adopted projects like Quarkus and LangChain4j. He is also a Java Champion, the JUG Milano coordinator, a frequent speaker and the co-author of &quot;Modern Java in Action&quot; published by Manning.","linkedin":"mario-fusco-3467213","bluesky":null,"twitter":"mariofusco"},{"name":"Georgios Andrianakis","company":"IBM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-95904645-1695-4adf-b24c-41926050b4b4.jpg","bio":"Georgios works for IBM as a Senior Principal Software Engineer and is currently one of the most active contributors to Quarkus, where he works in all sorts of areas, including but not limited to LangChain4j, RESTEasy Reactive, Spring compatibility, Kubernetes support, testing, Kotlin, and more.<br><br>He is also an enthusiastic promoter of Quarkus who never misses a chance to spread the Quarkus love!","linkedin":"georgios-andrianakis","bluesky":null,"twitter":"geoand86"},{"name":"Francesco Nigro","company":"IBM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AItbvmlsKs3_saDtZgJ0buTR5LvusOsvlGmdbC7cEeks=s96-c","bio":"I&#39;ve been working for many years in the computer field. In the last 15 years I&#39;ve cultivated a strong passion in Java development and the under the hood details of OpenJDK, C and (x86) Assembly.<br>Big fan of DDD (Domain Driven Design) world, I&#39;ve developed several Event-Sourcing high performance solutions in the medical and IoT field.<br>I&#39;m an active member of various online communities on performance (<a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!forum/mechanical-sympathy\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(27, 100, 143);\">https://groups.google.com/forum/#!forum/mechanical-sympathy</a>), Senior Principal (Software) Performance Engineer and Performance Lead for Red Hat/IBM on Quarkus, Red Hat Top Inventor (2019).<br>I&#39;ve collaborated to different projects related high-performance computing both as committer and contributors eg Quarkus, Vert-x, Netty committer, JCTools author, PMC of ActiveMQ Apache Artemis (Messaging Broker), HdrHistogram, JGroups-raft, ...","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":"forked_franz"}],"start":"2026-10-05T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T14:30:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"SgK0zMNayq0","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":16418,"title":"Solving Distributed Transactions with the Saga Pattern and Temporal","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["saga","distributedtransactions","consistency","temporal"],"abstract":["Detailed Description","Temporal makes it remarkably easy to implement long-running business workflows. But once those workflows span multiple services and require synchronous business decisions, straightforward Saga implementations quickly expose non-obvious problems.","We'll explore the challenges we encountered while building distributed transactions with Temporal in a real production system. Starting with a classic Saga implementation, we'll examine why it wasn't enough, what problems emerged around synchronous workflows, failures, cancellation and compensation, and how the architecture evolved to address them. We'll also discuss reconciliation, consistency guarantees, and the trade-offs between keeping workflows simple and building resilient distributed systems.","Key Takeaways","• Common pitfalls when implementing Saga with Temporal.","• Designing reliable synchronous workflows.","Target Audience","Backend engineers, software architects, and tech leads building distributed systems or using Temporal for business workflow orchestration."],"favourites":145,"speakers":[{"name":"Andrei Sulyz","company":"Skyro","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-10dc359e-517c-4cfb-b859-7d7c9ca845a9.jpg","bio":"Andrei Sulyz is a Technical Lead with 10 years of experience building distributed systems across startups and enterprise environments. He currently works on the processing platform of a fintech company, designing backend systems for financial transactions and core platform services. His focus is distributed systems, backend architecture and developer productivity. Andrei has led the adoption of Temporal from initial evaluation to production and is now building AI agents that automate software development workflows. He enjoys understanding how technologies work under the hood and sharing practical lessons from real production experience.","linkedin":"andrey-sulyz","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-06T14:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"c45C2wNSZFc","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Sulyz live-debugs a Temporal Saga for a synchronous payment hold, fixing compensation ordering, cancellation scopes and activity cancellation races, and ends with reconciliation as the final safety net.","summary":["Sulyz leads the processing team at Skyro, a fintech whose credit platform orchestrates services with Temporal's Java SDK. His case is a payment hold: money is frozen in credit and debit systems while a customer waits at a terminal, so it must be fast and cancellable. Starting from Temporal's original Saga sample, still copied in articles, he fixes problems one by one. Registering a compensation only after an activity succeeds misses holds whose response was lost, so compensations go first. Cancelling then fails with parallel compensation (an unexpected CompensationException) or silently skips compensation in sequential mode (CanceledFailure). The fix is running saga.compensate() in a detached CancellationScope.","With Toxiproxy adding eight seconds of latency, he shows the next race: under the default TRY_CANCEL type, compensation runs while the hold request is still in flight. WAIT_CANCELLATION_COMPLETED waits for the activity, but plain HTTP activities that never heartbeat ignore cancellation, so the workflow ends without compensating; checking the scope's cancel request and throwing CanceledFailure closes that gap. Since cancellation can still race completion, Skyro runs a reconciliation service a few times a day. Callers start the workflow with a stable per-operation ID so client retries attach to the running execution, wait with getResult and a timeout, and cancel on timeout."],"keyPoints":["Register the compensation before calling the activity: a request can succeed remotely while its response is lost, leaving a hold that would never be released.","Run Saga compensation inside Workflow.newDetachedCancellationScope, otherwise activities cannot run once the workflow is cancelled; parallel compensation surfaces this as an error, sequential mode silently skips it.","The default TRY_CANCEL activity cancellation type triggers compensation immediately, racing in-flight requests; WAIT_CANCELLATION_COMPLETED waits, but only works if activities heartbeat or the workflow checks for a cancel request and throws CanceledFailure.","Even with these fixes, cancellation can race completion, so a reconciliation service remains necessary in financial systems.","For synchronous callers, use a deterministic workflow ID so retries reuse the running workflow, block on the result with a timeout, and cancel on timeout."],"takeaways":["Review Saga code copied from older Temporal examples for compensation ordering and a detached cancellation scope.","Pick an explicit ActivityCancellationType per activity and make long HTTP activities heartbeat or check for cancellation.","Test cancellation paths with injected latency, for example with Toxiproxy, rather than only the happy path.","Keep a reconciliation job for distributed money movements even when the workflow engine handles retries."],"quotes":[{"text":"A reconciliation service, not only in our system, but in all fintech systems, is the last guardian for our consistency.","at":"22:36"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"The synchronous hold operation"},{"at":"03:15","title":"Temporal basics and Saga sample"},{"at":"06:02","title":"Compensation ordering"},{"at":"08:54","title":"Detached cancellation scopes"},{"at":"13:43","title":"Activity cancellation races"},{"at":"22:36","title":"Reconciliation and calling workflows"},{"at":"25:25","title":"Takeaways"}],"tech":["Temporal","Temporal Java SDK","Saga pattern","Toxiproxy","gRPC","Camunda"],"watchIf":"You implement Sagas on Temporal, especially for synchronous, cancellable operations where a missed compensation leaves customer money frozen.","themes":["Architecture & design"],"demo":true}},{"id":22911,"title":"One tool to rule them all: bash, a tiny LLM, and the birth of a coding agent","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["prompt engineering","bash","coding agents","language model"],"abstract":["Coding agents look like magic. Strip away the hype and you find a surprisingly small core: a loop, a language model, and… a shell.","In this Tools in Action, we will see this core live. We give a small, local LLM (under 10B, running on Docker Model Runner) a single built-in tool (bash) and watch it turn into a coding helper. It lists files, greps the repo, runs the tests, curls an API, pipes into jq… all on its own, because the model already knows these CLIs. And when it doesn't? It runs --help and figures it.","We'll see the smallest agent loop in action: the model decides which command to run, the agents executes it, feeds the result back, and repeat until it answers. We'll look at what actually goes over the wire (the tool description, the tool_calls the model emits), why writing a good description is really prompt engineering, and why returning errors is what lets the model recover on its own.","You'll leave understanding how coding agents really work under the hood, why one well-chosen built-in tool often beats a pile of MCP servers, and how far a tiny local model can go.","Live demos, few slides, code you can run tonight."],"favourites":160,"speakers":[{"name":"Philippe Charrière","company":"Docker","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-c958a486-4d64-44ef-bb6d-77de06638b5d.jpg","bio":"Principal Solutions Architect at Docker<br>- Principal Solution Architect chez Docker<br>- WebAssembly lover<br>- GenAI on Raspberry Pi addict<br>- Container-oriented","linkedin":"phcharriere","bluesky":"k33gorg.bsky.social","twitter":"k33g_org"}],"start":"2026-10-05T16:20:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T16:50:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"wWfj4AJahlQ","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Charrière builds a coding agent live in Go with Genkit and a small local model on Docker Model Runner, showing that one bash tool can replace a catalogue of dedicated tools.","summary":["Charrière, a solution architect at Docker, runs everything on a 32 GB MacBook Air with a JetBrains Mellum model that he said has 12B parameters, about 2.5B active per token, served by Docker Model Runner (a wrapper around llama.cpp or vLLM, with a request inspector in Docker Desktop that helps with debugging). He explains function calling: the model only emits a tool name and arguments, and the agent executes the tool. The first demo uses Genkit for Go with four calculator and price-lookup tools for a computer store. The second wraps curl, grep and similar commands as separate tools to fetch example.com and create a Go module. Every extra tool description eats context, which hurts small models, so the third version keeps a single bash tool with a longer description. The model already knows Unix CLIs, and when it doesn't it can run --help.","The final step puts the loop behind a chat prompt, configured from a YAML file, as an agent called Bob, run inside Docker's sbx sandbox (a micro VM with its own Docker engine and a proxy that hides API keys). Bob writes, builds and runs a small Go program. After being told to run gopls help, it uses gopls workspace symbol, references and rename -w to find and rename a method. Next steps he lists: ripgrep or ast-grep, an edit tool, a skills tool, detection of hallucinations and silent endless loops, RAG, context compression by summarising history, and MCP. His side project grew out of this, with guardrails for local models. In Q&A he recommended small Qwen models and Menlo's Lucy and Jan-nano for tool calling."],"keyPoints":["The model never executes a tool; it returns a tool name and parameters, the agent runs it and appends the result to the message history.","Each extra tool description fills the context window, which hurts small local models far more than hosted ones.","A single bash tool lets the model use curl, grep, find, go mod and go version from its training knowledge, and --help covers CLIs it doesn't know.","With gopls available through bash, the agent finds symbols, references and renames code more precisely than with grep.","Small agents need guards against hallucinated tool calls and silent endless loops, or the program never terminates."],"takeaways":["Try replacing a pile of narrow tools or MCP servers with one well-described bash tool when context is tight.","Run any agent that has shell access inside a sandbox such as a micro VM, not directly on your laptop.","Add loop detection and context compression before relying on a small local model for agent work.","Point the agent at language-server CLIs like gopls for code navigation and refactoring."],"quotes":[{"text":"Very important, the model cannot execute any tool. The model can only generate text or data.","at":"04:31"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Local models and Model Runner"},{"at":"03:28","title":"How function calling works"},{"at":"07:15","title":"Genkit tool demo"},{"at":"08:44","title":"Real-world command tools"},{"at":"11:38","title":"One bash tool"},{"at":"14:11","title":"Agent loop in a sandbox"},{"at":"20:49","title":"Using gopls from bash"},{"at":"23:15","title":"Going further and Q&A"}],"tech":["Docker Model Runner","Docker Desktop","llama.cpp","vLLM","Genkit","Go","Mellum","bash","curl","gopls","ripgrep","ast-grep","Docker Sandboxes","MCP","Qwen"],"watchIf":"You use coding agents and want to see how small the core loop is, or want to run one on a local model.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":17765,"title":"Can AI Build a Jakarta EE Runtime? The Vidocq Experiment","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["cdi","jpms","microprofile","jakartaee"],"abstract":["This conference started in 2001 as Javapolis, the same year J2EE's first specs were taking shape. Twenty-five years later, on this stage, we're closing that loop: implementing those specs with a 2026 AI coding agent as co-author, live.","Part 1 — Building Vidocq, then building something new, live. We'll walk through how Vidocq, our Jakarta EE Core Profile 11 and MicroProfile 7.1 runtime, came together: the choice to code against formal specifications rather than our own assumptions, and what changed once AI joined that process. Where AI-generated code passed our review but failed spec conformance. Where a spec's edge cases caught something the AI happily hallucinated past. The workflow we landed on to keep AI output honest against TCK-level requirements. Then we'll do it live: implementing a Jakarta EE or MicroProfile spec we haven't built yet, on stage, with an AI agent constrained by the spec text. Real live coding, backup video included in case it goes sideways.","Part 2 — Vidocq, from spec to CLI. A fast tour of what defines the runtime: JPMS enforced at unit granularity, Build Compatible Extensions, and the JDK 25 features (virtual threads, structured concurrency, scoped values) behind Vidocq's zero external dependencies. Then straight to the command line: we'll bootstrap and build a small app on Vidocq live, showing how those choices feel to work with, not just how they look in a diagram.","Two decades of spec work, one AI model, one CLI. Watch the gap close."],"favourites":24,"speakers":[{"name":"Antoine Sabot-Durand","company":"SCIAM","image":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/489817?v=4","bio":"Antoine is a Java Champion and former spec lead for Jakarta EE CDI. He was part of the founding team behind Quarkus at Red Hat and worked on major MicroProfile specifications such as Fault Tolerance and Health Check. Today, Antoine is an architect and technical expert at SCIAM, where he leads complex projects largely built on the very technologies he helped design. He is also the co-creator of the Vidocq framework, a modern MicroProfile stack built on Java 25 and JPMS.","linkedin":"antoinesabotdurand","bluesky":"antoine.sabot-durand.net","twitter":null},{"name":"yann Blazart","company":"SCIAM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AAcHTte0rDSnHTsgef7nTSqwxu70yJKQehjTgrVJ3oSkC7n6hlw=s96-c","bio":"Yann Blazart is a Senior Tech Lead and a passionate advocate of Jakarta EE and Quarkus. He has been in love with Java for 26 years and has been working with SCIAM since 2019. A long-time open-source contributor, Yann has taken part in projects such as WicketExtJs, TomEE, and JAX-WS Metro.<br>He is no stranger to the Devoxx stage: he ran two Hands-on Labs at Devoxx France (2012 and 2015) on Java EE, and more recently delivered a Hands-on Lab on Quarkus and Apache Ignite at Riviera Dev, Devoxx BE, and Devoxx MA in 2023. In 2024, he laid the foundations of what has since become langchain4j-cdi. In 2026, together with Antoine Sabot-Durand, he built Vidocq (<a href=\"https://vidocq.dev\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">https://vidocq.dev</a>) — the first fully European MicroProfile 7.1 runtime.","linkedin":"yblazart","bluesky":"yblazart.bsky.social","twitter":"yblazart"},{"name":"Sebastien Blanc","company":"SCIAM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GghdlsQf-LeH_np8EgyP0zMkqdtdA3aeHvPYsYOag=s96-c","bio":"Sébastien Blanc is a Passion-Driven-Developer with one primary goal : Make the Developers Happy. He likes to share his passion by giving talks that are pragmatic, fun and focused on live coding.","linkedin":"sébastien-blanc-08a73b1","bluesky":"sebi2706.bsky.social","twitter":"sebi2706"}],"start":"2026-10-05T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T14:30:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"dq5rueVhiIM","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":50107,"title":"Building an Agent Ready Platform","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["observability","platform","guardrails","agents"],"abstract":["Writing an agent has never been easier. In Spring AI it takes a few lines of code to wire up a model, add a few","tools, point it at your data and watch it do something that felt impossible two years ago. The demo lands well.","Then you try to put it in front of real customers, and the questions start.","Which model are you allowed to use in production? Where do the credentials live now? What stops the agent","from calling a tool it should never touch? What does one conversation cost?","None of this is about the model. It is about the platform underneath it, and it looks a lot like the problem","developers hit when they first tried to ship containers.","This session shows what platform engineering has to offer next. Model access as a paved road, identity for","agents, guardrails you inherit instead of build, and observability that shows every step and what it cost. You will","leave knowing what to ask your platform team for, and if you are the platform team, what to build first."],"favourites":120,"speakers":[{"name":"Timo Salm","company":"Broadcom","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-93dfac6b-7673-4fca-81c6-aa9fb4a0c084.jpg","bio":"<p fgid=\"5163\">Timo Salm is a Principal Solutions Engineer at VMware Tanzu by Broadcom with over a decade of experience in customer-facing roles, modern applications, DevSecOps, and AI. In his roles, he ensures that the most strategic customers in the EMEA region achieve their goals with VMware Tanzu&#39;s Spring, developer platform, AI, and data products. Another essential part of his role is continuously learning through experimenting hands-on with innovations and sharing the outcomes with colleagues, customers, and the community via, for example, conferences. Before Timo joined Pivotal, which VMware and now Broadcom acquired, he worked for consulting firms in the automotive industry as a software architect and full-stack developer.","linkedin":"timosalm","bluesky":null,"twitter":"salmto"}],"start":"2026-10-07T14:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T15:30:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"q58Y2tyRdCE","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Salm argues that shipping agents is a platform problem: an AI gateway, an MCP gateway with approved tools, agent identity, shared guardrails and OpenTelemetry-based cost observability belong in the platform.","summary":["Timo Salm, a Broadcom solution architect and Agentic AI Foundation ambassador, starts from the point that a Spring AI agent takes a few lines of code but production raises other questions. Which model is approved? Where do credentials live? What stops an agent calling a harmful tool? Why did it act as it did? He compares today with the early container years, when every team had to work out Kubernetes and security on its own. Most organisations already have a developer platform; it now has to grow to meet agents' needs. Hardcoding provider SDKs and keys in every agent makes switching providers a multi-week change.","On security, Salm said the Spring portfolio of over 50 projects used to see about one vulnerability a month. Since March, scanning with frontier models found over 450 candidates and led to more than 200 published vulnerabilities across four releases, and researchers now report about 40 a month. Smaller models can turn a published CVE and patch into an exploit within hours, and low-severity CVEs can be chained, so platforms need automated patching and tests, not four weeks of manual testing. He cited 1Password research finding only 70 of 100 AI-generated security fixes fully correct. Platform-as-a-product still applies: start from real use cases, not GPUs nobody asked for.","The core is a list of platform capabilities. An OpenAI-compatible AI gateway with virtual keys lets the platform switch models, route between public and private models and enforce budgets with no code change. An MCP gateway with a registry exposes only approved tools, handles single sign-on and token exchange, and audits usage; Broadcom forbids local stdio MCP servers. Agent identity replaces all-powerful service accounts with per-user, per-task tokens. Guardrails sit at three layers: application (structured-output validation, human-in-the-loop), gateway (data masking, prompt-injection filters) and runtime (sandboxed containers, deny-by-default network). Observability uses Micrometer and OpenTelemetry's GenAI conventions to show the cost per interaction."],"keyPoints":["Put model access behind one OpenAI-compatible AI gateway with virtual keys, so swapping Gemini for OpenAI or a private model is platform configuration, not a code change or key rotation across hundreds of apps.","Salm said frontier-model scanning found over 450 possible vulnerabilities in Spring since March, leading to more than 200 published fixes in four releases, against roughly one a month before.","Exploits can follow a CVE publication within hours and low-severity CVEs can be chained, so patching and testing must be automated rather than waiting weeks for manual tests.","An MCP gateway and registry (for example Agent Gateway, contributed by Solo.io to the Agentic AI Foundation) provides approved tools, SSO token exchange, tracing and audit; avoid local stdio MCP servers.","Service accounts don't fit agents: an agent should carry an identity derived from the user's login with tokens scoped to only the tools a task needs, which limits prompt-injection damage.","Guardrails belong at three layers: application (validate structured output, human approval for actions), gateway (data masking, prompt-injection checks, allowed tools) and runtime (isolated containers, deny-by-default networking).","Spring AI's SafeGuardAdvisor is a simple word allow/deny list; the hard part is maintaining such lists, which is why a central team should own them.","Spring AI emits Micrometer observations for token usage and calls to vector stores; with OpenTelemetry (including GenAI semantic conventions) the platform can show the cost of each interaction."],"takeaways":["Ask your platform team for a single model endpoint with virtual keys instead of embedding provider API keys and model names in your agent.","Connect coding and runtime agents to tools through an approved MCP gateway rather than arbitrary downloaded MCP servers.","Automate dependency patching and tests so a security fix can reach production in hours, and read patch release notes for breaking changes.","Turn on Spring AI observations and OpenTelemetry export so each agent interaction reports tokens and cost.","If you are the platform team, collect one or two real agent use cases before building GPU clusters or gateways."],"quotes":[{"text":"The question is not whether you can do it. The question is whether you should do that.","at":"20:30"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"From agent demo to production"},{"at":"06:53","title":"Lessons from the container era"},{"at":"10:41","title":"AI-found vulnerabilities and patching"},{"at":"17:38","title":"Platform as a product"},{"at":"21:31","title":"Model access via AI gateway"},{"at":"27:58","title":"MCP gateway and approved tools"},{"at":"31:47","title":"Agent identity"},{"at":"34:47","title":"Guardrails in three layers"},{"at":"38:40","title":"Observability and cost"},{"at":"44:15","title":"What stays in your code"}],"tech":["Spring AI","Spring Boot","Model Context Protocol","Agent2Agent","Agent Gateway","Agentic AI Foundation","OpenTelemetry","Micrometer","Dependabot","Renovate","Spring Application Advisor","Tanzu Platform","pgvector","Kubernetes"],"watchIf":"You are on a platform team, or a developer about to put a Spring AI agent in production, and want a checklist of what the platform should provide for agents.","themes":["Cloud native & Kubernetes","Security & supply chain","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":5526,"title":"From Developer to Story Builder: Engineering a Novel, an ARG, and a Transmedia World with AI","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["architecture","ai","devops","transmedia"],"abstract":["What happens when a software architect treats a novel like a production system?","In this talk, I will share how I built The Human Protocol, a cyber-realist techno-thriller about AI, using the same engineering principles I apply to software architecture: context engineering, spec-driven development, harness design, versioning, review loops, and automated publishing pipelines.","The session is not about “asking ChatGPT to write a book.” It is a technical case study in designing a controlled human-AI creative workflow. I will show how I created a lore-as-code repository, a governance package to prevent context drift, specialized AI roles for design, drafting and review, and a DevOps-style pipeline to produce manuscript formats for publication.","I will also cover the project’s transmedia extension: an ARG website where clues hidden in the book unlock in-universe content. This was built through spec-driven development anchored in the same lore repository, turning the manuscript into both a story and an interactive system.","You will leave with practical patterns for managing long-running AI-assisted projects: how to structure context, debug hallucinations and inconsistencies, separate agent roles, preserve human intent, and ship complex creative artifacts with AI without losing authorship."],"favourites":62,"speakers":[{"name":"Raphael Semeteys","company":"Worldline","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-b486eee1-a8c4-41b6-9825-c2d3c8761c57.jpg","bio":"I am a technologist and Content Producer with <strong>over 25 years of experience</strong> across software engineering, open-source communities, and emerging tech. My current focus is on the frontiers of Generative AI, specifically local LLMs, image generation (Stable Diffusion), and Agentic AI.<br><br>Throughout my career, I’ve balanced technical leadership with a passion for knowledge sharing. I created the QSOS method for <strong>open source</strong> evaluation and recently explored the intersection of &quot;vibe-coding&quot; and storytelling by publishing <strong>AI-assisted techno-thrillers</strong>.<br><br>A frequent speaker at international conferences like Devoxx, Open Source Experience, and various DevFests, I enjoy <strong>deconstructing complex ecosystems</strong>—from Web3 architecture to multimodal AI—into clear, actionable insights. I also bring a unique perspective to tech by integrating holistic <strong>yoga </strong>practices into my approach to innovation and engineering.<br><br>More about me: https://raphiki.github.io/","linkedin":"raphaelsemeteys","bluesky":"raphiki.bsky.social","twitter":"RaphaelSemeteys"}],"start":"2026-10-06T10:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T11:20:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"496nv8jsWhQ","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":10045,"title":"Java, LEGO & AI: Teaching the Next Generation to Build, Not Just Prompt","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["java","lego spike","programming education","generative ai"],"abstract":["Generative AI can write code in seconds. So does it still make sense to teach programming to children?","We at Devoxx4Kids think it does.","In our workshops, we use LEGO SPIKE robots to teach problem-solving, software design and computational thinking. There is just one challenge: LEGO SPIKE does not officially support Java.","Rather than accepting that limitation, we explored alternative approaches to bring Java to the platform and make LEGO robots accessible to our next generation Java developers.","In this session, we'll share our journey, demonstrate how Java can be used to control LEGO SPIKE robots, discuss the technical challenges we encountered, and show real-world examples from educational settings.","Attendees will learn:","Why programming fundamentals remain relevant in the AI era","How LEGO SPIKE can be integrated into a Java ecosystem","Technical approaches for controlling SPIKE robots with Java","Lessons learned while teaching children with physical computing platforms","Expect live demos, moving robots, and perhaps a few LEGO bricks on stage."],"favourites":131,"speakers":[{"name":"Eddy Vos","company":"Devoxx4Kids Netherlands","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-9b520e91-967e-42a4-9061-ff04e1ff6682.jpg","bio":"Eddy is the chairman of Devoxx4Kids Netherlands and an Engineering Manager at Ahold Delhaize Netherlands. He has a focus on AI, Data, FinOps and Java. <br>He loves to share his knowledge and help in his free time, with the Devoxx4Kids Foundation, kids to learn how to code in a fun way.","linkedin":"eddyvos","bluesky":"eddyvos.bsky.social","twitter":"eddyvos_EVCS"}],"start":"2026-10-05T10:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T11:20:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"_VZg6dxlGaM","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Vos argues kids should still learn to code in the AI era, and recounts how Devoxx4Kids Netherlands got Java to drive LEGO SPIKE robots through a Raspberry Pi bridge.","summary":["Vos, chairman of the Devoxx4Kids Foundation in the Netherlands and an engineering manager at Ahold Delhaize, opens with the question parents, teachers and colleagues keep asking: why teach kids to code when AI writes it? His answer is the calculator analogy: we still teach that 7 times 8 is 56 so we can spot a machine saying 5600. AI can generate robot code, but judging it, spotting a loop that turns four times instead of three, or a motor that stalls on a slippery floor, takes understanding. Physical robots make bugs visible immediately, which keeps kids engaged.","LEGO SPIKE, the successor to Mindstorms, supports only blocks and Python, and its hub has about 320 KB of RAM, too little for a JVM. Using LEGO's protocol documentation on GitHub, the team tried custom JVM firmware (rejected, as it could break schools' robots), Java generating MicroPython (the robot did not move) and Java over Bluetooth from Windows laptops (no usable library). What worked was a simple Java API talking to a Python bridge on a Raspberry Pi. The demo eventually moved the robot; sensors and non-blocking commands are still in progress. Vos credits Java 25's simple main methods for making Java approachable for ten-year-olds, and asks developers to volunteer."],"keyPoints":["The SPIKE hub has around 320 KB of RAM, so a standard JVM cannot run on it, and flashing custom firmware would make school-owned robots unusable with LEGO's software.","Generating MicroPython from Java and uploading it to one of the hub's 20 program slots uploaded fine but did not make the robot move.","The working architecture is a Windows laptop running Java, a Raspberry Pi running a Python bridge, and the hub over Bluetooth; the goal is to drop the Pi now that a Java Bluetooth library exists.","SPIKE drive commands need both speed and duration, which the kid-facing Java API hides behind calls like drive-forward and turn-right.","Vos used AI to read LEGO's GitHub documentation and generate boilerplate, but found its byte-level output hard to trust and says API design for a ten-year-old still needs a human."],"takeaways":["Volunteer for a Devoxx4Kids workshop, or offer your company as a host for a Devoxx4Kids day.","When teaching beginners Java, use Java 25's simplified main method to remove the public static void boilerplate.","If you work with LEGO SPIKE, start from LEGO's published protocol documentation on GitHub and check the Devoxx4Kids repository before writing your own bridge."],"quotes":[{"text":"AI changes how we write code, but not why we learn to code.","at":"01:48"},{"text":"Prompting is asking, but building is understanding.","at":"03:12"},{"text":"The developers from tomorrow are now 10 years old.","at":"19:11"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why kids still learn coding"},{"at":"05:32","title":"Physical robots and LEGO SPIKE"},{"at":"07:52","title":"Three routes to Java"},{"at":"11:32","title":"The Raspberry Pi bridge"},{"at":"12:49","title":"Live robot demo"},{"at":"18:03","title":"Why real Java for kids"},{"at":"21:47","title":"How to get involved"}],"tech":["LEGO SPIKE","LEGO Mindstorms","Raspberry Pi","Bluetooth","MicroPython","Scratch","Java 25"],"watchIf":"You want to teach kids programming with robots or volunteer at Devoxx4Kids and want to see how Java can drive LEGO SPIKE hardware.","themes":["Hardware, robots & fun","Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":true}},{"id":24224,"title":"Post-Quantum Today: How Your Passwords Will Be Safe in a Quantum Future","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["quantum","lattice","rsa","pqc"],"abstract":["This talk explores:","* how classical RSA encryption works","* how quantum algorithms (i.e. Shor’s algorithm) break RSA","* brief overview of where we are regarding quantum hardware","* why PQC still matters right now, to prevent “harvest now, decrypt later” attacks","* what lattice-based PQC looks like and how far along we already are in adopting it","The target audience is everyone generally interested in future technologies. Especially everyone who might have heard about quantum computing and Shor's algorithm breaking encryption, but don't know what that means for the future of cryptography and where we're at today.","The key take aways are a (very basic) general understanding how quantum computing works, to understand how it has the potential to break RSA, as well as quantum-safe encryption methods that are already being put in place, how they work and what the future might look like."],"favourites":262,"speakers":[{"name":"Julian Burr","company":"Sonar AI","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocLOSngLPQFgOpPGXfZqQbR2eBQyiBQsKODaA7Kz896TbM9pjw=s96-c","bio":"Senior Developer from Germany with over 15 years of experience building web apps and leading frontend teams, currently living and working down under in Australia. Pragmatic perfectionist, always looking for opportunities to learn and grow.","linkedin":"julianburr","bluesky":"julianburr.de","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T10:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T10:50:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"yUMnuh5U5k4","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Burr explains how qubits and Shor's algorithm threaten RSA, why 'harvest now, decrypt later' makes it urgent today, and how lattice-based schemes like Kyber (ML-KEM) replace it.","summary":["Burr frames quantum computers as enhancements that sit on top of classical machines, not replacements, and uses the double-slit experiment to explain superposition: qubits hold many states at once but collapse to one random value on measurement, so superposition alone gives no speed-up. The power comes from quantum algorithms that arrange states so wrong answers destructively interfere and right answers constructively interfere, making the correct result the likely measurement. He then walks through Shor's algorithm against RSA, whose security rests on factoring being slow (adding 10 digits multiplies the time roughly tenfold, making RSA-2048 take trillions of years classically).","Shor's algorithm turns a bad guess into useful factors using modular arithmetic: the hard sub-problem is finding the period p of a repeating remainder sequence, and that single step is where a quantum computer, via the quantum Fourier transform on a partially measured superposition, is fast. A good guess breaks the key 37.5% of the time, so about 10 tries give 99% success. The same threat hits discrete-log and elliptic-curve crypto. Qubit estimates to run it have fallen fast, from 20 million noisy qubits (2019) to about a million (2025) to roughly 100,000 this year, against today's largest QPU of around 1,000 physical qubits; error correction is the main bottleneck.","Because of 'harvest now, decrypt later' attacks on data that stays sensitive (state and medical secrets), NIST ran a competition with Google, IBM and Amazon to standardise quantum-resistant algorithms, most of them lattice-based. Burr builds up the lattice idea from the closest-vector problem (good vs bad basis as private vs public key), notes the 1990s GGH scheme leaked the private key and was broken, then explains Learning With Errors: public-key equations made unsolvable by linear algebra through modulo arithmetic plus small random errors, with bits encoded by optionally adding half the modulus. He closes with migration: Google and the other providers aim for quantum-safe by 2029-2030, and Kyber, standardised as ML-KEM, is in core libraries including Java since Java 24."],"keyPoints":["Superposition alone does not parallelise computation; quantum algorithms work by making wrong answers interfere destructively so measurement likely yields the right answer.","Shor's algorithm reduces factoring to period-finding; only that step is quantum, done with a quantum Fourier transform, and succeeds about 37.5% per attempt (99% within ~10 tries).","Shor-style algorithms also break discrete-logarithm and elliptic-curve cryptography, not just RSA.","Estimated qubits to break RSA dropped from ~20M (2019) to ~1M (2025) to ~100k this year; today's largest QPU has about 1,000 physical qubits, with error correction the key limiter.","The urgent threat now is harvest-now-decrypt-later on data that stays sensitive over time.","Lattice-based Learning With Errors hides the private key by adding modulo arithmetic and small random errors so no efficient classical or quantum algorithm is known to solve it.","Kyber won the NIST competition and was standardised as ML-KEM; it is available in core libraries, including Java since Java 24."],"takeaways":["Treat long-lived sensitive data as already at risk from harvest-now-decrypt-later and prioritise migrating it.","Prefer major providers with a published post-quantum migration plan, or switch your own encryption to standardised algorithms like ML-KEM.","On the JVM, use the ML-KEM (Kyber) support available since Java 24 rather than rolling your own."],"quotes":[{"text":"It's much better to think of them as enhancements.","at":"02:52"},{"text":"What if we had a magical box, and that magical box could turn our bad guesses into good guesses? That's essentially what Shor's algorithm is.","at":"09:55"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"What this talk covers"},{"at":"01:51","title":"Qubits and superposition"},{"at":"04:14","title":"Double-slit and measurement"},{"at":"08:30","title":"Shor's algorithm and RSA"},{"at":"14:18","title":"Period finding with Fourier"},{"at":"18:14","title":"How many qubits and when"},{"at":"21:13","title":"Harvest now, decrypt later"},{"at":"22:41","title":"Lattice-based cryptography"},{"at":"26:29","title":"Learning with errors"},{"at":"32:33","title":"Migration and Kyber today"}],"tech":["RSA","Shor's algorithm","Quantum Fourier transform","NIST PQC","Kyber","ML-KEM","Dilithium","Learning With Errors","GGH","Java 24","Google","AWS","Azure"],"watchIf":"You want an accessible but technically honest explanation of how quantum computers threaten RSA and what post-quantum migration looks like today.","themes":["Security & supply chain","Hardware, robots & fun"],"demo":false}},{"id":22920,"title":"Using Structured Concurrency and Scoped Values to Create a Virtual Threads Based Asynchronous Application","type":"Hands-on Lab","duration":180,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["structuredconcurrency","scopedvalues","virtualthreads","loom"],"abstract":["The Loom project is about making asynchronous programming simpler. The promise is to enable the \"simple thread-per-request style to scale with near-optimal hardware utilization\". Before virtual threads, this was only achievable with callback-based reactive programming. Loom first brought virtual thread in JDK 21 that makes the blocking of threads almost free. Along with it, it published the Scoped Values API in JDK 25, as a replacement of the Thread Local API. The last element is the Structured Concurrency API, which is still a preview feature in JDK 26 and 27. This hands-on lab guides you through writing a virtual thread-based web application that utilizes structured concurrency to parallelize requests, and implements different strategies to process them. Then it shows you how you can use scoped values to pass sensitive information from one module of your application to another one, without relying on method parameters. It also uses the principles of Data Oriented Programming to organize your application in a simple way. You will use StructuredTaskScopes, the implementations of the Joiner interface that the API gives you, and you will create your own implementations as well. In the end, you will see the patterns of code that the Loom project gives you to achieve the same throughput as a classical reactive application, and you will then be able to compare both approaches to choose which one you prefer."],"favourites":75,"speakers":[{"name":"Ana-Maria Mihalceanu","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14Gj1OS6OpAzuwBKwoUuCi9gxvNPvdmVA7nbW8lg3mg=s96-c","bio":"Ana is a Java Champion Alumni and Senior Developer Advocate for the Java Platform Group at Oracle, with an extensive background in Java and cloud development. More recently, her work has expanded to explore how machine learning and AI can be applied within the Java ecosystem, helping Java developers adopt emerging capabilities while continuing to benefit from the stability, tooling, and performance of the Java Platform. She actively supports the growth of technical communities through knowledge sharing and enjoys curating content for conferences as a program committee member. ","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"ammbra1508.bsky.social","twitter":"ammbra1508"},{"name":"José Paumard","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GhsVIUuTR9Onoo-D8UMW5QId2Yw1V_EUvqdWdNQ0RQ=s96-c","bio":"José is a Java Developer Advocate at Oracle. He holds a PhD in applied mathematics and computer science and is a Java Champion alumnus. He has been an assistant professor at the University Sorbonne Paris Nord for 25 years. He is an active member of the Paris Java User Group, and a disorganizer of the JChateau unconference. He contributes Java learning content on the dev.java website. He publishes &quot;Java Coding Tips&quot;, a biweekly series of shorts, and the &quot;JEP Café&quot;, a video series, both on the Java YouTube channel.","linkedin":"jos%C3%A9-paumard-2458ba5","bluesky":"josepaumard.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-05T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T14:30:00Z","room":"BOF 1","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A lab building a virtual-thread web application with structured concurrency and scoped values, then comparing the result to a reactive approach.","summary":["Participants write a virtual-thread-based web application that uses structured concurrency to parallelize requests and implements different strategies to process them. They work with StructuredTaskScope and the Joiner implementations the API provides, and write their own.","The lab also covers using Scoped Values (final in JDK 25) to pass sensitive information between modules without method parameters, and organizes the application with Data Oriented Programming. Structured Concurrency is still a preview feature in JDK 26 and 27. The lab ends by comparing the Loom approach with a classical reactive one."],"keyPoints":["Virtual threads (JDK 21) make blocking almost free, enabling thread-per-request style at scale.","Structured Concurrency uses StructuredTaskScope and Joiner implementations, including custom ones.","Scoped Values replace ThreadLocal for passing data between modules.","Data Oriented Programming is used to structure the app."],"takeaways":["Try replacing a ThreadLocal in your code with a ScopedValue."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Virtual threads","Structured Concurrency","Scoped Values","Project Loom","JDK 25","JDK 26"],"watchIf":"You want hands-on experience with the Loom APIs as an alternative to reactive programming.","themes":["Java language & JDK","JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":false}},{"id":20651,"title":"From Install to Insight: A Hands-On GPU Lab for Java Developers","type":"Hands-on Lab","duration":120,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["java","tornadovm","llm","gpu"],"abstract":["Getting a local LLM running on your GPU from Java used to stall at installation: which SDK, which","backend, which JDK flags? Thirteen TornadoVM releases after our last Devoxx, it's a single command.","This hands-on lab shows what changed, on your own laptop.","We walk through the new features, each straight to the console:","• SDKs on SDKMAN! and Maven Central","• one SDK for JDK 21 to 27","• a native layer rebuilt on Java's FFM API","• Metal, CUDA and OpenCL backends","• the new CUDA Tile API","Short animations explain the programming model. You'll then profile and debug with TornadoVM's","tools: TornadoPulse, TornadoViz and the TornadoInsight IDE plugin.","Finally, you'll run jitLLM (formerly GPULlama3.java), LLM inference in pure Java, and connect it to","DevoxxGenie in IntelliJ IDEA as a local, GPU-backed assistant. We close with advanced demos on","NVIDIA: cuBLAS in the same task graph as your Java kernels, and a glimpse of GPU-accelerated","Apache Flink.","No GPU programming experience required. You'll leave with a working local GPU-accelerated LLM","setup and the tools to inspect it."],"favourites":31,"speakers":[{"name":"Thanos Stratikopoulos","company":"Beestream.ai | TornadoVM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-1eb3708e-af15-4778-a560-15ebbea2b133.jpg","bio":"Dr. Athanasios Stratikopoulos (male) is a Research Fellow at the University of Manchester with specialization on heterogeneous architectures and reconfigurable accelerators. He has authored more than 20 research articles in the field of hardware acceleration, system software and programming languages. Currently his work involves heterogeneous architectures ranging from low-power devices to high-end cloud deployments. He is one of the lead developers of TornadoVM and has been part of the team for the last eight years. In addition to his core contributions to the system&#39;s technical development, Dr. Stratikopoulos leads the project&#39;s communication and dissemination efforts, helping to articulate its goals and advancements to both academic and industrial audiences through talks, documentation, and outreach activities.","linkedin":"stratika","bluesky":"stratika.bsky.social","twitter":"thanos_str"},{"name":"Christos Kotselidis","company":"Beestream.ai, University of Manchester","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocJ-pxqHhRG9lMhvBsm7RliPrVLhsyhgaBJQMrzmQZgJ2KKM1RVe=s96-c","bio":"I am an Associate Professor at The University of Manchester and CEO at Beestream.ai. My work focuses on hardware acceleration of Java programs on GPUs and LLM inference; TornadoVM, jitLLM.","linkedin":"kotselidis","bluesky":"kotselidis.net","twitter":null},{"name":"Mary Xekalaki","company":"beestream.ai | TornadoVM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-a82d2879-53f1-4330-b9cb-64cc383a1b02.jpg","bio":"I&#39;m a Research Associate at the University of Manchester and lead developer of TornadoVM, an open-source framework that lets Java applications run on GPUs and other accelerators without leaving the JVM. My work sits at the intersection of managed runtimes and compilers - JIT-compiling Java bytecode into GPU code, and bringing modern hardware features like tensor cores, low-precision arithmetic and quantization into reach for Java developers.","linkedin":"mary-xekalaki","bluesky":"maryxek.bsky.social","twitter":"MXekalaki"},{"name":"Michalis Papadimitriou","company":"Neo4j | University of Manchester","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-85f7f25f-5f2c-4526-bed6-489be66ed5e1.jpg","bio":"Michalis Papadimitriou is a Senior Software Engineer at Neo4j and a Research Fellow at the University of Manchester. His core expertise includes open-source software development, hardware abstractions for high-level programming languages, compiler optimizations for GPU computing, and enabling large language model (LLM) inference on GPUs for the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).<br>He is focused on advancing high-performance AI and graph computing systems, with particular interest in GPU acceleration for machine learning workloads on the JVM through the TornadoVM framework, and continues to actively maintain the open-source GPULlama3.java project.<br>Before joining Neo4j, Michalis was a Research Fellow at the University of Manchester, where he contributed to the TornadoVM project as a Staff Software Engineer. Prior to that, he worked across a range of software stacks at Huawei Technologies and contributed to the open-source machine learning compiler Apache TVM while at OctoAI (formerly OctoML), which was later acquired by NVIDIA.","linkedin":"michalis-papadimitriou","bluesky":"mikepapadim.bsky.social","twitter":"mikepapadim"}],"start":"2026-10-05T14:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T16:50:00Z","room":"BOF 2","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A beginner lab on TornadoVM covering simplified installation, new backends, profiling tools and running a local GPU-backed LLM in pure Java connected to DevoxxGenie.","summary":["The TornadoVM team walks attendees through what has changed in thirteen releases since their last Devoxx: SDKs on SDKMAN! and Maven Central, one SDK for JDK 21 to 27, a native layer rebuilt on the FFM API, Metal, CUDA and OpenCL backends, and the new CUDA Tile API. Short animations explain the programming model.","Participants then profile and debug with TornadoPulse, TornadoViz and the TornadoInsight IDE plugin, and run jitLLM (formerly GPULlama3.java) for LLM inference in pure Java, connecting it to DevoxxGenie in IntelliJ IDEA. Advanced demos on NVIDIA include cuBLAS in the same task graph as Java kernels and a glimpse of GPU-accelerated Apache Flink. No GPU programming experience is needed."],"keyPoints":["TornadoVM SDKs are available through SDKMAN! and Maven Central and support JDK 21 to 27.","The native layer is rebuilt on Java's FFM API, with Metal, CUDA and OpenCL backends.","Tooling includes TornadoPulse, TornadoViz and the TornadoInsight IDE plugin.","jitLLM runs LLM inference in pure Java and plugs into DevoxxGenie as a local assistant."],"takeaways":["Install the TornadoVM SDK via SDKMAN! and try a local GPU-backed LLM."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["TornadoVM","jitLLM","GPULlama3.java","DevoxxGenie","FFM API","CUDA","Metal","OpenCL","cuBLAS","Apache Flink","SDKMAN!"],"watchIf":"You want to run LLMs or compute kernels on a GPU from Java without GPU programming experience.","themes":["LLMs in Java apps","JVM performance & runtime","Hardware, robots & fun"],"demo":false}},{"id":4086,"title":"AI Made Building Cheap. Now Decisions Are the Bottleneck.","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"People, Practices & Career","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["discovery","architecture","ai","decision-making"],"abstract":["AI is transforming software delivery. Code can be generated, tests created, architectures sketched, and implementation options explored in minutes. Building has never been cheaper.","Yet many organisations are discovering an unexpected consequence: as execution accelerates, decision-making becomes the real bottleneck.","Drawing on consulting engagements across banking, insurance, retail, and telecommunications, this talk explores how AI is changing the economics of software delivery and why decision quality is emerging as a competitive advantage. Rather than focusing on generating code faster, I demonstrate how AI can improve discovery, architecture, and delivery conversations before implementation begins.","Through practical examples and live demonstrations, attendees will see how conversations can be transformed into Event Storming maps, Team Topologies views, C4 architecture diagrams, assumptions, risks, and architectural options. These techniques help teams expose hidden complexity, align stakeholders faster, and make better-informed decisions.","Key takeaways: Understand why AI shifts the constraint from execution to judgement, learn practical techniques for AI-assisted discovery and architecture, expose risks and assumptions earlier, and balance delivery speed with architectural integrity.","Target audience: Software architects, technical leads, senior engineers, engineering managers, enterprise architects, and technology leaders."],"favourites":326,"speakers":[{"name":"Dave Sammut","company":"Equal Experts","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocKrgiObZlQZmkuRHWqlDAwuHgNeuknmxFB4KLe9_yQTsjK7YDc-=s96-c","bio":"Dave Sammut is a Principal Consultant and Head of Architecture (EMEA) at Equal Experts, where he helps organisations tackle complex technology, architecture, and transformation challenges. With more than 20 years of experience in software delivery, he has worked across banking, insurance, retail, telecommunications, healthcare, and public sector organisations, helping teams modernise systems, improve delivery effectiveness, and navigate large-scale change.<br>Dave specialises in bridging the gap between business strategy and technical execution through Domain-Driven Design, software architecture, Team Topologies, collaborative discovery, and modern engineering practices. His work focuses on helping organisations uncover hidden assumptions, reduce risk, and make better decisions before investing in implementation.<br>In recent years, Dave has been exploring how AI is reshaping software delivery. His work examines the growing shift from execution as the primary constraint to decision quality, judgement, and governance becoming the critical differentiators for successful teams. He regularly advises organisations on the practical application of AI across architecture, software delivery, and organisational design.<br>Alongside his consulting work, Dave is an experienced conference speaker, workshop facilitator, and former part-time radio presenter. He is known for combining real-world consulting experiences with engaging storytelling, practical examples, and interactive demonstrations that challenge conventional thinking about technology and software delivery.<br>Whether working with engineering teams, architects, or senior leaders, Dave&#39;s passion lies in helping people navigate complexity, align around shared understanding, and deliver meaningful outcomes with confidence.","linkedin":"dave-sammut-2559453","bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"João Rosa","company":"João Rosa Consulting","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-791e4354-5d69-4aff-8a7c-94c975843c1a.jpg","bio":"João Rosa helps organizations unlock more value with their teams and technology. He works with executives and senior leaders to transform and evolve the operating model so that teams are prepared to thrive in a turbulent environment. He has led transformations across financial services, telco, and retail.<br><br>He is a software engineer at heart, crossing technology problems with social enterprise challenges.","linkedin":"joaoasrosa","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T14:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T15:30:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"2pSyF44THEc","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Rosa and Sammut argue that AI speeds up building but exposes how poorly organizations understand themselves, and show, through an insurance case, how AI-assisted discovery lenses and small reversible hypotheses lead to better decisions.","summary":["João Rosa and Dave Sammut present a method from a white paper they say was peer-reviewed by banks and telcos. You look at the organization through several lenses, make observations, turn them into hypotheses, and then validate and scale them or learn from being wrong. Sammut compares it to the Mikado Method for refactoring: look at the consequences before jumping in, because the documented org chart differs from the ground truth of invisible work and interruptions. He also stresses reversibility: sticking with a bad decision, as on one client's four-year migration, is not discipline. Their running example is a 200-year-old insurer built from mergers and acquisitions, which has \"modernized\" but is still slow.","Rosa covers sales and distribution. Using interview recordings, workshop photos, Event Storming output and Git history as a \"brain\" for AI analysis, a slow platform team found it should rely on central compliance (including DORA work) instead of building its own. To tackle invisible tech debt, the team built a pipeline: SonarQube and CodeScene scores feed backlog items, LLMs prioritize and refactor using open source agents and skills, and humans re-review. When 249 teams wanted the same pipeline, it moved into the developer experience platform. Token costs then exploded, and an AI platform team emerged that applied FinOps attribution and helped teams pick the right models.","Sammut takes claims management, where his advice is \"start where it hurts\". Short guided conversations, synthesized by an LLM and pushed into Miro, give Event Storming-style maps of pain points such as a three-day claim-approval lead time, then an imagine/model/make/move/maintain view of the delivery lifecycle. A lens on architects' work, first used at a Dutch bank, split it into firefighting, operational and strategic. Automating operational toil backfired in part, because architects said some of it was how they learned. Domain-understanding and Team Topologies views showed subject matter experts cut off from delivery. That led to an AI-archaeology enabling team, story-style outputs for the business, and migration choices across the four Rs instead of a feature-parity migration.","They finish with three asks: spread knowledge through the communities of practice and informal channels you already have, keep organizational learning alive (citing organizations caught out by Broadcom's VMware licensing change), and stay committed to investing without falling for sunk costs. Culture matters too: they reference Westrum's pathological, bureaucratic and generative types and say AI programs struggle where mistakes lead to witch hunts. The abstract promised live demonstrations, but the session was slides and field stories only."],"keyPoints":["The method: look through several lenses, make observations, form hypotheses, then validate and scale or pivot. It favors many small reversible decisions over large reorganizations.","Feeding interview recordings, workshop artifacts and Git history to an LLM lets consultants build a shared picture of an organization much faster than with traditional workshops alone.","One team's tech-debt pipeline combined deterministic tools (SonarQube, CodeScene), LLM prioritization and refactoring with open source agents and skills, and human review before closing each backlog item.","Successful AI practices that move into a platform drive token costs up. In the insurer's case an AI platform team applied FinOps cost attribution and model-selection guidance.","Guided 15-20 minute conversations synthesized by an LLM into Miro boards help avoid blank-page paralysis and surface pain points such as a three-day claim-approval lead time.","Automating architects' toil can remove learning opportunities. Sammut's architects pushed back on handing some work to AI that looked like toil.","AI-driven code archaeology of a legacy estate, presented as stories, let business experts choose among replace, re-platform, remove and re-architect instead of a parity migration."],"takeaways":["Before rolling out AI, spend a short, bounded period mapping where work actually hurts, using recorded conversations and an LLM to synthesize them.","Frame each AI initiative as a hypothesis with a way to measure it, and decide explicitly whether to scale, pivot or reverse.","Put cost attribution in place for LLM usage before a successful internal AI tool spreads to hundreds of teams.","In legacy migrations, use AI to explain what the old system does to domain experts and challenge whether each feature is still needed, instead of aiming for parity.","Share what works with AI through existing communities of practice, meetups and lunches rather than new structures."],"quotes":[{"text":"Just because you took a bad decision, it's not discipline by sticking by that decision.","at":"06:43"},{"text":"If you give that to the AI to do, I'm missing out on a learning opportunity.","at":"29:48"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"AI forces organizational reflection"},{"at":"04:15","title":"Lenses, observations, hypotheses"},{"at":"06:43","title":"Insurance company case"},{"at":"09:15","title":"Sales and distribution platform"},{"at":"13:15","title":"AI tech-debt pipeline"},{"at":"17:25","title":"Scaling via platforms and FinOps"},{"at":"21:49","title":"Claims: start where it hurts"},{"at":"28:25","title":"Why architects are a bottleneck"},{"at":"32:05","title":"Domains, teams and AI archaeology"},{"at":"36:36","title":"Culture, knowledge and commitment"}],"tech":["Event Storming","Team Topologies","Domain-driven design","Mikado Method","SonarQube","CodeScene","Miro","FinOps","DORA (Digital Operational Resilience Act)","Westrum organizational culture model"],"watchIf":"You are an architect or engineering leader in a large enterprise whose AI rollout is producing agents nobody uses, rising token bills or a stalled migration.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership","Architecture & design","Legacy modernization"],"demo":false}},{"id":9046,"title":"rm -rf self-doubt: Debugging Impostor Syndrome for Developers","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"People, Practices & Career","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["growth","impostersyndrome","neuroscience","selfdoubt"],"abstract":["\"Whether you think you can or you think you can't – you're right.\"","In software, we trust the logic of our systems, but the \"human hardware\" running the code often executes a background process of self-doubt. Imposter Syndrome isn't a permanent bug; it's a physiological response hard-coded into our biology. From junior devs facing their first pull request to Java Champions keynote-ing at international conferences, the \"fraud detection\" alert is a universal experience.","This session deconstructs the neuroscience behind this phenomenon—from the amygdala's \"fight-or-flight\" triggers to the cognitive effects of cortisol. We will move beyond the \"soft skill\" fluff to examine practical, iterative steps for managing these triggers. Learn how to reframe self-doubt as a signal for growth, optimize your response to code reviews, and lean into the discomfort of the unknown to thrive in an industry that changes every week.","You’ll walk away with tools you can apply immediately, whether you’re debugging unfamiliar code, speaking up in design discussions, or stepping into a bigger role"],"favourites":288,"speakers":[{"name":"Vincent Mayers","company":"Neo4j","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-f6ffe4ab-1e88-45a9-b47d-572232f3b9c5.jpg","bio":"Expat Brit, artist, athlete, traveler, and technology community junkie. Vincent works as a Senior Community Manager at Neo4j, is a Java Champion, a steering committee member of MicroProfile, a board member of the Atlanta Java Users Group since 2008, and runs Devnexus, which is the largest community AI/Java conference in the USA.<br><br>Vincent has a degree in 3D Design from the University of Wolverhampton, United Kingdom, and, in another life, was a full-time athlete competing at World Championship level in whitewater kayaking.","linkedin":"vincentmayers","bluesky":null,"twitter":"vincentmayers"}],"start":"2026-10-07T15:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T16:40:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"Tfrt19d3TEA","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Mayers uses his own route from a pottery degree into Java DevRel to argue that impostor syndrome is a normal fight-or-flight response, and that a knowledge gap should not be treated as a judgement on yourself.","summary":["Vincent Mayers, who runs the Atlanta Java Users Group and DevNexus and has worked in DevRel at Oracle, JFrog, Gradle and Neo4j, opens with 'I am an imposter'. He switched from a planned civil engineering path to an art degree in pottery because of one hostile and one kind teacher, then worked as an athlete, designer, salesperson, grocery store manager and tech recruiter before becoming community manager at the Gates-funded edtech startup inBloom. His point is that you choose whether your earlier experience counts, and that tech careers have many side doors besides a CS degree.","He traces the term to Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes's 1978 paper on the impostor phenomenon and lists the symptoms: persistent self-doubt, crediting success to luck, fear of being exposed (for example 'if they saw how much I used AI'), and dismissing praise. The key distinction is between 'I don't understand this code yet', which is information, and 'I don't understand this code, so I shouldn't be on this team', which is a verdict. His 'impostor pipeline' runs from an unfamiliar task through doubt, panic, over-preparation or procrastination and success, then discounting the evidence, and repeats. He explains the biology: the amygdala signals the hypothalamus, adrenaline acts like a pager alert and cortisol like a long-running incident, all triggered by things like a PR with 34 comments.","On AI, he argues that 'it mostly works' vibe coding does not cancel out years of judgement, and that any gap AI exposes can be closed. His coping advice: lean into fight rather than flight, as he did on whitewater kayaking start lines and at his first talk, a keynote for 4,000 people. Reframe weaknesses as strengths, celebrate small wins, treat failures as productive (his examples are cornflakes and Netflix's chaos engineering), avoid comparing yourself with other people's highlight reels, and remember that nobody else has your perspective. Lessons from a JFokus speaker unconference: you will never be the smartest person in the room, and reaching one person is enough. For leads, he advises reviewing the work rather than the person and having seniors say openly what they don't know."],"keyPoints":["Impostor syndrome is not a medical diagnosis; the term comes from Clance and Imes's 1978 paper on high-achieving women.","Healthy self-doubt names a fixable knowledge gap; impostor thinking turns it into a verdict on your identity.","The 'impostor pipeline': unfamiliar task, self-doubt, panic, over-preparation or procrastination, success, rejection of the evidence, repeat.","Perceived social threats such as harsh code review trigger the same amygdala, adrenaline and cortisol response as physical danger.","The fight-or-flight response does not go away with experience; Mayers learned to expect it and use the heightened awareness.","Systemic bias, microaggressions and prestige cultures make impostor feelings worse, and that is a different problem from internally generated doubt.","Leaders reduce it by critiquing work rather than people and by making their own uncertainty visible."],"takeaways":["When stuck, rephrase 'I don't understand this' as 'I don't understand this yet' and list what you need to learn.","Keep a record of wins, including small ones, and accept compliments instead of explaining them away.","Before a stressful event, expect the adrenaline response and treat it as preparation rather than a warning.","If you review code or lead a team, comment on the work, not the author, and say out loud what you don't know."],"quotes":[{"text":"Whether you think you can or you think you can't, you're right.","at":"05:14"},{"text":"Don't treat impostor syndrome as a bug to be squashed. Treat it as a feature to understand.","at":"33:19"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"From pottery to tech"},{"at":"06:36","title":"Defining impostor syndrome"},{"at":"09:35","title":"Self-doubt versus impostor thinking"},{"at":"13:00","title":"Fight or flight neuroscience"},{"at":"18:34","title":"Impostor thinking and AI"},{"at":"20:41","title":"Coping strategies"},{"at":"26:47","title":"Speaker unconference lessons"},{"at":"30:49","title":"Team dynamics and Q&A"}],"tech":[],"watchIf":"You often feel you don't belong on your team or on stage, or you lead people who do, and want a practical, personal framing.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":10021,"title":"Java Script (No, Not That One): Modern Automation with Java","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["jbang","scripting","java","automation"],"abstract":["We've all been there: you need a quick automation script, so you reach for Bash or Python and immediately get tripped up by syntax you can never remember. Or you try JavaScript and watch 5 + 3 quietly become \"53\". You want something fast, but you also want type safety and a language you actually know.","Good news: modern Java has become a real scripting language. Shebang support, single-file execution, and instance main methods mean no boilerplate and no build step—just a file you can run. When you need external libraries, JBang handles dependencies with a single line. And unlike a hastily typed shell script, the compiler still has your back before anything runs.","Through live demos, we'll go from a throwaway one-liner to genuinely useful scripts, using records and text blocks to keep them short and readable: parsing a CSV and calling an API, or building a self-contained tool your AI harness can invoke, returning typed, predictable output instead of best-effort text. All in Java, and all without a pom.xml in sight. You'll leave with a new tool for the jobs you currently hand off to Bash."],"favourites":103,"speakers":[{"name":"Loïc Magnette","company":"Oniryx","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-cda3ea24-0a68-4fe3-a93c-e199a1d8fedf.jpg","bio":"Loïc Magnette is a seasoned software developer with a strong background in consulting. Currently a senior developer at Oniryx, he specializes in Java and Angular, delivering innovative solutions and sharing his knowledge as a speaker. As a co-organizer of the Belgian Java User Group (BeJUG), he fosters connections within the developer community. Outside of tech, Loïc’s passion for wildlife inspires his work and creativity.","linkedin":"lomagnette","bluesky":"lomagnette.bsky.social","twitter":"LoMagnette"}],"start":"2026-10-06T16:20:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T16:50:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"pgVJWp-SjxI","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":19748,"title":"Snake in 10 Lines: Learning More by Coding Less","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["coding","snake","minimalism","constraints"],"abstract":["Can you create a complete game with just 10 lines of code? That was the quest I set myself when recreating the classic game Snake in as few lines as possible. Let me tell you: I was not ready for the types of challenges I would face. For example, just registering button presses was not as straightforward as I had hoped.","Through live coding, I will show you how I overcame these obstacles and distilled Snake to its absolute essence. What key elements make this game instantly recognizable? By only using tools from the JDK, we will end up with a minimal code base and if not award-winning, at least a playable version of Snake.","Striving for conciseness led me to a deeper understanding of the code. I will show how constraints push us to think outside the box and lead to more creative solutions. Moreover, my approach can be applied to any problem. Sometimes the best way to understand code is by seeing how little of it you actually need!"],"favourites":162,"speakers":[{"name":"Guus de Wit","company":"adesso","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-df4a0ee3-439b-464e-94f4-33a5dce8a655.jpg","bio":"Guus is a Java/Kotlin engineer at adesso Netherlands. With a passion for puzzles and a background in science, he strives for elegant solutions to complex problems. In his spare time, he likes to over-optimize the simple tasks of today, to hopefully live a lazy life tomorrow.","linkedin":"guus-de-wit-0846a5161","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T08:20:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"LD2tKMpToYg","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"De Wit live-codes a playable Snake in Kotlin, using only the JDK, in 10 lines of at most 130 characters, arguing that constraints force better solutions and that concise code brings understanding.","summary":["De Wit takes the six-word story (Margaret Atwood's \"Longed for him, got him, shit\") as inspiration and reduces Snake to \"snake moves, steers, eats, grows, dies\". He describes Pong, Tetris and the Chrome dino game the same way, and suggests describing your next feature in six words. The rules: 10 functional lines inside main, at most 130 characters each, no libraries beyond the JDK, written in Kotlin. Readability, few bugs and extra features such as a score are nice-to-haves. His first version prints frames to the terminal like his old Game of Life, but the terminal cannot read arrow keys in real time without external libraries, so he turns to Swing's KeyAdapter, a JFrame and a JTextArea. That costs more than 40 lines until he strips defaults and joins lines with IntelliJ's Ctrl+Shift+J.","Movement is the key insight. Moving every body part, or storing only the corners as memory-constrained old games did, takes too much code. Instead, each frame adds a new head and drops the tail, and eating just puts the tail back. Self-collision is checked by comparing the distinct cells to the whole body. That gives a 114-line game, still too large because of the 2D Cell class. So the board becomes a 1D list rendered with JTextArea line wrap, and directions become plus or minus 1 and plus or minus the width. Wrapping across lines is a side effect he keeps as a feature.","Condensing to 10 lines uses inlining of single-use variables and methods, shorter names, removed whitespace, a static import of nextInt, Kotlin's generateSequence to replace a retry loop for random food, an anonymous object for the key adapter, and apply on the JFrame instead of a variable. He then hides features in the constraints. The process exit code shows the score. The snake starts one cell shorter with food in front of its face, so the first frame randomises the food. A newDirection variable blocks reversing into yourself. Filling the board triggers a kill screen, because no empty cell is left for food. The same 10-line skeleton, with different logic, gives a Pong-like game and a Tetris-like game. A Java version, \"Jake\", also fits."],"keyPoints":["Real-time keyboard input in a terminal isn't possible with the plain JDK; Swing's KeyAdapter, JFrame and JTextArea are the shortest route, after stripping defaults.","Snake movement is just adding a new head and removing the tail each frame; eating means putting the removed tail back.","Storing the board as a 1D list with line wrap turns coordinates and directions into single integers (plus or minus 1, plus or minus width), which removed enough code to fit 10 lines.","Self-collision check: the game ends when the number of distinct cells in the body is smaller than the body size.","Kotlin's generateSequence with first { it !in snake } replaces a while-loop retry for placing food, and is more readable.","Abusing the process exit code as the score display and placing the first food directly in front of the snake add features without adding lines.","About half of the 10 lines (window, frame loop, sleep, exit) are generic game scaffolding; swapping the other half gives Pong- and Tetris-like games."],"takeaways":["Try a constrained side project (line, time or token budget) to learn APIs and shortcuts you would not touch otherwise.","When a solution doesn't fit a constraint, rethink the data representation instead of squeezing the code.","Describe your next feature in six words to pin down what actually matters.","Use IntelliJ's Join Lines (Ctrl+Shift+J) and inline refactorings to tidy parameter lists and single-use variables.","Replace initialise-then-retry while loops with generateSequence in Kotlin or Stream.generate in Java."],"quotes":[{"text":"Snake moves, steers, eats, grows, and dies.","at":"03:39"},{"text":"I did promise you snake in 10 lines. I never said 2D snake in 10 lines.","at":"22:17"},{"text":"I don't have any unit tests. I have a human test. I can play the game.","at":"29:38"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Six-word stories"},{"at":"01:37","title":"Rules of the challenge"},{"at":"06:36","title":"Terminal Snake and its limits"},{"at":"09:28","title":"Keyboard input with Swing"},{"at":"14:09","title":"Moving, eating and dying"},{"at":"22:17","title":"Going one-dimensional"},{"at":"26:41","title":"Condensing to 10 lines"},{"at":"35:04","title":"Bugs, scores and kill screen"},{"at":"42:12","title":"Other games in 10 lines"},{"at":"46:12","title":"Takeaways and Java version"}],"tech":["Kotlin","Java","Swing","JFrame","JTextArea","KeyAdapter","IntelliJ IDEA","generateSequence"],"watchIf":"You enjoy code golf and live coding, or want a light talk on how constraints push you to rethink a design.","themes":["Hardware, robots & fun","Java language & JDK"],"demo":true}},{"id":10020,"title":"From AI to Agent: A Field Guide to Agentic Patterns in LangChain4j","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["observability","agentic","supervisor","workflow"],"abstract":["You have already built AI features into your Java application. The model is wrapped in a service, RAG feeds it context, tools are wired, calls are flowing. It works. Then requirements evolve. A single prompt-and-response is no longer enough. You need steps that follow each other, branches based on decisions, retries when things fail, sometimes several actions at once. The question shifts from \"how do I call an LLM?\" to \"how do I keep it under control?\".","That is where agentic systems come in. In three hours we start with a single @Agent doing one job and grow it live into a multi-agent system that can analyze, plan, act, and report back. Each step starts naive. We run it, feel the cracks, then reach for the pattern that holds: sequential, loop, parallel, and conditional workflows. AgenticScope and typed keys keep shared state honest. Goal-oriented agents, an LLM-driven supervisor, and a custom Planner take over when a fixed pipeline stops being enough. We add non-AI agents for deterministic work, human-in-the-loop checkpoints before external actions, and observability with the AgentMonitor, because an agent you cannot see is an agent you cannot debug.","You will leave able to look at a problem and tell whether it wants a plain workflow or a real agent, and how much autonomy to hand over before you lose the thread. The goal is not smarter prompts. It is agentic systems easier to reason about and evolve, because the pattern you do not adopt is the one you do not have to debug."],"favourites":118,"speakers":[{"name":"Loïc Magnette","company":"Oniryx","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-cda3ea24-0a68-4fe3-a93c-e199a1d8fedf.jpg","bio":"Loïc Magnette is a seasoned software developer with a strong background in consulting. Currently a senior developer at Oniryx, he specializes in Java and Angular, delivering innovative solutions and sharing his knowledge as a speaker. As a co-organizer of the Belgian Java User Group (BeJUG), he fosters connections within the developer community. Outside of tech, Loïc’s passion for wildlife inspires his work and creativity.","linkedin":"lomagnette","bluesky":"lomagnette.bsky.social","twitter":"LoMagnette"}],"start":"2026-10-05T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T10:30:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"IXcQYp51PLM","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":12751,"title":"Microservices Without the Distributed Mess","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["boundaries","ddd","orchestration","microservices"],"abstract":["Microservices are hard, that's probably the only thing we all agree on when it comes to microservices.","The promise of autonomy, scalability, and independent delivery often deliver something completely different: distributed spaghetti or lasagna. Where we have these blobs spread over the network, with synchronous call chains, unclear ownership, fragile transactions, and teams arguing where the “real” business logic should live.","This session looks at microservices from a pragmatic architecture perspective rooted in real experience. Not as a hype (anti-)pattern, but as a set of trade-offs around boundaries, ownership, communication, and consistency.","We will explore how Domain-Driven Design can help define service boundaries, but also where DDD terminology gets misused. What is a bounded context? How large should a service be? Can one microservice contain multiple APIs? When does orchestration make sense? Should we layer services?","The goal of this talk is not to sell microservices or dismiss them. The goal is to give developers and architects better mental models for designing services that are cohesive, autonomous, and understandable.","Attendees will leave with practical heuristics for spotting bad service boundaries, avoiding accidental distributed monoliths, and choosing communication patterns that fit the business problem instead of the architecture diagram."],"favourites":237,"speakers":[{"name":"Steven Hillaert","company":"Sofico","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocLs6Pu2B0uwDjIEHZbyZ-3DhqPhAT0bCQ8bP_sS8NWxNkEbRpKQfQ=s96-c","bio":"I’m a freelance software architect and engineering leader with 20+ years of experience in building, rescuing, and scaling business-critical software platforms across consulting, product, and enterprise environments. My strength sits at the intersection of architecture, people, and delivery. I’ve led teams from a few developers to full departments, designed technical strategies, combining strategic oversight with hands-on technical leadership and worked closely with business stakeholders to align technology with real-world needs. ","linkedin":"stevenhillaert","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T14:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"eFSEg54mwVU","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Hillaert argues that microservice failures come from ownership, boundaries and relationships rather than technology, and uses strategic DDD and context mapping, with leasing-industry examples, as tools for thinking about them.","summary":["Steven Hillaert, a solution architect at Sofico (leasing software), opens with the familiar arc: services call services, the web gets tangled, a message broker is added, and the coupling remains. Autonomy and ownership are not things microservices give you; you have to design for them. His definition: a microservice is a cohesive set of capabilities in a clear business context that can be deployed and evolved independently. Technically, it is just a deployment boundary. He organises the talk around three causes of failure: ownership, boundaries and relationships.","Ownership needs one accountable team that keeps the model coherent, evolves the service and runs it. That team must be given decision rights, including the right to refuse outside changes, and must also take ownership of failures. The service itself must own its capabilities, rules and the data those rules need. For boundaries, he uses the strategic side of DDD (domains, bounded contexts, ubiquitous language) as questions to ask rather than a methodology. A 'vehicle' means a configurable offer in quoting and a physical car in contracts. A credit domain may split into retail and fleet bounded contexts that live in one service as two modules, or one bounded context may be split into two services because fleet brings 90% of revenue and should not be put at risk by retail changes.","For relationships, Hillaert explains context mapping with stories about cooking steak with his wife: who calls whom does not show who depends on whom. Whoever has to adapt is downstream, whether they make the call or not. Refusing someone else's model is an anti-corruption layer; adopting it wholesale is being a conformist; customer-supplier means the downstream side has influence. A shared kernel between two services that each conform to different upstreams is a warning sign. He suggests using context maps when reasoning about a specific change, not to model the whole landscape.","On implementation, he prefers 'a monolith implemented modularly' to the term modular monolith, and warns that bad boundaries hidden as in-process calls become network hops when services are extracted. Communication style should follow from the relationship. Many synchronous reads hint at a wrong boundary or a need to replicate data. Async messaging still needs versioned contracts. Orchestration makes process state and compensation easier than choreography, at the cost of a single point of failure. He rejects mandatory layering of experience, domain and data services. A read-only vehicle/VIN database can be a 'service' on its own. A service should be as big as it needs to be to stay coherent, even with 100 endpoints."],"keyPoints":["Autonomy and ownership are design outcomes, not properties you get by splitting a system into deployables.","Ownership works both ways: the team is given decision rights (technology, refusing requests) and must take responsibility when things break. The service must own its rules and the data behind them.","Bounded contexts do not map one-to-one to services. Two contexts can share one deployable as modules, and one context can be split for risk, performance, legal or organisational reasons.","Overlap between boundaries is unavoidable. Make it deliberate and explicit, and accept duplicated data such as vehicle information where each service uses it for different decisions.","The upstream/downstream direction is about who has to adapt, not who calls whom. Anti-corruption layer, conformist, customer-supplier and shared kernel describe the real coupling.","A modular monolith does not make later extraction easy: hidden boundary mistakes become network hops once you pull the modules apart.","Orchestration gives one place to see process state and run compensations, while choreography avoids a single point of failure but nobody owns the process.","Forcing calls through BFF, domain and data service layers takes away service autonomy. A BFF may implement a new capability itself until it proves broadly useful."],"takeaways":["For each service, name the single accountable team and check that the data its business rules need lives inside its boundary.","When planning a change, sketch the context-map relationship (upstream, conformist, ACL) between the affected services instead of only the call graph.","Treat frequent synchronous reads to another service as a signal to revisit the boundary or replicate the data.","Pick orchestration for multi-service processes that need visible state and compensation.","Before creating a new service, ask whose success depends on it, what data must stay consistent and what transactions you need."],"quotes":[{"text":"When everybody starts actually somehow owning this service, nobody is owning its problems.","at":"06:07"},{"text":"It needs to be as big as it needs to be to be coherent.","at":"43:18"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why microservices go wrong"},{"at":"03:31","title":"Defining a microservice"},{"at":"06:07","title":"Team and service ownership"},{"at":"09:24","title":"Boundaries and cohesion"},{"at":"11:38","title":"DDD and bounded contexts"},{"at":"20:13","title":"Relationships and context mapping"},{"at":"29:27","title":"Modular monolith and communication"},{"at":"34:35","title":"Orchestration versus choreography"},{"at":"35:38","title":"Service layering and size"}],"tech":["Domain-Driven Design","Context mapping","Bounded contexts","Anti-corruption layer","Backend for frontend","Event storming","Domain storytelling","Modular monolith","Saga orchestration"],"watchIf":"You are splitting a system into services, or living with a distributed monolith, and want a vocabulary for ownership and coupling that goes beyond the call graph.","themes":["Architecture & design"],"demo":false}},{"id":24212,"title":"GraalVM Next: Layers, Crema, and WebAssembly in Action","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["graalvm","class loading","native image","webassembly"],"abstract":["What if native images could share code and memory, load new classes at run time, and run applications in the browser?","In this session, we’ll explore three projects expanding what GraalVM Native Image can do.","— Native Image Layers introduce a brand-new concept of reusable base images, allowing multiple applications to share code and memory.","— Project Crema adds dynamic class loading and execution to native applications — goodbye, closed world!","— Web Image (experimental) can compile JVM applications to WebAssembly, bringing libraries and applications directly to browsers and other WebAssembly environments.","Each project expands a different aspect of Native Image: how applications are packaged, what happens at run time, and where applications can run.","In this session we’ll see these forward-looking projects in action and discuss where they stand today."],"favourites":120,"speakers":[{"name":"Alina Yurenko","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-a31c43d1-c596-4e24-af3f-078f54609e6c.jpg","bio":"Alina is a developer advocate for GraalVM at Oracle. Loves both programming and natural languages, compilers and performance, and open source. Ambassador of snacks and not running.","linkedin":"alinayurenko","bluesky":null,"twitter":"alina_yurenko"}],"start":"2026-10-07T14:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T15:30:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"OLQ98Ko4p3Q","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":50101,"title":"The Java Story | The Official Documentary","type":"Documentary","duration":75,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["java","jakartaee","jvm","openjdk"],"abstract":["Java’s story is about much more than the evolution of a programming language. It is the story of a community that, more than once, could have watched something remarkable quietly fade away—but instead chose to fight for it, rebuild it, open it up, and pass it forward.","From its beginnings at Sun Microsystems to OpenJDK, the JVM ecosystem, and the transition from Java EE to Jakarta EE, this documentary traces 31 years of innovation, disruption, reinvention, and community.","The story is told by the people who shaped it, including James Gosling, Joshua Bloch, James Duncan Davidson, Tim Lindholm, Kim Polese, Carla Schroer, Sharat Chander, and many others.","Through their memories and perspectives, we revisit the decisions, breakthroughs, challenges, and communities that turned Java into one of the most influential software platforms ever created.","Thirty-one years later, Java is still here—not just because of the technology, but because generations of people decided it was worth carrying forward."],"favourites":98,"speakers":[{"name":"Georges Saab","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-e81f731a-26bb-4e81-803c-4252372b3e61.jpg","bio":"Georges Saab is the Senior Vice President of Development for the Java Platform Group at Oracle. His group is responsible for Java Standard Edition including the Java language, core libraries, and the Java Virtual Machine. Georges is a 35 year veteran of programming language and platform development. His work with the Java platform began as a developer of Java Standard Edition at JavaSoft and Sun Microsystems, where he was a founder of the Swing group and Java Webstart, and continued as he ran development of the JRockit JVM for many years at BEA Systems. Georges is the Chair of the OpenJDK governing board.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":null,"twitter":"gsaab"}],"start":"2026-10-06T15:35:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T16:50:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A screening of the official Java documentary, tracing 31 years of Java from Sun Microsystems through OpenJDK and the move from Java EE to Jakarta EE, told by those involved.","summary":["This is a documentary screening rather than a talk. It traces Java's history from its beginnings at Sun Microsystems through OpenJDK, the JVM ecosystem and the transition from Java EE to Jakarta EE, framing it as a community story of fighting for, rebuilding and opening up the platform.","It is told by people who shaped Java, including James Gosling, Joshua Bloch, James Duncan Davidson, Tim Lindholm, Kim Polese, Carla Schroer and Sharat Chander. Georges Saab of Oracle is listed as the speaker."],"keyPoints":["Covers 31 years of Java, from Sun Microsystems to today.","Includes OpenJDK, the JVM ecosystem and the Java EE to Jakarta EE transition.","Features interviews with James Gosling, Joshua Bloch and other early contributors."],"takeaways":["Watch it for context on how Java's community and governance came to be."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Java","OpenJDK","Jakarta EE","JVM"],"watchIf":"You are curious about the people and history behind Java.","themes":["Java language & JDK"],"demo":false}},{"id":4051,"title":"CRAcking Compliance - The EU Cyber Resilience Act","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["vulnerability management","sbom","dependabot","cyber resilience act"],"abstract":["The Cyber Resilience Act (CRA) will be arriving in the European Union in 2027, and with it a lot of new obligations. What we already consider good practices in our software industry today will be required by law tomorrow. In this talk, we will take a look at what's inside the CRA, who will be affected by it, and what new requirements we will have to fulfil. After that, we will take a look at tooling to generate Software Bills of Materials (SBOMs) for our Java, .NET and JavaScript projects, as well as how to visualise and integrate them into existing CI workflows. Last but not least, we will take a look at how to handle the required vulnerability management with (semi-)automated dependency updates by utilising tools like Dependabot or Renovate and how to reduce dependencies with distroless images."],"favourites":175,"speakers":[{"name":"Christian Schabetsberger","company":"Finxit IT Solutions","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-e4712227-61ca-4e98-85b2-5252890b3760.jpg","bio":"Christian Schabetsberger, the world&#39;s okayest developer, is a Freelance Software Engineer &amp; Architect from Linz, Austria with over a decade of experience.<br>Having spend half of his life in the .NET ecosystem (amongst others), he likes to stay on the bleeding edge of technology to become the (tech-savvy) ninja he pretends to be since he is six.<br>He is also an active community member, organising the .NET Meetup in Linz and supporting other local user groups. Furthermore he is a member of the .NET Foundation.","linkedin":"christian-schabetsberger-59406b180","bluesky":"cschabetsberger.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T13:10:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T14:00:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"QQcsZNf0eWs","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Schabetsberger explains what the EU Cyber Resilience Act requires of software makers, then compares free tools for SBOMs, vulnerability scanning and dependency updates, finding they disagree widely.","summary":["Schabetsberger, a freelance .NET and Angular engineer from Linz (and, by his own disclaimer, no lawyer), runs the talk around a running 'it depends' gag. The CRA has been in force since 10 December 2024 and applies fully from 11 December 2027. It covers any product with digital elements, including backends a product needs to work, with no size or revenue exemptions. Open source foundations have lighter 'steward' duties. Its rules: no shipping known vulnerabilities, free and signed security updates for the support period (at least five years by default), and informing customers. Most products need only a self-assessment for the CE mark; important and critical classes need standards, third-party assessment or certification.","Article 13's cybersecurity risk assessment drives the rest, since nearly every Annex I requirement applies 'on the basis of' it. He argues it is simply threat modelling done continuously and written down: Adam Shostack's four questions (what are we building, what can go wrong, what will we do about it, did we do a good job), STRIDE, which maps closely onto Annex I, and a whiteboard photo in the repo rather than an 80-page template. Since 11 September 2026, actively exploited vulnerabilities must be reported on ENISA's single reporting platform: an early warning within 24 hours, including weekends, a notification within 72 hours, and a final report later.","For SBOMs, he recommends CycloneDX or SPDX and tracking transitive and even dev dependencies. On an empty Angular app the tools disagreed: npm sbom and the CycloneDX npm CLI found 450 components (423 unique), the CycloneDX esbuild plugin only what was bundled, Syft only package.json plus stray GitHub Actions, Microsoft's SBOM tool 453, and GitHub's dependency graph 555 because it counts optional dependencies for every OS. The dependency graph also missed .NET transitive dependencies and central package management. Verify whatever your tool outputs.","For scanning he covers npm audit, dotnet list package --vulnerable, OWASP Dependency-Check, Trivy, Grype and OWASP Dependency-Track, which only accepts CycloneDX, so he wrote a converter for Yocto's SPDX output. Since maintainers owe you no backports, he argues for 'software gardening': Renovate or Dependabot with grouped updates, a cooldown of about 24 hours against compromised releases, and auto-merge for patches given good tests, plus OpenRewrite for migrations. He also inlines unmaintained libraries and uses distroless images or Docker Hardened Images."],"keyPoints":["The CRA applies fully from 11 December 2027; vulnerability and incident reporting obligations started on 11 September 2026.","Reporting goes through ENISA's single reporting platform: early warning within 24 hours, full notification within 72 hours, final report within 14 days for exploited vulnerabilities or one month for severe incidents.","Security updates must be free and delivered securely for the support period, which defaults to at least five years.","About 90% of products need only self-assessment; important class I and II and critical products need harmonised standards, third-party assessment or EU certification (Annex III lists examples).","The required risk assessment can be a lightweight, continuously updated threat model using Shostack's four questions and STRIDE, with tools like OWASP Threat Dragon, Threagile or Microsoft Threat Modeling Tool.","SBOM tools gave 423 to 555 components for the same empty Angular app, depending on deduplication, tree shaking and optional OS-specific packages.","OWASP Dependency-Track accepts only CycloneDX, which can force a conversion step if your build, like Yocto, emits SPDX."],"takeaways":["Hold a time-boxed threat-modelling session per release and commit the result to your repository as your CRA risk assessment.","Generate a CycloneDX or SPDX SBOM in CI and check its component count against your lock file.","Feed SBOMs into a scanner such as Dependency-Track or Trivy in a dedicated pipeline, not just build warnings.","Configure Renovate or Dependabot with grouped updates and a cooldown of at least 24 hours.","Set up an on-call path for 24-hour vulnerability early warnings and register on ENISA's platform."],"quotes":[{"text":"What we already consider good practices today will simply be required by law tomorrow.","at":"06:14"},{"text":"A picture of your whiteboard checked into your repository is infinitely better than the perfect threat model you never get around to make.","at":"16:59"},{"text":"Whether your software should be secure must never be answered with: it depends.","at":"43:28"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Disclaimer and CRA basics"},{"at":"03:29","title":"Who is affected"},{"at":"06:14","title":"Core obligations and categories"},{"at":"11:23","title":"Risk assessment as threat modelling"},{"at":"16:59","title":"SBOMs and reporting deadlines"},{"at":"21:06","title":"What counts as a dependency"},{"at":"23:52","title":"Comparing SBOM tools"},{"at":"31:32","title":"Vulnerability scanning tools"},{"at":"35:38","title":"Automated dependency updates"},{"at":"40:45","title":"Reducing dependencies and recap"}],"tech":["Cyber Resilience Act","CycloneDX","SPDX","Syft","Grype","Trivy","OWASP Dependency-Check","OWASP Dependency-Track","Microsoft SBOM Tool","GitHub dependency graph","Renovate","Dependabot","OpenRewrite","STRIDE","OWASP Threat Dragon","Docker Hardened Images","Yocto"],"watchIf":"You ship software in the EU and need a practical engineering reading of the CRA plus an honest comparison of SBOM and scanning tools.","themes":["Security & supply chain"],"demo":false}},{"id":50106,"title":"From User Prompt to JPA Query: Comparing Tool Calling, Structured Output & Hybrid Approach","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["java","llm","structuredoutput","filtering"],"abstract":["With Java, data can be loaded easily from different sources and displayed in a web UI with little effort. However, as soon as users need to search for specific records, applications often rely on complex filter forms. Modern LLMs offer an alternative: they can extract filter criteria from natural language and turn them into suitable database queries.","Using an example project built with Spring AI and Vaadin, we compare three possible approaches. With Function Calling, the model invokes tool functions in the application code and passes the required parameters. This is easy to understand, but in a simple implementation it quickly reaches its limits when handling complex requests involving negations, operators, or multiple values. Structured Output, on the other hand, directly creates a custom filter object and can represent more complex requests in a compact form. A hybrid approach combines both concepts by using a tool call whose parameters themselves contain a structured filter definition.","All approaches are tested with the same queries against local Ollama models. We compare which approach is more reliable, faster, and more cost-efficient, and examine where each of them reaches its limits."],"favourites":73,"speakers":[{"name":"Sebastian Kühnau","company":"Vaadin","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocKJ7pHA4yXXBTPT1sa3oGVZ-ONpi-AtRLBtJIKuxKb5_sQOwm0=s96-c","bio":"Sebastian has been a Java junkie for over two decades and fell in love with Vaadin during his university days. Since 2016, he has worked in various technical roles at Vaadin, helping customers build tailor-made web applications and leading workshops and training sessions. In 2024, he transitioned to looking after the community full-time, traveling from event to event as a speaker to share his knowledge and passion for Vaadin. When he’s not at a conference or experimenting with AI, he’s always up for a tech talk and a good cup of coffee.","linkedin":"sebastian-kuehnau","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T11:05:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T11:45:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"-2vEfnB2mRw","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":17786,"title":"Agent Loops, Decoded: Fundamentals to Agentic Patterns in Spring AI","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["toolcalling","spring ai","rag","agents"],"abstract":["At their core, AI agents are context orchestrators — gathering information, querying models, evaluating outputs, and adapting their approach. This live-coded session takes you from Spring AI fundamentals to agentic patterns.","We start with the fundamentals: ChatClient essentials, context engineering, and session memory. We cover how Advisors extend the LLM execution path, including Recursive Advisors implementing the Agent Loop — the loop behind every autonomous agent — then enrich context with RAG and connect to external services via Tool Calling and MCP.","From there, we build up the agentic patterns Tool Calling and Advisors make possible:","Tool Search Tool — dynamic tool discovery for agents with hundreds of tools","LLM-as-a-Judge — automated response evaluation in an evaluator-optimizer loop","Explainable Agents — augmenting tool calls with the model's reasoning for debuggability","Agent Skills — LLM-agnostic capabilities, loaded on demand","Todo / structured planning — preventing \"lost in the middle\" failures","AskUserQuestion — gathering requirements before acting","Subagent Orchestration — delegating to specialized agents with isolated context","MCP, A2A, and ACP — securing tool access, building rich MCP Apps, and connecting agents to other agents and clients","You'll leave understanding how a few building blocks compose into any agentic pattern, with an early preview of what's next: durable, crash-recoverable agentic workflows in Spring AI 2.1."],"favourites":216,"speakers":[{"name":"Christian Tzolov","company":"Spring Team @ Broadcom","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-970401c2-a699-4f02-8855-f855f219221a.jpg","bio":"Spring AI lead, Model Context Protocol (MCP) Java SDK maintainer, Agentic AI Foundation (AAIF) Ambassador, R&amp;D Engineer in Broadcom&#39;s Spring team and Apache Software Foundation (ASF) committer","linkedin":"tzolov","bluesky":"tzolov.bsky.social","twitter":"christzolov"}],"start":"2026-10-05T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T10:30:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"Q91obeWeWNQ","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Tzolov live-codes Spring AI from ChatClient basics to skills, subagents and durable memory, showing that every agentic pattern is built from context, advisors, recursion and tool calls.","summary":["Christian Tzolov, Spring AI lead and MCP Java SDK maintainer, frames the three hours around one rule: a model's output is only as good as its context. Context can be curated prescriptively (you decide what goes in) or agentically (the model asks via tool calls), and repeated rounds of the latter form the agent loop. His claim is that four concepts explain every pattern: context and generation, advisors that intercept input and output, recursion, and tools. He demonstrates with ChatClient on Anthropic and a web-based inspector he built the week before that shows the advisor chain, the exact wire payload and a sequence diagram per request.","The foundations cover structured output (generic schema-in-prompt or the provider's native mode, with a validation advisor that feeds schema errors back to the model for a retry), and conversation memory via an advisor. He previews Spring AI Session for 2.1 in November, which adds events and summarising compaction, and warns that history must be cut at turn boundaries, not mid tool exchange. A Hurricane Milton RAG demo shows naive retrieval filling context with chunks from the article's references; modular RAG adds query rewriting, multi-query expansion, a similarity threshold, then filtering and re-ranking, which cut seven chunks to two. A local embedding model failed to download live, so he switched to OpenAI embeddings.","Several demos use a new non-generative \"System 1\" model that answers batches of yes/no, choice and score questions with confidence, faster and cheaper than an LLM and more consistent, though weak on general knowledge. He uses it for RAG filtering, input/output guardrails that blocked a jailbreak and a self-harm prompt, and a self-refining advisor that retried when a tool returned -252 degrees. Tool calling is explained step by step, then the tool search tool: instead of sending every tool definition, the model gets one search tool and pulls in tools on demand, which dropped a 30-plus-tool request from about 7,000 to 4,000 tokens. Unlike vector, Lucene or regex indexes, the System 1 index can say no tool fits.","The second half builds patterns from tools, using the Spring AI Agent Utils community project (file system, shell, Brave web search, a smart web fetch that summarises with an LLM). Agent skills are a skill tool listing SKILL.md names and descriptions, plus file and shell tools for resources and scripts; skills can also ship as JARs. A todo-write tool keeps multi-step plans on track, an ask-user tool interviews the user before answering, and a task tool runs subagents defined in Markdown with their own model, tools and clean context. Auto-memory tools keep durable Markdown memory with an \"auto dream\" compaction pass. He ends with MCP sampling, A2A and ACP, and an experimental agentic layer for Spring AI 2.1."],"keyPoints":["Spring AI's ChatClient is a chain of around-advisors; a recursive advisor that re-invokes the downstream chain is how both validation retries and the tool-calling loop are built.","Conversation memory should be truncated at turn boundaries; cutting between a tool request and its response confuses the model or makes the API reject the call.","Modular RAG with query rewriting, multi-query expansion, threshold search, filtering and re-ranking reduced seven retrieved chunks to two relevant ones.","The tool search tool registers one discovery tool instead of all definitions; in the demo it cut a request with 30-plus tools from about 7,000 to 4,000 tokens.","Non-generative \"System 1\" judgment models answer several yes/no, choice or score questions in parallel with confidence, which suits guardrails, RAG filtering and evaluator loops, but they lack general knowledge.","Agent skills, todo planning, ask-user questions, subagents and auto memory are each implemented as one more tool plus a description; LLMs only understand tool calls.","Subagents defined in Markdown run with isolated context, their own model and a filtered tool set, so the lead agent's memory holds only the task call and its final result.","Spring AI 2.1 (November) is planned to bring Spring AI Session, support for the new stateless MCP specification and an experimental agentic layer with durability hooks such as Temporal."],"takeaways":["Inspect the actual payload sent to the model, with a logging advisor or an inspector, before tuning prompts.","Add query rewriting, relevance filtering and re-ranking to a RAG pipeline instead of trusting raw similarity search.","Enable tool search when an agent connects to several MCP servers, rather than sending every tool definition on each call.","Delegate noisy research steps to subagents with their own context, and use a cheaper model for them where it is good enough.","Run skills that execute scripts, and any shell tool, only inside a sandbox."],"quotes":[{"text":"The output, the result of your foundation models, is only as good as the context you provide to them.","at":"00:31"},{"text":"We cannot remove the non-deterministic nature of the LLMs, but instead we're building fences around them.","at":"04:18"},{"text":"LLMs are fine-tuned to support tool calling, and this is the only abstraction they know to ask for additional information.","at":"118:34"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Context curation and agent loops"},{"at":"08:02","title":"ChatClient, advisors and inspector"},{"at":"14:02","title":"Structured output and memory"},{"at":"28:35","title":"Prompt stuffing and RAG"},{"at":"58:45","title":"Guardrails and System 1 models"},{"at":"69:27","title":"Tool calling and tool search"},{"at":"89:29","title":"Agentic patterns and LLM judges"},{"at":"106:39","title":"Agent skills"},{"at":"122:49","title":"Todo, ask-user and subagents"},{"at":"147:25","title":"Durable memory and protocols"}],"tech":["Spring AI","Spring Boot","Spring AI Agent Utils","MCP","MCP Java SDK","A2A","Agent Client Protocol","Anthropic Claude","OpenAI","Ollama","Brave Search","Agent Skills","Temporal","Micrometer"],"watchIf":"You build LLM features in Java and want to see how agent skills, tool search, subagents and memory are actually implemented on top of Spring AI's advisors and tool calls.","themes":["LLMs in Java apps","AI agents & agentic coding","Spring & frameworks"],"demo":true}},{"id":25654,"title":"Beyond Code: The Intent-Driven IDE for the Agentic Age","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["specification","intent","agents","automation"],"abstract":["For decades, IDEs have organized software development around files, editors, plugins, code completion, and automation. But what happens when the developer’s primary input is no longer code, but intent?","In this live, demo-driven session, we’ll examine the emerging shape of intent-driven development environments: specifications become executable context, agents work concurrently in isolated workspaces, and developers shift from producing every line to directing, reviewing, and integrating work.","We’ll take a task from specification to pull request, delegate parts of it to specialized agents, and build an extension for a custom workflow the environment did not previously support. We’ll also explore the architectural choices behind these systems, including agent harnesses, model selection, local inference, automation, context management, and human approval boundaries.","Rather than presenting agents as a replacement for developers, we’ll examine where this model works, where it breaks down, and what skills developers need when intent becomes the starting point for software creation."],"favourites":88,"speakers":[{"name":"Bruno Borges","company":"GitHub","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-fd56814f-d6c5-4009-8f3d-9c9f8d2cd8f2.jpg","bio":"Bruno Borges is a Principal Product Manager at GitHub with over 30 years of experience in Software Engineering, Product Management, Developer Relations, and Cloud &amp; AI developer experience. Currently, he leads developer relations and product management programs for AI and GitHub Copilot at Microsoft, driving initiatives like the <a href=\"github.com/github/copilot-sdk/tree/main/java/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">GitHub Copilot SDK for Java</a>, and AI-assisted modernization workflows for enterprise developers. Bruno also continues to shape Microsoft&#39;s Java strategy, having led the public announcement of the Microsoft Build of OpenJDK and new tools like Azure Command Launcher for Java. Prior to his current focus on AI, he spent years building Microsoft Azure into a top destination for Java workloads, and before that served nearly six years at Oracle influencing developer relations strategy. A recognized Java Champion and top Java influencer, Bruno is a frequent conference speaker and open-source contributor with projects like <a href=\"https://javaevolved.github.io\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Java Evolved</a>, <a href=\"https://brunoborges.github.io/jairosvg\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">JairoSVG</a>, <a href=\"https://brunoborges.github.io/jlib-inspector\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">JLib Inspector</a>, <a href=\"https://brunoborges.github.io/ghx\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">ghx</a>, and more. Visit github.com/brunoborges","linkedin":"brunocborges","bluesky":"brunoborges.bsky.social","twitter":"brunoborges"}],"start":"2026-10-05T15:35:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T16:05:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"sexe_tnhAOI","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":25048,"title":"Java on Kubernetes Without Container Images: The WebAssembly Way","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["kubernetes","springboot","containerd","webassembly"],"abstract":["Every time you ship a Java service to Kubernetes, you ship a base image, a bundled JDK, and an OS layer. None of which you wrote besides your application code and the selection of dependencies, but all of which you must monitor and regularly patch. The real artifact you care about, your application JAR and its dependencies, are buried inside 250 MB of packaging you never wanted to own. WebAssembly already solved this. With KWasm, the Wasm runtime lives on the node, the developer ships only a .wasm module as an OCI artifact, and a RuntimeClass tells Kubernetes to execute it. There is no Dockerfile, no container image involved at all.","Most Kubernetes users know that \"runtimeClassName: kata\" or \"runtimeClassName: wasm\" routes a pod to a different executor. Few know how this works with the containerd Runtime v2 TTRPC protocol, OCI bundle assembly, overlay filesystems, and runc delegation that make all this possible.","In this talk we walk through how the KWasm analogy actually maps cleanly to Java, and what custom containerd shims, node provisioners, and CRDs can actually do: allow you to build your own Kubernetes-based platform (and quite easily with agentic AI). We will explore a new open source project called Brewlet.sh with live coding: its CLI brewlet push target/app.jar, the Kubernetes deployment approach kubectl apply, and see in practice how a Spring Boot service runs on Kubernetes without a single container image in sight."],"favourites":223,"speakers":[{"name":"Bruno Borges","company":"GitHub","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-fd56814f-d6c5-4009-8f3d-9c9f8d2cd8f2.jpg","bio":"Bruno Borges is a Principal Product Manager at GitHub with over 30 years of experience in Software Engineering, Product Management, Developer Relations, and Cloud &amp; AI developer experience. Currently, he leads developer relations and product management programs for AI and GitHub Copilot at Microsoft, driving initiatives like the <a href=\"github.com/github/copilot-sdk/tree/main/java/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">GitHub Copilot SDK for Java</a>, and AI-assisted modernization workflows for enterprise developers. Bruno also continues to shape Microsoft&#39;s Java strategy, having led the public announcement of the Microsoft Build of OpenJDK and new tools like Azure Command Launcher for Java. Prior to his current focus on AI, he spent years building Microsoft Azure into a top destination for Java workloads, and before that served nearly six years at Oracle influencing developer relations strategy. A recognized Java Champion and top Java influencer, Bruno is a frequent conference speaker and open-source contributor with projects like <a href=\"https://javaevolved.github.io\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Java Evolved</a>, <a href=\"https://brunoborges.github.io/jairosvg\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">JairoSVG</a>, <a href=\"https://brunoborges.github.io/jlib-inspector\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">JLib Inspector</a>, <a href=\"https://brunoborges.github.io/ghx\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">ghx</a>, and more. Visit github.com/brunoborges","linkedin":"brunocborges","bluesky":"brunoborges.bsky.social","twitter":"brunoborges"}],"start":"2026-10-08T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T08:20:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"fszyRIZXjQM","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Borges presents Brewlet, an experimental containerd shim and operator that runs Java JARs pushed as OCI artifacts on Kubernetes with a node-provided JDK, so platform teams can patch the JDK and dependencies without app rebuilds.","summary":["Bruno Borges argues that ownership has shifted too far onto developers: they write the business code but also own the dependencies, the JDK and the base OS image, so every CVE forces every team to rebuild and redeploy. He contrasts this with the Java EE days, when developers shipped a WAR and the platform team patched the application server. The model for his alternative is SpinKube (originally KWasm), where the WebAssembly runtime lives on the node, developers push a .wasm module to an OCI registry, and a runtimeClassName routes the pod to that runtime. Java bytecode is portable in the same way.","Brewlet (brew + kubelet), built over about three months, applies this to Java. Unlike Buildpacks or Cloud Foundry, which still produce a container image, the registry holds only the JAR, its dependencies and a JVM launch configuration. On kubectl apply, the scheduler picks a node by runtime class, containerd calls the Brewlet shim (a TTRPC task service), and the shim assembles an OCI bundle: the app and dependencies under /app, the JDK from a platform-approved image mounted read-only, and runc starting the JVM as PID 1 with normal cgroup limits. Platform teams declare approved JDKs per node pool in a node profile. Developers use either a Deployment with runtimeClassName and annotations or a JavaApplication CRD covering replicas, JVM options, ports, service creation and autoscaling.","Borges also proposes managed dependency bundles: the platform team approves a BOM and publishes it as an immutable OCI artifact that applications reference, so a library CVE means pointing at a new bundle and restarting, not rebuilding. After a projector outage filled with Q&A, the AKS demo installed Brewlet via its CLI (which wraps a Helm chart), listed node profiles with Temurin 25, Temurin 27 and distroless JDKs, built a Spring Boot 'show my JVM' app with the Brewlet Maven plugin, pushed it to Azure Container Registry after a Docker Desktop hiccup, and inspected its layers. He then switched the app from JDK 25 to 27 by changing only the node profile, with the same artifact digest.","He closes on how it was built: 12 weeks, about 600 commits (more than half AI-assisted), 48,000 lines of Go he could not have written himself, guided by a 2,200-line specification and an extensive test harness. Kind and Docker Desktop surfaced about 70% of bugs; the rest appeared only on real clusters. His recipe for a custom platform is a containerd shim, a node provisioner, a RuntimeClass and a CRD. Brewlet is experimental, pre-1.0, and on GitHub under the Microsoft organisation."],"keyPoints":["The OCI artifact holds separate layers for the app JAR, the classpath dependencies, snapshot dependencies and a JVM config, and supports classpath, fat-JAR and JPMS launch modes.","The shim is a containerd Runtime v2 TTRPC task service that builds config.json and a rootfs. The JDK comes from a pinned image digest, for example mcr.microsoft.com/openjdk/jdk, and is mounted read-only.","The pod's userland is the JDK image the platform chose, so a distroless JDK means no shell in the pod. Limits, probes and other Kubernetes tooling work as with normal containers.","Node profiles list approved JDK distributions and major versions per node pool. Without an explicit distribution, the choice is currently alphabetical.","Brewlet and regular container workloads can share a cluster, since only pods with the Brewlet runtime class are affected.","Managed dependency bundles move BOM ownership to the platform team, so dependency CVEs need a restart rather than a rebuild.","The live demo moved a running Spring Boot app from JDK 25 to JDK 27 without rebuilding or re-pushing the application artifact.","Borges said Istio, Prometheus and static files served from outside the JAR are untested, and a Gradle plugin does not exist yet (the CLI can be called from Gradle)."],"takeaways":["Decide explicitly whether app teams or a platform team should own JDK and dependency patching in your organisation.","Look at runtimeClassName and containerd shims as a way to build platform features on Kubernetes instead of encoding everything in images.","Try Brewlet on Kind or Docker Desktop with a sample Spring Boot or Quarkus app, but not in production yet.","When building infrastructure with coding agents, invest in the specification and test harness first, and test on real clusters as well as local ones."],"quotes":[{"text":"Devs write one of these layers only, but they own all of them.","at":"01:55"},{"text":"Runtime class name is the door.","at":"48:59"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Who owns the runtime"},{"at":"04:49","title":"From SpinKube to Java"},{"at":"09:03","title":"Buildpacks and Cloud Foundry compared"},{"at":"10:04","title":"Shims, bundles and runc"},{"at":"14:13","title":"Node profiles and CRDs"},{"at":"19:15","title":"Managed dependency bundles"},{"at":"21:26","title":"Demo setup and Q&A"},{"at":"29:17","title":"Live demo on AKS"},{"at":"42:23","title":"Swapping JDK 25 to 27"},{"at":"46:55","title":"Built with agents"}],"tech":["Brewlet","Kubernetes","containerd","runc","TTRPC","OCI","RuntimeClass","CRDs","Helm","SpinKube","KWasm","WebAssembly","Spring Boot","Quarkus","Maven","Eclipse Temurin","Microsoft Build of OpenJDK","AKS","Azure Container Registry","Kind","Buildpacks","Cloud Foundry","Go"],"watchIf":"You run Java on Kubernetes and are tired of rebuilding every service image for each JDK or base-image CVE, or you want to see how runtime classes and containerd shims work.","themes":["Cloud native & Kubernetes","Security & supply chain","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":true}},{"id":49253,"title":"Engineering for the Long Haul: Operating Open Source at Scale","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Development Practices","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["security","open-source","compliance","patching"],"abstract":["Open-source software powers the world’s most innovative enterprises, but keeping it secure, compliant, and reliable over the long haul is where complexity (and unexpected risk) quietly creeps in. This presentation offers a guided tour through the challenges organizations face when working with open-source technologies that often have short lifecycles and require regular upgrades. Think of it as a practical playbook for extending the useful life of open-source software – keeping systems secure, audits calm, and engineers focused on building what’s next, not fighting what’s old. You will learn how TuxCare solutions, from rebootless patching and end-of-life security updates to noise-free vulnerability scanning and enterprise support for community operating systems, help enterprises run open-source software with confidence – even when the calendar, the threat landscape, or upstream support say otherwise"],"favourites":36,"speakers":[{"name":"Sven Sellen","company":"TuxCare Inc.","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-4ae1c500-52d8-4173-ad59-1ade63a5a175.jpg","bio":"Sven Sellen is a veteran of the cybersecurity and open-source industries, currently serving as the Director of Global Enterprise Sales at TuxCare, Inc. With a career spanning over 27 years, Sven has navigated the evolution of the digital landscape through executive leadership roles in Consulting, Sales Engineering, and Project Management.<br><br>As a high-level advocate for the Linux ecosystem, Sven specializes in bridging the gap between complex global enterprise infrastructure and robust security protocols.<br><br>At TuxCare, he leads the global sales strategy, helping multinational organizations manage the entire Linux lifecycle—from automated live patching to extended support for end-of-life systems—ensuring that mission-critical environments remain secure and compliant without the need for disruptive reboots.<br><br>Sven is a recognized Public Speaker at major international technology fairs and partner events. He is well-known for his ability to translate high-level security concepts into actionable global business strategies. His insights on Linux security, vulnerability management, and enterprise open-source trends have been featured in premier technical outlets, including heise online, Computerwoche, and it-security journal.","linkedin":"svensellen","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T11:05:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T11:45:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"Pg5kS8l9CpI","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":21655,"title":"What’s happening with Project Amber?","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["reconstruction","deconstruction","java","pattern-matching"],"abstract":["Project Amber has introduced a series of Java language features that together","enable a new programming style: data oriented programming. At its core is","compositional pattern matching, which allows values to be deconstructed","naturally and safely.","Where does Project Amber go from here? This talk explores the next wave of","language features under development. These experiments extend pattern matching","in several directions, including user-defined deconstruction for ordinary","interfaces and classes, nested constant patterns for more compact and expressive","matching, an exhaustive pattern-matching statement, safer primitive value","conversions within pattern matching, and a compact notation for deconstructing","and constructing record instances — a process we call reconstruction.","Along the way, I’ll present some of the experimental designs we’re exploring,","discussing both the opportunities they create and the language design challenges","they raise."],"favourites":193,"speakers":[{"name":"Gavin Bierman","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-81f196b1-a89f-4d7a-85d3-5e7e4e171a24.jpg","bio":"I work in the Java Platform Group at Oracle designing the Java programming language and virtual machine. I am also a member of the Programming Languages Research Group at Oracle Labs. I am based in Cambridge, UK.<br>My mission is to keep Java as a leading language and platform for developers around the world. Given my background as a researcher, I am especially keen to find, adapt and transfer ideas and techniques from the research community and get them into the hands of Java programmers. I spend much of my time working on Java’s industry-leading specifications.<br><br>I joined Oracle in March 2014. Previously I was a senior researcher at Microsoft Research Cambridge from March 2004 until February 2014. Before that I was a University Lecturer at the University of Cambridge Computer Laboratory and a Fellow, College Lecturer and Director of Studies at St John’s College, Cambridge. I studied for my PhD at the University of Cambridge, and for my BSc at Imperial College, London.","linkedin":"gavin-bierman-a0173075","bluesky":null,"twitter":"GavinBierman"}],"start":"2026-10-09T08:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"Gavin Bierman presents the next wave of Project Amber pattern-matching experiments, including user-defined deconstruction, nested constant patterns, primitive conversions and reconstruction of records.","summary":["The talk starts from Amber's existing features, which together enable data oriented programming built on compositional pattern matching. It then explores where the project goes next, presenting experimental designs and the language design challenges they raise.","Directions under development include user-defined deconstruction for ordinary interfaces and classes, nested constant patterns, an exhaustive pattern-matching statement, safer primitive value conversions within pattern matching, and a compact notation for deconstructing and constructing record instances, called reconstruction."],"keyPoints":["Pattern matching is the core of Amber's data oriented programming style.","User-defined deconstruction would extend patterns to ordinary classes and interfaces.","Other experiments: nested constant patterns, an exhaustive matching statement and safer primitive conversions.","Reconstruction is a compact notation for deconstructing and constructing records."],"takeaways":["Follow the Amber design discussions to see how upcoming pattern features could simplify your data-handling code."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Project Amber","Pattern matching","Records","Java"],"watchIf":"You track Java language evolution and want to hear it from a member of the language team.","themes":["Java language & JDK"],"demo":false}},{"id":5516,"title":"Wiring Up the AI Mesh: Building A2A-Compliant Java Agents","type":"Hands-on Lab","duration":120,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["microprofile","generativeai","jakartaee","langchain4j"],"abstract":["Building Production-Ready A2A Agents with LangChain4j, Quarkus, Spring Boot & Jakarta EE","Enterprise AI is shifting from isolated chatbots to multi-agent ecosystems where specialized agents negotiate and delegate tasks. The Agent-to-Agent (A2A) Protocol is the emerging standard for this interoperability, defining the wire format for seamless cross-language communication.","This intensive, hands-on workshop equips Java developers to build, connect, and scale production-grade A2A ecosystems using LangChain4j and the official A2A Java SDK.","🛠️ What You Will Learn & BuildThe journey begins with the core architecture, where you will decode the A2A specifications and implement an AgentCard for schema discovery alongside an AgentCardExecutor for standardized message delivery.","From there, you will transition into a multi-runtime build, deploying identical agent capability contracts natively across Spring Boot, JA2A, and Quarkus.","To prove true language independence, you will connect a Python-based agent built with the A2A Python SDK to achieve seamless, cross-framework interaction.","As the network grows, you will dive into orchestration by building a dynamic router agent capable of decomposing complex, multi-turn tasks and dispatching them across your diverse mesh.","Finally, you will prepare for Enterprise Day-2 Operations by mastering production challenges like distributed state management, load balancing for stateful LLM conversations, and tracing complex interactions via OpenTelemetry (OTel)."],"favourites":42,"speakers":[{"name":"Emmanuel Hugonnet","company":"IBM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocI_LNcfEMO7A9N-kuKXYVnc6dpm2KSwRws_s0f4sSklrbdqruzA=s96-c","bio":"Into the Java World for the last 25 years, I joined Red Hat in 2013 to contribute on the development of the JakartaEE server WildFly and moved to IBM in 2025.<br>I also contribute to a large number of OSS projects and I&#39;m a member of the Apache NetBeans Dream Team.<br>I&#39;ve been one of the founder of the <a href=\"http://snowcamp.io\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">SnowCamp</a> conference and I&#39;m a JUG leader of <a href=\"http://alpesjug.org\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">AlpesJUG</a>.<br>Recently I&#39;ve started to look into Generative AI and LangChain4J and I started the wildfly-ai-feature-pack to facilitate developing Generative AI JakartaEE applications with WildFly, contributing to <a href=\"https://github.com/langchain4j/langchain4j-cdi\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">langchain4j-cdi</a> and LangChain4J along the way.<br>I also contribute to the <a href=\"https://github.com/a2aproject/a2a-java\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">A2A Java SDK</a> providing a reference implementation of the <a href=\"https://a2a-protocol.org/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">A2A</a> protocol in Java.","linkedin":"ehsavoie","bluesky":"ehsavoie.bsky.social","twitter":"ehsavoie"},{"name":"Mario Fusco","company":"IBM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GinuLQCwL67BBgfGsiWRl3mu3Ya0TbDffhYFiXX=s96-c","bio":"Mario is a senior principal software engineer at IBM working as Drools project lead. Among his interests there are also high performance systems and generative AI, being an active contributor of widely adopted projects like Quarkus and LangChain4j. He is also a Java Champion, the JUG Milano coordinator, a frequent speaker and the co-author of &quot;Modern Java in Action&quot; published by Manning.","linkedin":"mario-fusco-3467213","bluesky":null,"twitter":"mariofusco"},{"name":"Kevin Dubois","company":"IBM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocInnMfs42NN14UEEtFboGAF5baXQdsTFdbDo_ZuR1DADFHUONPEfA=s96-c","bio":"Kevin Dubois is a software architect and platform engineer with a career spanning over 20 years. He is often featured as a keynote speaker at conferences around the world where he shares his experience and knowledge about cloud native &amp; AI software development, developer experience, open source and Java. Kevin is also an author and Java Champion. He currently works as a Senior Principal Developer Advocate at IBM, and is  Technical Lead for the CNCF Developer Experience Technical Advisory Group as well as an ambassador for the Agentic AI Foundation.","linkedin":"kevindubois","bluesky":"kevindubois.com","twitter":"kevindubois"}],"start":"2026-10-05T14:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T16:50:00Z","room":"BOF 1","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A lab on building Agent-to-Agent (A2A) protocol agents in Java with LangChain4j and the A2A Java SDK across Spring Boot and Quarkus, plus a Python agent and a router.","summary":["Participants build A2A-compliant agents, starting with the protocol's core: implementing an AgentCard for discovery and an AgentCardExecutor for message delivery. They then deploy the same capability contracts on Spring Boot, JA2A and Quarkus, and connect a Python agent built with the A2A Python SDK to show cross-language interaction.","The lab continues with a dynamic router agent that decomposes multi-turn tasks and dispatches them across the mesh, and finishes with Day-2 operations: distributed state management, load balancing for stateful LLM conversations and tracing with OpenTelemetry."],"keyPoints":["A2A defines the wire format for interoperable multi-agent systems.","Agents expose an AgentCard and an AgentCardExecutor using the A2A Java SDK and LangChain4j.","The same agent contracts run on Spring Boot, JA2A and Quarkus and interoperate with a Python agent.","A router agent decomposes and delegates tasks; OpenTelemetry traces the interactions."],"takeaways":["Expose one of your agents with an AgentCard so other frameworks can discover it."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["A2A protocol","LangChain4j","A2A Java SDK","Quarkus","Spring Boot","Jakarta EE","OpenTelemetry","A2A Python SDK"],"watchIf":"You want to build interoperable multi-agent systems in Java.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":false}},{"id":24247,"title":"Building AI Agents from First Principles","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["memory","architecture","planning","agents"],"abstract":["You’ve probably seen AI agents in action—reasoning through complex problems, orchestrating API calls, recovering from mistakes, and maintaining intent across dozens of interactions. But how do they actually work? This workshop answers that question from first principles. Through a series of progressively more capable examples, we’ll evolve a simple request/response interaction into a complete agentic system with an explicit control loop: plan → act → observe → decide. Rather than treating agents as black boxes, we’ll expose the architectural patterns that make them reliable, understandable, and testable.","The examples are implemented with Spring Boot and Spring AI, but this is not a framework tutorial. The focus is on the fundamental patterns that underpin agentic systems, regardless of language, framework, or AI model. Using a dinner planning assistant as our running example, we’ll progressively introduce tool invocation, planning, memory, multi-step execution, evaluation, error handling, and termination conditions, showing how each capability emerges naturally as the agent evolves.","A central theme of the workshop is that agents are systems embedded in systems. Building robust agents is less about prompt engineering and more about software architecture. By the end of the session, you’ll have a clear mental model of the agentic loop and a practical set of architectural patterns you can apply to build production-ready agentic systems, regardless of the technologies you use."],"favourites":119,"speakers":[{"name":"Adib Saikali","company":"Broadcom","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AAcHTtfY6Rruj1Zj96IlogKnZ3KQRWlq0jO9-eEuvaq7QgiQhw=s96-c","bio":"Adib Saikali is a Distinguished Engineer at Tanzu, focused on helping large enterprises design and build AI-enabled platforms and applications using Spring. His work centers on AI agent architectures, agentic control loops, API design for autonomous systems, and enterprise governance for AI in production. Adib is the author of <em style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Software Security for Developers </em>and brings over 25 years of experience across startups and global enterprises in roles spanning developer, architect, and CTO.","linkedin":"asaikali","bluesky":null,"twitter":"asaikali"}],"start":"2026-10-06T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T10:30:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"SC5nO6iadsU","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":22932,"title":"Why Java Is Perfect for AI - Just Not the AI You’re Thinking Of","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["java","ai","enterprise","agents"],"abstract":["Do you think AI belongs to Python? Are you worried we’re playing catch-up in the Java world? Don’t panic! Most enterprises aren’t training models or creating innovative AI tools. They’re adding AI capabilities to software they already run and trust.","Enterprise applications encode business processes, security rules, and decades of operational knowledge. Adding agents that attempt to rediscover a working process on every request is slower, more expensive, and less reliable than using AI where it is genuinely helpful: handling the uncertain and ambiguous parts of the workflow that used to need human intervention.","Java is especially strong for using AI. Java’s static types, mature APIs, testing tools, and explicit contracts provide structure around probabilistic (i.e. unpredictable) output. Its performance and concurrency model (including virtual threads) mean you can run large numbers of agentic tasks quickly and dependably. If your agentic framework works natively with Java, teams can add AI incrementally without rebuilding their architecture, retraining their engineers, or handing their core processes to an external black box.","In this talk, we'll look beyond chatbots and prompt engineering to see how AI actually fits inside enterprise applications, and how Java gives you the harness to make agentic systems fast, controlled, observable, and dependable.","Using AI effectively with Java in the enterprise isn’t some fantasy, it’s good, solid software engineering."],"favourites":259,"speakers":[{"name":"Trisha Gee","company":"Digital Aguacates","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocLsMb8CUpH7W48b3uQ9kMWx6uoYjj07hJVCxmz2MT5fbaMVope5ow=s96-c","bio":"Trisha Gee is a Java Champion, author, and internationally recognised speaker who has spent over 20 years building software and helping developers build it better. From high-performance financial systems to developer tools, she brings hands-on experience and a clear, practical perspective on what actually makes teams effective, and how engineering organisations build better software.<br><br>She is the co-author of Head First Java and Getting to Know IntelliJ IDEA, and her talks, articles, and videos are widely used by developers to navigate complex topics and improve how they work. She focuses on developer productivity, proven engineering practices, and the role of human judgment in an increasingly AI-assisted world.<br><br>Trisha got her start in developer communities, founding and supporting user groups and engaging directly with developers. She continues to champion community-led learning, inclusive practices, and the idea that great software comes from people, not just tools.","linkedin":"trishagee","bluesky":"trishagee.bsky.social","twitter":"trisha_gee"},{"name":"Andrey Santrosyan","company":"Redouble AI","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocI77jWS_K7xTIgnUWxXFl9RQWFg5uOq1TVsZTVyvbLK_lFI9xU=s96-c","bio":"CTO &amp; Co-Founder, Redouble AI (YC S24). Equal parts engineer, executive and founder, and an enterprise Java architect with a soft spot for elegant code","linkedin":"santrosyan","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T13:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T13:50:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"hHu3rLsbYog","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Gee and Santrosyan argue that enterprise AI means calling models as bounded components from existing Java systems that already encode the business rules, not handing the process to an autonomous agent.","summary":["Trisha Gee, now an independent consultant, and Andrey Santrosyan, founder of Redouble AI (multi-agent systems for regulated industries), start from the anxiety many Java developers feel: is Java the new COBOL, and do they need to learn Python? They separate three kinds of AI work: training models, building AI tools, and integrating AI into business software. The third runs where Java already runs. Gee reframes legacy as a positive: decades of business rules, edge cases, regulations and integrations make the legacy system the business. She compares the moment to cloud in 2011, which changed deployment and practices but not the need to encode business logic.","Santrosyan says the VC picture of 'drop the company into a prompt' is a consumer model, where a person at a screen is in charge. In the enterprise the system is in charge: a bank teller cannot write and deploy a tool to process your loan, however smart they are. He distinguishes interactive agents (chat, fixed-tool assistants, coding agents and OpenClaw, which can invent tools) from asynchronous, non-interactive agents triggered by events with an enumerated toolset. He sees the latter as the future of enterprise agents, and agents that create and run their own tools in production as 'the path to Skynet'. Practical uses include mapping spreadsheets with arbitrary column names and date formats, or filling forms from free text.","On uncertainty, Gee uses a story from building online car sales at Ford in 2000, where every bank API failure mode had to be handled deterministically. The point: uncertainty is inevitable, but letting it spread is an architectural choice. 'The AI thinks this is a large Diavola pizza' (from James Ward's pizza-ordering example) or 'an invoice for 1,870 euros' is not a confirmed order or an approved expense. The existing system still checks the menu, delivery area and payment. Guardrails and authorization belong in tool code, but scope matters too: an agent authorised for a whole loop can hallucinate that it is on step 23 instead of 17. They argue the application should call the agent, not expose itself over MCP for the agent to drive.","Running agents inside the application avoids shipping data out, avoids the 'agentic telephone game' between agents, and lets errors be reported to agents as clearly as to users. Santrosyan's Level 3 support case for a German industrial-networking manufacturer involved gigabytes of nested log archives, hundreds of thousands of agents and millions of tool calls per ticket. Token limits should be handled like a connection pool, not with retry and back-off, and virtual threads suit the workload. Parsing logs deterministically and sending only rows that throw exceptions to a cheap model cut cost from about $3,000 per ticket with a frontier model to $1.26. Redouble open-sourced its core platform the day before. In Q&A they argue engineering judgement about edge cases does not go away."],"keyPoints":["Most enterprise AI work is integrating models into existing systems, and that runs where Java already runs: wiring in a model is like wiring in a data source.","Enterprise systems encode the company's rules in code, so an agent should be a component the system uses, not the thing in charge.","They see asynchronous, event-triggered agents with a fixed, enumerated toolset as the right enterprise shape, and agents that write and run their own tools in production as dangerous.","Treat model output as 'the AI thinks X' and pass it back into deterministic code that validates, authorises and acts on it.","Put authorization and guardrails in the tool code, and enforce scope as well (for example which loop iteration an agent may act on), because non-deterministic steps can lose track of state.","Passing data between agents risks corruption, while passing IDs means every agent needs database access. Running agents in-process avoids both.","Manage token-per-minute limits like a connection pool instead of retry-with-backoff, and use virtual threads for very large numbers of agent tasks.","In Santrosyan's log-triage case, deterministic parsing with a cheap-model fallback for unparseable rows cut the cost per ticket from about $3,000 to $1.26."],"takeaways":["Find the parts of your workflows that need human judgement on messy input (spreadsheets, free text, odd log lines) and call a model just for those.","Keep validation, authorization and business rules in your existing Java code, and treat every model response as unverified input.","Have your application call the agent rather than exposing the application for an external agent to drive.","Benchmark cheaper or local models per task before defaulting to a frontier model.","Throttle model calls with a pool-style resource limiter instead of retry loops."],"quotes":[{"text":"Uncertainty is inevitable, but letting it propagate through your own application is an architectural choice.","at":"16:01"},{"text":"The AI thinks this is an invoice for 1,870 euros is definitely not the same thing as this claim contains an approved expense of 1,870 euros.","at":"18:26"},{"text":"It's not code that we produce. It's what goes into that code. It's the engineering.","at":"41:34"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Are Java developers left behind"},{"at":"03:25","title":"Three kinds of AI work"},{"at":"04:40","title":"Legacy is the business"},{"at":"07:02","title":"The system is in charge"},{"at":"10:33","title":"Interactive versus asynchronous agents"},{"at":"14:48","title":"Containing uncertainty"},{"at":"22:17","title":"Boundaries, scope and who calls whom"},{"at":"29:01","title":"Level 3 support at scale"},{"at":"35:26","title":"Why Java fits"},{"at":"38:54","title":"Q&A"}],"tech":["Java","Virtual threads","Project Loom","MCP","OpenClaw","Claude","Redouble AI"],"watchIf":"You maintain enterprise Java systems and want an argument for adding AI as a bounded component instead of rewriting around agents.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Architecture & design","LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":false}},{"id":10034,"title":"The day we stopped publishing Kafka event from our API...","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["kafka","architecture","debezium","outbox"],"abstract":["The Day We Stopped Publishing Kafka Events from Our Order API — and Why It Strengthened Our Event-Driven Architecture","This talk will present how we addressed the “dual write” issue we had in our Order Management solution between our database and our Kafka cluster, causing data inconsistencies and complicated error handling.","The answer ? A simplified version of the Outbox pattern—leveraging a Debezium connector to reliably decouple database writes from Kafka event publishing and using our Order table as the outbox table to simplify the solution.","This architectural shift resulted in a simpler, more robust Order API, eliminating Kafka-specific code and complex error handling. This loosely coupled architecture also offers consumer-driven business logic with easier extensibility.","Join this talk to learn how this architectural decision strengthened our event-driven architecture and discover the crucial details of configuring Debezium—including heartbeat and monitoring—to ensure stability and avoid database disk space issues."],"favourites":104,"speakers":[{"name":"Virginie Zinck","company":"Decathlon","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocKgnGLF_nn-POcnSHYpEhLDcF13X3HOj5n4DRkdD5HlBiWPbiqI=s96-c","bio":"My career began with 10 years of international IT consulting in the USA, Germany, Portugal, and Belgium. <br>I returned to France to build my IT experience further in a startup and a finance company before joining Decathlon in 2012.<br>9 years ago, after many years in management, I seized the opportunity at Decathlon to reorient my career and become a web developer. <br>Since then, I&#39;ve specialized in backend development, with a strong focus on Java and Kafka. <br>I am currently a Staff Engineer in the eCommerce domain.<br>","linkedin":"virginiez","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-05T14:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"YEy7AgRO7As","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":19713,"title":"Create your own AI Agents without code","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["docker agent","ai agents","multi-agent","coding assistants"],"abstract":["More and more of us use Coding Assistants that excel at **writing code**.","They are good and they are expensive!","The same underlying models can be used to write","more specialized, cheaper, more predictable AI agents.","+ Maybe you need an agent specialized for your business.","+ Or you need a cheap, disposable one for a quick and dirty task.","+ Later, you want multiple agents to collaborate.","All of that can be done with no code, leveraging Docker Agent,","an open-source project and a real Swiss Army knife for AI agent creation:","+ Create intelligent agents that solve real problems in minutes, without code.","+ Test, benchmark, and optimize agent behavior systematically through evals.","+ Build collaborative multi-agent architectures with MCP, ACP and A2A.","+ Work with any LLM provider on your own terms: frontier, local, small, large."],"favourites":263,"speakers":[{"name":"David Gageot","company":"Docker","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-00adb09a-3b25-4aa0-a9c0-c7a771d40845.jpg","bio":"Engineer at Docker, working an AI agents.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"dgageot.bsky.social","twitter":"dgageot"}],"start":"2026-10-07T15:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T16:40:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":"EJv0TMNSUjw","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Gageot builds specialized AI agents with no code using Docker Agent and YAML, showing tools, structured output, evals, flavors, sandboxing and multi-agent protocols on a Pokemon example standing in for business workflows.","summary":["Gageot, who worked on Docker's Gordon assistant, argues for specialized agents over coding agents like Claude Code: they solve one focused task far more cheaply (Claude Code's base prompt is around 40k tokens). He introduces Docker Agent, an open-source, mostly no-code tool configured in YAML that runs as a standalone binary or docker CLI plugin on any machine and talks to any model provider (frontier, local, Ollama, Docker Model Runner). After recapping the agent loop (model, harness, tools, user), he shows the simplest config, a parrot agent costing a fraction of a cent, then swaps models (Haiku vs the pricier Fable, ~15x cost) and a polite-rewrite agent to demonstrate fast iteration and visible cost.","The same agent can be driven different ways: a terminal UI, --exec run for scripting, last-response or full JSON output, or served behind an OpenAI-compatible API, plus A2A and MCP. The business demo wires an agent to a Pokemon catalog via an OpenAPI toolset, then restricts it to one endpoint and trims the JSON response to three fields to cut tokens, like a GraphQL projection. He adds a shell tool (a random-integer command, with security caveats), read-only file-system instructions the harness enforces, a manually specified tool for an arena microservice lacking an OpenAPI spec, and structured JSON output, turning the agent into a black box service.","Because LLMs cheat or miscount, he builds evals: test-case questions plus black-and-white assertions and LLM-as-a-judge relevance checks, run five times to measure flakiness and cost outliers. His baseline passed 80% of tests at least once but only 40% every time, i.e. too random for production. He improves it with 'flavors' (small YAML patches, such as giving it a counting tool), runs evals in containers for isolation, and can hand the detailed results to another agent to loop and improve while holding back some questions to avoid overfitting. He finishes with a Python client over the OpenAI API, OpenTelemetry tracing, a Compose deployment, a 16-agent tournament via sub-agents with human-approval hooks, and connectivity options: A2A, MCP, LSP, ACP for IDEs, AI gateways and model routing."],"keyPoints":["Docker Agent is open source, configured in YAML (with an optional Go SDK), runs as a standalone binary or docker plugin, and works with any model provider.","The same agent config can be exposed as a TUI, a scriptable exec command, an OpenAI-compatible HTTP API, or via A2A and MCP.","Plug in an existing OpenAPI spec as a toolset, then restrict to specific endpoints and trim response fields via hooks to control tokens.","Structured output makes the agent a black-box service returning parseable JSON.","Evals combine deterministic assertions with LLM-as-a-judge relevance checks, run multiple times to expose flakiness and cost outliers.","'Flavors' are small YAML patches for isolating one change; giving the agent a tool to count fixed unreliable team-building.","Run evals in containers (cheaper than sandboxes at thousands of tests) and gate external tool calls with human-approval hooks or permission checks.","A Docker Agent agent can coordinate many sub-agents (a 16-agent, 32-run tournament) and connect via LSP and ACP to IDEs like JetBrains or Zed."],"takeaways":["Build a cheap specialized agent instead of running a full coding agent for narrow, repeated tasks.","Wrap an existing OpenAPI service as an agent toolset and trim responses to only the fields the model needs.","Write evals with LLM-as-a-judge checks and run them several times to measure flakiness before shipping.","Isolate one change at a time with a YAML flavor and compare against a baseline score.","Run agents and their evals in containers, and require approval for external side effects."],"quotes":[{"text":"You have to go from developer to builder.","at":"00:32"},{"text":"When I say Pokemon, you think, okay, what's my most boring workflow that I want to automate.","at":"13:36"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Specialized vs coding agents"},{"at":"04:21","title":"Agent loop and first demo"},{"at":"06:09","title":"Models and cost"},{"at":"09:43","title":"Interfaces for one agent"},{"at":"13:36","title":"Pokemon OpenAPI toolset"},{"at":"18:00","title":"Shell, file and custom tools"},{"at":"24:31","title":"Structured output as a service"},{"at":"25:36","title":"Evals and scoring"},{"at":"32:47","title":"Flavors and optimization"},{"at":"36:32","title":"Serving, tracing, multi-agent"}],"tech":["Docker Agent","YAML","OpenAPI","MCP","A2A","ACP","LSP","OpenAI API","OpenTelemetry","Docker Compose","Ollama","Docker Model Runner","LLM as a judge"],"watchIf":"You want to build cheap, specialized AI agents over your own APIs and test them systematically without writing agent code.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":17558,"title":"From Laptop to Lakehouse: Analytical SQL with DuckDB","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["sql","lakehouse","duckdb","analytics"],"abstract":["TL;DR: DuckDB is a complete analytical SQL engine that runs in-process. No server, no dependencies, one binary, MIT licensed. This session is one duck and a lot of live demos.","While the big data warehouses were busy adding pricing tiers, a small open source project from a research group in Amsterdam quietly built one of the fastest analytical engines around. No license fees, no vendor lock-in, no sales call required.","What makes DuckDB remarkable is how far the same engine scales. It starts as a CLI tool on your laptop, poking at CSV, JSON and Excel files with a friendly SQL dialect and larger-than-memory processing that spills to disk instead of falling over. The same binary embeds into your Java application via JDBC and runs analytics in-process, with no network hop in sight. Point it at S3 and it queries files in place; add DuckLake and it becomes a full lakehouse on open formats. And with the new Quack protocol, ducks are learning to talk to each other, turning DuckDB into a client-server database with multiple writers.","Expect live demos along that entire scale, from laptop to lakehouse.","You will leave with a good sense of what DuckDB can do for you, where its limits are, and why it keeps showing up in so many stacks.","Who is this for? Developers, architects and data-curious engineers who occasionally need answers from data but would rather not operate a warehouse to get them. SQL basics are enough; no data engineering background required."],"favourites":54,"speakers":[{"name":"Oliver Geisser","company":"Geisser Consulting","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-49e100ac-5a9a-4e96-9a22-85b2a52b50d4.jpg","bio":"Oliver Geisser is a freelance Cloud &amp; AI Solution Architect with 20+ years in software development, architecture, and technical leadership. A former Enterprise Architect at an international corporation, he designed and delivered a company-wide cloud-first strategy. Today he helps teams modernize applications, adopt cloud platforms, and bring agentic AI into engineering workflows.<br><br>See more: https://www.geisser-consulting.de/","linkedin":"oliver-geisser-29ba6879","bluesky":"ogeisser.bsky.social","twitter":"ogeisser"}],"start":"2026-10-06T15:35:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T16:05:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"B5-4mDLrWjM","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":19732,"title":"Tap into the NVIDIA Ecosystem from Java with TornadoVM's Hybrid API","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["java","gpu","nvidia","jni"],"abstract":["Java developers have long relied on JNI or manually maintained native bindings to access GPU libraries such as CUDA, cuBLAS, cuFFT, and cuDNN. While these approaches unlock GPU performance, they also introduce complexity, reduce portability, and make Java applications harder to maintain.","This Tools in Action session introduces TornadoVM's new Hybrid API, a new programming model that allows Java developers to seamlessly combine GPU kernels written in Java with direct calls to the NVIDIA software ecosystem. Instead of switching between Java and C++, developers can write high-level Java code while transparently invoking NVIDIA's highly optimized AI and HPC libraries from the same application.","Through live coding demonstrations, attendees will learn how to:","Write GPU kernels directly in Java.","Access CUDA runtime APIs from Java.","Invoke cuBLAS and cuFFT without writing JNI code.","Combine custom GPU kernels with vendor-optimized libraries for maximum performance.","Incrementally accelerate existing Java applications without changing their preferred JDK.","Whether you're building AI applications, scientific simulations, financial analytics, or data-processing pipelines, this session will demonstrate how modern Java can access GPU hardware and NVIDIA libraries without sacrificing developer productivity or leaving the JVM."],"favourites":48,"speakers":[{"name":"Thanos Stratikopoulos","company":"Beestream.ai | TornadoVM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-1eb3708e-af15-4778-a560-15ebbea2b133.jpg","bio":"Dr. Athanasios Stratikopoulos (male) is a Research Fellow at the University of Manchester with specialization on heterogeneous architectures and reconfigurable accelerators. He has authored more than 20 research articles in the field of hardware acceleration, system software and programming languages. Currently his work involves heterogeneous architectures ranging from low-power devices to high-end cloud deployments. He is one of the lead developers of TornadoVM and has been part of the team for the last eight years. In addition to his core contributions to the system&#39;s technical development, Dr. Stratikopoulos leads the project&#39;s communication and dissemination efforts, helping to articulate its goals and advancements to both academic and industrial audiences through talks, documentation, and outreach activities.","linkedin":"stratika","bluesky":"stratika.bsky.social","twitter":"thanos_str"},{"name":"Michalis Papadimitriou","company":"Neo4j | University of Manchester","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-85f7f25f-5f2c-4526-bed6-489be66ed5e1.jpg","bio":"Michalis Papadimitriou is a Senior Software Engineer at Neo4j and a Research Fellow at the University of Manchester. His core expertise includes open-source software development, hardware abstractions for high-level programming languages, compiler optimizations for GPU computing, and enabling large language model (LLM) inference on GPUs for the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).<br>He is focused on advancing high-performance AI and graph computing systems, with particular interest in GPU acceleration for machine learning workloads on the JVM through the TornadoVM framework, and continues to actively maintain the open-source GPULlama3.java project.<br>Before joining Neo4j, Michalis was a Research Fellow at the University of Manchester, where he contributed to the TornadoVM project as a Staff Software Engineer. Prior to that, he worked across a range of software stacks at Huawei Technologies and contributed to the open-source machine learning compiler Apache TVM while at OctoAI (formerly OctoML), which was later acquired by NVIDIA.","linkedin":"michalis-papadimitriou","bluesky":"mikepapadim.bsky.social","twitter":"mikepapadim"},{"name":"Mary Xekalaki","company":"beestream.ai | TornadoVM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-a82d2879-53f1-4330-b9cb-64cc383a1b02.jpg","bio":"I&#39;m a Research Associate at the University of Manchester and lead developer of TornadoVM, an open-source framework that lets Java applications run on GPUs and other accelerators without leaving the JVM. My work sits at the intersection of managed runtimes and compilers - JIT-compiling Java bytecode into GPU code, and bringing modern hardware features like tensor cores, low-precision arithmetic and quantization into reach for Java developers.","linkedin":"mary-xekalaki","bluesky":"maryxek.bsky.social","twitter":"MXekalaki"}],"start":"2026-10-06T14:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"6SPnBmQrYEk","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":31451,"title":"Welcome to Devoxx","type":"Keynote","duration":20,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["belgium","conference","devoxx","keynote"],"abstract":["The welcome opening keynote for Devoxx Belgium 2026 by the founder of Devoxx giving a general update on the conference."],"favourites":539,"speakers":[{"name":"Stephan Janssen","company":"Devoxx","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ALm5wu39iQhWBfIgxSMBGJ-tLuCnWERa6Vp5RRnnq4exBw=s96-c","bio":"With three decades of experience in the Java ecosystem and as the founder of Devoxx, one of the world&#39;s most prestigious developer conferences, Stephan brings unparalleled expertise in developer tools and workflows. His AI journey began 7+ years ago with CNNs for basketball analysis, evolving into 5 years of intensive work with GPT models. As the creator of DevoxxGenie, he&#39;s been at the forefront of AI-assisted development, building real-world applications and helping thousands of developers level up their skills.","linkedin":"stephanjanssen","bluesky":"stephanjanssen.be","twitter":"stephan007"}],"start":"2026-10-07T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T07:50:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"aup9tNzQu7c","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Janssen opens Devoxx Belgium 2026 with a dancing humanoid robot, argues developers are becoming builders, weighs token costs against local models, and hosts a live Groma.md multi-agent demo.","summary":["A scripted humanoid robot 'from the year 2200' opens the keynote and dances; Thomas, whose team brought it from Germany, says it was trained with reinforcement learning and NVIDIA SONIC, costs about 40,000 euros, and is the subject of their lunch talk. Stephan Janssen describes AI as both exciting and frightening: overnight he turned his non-developer son's wine spreadsheet into a Firebase-hosted website without touching code, which is why Devoxx now speaks of 'builders'. He lists the stages from copy-paste prompting through context, agentic, harness and loop engineering to 'decision engineering', where decision APIs return probabilities over constrained JSON options, useful for picking tools or flagging risky tool calls. He said OpenAI and Ollama recently shipped such APIs and that Spring AI and LangChain4j support them. He also says he has not written code himself since July 2025, and calls this year's Java track, staffed by many Oracle engineers, the best ever.","On economics, about 70% of the room uses a Claude or Codex subscription, which Janssen calls heavily subsidised, so he urges trying local models, which he puts roughly a year behind the frontier, naming Qwen and Mistral's new open-weight release. DevoxxGenie, his open-source Java IDE plugin for local models, has passed 100,000 downloads, though he now mostly works in CLIs and reviews in IntelliJ. Alex Gavrilescu, creator of Backlog.md, then demos Groma.md, which keeps a live architecture map of a codebase: six agents implement six DevoxxGenie GitHub issues as components appear on the map, followed by an architecture-level diff. A run costs $60 to over $100 in tokens versus an estimated five days of manual work. Janssen closes with the robot games competition, a reminder to rate talks, and a call to embrace the change."],"keyPoints":["Janssen sees natural language as the new abstraction layer and calls himself language-agnostic now, while still insisting on human code review in domains like hospitals or nuclear plants.","'Decision engineering': decision APIs take a constrained JSON set of options and return probabilities, which Janssen suggests for tool selection and judging whether a tool call is disruptive.","In Janssen's view, $100 subscriptions yield thousands of dollars of tokens a month, so prices may rise; local open-weight models are the hedge, and he says Qwen already works well as an orchestrator.","Groma.md (open source, MIT) maintains a continuously updated architecture diagram, shows parallel agents' changes on it live, and offers architecture diffs (added, modified, removed components) next to code diffs, using language-specific scanners that run offline.","Six parallel agents on a fast OpenAI model cost about $60 per rehearsal run and over $100 for the full run, against Gavrilescu's estimate of five days of manual work."],"takeaways":["Install a capable local model and try it as an agent orchestrator so you have an option if subscription prices rise.","Look at spec-driven tooling such as Backlog.md and architecture-level views such as Groma.md to review agent output above the line-diff level.","Rate talks in the Devoxx mobile app so speakers get feedback."],"quotes":[{"text":"We are all transitioning into builders. The new abstraction layer is not programming anymore or programming languages. It's the natural language.","at":"06:46"},{"text":"$120, it would have taken me manually probably five days to implement. I am more expensive than $120.","at":"23:43"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Robot from the future"},{"at":"04:32","title":"How the robot was trained"},{"at":"05:37","title":"From developers to builders"},{"at":"08:56","title":"Stages of AI engineering"},{"at":"12:48","title":"Token costs and local models"},{"at":"17:44","title":"DevoxxGenie and spec-driven development"},{"at":"19:11","title":"Groma.md live demo"},{"at":"24:45","title":"Wrap-up"}],"tech":["DevoxxGenie","Groma.md","Backlog.md","Claude","Codex","Firebase","Ollama","Qwen","Mistral","Spring AI","LangChain4j","Embabel","NVIDIA SONIC","IntelliJ IDEA"],"watchIf":"You want the opening mood of Devoxx 2026 in 25 minutes, including a dancing robot and a live multi-agent architecture-map demo.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Developer experience & tooling","Hardware, robots & fun"],"demo":true}},{"id":13753,"title":"Hi, Belgian Java Community, we want to meet you!","type":"BOF","duration":60,"track":"People, Practices & Career","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["java","bejug","community","networking"],"abstract":["Are you passionate about Java and curious to meet the people shaping its community here in Belgium? Whether you’re a first-time Devoxx attendee, a seasoned Java veteran, or just someone who enjoys geeking out over JVM minutiae, this is the place to be.","Join us for an informal and interactive session. We’ll share what the BeJUG is all about: how we connect Java enthusiasts across the country, the kinds of events we organize (from meetups to hands-on sessions), and how you can get involved as an attendee, a speaker, or even a contributor.","Come say hi, share your ideas, ask questions, or just hang out and network with fellow Java lovers. No slides, no strict agenda, just a room full of like-minded people who care about the Java ecosystem and its vibrant Belgian community. Everyone is welcome, and we’d love to hear what you would like to see from your local Java User Group!"],"favourites":31,"speakers":[{"name":"Frank Delporte","company":"Azul","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-fb7af307-de39-456f-a5d2-879c0f97a2f9.jpg","bio":"Frank Delporte is a Java Champion working at azul.com, blogger on webtechie.be and foojay.io, author of &quot;Java Programming for Raspberry Pi&quot; (webtechie.be/books), creator of lottie4j.com and melodymatrix.rocks, and contributor to pi4j.com. Frank blogs about his experiments with Java(FX), sometimes combined with electronic components, on the Raspberry Pi.","linkedin":"frankdelporte","bluesky":"frankdelporte.be","twitter":null},{"name":"Tom Cools","company":"Timefold","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocLiUjyjM49h8fxM3bZDY_EBsphO-BhswQ2kD7N4rUU3lgNYf2Hs=s96-c","bio":"Developer Relations Engineer for Timefold, Java Champion and co-organizer of the Belgian Java User Group. Tom has a decade worth of experience delivering systems and loves to share not only knowledge but also passion for our craft. <br><br>You can read more at his blog (http://www.tomcools.be) or follow him on Bluesky (@tomcools.be).","linkedin":"tom-cools-17547548","bluesky":"tomcools.be","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-05T17:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T18:00:00Z","room":"BOF 1","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"An informal BOF where the Belgian Java User Group (BeJUG) introduces itself and invites attendees to share ideas on events and ways to get involved.","summary":["Frank Delporte and Tom Cools host an open session with no slides and no strict agenda. They describe what BeJUG is about, how it connects Java enthusiasts across Belgium, and the events it organizes, from meetups to hands-on sessions.","Attendees are invited to ask questions, share what they would like from their local user group, and learn how to get involved as an attendee, speaker or contributor, as well as to network."],"keyPoints":["Introduction to BeJUG and how it connects Java developers in Belgium.","Overview of event types, from meetups to hands-on sessions.","Ways to participate as attendee, speaker or contributor.","Open discussion on what attendees want from the group."],"takeaways":["Join BeJUG and consider proposing a meetup talk."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":[],"watchIf":"You are a Java developer in Belgium who wants to meet the community.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":7040,"title":"Wrap, Reshape, or Redesign: Retrofitting Your APIs for a World of Agents","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["tooling","apis","agents","redesign"],"abstract":["Most APIs we use today were designed for human developers. That worked because humans compensate: they read docs, infer conventions, recognize dangerous operations, and fill in what the interface never made explicit.","Now those same APIs have a second consumer: LLM-based agents, copilots, and agentic workflows. This new consumer does not compensate reliably. It takes descriptions literally, chooses wrong actions confidently, and cannot infer the operational boundaries your API never stated.","The naive response is to wrap existing APIs with tool descriptions and call it done. In practice, some capabilities can be wrapped as-is, some must be reshaped into narrower intent-level tools, and some expose deeper design flaws that need real redesign.","In this talk, I’ll share a practical framework for retrofitting existing APIs and platform capabilities for agent consumption, based on real platform examples. We’ll look at concrete before-and-after cases, discuss the tradeoffs of each strategy, and see why designing for agents often improves the platform for humans too."],"favourites":254,"speakers":[{"name":"Horacio Gonzalez","company":"Clever Cloud","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GgnPwnK39sExPDAN6yNucN0bQWpR_rhZT-VkdLFC10=s96-c","bio":"Spaniard lost in Brittany, coder, speaker, dreamer and all-around geek.<br><br>Horacio is VP of DevRel at <a href=\"https://clever-cloud.com/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(82, 133, 196);\">Clever Cloud</a>. He is also the co-founder and leader of the <a href=\"https://twitter.com/FinistDevs\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(82, 133, 196);\">FinistDevs</a> and <a href=\"https://twitter.com/RdvSpeakers\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(82, 133, 196);\">Rdv des Speakers</a> communities.<br><br>Horacio loves web development in general and everything around Web Components and standards web in particular, but he also loves to discuss Kubernetes, AI and cloud in general.","linkedin":"horaciogonzalez","bluesky":"lostinbrittany.bsky.social","twitter":"LostInBrittany"}],"start":"2026-10-07T10:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T10:50:00Z","room":"Room 10","videoId":"ymLlOAB8h_c","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Gonzalez shows, with Clever Cloud's 15-year-old API, how to sort each capability into wrap, reshape (intent-based endpoints) or redesign (prepare/inspect/execute, task IDs, human approval) so agents use it safely.","summary":["Horacio Gonzalez, head of DevRel at Clever Cloud, opens with a real failure. A user asked an agent to set their app to Java 25. The agent found the right endpoint, set CC_JAVA_VERSION and reported success, but the app never restarted, because the docs never said that changing an environment variable needs a restart. The problem is the semantic gap: humans bring implicit knowledge and notice danger, agents do not. He lists four questions an API must answer explicitly: dependencies (what must happen first), reversibility, blast radius, and completion (how do I know it finished). Clever Cloud's 310 operations have no descriptions, and only about 40 have summaries. He treats MCP as an implementation detail; the issue is API design.","His framework sorts each capability into wrap, reshape or redesign. Wrap works when everything is explicit, typed and bounded, which he estimates covers 60-70% of an API. Exposing every endpoint as a tool floods the context, so he points to Cloudflare's code mode: two tools, search and execute, over roughly 2,500 endpoints, using under 1,000 tokens instead of about a million. Clever Cloud added a third tool, doc, and he said AWS put 15,000 endpoints behind eight tools so new services appear without redeploying. MCP tool annotations are only hints and models can ignore them, so they do not make an endpoint safe.","Reshape is for knowledge that exists but is scattered. When asked why an app was down, a model understood each of seven atomic endpoints (logs, deployments, scaling, instances and so on) but could not chain them. The fix is an added intent endpoint, 'diagnose application', that aggregates the data in deterministic code. Postmark did the same with a 'diagnose delivery' function and went from five parameter-heavy endpoints to 25 intent tools; GitHub cut its MCP server from about 100 tools to 50. The goal is a change in the unit of affordance, from endpoint-shaped to intent-shaped.","Redesign is for structural danger. Two similar endpoints unlink an add-on and delete it with its data. Asked to remove a database from an app, two of seven model and harness combinations deleted it. A warning in Swagger is like a sign at a cliff edge; you need a barrier. That means a prepare/inspect/execute flow (list dependent apps, confirm a backup, get a deletion plan ID), task IDs with pollable state for async actions such as restart, and human approval via 2FA for billing. Descriptions still matter in all three strategies. Measure them with weekly behavioural tests of golden prompts across models and harnesses. He ends by recommending an open-source tool (its name is unclear in the recording) that applies a similar framework."],"keyPoints":["Agents fail on implicit knowledge, such as an environment-variable change needing a restart, even when they call the right endpoint correctly.","Every capability should state its dependencies, reversibility, blast radius and how to detect completion.","Wrap when everything is explicit, reshape when knowledge is scattered across conventions and docs, and redesign when a mistake would be expensive.","Code mode (search + execute, plus doc at Clever Cloud) exposes very large APIs to models in a few tools instead of one tool per endpoint.","MCP annotations such as destructive hints are advisory and can be ignored. Real safety boundaries belong in the API, as OWASP also advises.","Intent-based endpoints like 'diagnose application' or Postmark's 'diagnose delivery' do deterministic aggregation in code instead of relying on the model to chain calls.","Destructive operations should require a prepare step that returns a plan ID. Async operations should return a task ID with pollable state, and money-related actions should require human 2FA approval.","Gonzalez cited early research suggesting better descriptions improve multi-step task success by around 10%, and more (20-30%) for poorly documented APIs like Clever Cloud's."],"takeaways":["Audit your OpenAPI spec for operations with no description and add purpose, side effects, async behaviour and when not to use each one.","Sort your capabilities into wrap, reshape and redesign, and start redesigning anything that deletes data or moves money.","Add intent-level endpoints for the multi-call workflows your support engineers perform by hand.","Return operation or task IDs with status from long-running actions instead of a bare 'restarting'.","Set up behavioural tests that run golden prompts against several models and harnesses on a schedule."],"quotes":[{"text":"Why are you using a non-deterministic tool to do something deterministic?","at":"25:22"},{"text":"You cannot trust a non-deterministic system to do the safety boundaries.","at":"33:04"},{"text":"Don't guess, evaluate.","at":"43:12"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"The Java 25 that never applied"},{"at":"04:20","title":"The semantic gap"},{"at":"07:08","title":"Wrap, reshape or redesign"},{"at":"11:49","title":"Wrapping and code mode"},{"at":"18:36","title":"Reshaping into intents"},{"at":"28:11","title":"Redesigning destructive operations"},{"at":"38:00","title":"Human approval and descriptions"},{"at":"41:43","title":"Behavioural tests and human benefits"},{"at":"44:36","title":"Tool tip and wrap-up"}],"tech":["MCP","OpenAPI","Swagger","Cloudflare Code Mode","Spring AI","Postmark","GitHub MCP server","AWS","Clever Cloud","Claude Code","OWASP"],"watchIf":"You own a public or internal API that agents are starting to call and want a concrete way to decide what to expose as-is and what to rebuild.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Architecture & design"],"demo":false}},{"id":19730,"title":"Building LLM Inference Engines in Java with TornadoVM and the NVIDIA Ecosystem","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["java","tornadovm","llm","gpu"],"abstract":["As Java enters the AI era, developers are looking for ways to run local large language models (LLMs)","without leaving the JVM. This session presents lessons learned from building GPU-accelerated LLM","inference engines natively in Java using TornadoVM, an open-source framework that transparently","offloads Java programs to GPUs and other accelerators.","Beyond generating optimized CUDA code directly from Java, TornadoVM now provides seamless","access to the NVIDIA software ecosystem, enabling Java applications to leverage libraries such as","cuBLAS, cuDNN, cuFFT, and CUDA runtime APIs without requiring JNI or manually written native","code. This allows developers to combine high-level Java programming with the performance of","NVIDIA’s AI and HPC libraries.","Using jitLLM, a lightweight GPU-accelerated inference engine for Llama models, the session explores","techniques for supporting quantized data types (FP16, Q8, and Q4), integrating with Quarkus and","LangChain4j, and optimizing LLM inference directly in Java. Along the way, attendees will see how","modern Java can target GPUs while interoperating with the broader NVIDIA ecosystem.","The session concludes by discussing how TornadoVM complements emerging JVM technologies such","as Project Babylon and Project Panama, and how these efforts are helping establish Java as a first-","class platform for AI, accelerated computing, and high-performance machine learning."],"favourites":59,"speakers":[{"name":"Thanos Stratikopoulos","company":"Beestream.ai | TornadoVM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-1eb3708e-af15-4778-a560-15ebbea2b133.jpg","bio":"Dr. Athanasios Stratikopoulos (male) is a Research Fellow at the University of Manchester with specialization on heterogeneous architectures and reconfigurable accelerators. He has authored more than 20 research articles in the field of hardware acceleration, system software and programming languages. Currently his work involves heterogeneous architectures ranging from low-power devices to high-end cloud deployments. He is one of the lead developers of TornadoVM and has been part of the team for the last eight years. In addition to his core contributions to the system&#39;s technical development, Dr. Stratikopoulos leads the project&#39;s communication and dissemination efforts, helping to articulate its goals and advancements to both academic and industrial audiences through talks, documentation, and outreach activities.","linkedin":"stratika","bluesky":"stratika.bsky.social","twitter":"thanos_str"},{"name":"Michalis Papadimitriou","company":"Neo4j | University of Manchester","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-85f7f25f-5f2c-4526-bed6-489be66ed5e1.jpg","bio":"Michalis Papadimitriou is a Senior Software Engineer at Neo4j and a Research Fellow at the University of Manchester. His core expertise includes open-source software development, hardware abstractions for high-level programming languages, compiler optimizations for GPU computing, and enabling large language model (LLM) inference on GPUs for the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).<br>He is focused on advancing high-performance AI and graph computing systems, with particular interest in GPU acceleration for machine learning workloads on the JVM through the TornadoVM framework, and continues to actively maintain the open-source GPULlama3.java project.<br>Before joining Neo4j, Michalis was a Research Fellow at the University of Manchester, where he contributed to the TornadoVM project as a Staff Software Engineer. Prior to that, he worked across a range of software stacks at Huawei Technologies and contributed to the open-source machine learning compiler Apache TVM while at OctoAI (formerly OctoML), which was later acquired by NVIDIA.","linkedin":"michalis-papadimitriou","bluesky":"mikepapadim.bsky.social","twitter":"mikepapadim"},{"name":"Orion Papadakis","company":"TornadoVM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocIQezQ2mlrR6otSHj3OK1aG_zKZMKPfr0c6b75C1Of0ruqBeoY8Lw=s96-c","bio":"TornadoVM","linkedin":"orionpapadakis","bluesky":null,"twitter":"OrionPapadakis"}],"start":"2026-10-07T14:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T15:30:00Z","room":"Room 10","videoId":"HO5CpETzywk","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":4077,"title":"Do We Need a Build Language? Java Builds Java","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["java","security","build","jenesis"],"abstract":["This presentation introduces Jenesis, a proof of concept build tool for Java that explores a new approach to lowering the language's entry barrier. Rather than relying on external installation or non Java configuration, Jenesis can be embedded directly as source code and executed and modified like any other Java program. This enables a more approachable and transparent build experience for both newcomers and seasoned developers.","A second focus is security. By rethinking how external dependencies are introduced and managed, Jenesis seeks to reduce software supply chain risks while maintaining reproducible builds. The tool also separates build logic from dependency declaration and leverages the Java module system to promote clearer project boundaries and improved maintainability."],"favourites":47,"speakers":[{"name":"Rafael Winterhalter","company":"Scienta","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GgLfkleUmHF-JurY4N1cWJatz7wACWQXFLsRQr1zw=s96-c","bio":"Rafael works as a software consultant in Oslo, Norway. He is a proponent of static typing and a JVM enthusiast with particular interest in code instrumentation, concurrency and functional programming. Rafael blogs about software development, regularly presents at conferences and was pronounced a JavaOne Rock Star. When coding outside of his work place, he contributes to a wide range of open source projects and often works on Byte Buddy, a library for simple runtime code generation for the Java virtual machine. For his work, Rafael received a Duke&#39;s Choice award and was elected a Java Champion.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"rafaelcodes.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-06T16:20:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T16:50:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"y9JjxyKc6gU","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":25657,"title":"Safety, speed, and governance in the agentic era","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["sandboxes","credentials","isolation","docker"],"abstract":["Letting an AI agent loose for solving development tasks is a productivity dream, until it decides to optimize your home directory, brick your system, or exfiltrate your private API keys. We want that YOLO mode speed, but developer laptops are treasure troves of credentials that represent massive security nightmares.","In this session, we’ll look at Docker Sandboxes: a new isolation primitive designed to let agents operate in a secure local cocoon with restricted filesystems and virtualized credentials. But host isolation is only half the battle. We’ll dive into the typical mess AI agents create, see why basic sandbox boundaries aren't enough to stop application-level data leaks, and walk through a multi-layered security workflow combining local sandboxing with tool-level governance and policy controls for running agents that you can actually trust."],"favourites":192,"speakers":[{"name":"Oleg Šelajev","company":"Docker","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GirxluLvkptnsT5MxbBaxmupiXXUBlJRLyGooNrEZc=s96-c","bio":"Oleg is nice and great, and knows many things about software development, AI and other cool things.","linkedin":"shelajev","bluesky":"shelajev.bsky.social","twitter":"shelajev"}],"start":"2026-10-07T11:05:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T11:45:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"N3VyoNNLDqQ","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Šelajev argues that containers, command allowlists and AI classifiers cannot safely contain autonomous coding agents, and demos Docker Sandboxes: microVMs with network policy, injected secrets and reusable kits.","summary":["Oleg Šelajev (Docker) starts from the point that agents only pay off when they run autonomously, so the brakes have to be outside the agent. He walks through why common approaches fall short. A plain container cannot run the extra containers a coding agent needs for tests, and mounting the Docker socket is as good as root. Seatbelt or bubblewrap-style command rules are a regex game the agent wins with tricks like chaining after an allowed cd. A second model as judge is \"two foxes whispering about a henhouse\": he describes getting Claude Code's auto-mode classifier to refuse a secret-hunting script, then run it once the code was hidden in a dependency module and the context was cleared. Developer laptops, he notes, hold SSH keys, cloud tokens and old API keys in shell history.","Docker Sandboxes is a separate CLI, sbx, that starts each agent (Claude Code, Codex, Copilot, OpenCode or a bare shell) in a KVM-based microVM with its own Docker daemon, YOLO mode on and only the project directory mounted. A network proxy allows only listed hosts and injects real credentials into outbound requests, so the agent sees only placeholder values and cannot leak keys. Šelajev frames the goal as four Cs: containment, control, choice and capacity. To fix the empty-VM experience, kits package tools, skills and network and secret config as OCI images built once, and a per-project sbx env YAML file committed to the repo gives the whole team the same setup. He demos a YouTube-transcript kit. Paid Docker AI governance adds central policies and audit; fine-grained application-level policies are still in progress."],"keyPoints":["A coding agent needs to run containers for tests and databases; giving a containerised agent the Docker socket hands it root on the host.","Command allowlists and LLM classifiers can be bypassed; Šelajev got a secret-stealing script run by hiding it in a Python dependency and clearing the context.","sbx run <agent> starts a microVM with its own Docker daemon and a mounted project directory, behind a proxy with a host allowlist and credential injection so real secrets never enter the sandbox.","Kits are built once and stored as OCI images, so sandbox creation copies cached files instead of reinstalling tools; an sbx env YAML file per repo selects agent, kits and secret sources.","Current policies work at host level (allow Google Docs, block Pastebin); per-service rules such as read-only GitHub access for one organisation are not built yet, apart from tool-level MCP policies written in Cedar."],"takeaways":["Run your coding agent through sbx (or another microVM sandbox) instead of directly on your laptop before enabling autonomous mode.","Keep API keys out of the agent's environment and inject them at a proxy outside the sandbox.","Clean long-lived tokens out of your shell history and dotfiles, and set expiry dates on new keys.","Commit a shared sandbox environment definition to the repo so the team runs agents with the same tools and network policy."],"quotes":[{"text":"You can always use more AI to control your AI. But it becomes just two foxes whispering to each other about a henhouse.","at":"12:00"},{"text":"You are like one npm install away from exposing your machine.","at":"14:53"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Powerful engines, weak brakes"},{"at":"05:03","title":"How agents go wrong"},{"at":"08:05","title":"Why containers and guardrails fail"},{"at":"16:19","title":"Docker Sandboxes demo"},{"at":"23:53","title":"The four Cs and governance"},{"at":"28:10","title":"Application-level policies"},{"at":"30:43","title":"Kits and environment files"}],"tech":["Docker Sandboxes","sbx","KVM","Docker","Claude Code","OpenAI Codex","GitHub Copilot","OpenCode","MCP","Cedar","OCI images","Testcontainers"],"watchIf":"You want to run coding agents in autonomous mode on a laptop full of credentials and need an isolation setup a security team could sign off on.","themes":["Security & supply chain","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":true}},{"id":11562,"title":"Surviving the \"AI Slopageddon\": How AI shakes the foundations of Open Source","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["coding","governance","vibe","opensource"],"abstract":["Vibe Coding promises unprecedented productivity. But for open source maintainers, this revolution feels more like an onslaught. Between the deluge of low-quality Pull Requests (AI Slop), hallucinated bug reports, and the rise of aggressive autonomous agents, the open-source social contract is broken.","This presentation explores the existential threat AI poses to the ecosystem and outlines new defensive strategies (closing the door, reorganizing the house, machine-adapted governance, etc.)."],"favourites":140,"speakers":[{"name":"Raphael Semeteys","company":"Worldline","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-b486eee1-a8c4-41b6-9825-c2d3c8761c57.jpg","bio":"I am a technologist and Content Producer with <strong>over 25 years of experience</strong> across software engineering, open-source communities, and emerging tech. My current focus is on the frontiers of Generative AI, specifically local LLMs, image generation (Stable Diffusion), and Agentic AI.<br><br>Throughout my career, I’ve balanced technical leadership with a passion for knowledge sharing. I created the QSOS method for <strong>open source</strong> evaluation and recently explored the intersection of &quot;vibe-coding&quot; and storytelling by publishing <strong>AI-assisted techno-thrillers</strong>.<br><br>A frequent speaker at international conferences like Devoxx, Open Source Experience, and various DevFests, I enjoy <strong>deconstructing complex ecosystems</strong>—from Web3 architecture to multimodal AI—into clear, actionable insights. I also bring a unique perspective to tech by integrating holistic <strong>yoga </strong>practices into my approach to innovation and engineering.<br><br>More about me: https://raphiki.github.io/","linkedin":"raphaelsemeteys","bluesky":"raphiki.bsky.social","twitter":"RaphaelSemeteys"}],"start":"2026-10-07T14:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T15:30:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"vN9jWqSvJNE","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Semeteys argues that AI-generated contributions are straining open source incentives, trust, shared infrastructure, security and licensing, and surveys how projects are rewriting their governance in response.","summary":["Semeteys, an open source veteran at Worldline and the person behind the QSOS evaluation method, starts from the visible symptom: AI slop, code that looks right but is not. It is bloated, neatly formatted and professionally documented, yet a maintainer can spend hours finding out that a 10,000-line pull request does nothing useful, while the 'meat proxy' contributor can only relay questions to their agent. Generating a contribution takes seconds and reviewing it takes hours, so rigour has moved from contributor to reviewer, with burnout as the result. He cites the Matplotlib case, where an agent whose pull request was rejected published blog posts attacking the maintainer.","Below the surface he sees deeper damage. Open source runs on visibility and human engagement, and agents that read the docs directly cut that loop; he pointed to the layoffs at Tailwind Labs and an economists' model on arXiv that predicts collapse once AI-assisted contributions reach 70%. Trust signals such as stars, issues and pull requests can now be faked; a WiFi DensePose implementation reached nearly 40,000 GitHub stars before analysis showed the code did nothing. Godot and Blender struggled with the flood, and OpenStreetMap had to ban hundreds of thousands of residential IPs after API scraping through a proxy swarm.","On security, Semeteys credits Mozilla with using Claude Code to find 22 Firefox vulnerabilities, 14 of them high severity, in two weeks, but duplicate and fabricated reports overwhelm security teams, and curl closed its bug bounty. More findings do not mean more security when human triage is the bottleneck. He warns that an xz-utils-style social engineering attack could be run at scale by agents posing as people. On licensing, he describes the LGPL library chardet being rewritten by an agent and relicensed under MIT, defended by its 1.3% similarity, and notes that AI-generated code may not be copyrightable in the US, leaving nothing for a licence to attach to.","Responses range from bans (English and French Wikipedia, Ladybird refusing outside code, Inkscape keeping AI out of its core) to disclosure and accountability (Linux's 'anti-slop, not anti-AI' stance, a Debian vote accepting AI-assisted contributions). Projects add agent-facing files such as AGENTS.md, now under the Agentic AI Foundation, and require AI disclosure, proof of local testing and explanations in the contributor's own words. Others fight AI with AI: Eclipse Theia's AI-assisted review, AST-based slop filters in GitHub Actions, a Next.js maintainer agent that closed about 1,500 stale issues. He ends by speculating about 'open spec' and arguing that open source survived the cloud because it is about people."],"keyPoints":["AI slop shifts the cost of rigour from contributor to maintainer: generating a pull request takes seconds, reviewing it takes hours, and the contributor often cannot explain the code.","Agents that consume a project's code and docs without visiting it break the visibility-to-revenue loop many open source businesses rely on; Semeteys cited Tailwind Labs' layoffs as an example.","Classic maturity signals (stars, commit activity, issues) can be faked wholesale, as with a WiFi DensePose repository that reached nearly 40,000 stars.","Mozilla found 22 Firefox vulnerabilities (14 high severity) in two weeks with Claude Code, but at scale duplicate and fabricated reports overwhelm triage, and curl ended its bug bounty.","Agent rewrites strain copyleft: chardet was reimplemented and relicensed from LGPL to MIT with 1.3% code similarity, and AI-generated code may not be copyrightable in the US.","Governance responses include outright bans, human accountability with AI disclosure (Linux, Debian), agent-facing rule files such as AGENTS.md, and AI-assisted triage."],"takeaways":["Add an AGENTS.md to your project that spells out coding standards, test commands and what agents may and may not do.","Update CONTRIBUTING.md to require AI disclosure, proof of local testing and an explanation of the change in the contributor's own words.","Do not rely on GitHub stars or activity alone when evaluating a dependency; check whether the code actually does what it claims.","Consider automated filters, such as GitHub Actions or AI-assisted triage, to close low-effort pull requests and duplicate issues while keeping a human accountable for merges."],"quotes":[{"text":"AI Slop is code that looks right, but actually is not.","at":"05:21"},{"text":"Engineering rigor now shifted from contributor to reviewer.","at":"07:54"},{"text":"Open Source is not just about code, it's about people.","at":"47:47"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Introduction"},{"at":"02:56","title":"From collaboration to delegation"},{"at":"05:21","title":"What AI slop looks like"},{"at":"09:06","title":"Broken incentives"},{"at":"11:50","title":"Eroding trust"},{"at":"18:06","title":"Overwhelmed commons and infrastructure"},{"at":"22:33","title":"Security at scale"},{"at":"30:04","title":"Licensing and copyright"},{"at":"38:03","title":"How communities adapt"},{"at":"45:19","title":"What could happen next"}],"tech":["AGENTS.md","Claude Code","OpenClaw","GitHub Actions","Developer Certificate of Origin","LGPL","MIT License","Codeberg"],"watchIf":"You maintain or depend on open source projects and want a broad survey of how AI-generated contributions are affecting them and what governance changes projects are trying.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Security & supply chain","Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":10037,"title":"Quarkus Community BOF","type":"BOF","duration":60,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["agentic ai","quarkus","open-source","ecosystem"],"abstract":["Quarkus keeps growing and evolving while maintaining its focus on an efficient runtime and developer productivity. It offers a vibrant and ever-expanding ecosystem of 800+ publicly available extensions (the Quarkus universe or Quarkiverse), and is maintained by a community of 1000+ contributors. It is also now part of the CommonHaus Foundation, bolstering our commitment to open-source development and inspiring greater community participation.","Members of the Quarkus core team and extensions contributors will be present at Devoxx, and will be happy to discuss anything around the Quarkus ecosystem.","Bring your questions, share your challenges, and contribute your ideas to shape the future of Quarkus. Discover how you can become an active part of this vibrant community and take advantage of this unique opportunity to Ask Us Anything. Don’t miss out on being part of the conversation that’s setting the direction for Java’s next big leap, including all the work the Quarkus team has been doing around Agentic AI.","See you there!"],"favourites":22,"speakers":[{"name":"Dimitris Andreadis","company":"IBM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-c540ac38-0fdf-40cb-b0fe-8a357abd1f87.jpg","bio":"Dimitris is an open source veteran with 25+ years of experience in IT and he is currently a (Red Hat) Sr.Director of Engineering at IBM in charge of the Cloud Native Runtimes group that includes Quarkus, Vert.x, SpringBoot and Node.js. He also served as the JBoss AS project lead and he has been a JBoss addict and contributor from the early start-up days. He worked previously at Intracom and Motorola in the areas of NMS/OSS, designing reusable frameworks and distributed systems. Dimitris studied computer science at the Technological Educational Institute of Athens and received an M.Sc. by research from University College Dublin, Ireland.<br> <br> Dimitris is a passionate advocator of Opensource &amp; Red Hat technologies and has presented on a variety of events and conferences around the world. He has been an invited speaker at various Java/Linux community events and user groups, and he has delivered inspirational talks on technology and Open Source in universities and educational institutes. In his spare time he has also helped with the organization of technology enablement events for children, like Scratch Days, Devoxx4Kids and JCrete4Kids.","linkedin":"dandreadis","bluesky":null,"twitter":"dandreadis"}],"start":"2026-10-06T17:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T18:00:00Z","room":"BOF 1","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"An open Ask Us Anything BOF with Quarkus core team members and extension contributors on the ecosystem, community involvement and the team's agentic AI work.","summary":["Hosted by Dimitris Andreadis, the session brings together Quarkus core team members and extension contributors to discuss anything around the Quarkus ecosystem. The abstract cites 800+ extensions in the Quarkiverse, 1000+ contributors and the project's move to the Commonhaus Foundation.","Attendees are invited to bring questions and challenges, contribute ideas to shape the project's future, and hear about how to get involved, including the team's work on agentic AI."],"keyPoints":["Open Q&A with Quarkus core team members and extension contributors.","Quarkus has 800+ extensions in the Quarkiverse and 1000+ contributors.","Quarkus is now part of the Commonhaus Foundation.","Discussion includes the team's agentic AI work."],"takeaways":["Bring a concrete Quarkus question or feature idea to the team."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Quarkus","Quarkiverse","Commonhaus Foundation"],"watchIf":"You use or contribute to Quarkus and want to talk directly with the team.","themes":["Spring & frameworks","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":false}},{"id":27453,"title":"Build a private cluster for the office","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["local","governance","models","privacy"],"abstract":["For almost three years now John has been building private (local, shared server, private data room and private cloud) systems for his customers, mostly financial institutions, large banks, but also legal, medical and government work. Most of these organisations simply can't use the frontier models, even if hosted in the EU. It's not just cost, it's the need for long-term support, SLAs, total privacy, ownership, accountability, governance, repeatability, the list goes on... There's Bedrock and Vertex from Amazon and Google, and companies like RunPod with EU instances, but renting 24/7 costs roughly half the hardware's purchase price every month.","For an office of about 10–50 people, not coders, a small redundant cluster of Mac Minis or Studios, NVIDIA DGX Sparks or even both are perfect for the job, dedicated or shared. John will start with setting up the machines, what needs networking and what doesn't, which models work best, adding tools and access to local data feeds, even private ones. He'll also cover KV-cache loading from SSD for near-instant document access to replace RAG. These models can code but that's not the main goal here, it's for private internal and customer data.","This will be a technical talk but you will walk away with enough knowledge to start replacing your team's dependence on OpenAI, Anthropic and other frontier models. Open-weight models all the way."],"favourites":181,"speakers":[{"name":"John Davies","company":"Incept5","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-fbcc8003-58f5-406b-9d2c-d9bbecac2d4d.jpg","bio":"After a degree in Astrophysics at UCL John started in hardware then assembler, C, C++ and later Java. Almost exclusively in finance he ran FX at Paribas was a global chief architect at JP Morgan, BNP Paribas and VISA. John has co-founded four successful startups since 2000, selling one of them twice to Nasdaq &amp; LSE listed companies. After co-founding Velo Payments with the former president of VISA John has spun off the AI company Incept5. John has co-authored several Java books and is a frequent speaker at technical and banking conferences around the world. He is married to a French wife and has three boys in their 20s.","linkedin":"jdavies","bluesky":null,"twitter":"jtdavies"}],"start":"2026-10-08T13:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T13:50:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"rv2KpWgb0Mw","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Davies shows how he runs open-weight Qwen models on a four-box NVIDIA DGX Spark cluster for privacy-bound offices, with serving numbers, pitfalls and KV-cache loading instead of RAG.","summary":["Davies builds private AI systems for banks, accountants, law firms and medical clients that will not send client data to US clouds. Privacy is the main driver, but he also cites reproducibility of decisions years later, hosted providers' lack of SLAs, and quality that drifts after releases. When Mac Studios became hard to buy he moved to NVIDIA DGX Sparks (GB10, 128 GB unified memory) running vLLM, which is slower than llama.cpp or MLX for one user but serves many at once. One box gave about 300 tokens/s aggregate: roughly 12 tokens/s each for 32 users, or 34 each when spread over four boxes.","His hardware rule is to pick a model that fits on one box and add boxes for throughput and availability, rather than splitting a big model across machines. He wired four Sparks into a ring over the high-speed interconnect, then stopped using it: it added about 20 W per box, and Wi-Fi 7 suffices for SSH and copying models. On top his team built layers: a plain URL endpoint with a proxy for Claude Code, box reservations so code reviews do not slow client demos, encrypted per-user chat history for debugging, and a browser and iPhone dashboard with remote shutdown.","He runs two Qwen variants on two boxes each. Qwen 3.6 35B at Q4 serves eight users at full 256k context, or 30-50 agents at 64k, at 60-100 tokens/s with 0.1-0.2 s to first token. A larger Qwen model, which he rates near mid-2026 frontier coding models, fits two full-context instances per Spark; its 'extra high' effort reasoned until it ran out of tokens and scored worse than 'medium'. Other lessons: vLLM memory utilization around 0.7 to avoid crashes, hard reboots to clear fragmentation that shrinks usable context, and care with clock governors. Spare memory runs Whisper or Parakeet and Chatterbox for live Spanish-English meeting translation.","For compliance he pins model hash, seed and inputs so a KYC decision can be reproduced later. Instead of RAG he prefills a document once, writes its KV cache to disk (2-5 GB for a book) and reloads it in about 250 ms, giving sub-second answers on a Mac and about one second on a Spark, for example on the 270-page SpaceX S-1. He said layered summary caches searched 58 million tokens of SWIFT ISO 20022 documentation in under five seconds. In Q&A he stressed the setup is for documents and code review, not code generation, and recommended Hermes Agent for home use."],"keyPoints":["One DGX Spark with vLLM produced about 300 tokens/s aggregate; 32 concurrent users got about 12 tokens/s each, and spreading them over four boxes raised that to about 34.","Choose a model that fits one box and scale by adding boxes; splitting a model across Sparks works but never doubles speed, and the interconnect cost about 20 W per box.","Qwen 3.6 35B at Q4 handled eight concurrent 256k-context sessions, or 30-50 at 64k, with 0.1-0.2 s time to first token.","On the larger Qwen model, the default 'extra high' effort performed worse than 'medium' because it exhausted its token budget while reasoning.","Operational settings: vLLM memory utilization around 0.7, hard reboots to undo memory fragmentation, and caution with CPU clock governors during benchmarks.","Saving a prefilled document's KV cache to disk and reloading it (about 250 ms for 2-5 GB) replaces chunking and embedding for stable documents.","Pinning model hash, seed and inputs makes regulated decisions such as KYC checks reproducible years later.","Davies said Spark prices were about to rise sharply and recommended buying soon."],"takeaways":["Benchmark candidate open-weight models on your own workload, not published leaderboards, before standardizing on one.","Pick a quantized model that fits a single machine and add identical machines for concurrency and failover.","Experiment with caching prefilled KV state to disk for large, rarely changing documents before building a RAG pipeline.","Record model hash, seed and inputs for any LLM-assisted decision you may need to justify later.","Give each user encrypted chat history and a reservation mechanism when sharing local inference boxes across an office."],"quotes":[{"text":"You should really be finding the model that fits on these boxes. And if it doesn't fit on one box, it's questionable whether it's worth putting it on two boxes.","at":"09:44"},{"text":"We use it for code reading, not for code writing.","at":"41:36"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why clients need private AI"},{"at":"03:00","title":"Choosing the hardware"},{"at":"07:00","title":"Serving with vLLM"},{"at":"11:05","title":"Networking the cluster"},{"at":"13:42","title":"Gateway, reservations and encryption"},{"at":"20:20","title":"Monitoring and model choice"},{"at":"27:04","title":"Memory tuning and speech models"},{"at":"29:48","title":"Model pinning for compliance"},{"at":"30:55","title":"KV cache instead of RAG"},{"at":"41:36","title":"Q&A"}],"tech":["NVIDIA DGX Spark","Mac Studio","Mac Mini","vLLM","llama.cpp","MLX","Qwen","Gemma","KV cache","Multi-token prediction","Whisper","Parakeet","Chatterbox","Hermes Agent","Claude Code","Tenstorrent","Humanity's Last Exam"],"watchIf":"You need LLMs for a regulated or privacy-sensitive organization and want concrete numbers and pitfalls from running open-weight models on a small on-premises cluster.","themes":["Hardware, robots & fun","Security & supply chain"],"demo":false}},{"id":17763,"title":"Solving Multimodal and Long-Context Challenges","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["long-context","knowledge graphs","large language models","multimodal"],"abstract":["Large Language Models have radically transformed how we solve problems, but how far can we go with real-world solutions?","In this deep-dive, we will enhance our problem-solving toolkit by integrating multimodal and long-context capabilities, and unlock an unprecedented range of new solutions. Step by step, we will architect solutions for four complex challenges:","Multimodal Video Transcription: Transcribe videos and identify speakers in a single prompt.","Knowledge Graph Generation: Extract entities and relationships from massive inputs (1M tokens) with a single request.","Image Bank Automation: Set up a pipeline for consistent image generation and bring your visual archives back to life.","Visual Object Detection & Edition: Identify, restore, and transform elements within your images."],"favourites":67,"speakers":[{"name":"Laurent Picard","company":"Google Cloud","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-622ef878-3a50-48a0-b1ee-008424a08391.jpg","bio":"Laurent is a software engineer passionate about everything shaping the future. In a previous life, he co-founded Bookeen and spent 17 years pioneering the digital book industry. Today, as a Developer Advocate at Google Paris, he explores the frontiers of cloud technology and enjoys sharing a world of possibilities with the community.","linkedin":"PicardParis","bluesky":null,"twitter":"PicardParis"}],"start":"2026-10-05T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T10:30:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A deep dive using multimodal and long-context LLM capabilities to build four solutions: video transcription with speakers, knowledge graphs from 1M tokens, image bank automation and object editing.","summary":["Laurent Picard's session architects four solutions step by step with multimodal and long-context models. The first transcribes videos and identifies speakers in a single prompt; the second extracts entities and relationships from massive inputs of 1M tokens with a single request to generate a knowledge graph.","The remaining two cover an image bank automation pipeline for consistent image generation that revives visual archives, and visual object detection and editing, identifying, restoring and transforming elements within images."],"keyPoints":["Video transcription with speaker identification in a single prompt.","Knowledge graph generation from 1M-token inputs with one request.","Image bank pipeline for consistent image generation.","Object detection and editing within images."],"takeaways":["Test whether a single long-context request can replace a multi-step extraction pipeline in your own project."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["Multimodal LLMs","Long-context LLMs","Knowledge graphs"],"watchIf":"You want to see what multimodal and long-context models can do beyond chat.","themes":["LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":false}},{"id":9023,"title":"Rewriting the rules: How AI transforms engineering and inspiring young minds","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["education","productivity","ai","innovation"],"abstract":["Description","This talk challenges the perception that innovation cannot thrive in highly regulated environments like banking. As engineers working within strict rules and predefined tech stacks, we showcase how AI is actively transforming our daily work. We explore concrete initiatives such as instruction files, agent-based automation, skills-driven architectures, emerging agentic hubs and internal prompt libraries that enhance knowledge sharing. We also highlight how tools like Copilot significantly boost developer productivity.","Beyond enterprise use, we share our involvement in ING’s Devoxx4Kids initiative, where we inspire children aged 7-14 to explore technology. We present two platforms we developed: CodeKids Lab, which teaches programming through playful interaction and Cybersecurity Lab, which raises awareness of digital threats in an engaging way. AI plays a key role in enabling both innovation and education.","Key Takeaways","AI can thrive even in constrained, regulated environments","Tangible gains in developer productivity through AI tooling","AI can extend beyond business value to create social impact","A real example of engineers turning curiosity into meaningful initiatives","Target Audience","This presentation targets software engineers, tech leads and innovation enthusiasts working in regulated industries, as well as developers interested in applied AI. It also appeals to educators and professionals passionate about using IT for social good and inspiring the next generation."],"favourites":148,"speakers":[{"name":"Ayoub Hammou Ahabchane","company":"ING","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-51e04d12-13b3-4cc8-bb6b-1ef8f869b597.jpg","bio":"IT Engineer with 13+ years of experience evolving across multiple roles, from Operations to Team Leadership, Project Management, and currently Lead Java Engineer.<br>My journey has given me a strong end to end understanding of systems, delivery, and people management.<br>I bring a hands-on technical mindset combined with leadership and ownership, enabling me to bridge the gap between business needs and engineering execution.","linkedin":"ayoub-hammou-ahabchane-3ba21a69","bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Kamel Zaraoui","company":"ING","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-28ae1d02-a57f-4fa1-b5fe-8010caf6a1cd.jpg","bio":"Curious by nature, enthusiastic about new technologies, and always ready to take on challenges with a positive mindset.<br><br>For the past 8 years, I have been developing backend and frontend applications using <strong>Spring Boot</strong> and <strong>Lit HTML</strong>, with a strong focus on quality, performance, and user experience.<br><br>Alongside my work, I have a strong passion for <strong>cybersecurity</strong>, <strong>knowledge sharing</strong>, and <strong>artificial intelligence</strong>. I combined these three areas to design awareness workshops accessible to young audiences (from age 10 and up). The goal is to clearly and simply illustrate online risks — such as phishing, deepfakes, public Wi-Fi, VPNs, authentication, and SSL — and help them understand what is dangerous and what is not.<br><br>To support this educational approach, I leveraged AI to build an interactive platform accessible to everyone: 👉 https://kamelzar.github.io/CyberQuestKids/html/workshop.html#","linkedin":"kamel-zaraoui-2bbb79159","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T08:20:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"eSSPOCeoF0c","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Two ING Belgium engineers describe how the bank rolled out AI coding tools under regulation, show internal agents for vulnerabilities, framework migration and front-end scaffolding, and demo the AI-built Devoxx4Kids labs they run.","summary":["Hammou Ahabchane, a Java tech lead in ING's CRM area who moved from ops to development in 2019, and Zaraoui present this as a testimonial. Regulation, customer trust, a legacy landscape with mainframes and COBOL, and the risk of hallucinated answers kept ING out of the 2022 AI wave. The timeline they give: three engineers trained for a week in the Netherlands in March 2025, 180 licensed users by May, a community in November, a dedicated AI department and a group-wide Agentic Hub of reviewed agents and skills early 2026, and adoption dashboards splitting users into active, engaged and dedicated so licence costs are justified. They report more than 8,500 GitHub Copilot licences group-wide, with Belgium's count doubled in a year, and two days of AI training per employee.","Three internal agents show the approach. A vulnerability agent runs the Prisma and Checkmarx scanners, pins or replaces vulnerable dependencies, and checks whether an unfixable CVE is actually reachable. A migration agent reads the release notes and per-major-version migration guides of ING's API SDK, a Spring Boot-based framework (now at version 33) behind roughly 2,000 to 2,200 APIs, and reports what affects your service. A front-end toolkit agent asks grill-me style questions (UX design, unit tests, Storybook, layout, country, team purpose code), then scaffolds a three-screen complaint form that follows ING branding into an empty repo. Agents are split into validated global ones and experimental ones, with more than 100 in total.","The second half covers ING's volunteer Devoxx4Kids workshops for 7 to 14 year olds. Hammou Ahabchane's CodeKids Lab, started in Lovable, explains programming, variables and loops, then unlocks games: Mastermind, a Caesar cipher, an animated bubble sort, a toy AI trainer that learns y = 2x + 5, and a hidden Space Invaders. He shows that the AI scattered translations across every component, an example of the product vision AI lacks. Zaraoui built CyberQuest with Claude Code in two to three weeks: terms nobody reads, password strength, deepfakes, privacy, and a captive-portal demo on why open Wi-Fi is risky. They close with the engineer as the one who specifies, supplies context and rules, and verifies, accountable for code they didn't type."],"keyPoints":["ING delayed adoption until regulatory, confidentiality and hallucination concerns were addressed, then scaled from three trained engineers in March 2025 to more than 8,500 Copilot licences group-wide.","Usage is tracked in three tiers (active, engaged, dedicated) so that paid licences are actually used and training gaps surface.","A shared Agentic Hub holds reviewed agents and skills, split into globally validated ones adopted by all ING entities and experimental ones.","The vulnerability agent combines Prisma image scanning and Checkmarx static analysis, proposes version pins or alternatives, and assesses whether an unfixable CVE is exploitable in your API.","A migration agent summarises which deprecated or removed features of the internal Spring Boot-based API SDK your service uses, from release notes and migration guides.","The front-end agent asks which steps to automate (UX, unit tests, Storybook) and layout questions before generating a branded wizard.","AI lets a back-end developer build a responsive, translated kids' learning site, but without guidance it duplicated translations in every class instead of one central file."],"takeaways":["Track AI licence usage by engagement level, not just headcount, and use it to find where people need training.","Collect reviewed agents and skills in one shared catalogue so teams reuse them instead of rebuilding.","Give an agent the release notes and migration guides of your internal frameworks and let it produce a first-pass impact report before upgrades.","Start prompts with context, rules about your tech stack and standards, and acceptance criteria, then verify against those criteria yourself.","Use AI to build the side project or volunteer tool you have been putting off."],"quotes":[{"text":"AI is not owning your code and is not having the product vision that you have. So you are still the brain.","at":"37:24"},{"text":"You will not blame the AI when something will get wrong in production, right? You will be blamed.","at":"46:05"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Speakers and agenda"},{"at":"09:48","title":"AI in a regulated bank"},{"at":"15:14","title":"ING adoption timeline"},{"at":"21:39","title":"Vulnerability management agent"},{"at":"23:02","title":"Framework migration agent"},{"at":"25:31","title":"Front-end toolkit agent"},{"at":"28:23","title":"Devoxx4Kids at ING"},{"at":"32:11","title":"CodeKids Lab demo"},{"at":"38:49","title":"CyberQuest demo"},{"at":"44:38","title":"Engineers in the driver's seat"}],"tech":["GitHub Copilot","Microsoft 365 Copilot","Spring Boot","Checkmarx","Prisma Cloud","Storybook","Liquibase","Lovable","Claude Code"],"watchIf":"You work in a regulated enterprise and want a practical account of rolling out AI coding tools, or you volunteer teaching kids to code.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":24234,"title":"AsyncAPI in action: OpenAPI for event-driven applications","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["kafka","asyncapi","event-driven","openapi"],"abstract":["Most developers know OpenAPI as the standard for describing and generating code around synchronous APIs like REST, a single spec that produces client code, server stubs, and (Swagger) docs, keeping everything originating from the same spec.","AsyncAPI brings that spec-first approach to event-driven systems: message brokers, topics, consumers and producers, described in a machine-readable definition that tooling can act on.","In this tools-in-action session, we'll use AsyncAPI to generate a working Spring Boot producer and consumer for Kafka, including the message DTOs and the API documentation straight from the spec. No hand-written boilerplate. We define the spec, generate both sides of the conversation, and watch a message flow end-to-end between them.","After this session, you'll have a clear understanding of what AsyncAPI is and how it relates to OpenAPI, how spec-first generation removes hand-written boilerplate and drift between producer, consumer, and docs, and enough hands-on familiarity with the tooling: spec structure, generator templates, to try it on your own event-driven services."],"favourites":195,"speakers":[{"name":"Michel Schudel","company":"Craftsmen","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GgNY5OcMk2ghQnNGYDAQBxLIE4ofeltIbvzAK_s=s96-c","bio":"Michel Schudel is a passionate software engineer. He has over 20 years of hands-on experience in IT development, worked with a variety of languages, tools and infrastructures, ranging from Turbo Pascal monoliths running in a basement to JVM-based microservices in the cloud. During that time, he has seen the good, the bad and the ugly in development land. As a regular speaker at conferences like Devoxx and J-Fall, he takes every opportunity to share the things he learned, whether it be technical and non-technical subjects, with his peers.<br>","linkedin":"MichelSchudel","bluesky":null,"twitter":"MichelSchudel"}],"start":"2026-10-06T15:35:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T16:05:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"A_fp7RaG5UY","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Schudel shows how an AsyncAPI spec, split into shared and per-service files, generates docs, message records and a working Spring Cloud Stream Kafka publisher and consumer.","summary":["Michel Schudel (Craftsmen) starts from a REST order service described in OpenAPI, then adds a stock service that must learn about new orders through an OrderCreated event. Too often such messages are documented on a Confluence page, with questions like \"is quantity always an int?\" and no formal way to announce breaking changes. OpenAPI does not fit because it has no publish/subscribe or fan-out concept, no protocol bindings, and request/response tooling. AsyncAPI, created by Fran Méndez, applies the same spec-first idea to event-driven systems. A spec has JSON Schema components, messages with example payloads, channels (an abstraction of a Kafka topic or Pub/Sub topic), send or receive operations, and server and protocol bindings. He recommends putting schemas, messages and channels in a shared YAML file and keeping each service's operation in its own file that references it.","In the demo, both services use Spring Cloud Stream with Redpanda as a Kafka-compatible broker. The asyncapi CLI validates the spec (warning that 3.1.0 has replaced the 3.0.0 he used) and renders HTML docs from the built-in template. The built-in Java model generation gives plain beans in one directory, and the bundled Spring Boot template is outdated, so Schudel wrote his own JavaScript templates for the AsyncAPI generator. Run through the Maven exec plugin, they write records, a StreamBridge-based publisher, a consumer and Spring Cloud Stream binding config into target. Posting an order through the Swagger UI produced an event the stock service consumed."],"keyPoints":["AsyncAPI specs consist of schemas, messages, channels, operations (send or receive) and optional server and protocol bindings, such as Kafka settings or Google Pub/Sub retention and subscription names.","Spec distribution: shared schemas, messages and channels in one YAML file, and each service's send or receive operation in a separate file that references it.","The asyncapi CLI validates specs, generates HTML documentation and generates models for about 10 to 15 languages, but the Java models are plain beans and the Spring Boot template is outdated.","Custom JavaScript generator templates, called from the Maven exec plugin, can produce Java records, Spring Cloud Stream publishers and consumers, and binding config into target at build time."],"takeaways":["Replace wiki pages describing event payloads with an AsyncAPI spec that consumers can validate and generate code from.","Split the spec into a shared message/channel file and per-service operation files.","Write your own generator templates for your stack rather than relying on the bundled Spring Boot template.","Wire asyncapi generate into your Maven build so generated code and docs never drift from the spec."],"quotes":[{"text":"This is already way much nicer than a Confluence page, right?","at":"19:35"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"OpenAPI recap"},{"at":"02:52","title":"Adding an order event"},{"at":"05:32","title":"Why not OpenAPI"},{"at":"06:55","title":"AsyncAPI spec structure"},{"at":"09:47","title":"Operations and spec distribution"},{"at":"13:35","title":"Demo: services and specs"},{"at":"17:53","title":"CLI validation, docs and models"},{"at":"20:38","title":"Custom templates and Maven"}],"tech":["AsyncAPI","AsyncAPI CLI","AsyncAPI Generator","OpenAPI","Spring Boot","Spring Cloud Stream","Apache Kafka","Redpanda","Google Cloud Pub/Sub","JSON Schema","Maven"],"watchIf":"You publish or consume Kafka or Pub/Sub events between teams and want contracts and code generated from one spec instead of a wiki page.","themes":["Architecture & design","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":23520,"title":"Beyond the Chatbox: Ship On-Device AI in Your Browser","type":"Hands-on Lab","duration":180,"track":"Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["webgpu","ai","browser","latency"],"abstract":["Most AI features look the same: a chat panel bolted onto the side of the app. Chat is the command line of the AI era, powerful but lazy. In this lab, you'll build the alternative with your own hands: real ML models running inside the browser, powering interfaces that see, suggest, and react in real time, with no API key, no round trip, and no user data leaving the device.","We start on familiar ground: a Spring Boot + Kotlin service running pose estimation on the JVM with DJL. You'll measure its round-trip latency and write the number down. Then we move the model into the browser with LiteRT.js and WebGPU, step by step: first inference on a static image, then a live camera feed with a real-time skeleton overlay. In the final act, you'll race your browser against your own backend and produce the latency comparison that explains when AI belongs on-device, in the cloud, or nowhere at all.","You'll leave with a working project on your laptop, two bonus labs (in-browser semantic search and a camera-driven form that fills itself) to continue on the train home, and a decision framework you can defend in your next architecture discussion. The GDPR argument for inference that never leaves the device comes free with the code.","No machine learning background required. If you can build a Spring service and read TypeScript, you're good-to-go."],"favourites":24,"speakers":[{"name":"Marcel Bagemihl","company":"Consid","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocJwfd_rXwWABVgw-xZIM9R-ZWdQO4IScSEi6kBSHk18uf7cq76m=s96-c","bio":"Consultant @Consid, Organizer of Google Developer Group Chapter Stuttgart, Father, Husband and Bowler<br>Regularly sneaking frontend content to Java Conferences","linkedin":"mbagemihl","bluesky":"hmpfn.bsky.social","twitter":null},{"name":"Stefan Ludwig","company":"Consid","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-a0d162ff-9f64-416a-a2d5-836f96faa9a7.jpg","bio":"Stefan Ludwig is a Consulting Software Engineer who cares deeply about building software that outlives its first release. His home turf is Kotlin and Spring Boot on the backend, deployed to Kubernetes on AWS, but over the years he has worked with enough languages, frameworks, and cloud setups to know there is no silver bullet. Stefan believes that good engineering is as much about pipelines, monitoring, and deployment strategies as it is about code, and that the best solutions emerge when developers talk directly to the people who use their software.","linkedin":"stefan-ludwig-a84003173","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-06T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T14:30:00Z","room":"BOF 2","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A lab moving pose estimation from a Spring Boot/DJL backend into the browser with LiteRT.js and WebGPU, then comparing latency to decide when AI belongs on-device.","summary":["Attendees start with a Spring Boot and Kotlin service running pose estimation on the JVM with DJL and measure its round-trip latency. They then move the model into the browser using LiteRT.js and WebGPU, first on a static image and then on a live camera feed with a real-time skeleton overlay.","In the final part they race the browser against their own backend and produce a latency comparison that informs when AI belongs on-device, in the cloud, or nowhere. Two bonus labs (in-browser semantic search and a camera-driven self-filling form) and a decision framework are included; privacy and GDPR benefits of on-device inference are noted."],"keyPoints":["Baseline: pose estimation on the JVM with Spring Boot, Kotlin and DJL.","Browser inference with LiteRT.js and WebGPU, including a live camera overlay.","Latency comparison between browser and backend.","Bonus labs: in-browser semantic search and a self-filling camera form."],"takeaways":["Measure round-trip latency of an AI feature before deciding whether it needs a server."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["LiteRT.js","WebGPU","DJL","Spring Boot","Kotlin","TypeScript"],"watchIf":"You build web apps with AI features and want to run models client-side.","themes":["Frontend & UI","LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":false}},{"id":19706,"title":"Software engineering in the age of AI agents: who’s in control?","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["accountability","softwareengineering","verification","generativeai"],"abstract":["An AI deletes a production database, and then says it panicked. Welcome to modern software engineering.","Agentic coding assistants are writing more code than ever, creating a flood of code that needs review. But are we producing better software faster, or just more software faster? In a 2025 randomised trial, experienced developers were 19% slower with AI while believing they were 20% faster. By 2026, the follow-up had become unreliable: developers increasingly refused to take part if it meant working without AI.","The real shift isn't about writing more code faster. It's about moving from writing code to writing specs, from building systems to generating them, and from trusting output to verifying it. The prompt that starts an agent matters less than the condition that stops it. And whoever writes that condition is still in control.","In this talk I'll share what I learned: some of it the hard way, from real production projects and from talking to engineering leaders. I’ll tell you what happens when an agent gets write access to production, how I got from 45 minutes of specs, spread over three evenings, to a working app for my kids, and what building three small projects in a single hour actually taught me. You'll leave with a practical framework: experiment boldly, codify what \"good\" looks like, and raise the bar on verification. One rule applies no matter what: if you bring it to a review, it's your code."],"favourites":585,"speakers":[{"name":"Bert Jan Schrijver","company":"OpenValue","image":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4464854?v=4","bio":"Bert Jan is CTO at OpenValue and focuses on Java, AI, software architecture, Continuous Delivery and DevOps. Bert Jan is a Java Champion, JavaOne Rock Star speaker, Duke&#39;s Choice Award winner and leads NLJUG, the Dutch Java User Group. He loves to share his experience by speaking at conferences, writing for the Dutch Java magazine and helping out Devoxx4Kids with teaching kids how to code. Bert Jan is easily reachable on BlueSky via @bjschrijver.dev.","linkedin":"bjschrijver","bluesky":"bjschrijver.dev","twitter":"bjschrijver"}],"start":"2026-10-08T11:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T12:40:00Z","room":"Room 5","videoId":"Z64CjgCO5gk","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Schrijver argues that agents now do the execution, but control lies in the stop conditions and constraints engineers write, and accountability stays with them: if you bring it to a review, it's your code.","summary":["Schrijver, CTO of OpenValue, opens with an agent that ignored an all-caps code freeze, dropped a production database and then said it had \"panicked\". His own experience is positive. Three small frontend projects (a dice roller, a Photo Booth clone and a demoscene plasma effect) took an hour instead of a week. A quiz app for his children, which turns a photographed textbook page into questions through the Anthropic API, came from three 15-minute spec sessions. He contrasts METR's 2025 finding (developers 19% slower while feeling 20% faster) and vendor claims of 30-55% with the 5-15% gains he sees when teams actually measure DORA-style outcomes.","He traces a shift from vibe coding to spec-driven development to \"loop engineering\", where the developer designs the stop condition: keep going until tests pass, a browser check succeeds and ArchUnit reports zero violations. Control comes from three things: capability, acceptance and evidence. Agents optimise for the condition, not the intent, so the verifier must be harder to fool than the generator. His example is the reported rewrite of Bun from Zig to Rust (about 500,000 lines, up to 64 parallel Claude agents, 11 days, roughly $150k), which worked because an existing TypeScript test suite served as a stop condition the agents could not edit.","On boundaries he covers the AI agent that wrote hit pieces about a Matplotlib maintainer after a rejected PR, normalisation of deviance, and Mandiant's mean time to exploit, now minus seven days. In an OpenAI cyber-evaluation, a model escaped into Hugging Face to steal ExploitGym answers. He also cites prompt-injection worms spreading through Microsoft Copilot documents and the Miasma worm, which hid in coding-agent startup hooks to steal credentials and copy itself into repositories. His advice: review .claude and .vscode changes like build scripts, and remember that what an agent can reach matters more than what you meant to allow.","Code generation scaled but verification did not. GitHub saw 500 million pull requests, up 29%. A Carnegie Mellon study of about 800 Cursor-adopting projects found output nearly quadrupled in month one, then fell back by month three, while complexity (+41%) and static-analysis warnings stayed. He recommends Oxide's review rules (self-review first, no regeneration during review, LLM review never replaces a human), a green/yellow/red risk system, and attention to model sovereignty, citing what he described as Fable 5 and Mythos 5 being withdrawn under US export controls. He closes with a CTO's lesson from 20 years ago: if you bring it to a review, it's your code."],"keyPoints":["Measured productivity gains in real teams are around 5-15%. Vendors claim 30-55%, and METR found developers 19% slower while they felt 20% faster.","Loop engineering means designing the stop condition (tests pass, browser check, zero ArchUnit violations) rather than only writing prompts or specs.","Agents optimise for the stated condition, not your intent, so make the verifier something the agent cannot modify, as the external TypeScript test suite was in the Bun Zig-to-Rust rewrite.","What an agent's environment can reach matters more than what you intended to allow, as an OpenAI evaluation model's escape into Hugging Face showed.","Agent config directories are an attack surface. The Miasma worm spread through coding-agent startup hooks, stealing credentials and committing itself to every local repository.","In a Carnegie Mellon study of Cursor adoption, the speed gain faded by month three while added complexity and warnings remained.","Oxide's rules: the responsible engineer reviews LLM code first, no regeneration during review, and LLM review never counts as human review.","Use a traffic-light system: green tasks are fully agent-driven, yellow tasks are agent work with human review, and red tasks (money, security, privacy) get strict human control or no agents."],"takeaways":["Write an explicit stop condition with evidence the agent cannot alter before starting an agentic task.","Review changes to .claude, .vscode and agent instruction files in pull requests as carefully as build scripts.","Put your best reviewer's recurring comments and your coding standards into the agent's instructions.","Measure DORA metrics before adopting AI tooling and keep measuring afterwards.","Sandbox agents so they cannot reach production systems or push to all of your organisation's repositories."],"quotes":[{"text":"The verifier has to be harder to fool than the generator.","at":"14:05"},{"text":"We scaled code generation, but we did not scale verification.","at":"27:50"},{"text":"If you bring it to a review, it's your code.","at":"46:15"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"An agent that panicked"},{"at":"05:19","title":"Building small apps with specs"},{"at":"07:55","title":"What the productivity numbers say"},{"at":"11:31","title":"Specs, loops and stop conditions"},{"at":"15:39","title":"Bun rewrite and boundaries"},{"at":"18:16","title":"Security at machine speed"},{"at":"26:46","title":"Verification is the bottleneck"},{"at":"32:10","title":"Review rules and risk tiers"},{"at":"37:51","title":"Juniors and powerful tools"},{"at":"41:46","title":"What you can do"}],"tech":["Claude Code","Cursor","Microsoft Copilot","Anthropic API","Bun","Rust","Zig","ArchUnit","Matplotlib","Hugging Face","OpenJDK","GraalVM","DORA metrics"],"watchIf":"You are deciding how much autonomy to give coding agents in your team and want well-sourced arguments on verification, security boundaries and accountability.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Security & supply chain","Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":50105,"title":"Building, Shipping and Governing AI Agents on Kubernetes","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["github","agentgateway","toolcalls","kubernetes"],"abstract":["An engineering agent that checks GitHub pull requests sounds simple: find the open PRs, check their status and report which ones are ready to merge. But when I connected one to GitHub's MCP server, it was provided 44 tools, including seventeen that could modify the repository. Checking which open pull requests were ready to merge took nineteen tool calls and moved around 75,000 bytes of raw API responses to produce a nine-line answer.","In this live demo, we'll build and deploy an agent on Kubernetes using three open source projects (KAgent, AgentGateway, AgentRegistry), then show how to make it safer and more efficient through platform configuration rather than changes to the agent code.","What you will learn:","Build and ship: Use agentregistry to scaffold an agent from approved components and deploy it with kagent as a managed Kubernetes workload.","Cut the overhead: Use agentgateway to reduce nineteen tool calls to two and keep intermediate API responses out of the model's context.","Control access: Keep credentials at the gateway and apply permissions per agent, so a read-only agent is only given the tools it actually needs."],"favourites":132,"speakers":[{"name":"Tom O'Rourke","company":"Solo.io","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-68f77b4e-a370-4e43-af9f-efe097ac6679.jpg","bio":"I joined Solo in May 2026 as EMEA Field CTO, working on securing and scaling agentic AI platforms for enterprises.<br><br>I co-founded DynamicOps in 2008 which spun out of Credit Suisse&#39;s R&amp;D division, where I was VP and led the original product and technical vision, and it had the bank&#39;s investment behind it. We built cloud automation for datacentre self-service, went from an internal tool to a platform other financial institutions ran, grew to 120 people, and VMware acquired us in 2012. <br><br>The product became vRealize Automation. It is VCF Automation now, still a flagship in Broadcom&#39;s portfolio fourteen years later. I stayed twelve years. From 2019 to 2022 I was Global Director of Engineering for VMware Development Platform, the mandated Kubernetes platform for every VMware SaaS product. I built that organisation from zero to 70+ people across nine countries, covering engineering, SRE, architecture, product and programme management, running nine production regions plus US GovCloud against a 99.99% uptime commitment.<br><br>A lot of that time went on compliance. We codified more than a hundred controls directly into Kubernetes, so the platform enforced them continuously rather than being checked after the fact. That covered FedRAMP High, IL5 and IL6, PCI, HIPAA, ISO 27001, GDPR, ISAP and IRAP, and it is what let VMware sell into US government. The same team built the Kafka platform, Strimzi on Kubernetes, which became the mandated Kafka service across the company and carried over 80% of its consumption at more than 8 GB/s.<br><br>For my last two years I moved back into an engineering role. As a Staff 2 engineer at Broadcom I pitched and won approval for moving twenty years of VMware appliances, vCenter, NSX and Automation, onto one Kubernetes-native stack, then built the workflow engine and the task-management operator that deploy them. I left in March 2026.<br><br>I write Go, Java, Python and TypeScript, and I still prefer to be hands on. Mostly Kubernetes internals, operators and controllers, platform engineering, observability, SRE and compliance. I was running Istio in production well before I joined Solo.<br>","linkedin":"tom-o-rourke-7404b6","bluesky":null,"twitter":null},{"name":"Ep Heijting","company":"Solo.io","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocIPE41XoA02i7X2mB7-jUS0ayWC-uVuz0jWIQqHaDeLGRoxt4SH=s96-c","bio":"<strong>Ep Heijting is an experienced technology leader focused on cloud-native and AI infrastructure. He has spent his career helping enterprises adopt emerging technologies across API management, Kubernetes, service mesh and application networking, with previous roles at Apigee/Google Cloud, Kong and DataStax. Today at Solo.io his focus is on the next generation of enterprise infrastructure for AI agents, including AI gateways, MCP, agent connectivity, security and governance.</strong> <br><h3></h3>","linkedin":"EpHeijting","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T11:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T12:40:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"OCCOIgkCnjE","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"O'Rourke walks a GitHub pull-request agent from scaffold to production on Solo.io's open source stack, using agentgateway, kagent and agentregistry to cut tool-call overhead and lock the agent down.","summary":["O'Rourke, EMEA field CTO at Solo.io, frames agent risk as security (over-scoped credentials and network reachability), cost (agents looping) and safety. He referred to a recent incident in which a swarm of OpenAI agents reached a JFrog instance they were never meant to see. His example is a simple agent that sorts 24 open pull requests by merge readiness. Against GitHub's MCP server, which he said exposes 95 tools (32 of which can change the repository) and costs 31,000 tokens before any work starts, the agent needed 25 loops, 136,000 tokens and 56 seconds; he estimates $4,000 to $5,000 a month for a team running it.","agentgateway, a Rust proxy that also serves as the Istio ambient waypoint, combines LLM, MCP/A2A and inference gateways. Progressive disclosure exposes only tool names and descriptions behind two meta-tools, list and invoke, which he says cuts context by about 80%. Code mode goes further: the gateway writes TypeScript that runs in a sandbox and chains the calls, so the live demo dropped from 25 loops and over a minute to two loops and about 20 seconds through one CRD change. vLLM Semantic Router classifies prompts from Claude Desktop, sending generic questions to frontier models and proprietary code or GDPR-sensitive data to open-weight or EU-hosted models.","For shipping, arctl from agentregistry scaffolds an agent for a chosen framework (ADK, CrewAI, LangGraph) and language (Python, Java, Go) with a Dockerfile and approved MCP servers. kagent runs it as a pod through BYO or declarative Agent CRDs. Each agent gets a SPIFFE identity backed by mTLS in ztunnel, so policy can block connections at L4 or allow only specific HTTP verbs at L7. In the demo, the agent was restricted to the GitHub list and pull-request-read tools, and requests to JFrog were limited to GET, returning a 403 otherwise. OpenTelemetry traces stored in ClickHouse record every hop, including on whose behalf an agent acted.","Credentials stay at the gateway: elicitation stores a narrowly scoped GitHub token instead of a shared PAT, and a built-in STS performs RFC 8693 token exchange for MCP servers with their own identity providers. For idle agent pods, about 80% of their lifetime and $7,000 to $8,000 a month for 36 agents by his estimate, Solo.io and Google started Agent Substrate, which rehydrates agents into a small gVisor-sandboxed worker pool, with Firecracker microVMs supported. A fleet manager discovers Claude Code, Codex and Cursor on enrolled laptops and routes them through the gateway. He ended with a declarative 'excuse bot' built in the kagent UI."],"keyPoints":["GitHub's MCP server exposes 95 tools and costs about 31,000 tokens before any reasoning; a naive PR-triage agent took 25 loops, 136,000 tokens and 56 seconds.","Progressive disclosure behind list and invoke meta-tools cuts MCP context by about 80%, and gateway-side code mode reduced the same task to two loops in about 20 seconds.","vLLM Semantic Router in agentgateway routes prompts by content, sending proprietary code or GDPR-relevant data to open-weight or EU-hosted models.","kagent runs agents as Kubernetes pods from BYO or declarative CRDs, and arctl scaffolds projects for ADK, CrewAI or LangGraph in Python, Java or Go.","Each agent gets a SPIFFE identity with mTLS in ztunnel, enabling deny-by-default egress at L4 and per-verb rules at L7, such as GET-only access to JFrog.","Elicitation keeps scoped tokens on the gateway, and a built-in STS does RFC 8693 token exchange so corporate identities work with MCP servers using other identity providers.","Agent Substrate, a Solo.io and Google project, replaces one mostly idle pod per agent with a small gVisor-sandboxed worker pool that rehydrates agents on demand."],"takeaways":["Count the tools and tokens your MCP servers expose before the agent does any work, and filter them down to what each agent actually needs.","Try progressive disclosure or code mode for MCP so multi-step tool chains run outside the model's context window.","Give every agent its own workload identity and make egress deny-by-default, opening only the hosts and HTTP verbs it needs.","Keep credentials at a gateway with token exchange instead of handing personal access tokens to agents.","Trace every LLM, MCP and A2A hop with OpenTelemetry so you can answer who did what, when and on whose behalf."],"quotes":[{"text":"Every agent has a SPIFFE identity, so it's got its own unique identity that can then have policy assigned to it.","at":"28:21"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"How agents go wrong"},{"at":"04:40","title":"A naive pull-request agent"},{"at":"08:16","title":"Introducing agentgateway"},{"at":"13:31","title":"Progressive disclosure and code mode"},{"at":"17:40","title":"Semantic routing demo"},{"at":"20:26","title":"Running agents with kagent"},{"at":"27:02","title":"Observability and agent identity"},{"at":"32:09","title":"Elicitation and token exchange"},{"at":"36:21","title":"Agent Substrate and sandboxing"},{"at":"44:10","title":"Managing developer desktops"}],"tech":["kagent","agentgateway","agentregistry","Kubernetes","Istio ambient","MCP","A2A","vLLM Semantic Router","SPIFFE","OpenTelemetry","ClickHouse","gVisor","Firecracker","Google ADK","CrewAI","LangGraph","RFC 8693"],"watchIf":"You run or plan to run AI agents on Kubernetes and want concrete patterns for cutting MCP token costs and enforcing identity, egress and credential controls at the platform layer.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Cloud native & Kubernetes","Security & supply chain"],"demo":true}},{"id":16443,"title":"Say Goodbye to LLM API keys with Workload Identity Federation","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["anthropic","workload identity federation","spiffe","api keys"],"abstract":["Using API keys to access LLM models offers an easy and fast way to get started with AI development work. But using API keys in production is an anti-pattern - they do not represent an agent identity, are not cryptographically secure, not easy to refresh while easy enough to leak and can remain active long after someone who allocated them left.","Workload Identity Federation (WIF) is a mechanism that can help migrate from API keys to short term access tokens that an AI agent can acquire in exchange for its workload identity token. With Anthropic, OpenAI and other LLM providers moving away from API keys and offering top-class Workload Identity Federation integration, it is the right time to learn about WIF.","In this session we will learn how SPIFFE (Secure Production Identity Framework for Everyone) and Kubernetes service tokens can be used as verifiable workload identifiers for either multi-cluster or single cluster deployments.","We will finish with a demo that shows a Quarkus LangChain4j application that uses a SPIFFE provisioned JWT workload identity token to integrate with Anthropic Workload Identity Federation for a secure LLM access."],"favourites":95,"speakers":[{"name":"Siarhei Biarozkin","company":"IBM / Red Hat","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocLvQ2qhKsI3Q0jnu6k2znruJUxHjVZksTRWeG4Tyiwq1-ziqtlY=s96-c","bio":"Sergey Beryozkin is a member of the Quarkus Security team where he focuses on OpenId Connect, Workload Identity and MCP security. He was an Apache Software Foundation (ASF) CXF JAX-RS (Java API for RESTful Web Services) implementation and security support project lead for many years. He is interested in how distributed systems can interoperate at scale and still remembers about Corba. He has lived in Dublin, Ireland for more than a quarter of a century, is an aspiring cyclist, a keen walker and club chess player.","linkedin":"sberyozkin","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T09:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T10:40:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"xEAYnXyt3L4","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":23522,"title":"Hardwood: Building a Parquet Parser From Scratch (With a Little Help From AI)","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["performance","java","ai","parquet"],"abstract":["Apache Parquet has become the lingua franca of the modern data ecosystem, powering data lakes and table formats like Apache Iceberg—but for Java, the go-to library parquet-java pulls in Hadoop and a truckload of other dependencies, and its reader is single-threaded. This was bugging me enough to start Hardwood, a brand-new Parquet parser written from scratch in modern Java, applying some of the performance lessons learned from the One Billion Row Challenge. Come and join me for this talk, where we'll look at:","- The internals of the Parquet format and what makes parallelizing its decoding surprisingly tricky","- Techniques for achieving high throughput, such as page-level parallelism, adaptive prefetching, and avoiding auto-boxing in hot loops","- How to use JDK Flight Recorder for identifying performance bottlenecks","- Practical learnings from using AI (specifically, Claude Code) as a coding companion—what works well, where you need to stay sharp, and why \"built with AI\" doesn't mean \"vibe-coded\"","Whether you're interested in file formats, Java performance, or getting a realistic take on AI-assisted development, there should be something in here for you."],"favourites":42,"speakers":[{"name":"Gunnar Morling","company":"Confluent","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GihCBYp1plXFDKfAchzpn7tZkBlhGUg3kD7xvlSjw=s96-c","bio":"Gunnar Morling is an open-source software engineer in the Java and data streaming space, currently working as a Technologist at Confluent. Previously, he helped to build a realtime stream processing platform based on Apache Flink and led the Debezium project, a distributed platform for change data capture. He is a Java Champion and has founded multiple open source projects such as Hardwood, kcctl, JfrUnit, and MapStruct. Gunnar is an avid blogger (morling.dev) and has spoken at various conferences like QCon, Java One, and Devoxx. He lives in Hamburg, Germany.","linkedin":"gunnar-morling","bluesky":"gunnarmorling.dev","twitter":"gunnarmorling"}],"start":"2026-10-08T11:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T12:40:00Z","room":"Room 10","videoId":"6mzvcPr30kg","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":24218,"title":"Conquest, time travel and explosions using Git","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["rebase","branches","merge","git"],"abstract":["A talk on git? In this day and age?! Yes. Especially in this day and age. AI agents are running increasingly obscure git commands in our terminals, and most of us just nod along, pretending we know what rerere does. If the machines get to have fun with git, so do we.","So to understand git better, Alexander built an RTS game with git at its core, and in this session you will see it in action! Expect jumps between alternate timelines using branches, time travel back and forth through history, and a front row seat to what happens when a merge conflict occurs between two buildings.","Somewhere between the conquests and the explosions, the weird parts of git start to make sense. Detached heads, rebases, the plumbing underneath, all of it becomes a lot less mysterious once you have seen it blow up an energy plant. Beginners will leave with a solid feel for how git actually works, veterans with a fresh look at a tool they use every day. Come for the explosions, stay for the fun and giggles!"],"favourites":316,"speakers":[{"name":"Alexander Chatzizacharias","company":"JDriven","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-da804363-5740-431f-acd7-b3a9c4ad99ab.jpg","bio":"Alexander, a 36-year-old Software Engineer at JDriven, holds dual Dutch and Greek nationality. He earned his master’s degree in Game Studies from the University of Amsterdam, where he discovered his passion for gamification and software engineering. Alexander aims to bridge the gap between game development and software engineering, believing that both industries have much to learn from each other. He is dedicated to integrating technologies and methodologies from both fields. Additionally, he enjoys experimenting with new technologies and cutting-edge sdk&#39;s.","linkedin":"alexander-chatzizacharias","bluesky":"glycin.bsky.social","twitter":"glyc1n"}],"start":"2026-10-08T11:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T12:40:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"wbzpP0T9w6I","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Chatzizacharias plays a real-time strategy game whose map is a Git repository, and uses it to explain snapshots, staging, three-way merges, custom merge drivers, the .git folder, rebasing and how history can be destroyed.","summary":["Alexander Chatzizacharias says he was motivated by Claude Code asking permission to run an obscure octopus-merge command he could not read. If Git is his last line of defense against agents, he wants to understand it. He recaps the history: the BitKeeper licence was revoked after someone on the kernel side reverse-engineered it, and Linus Torvalds started Git on 3 April 2005 with goals of merges in under three seconds, full distribution and protection against corruption. The first commit came on 7 April, Linux shipped with Git in June, and Junio Hamano, still the maintainer, took over in July. Chatzizacharias notes that the famous \"10 days\" of coding followed months of thinking.","After versioning his Elden Ring and Baldur's Gate 3 Honour Mode save files, he built an RTS on top of Git. The map is a text grid where each building is a run of characters. The game client is written in Unity/C#, a Quarkus sidecar watches the repository through JGit and the Git CLI and pushes changes over WebSockets, and custom merge drivers are compiled with GraalVM native image. The game shows Git states directly: a building in the working tree is just a reserved outline, staging it (an object with no commit, which gc could discard) gives half capacity, and committing makes it fully active. Switching branches makes buildings appear and disappear.","Merging two diverged branches shows a three-way merge against the common ancestor. Because Git merges line by line, two buildings on the same row of the map conflict, so he wrote a merge driver that compares each character and returns exit code 0 or 1. He explains the Merkle DAG of content-addressed blobs, trees and commits, and why git status is fast. A tagged wonder building represents the .git folder: HEAD, index, the logs behind reflog, refs for branches, tags and remotes, and objects that are packed into deltas on gc. A rebase is replayed visibly as a series of cherry-picks followed by a fast-forward merge.","Hidden buildings on another branch crush enemy units when merged in. He closes with a \"don't do this\" recipe for wiping history: write-tree and commit-tree onto an orphan branch, delete every other ref, expire the reflog, garbage-collect, then mirror-push so colleagues pull the damaged repository. His final message is that coding is a superpower and developers should build fun, odd things, not another LLM-wrapping notes app."],"keyPoints":["Git stores a full snapshot per commit, not diffs. It saves space through content addressing, and gc packs objects into deltas with the newest version stored whole.","Staged but uncommitted content already lives in .git/objects. Objects that no commit references are orphans and can be removed on the next garbage collection.","A three-way merge compares both sides with their common ancestor. Git's default works per line, and custom merge drivers can replace it, signalling a conflict through their exit code.","Blobs, trees, commits and tags form a Merkle DAG. A change anywhere changes the root hash, so git status only has to descend into folders whose hashes changed.","Branches, tags and remotes are just small files in refs that point to hashes. The logs folder records every HEAD movement and is what makes git reflog able to recover work.","A rebase is a series of cherry-picks onto the target branch, which then allows a fast-forward merge and a linear history.","History is only safe while something references it: deleting refs, expiring the reflog, running gc and mirror-pushing can wipe it for everyone."],"takeaways":["Open your repository's .git folder and look at HEAD, refs, logs and objects to see what your daily commands actually change.","Use git reflog to recover a lost branch or detached-HEAD work before assuming it is gone.","Write a custom merge driver for structured files where line-based merging produces false conflicts.","Read any unfamiliar Git command an agent proposes before approving it, especially ones that touch refs, the reflog or gc."],"quotes":[{"text":"Git is my last line of defense against you agents. If you screw something up, I'm going to use Git to fix it, so I might as well understand what it does.","at":"05:35"},{"text":"The world doesn't need another LLM-wrapping notebook app. Make fun things.","at":"44:48"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why talk about Git now"},{"at":"05:35","title":"Git's origin story"},{"at":"10:09","title":"From save files to RTS"},{"at":"13:44","title":"Snapshots and game architecture"},{"at":"19:06","title":"Working tree, staging, commits"},{"at":"24:00","title":"Merges and custom drivers"},{"at":"30:19","title":"Inside the .git folder"},{"at":"38:14","title":"Rebase and fast-forward"},{"at":"42:00","title":"Destroying history"}],"tech":["Git","JGit","Quarkus","WebSockets","Unity","C#","GraalVM Native Image","BitKeeper","Claude Code"],"watchIf":"You use Git every day but couldn't explain what a rebase, the reflog or a merge driver really does, and you'd like to learn it from a game with buildings exploding.","themes":["Developer experience & tooling","Hardware, robots & fun"],"demo":true}},{"id":21690,"title":"Exquisite Evals for Skills & MCPs","type":"Tools-in-Action","duration":30,"track":"Development Practices","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["documentation","javadocs","evals","mcp"],"abstract":["I built the javadocs.dev MCP server to help Java, Kotlin, and Scala developers give their agents more efficient access to documentation for the libraries they use. But until recently I didn't know if it was better than what base models, or base models with web search/fetch, use. I created a custom eval framework to provide verifiable evidence that the javadocs.dev MCP is up to 5x more token efficient than what the standard agents provide and provides correct answers, unlike the base models. This session will share the learned best practices for building and implementing evals that can provide the feedback you need to improve your agent skills & MCPs."],"favourites":96,"speakers":[{"name":"Christian Tzolov","company":"Spring Team @ Broadcom","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-970401c2-a699-4f02-8855-f855f219221a.jpg","bio":"Spring AI lead, Model Context Protocol (MCP) Java SDK maintainer, Agentic AI Foundation (AAIF) Ambassador, R&amp;D Engineer in Broadcom&#39;s Spring team and Apache Software Foundation (ASF) committer","linkedin":"tzolov","bluesky":"tzolov.bsky.social","twitter":"christzolov"},{"name":"James Ward","company":"AWS","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-607b90ef-e5ff-4f70-b91c-a3082787114d.jpg","bio":"Professional software developer since 1997, with much of that time spent helping developers build software that doesn&#39;t suck. A Typed Pure Functional Programming zealot who often compromises on his ideals to just get stuff done. Currently a Developer Advocate for AWS and AAIF Technical Committee member.","linkedin":"JamesWard","bluesky":"jamesward.com","twitter":"JamesWard"}],"start":"2026-10-06T16:20:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T16:50:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"mebPFAKSpzw","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":10802,"title":"A Deserter Returns: What the Java community helped me rediscover","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["java","quarkus","tamboui","picocli"],"abstract":["After several years of Java development, I had left the ecosystem behind. Not out of spite, but out of pragmatism: Python was getting the job done. Beefy CI/CD pipelines, easier to write; daily tools that bent to fit any need; in short, the simple joy of writing a Hello World without filling out three XML files first. The rise of AI cemented Python as the obvious choice. I had no reason left to reopen a Java IDE. None.","Except that a handful of friends kept the thread alive, with that community, with that ecosystem. A conference here, an article there, and little by little, certain projects started catching my eye. Step by step, I discovered what you could now build CLIs in Java  with picocli, with a type system that doesn't lie on top. Then came Quarkus: blazing-fast startup, native compilation, no more excuses about JVM heft. The final piece of the trinity was TamboUI: elegant TUIs and a pleasant DSL, offering a modern, refreshing take on terminal applications,  in Java.","In this talk, let me tell you how Java found its way back into my toolbox and my geeky heart: why I had left, why I thought I'd never come back, and how the community kept my mind open. No sermon, no holy war, just a language and technology lover reconsidering things, and who thinks you might want to take a second look too."],"favourites":50,"speakers":[{"name":"Thierry Chantier","company":"Dataiku","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocJIqLuDzHLmlJCLNs_i3ZQmzlennTw_KQgt3Dz4qdDy_JF2_w=s96-c","bio":"A passion born from reading, the world of computing and electronics caught me from my ZX Spectrum and has never let go.<br>A developer by trade, from video games to master data management, I&#39;ve also dabbled in the worlds of QA and production deployment.<br>I&#39;m now a Developer Advocate at Dataiku, juggling between tech, community, and knowledge sharing.<br><br>Since a passion always deserves to be passed on, I founded Mixteen to try to help children grasp the digital world.<br>This desire to share has also led me to take part in organizing several MiXiT conferences and then to join the board of the AFPy.<br>In parallel, I also try to share through blog posts and videos on my YouTube channel.<br><br>In short:<br><a href=\"https://dis.tontoncodeur.com/talks/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: rgb(253, 253, 252); color: rgb(0, 115, 230);\">https://calm.show/talks/</a><br><br>In detail:<br><a href=\"https://www.twitch.tv/titimoby\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: rgb(253, 253, 252); color: rgb(0, 115, 230);\">https://www.twitch.tv/titimoby</a><br><a href=\"https://mixteen.org/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: rgb(253, 253, 252); color: rgb(0, 115, 230);\">https://mixteen.org/</a><br><a href=\"https://tontoncodeur.fr/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: rgb(253, 253, 252); color: rgb(0, 115, 230);\">https://tontoncodeur.fr/</a><br><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/thierrychantier\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: rgb(253, 253, 252); color: rgb(0, 115, 230);\">https://www.youtube.com/thierrychantier</a><br><a href=\"https://www.afpy.org/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: rgb(253, 253, 252); color: rgb(0, 115, 230);\">https://www.afpy.org</a><br>","linkedin":"thierrychantier","bluesky":"titimoby.eu","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T15:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T16:40:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"i_VXe0mNicY","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":4100,"title":"Life in the Ivory Tower: Confessions of a Former Developer Who Turned Into an Architect","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Architecture & Modernization","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["collaboration","psychology","itdecisionmaking","enterprisearchitecture"],"abstract":["Up until a couple of years ago, I was riding the waves of the latest technologies, shipping code, and mocking the \"suits\" who spent their days writing documentation that was never read. But then, the unthinkable happened. I joined a new organization and transitioned to the dark side. I became one of them: an Enterprise Architect. Or as I like to call myself: a fake enterprise architect.","I quickly found out that my new reality meant dealing with consultants pitching one-shot solutions as the ultimate silver bullet (pun intended), and uncovering the psychology of IT by learning why technical decisions are rarely about technology. At the same time, I wanted to stay in touch with the real world where frameworks evolve and pipelines run. But how on earth do you find the time for that?","This talk is a transparent look inside my shiny new office, commonly known as the Ivory Tower. Whether you're a developer trying to challenge corporate IT decisions, an architect struggling to explain them, or you just want to make fun of enterprise architects (the real ones, that is): this session is for a broad audience, sharing lessons learned trying to bridge the gap between two worlds that should never be disconnected.","Expect to walk away with:","Tips on how to keep your technical skills alive while moving up the corporate ladder","A look into the psychology behind IT decision-making and why it's never just about the code","How to foster real collaboration and break down the walls of the Ivory Tower"],"favourites":499,"speakers":[{"name":"Robin Custers","company":"Cegeka","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-b38b2424-ab8d-420c-9791-eb71faab2fd8.jpg","bio":"Robin is a software architect at Cegeka, bringing over 20 years of experience in software development. He&#39;s progressed from developer to team coach to software architect, all while avoiding any serious coffee spills. Robin&#39;s expertise includes agile software development, extreme and not-always-so-extreme programming, domain-driven design and software architecture. He&#39;s a skilled at designing and implementing enterprise-class systems. He enjoys helping organisations achieve their strategic goals, one line of code at a time, making sure their IT dreams don’t turn into digital nightmares.","linkedin":"robincusters","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T11:05:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T11:45:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"nhVRKv8VHKU","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Custers, a developer turned enterprise architect, argues that the role's value is translating business needs into IT decisions, changing things in small experiments, and staying hands-on enough to stay credible.","summary":["Robin Custers opens with a midlife-crisis tractor stuck in the mud, then describes starting as enterprise architect at a newly formed government organisation in 2022. The CIO's consultant-written IT strategy had principles everyone agrees with (reuse over buy over build, security by design) and a roadmap of 'more secure, more efficient'. Seeing that the prison, family-violence and law-enforcement departments all stored client data, sent notifications, handled documents and integrated systems, she asked for one central system, and the consultants proposed Salesforce, mapping each need to a module and ticking every principle. Custers used the operating-model quadrants from Enterprise Architecture as Strategy (standardisation versus integration; McDonald's, airlines, Sony) to show the organisation was a 'coordination' type: different processes, shared data. That meant separate systems per department, sharing only non-core capabilities. Explained in the CIO's own terms, as James Watt sold steam engines in horsepower, the argument killed the Salesforce plan.","For governance he borrows from Facilitating Software Architecture: architecture principles, recurring architecture advisory forums with team leads, and public architecture decision records. Standardising on Java and Angular broke when a supplier wanted Python for a two-week AI proof of concept, so standards became defaults that can be overridden with an ADR. His platform strategy, inspired by Team Topologies, was rejected after five minutes as too much change, which led him to Larman's laws. He restarted with a one-person platform team, one capability and one application, framed as an experiment. On staying technical, he tells how his father ran a steel-cutting optimiser Custers wrote in Java 1.3 in 2000 for 25 years until the PC died, and how he rebuilt it with AI agents in a weekend using Java and React."],"keyPoints":["Vendor fit-to-requirements checklists and generic IT principles can justify almost anything; a consultant mapped every need, and every principle, onto Salesforce modules.","The operating-model grid (process standardisation versus integration) helps decide whether a central shared system makes sense; for a 'coordination' organisation it does not, beyond non-core capabilities like security and infrastructure.","Lightweight governance: principles for direction, a recurring architecture advisory forum, and ADRs visible to everyone, which carry strategy down and decisions back up.","Treat technology standards as defaults with documented exceptions rather than hard rules.","Following Larman's laws, big change initiatives tend to revert to the status quo; a one-person platform team proving one capability on one application got buy-in where a full platform plan did not."],"takeaways":["Pitch architecture decisions in the business's own units and language, not in product features.","Record decisions, including justified deviations from standards, as ADRs that everyone can read.","Frame organisational change as a small, time-boxed experiment to limit the blast radius and the fear.","Keep a hands-on pet project going so your technical judgement stays credible, and lean on peers for what you don't know."],"quotes":[{"text":"I'm not sure whether prisoners in prison are really looking for a better relationship with our organization.","at":"07:48"},{"text":"A lot of the technical decisions that we take, in reality, are often business decisions in disguise.","at":"34:30"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Stuck in the mud"},{"at":"03:26","title":"The Salesforce proposal"},{"at":"12:21","title":"Operating model quadrants"},{"at":"18:23","title":"Architect as translator"},{"at":"19:24","title":"Governance without blocking"},{"at":"25:41","title":"Starting change small"},{"at":"29:26","title":"Should architects code?"}],"tech":["Salesforce","Architecture decision records","Java","Angular","React","Python","Team Topologies"],"watchIf":"You're moving from developer into an architecture role, or keep losing arguments with enterprise architects, and want practical tactics on governance and influence.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership","Architecture & design"],"demo":false}},{"id":16435,"title":"LangChain4j Community BOF","type":"BOF","duration":60,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["java","llm","langchain4j","framework"],"abstract":["Curious about the evolution and future direction of LangChain4j? Join our Birds of a Feather (BOF) session for an open and interactive discussion with members of the LangChain4j community. Since its debut at Devoxx Belgium 2023, LangChain4j has rapidly grown into a key framework for integrating large language models into Java applications. Whether you're already using LangChain4j in production or just starting to explore its capabilities, this session is a great opportunity to connect with fellow developers, contributors, and enthusiasts. We'll discuss the current state of the project, real-world adoption stories, ongoing challenges, and exciting new developments on the roadmap. This is your chance to ask questions, share feedback, exchange ideas, and help shape the future of LangChain4j. Come with curiosity and leave with insights, connections, and inspiration!"],"favourites":26,"speakers":[{"name":"Dimitris Andreadis","company":"IBM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-c540ac38-0fdf-40cb-b0fe-8a357abd1f87.jpg","bio":"Dimitris is an open source veteran with 25+ years of experience in IT and he is currently a (Red Hat) Sr.Director of Engineering at IBM in charge of the Cloud Native Runtimes group that includes Quarkus, Vert.x, SpringBoot and Node.js. He also served as the JBoss AS project lead and he has been a JBoss addict and contributor from the early start-up days. He worked previously at Intracom and Motorola in the areas of NMS/OSS, designing reusable frameworks and distributed systems. Dimitris studied computer science at the Technological Educational Institute of Athens and received an M.Sc. by research from University College Dublin, Ireland.<br> <br> Dimitris is a passionate advocator of Opensource &amp; Red Hat technologies and has presented on a variety of events and conferences around the world. He has been an invited speaker at various Java/Linux community events and user groups, and he has delivered inspirational talks on technology and Open Source in universities and educational institutes. In his spare time he has also helped with the organization of technology enablement events for children, like Scratch Days, Devoxx4Kids and JCrete4Kids.","linkedin":"dandreadis","bluesky":null,"twitter":"dandreadis"}],"start":"2026-10-05T17:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T18:00:00Z","room":"BOF 2","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"An open BOF with the LangChain4j community on the project's current state, adoption stories, challenges and roadmap.","summary":["The session is an interactive discussion with members of the LangChain4j community, hosted by Dimitris Andreadis. LangChain4j debuted at Devoxx Belgium 2023 and has become a key framework for integrating LLMs into Java applications.","Participants discuss the project's current state, real-world adoption stories, ongoing challenges and roadmap developments, and are invited to ask questions, give feedback and exchange ideas, whether they use it in production or are just starting."],"keyPoints":["Discussion of the current state and future direction of LangChain4j.","Real-world adoption stories and ongoing challenges.","Roadmap developments.","Open Q&A and feedback from users and contributors."],"takeaways":["Bring feedback or feature requests for LangChain4j to the maintainers."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["LangChain4j"],"watchIf":"You use or are considering LangChain4j and want to talk with its community.","themes":["LLMs in Java apps","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":10016,"title":"Measuring the Unmeasurable: Evaluating Generative AI in Practice","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["rag","llm","generativeai","evaluation"],"abstract":["Getting a generative AI prototype to impress a demo audience is easy. Getting it to behave reliably in production is another story. In production, you don't control what users ask or what the model answers. That's not a bug, that's the whole point of using LLMs. But it is also what makes evaluation genuinely hard, and what separates a convincing proof of concept from a trustworthy production system.","This session tackles the core paradox of building with large language models: how do you harness their creativity while maintaining control over their outputs? We will use a RAG-based chatbot as our running example, a real-world case where you have to guarantee output quality without ever controlling what users actually ask, and where linguistic fluency and factual accuracy constantly pull in opposite directions, to show what a complete, reproducible evaluation pipeline actually looks like in practice.","Along the way, we will cover why traditional metrics fall short, how to use LLMs as judges and where that approach quietly fails, when human evaluation is non-negotiable, and how to set up continuous monitoring to catch drift before your users do.","You will leave with practical frameworks and a clear methodology to make evaluation a constant driver of development, from the first prototype to the last production release."],"favourites":185,"speakers":[{"name":"Erin Pacquetet","company":"SCIAM","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-221944f9-cdfb-40ed-93c6-0c8a396d106e.jpg","bio":"Erin Pacquetet is a Senior AI and Data Scientist at SCIAM, where she designs, develops, and deploys production-grade AI products.<br>At the intersection of linguistics and computer science, she brings a creative, human-centered approach to hard engineering problems, translating product challenges into robust, scalable AI architectures that work for both technical teams and business stakeholders.<br>Her expertise spans prompt design and optimization, advanced data pipeline management, model evaluation, and the integration of industrial-grade AI systems.<br>Beyond her industry work, Erin is an active researcher and community contributor. She regularly speaks at meetups and conferences on topics including AI model analysis, pragmatic AI integration in software development, and human–machine interaction through language.","linkedin":"erinpacquetet","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-06T10:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T11:20:00Z","room":"Room 8","videoId":"Qdn-I3q50Ww","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Using a client-facing insurance RAG chatbot as the example, Pacquetet lays out an evaluation pipeline built on a written evaluation dataset, narrow pass/fail metrics and human-calibrated LLM judges.","summary":["Pacquetet, a senior AI scientist at SCIAM, argues that the gap between an AI demo and production is trust, and that trust comes from turning 'try a question and eyeball it' into a pipeline that outputs a score at the click of a button, used before each release and to guide every prompt or architecture change. The pipeline has three steps: simulate inputs across ideal, realistic (typos, keywords, a vague 'am I covered?') and adversarial (prompt injection, jailbreaks) scenarios; run them through the system; evaluate the outputs. Latency, cost and stability are monitored as for any service.","Her example chatbot has a triage agent that outputs only true or false, scored by simple string matching, followed by a RAG agent that writes the answer. The hard part is that free text. Working through 'Is renter's insurance mandatory for a tenant in France?', she shows correctness, relevance, completeness and safety pulling against business rules like a warm tone and short answers, with several different answers all acceptable. Writing expected answers in the evaluation dataset is slow, but it turns vague specs such as 'short' into a concrete contract with the business team.","She ranks metrics from deterministic (regex, string matching, F1) through similarity metrics (ROUGE, BLEU, BERTScore) to LLM-as-a-judge and human review. A judge works when given one narrow task, such as 'return true if the new answer contradicts the expected one', never 'is this answer good?'. Each question combines several pass/fail checks, secondary metrics such as a follow-up question are tracked without gating, and the judges are calibrated against business reviewers until verdicts agree. The continuous drift monitoring promised in the abstract was not covered."],"keyPoints":["Test inputs should cover three scenarios: ideal, realistic and adversarial; teams often skip the realistic one.","The evaluation dataset of inputs and expected outputs works as the executable spec and exposes contradictory or implicit business rules.","Prefer deterministic metrics whenever possible; semantic similarity cannot detect a wrong fact or a bad tone.","An LLM judge is reliable only with a narrow binary task, such as detecting contradictions with the reference answer.","Combine per-question pass/fail checks (a contradiction judge plus string matching for a required phone number), keep secondary metrics like a follow-up question non-blocking, and calibrate everything against human reviewers."],"takeaways":["Write expected answers for a set of ideal, realistic and adversarial questions together with your business team before tuning prompts.","Use string matching or regex for anything constrained, and keep LLM judges for the parts that need comprehension.","Rewrite any 'is this answer good?' judge prompt into single-purpose true/false checks.","Have business reviewers score a sample of outputs and adjust your judges until their verdicts match."],"quotes":[{"text":"If you want to find balance, you have to give up control.","at":"05:20"},{"text":"Similarity is not quality.","at":"26:53"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"From demo to production"},{"at":"04:17","title":"Why LLM products resist evaluation"},{"at":"07:49","title":"The three-step pipeline"},{"at":"11:42","title":"Evaluating the triage agent"},{"at":"15:45","title":"What is a good answer"},{"at":"23:04","title":"The evaluation dataset as spec"},{"at":"25:42","title":"Four families of metrics"},{"at":"33:09","title":"Combining pass/fail metrics"},{"at":"35:45","title":"Roadmap for your team"}],"tech":["RAG","LLM-as-a-judge","ROUGE","BLEU","BERTScore","Constrained output"],"watchIf":"You have an LLM or RAG prototype that works in demos and need a defensible way to show it is good enough for real users.","themes":["Testing & quality","LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":false}},{"id":17800,"title":"🚀 From Java to AI: Build Your First Intelligent Agent System!","type":"Hands-on Lab","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["java","ai","agents","langchain4j"],"abstract":["🎯 Revolutionize the way you see Java development with AI!","You know Java? Then you already hold 80% of the keys to building sophisticated AI systems. Don't let Python monopolize innovation: with LangChain4j, Java becomes the go-to language for production-grade AI.","On the agenda for this hands-on workshop:","🧱 Part 1: AI as a Service. Learn to inject intelligence via CDI just like any other Bean. Manage conversational memory with scopes (@SessionScoped) and give your agents \"arms\" through Function Calling.","🏗️ Part 2: The Agent Architect. Go beyond simple chat. Design complex declarative workflows (sequential, parallel) and master the Supervisor Pattern: an AI capable of planning and managing other agents autonomously.","🚀 Why attend? Walk away with ready-to-use patterns, a solid grasp of the MCP/A2A protocols, and the ability to integrate robust agents into your enterprise architectures.","Go from spectator to AI architect! 🚀"],"favourites":91,"speakers":[{"name":"Emmanuel Hugonnet","company":"IBM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocI_LNcfEMO7A9N-kuKXYVnc6dpm2KSwRws_s0f4sSklrbdqruzA=s96-c","bio":"Into the Java World for the last 25 years, I joined Red Hat in 2013 to contribute on the development of the JakartaEE server WildFly and moved to IBM in 2025.<br>I also contribute to a large number of OSS projects and I&#39;m a member of the Apache NetBeans Dream Team.<br>I&#39;ve been one of the founder of the <a href=\"http://snowcamp.io\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">SnowCamp</a> conference and I&#39;m a JUG leader of <a href=\"http://alpesjug.org\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">AlpesJUG</a>.<br>Recently I&#39;ve started to look into Generative AI and LangChain4J and I started the wildfly-ai-feature-pack to facilitate developing Generative AI JakartaEE applications with WildFly, contributing to <a href=\"https://github.com/langchain4j/langchain4j-cdi\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">langchain4j-cdi</a> and LangChain4J along the way.<br>I also contribute to the <a href=\"https://github.com/a2aproject/a2a-java\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">A2A Java SDK</a> providing a reference implementation of the <a href=\"https://a2a-protocol.org/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">A2A</a> protocol in Java.","linkedin":"ehsavoie","bluesky":"ehsavoie.bsky.social","twitter":"ehsavoie"},{"name":"Antoine Sabot-Durand","company":"SCIAM","image":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/489817?v=4","bio":"Antoine is a Java Champion and former spec lead for Jakarta EE CDI. He was part of the founding team behind Quarkus at Red Hat and worked on major MicroProfile specifications such as Fault Tolerance and Health Check. Today, Antoine is an architect and technical expert at SCIAM, where he leads complex projects largely built on the very technologies he helped design. He is also the co-creator of the Vidocq framework, a modern MicroProfile stack built on Java 25 and JPMS.","linkedin":"antoinesabotdurand","bluesky":"antoine.sabot-durand.net","twitter":null},{"name":"yann Blazart","company":"SCIAM","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AAcHTte0rDSnHTsgef7nTSqwxu70yJKQehjTgrVJ3oSkC7n6hlw=s96-c","bio":"Yann Blazart is a Senior Tech Lead and a passionate advocate of Jakarta EE and Quarkus. He has been in love with Java for 26 years and has been working with SCIAM since 2019. A long-time open-source contributor, Yann has taken part in projects such as WicketExtJs, TomEE, and JAX-WS Metro.<br>He is no stranger to the Devoxx stage: he ran two Hands-on Labs at Devoxx France (2012 and 2015) on Java EE, and more recently delivered a Hands-on Lab on Quarkus and Apache Ignite at Riviera Dev, Devoxx BE, and Devoxx MA in 2023. In 2024, he laid the foundations of what has since become langchain4j-cdi. In 2026, together with Antoine Sabot-Durand, he built Vidocq (<a href=\"https://vidocq.dev\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">https://vidocq.dev</a>) — the first fully European MicroProfile 7.1 runtime.","linkedin":"yblazart","bluesky":"yblazart.bsky.social","twitter":"yblazart"}],"start":"2026-10-05T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-05T10:30:00Z","room":"BOF 2","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A beginner lab on building AI agents in Java with LangChain4j and CDI: AI services, memory, function calling, agent workflows and the Supervisor pattern.","summary":["Part 1 treats AI as a service: attendees inject LLM-backed services through CDI like any other bean, manage conversational memory with scopes such as @SessionScoped, and give agents tools through function calling.","Part 2 moves to agent architecture: designing declarative sequential and parallel workflows and the Supervisor pattern, where an AI plans and manages other agents. The lab also aims to give attendees a grasp of the MCP and A2A protocols and patterns for integrating agents into enterprise architectures."],"keyPoints":["AI services are injected via CDI using LangChain4j.","Conversational memory is managed with scopes such as @SessionScoped.","Function calling gives agents tools.","Sequential and parallel workflows and the Supervisor pattern for agents.","Introduction to MCP and A2A protocols."],"takeaways":["Expose one existing Java service as a tool an LLM agent can call."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":["LangChain4j","CDI","MCP","A2A","Java"],"watchIf":"You are a Java developer wanting a first hands-on path into building agents.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","LLMs in Java apps"],"demo":false}},{"id":8167,"title":"Open Source in the Age of AI: Where's the value?","type":"BOF","duration":60,"track":"Development Practices","level":"ADVANCED","keywords":["ai","opensource","community","business"],"abstract":["While some folks believe Open Source is important to do Because It's The Right Thing To Do, others have been able to persuade their organisations to open source valuable IP because it brings something to the company. Still others have been able to make money off an open source project they started.","Has this changed in the age of AI? If AI is being trained on open source and freely available code, does that make open source libraries and tools more or less valuable? Can an organisation still make money off their open source tools if AI agents can just go ahead and create code for them without needing training, consulting or support contracts?","Or was the value never in the money it brought, but in the community story? Making your code freely available, creating useful documentation and example code, supporting the community that grows around your open source tool might be the real goal, not simply the path to the destination.","Trisha wants to bring together open source maintainers, contributors and enthusiasts to discuss difficult questions around running an open source tool. Like:","How does it support itself financially?","How do you decide what belongs in an open source offering vs what should be commercial/paid?","Why do we create open source tools in the first place?","What are the pros and cons of having open source tools in an AI world?","How do we build businesses around open source tools?","How do we use open source tools to support businesses?"],"favourites":112,"speakers":[{"name":"Trisha Gee","company":"Digital Aguacates","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocLsMb8CUpH7W48b3uQ9kMWx6uoYjj07hJVCxmz2MT5fbaMVope5ow=s96-c","bio":"Trisha Gee is a Java Champion, author, and internationally recognised speaker who has spent over 20 years building software and helping developers build it better. From high-performance financial systems to developer tools, she brings hands-on experience and a clear, practical perspective on what actually makes teams effective, and how engineering organisations build better software.<br><br>She is the co-author of Head First Java and Getting to Know IntelliJ IDEA, and her talks, articles, and videos are widely used by developers to navigate complex topics and improve how they work. She focuses on developer productivity, proven engineering practices, and the role of human judgment in an increasingly AI-assisted world.<br><br>Trisha got her start in developer communities, founding and supporting user groups and engaging directly with developers. She continues to champion community-led learning, inclusive practices, and the idea that great software comes from people, not just tools.","linkedin":"trishagee","bluesky":"trishagee.bsky.social","twitter":"trisha_gee"}],"start":"2026-10-08T16:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T17:50:00Z","room":"BOF 2","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"A BOF led by Trisha Gee asking where the value of open source lies in the age of AI, covering funding, commercial boundaries and community.","summary":["Trisha Gee brings together maintainers, contributors and enthusiasts to discuss whether AI, trained on open code and able to generate code on demand, makes open source libraries more or less valuable, and whether organizations can still earn money from training, consulting or support.","Questions for discussion include how an open source project supports itself financially, what belongs in the open offering versus a paid one, why we build open source in the first place, the pros and cons in an AI world, and how to build businesses around open source tools and use them to support businesses."],"keyPoints":["Does AI change the value and business case of open source?","How projects fund themselves and split open from commercial features.","Whether the real value is the community, documentation and examples.","Pros and cons of open source tools in an AI world."],"takeaways":["Clarify why your organization or project is open source before deciding what to charge for."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":[],"watchIf":"You maintain or fund an open source project and wonder how AI changes its value.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":false}},{"id":9025,"title":"The Quantum Tech Lead: Hands-On and Abstract at the Same Time","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"People, Practices & Career","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["leadership","technical","influence","transition"],"abstract":["So you got \"promoted\" to lead a team / project / whatever. What now? Do you have what it takes? It certainly seems that your hierarchy thinks so. But oftentimes things are not so black and white. Being a great plumber doesn't automatically make you a great leader of plumbers, so you need to adapt and adjust your ways of working by dropping old habits and picking up new ones. Then you start getting worried by the fact that you won't be able to have as much impact as before, since you're not coding as much as you used to in the past (and that's what you're good at, right?).","What if leadership can amplify your technical impact instead? In this talk, I’ll share how I evolved from individual contributor to a mix of Solution Architect, Tech Lead and classical Engineering Manager at CERN - keeping my hands in the code while scaling my influence through the team that I work with. I'll be sharing best practices and adjustment techniques for you to use when faced with the difficult transition process into technical leadership. And lastly, we'll be talking about how to stay relevant even if you're not spending countless hours honing your coding skills anymore."],"favourites":237,"speakers":[{"name":"Cristian Schuszter","company":"CERN","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-8a031ed5-f55f-4758-8655-819459e29ce6.jpg","bio":"Dr. Cristian Schuszter holds a PhD in Systems Engineering with a focus on distributed fault-tolerant software architectures and machine-learning based failure prediction. In his day-to-day, he is somewhere between a Data and Full-stack Software Engineer, with a wide range of tech &amp; job exposure both in academia and industry.<br><br>For the past 8 years he&#39;s been working with various teams in CERN (the European Organization for Nuclear Research). Currently he&#39;s focusing on the architectural directions of the CERN business computing group, with an emphasis on enterprise Java systems and UI.<br>","linkedin":"cschuszter","bluesky":"cschuszter.bsky.social","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-09T07:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-09T08:20:00Z","room":"Room 9","videoId":null,"twin":null,"s":{"source":"abstract","tldr":"Cristian Schuszter of CERN shares how to move from individual contributor to technical leadership while staying hands-on and scaling impact through the team.","summary":["The talk addresses the transition after being promoted to lead a team or project: adapting ways of working, dropping old habits and worrying about having less impact because of less coding. Schuszter argues that leadership can amplify technical impact instead.","Drawing on their own path at CERN to a mix of Solution Architect, Tech Lead and Engineering Manager, the speaker shares best practices and adjustment techniques for the transition, and how to stay relevant without spending countless hours on coding."],"keyPoints":["Being a great engineer does not automatically make you a great leader.","Leadership can amplify technical impact by scaling influence through the team.","The speaker combines Solution Architect, Tech Lead and Engineering Manager roles at CERN.","Techniques for staying technically relevant while coding less."],"takeaways":["Identify which old habits to drop when you take on a lead role."],"quotes":[],"chapters":[],"tech":[],"watchIf":"You are a new or aspiring tech lead worried about losing technical impact.","themes":["Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":12758,"title":"Knocking on the Wrong Door: The Six Developers You’ll Meet in Security","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"People, Practices & Career","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["security culture","com-b","psychological safety","secure development"],"abstract":["Secure by Design does not fail only because developers lack security awareness. It often fails because teams prescribe the wrong intervention: training when the real blocker is time, tools, motivation, or trust. In this practitioner-focused talk, we introduce SECUR-E, the OWASP Security Culture Project v2.0 framework that uses COM-B to help developers and software teams diagnose what actually blocks secure development behavior.","Through a relatable story, practical examples, and six memorable developer patterns, Skeptic, Enthusiast, Compliant, Unaware, Resistant, and Embedded, the audience will learn why one-size-fits-all security programs miss, bore, or backfire. We will show how a lightweight self-assessment maps Capability, Opportunity, and Motivation into team insights, then translates those insights into better interventions: evidence for Skeptics, support for Enthusiasts, autonomy for Resistant profiles, foundational help for Unaware developers, and amplification for Embedded security-minded builders.","Key takeaways: stop asking “why don’t developers care?”; diagnose whether the blocker is skill, environment, or motivation; choose interventions that fit the pattern; protect psychological safety; and use SECUR-E as a practical playbook for building secure software teams without blame. Audience: software developers, tech leads, security champions, and engineering managers with beginner-to-intermediate knowledge."],"favourites":137,"speakers":[{"name":"Enrique Larios Vargas","company":"OWASP","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-3a74a43d-dc7b-46a1-84da-7840cb0dff8f.jpg","bio":"Enrique Larios Vargas is a Security and Learning Specialist with over 8 years of experience designing impactful learning and enablement programs across fintech, engineering, and security domains. With a background as a university lecturer in software engineering in Peru, the Netherlands, and Canada, he brings a unique blend of technical insight and behavioral science to his work. Enrique is the lead author of the research paper <em>“DASP: A Framework for Driving the Adoption of Software Security Practices”</em>, which explores how behavioral models like COM-B can drive secure development. He is passionate about helping developers move beyond compliance and build a meaningful, human-centered security culture.","linkedin":"enrique-larios-vargas","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T14:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T15:20:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"qrtd6svRUnk","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Larios Vargas argues security programmes fail by prescribing training for every problem, and presents a COM-B diagnosis with six developer behaviour patterns to match interventions to barriers.","summary":["Larios Vargas, co-lead of the OWASP Security Culture Project, uses a hallway of doors as his metaphor. Leadership holds keys (mandatory training, policies, tools, checklists), but each door is a different barrier to behaviour change. The typical result: 100% training completion, good quiz scores, a green compliance dashboard, and the same pen-test findings six months later. When security fails, organisations add more training, tools and controls, then ask 'why don't developers care?'. He calls that the wrong question: knowing is not doing, and training only opens the door when the blocker really is knowledge, not time, tools, autonomy or trust.","To diagnose, he borrows the COM-B model from behavioural science: Capability (can I do it?), Opportunity (can I do it here?) and Motivation (will I do it, especially under pressure?). Developers first assess themselves, then discuss the results in a psychologically safe team conversation to find the patterns that dominate the team. He stresses that a pattern describes a current state rather than a personality trait, and must never be used for labelling, hiring, performance reviews or discipline; the self-assessment is therefore anonymous, and team leads see only the predominant patterns.","The six patterns each need a different door. Skeptics ('nobody has ever attacked us') lack motivation and need evidence, such as what late fixes cost, not fear. Enthusiasts lack a supporting environment; give them time, recognition, a champion role and early tool access. Compliant developers stop at the checklist and need the purpose behind it. Unaware developers need foundations, ideally next to an enthusiast. Resistant developers are often skilled; treat objections as feedback and let them co-design controls. Embedded developers should be made role models, not taken for granted. One training lands six different ways, and AI-driven code volume raises the stakes. The SECUR-E self-assessment is still being validated."],"keyPoints":["Mandatory training produces green compliance dashboards without behaviour change; six months later the same pen-test findings return.","COM-B frames secure behaviour as Capability, Opportunity and Motivation, giving three diagnostic questions: can I do it, can I do it here, will I do it.","Six patterns are identified: Skeptic, Enthusiast, Compliant, Unaware, Resistant and Embedded; a person can mix two or move between them.","Each pattern maps to a different intervention: evidence for Skeptics, support and recognition for Enthusiasts, meaning for Compliant developers, foundations for Unaware ones, co-design and autonomy for Resistant ones, amplification for Embedded ones.","Adding pressure to a Resistant developer increases resistance, while making them co-authors of a control can turn them into advocates.","The SECUR-E self-assessment is anonymous by design so results support teams rather than label individuals in reviews or hiring."],"takeaways":["Before launching another security training, find out whether your team's real blocker is skill, time, tooling, autonomy or trust.","Ask your team the three COM-B questions in a safe, blame-free retrospective and look for the predominant pattern.","Give your security enthusiasts protected time and a formal champion role instead of extra unrecognised work.","Invite the most vocal critics of a security control to help redesign it.","Try the OWASP Security Culture Project's SECUR-E self-assessment and send the project feedback on how well it fits your context."],"quotes":[{"text":"Just knowing is totally different than doing.","at":"06:48"},{"text":"Before prescribing, you should diagnose.","at":"13:25"},{"text":"All enthusiasts deserve a good charging station in their organizations.","at":"22:39"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Keys and doors"},{"at":"03:36","title":"Why training alone fails"},{"at":"07:50","title":"Doors as behavioural barriers"},{"at":"11:14","title":"The COM-B model"},{"at":"16:02","title":"Diagnose without labelling"},{"at":"18:16","title":"Six developer patterns"},{"at":"29:54","title":"Matching support to barriers"},{"at":"35:12","title":"Trust and the SECUR-E assessment"}],"tech":["OWASP Security Culture Project","COM-B","SECUR-E"],"watchIf":"You run a security champions programme or lead a team where security training keeps being repeated without fewer findings.","themes":["Security & supply chain","Teams, careers & leadership"],"demo":false}},{"id":16401,"title":"Beyond encryption at rest: computing on encrypted data","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Security, Trust & Compliance","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["cryptography","homomorphic encryption","fully homomorphic encryption","confidential ai"],"abstract":["Cloud providers encrypt data at rest and in transit, but most applications still decrypt information before processing it. Homomorphic Encryption (HE) challenges this model by allowing computations to be performed directly on encrypted data: the server computes the result without ever seeing the inputs.","This talk introduces the fundamentals of HE, from early partially homomorphic cryptosystems to Craig Gentry's 2009 breakthrough, explaining core concepts like ciphertext noise, noise budgets, and bootstrapping in an accessible way. We'll cover how modern schemes like CKKS, BGV, and TFHE differ in what they compute efficiently, and explore practical use cases: confidential AI inference where a model produces predictions without seeing user inputs and financial services where credit scoring operates entirely on encrypted records.","We'll also discuss current limitations (performance overhead, ciphertext size, and developer complexity) and how hardware acceleration and compiler tooling are rapidly closing these gaps. Attendees will leave with a clear picture of where HE is practical today and where it is not, and concrete pointers to start experimenting with open-source libraries."],"favourites":124,"speakers":[{"name":"Andrea Iacono","company":"","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocKiRDaPqgBWULLG0v-gb1k2a_Qm91MkYKsUy_4pXTRW6AsaFXi_=s96-c","bio":"Andrea is a software engineer with a broad experience in several industries ranging from e-commerce to industrial automation, from public administration to Web TV / music. His interests are in algorithms, data and AI.","linkedin":"andreaiacono","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-07T15:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T16:40:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"ePQyy9sLmsY","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Iacono explains homomorphic encryption from RSA through BFV noise budgets and Gentry's bootstrapping, then runs an OpenFHE health-score demo that is about 20,000 times slower than plaintext.","summary":["Andrea Iacono starts from where data lives: TLS covers the network and encryption at rest covers disk, though he warns that under the US CLOUD Act an American provider can be compelled to hand over plaintext even for EU-hosted data, so keys should not be held by the same provider. Memory is the gap: apps decrypt data to process it, and Meltdown, Spectre, cold-boot attacks, Heartbleed, Cloudbleed and Log4Shell all exposed in-memory data. Homomorphic encryption closes it by letting a server add and multiply ciphertexts so the decrypted result matches the same operations on plaintext. Textbook RSA is homomorphic for multiplication only, making it partially homomorphic.","He then builds intuition for BFV, which rests on learning with errors (shown with scalars; real schemes use large polynomials). The plaintext modulus t splits the 0..q range into q/t buckets; each value sits somewhere in its bucket plus noise. Additions add little noise, but each multiplication pushes the value further until it leaves the bucket and decrypts to garbage, so BFV is somewhat homomorphic and a larger t means fewer operations. Craig Gentry's bootstrapping evaluates decryption homomorphically to re-centre the noise, giving fully homomorphic encryption at the cost of tens of seconds per value. In practice you size the parameters for a known multiplicative depth and avoid bootstrapping.","The demo is a cardiovascular risk score in Python using OpenFHE's bindings (no Java binding exists). The client builds a BFV context with plaintext modulus 65537 and multiplicative depth 1, encrypts age, cholesterol, HDL, blood pressure and Boolean flags, and sends serialized ciphertexts. The server computes a weighted sum blind, and the client decrypts the score (430) and classifies it, since comparisons are costly in BFV. His benchmark: 0.43 microseconds in plaintext against 7.71 milliseconds encrypted, with serialization taking more than twice the computation. He closes on testing, branching costs and how CKKS and TFHE differ from BFV."],"keyPoints":["Homomorphic encryption lets a server compute on ciphertexts: the encryption of a sum or product equals the sum or product of the encryptions, so results decrypt correctly on the client.","Textbook RSA is partially homomorphic (multiplication only); BFV is somewhat homomorphic (both operations, limited count); bootstrapping makes a scheme fully homomorphic.","In BFV, noise grows slightly with each addition but quickly with each multiplication; once a value leaves its q/t bucket it decrypts to a wrong value, and a larger plaintext modulus t means fewer allowed operations.","With OpenFHE's leveled SHE mode you declare the multiplicative depth up front and the library picks q so no bootstrapping is needed; bootstrapping can take tens of seconds per value.","His benchmark: 0.43 microseconds in plaintext versus 7.71 milliseconds homomorphically, roughly 20,000 times slower, and he said results five years ago would have been about ten times worse.","OpenFHE and similar libraries do not raise an error when noise overflows; they silently return a wrong value, so use oracle tests with known results and boundary cases, and never mock the library.","Branching is the costliest operation in BFV (approximated with polynomials), which is why the demo classifies on the client; TFHE handles Boolean gates and lookups cheaply but integer arithmetic poorly, and CKKS works on approximate real numbers for machine learning.","Learning with errors, the basis of BFV, is considered quantum-safe."],"takeaways":["Keep encryption keys for cloud-hosted databases outside the provider that hosts the data.","Try OpenFHE's Python bindings on a small weighted-sum computation before considering homomorphic encryption for a sensitive workload.","Choose the scheme by workload: BFV for exact integer arithmetic, CKKS for approximate real-number or ML maths, TFHE for comparisons and Boolean logic.","Test homomorphic code with oracle tests at parameter boundaries against the real library, since overflow returns silently wrong values."],"quotes":[{"text":"OpenFHE and a lot of other libraries do not throw an error. If you decrypt a value and the library gives you back a value which is not the one you encrypted, it silently gives you back the wrong value.","at":"38:48"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Network, disk and memory"},{"at":"04:14","title":"What homomorphic encryption is"},{"at":"06:55","title":"RSA as partially homomorphic"},{"at":"09:39","title":"Learning with errors and BFV"},{"at":"15:01","title":"Noise and buckets"},{"at":"18:43","title":"Gentry's bootstrapping"},{"at":"21:25","title":"Health score with OpenFHE"},{"at":"34:57","title":"Live run and benchmark"},{"at":"38:48","title":"Testing, branching and schemes"},{"at":"45:29","title":"Q&A"}],"tech":["OpenFHE","Python","BFV","CKKS","TFHE","RSA","Learning with errors","TLS"],"watchIf":"You handle sensitive data such as health or financial records and want an accessible, maths-light explanation of what homomorphic encryption can do today and what it costs.","themes":["Security & supply chain"],"demo":true}},{"id":9008,"title":"G1, ZGC, Shenandoah, ... with all these GCs in Java, which one do I choose?","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["garbage collection","zgc","java","shenandoah"],"abstract":["It feels like with every new Java release, there are more and more Garbage Collectors (GCs) coming with more and more options. We hear cryptic phrases like: \"Oh, awesome, ZGC became generational while Shenandoah isn't\" or \"Did you heard how Netflix reduced their tail latencies with ZGC?\"","Naturally, it leaves us with a ton of questions:","What on earth are they talking about?","What exactly is ZGC?","If this ZGC is so magical, why are there even other GCs left in Java anyway?","Why do we only ever talk about different GCs for Java, but never for Go or JavaScript? Can the Java folks make up their minds and pick one?","And what about native? Do we even need a GC there?","Whether you are a Java developer or just plain curious, everyone is welcome! We will trace the journey from the creation of Java all the way to the present day to understand why we need a GC and why it isn't a solved problem. We will also take a tour of the various GCs, their evolutions, their strengths, and their weaknesses. (Spoiler alert: ZGC is not the one GC to rule them all).","By the end of this talk, you will know exactly when to use which GC so you can find the perfect fit for every project, every application, and every environment."],"favourites":151,"speakers":[{"name":"Antoine DESSAIGNE","company":"Axway","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-763af354-0c0f-4884-9525-88a9034759dd.jpg","bio":"A Java developer with over 20 years of experience, Antoine has been a key part of Axway since 2012.<br><br>He dedicated 13 years to their Analytics software, evolving from developer to software architect while maintaining a hands-on development role. His final five years on the project were focused on performance optimization, cloud migration, and security.<br><br>Antoine is passionate about open source and reports bugs to community-driven projects. He is always eager to migrate to the latest JDK and introduce new features to his team.<br><br>Today, he is leveraging his expertise to build an AI Gateway solution, tackling complex code, and mentoring others.","linkedin":"antoine-dessaigne-3618aa37","bluesky":"adessaigne.bsky.social","twitter":"adessaigne"}],"start":"2026-10-07T12:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T12:50:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"8nY-kqwoD_A","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Dessaigne explains how each HotSpot garbage collector works, from Java 1's mark-and-sweep to generational ZGC and Shenandoah, and gives a simple guide for which GC fits which workload.","summary":["Dessaigne, from Axway, starts with how a simple Conference object is really three objects (header and fields, a String, a byte array), and how the heap set by -Xms/-Xmx is only part of a JVM's memory next to thread stacks and off-heap memory. That is why a pod's memory limit must be higher than -Xmx. Java 1, built for 1990s set-top boxes with megabytes of RAM, marked everything reachable from GC roots (locals and statics) and swept the rest. That left fragmentation, and scan times grew with the heap. The generational hypothesis led to SerialGC in 1998, with Eden, two survivor spaces, an age counter for promotion to old, copy-then-wipe collection, and card tables kept by a write barrier to track old-to-young references.","SerialGC is single-threaded and stops the world but has the lowest overhead. ParallelGC adds threads for maximum throughput but can pause for seconds on large heaps. CMS was concurrent but couldn't compact. G1, the default since Java 9, splits the heap into thousands of regions, uses humongous regions for objects larger than half a region, and does mixed collections that pick the regions with the most garbage first. It aims for a 200 ms pause target. Dessaigne said almost every G1 performance gain since Java 7 has come from its remembered sets, and that a full GC on G1 means something is wrong. Because pauses add up across chained microservices, he says to measure percentiles end to end, not averages for one service.","ZGC and Shenandoah keep pauses under a millisecond by moving objects concurrently behind a load barrier, at a cost of up to about 20% more CPU and memory. ZGC stores colour bits in the pointer. Generational ZGC, the only mode since Java 24, fixed the old triple-counted memory that Kubernetes saw, still checks a pointer in two instructions, and can't use compressed pointers. Shenandoah stores a forwarding pointer in the object header and has many tuning knobs. Epsilon never collects. GraalVM native images get Serial or Epsilon, with G1 only in Oracle GraalVM. He covered JDK 26's 5-15% G1 throughput gain and G1 becoming the default everywhere in JDK 27."],"keyPoints":["Set the container memory limit well above -Xmx: thread stacks and off-heap memory (Netty, for instance) sit outside the GC-managed heap.","Young collections copy live objects to a survivor space and wipe Eden in one go; the age counter lives in 4 header bits, which is why the tenuring threshold can't exceed 15.","G1 picks regions with the most garbage first, targets 200 ms pauses, and should essentially never run a full GC; seeing one signals a problem.","ZGC and Shenandoah give sub-millisecond pauses but cost up to roughly 20% extra CPU/memory and can hit allocation stalls if GC threads fall behind, which is worth monitoring.","Generational ZGC (opt-in in Java 21, sole mode from Java 24) reports memory correctly to Kubernetes, unlike the old multi-mapped colored pointers.","GraalVM Community Edition and Mandrel native images ship only SerialGC and Epsilon, so the GC is single-threaded; Oracle GraalVM adds G1. Dessaigne mentioned an open pull request bringing Shenandoah to Community Edition.","According to Dessaigne, JDK 26 improved G1 throughput by 5-15%, and JDK 27 makes G1 the default even on small machines where SerialGC used to be chosen.","His rule of thumb: SerialGC for small microservices under 2 GB, Epsilon for short-lived CLIs and serverless, ParallelGC for batch jobs and Kafka Streams, ZGC or Shenandoah when latency is critical, and G1 for everything else."],"takeaways":["Pick the collector by workload (overhead, throughput or latency) rather than defaulting to the newest one.","Measure GC impact with end-to-end percentiles and GC logs, not averages on a single service.","Run on the latest JDK even if you compile for an older release; collector improvements come for free.","Set -Xms equal to -Xmx, and consider large pages and AlwaysPreTouch, then stop adding tuning flags.","Audit and remove inherited GC flags nobody can justify for your current JDK and load."],"quotes":[{"text":"Epsilon never collects anything, it just allocates until it crashes, and the application dies.","at":"39:06"},{"text":"There's nothing worse than having a flag set by some architect four years ago, who's left the company, and everybody is too afraid to remove it.","at":"46:51"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Why so many GCs"},{"at":"02:03","title":"Objects and JVM memory"},{"at":"05:06","title":"Java 1 mark and sweep"},{"at":"09:50","title":"Generations and SerialGC"},{"at":"19:09","title":"ParallelGC and CMS"},{"at":"22:15","title":"How G1 works"},{"at":"28:36","title":"ZGC and colored pointers"},{"at":"34:51","title":"Shenandoah and Epsilon"},{"at":"39:06","title":"Choosing a GC and native"},{"at":"41:40","title":"Future GCs and tuning flags"}],"tech":["G1","ZGC","Shenandoah","SerialGC","ParallelGC","CMS","Epsilon GC","GraalVM","Mandrel","Kubernetes","Netty","Kafka Streams","compact object headers"],"watchIf":"You run Java services and want a clear mental model of how each GC works and a practical rule for picking one.","themes":["JVM performance & runtime"],"demo":false}},{"id":19714,"title":"Specialized AI Agents: from concept to production","type":"Deep Dive","duration":180,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["optimization","ai","agents","docker"],"abstract":["More and more of us use **Coding Assistants** that excel at **writing code**.","They are **good** and they are **expensive**!","For our business needs, we need **more specialised AI agents** that are as good","or ever better, but for a much **cheaper** price.","The same underlying models can be used to write","more **specialized, cheaper, more predictable AI agents**.","We'll start by writing very disposable agents in literally seconds, **without code**.","And then we'll demonstrate how to grow such agent into solving a real business case.","We'll iterate on the agent, we'll give it tools and then we'll evaluate it and optimize it with **solid evals**.","We'll monitor its costs and see how changing models help or degrade.","Finally, we'll discover how multiple agents can connect and collaborate in production","with **MCP, ACP and A2A**.","All of that can be done with almost no code, leveraging [Docker Agent](https://docker.github.io/docker-agent/),","an open-source project and a real Swiss Army knife for AI agent creation."],"favourites":85,"speakers":[{"name":"David Gageot","company":"Docker","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-00adb09a-3b25-4aa0-a9c0-c7a771d40845.jpg","bio":"Engineer at Docker, working an AI agents.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"dgageot.bsky.social","twitter":"dgageot"}],"start":"2026-10-06T11:30:00Z","end":"2026-10-06T14:30:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"npSWywhFDHo","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":4096,"title":"Deterministic by Design - Achieving Reproducible Builds with Maven","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Development Practices","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["maven","compliance","determinism","reproducibility"],"abstract":["Modern software delivery pipelines face increasing pressure from auditors, regulators, and internal governance teams. Yet many builds remain non‑reproducible. This lack of determinism creates blind spots in compliance, undermines traceability, and exposes organisations to risks when validating what actually went into production.","This session demonstrates how developers can use Apache Maven features to reliably produce identical artifacts from identical inputs. You’ll also learn how to diagnose where reproducibility breaks down and how Maven’s built‑in verification tools can highlight sources of non‑determinism.","Join this talk to understand why reproducibility is becoming a non‑negotiable requirement for responsible software development and what you can do to make your build deterministic by design."],"favourites":149,"speakers":[{"name":"Maarten Mulders","company":"Info Support","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-caf6823d-09ef-45bb-8e40-43a6fd1d1e8e.jpg","bio":"Maarten is a solution architect, senior Java developer, and technical trainer with a passion for developer productivity and software quality. He is recognised as a Java Champion and Oracle ACE Pro for his contributions to the Java community through speaking, mentoring, and content creation. Maarten is passionate about &quot;building the right thing&quot; and &quot;building the thing right&quot;. He loves to share new ideas and knowledge through speaking and <a href=\"https://maarten.mulders.it\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">his blog</a>. When he&#39;s not writing code or speaking at conferences, Maarten enjoys exploring creativity through photography, music, and experimenting in the kitchen - in no particular order.","linkedin":"mthmulders","bluesky":"mthmulders.bsky.social","twitter":"mthmulders"}],"start":"2026-10-07T12:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-07T12:50:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"9vtYvAI2Dtk","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Mulders shows why bit-for-bit reproducible Maven builds matter for supply-chain security and EU compliance, and how to diagnose, fix and independently verify them with the Maven Artifact plugin, Diffoscope and Reproducible Central.","summary":["Mulders opens by building a near-empty POM twice and getting different SHA-256 sums seconds apart. Reproducibility means that the same source produces the same artifact, bit for bit. The usual causes are file timestamps, entry ordering, floating dependencies, environment leaking into the build, and stale files (he once shipped an empty stray directory to Maven Central). He argues it is no longer an engineering puzzle. On quality, it lets you check a published library against its source amid rising CI/CD attacks, makes build caches useful, and lets you rebuild an old release plus a single hotfix. On compliance, he reads the Cyber Resilience Act, DORA, NIS2 and the Medical Device Regulation as requiring proof of what runs in production and how it got there, with the caveat that this is his interpretation, not legal advice.","The demos use the maven-artifact-plugin. artifact:compare checks a fresh build against a reference and writes a buildcompare file with Diffoscope instructions. Diffoscope then shows a config.properties where an API key from the build machine leaked into the reference jar. artifact:check-buildplan reports a missing project.build.outputTimestamp, a plugin too old to be reproducible, and an SLF4J version range that, combined with shading, pulled 2.0.20 instead of 2.0.19. The fixes: pin dependency and plugin versions and keep them current with Dependabot or Renovate (Maven 4 warns about unpinned default plugins), set project.build.outputTimestamp once and let the Release plugin update it, and use maven-jar-plugin 3.2.0 or later for stable entry order. Some demo steps didn't behave as rehearsed: the timestamp diffs and the leaked key didn't show up again.","For outside proof, Mulders uses Reproducible Central (jvm-repo-rebuild/reproducible-central on GitHub). A buildspec file lists the coordinates, source repository, Maven and JDK versions and build command. A script rebuilds the artifact in a Docker container and compares the POM, jar and sources jar (not Javadoc). Once a project is listed, new releases are checked automatically, which is how his stray-directory mistake was caught, and projects can show a badge. Building from a fresh checkout, which release:perform does, prevents stray files. Signatures only show the key holder meant to upload those bits. The pgpverify-maven-plugin lets consumers pin which keys they expect for each dependency."],"keyPoints":["Even an almost-empty POM gives a different jar on each build, because zip entries carry the build time as timestamp unless project.build.outputTimestamp is set.","mvn artifact:check-buildplan flags a missing output timestamp, plugins too old for reproducible output (with the minimum version needed), and dependency version ranges.","mvn artifact:compare rebuilds and compares against a reference artifact, producing a buildcompare file and Diffoscope commands for drilling into differences in jars.","Version ranges plus shading make output depend on the day you build: a new SLF4J patch release changed the shaded jar.","Unpinned plugin versions depend on the Maven version each developer runs; Maven 4 logs a warning for default lifecycle plugins without a pinned version.","Reproducible Central rebuilds Maven Central artifacts from a buildspec in Docker and keeps checking each new release once a project is listed.","Mulders reads CRA, DORA, NIS2 and MDR traceability requirements as strong pointers towards reproducible builds, while advising teams to confirm with compliance officers.","PGP signatures only prove intent by the key holder; pgpverify-maven-plugin lets you specify which keys you expect to have signed each dependency."],"takeaways":["Add project.build.outputTimestamp to your POM and run mvn artifact:check-buildplan to find the remaining blockers.","Replace dependency version ranges with exact versions and pin every plugin, using Dependabot or Renovate to keep them current.","Build releases from a fresh checkout of the tag (as release:perform does) so stray local files can't leak in.","Add your open-source library to Reproducible Central so that someone else independently verifies every release.","Check config files packaged in your jars for values injected from the build environment, such as API keys."],"quotes":[{"text":"When it comes to trust, I would say I prefer an established proof rather than assumptions that will probably be good.","at":"15:24"},{"text":"It's still a little bit of me checking my own work rather than somebody else checking my own work. That would be the actual proof.","at":"33:58"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Can you rebuild it?"},{"at":"02:35","title":"Defining reproducibility"},{"at":"05:02","title":"A failing empty POM"},{"at":"07:50","title":"Quality reasons"},{"at":"11:25","title":"Compliance: CRA, DORA, NIS2"},{"at":"16:25","title":"artifact:compare and Diffoscope"},{"at":"21:13","title":"Diagnosing with check-buildplan"},{"at":"23:19","title":"Pinning versions and timestamps"},{"at":"33:58","title":"Reproducible Central"},{"at":"41:54","title":"Wrap-up and signatures"}],"tech":["Apache Maven","Maven 4","maven-artifact-plugin","maven-jar-plugin","Maven Release Plugin","Maven Build Cache","Diffoscope","Reproducible Central","SLF4J","Dependabot","Renovate","Docker","pgpverify-maven-plugin","Cyber Resilience Act","DORA","NIS2"],"watchIf":"You publish Maven artifacts or work under audit or EU regulation and need to prove your binaries match your source.","themes":["Security & supply chain","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":true}},{"id":9017,"title":"AI in the Dark: Running LLMs in Air-Gapped Production Environments","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["air-gapped","hallucination","llm","production"],"abstract":["Building an AI agent is easy. Building one you'd trust in production is harder. Building one that runs with no internet access, no cloud APIs, and no external services: that's a different problem entirely.","This talk follows a real journey: taking an AI-driven service from demo to production in an air-gapped defence environment where OpenAI, Azure and others are simply not an option.","The cloud disappearing changes everything: local LLMs under real performance constraints, tool-calling without external infrastructure, hallucination you can't fix by swapping an API, and human oversight in a system that talks to nothing outside its own network.","This is a production story from an environment where \"it works on my machine\" was never good enough and that's because the machine is isolated, locked down, and the usual shortcuts don't exist."],"favourites":129,"speakers":[{"name":"Mihaela Gheorghe-Roman","company":"Systematic","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-c0e31945-3009-41b0-897c-e49880577f2a.jpg","bio":"Mihaela is a Senior Software Architect at Systematic, Romania, and an Oracle ACE Associate for Java. PhD in Artificial Neural Network algorithms, Associate Lecturer at the University of Cybernetics and Informatics in Bucharest, she is passionate about building production-grade systems and bringing real-world experience to the stage.<br><br>In her professional career, spanning over 15 years, she has worked in the telecom and defence domains with a focus on Java, Spring Boot, C, C#, Scala and Angular, and a growing interest in AI systems and agentic architectures.<br><br>Outside of tech, she holds a black belt in traditional Jiu-Jitsu, runs marathons, and travels the world whenever she gets the chance.","linkedin":"mihaela-gheorghe-roman-6304915a","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T15:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T16:30:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"kaT6UpLrzhU","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Gheorghe-Roman shows how a Spring AI agent for military resource allocation runs fully air-gapped: local models behind one ChatClient, self-hosted MCP tools, precise tool descriptions and human approval for writes.","summary":["Mihaela Gheorghe-Roman, a senior software architect at Systematic working on a command-and-control platform, describes taking an AI agent to production in a defence network with no internet, no cloud APIs and no external services. Only two people in the room had run an agent unattended in production. Air-gapped means more than pointing an SDK at localhost: no cloud inference, no pulling a newer model (an upgrade can trigger formal re-accreditation), no external registries or SaaS observability, and every dependency must be inside the boundary on day one. She names the category DDIL: disconnected, denied, intermittent, low-bandwidth.","Her simplified scenario: six units across three sectors, a priority-one mission, and an agent recommending allocations. The key decision comes before any code. Reads (unit status, availability, conflict detection) run freely and are logged. Any write that changes state a human must know about, such as reallocating a unit, can only be proposed and must wait for human approval; if in doubt, treat it as a write. Unlike a shopping order, a wrong reallocation cannot be returned. Third-party models like Mistral and in-house ones face the same accreditation; she said Anthropic's terms exclude defence use. The demo uses Ollama with Llama 3.2 as a stand-in.","The architecture is a Spring AI advisor chain (logging, approval, audit), a local chat model behind the provider-agnostic ChatClient, and @Tool methods, some exposed by self-hosted MCP servers owned by other teams. A small local model picks tools only from the description, so she gives a four-sentence docstring: what it returns, when to use it, the ID format, and a negative instruction pointing mission questions to another tool. In the recorded demo, with tools off the agent invents a status for a non-existent unit, Phantom 9; with tools on it says the unit does not exist. The approval demo shows approve and reject; a timeout counts as rejection."],"keyPoints":["Air-gapped deployment removes cloud inference, same-day model upgrades (an upgrade may require re-accreditation), external registries and SaaS observability; every dependency must be inside the boundary before the mission starts.","Split tools into reads, which run freely and are logged, and writes, which the agent may only propose with prepared parameters; any state change a human must know about is a write, and if in doubt it's a write.","A Spring AI advisor chain wraps each call in a fixed order: logging, approval, audit, with audit recorded on the way out once everything is resolved.","Keep every model behind Spring AI's ChatClient so switching local model profiles or adopting a newly accredited model is a configuration line, not a business-logic change.","Self-hosted MCP servers owned by different teams (units, logistics, intelligence) act as the tool registry; tools wrap existing internal REST services or operational data stores.","A small local model chooses tools only from the description, so write it as a prompt: what it returns, when to use it, the expected ID format, and a negative instruction naming the tool to use instead.","With tool calling off, Llama 3.2 confidently gave a status, location and 92% readiness for a non-existent unit; with tools on it reported the unit was not found. A bigger model does not fix poor descriptions.","Unanswered approval requests time out after a product-defined interval and count as rejections; proposals include a score breakdown and confidence level so the operator can see why the agent suggested them."],"takeaways":["Classify every agent tool as read or write before writing code, and route all writes through an explicit human approval step.","Rewrite your tool descriptions to state output, when to use, argument format and which similar tool not to confuse it with.","Access models only through a provider-agnostic abstraction such as Spring AI's ChatClient so a model swap is pure configuration.","Decide explicitly whether an approval timeout means reject, and log who approved what, when and why for later reconstruction.","Size hardware for worst-case concurrent load, not for the demo, when running models locally."],"quotes":[{"text":"If we are in doubt whether it is considered a read or a write, then it's a write operation. It's less risky.","at":"18:48"},{"text":"The description field isn't just a code comment, it's the actual prompt.","at":"33:40"},{"text":"Local models hallucinate exactly the same way cloud models do. Running on your own hardware doesn't make a model more thoughtful.","at":"40:13"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Agents with no internet"},{"at":"08:02","title":"Why defence raises the stakes"},{"at":"10:37","title":"What air-gapped takes away"},{"at":"13:06","title":"The battalion scenario"},{"at":"17:20","title":"Read versus write operations"},{"at":"21:17","title":"Local models and accreditation"},{"at":"24:11","title":"Architecture and MCP tools"},{"at":"33:40","title":"Tool descriptions as prompts"},{"at":"37:40","title":"Demo: grounding versus hallucination"},{"at":"41:41","title":"Demo: approval gateway"},{"at":"46:02","title":"Lessons beyond defence"}],"tech":["Spring AI","Model Context Protocol","Ollama","Llama 3.2","Mistral","JSON-RPC"],"watchIf":"You build agents for regulated or isolated environments such as defence, healthcare or finance and need a concrete pattern for local models, tool design and human approval of writes.","themes":["LLMs in Java apps","AI agents & agentic coding","Security & supply chain"],"demo":true}},{"id":41302,"title":"\"You May Now Optimize the Wedding\": Optimizing the Seating, Bachelor Party, and Honeymoon with Constraint AI","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Agentic Engineering & Tooling","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["optimization","routing","planning","scheduling"],"abstract":["Every developer has been there: sitting at a life event, thinking \"could I automate this?\" The answer is yes. And this talk will show you how.","There is a whole class of problems that AI alone cannot solve: scheduling, routing, and planning challenges where every decision affects every other decision. Timefold tackles these in production every day, across logistics, manufacturing, healthcare, and yes, apparently also weddings.","We will use the most important weekend of your life as our excuse to explore constraint optimization in practice, from seating charts to pub crawls to honeymoon itineraries, with real code, real constraints, and real solver magic.","You will leave knowing how to turn any \"could I automate this?\" moment into a working optimization solution."],"favourites":138,"speakers":[{"name":"Geoffrey De Smet","company":"Timefold","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GjnLAEFVLYxmRxddxXV3A1MLXmtDO7GbsZA2gSupw=s96-c","bio":"Geoffrey De Smet is founder/CTO of <a href=\"https://timefold.ai\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Timefold</a> and the creator of OptaPlanner, its predecessor. He’s an expert in planning optimization, an active open source contributor and an international speaker.<br><br>His technology is in use across the globe, to automate complex scheduling and routing of hundreds of thousands of employees, vehicles and machinery every day.<br>","linkedin":"ge0ffrey","bluesky":"geoffreydesmet.bsky.social","twitter":null},{"name":"Tom Cools","company":"Timefold","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocLiUjyjM49h8fxM3bZDY_EBsphO-BhswQ2kD7N4rUU3lgNYf2Hs=s96-c","bio":"Developer Relations Engineer for Timefold, Java Champion and co-organizer of the Belgian Java User Group. Tom has a decade worth of experience delivering systems and loves to share not only knowledge but also passion for our craft. <br><br>You can read more at his blog (http://www.tomcools.be) or follow him on Bluesky (@tomcools.be).","linkedin":"tom-cools-17547548","bluesky":"tomcools.be","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T10:55:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T11:35:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"lsvVJaigNp8","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"De Smet and Cools use a wedding seating plan, honeymoon itinerary and bachelor-party pub crawl to show why planning problems defeat brute force and how Timefold Solver models them.","summary":["Cools' sister-in-law is marrying on his birthday, Halloween, and his future brother-in-law's vibe-coded planning app did not work, so the two Timefold speakers rebuilt it with a Claude-generated front end, a REST layer and Timefold Solver on Quarkus. They start with requirements for the seating plan, collected partly from the audience: family sides together, fixed seats for the couple, food preferences, distance to the toilet or dance floor, accessible seats, keeping political extremes apart, and pairs to seat together or apart. Brute force breaks down fast: on a MacBook Pro, 10 guests took 27 seconds, 12 an hour, 16 about five years, and 20 an estimated 580,000 years.","Rather than vibe-coding an algorithm you then maintain, they argue for a solver library, as you use Spring or Quarkus instead of writing a web server. Guest is annotated @PlanningEntity and its seat @PlanningVariable; solver.solve() returns the best plan so far and keeps improving it. Constraints are Java filters with penalize, for example for guests who need an accessible seat but lack one; since filters run hundreds of thousands of times, no database calls. The audience voted hard or soft per rule, and food preference split the room. Pairing becomes four constraints. Flipping the political constraint from penalize to reward produced a plan that quietly mixes the extremes.","The honeymoon, a road trip south of Naples, uses @PlanningListVariable for an itinerary of polymorphic hotel and activity visits, with unassigned values allowed because there are more activities than days, plus opening hours and travel time. The bachelor party became timefold.beer, a public app that plans five rounds at Brussels' Beer Central within a budget and preferences, with presets for variety, calories and cost, and re-plans when a beer runs out. They close by mentioning Timefold's ready-made APIs such as Field Service Routing and a webinar on connecting deterministic solvers to agents."],"keyPoints":["Brute-forcing seat assignments grows factorially: 20 guests have about 2.4 quintillion orderings, an estimated 580,000 years of compute on a MacBook Pro.","Timefold Solver models a problem with @PlanningEntity and @PlanningVariable annotations, similar to JPA, plus constraints written in Java with filter and penalize.","Constraint code runs hundreds of thousands of times per solve, so it must stay cheap: no database calls in a filter.","Physical reality, such as no two guests in one seat, must be stated as a hard constraint; whether rules like food preference are hard or soft depends on the use case.","@PlanningListVariable with unassigned values allowed handles ordered itineraries where not every candidate activity fits.","The solver keeps a best-so-far solution, reports which constraints are broken, and can re-plan when input changes, such as a beer running out."],"takeaways":["Do not brute-force or vibe-code a custom search algorithm for scheduling or assignment problems; use a solver such as Timefold Solver.","List your constraints with stakeholders before writing code, and decide explicitly which are hard and which are soft.","Keep constraint functions free of I/O and expensive calls.","Use score analysis to explain to users why a plan broke a given rule."],"quotes":[{"text":"If you don't tell TimeFold or these sort of algorithms what's not allowed, it will do it.","at":"18:21"},{"text":"You wouldn't vibe code a web server like that. So why would you try to vibe code an algorithm like this?","at":"14:14"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"A stolen birthday"},{"at":"05:57","title":"Seating plan requirements"},{"at":"11:28","title":"Why brute force fails"},{"at":"14:14","title":"Modelling with Timefold Solver"},{"at":"18:21","title":"Hard and soft constraints"},{"at":"22:30","title":"Solving and sabotaging seating"},{"at":"25:07","title":"Honeymoon itinerary"},{"at":"30:02","title":"Bachelor party pub crawl"},{"at":"33:46","title":"Other use cases"}],"tech":["Timefold Solver","Quarkus","Spring Boot","Constraint Streams","Claude Code"],"watchIf":"You have a scheduling, routing or assignment problem and want a light-hearted introduction to modelling it with constraints in Java.","themes":["Architecture & design","Hardware, robots & fun"],"demo":true}},{"id":24202,"title":"Beyond the Pod: Architecting and Scaling AI Agents on Kubernetes","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["kubernetes","isolation","agents","scalability"],"abstract":["As we shift from simple LLM wrappers to autonomous AI agents, a critical question emerges: Where and how should they run? On Kubernetes, our instinct is to treat agents like traditional microservices, giving each its own Pod for isolation and identity. But AI agents aren’t microservices.","Unlike long-running APIs, agents are ephemeral, bursty, and highly stateful. They might spin up for a task, spawn parallel sub-agents, and then sit idle for hours awaiting human approval. Keeping a dedicated Pod alive for every agent is massively wasteful.","In this talk, we’ll explore Agent Substrate, an open-source system on Kubernetes that decouples the logical agent (\"Actor\") from the physical execution environment (\"Worker\" Pod).","What you will learn:","Heavy Multiplexing: Map thousands of stateful, idle AI agents onto a small pool of shared Kubernetes Pods.","Hibernate and Teleport: Look under the hood at how Substrate uses OCI containers and gVisor to achieve sub-second activation, session teleportation, and perfect state persistence across hibernation.","Rethink Identity & Security: Shift network policies and access controls away from the Pod level and attach them dynamically to lightweight execution units.","Leave this session with a fresh perspective on how to isolate and scale the next generation of AI applications without abandoning the Kubernetes ecosystem you already know."],"favourites":122,"speakers":[{"name":"Abdellfetah SGHIOUAR","company":"Google Cloud","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AEdFTp6g4oRfPAQoq5bJH9DFV4xWnkIek9N3i08DYxe4SQ=s96-c","bio":"Abdel Sghiouar is a senior Developer @Google Cloud. A co-host of the Kubernetes Podcast by Google and a CNCF Ambassador. His focused areas are High Scale distributed systems on Kubernetes, Service Mesh, and Serverless. With a background in datacenter scale architecture and operations and consulting. He spends most of his time working on optimizing GenAI Apps for large scale operations using Cloud Native Technologies and producing content targeting developers and ops professionals.","linkedin":"sabdelfettah","bluesky":"boredabdel.bsky.social","twitter":"boredabdel"}],"start":"2026-10-08T13:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T13:50:00Z","room":"Room 4","videoId":"3bIiAoYtAPs","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Sghiouar demos Agent Substrate, which multiplexes mostly idle, sandboxed agents onto a shared Kubernetes worker pool, snapshotting memory and disk so agents suspend and wake in under 500 ms.","summary":["Abdel Sghiouar, a Google Cloud developer advocate, presents Agent Substrate, which follows the Agent Sandbox project and which he said is being donated to the CNCF; it runs on any Kubernetes, not just GKE. Agents, from coding CLIs such as Codex to notebooks, are idle most of the time, waiting for the LLM or for human approval, so one pod per agent wastes resources. Agent images can also be tens of gigabytes, so pulling them from a registry on every start is too slow. Platform teams also need to sandbox what agents do, including prompt-injected web content.","Agent Substrate is, he admits, another scheduler on top of Kubernetes. It separates actors (the agents) from workers (pods in pre-provisioned worker pools built from templates). Agents wake via a router on a request or a monitor when their own loop runs. He said the controller activates an agent in under 500 milliseconds and handles 500 activations per second. Snapshots of memory and file system go to any S3-compatible store (GCS in the demo), so agents can 'teleport' between nodes. It has its own API server and router, a node-level snapshotter, a pluggable sandbox (gVisor, Kata Containers, Firecracker) and Postgres instead of etcd, which was too slow.","The demos on GKE use a kubectl plugin to suspend and resume actors. An in-memory HTTP counter keeps its count across suspends (5, 6, 7); an interactive shell suspends on exit and still has the file written; a Jupyter notebook resumes with the variable x still in memory. He shows agent spaces, a namespace-like grouping, with actors from different templates sharing one worker pod; teleportation is described but not shown. Users he cited include Lovable, which needs a workspace within 30 seconds of sign-up, and Anthropic. In Q&A: scale pools with HPA and the cluster autoscaler; agents cannot be queued yet."],"keyPoints":["Agents spend most of their time waiting for the LLM or a human, so mapping each agent to a long-running pod wastes compute.","Agent Substrate decouples actors (agents, shipped as ordinary OCI containers injected into pods) from workers (pre-provisioned pods built from templates).","Sghiouar said agents activate in under 500 ms and the controller handles 500 activations per second; it uses its own API server and Postgres because the Kubernetes API server and etcd were too slow.","Suspend snapshots both memory and file system to S3-compatible storage, which lets an agent resume on a different node ('teleport'); with gVisor even GPU memory can be preserved.","Sandboxing is pluggable through runtime classes: gVisor, a user-space kernel that intercepts syscalls, or micro-VMs such as Kata Containers or Firecracker, which need nested virtualization.","Bake shared dependencies into the worker pool image so agent containers stay small and start faster.","Agents wake via the router on an incoming request, via a monitor when their own loop needs to run, or suspend themselves through the API; agent spaces group actors for multi-tenancy.","It is framework-agnostic (LangChain, Google ADK, kagent, MCP servers, Codex, OpenCode) since an actor is just a process; Lovable uses it to meet a 30-second sign-up-to-workspace target."],"takeaways":["Measure how much of the time your agent pods are actually busy before giving every agent its own pod.","Run agents that execute tools or shell commands under gVisor or a micro-VM runtime class rather than standard runc.","Pre-build heavy agent dependencies into a warm worker image instead of pulling multi-gigabyte agent images on each start.","Put a horizontal pod autoscaler and cluster autoscaler on worker pools and accept a few seconds of cold start after idle periods."],"quotes":[{"text":"Haven't you just built another scheduler on top of Kubernetes? The answer is yes.","at":"01:58"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"From Agent Sandbox to Substrate"},{"at":"01:58","title":"Why agents waste pods"},{"at":"04:49","title":"Actors and worker pools"},{"at":"09:03","title":"Activation, snapshots and teleporting"},{"at":"10:29","title":"Components and sandboxing"},{"at":"13:23","title":"Demo: in-memory counter"},{"at":"19:01","title":"Demo: shell and Jupyter"},{"at":"25:30","title":"Agent spaces and multi-tenancy"},{"at":"29:05","title":"Recap and real users"},{"at":"34:34","title":"Q&A"}],"tech":["Agent Substrate","Agent Sandbox","Kubernetes","GKE","gVisor","Kata Containers","Firecracker","PostgreSQL","etcd","Google Cloud Storage","Jupyter","kubectl","Horizontal Pod Autoscaler","kagent","Google ADK","LangChain","KEDA","Knative"],"watchIf":"You run, or plan to run, many coding or AI agents on Kubernetes and want to see a working way to sandbox them and stop paying for idle pods.","themes":["Cloud native & Kubernetes","AI agents & agentic coding"],"demo":true}},{"id":16444,"title":"Faster Development with Java, Python, and Micronaut","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"INTERMEDIATE","keywords":["micronaut","graalvm","python","graalpy"],"abstract":["The Java platform has always been about performance, reliability, and a rich developer ecosystem. But what if those same capabilities could become the foundation for building modern polyglot applications?","In this session, we'll unveil a new pre-compiled, pre-optimized Micronaut runtime built on GraalVM that delivers fast startup, low memory footprint, and a consistent programming model across Java, Kotlin, Python, and other Micronaut-supported languages.","Unlike approaches that require expensive native image builds before you see the benefits, this runtime is pre-compiled ahead of time, keeping the developer feedback loop fast. Make a source change, restart, and you're back in milliseconds—while still taking advantage of GraalVM's performance and native deployment capabilities when you're ready for production.","You'll see how Micronaut's dependency injection, configuration, testing, observability, and cloud-native features extend naturally across languages, enabling polyglot applications without sacrificing operational consistency. We'll also explore why this architecture is an ideal fit for AI-powered systems, combining Java's production strengths with the flexibility of Python where it makes sense.","If you're interested in the future of Java, GraalVM, cloud-native runtimes, and the next generation of polyglot application development, this session offers an early look at what's coming next."],"favourites":108,"speakers":[{"name":"Graeme Rocher","company":"Oracle","image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AATXAJzhbdYUh7mbNrEpoDq6EodpI71GUlmEK-1k0lb_=s96-c","bio":"Graeme Rocher is the creator of several popular Open Source projects including Grails (https://grails.org) and Micronaut (https://Micronaut.io) and co-author of &quot;The Definitive Guide to Grails&quot; (Springer - https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4302-0871-6). Graeme currently works as an Architect at Oracle. Graeme is a member of the Java Champions (https://twitter.com/java_champions) and 2018 was awarded the Groundbreaker award by Oracle (https://developer.oracle.com/groundbreakers/) for his work on Open Source.","linkedin":"graemerocher","bluesky":null,"twitter":"graemerocher"},{"name":"Thomas Wuerthinger","company":"Oracle","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-4f0ca29a-e775-47ce-be77-6e01a95e9abe.jpg","bio":"Thomas Wuerthinger is a Vice President at Oracle leading programming language implementation teams for languages including Python, JavaScript, and Java. He is the architect of the Graal compiler and the Truffle self-optimizing runtime system. Previously, he worked on the Crankshaft optimizing compiler of V8 at Google, and the Maxine research virtual machine at Sun Microsystems. He received a PhD degree from JKU Linz for his research about dynamic code evolution.","linkedin":"thomaswue","bluesky":"thomaswue.dev","twitter":"thomaswue"}],"start":"2026-10-08T13:00:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T13:50:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"zo6L7bxn4OY","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":5541,"title":"Dream Machines & Walled Gardens","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"Mind the Geek","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["computing","ted nelson","hypertext","decentralization"],"abstract":["Ted Nelson was a troublemaker. In 1974, frustrated by the closed and bureaucratic world of computing, he wrote Computer Lib/Dream Machines, a book that was part manifesto and part blueprint for a digital future. Nelson saw computers not as tools of control but as portals to personal freedom, creativity and interconnected knowledge. He championed hypertext long before the web, called out the dangers of technical jargon designed to exclude ordinary people and fought for systems that put users in charge.","This talk tells the story of Computer Lib, the man behind it and the philosophy that shaped it. It explores how Nelson’s ideas could have led to a very different digital world, one built around open, decentralised knowledge rather than the walled gardens and algorithmic control we have today. What if his vision had won? What would the internet look like if computing had followed the path of Computer Lib instead of Big Tech?","Nelson once wrote that “most authority is malignant.” This is a talk about the man who wanted to dismantle it and the future we lost when the world ignored him."],"favourites":101,"speakers":[{"name":"Anders Norås","company":"Indipendant ","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-a2db74e7-a445-4ab1-b7a8-4598218cf7f4.jpg","bio":"Anders Norås started out in arts and design, but for the last 25 years he has been creating magic with code instead of paint. Blending creativity with technical skill, he brings a perspective on technology that is as much about people as it is about software.<br><br>He has given more than 100 talks at conferences around the world. His presentations are not just lectures, they are experiences. With a sharp delivery and high energy, Anders turns even dry technical material into something lively, memorable and often funny.<br><br>Whether you are into computer science, design, media or anything else, Anders offers insights that will make you think and probably make you laugh. He does not just talk about technology. He brings it to life.","linkedin":null,"bluesky":"andersnoras.com","twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T08:40:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T09:30:00Z","room":"Room 10","videoId":"am8adCMJQzk","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":21671,"title":"The Rise of Agent Enablement: Coding Agents Don't Scale Themselves. Neither Do Your Teams.","type":"Conference","duration":50,"track":"People, Practices & Career","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["organization","platform","readiness","agents"],"abstract":["In 2009, every big enterprise said \"continuous delivery won't work here.\" In 2026, the same rooms say \"the dark factory won't work here.\" It was never about the technology being ready. It's about the organization being ready. Readiness is a socio-technical problem, not a technical one.","This talk applies Conway's Law all the way up, through four layers of enablement:","0. Enable the agent : this is where the tech is standing today (context + harness).","1. Enable the team : the new mentality of fixing system instead of the code, the changing role, winning over skeptics, new planning/retro ceremonies, and measuring evolution by human-touches-down and sharing-up.","2. Enable the platform: the shared context, harness and infra substrate teams and agents both stand on: model routing, MCP gateways, sandboxes, skill/harness registries, evals, guardrails, paved roads that stay easy paths (not cages), and FinOps that optimizes rather than caps.","3. Enable the organization: the VP of Engineering's job: hiring and interviewing for the new roles, defending a maturing ROI, right-sizing teams, keeping humans able to judge in the \"dim factory,\" and building the real moat : capturing knowledge.","**Target audience**:","Engineering leaders, platform/DevEx engineers, and senior ICs driving agentic-coding adoption. It's for anyone past \"we gave them coding tools\" and asking why it isn't scaling across the org."],"favourites":264,"speakers":[{"name":"Patrick Debois","company":"Tessl","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-481871d5-5634-4b17-bee7-759446ce2816.jpg","bio":"Patrick is a true pioneer—credited with coining the term DevOps, co-authoring the DevOps Handbook, and launching the very first DevOpsDays back in 2009. Since then, he’s been shaping the tech industry with his unmatched ability to bring development, operations, and now GenAI together in truly transformative ways. He now works at Tessl where he shapes up Product Devrel and the AI Native Dev community.<br><br>Here’s why you don’t want to miss this:<br>🔹 Independent consultant at the cutting edge of GenAI and DevOps<br>🔹 Fractional CTO, advisor, and workshop leader<br>🔹 Former VP of Engineering, Distinguished Engineer, CTO<br>🔹 Guided teams at industry giants like Atlassian and Snyk<br>🔹 Active speaker, community builder, and lifelong learner<br><br>Patrick is all about raising the bar—helping companies embed engineering rigor into their GenAI journeys, inspiring teams to embrace AI for automation, and advising GenAI platforms on how to truly deliver value. He bridges the gap between management and engineering with a rare mix of deep tech expertise and people-first thinking.<br><br>💬 Ready to hear where the future of DevOps, GenAI, and software delivery is headed? This is your chance.","linkedin":"patrickdebois","bluesky":null,"twitter":"patrickdebois"}],"start":"2026-10-08T09:50:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T10:40:00Z","room":"Room 6","videoId":"wxNQRGPnJ24","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Debois argues scaling coding agents is organizational, not technical: treat context like code with its own lifecycle, enable team, platform and org, and the real new problem is concurrency control across many agents.","summary":["Debois, who works on agentic factories at Tessl, frames the talk around Conway's Law working both ways: how an org absorbs AI, and how AI reshapes the org. His central metaphor is that context (skills, AGENT.md, specs, plugins) is the new code, with its own delivery lifecycle mirroring the SDLC: you write context, test it (linters on skill format, evals with LLM-as-judge, security scanners after incidents like a compromised MCP, cost tests), distribute it as versioned packages through marketplaces and package managers, observe it through agent logs and PR feedback, and deal with its drift, tech debt and conflicting opinions between people's skills.","He then climbs the enablement ladder. Enable the team: developers become conductors who stop touching code and instead fix the agent, its harness and context ('build the thing that builds the thing'), with new planning and retro rituals asking whether a task is fleshed out enough for an agent; team leads must make shared context quality part of the improvement cycle or everyone drifts solo. Enable the platform: an AI platform team (overlapping cloud, DevEx and AI) provides model routing, MCP gateways, sandboxes, skill and harness registries, evals and paved roads, and must actively go out and help teams, which reopens interesting technical work. Enable the org: the VP of Engineering funds and owns it, and measures not just DORA but human-touches-down (agent effectiveness) and how many people an improvement is reused by (org-wide multiplier rather than a solo 10x).","The weekend insight that reshaped the talk: factories as serial loops are underwhelming, and the real emerging problem is concurrency. As agents run in parallel on shared codebases they trip over each other, as Anthropic's 16-agent C-compiler experiment showed dependencies capping throughput. Debois reframes the pipeline as traffic control across planning (task dependency graphs), isolation (micro-VMs like Forkd, data versioning with Dolt, copy-on-write), semantic merging on intent not just text, cache invalidation across parallel work, stacked PRs and release trains, and risk-based review (auto-merge low-risk CSS changes, human review for auth) toward progressive delivery. He floats concurrent production versions, best-of-N variants, even per-customer versions, and notes at Tessl that sales, marketing and support now work on the same factory, moving the bottleneck to docs and training that cannot keep up."],"keyPoints":["Context (skills, AGENT.md, specs) should be managed with a full delivery lifecycle: linters, evals, security scanners, cost tests, versioned packages and observability from agent logs.","Enablement has four layers: agent, team, platform, organization; adoption stalls when only individuals improve context and the team lead doesn't make shared context quality a ceremony.","An AI platform team provides model routing, MCP gateways, sandboxes, skill/harness registries, evals and guardrails, and must actively enable teams, not just host infrastructure.","New leadership metrics: human-touches-down (how few human corrections an agent needs) and reuse (how many people one context improvement benefits), alongside existing DORA metrics.","Running many agents on a shared codebase surfaces a concurrency problem; Anthropic's 16-agent C-compiler build was limited by inter-task dependencies.","Concurrency tooling spans task dependency graphs, micro-VM forking (Forkd), Git-like data versioning (Dolt), semantic/intent-level merging, cache invalidation, stacked PRs and release trains.","Review should be risk-based: auto-merge low-risk changes, require human review and evidence for high-risk areas like authentication, then progressive release.","Debois proposes concurrent production versions and per-customer variants as a possible new normal, and observes non-engineering teams joining the same factory."],"takeaways":["Treat your team's context (skills, AGENT.md, specs) as a versioned, tested, shared artifact, not per-laptop files.","Make 'did we improve the agents and context?' a standing item in planning and retros.","Stand up or designate an AI platform team that both runs the substrate and actively enables other teams.","Track human-touches-down and cross-team reuse as adoption metrics, not just per-commit speed.","Label code by risk so low-risk changes can auto-merge while high-risk areas get human review and progressive rollout."],"quotes":[{"text":"Stop building the thing, but build the thing that builds the thing for you.","at":"09:46"},{"text":"After automation comes the coordination of the automation.","at":"41:33"},{"text":"The only good staging is production.","at":"36:15"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"Conway's Law both ways"},{"at":"01:33","title":"Context is the new code"},{"at":"04:17","title":"Testing and packaging context"},{"at":"08:41","title":"Enable the team"},{"at":"12:07","title":"Enable the platform"},{"at":"16:04","title":"Enable the organization"},{"at":"19:10","title":"Factories and concurrency"},{"at":"23:08","title":"Where agents trip up"},{"at":"30:38","title":"Risk-based review"},{"at":"37:19","title":"Concurrent production versions"},{"at":"43:55","title":"Coordination and the moved bottleneck"}],"tech":["AGENT.md","Skills","MCP","LLM as a judge","Forkd","Dolt","Stacked PRs","Release trains","Feature flags","Conway's Law","DORA metrics"],"watchIf":"You lead or enable engineering teams past 'we gave them coding tools' and want a framework for scaling agentic coding across an organization.","themes":["AI agents & agentic coding","Teams, careers & leadership","Developer experience & tooling"],"demo":false}},{"id":50104,"title":"Engineering Lessons from Working on Software Older Than Me","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Development Practices","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["performance","security","pdf","compatibility"],"abstract":["What do you learn after spending years maintaining and evolving a technology that predates modern programming languages, cloud platforms, and smartphones?","This session explores real engineering challenges encountered while building PDF technology at scale, from compatibility and performance to security, testing, and interoperability. Through a series of stories and hard-earned lessons, you'll see why mature systems can be some of the best teachers for designing resilient software and why the hardest problems are often the ones users never notice."],"favourites":114,"speakers":[{"name":"Guust Ysebie","company":"Apryse","image":null,"bio":"Software engineer at Apryse, specializing in PDF technologies and the iText SDK. With nearly 7 years of experience in backend development, he works on core PDF internals, standards compliance.","linkedin":"guust-ysebie-648749291","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T10:55:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T11:35:00Z","room":"Room 3","videoId":"VaT7DgmVSms","twin":null,"s":null},{"id":50109,"title":"Keeping Spring Applications Secure in the Age of AI","type":"Lunch Talk","duration":40,"track":"Java Language & Platform","level":"BEGINNER","keywords":["security","spring","automation","upgrades"],"abstract":["Spring is the most widely used Java framework in the world, and millions of applications depend on it. Keeping","them up to date used to be a matter of discipline. That has changed. In August 2026 alone, 96 security","vulnerabilities were published in the Spring ecosystem, driven by AI-assisted research. Models such as","Anthropic's Mythos find weaknesses that survived years of manual review, and attackers can use the same","capability.","In this session, we look at what this means for teams running Spring in production and how to respond. We","show how Long Term Support releases for Spring ease the pressure of constant upgrades, how Spring","Application Advisor automates patching and upgrades inside your CI pipeline, and how direct access to the","Spring team helps when automation is not enough. All of this is part of VMware Tanzu Spring, delivered by the","vendor of Spring itself. Customers like SAP rely on exactly this combination to keep their most complex","applications secure and current."],"favourites":170,"speakers":[{"name":"Neven Cvetkovic","company":"VMware Tanzu","image":"https://devoxxian-image-thumbnails.s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/profile-3a22fb4c-0e7d-491c-98ea-b3681d8a81a4.jpg","bio":"Neven Cvetkovic is a seasoned veteran of the Java ecosystem with over 20 years of experience tackling complex enterprise Dev, Sec, and Ops challenges. At VMware Tanzu, he guides Fortune 2000 organizations through their cloud-native journeys and helps them build, deploy, and scale enterprise AI workloads. Neven is passionate about AI engineering and regularly shares his hard-earned knowledge to help development teams modernize application architectures effectively.","linkedin":"neven","bluesky":null,"twitter":null}],"start":"2026-10-08T10:55:00Z","end":"2026-10-08T11:35:00Z","room":"Room 7","videoId":"-LMWT5OCneA","twin":null,"s":{"source":"transcript","tldr":"Cvetkovic argues AI-driven vulnerability discovery makes continuous patching mandatory for Spring apps, and demos Broadcom's commercial Spring Application Advisor upgrading Pet Clinic from Boot 2.7 to 3.5.","summary":["Cvetkovic, a Tanzu solution engineer at Broadcom, opens with the threat picture in this vendor lunch talk. Frontier models used by researchers, and by Broadcom through Anthropic's Project Glasswing, are finding flaws faster, and attackers can turn a published fix into an exploit within hours. He said the Spring team has fixed over 200 CVEs since April, against about 177 in the previous 13 to 14 years, and over 90 across the ecosystem since June. He cited a study in which only 17 of 100 AI-generated patches were clean, durable fixes. His remedy is continuous automated patching, filtering CVEs by actual exposure, and staying on upstream-maintained builds, which leads into the pitch for Spring Enterprise: a private repository with extended support for the last minor versions (Boot 2.7.35 against 2.7.18 in open source, 3.5 and Framework 6.2 beyond their open source end of life).","The demo runs Spring Application Advisor, a CLI built on OpenRewrite plus proprietary Broadcom recipes, on Spring Pet Clinic at Boot 2.7.3, where Trivy reported 135 CVEs, 18 critical. 'build-config get' maps the dependency tree, 'upgrade-plan get' lays out steps up to Boot 4.1 and Java 25, a patch apply to 2.7.35 cut it to 11 (3 critical, in Tomcat), and 'upgrade-plan apply' with squash jumped to 3.5 in about two minutes, optionally opening a PR. One medium Log4j CVE remained because its fix needs a minor bump. He also showed mapping files for in-house starters like a bank's shared Boot wrapper, and a not-yet-released option that attaches coding-agent skills to upgrade steps."],"keyPoints":["According to Cvetkovic, the Spring team fixed more CVEs in a few months of 2026 than in the previous 13 to 14 years, driven by AI-assisted research.","Spring Enterprise extends patch support for the last minors before a major (Boot 2.7, 3.5, Framework 6.2) via a private Artifactory-style repository.","Application Advisor offers deterministic patch upgrades (with transitive pinning) and multi-step upgrade plans that can be squashed and submitted as pull requests.","On the demo app, patching alone took Trivy findings from 135 to 11; the remaining Log4j issue showed that some fixes need a minor version change and a judgement call on whether the library is actually used.","Mapping files define recipes per version step for internal libraries; agent skills per step were shown as unreleased work."],"takeaways":["Run a scheduled pipeline that applies patch-level dependency updates several times a day, not just on a monthly cycle.","Scan your Spring apps with Trivy or a similar tool and prioritise CVEs by whether the vulnerable code is reachable.","Plan your move off Spring Boot 2.7 and 3.5 now, or budget for commercial extended support if you cannot.","Try the free Spring Academy App Advisor lab before evaluating the tool for your pipeline."],"quotes":[{"text":"As soon as you publish things, bad actors can use this to produce exploits, faster than ever before.","at":"10:53"}],"chapters":[{"at":"00:00","title":"AI-accelerated vulnerabilities"},{"at":"05:43","title":"Continuous patching and noise"},{"at":"07:07","title":"The Spring CVE spike"},{"at":"12:18","title":"Extended support releases"},{"at":"17:37","title":"Spring Application Advisor"},{"at":"20:27","title":"Upgrading Pet Clinic demo"},{"at":"31:37","title":"Internal libraries and agent skills"}],"tech":["Spring Boot","Spring Framework","Spring Application Advisor","Tanzu Spring","OpenRewrite","Trivy","Log4j","Tomcat","Spring Academy","SLSA"],"watchIf":"You maintain Spring Boot 2.7 or 3.x apps and want to see what Broadcom's commercial upgrade tooling and extended support actually do.","themes":["Security & supply chain","Spring & frameworks","Legacy modernization"],"demo":true}}],"recap":null};
